// AUTO-GENERATED authored deep overlay for the test screen: concept-from-scratch +
// why EVERY wrong option is wrong, for the constructed mocks that had only one-line explanations
// (pep 1/2, MET mock 1/3/4, diagnostic, bio, nimhans). Keyed by mock id -> ordered array
// index-matched to data-all.js (stem-guarded in explainHTML).
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"pep-mock-1":[{"q":"Which ministry is the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) under?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) began in 1986 as a registered society and became a statutory body through the Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 (RCI Act 1992). An amendment in 2000 widened its role to include promoting research in rehabilitation and special education. Its jobs are to regulate training programmes for rehabilitation professionals, recognise qualifications (such as the M.Phil and the Professional Diploma in Clinical Psychology), and maintain the Central Rehabilitation Register (CRR) of qualified professionals. Because the RCI deals with disability and rehabilitation, it sits under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, within its Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities (Divyangjan). The same department administers the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 and the National Trust Act 1999. Bodies under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare include the National Medical Commission (NMC Act 2019), NIMHANS and the Central Mental Health Authority set up under the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA) 2017. The Ministry of Education oversees the UGC and NCERT, and the Ministry of Labour and Employment runs employment and social security schemes. An easy rule: medical councils follow Health, the disability and rehabilitation council follows Social Justice. This is why an Indian clinical psychologist, though working in hospitals and named as a mental health professional in the MHCA 2017, is registered through a disability-focused body rather than a health one. Option d is right because the RCI is a statutory body under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, through its Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities.","traps":{"a":"The Ministry of Education oversees school and higher education through bodies such as the University Grants Commission (UGC), NCERT and the National Council for Teacher Education. It tempts because the RCI recognises university courses and special-education teacher training such as the B.Ed in Special Education, so it looks like an education regulator. But its parent is the disability department, and RCI course recognition is separate from UGC approval.","b":"The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare controls the National Medical Commission, NIMHANS, the National Mental Health Programme (1982) and the machinery of the Mental Healthcare Act 2017, such as the Central and State Mental Health Authorities. It is the most tempting distractor because clinical psychologists work in hospitals and are named as mental health professionals in the MHCA 2017. But the RCI itself is not a health ministry body.","c":"The Ministry of Labour and Employment handles employment, wages, industrial relations and social security bodies such as ESIC and EPFO. Its link to disability is limited to employment services for persons with disabilities. It tempts because 'rehabilitation' can sound like the vocational rehabilitation of workers, but the RCI regulates the training and registration of professionals, which is not a labour function."}},{"q":"Q) Who coined the term “mental tests”?","concept":"James McKeen Cattell (1890) coined the phrase 'mental tests' in his paper 'Mental Tests and Measurements', published in the journal Mind. Cattell had earned his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (who founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879) and then worked with Francis Galton in England, so he combined Wundt's laboratory precision with Galton's interest in individual differences. Galton had already measured thousands of people in his Anthropometric Laboratory (1884) in London, assuming that keen senses meant a keen intellect. Cattell's ten tests followed the same logic: grip strength, rate of movement, two-point sensation threshold, pressure causing pain, weight discrimination, reaction time to sound, colour naming, line bisection, judging ten seconds of time, and memory span for letters. The approach collapsed when Clark Wissler (1901), Cattell's own student at Columbia, found that these scores barely correlated with college grades or with each other. The real turn came from Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), whose scale sampled higher processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning; the 1908 revision introduced mental age. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied this by 100 as the IQ of the Stanford-Binet, and David Wechsler (1939) replaced the ratio with the deviation IQ. Examiners like to ask who coined 'mental tests' (Cattell), who is the father of psychometrics (Galton), and who built the first practical intelligence test (Binet). Option b is right because Cattell introduced the term 'mental tests' in his 1890 paper.","traps":{"a":"Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, produced the first practical intelligence test in 1905 to identify French schoolchildren who needed special education, and introduced mental age in the 1908 revision. His items tested reasoning and judgement rather than the senses. It tempts because Binet is the best-known name in early testing, but the phrase 'mental tests' was already in print in Cattell's 1890 paper.","c":"David Wechsler published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939, followed by the WISC (1949) and the WAIS (1955). He defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment, and replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). It tempts because his scales dominate clinical testing today, but he came half a century after the term.","d":"Lewis Terman of Stanford University revised the Binet-Simon scale into the Stanford-Binet (1916), popularised the IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) and began a famous longitudinal study of gifted children in 1921. It tempts because he turned testing into a mass movement in America, but he adapted an existing test and borrowed the IQ idea from William Stern rather than coining 'mental tests'."}},{"q":"Q) Who was the psychologist that introduced the term scholar-professional for the new model of training?","concept":"Models of clinical psychology training answer one question: how far should a clinician be a researcher and how far a practitioner. The first formal answer came from the Boulder Conference (1949) in Colorado, which adopted the scientist-practitioner model, built on the 1947 training report of a committee chaired by David Shakow: the clinician earns a PhD, is trained equally in research and practice, and uses science to guide therapy. Critics felt this produced researchers who did little practice and practitioners who did little research. Gordon F. Derner of Adelphi University in New York argued for a professional model whose main aim is skilled practice informed by scholarship rather than original research, and introduced the term scholar-professional for it. The Adelphi programme is often described as the first university-based professional school of psychology. The idea was endorsed at the Vail Conference (1973), which approved the practitioner-scholar model and the PsyD degree; the first PsyD programme began at the University of Illinois in 1968 under Donald Peterson, and the first freestanding professional school, the California School of Professional Psychology, opened in 1969. A later reaction, the clinical scientist model, put research first and is linked to Richard McFall's 'Manifesto for a Science of Clinical Psychology' (1991). For variants, remember the pairs: Boulder, scientist-practitioner, PhD; Vail, practitioner-scholar, PsyD; Derner, scholar-professional; McFall, clinical scientist; Lightner Witmer (1896), first psychological clinic. Option a is right because Gordon Derner introduced the term scholar-professional for the professional model of training.","traps":{"b":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, with landmarks such as The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) and the structural model of id, ego and superego (1923). Analysts were trained through personal analysis and supervision in psychoanalytic institutes, but Freud played no part in the American debate on university training models. It tempts only because he is the most famous name in clinical work and feels like a safe guess for anything about therapy.","c":"'F. Skinner' is B. F. Skinner, who developed radical behaviourism and operant conditioning, set out in The Behavior of Organisms (1938), and introduced schedules of reinforcement and the operant chamber. His work fed into behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis, not into naming training models for clinicians. It tempts because behavioural methods are a large part of clinical training, and the truncated name may confuse candidates.","d":"Carl Gustav Jung founded analytical psychology after breaking with Freud around 1913, and introduced the collective unconscious, archetypes, complexes and the introversion and extraversion attitudes in Psychological Types (1921). He wrote nothing on scholar-professional university education. It tempts because, like Freud, he is a famous clinical theorist whose name feels right for any question about therapists and their training."}},{"q":"Q) Which Act mandates all rehabilitation professionals to be registered with the RCI?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 turned the RCI, which had existed as a registered society since 1986, into a statutory body, and it is the source of the registration mandate. The Act empowers the RCI to regulate training courses, recognise qualifications and maintain the Central Rehabilitation Register (CRR). Its key rule is that a person without a recognised qualification and registration on the CRR may not practise as a rehabilitation professional, and doing so is a punishable offence. An amendment in 2000 added the promotion of research in rehabilitation and special education. Clinical psychologists are among the professionals it covers, so an Indian clinical psychologist needs an RCI-recognised M.Phil or equivalent and a CRR number. Examiners pair this with laws that look similar. The Mental Health Act 1987 (misnamed in one option) replaced the Indian Lunacy Act 1912 and dealt with admission procedures and licensing of psychiatric hospitals. It was repealed by the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA) 2017, which is rights-based, effectively decriminalised attempted suicide (Section 115), introduced advance directives and nominated representatives, and defines a clinical psychologist through RCI-recognised qualifications. The Persons with Disabilities Act 1995 was replaced by the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016, which raised the recognised disabilities from 7 to 21. So the MHCA 2017 relies on RCI qualifications, but only the RCI Act creates the duty to register. Option d is right because the RCI Act 1992 makes registration with the RCI compulsory for anyone practising as a rehabilitation professional.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 is a rights-based law under the Health ministry: right to access mental healthcare, advance directives, nominated representatives, Mental Health Review Boards and the effective decriminalisation of attempted suicide. It names clinical psychologists as mental health professionals by referring to RCI-recognised qualifications, which is why it tempts. But it borrows the RCI standard; it does not create the duty to register.","b":"The Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act 1995 recognised seven disabilities, including mental illness and mental retardation, and provided for education, job reservation and a Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities. It tempts because it is a disability law under the same ministry as the RCI. But it came after the RCI Act, did not set up registration, and was replaced by the RPwD Act 2016.","c":"The law of 1987 was the Mental Health Act 1987, so this option's title is itself wrong. That Act replaced the Indian Lunacy Act 1912, regulated the licensing of psychiatric hospitals and procedures for voluntary and involuntary admission, and was repealed by the MHCA 2017. It tempts because candidates remember a mental health law from the 1980s, but it said nothing about registering rehabilitation professionals."}},{"q":"Q) How do clinicians tend to conceive assessment information?","concept":"When a clinician gets a test result, the question is what the result stands for. Norman D. Sundberg and Leona E. Tyler (1962) described three ways clinicians conceive assessment information: as samples, correlates or signs, and the three differ in how much inference they need. A sample treats the test behaviour as a small piece of the very behaviour you want to know about. A typing test samples typing; observing a child's tantrums in class samples tantrums. Inference is minimal, and this is the logic of behavioural assessment. A correlate treats the score as something shown statistically to go with an outside criterion, whether or not the content looks related. The empirical keying of the MMPI by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) is the classic case: an item is scored on the depression scale simply because depressed patients answered it differently from controls. A sign treats the response as an indicator of an underlying, unobserved state or trait, such as reading Rorschach responses as signs of thought disorder or ego strength. This is the most inferential level and typical of projective and psychodynamic assessment. Marvin Goldfried and Ronald Kent (1972) used the same contrast to separate behavioural assessment (sample) from traditional personality assessment (sign). A neighbouring idea is the comparison of clinical and statistical (actuarial) prediction by Paul Meehl (1954), who showed that formula-based prediction usually equals or beats clinical judgement. Option d is right because clinicians conceive assessment information as samples, correlates or signs.","traps":{"a":"Factual, hypothetical or imaginary is not a recognised framework in assessment. It sounds like a scale of how real or certain information is, and 'hypothetical' echoes the real practice of forming working hypotheses from test data. That echo is why it tempts. But the standard triad from Norman Sundberg and Leona Tyler (1962) sorts information by level of inference (sample, correlate, sign), not by how true it is.","b":"Predictions, estimates and suggestions describe what a clinician does with data after interpreting it, for example predicting relapse or suggesting a treatment. Prediction is central to the clinical versus statistical prediction debate started by Paul Meehl (1954), which makes this option sound academic. It tempts for that reason, but it describes the outputs of assessment, not the ways the information itself is conceived.","c":"Examples, prototypes and categories come from concept formation in cognitive psychology, especially the prototype theory of Eleanor Rosch (1975), in which a category is organised around its best example. Prototype matching does appear in diagnosis, which is why it tempts. But it describes how diagnostic categories are formed and recognised, not how individual pieces of test information are interpreted by the clinician."}},{"q":"Q) Which of the following affect factors do NOT affect the outward objective expression of immediate emotion?","concept":"The mental status examination separates mood from affect. Mood is the sustained, pervasive emotional state the patient experiences and reports in their own words ('I feel low'), described by its quality, intensity, persistence over days or weeks, and diurnal variation. Affect is the outward, observable expression of emotion during the interview, the moment-to-moment display the examiner sees in face, voice and posture. A common teaching analogy says mood is the climate and affect is the weather. Affect has several parameters. Quality is the dominant tone (euthymic, depressed, anxious, irritable, elated). Range is the variety of emotion shown: full, restricted, blunted or flat, where flat affect is a negative symptom of schizophrenia. Reactivity, or mobility, is how readily expression shifts with the conversation; rapid excessive shifts are labile affect, and failure to brighten is non-reactive affect. Intensity, stability, appropriateness to the situation and congruence with thought content are also rated. Examiners also test reactivity in depression: in DSM-5 (2013), mood reactivity, brightening in response to positive events, is the core of the atypical features specifier, while its loss marks melancholia. Persistence, by contrast, is about how long a state lasts, so it belongs to mood, the inner climate, and cannot describe a display seen in the next few seconds. Related terms include alexithymia, difficulty identifying and describing feelings, named by Peter Sifneos (1973). Option b is right because persistence describes the sustained internal state of mood, not the immediate outward expression of emotion that defines affect.","traps":{"a":"Quality of affect is the dominant emotional tone the examiner observes: euthymic, depressed, anxious, irritable, elated or perplexed. It is the first thing recorded under affect and is plainly part of the outward expression of immediate emotion, so it cannot be the exception. It tempts because 'quality' is also used to describe mood, and candidates who mix up mood and affect may pick it.","c":"Range of affect is the variety of emotional expression shown during the interview, graded as full or broad, restricted (constricted), blunted and flat. Flat affect, with almost no expression, is a classic negative symptom of schizophrenia. Range is observed directly, moment to moment, so it belongs to outward affect. It tempts because a blunted range can look like a long-standing trait rather than an immediate display.","d":"Reactivity is how readily emotional expression changes in response to the conversation or events, for example a depressed patient who smiles at a joke. Excessive reactivity is lability; absent reactivity points to melancholic depression in DSM-5. Although the option says 'of mood', reactivity is seen on the spot, so it describes outward expression. It tempts because the phrase 'of mood' makes it sound like a mood-only feature."}},{"q":"Which aspect of cognitive function is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale?","concept":"The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) was published by Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett (1974) of Glasgow as a simple, reliable bedside way to grade level of consciousness, mainly after head injury. Consciousness has two parts: arousal or wakefulness, maintained by the ascending reticular activating system in the brainstem, and awareness, the content of consciousness, served by the cerebral hemispheres. The GCS scores three observed behaviours. Eye opening is scored 1 to 4 (spontaneous, to sound, to pain, none). Best verbal response is scored 1 to 5 (oriented, confused, inappropriate words, incomprehensible sounds, none). Best motor response is scored 1 to 6 (obeys commands, localises pain, withdraws, abnormal flexion, extension, none). Totals run from 3 to 15. A score of 13 to 15 indicates mild, 9 to 12 moderate and 3 to 8 severe head injury, and 8 or less is usually taken as coma. In the mental status examination consciousness is assessed first, because every later cognitive test assumes the patient is awake. Clinical labels move from alert to drowsy, obtunded, stuporous and comatose, and clouding of consciousness with fluctuating attention is the hallmark of delirium. Neighbouring domains have their own tests: attention and concentration by digit span and serial sevens, memory by immediate, recent and remote recall, and intelligence by standardised tests such as the Wechsler scales. Examiners may also ask about the AVPU scale (alert, voice, pain, unresponsive). Option a is right because the Glasgow Coma Scale measures level of consciousness through eye, verbal and motor responses.","traps":{"b":"Attention is the ability to focus on a stimulus and sustain that focus. In the MSE it is tested by digit span (a normal forward span is at least five digits), serial sevens, or spelling WORLD backwards. A patient can be fully conscious yet inattentive, as in mania or ADHD. It tempts because attention is impaired in delirium and closely tied to consciousness, but the GCS does not test it.","c":"Memory is assessed in the MSE as immediate retention (repeating digits or words at once), recent memory (recall of three objects after about five minutes, recent events) and remote memory (personal and historical facts). The Mini-Mental State Examination of Marshal Folstein and colleagues (1975) includes such items. It tempts because the GCS verbal score asks about orientation, which overlaps with memory, but recall is not scored.","d":"Intelligence is the general capacity to reason, learn and solve problems. It is estimated clinically from education, vocabulary and general knowledge, and measured formally by tests such as the Wechsler scales or, in India, Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests. It tempts because the GCS appears in lists of cognitive functions, but a coma scale says nothing about intellectual level; a highly intelligent person in coma scores 3."}},{"q":"What is assessed in the Insight category of the cognitive assessment?","concept":"Insight, in psychiatry, is the patient's awareness and understanding of their own illness. Aubrey Lewis (1934) defined it as 'a correct attitude to a morbid change in oneself'. Anthony David (1990) showed that insight has three separable dimensions: recognising that one has a mental illness, being able to relabel unusual experiences such as hallucinations as abnormal, and accepting treatment. In the mental status examination, insight is commonly graded in six levels, as in Kaplan and Sadock's textbook: complete denial of illness; slight awareness of being sick and needing help while denying it at the same time; awareness of being sick but blaming others, external factors or physical causes; awareness that the illness is due to something unknown in oneself; intellectual insight, where the patient admits the illness and its link to their own feelings but does not use this to change; and true emotional insight, which leads to change in behaviour. Poor insight is common in schizophrenia and mania and predicts poor treatment adherence. Insight is recorded near the end of the examination, next to judgement, the ability to assess a situation and act appropriately. Do not confuse it with anosognosia, unawareness of a neurological deficit such as hemiplegia, described by Joseph Babinski (1914). Rating scales include the Scale to Assess Unawareness of Mental Disorder by Xavier Amador and colleagues (1993) and the Beck Cognitive Insight Scale of Aaron T. Beck and colleagues (2004). Option c is right because insight refers to the degree of awareness a patient has regarding their own illness.","traps":{"a":"Orientation is awareness of time, place and person, tested by asking the date, the location, and who the patient and others are. Orientation to time is usually lost first and to person last, a useful pattern in delirium and dementia. It tempts because the word 'awareness' appears in both definitions, but orientation is awareness of one's surroundings, while insight is awareness of being ill.","b":"Level of consciousness is the degree of arousal and awareness, from alert through drowsy and stuporous to coma, graded objectively by the Glasgow Coma Scale of Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett (1974). It is assessed first in the cognitive examination, because all other tests depend on it. It tempts because consciousness and insight both sound like awareness, but a fully alert patient can still have no insight.","d":"Immediate retention and recall is the registration stage of memory, tested by asking the patient to repeat digits or three words at once and recall them a few minutes later. It reflects attention and short-term memory and sits under the memory heading of the MSE. It tempts as a filler because it is a cognitive function listed in the same section, but it has nothing to do with awareness of illness."}},{"q":"What is flight of ideas?","concept":"Flight of ideas is a formal thought disorder, a disturbance of the form or flow of thinking rather than its content. Thoughts come so fast that speech jumps from one idea to the next before any is finished, so the patient never reaches the goal of the original idea. Unlike the breakdowns seen in schizophrenia, the links between successive ideas can usually be followed: they are made through chance associations, rhymes and clang associations, puns, or things in the room that catch attention (distractibility). It is the classic thought disorder of mania and comes with pressure of speech; the subjective version is racing thoughts. DSM-5 (2013) lists flight of ideas or racing thoughts among the criterion B symptoms of a manic episode. A milder form in hypomania, where speech wanders but still gets there, is called prolixity. Examiners build variants from neighbouring terms. Circumstantiality is over-inclusion of unnecessary detail with eventual arrival at the point. Tangentiality departs from the question and never returns. Loosening of associations, the term of Eugen Bleuler (1911), means links the listener cannot follow, typical of schizophrenia. Perseveration is repetition of the same word or idea despite a new question, seen in organic brain disease. Poverty of speech is reduced spontaneous speech, seen in depression and, as alogia, in schizophrenia. The Thought, Language and Communication scale of Nancy Andreasen (1979) defines many of these. Option b is right because flight of ideas is a rapid shift from one idea to another without any of them being completed.","traps":{"a":"Repeating the same idea or word over and over, even when a new question is asked, is perseveration. It reflects difficulty in shifting mental set and is a classic sign of organic brain disease, especially frontal lobe damage and dementia. It tempts because both perseveration and flight of ideas are abnormalities of the flow of ideas, but perseveration is stuck on one idea, while flight of ideas races through many.","c":"Loading speech with irrelevant or unnecessary details before eventually reaching the point is circumstantiality. The goal is reached, but slowly and by a long detour. It is seen in epilepsy, obsessional personalities and some people with intellectual disability. It tempts because both involve speech that wanders from the point, but circumstantiality is slow and overdetailed, while flight of ideas is fast and never arrives.","d":"Lack of spontaneity in speech is poverty of speech: brief, unelaborated replies given only when asked. It is seen in depression with psychomotor retardation and, as alogia, among the negative symptoms of schizophrenia. It tempts as the paired opposite in a list of speech disturbances, but flight of ideas is overproductive and pressured, the very reverse of reduced spontaneous speech."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT included in the General Principles of the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association (APA) published its first ethics code in 1953, and the current Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct was adopted in 2002, with amendments in 2010 and 2016. The Code has two parts. The General Principles are aspirational: they describe ideals psychologists should aim for and are not themselves enforceable rules. The Ethical Standards are enforceable and grouped into ten sections, from Resolving Ethical Issues and Competence through Privacy and Confidentiality, Research and Publication, Assessment and Therapy. There are exactly five General Principles, lettered A to E. A is Beneficence and Nonmaleficence: benefit those you work with and do no harm. B is Fidelity and Responsibility: establish trust, clarify roles, manage conflicts of interest and accept responsibility for one's conduct. C is Integrity: promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness. D is Justice: all persons are entitled to equal access to and benefit from psychology, and psychologists guard against bias and the limits of their competence. E is Respect for People's Rights and Dignity: privacy, confidentiality, self-determination and sensitivity to cultural and individual differences. Equity is an everyday idea close to Justice, which is why it works as a distractor, but it is not the title of any principle. Neighbouring lists are tested too: Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) gave four principles of biomedical ethics (autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence, justice), and Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five for counselling psychology by adding fidelity. Option d is right because Equity is not one of the five General Principles; the fairness principle is called Justice.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence is Principle A of the APA Code: psychologists strive to benefit those they work with, take care to do no harm, resolve conflicts among obligations in a way that minimises harm, and stay alert to how their own health may affect their work. It is genuinely in the list, so it cannot be the answer. It tempts because it pairs two ideas, which can make it look like an invented heading.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility is Principle B: psychologists establish relationships of trust, uphold professional standards, clarify roles, manage conflicts of interest, cooperate with other professionals, and are encouraged to give some time for little or no fee. It is a real principle. It tempts because 'fidelity' is less familiar to students than terms like confidentiality or consent, so it may sound made up.","c":"Integrity is Principle C: psychologists promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness, do not steal, cheat or misrepresent facts, keep their promises, and must weigh carefully any use of deception in research. It is a real principle. It tempts because integrity is also a common virtue word, and some candidates assume that a short one-word title, like Integrity or Equity, must be the odd one out."}},{"q":"Which General Principle emphasizes the importance of promoting accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology?","concept":"The Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association (adopted 2002, amended 2010 and 2016) opens with five aspirational General Principles, lettered A to E, followed by ten sections of enforceable Ethical Standards. Principle C, Integrity, is the honesty principle. Its text says psychologists seek to promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness in the science, teaching and practice of psychology; they do not steal, cheat or engage in fraud, subterfuge or intentional misrepresentation of fact; and they keep their promises and avoid unwise or unclear commitments. It also covers deception: where deception may be ethically justified to maximise benefit, as in some research, psychologists must consider whether it is needed, weigh its consequences, and correct any mistrust it causes. The enforceable standards that put Integrity into practice include the rules on deception in research, debriefing, and not fabricating data or plagiarising. Variants give a principle's key words and ask for its name. Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (A): benefit, do no harm, safeguard welfare. Fidelity and Responsibility (B): trust, professional relationships, roles, conflicts of interest, responsibility to society. Integrity (C): accuracy, honesty, truthfulness. Justice (D): fairness, equal access and quality, guarding against bias. Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (E): privacy, confidentiality, self-determination, cultural and individual differences. The commonest confusion is between B and C, because trust sounds like honesty; trust concerns relationships and obligations, honesty concerns truthful information. Option c is right because Integrity is the principle that promotes accuracy, honesty and truthfulness in the science, teaching and practice of psychology.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (Principle A) asks psychologists to benefit those they work with and do no harm, safeguard the welfare and rights of clients and research participants, and guard against misuse of their influence. It is about welfare and harm, not truth. It tempts because it is the first and most familiar principle, and because harming people through deception sounds like a beneficence issue.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility (Principle B) is about establishing relationships of trust, upholding professional standards, clarifying roles, managing conflicts of interest and accepting responsibility for one's behaviour. It is the strongest distractor, because 'fidelity' means faithfulness and sounds close to honesty. But its focus is trust and obligations within relationships and to society, while the truthfulness of information belongs to Integrity.","d":"Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (Principle E) concerns the worth of all people, their rights to privacy, confidentiality and self-determination, special safeguards for vulnerable persons, and awareness of differences such as age, gender, culture, religion, disability and language. It tempts because confidentiality and informed consent feel like honesty issues, but this principle protects rights and dignity, not the accuracy of information."}},{"q":"How does the deontological approach to ethics differ from the utilitarian perspective?","concept":"Ethical theories differ on what makes an action right. Deontological ethics, from the Greek deon (duty), holds that some acts are right or wrong in themselves, whatever their results. Its best-known form comes from Immanuel Kant (1785) in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals: the categorical imperative says act only on a rule you could will to be a universal law, and treat people always as ends, never merely as means. On this view lying is wrong even if a lie would produce a good outcome. William David Ross (1930) softened this with prima facie duties, such as fidelity, non-maleficence and justice, which can conflict and must be weighed. Utilitarianism is the main consequentialist theory: an act is right if it produces the greatest good for the greatest number. Jeremy Bentham (1789) proposed a felicific calculus that adds up pleasures and pains, essentially a cost-benefit analysis, and John Stuart Mill (1861) distinguished higher from lower pleasures. Act utilitarianism judges each act; rule utilitarianism judges rules by their usual consequences. Clinical ethics uses both. Confidentiality and informed consent are duty-based, while breaking confidentiality to protect a third party, as after the Tarasoff ruling (1976) in California, rests on weighing harms and benefits. A third approach, virtue ethics from Aristotle (fourth century BCE), asks what a good person would do rather than which rule or outcome applies. Option b is right because deontology judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness, while utilitarianism judges them by a cost-benefit analysis of their consequences.","traps":{"a":"This option swaps the two theories. Cost-benefit analysis of outcomes is the core of utilitarianism (Jeremy Bentham, 1789; John Stuart Mill, 1861), while moral duties and obligations are the core of deontology (Immanuel Kant, 1785). It tempts because it uses all the right terms, and candidates who remember the key words but not which theory owns which can pick it in a hurry. Always check the pairing.","c":"Saying the two approaches are identical is plainly false. They are usually presented as the two main rival families of normative ethics: deontology is non-consequentialist and judges the act itself, while utilitarianism is consequentialist and judges only outcomes. They can disagree sharply, for example on whether to lie to save a life. It tempts only as a filler for someone who knows neither theory.","d":"Only deontology is built on moral duties and obligations. Utilitarianism has one overarching aim, to maximise overall good, and treats any rule as useful only if it serves that aim, as in rule utilitarianism. It tempts because rule utilitarianism follows rules that look like duties, and because both theories tell people what they ought to do. But the source of the ought differs: duty versus consequences."}},{"q":"What are the four pillars of principle-based ethics?","concept":"Principle-based ethics, also called principlism, comes from Tom L. Beauchamp and James F. Childress (1979) in Principles of Biomedical Ethics. They proposed four mid-level principles that people holding different moral theories can agree on and apply to clinical cases. Respect for autonomy means respecting a competent person's right to make informed decisions about their own life; it underlies informed consent, confidentiality and truth telling. Non-maleficence means do no harm, the old maxim primum non nocere. Beneficence means acting for the person's good, preventing and removing harm, and balancing benefits against risks. Justice means fair distribution of benefits, risks and costs, and treating similar cases alike. The principles are prima facie: none automatically wins, so in a dilemma they must be weighed. A depressed client refusing treatment, for example, pits autonomy against beneficence. Examiners build variants from neighbouring lists. The Belmont Report (1979), on research ethics in the United States, gave three principles: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five for counselling psychology: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice and fidelity. The APA Ethics Code (2002) has five General Principles, including Integrity, which is why integrity is planted in the wrong options here. In India, the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) govern research with human participants. Remember the four with the letters A, B, N, J: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice. Option c is right because beneficence, non-maleficence, justice and respect for autonomy are the four pillars set out by Beauchamp and Childress.","traps":{"a":"This list keeps autonomy, beneficence and non-maleficence but replaces justice with integrity. Integrity, meaning accuracy and honesty, is Principle C of the APA Ethics Code (2002), not one of the biomedical pillars of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979). It tempts because candidates who study both lists tend to merge them, and integrity sounds like a natural ethical pillar.","b":"This list keeps autonomy, beneficence and justice but drops non-maleficence and adds a vague 'respect'. Respect is already part of the first pillar, properly called respect for autonomy, so the list counts one principle twice and loses another. It tempts because 'respect' appears in the correct full title and in the APA principle of Respect for People's Rights and Dignity.","d":"This list has three correct pillars but swaps justice for integrity. Justice, the fair distribution of benefits, burdens and resources, is one of the four pillars, while integrity belongs to the APA General Principles. It tempts more than the other distractors because it keeps the precise wording 'respect for autonomy', so it looks exact. Check that justice is present before choosing."}},{"q":"Which types of psychotherapy are most effective in treating survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"Sexual assault is one of the traumas most likely to lead to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Ann Burgess and Lynda Holmstrom (1974) described rape trauma syndrome, with an acute disorganisation phase followed by a long-term reorganisation phase, and later research showed that many survivors develop PTSD, depression and substance problems. The treatments with the strongest evidence are trauma-focused. Trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy is a family that includes Cognitive Processing Therapy, developed by Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992) for rape survivors, which targets stuck points such as self-blame; Prolonged Exposure, set out by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998) in Treating the Trauma of Rape, which uses imaginal and in vivo exposure to extinguish fear; and, for children, TF-CBT. Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR), introduced by Francine Shapiro (1989), has the client hold the traumatic memory in mind while following bilateral stimulation such as eye movements. WHO guidance (2013) recommends trauma-focused CBT and EMDR for adult PTSD. Psychodynamic psychotherapy has a weaker evidence base for PTSD, but it is used to work on shame, relationships and longer-term personality effects, which is why it appears in the keyed list. Therapies without a trauma focus, such as general CBT, mindfulness-based approaches, acceptance and commitment therapy, interpersonal therapy and dialectical behaviour therapy, can help with associated problems but are not first-line PTSD treatments. Option c is right because it is the only list that includes the two trauma-specific, evidence-based treatments, trauma-focused CBT and EMDR.","traps":{"a":"This list has psychodynamic therapy, general CBT and mindfulness-based therapy. General CBT, as set out by Aaron T. Beck (1979) for depression, is not the same as trauma-focused CBT, which deliberately processes the trauma memory. Mindfulness-based approaches, from the stress reduction programme of Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979), reduce general distress but are not first-line PTSD treatment. It tempts because CBT sounds evidence-based, but the trauma focus is missing.","b":"Interpersonal therapy, set out by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), treats depression by working on grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits. Dialectical behaviour therapy, by Marsha Linehan (1993), was built for chronic suicidality and borderline personality disorder. Both may help survivors with depression or self-harm, which is why the list tempts, but neither is a trauma-focused treatment for PTSD itself.","d":"This list is all general or third-wave CBT: standard CBT, mindfulness-based therapy, and acceptance and commitment therapy, developed by Steven Hayes and colleagues (1999), which builds psychological flexibility through acceptance and values. They are modern and research-based, which is why the list tempts. But none of them requires processing the trauma memory, and none has the PTSD evidence of trauma-focused CBT or EMDR."}},{"q":"What is the main goal of TF-CBT?","concept":"Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (TF-CBT) was developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger and set out in their manual Treating Trauma and Traumatic Grief in Children and Adolescents (2006). It is a short-term treatment for children and adolescents, roughly 3 to 18 years old, with PTSD symptoms after events such as sexual abuse, and a non-offending parent or caregiver takes part. Its components are remembered by the acronym PRACTICE: Psychoeducation and Parenting skills, Relaxation, Affective expression and modulation, Cognitive coping, Trauma narrative and processing, In vivo mastery of trauma reminders, Conjoint child-parent sessions, and Enhancing safety and future development. These fall into three phases: stabilisation and skills, trauma narrative and processing, and integration and consolidation. Gradual exposure runs through every component, but its peak is the trauma narrative, in which the child describes the traumatic experience in detail over several sessions, often as a book, while the therapist helps identify and correct distorted thoughts such as self-blame. Repeated, controlled recall reduces the distress and avoidance linked to the memory, which is desensitisation. The coping skills of the first phase exist to prepare the child for this work. Examiners contrast TF-CBT with EMDR of Francine Shapiro (1989), which uses bilateral stimulation; Prolonged Exposure for adults, described by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998); and Cognitive Processing Therapy of Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992), which works mainly on beliefs. Option c is right because building the trauma narrative and desensitising the child to traumatic memories is the central, defining goal of TF-CBT.","traps":{"a":"Closure and relief describe a hoped-for outcome of any trauma therapy, not the specific mechanism of TF-CBT. 'Closure' is also a technical term in EMDR, where phase 7 returns the client to calm at the end of each session. It tempts because it sounds compassionate and goal-like, but it names no technique; examiners want the process that produces relief, which is narrative and desensitisation.","b":"Emotional regulation skills belong to the early stabilisation phase of TF-CBT (Relaxation and Affective modulation in PRACTICE). Mindfulness is the core of other models, such as dialectical behaviour therapy by Marsha Linehan (1993) and the mindfulness-based therapies. It tempts because these skills are taught in TF-CBT, but they prepare the child for trauma processing rather than forming the main goal, and mindfulness is not a defining component.","d":"Teaching coping skills is a real and important part of TF-CBT, covered by the Relaxation, Affective modulation and Cognitive coping components, so this is the strongest distractor. But these skills form the first phase, preparing the child to tolerate trauma processing. A treatment that taught only coping skills would be skills-based, not trauma-focused; the trauma narrative is what makes TF-CBT trauma-focused."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of the eight phases of EMDR?","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by Francine Shapiro, who in 1987 noticed that quick eye movements seemed to reduce the distress of disturbing thoughts, and who published the first controlled study in 1989. Her Adaptive Information Processing model holds that trauma memories are stored in an unprocessed form, and that bilateral stimulation (eye movements, taps or tones) helps link them to adaptive memory networks. The standard protocol has eight phases. Phase 1, history taking and treatment planning, identifies targets: past events, present triggers and future templates. Phase 2, preparation, explains the method and builds stabilising resources such as the safe or calm place. Phase 3, assessment, activates the target: an image, a negative cognition, a desired positive cognition rated on the Validity of Cognition (VoC) scale from 1 to 7, the emotions, a rating on the Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale of Joseph Wolpe (1969) from 0 to 10, and the body location. Phase 4, desensitisation, uses sets of bilateral stimulation until SUD falls to 0 or 1. Phase 5, installation, strengthens the positive cognition towards a VoC of 7. Phase 6, body scan, clears residual tension. Phase 7, closure, returns the client to calm at the end of every session. Phase 8, reevaluation, checks the results at the start of the next session. Option b is right because it is the only list with all eight phases in Shapiro's order, with closure ending each session as phase 7 and reevaluation opening the next session as phase 8.","traps":{"a":"This sequence starts with preparation and pushes history taking to phase 3, after assessment. In the protocol of Francine Shapiro you cannot prepare a client or assess a target before taking a history and choosing targets, so history taking is always phase 1. It also puts closure before body scan. It tempts because preparation sounds like a natural first step in any therapy.","d":"This sequence makes preparation phase 1 and history taking phase 2, and puts body scan before installation. Body scan only makes sense after the positive cognition is installed, since it checks whether any disturbance remains in the body while the client holds the target and the positive cognition together. It tempts because it contains all eight correct names, so only careful checking of order reveals the errors.","c":"This list has the first six phases right but swaps the last two, putting reevaluation at 7 and closure at 8. In the protocol of Francine Shapiro, closure ends every session (phase 7) and reevaluation opens the next session to check whether the gains held (phase 8), so reevaluation must come last. It tempts because it differs from the correct order only at the very end, where readers stop checking."}},{"q":"How does the syndromal approach of classifying psychiatric disorders differ from the historical approach?","concept":"Psychiatric classification can be organised by cause (etiology) or by description (signs, symptoms and course). In general medicine the ideal is etiological: general paresis of the insane was a puzzling syndrome until Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore (1913) found Treponema pallidum in patients' brains, after which it became a disease defined by its cause. For most mental disorders, however, causes are still unknown. Older classifications, which this question calls the historical approach, sorted disorders by known or presumed causes: organic versus functional, or, in DSM-I (1952) and DSM-II (1968), categories shaped by psychodynamic theory, with many disorders labelled 'reactions' and neuroses defined by unconscious conflict. Clinicians could not reliably agree on such presumed causes, so diagnostic reliability was poor. The syndromal or descriptive approach groups disorders by clusters of signs and symptoms that occur together and share a course, without assuming a cause. Its roots lie in the separation of dementia praecox from manic-depressive insanity by Emil Kraepelin (1899), based on symptoms and outcome. It became dominant with the research criteria of John Feighner and colleagues (1972) and with DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, which was explicitly atheoretical and used operational criteria; ICD-10 (1992), DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11 follow it. Etiology survives in a few places, such as organic disorders, substance-induced disorders, PTSD and adjustment disorder, which require a cause or stressor in the definition. Option b is right because the syndromal approach rests on clinical signs and symptoms, while the historical approach classified disorders by known or presumed etiology.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two definitions. Symptom-based description is the syndromal approach of DSM-III (1980) onwards, and classification by cause is the older, etiological style. It tempts because it uses the right two ideas, and because 'syndrome' can sound technical and causal. Remember: a syndrome is a cluster of signs and symptoms whose cause is unknown or unspecified; once the cause is known it becomes a disease.","c":"The syndromal approach is not absent from psychiatry; it is the backbone of both current systems, DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11, where nearly every disorder is defined by a list of symptoms, a minimum duration and impairment. It is the older, cause-based approach that has largely been abandoned. It tempts only because the word 'historical' suggests a traditional, established method that is still in use.","d":"No rule restricts syndromal classification to medical illness or the historical approach to psychiatry. Both approaches are used across medicine: many medical conditions began as syndromes, for example AIDS before HIV was identified, and moved to causal definitions. It tempts because candidates may link the word 'syndrome' to medical labels such as Down syndrome and assume psychiatry must use something else."}},{"q":"Which category of mental disorders in ICD-10 includes delirium and dementia?","concept":"Chapter V of ICD-10, published by the World Health Organization (1992) and coded with the letter F, divides mental and behavioural disorders into blocks of ten. F00 to F09 covers organic, including symptomatic, mental disorders: those with a demonstrable cause in cerebral disease, brain injury or a systemic illness affecting the brain. Symptomatic means the brain is affected secondarily by a disease elsewhere in the body, such as hypothyroidism. The block contains F00 dementia in Alzheimer's disease, F01 vascular dementia, F02 dementia in other diseases (for example Pick's, Huntington's, Parkinson's or HIV disease), F03 unspecified dementia, F04 organic amnesic syndrome, F05 delirium not induced by alcohol and other psychoactive substances, F06 other organic disorders (hallucinosis, catatonic, delusional, mood and anxiety disorders), and F07 personality and behavioural disorders due to brain disease. A classic variant trap: delirium in alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens) is coded F10.4, and alcohol-induced amnesic (Korsakoff) syndrome F10.6, because substance causes go to F10 to F19. The other blocks are F20 to F29 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional; F30 to F39 mood; F40 to F48 neurotic, stress-related and somatoform; F50 to F59 behavioural syndromes with physiological disturbances; F60 to F69 adult personality and behaviour; F70 to F79 mental retardation; F80 to F89 psychological development; F90 to F98 childhood-onset behavioural and emotional disorders. ICD-11 (adopted 2019) and DSM-5 (2013) drop the word organic and speak of neurocognitive disorders. Option a is right because delirium and dementia are organic mental disorders, classified in F00 to F09.","traps":{"b":"F10 to F19 covers mental and behavioural disorders due to psychoactive substance use. The third character names the substance (F10 alcohol, F11 opioids, F12 cannabinoids) and the fourth the clinical state (.0 intoxication, .2 dependence, .3 withdrawal, .4 withdrawal with delirium). It tempts because substance-induced delirium and Korsakoff syndrome are coded here, but delirium and dementia as categories sit in F00 to F09.","c":"F20 to F29 contains schizophrenia (F20), schizotypal disorder (F21), persistent delusional disorders (F22), acute and transient psychotic disorders (F23), induced delusional disorder (F24) and schizoaffective disorders (F25). These are functional psychoses with no demonstrable brain disease as cause. It tempts because delirium can include hallucinations and delusions, but psychotic features caused by brain disease are coded F06, not in the F20s.","d":"The mood block actually runs F30 to F39 (the option misprints its start), covering manic episode (F30), bipolar affective disorder (F31), depressive episode (F32), recurrent depressive disorder (F33) and persistent mood disorders such as dysthymia (F34). It tempts because dementia often begins with depressive symptoms and severe depression can mimic dementia (pseudodementia). But an organic mood disorder is coded F06.3, not here."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of steps involved in working on self-acceptance for LGBTQ individuals?","concept":"Affirmative therapy treats same-sex attraction and gender diversity as normal variations and sees much of the client's distress as a product of stigma. The background is examinable. The American Psychiatric Association removed homosexuality from the DSM in 1973; DSM-III (1980) kept ego-dystonic homosexuality, which was dropped in 1987. ICD-10 (1992) kept egodystonic sexual orientation (F66.1), and ICD-11 removed that group. In India, the Supreme Court in Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018) decriminalised consensual same-sex relations, and the Indian Psychiatric Society stated in 2018 that homosexuality is not an illness. The minority stress model of Ilan Meyer (2003) explains why sexual minorities show more mental health problems: distal stressors (discrimination, violence) and proximal stressors (expecting rejection, concealment, internalised homophobia) add to ordinary life stress. Self-acceptance work builds on this model in a set order. The therapist first raises awareness of and explores sexual minority stress, so clients can see where their pain comes from. Next comes validation of their feelings and identity. This prepares the key reframing, the shift from ego-dystonicity (believing something is wrong with me) to social-dystonicity (recognising that the conflict lies in society's prejudice). Therapeutic or support groups then reduce isolation, and finally clients are connected to gay-affirmative resources and community. A related exam favourite is the six-stage identity formation model of Vivienne Cass (1979): confusion, comparison, tolerance, acceptance, pride and synthesis. Option c is right because it gives the sequence that begins with minority stress, moves through validation and reframing, and ends with groups and community resources.","traps":{"a":"This sequence opens with validation, which is right in spirit but too early, and brings in the therapist's 'expert position', meaning professional authority used to correct myths, such as stating that homosexuality is not a disorder. Expert position is a useful technique but not a step in the keyed sequence, and the list omits the central shift from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity. It tempts because validation sounds like a natural first step.","b":"This sequence starts with mirroring, a term from the self psychology of Heinz Kohut (1971) for reflecting back the client's worth, and puts the reframing from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity second, before minority stress has been explored. It ends with awareness of minority stress, which should come first, and leaves out validation and resources. It tempts because it contains the key reframing phrase.","d":"This sequence leads with the therapist's expert position, places awareness of minority stress fourth, and leaves out the reframing from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity altogether. Starting from authority rather than the client's own experience runs against affirmative practice, which first explores how stigma has shaped the client's distress. It tempts because it ends correctly, with connection to gay-affirmative resources."}},{"q":"How is normality defined in the Subjective Model of normality?","concept":"There is no single definition of normality, so textbooks describe models, each with its own criterion. Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966) set out four perspectives: normality as health (the absence of psychopathology, the traditional medical view), normality as utopia (an ideal of optimal functioning), normality as average (the statistical middle of a bell curve), and normality as process (healthy development across the life span). Other textbooks add the subjective model and the social or normative model. In the subjective model, a person is normal if they feel no distress, suffer no disability in daily functioning, and do not seek help. The judgement rests on the individual's own experience: if I am not troubled and not asking for help, I am normal. Its strength is respect for personal experience; its weakness is that people with antisocial personality disorder, mania, or delusions with poor insight may feel no distress, while someone grieving normally may feel great distress. The medical model asks whether a diagnosable disorder or pathology is present. The ideal model asks whether functioning is optimal, as in the six criteria of positive mental health of Marie Jahoda (1958), including self-actualisation, autonomy and environmental mastery. The social model asks whether behaviour conforms to cultural norms, which vary across cultures and eras. The harmful dysfunction analysis of Jerome Wakefield (1992) tries to combine a scientific element (dysfunction) with a value element (harm). Option b is right because the subjective model defines normality as the absence of distress, disability or help-seeking behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Optimal functioning is the ideal or utopian model, called normality as utopia by Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966), and the basis of the positive mental health criteria of Marie Jahoda (1958). Very few people meet it, so most would count as abnormal. It tempts because it can be described from the person's point of view, but it sets a high external ideal rather than asking about personal distress.","c":"Absence of any psychiatric disorder or psychopathology is the medical model, which Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966) called normality as health. Normality is judged by a clinician from signs and symptoms, not by the person's own experience. It tempts because the words 'absence of' appear in both definitions, but the subjective model asks whether the person feels distressed, not whether a diagnosis can be made.","d":"Socially acceptable behaviour is the social or normative model, which judges normality by conformity to the norms of a culture at a given time. Its classic weakness is cultural relativity: hearing the voices of ancestors may be normal in one community and pathological in another. It tempts because social judgement is often how families first label a person abnormal, but the standard is external, not subjective."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not one of the gunas of the individual Jiva?","concept":"The three gunas come from Samkhya, one of the six orthodox (astika) schools of Indian philosophy, traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and set out systematically in the Samkhya Karika of Ishvarakrishna. Samkhya is dualist: purusha is pure consciousness, and prakriti is matter or nature. Prakriti is made of three gunas (literally 'strands' or 'qualities'). Sattva is lightness, clarity, knowledge and calm. Rajas is activity, passion, desire and restlessness. Tamas is heaviness, inertia, ignorance and dullness. Before creation the three are in balance; when the balance is disturbed, prakriti evolves into intellect, ego, mind, senses and the physical elements. Because every jiva (the individual embodied self) is purusha bound up with prakriti, every person carries all three gunas, and temperament depends on which one dominates at a given time. The Bhagavad Gita, chapter 14, describes the sattvic, rajasic and tamasic person, and chapter 17 extends this to food, faith and charity. Modern Indian psychology treats the triguna model as a personality typology and has turned it into self-report inventories. Examiners build variants from neighbouring lists, so keep them apart: the three doshas of Ayurveda (vata, pitta, kapha) are bodily humours, not gunas; the four purusharthas are goals of life (dharma, artha, kama, moksha); and ahimsa (non-violence) is the first of the five yamas in the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, alongside satya, asteya, brahmacharya and aparigraha. Ahimsa is also the central vow of Jainism. Option d is right because ahimsa is an ethical restraint and not one of the three gunas of sattva, rajas and tamas.","traps":{"a":"Rajas is a genuine guna. It stands for activity, passion, desire and restlessness, and in Samkhya it is the force that sets prakriti in motion. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) links it to craving and attachment to work and its rewards. It cannot answer a 'not one of the gunas' stem. It tempts only if you misread the stem as asking which option is a guna.","b":"Tamas is a genuine guna, the quality of heaviness, inertia, darkness, ignorance and sleepiness. In Samkhya it is the principle of resistance that holds things in place; in the Gita the tamasic person is lazy, confused and careless. It belongs to the triad, so it is not the exception. It tempts because 'tamas' sounds negative, and candidates sometimes assume a negative quality cannot be a basic constituent.","c":"Sattva is a genuine guna, the quality of lightness, purity, clarity, knowledge and harmony. Indian psychology treats a sattva-dominant temperament as the healthiest, calm and balanced. It is part of the triad, so it cannot be the odd one out. It tempts because sattva sounds like a virtue, and a candidate may wrongly group it with ethical ideals such as ahimsa rather than with the gunas."}},{"q":"What is the Sanskrit term for the waking state according to the Mandukya Upanishad?","concept":"The Mandukya Upanishad is the shortest of the principal Upanishads, only twelve verses, and belongs to the Atharva Veda. It analyses the Self (Atman) through four states of consciousness and maps them onto the sacred syllable Om, split into A, U, M and the silence after them. The first state is jagrat, the waking state; the Self here is called Vaishvanara or Vishva (spelt Viswa in many papers). It is 'outward-knowing', experiencing gross external objects through the senses, and corresponds to the letter A. The second is svapna, the dream state; the Self is Taijasa, 'inward-knowing', enjoying subtle mental images, and corresponds to U. The third is sushupti, deep dreamless sleep; the Self is Prajna, a unified mass of consciousness with no desire and no dream, corresponding to M. The fourth is Turiya, literally 'the fourth', which is not really a state but the pure, non-dual witness underlying the other three, matching the silence after Om. Gaudapada's Mandukya Karika, the earliest systematic Advaita Vedanta text, is a commentary on this scheme. Later Vedanta links the first three states to the three bodies: gross (sthula), subtle (sukshma) and causal (karana). For exams, separate the names of the states (jagrat, svapna, sushupti, turiya) from the names of the experiencing Self in each (Vishva, Taijasa, Prajna); variants switch between the two lists. Option a is right because Vishva (Viswa), also called Vaishvanara, is the Upanishad's name for the Self in the waking state, and it is the only waking-state term among the options.","traps":{"b":"Taijasa ('the luminous one', from tejas, light) is the Self in svapna, the dream state. It is inward-knowing: it experiences subtle images made by the mind from waking impressions, and it corresponds to the letter U of Om. It does not answer a question about waking. It tempts because it is listed second, straight after Vishva, and candidates who memorise the sequence loosely swap the first two.","c":"Prajna is the Self in sushupti, deep dreamless sleep, where there is neither desire nor dream. The Upanishad calls it a unified mass of consciousness, full of bliss, and links it to the letter M of Om. Later Vedanta pairs it with the causal body. It is not the waking state. It tempts because the word prajna also means wisdom or awareness in general, which sounds like wakefulness.","d":"Turiya means 'the fourth'. It is pure consciousness, the non-dual Atman that witnesses waking, dream and deep sleep but is none of them, and it matches the silence after Om. The Upanishad describes it mostly by negation: neither inward-knowing nor outward-knowing. It is not the waking state. It tempts because it is the most famous term in the text and sounds like a heightened, fully awake state."}},{"q":"What is the principle of disinterestedness that the Gita is based on?","concept":"The Bhagavad Gita is a 700-verse dialogue between Krishna and the warrior Arjuna, set in the Bhishma Parva of the Mahabharata. Arjuna refuses to fight because he fears the results of killing his kin; Krishna's answer is a theory of action. Its core is verse 2.47: you have a right to your action, never to its fruits; do not let the fruit be your motive, and do not cling to inaction either. This is nishkama karma, literally 'desireless action': doing your duty (svadharma) fully and well while giving up attachment to success or failure. Its opposite is sakama karma, action done for a desired reward. The Gita adds samatva (evenness of mind in gain and loss) and lokasangraha (acting for the welfare of the world). It describes several paths (margas): karma yoga, the path of action; jnana yoga, the path of knowledge and discrimination; bhakti yoga, the path of loving devotion; and dhyana yoga, meditation. Nishkama karma is the attitude that turns ordinary work into karma yoga, so the two are linked but not identical: karma yoga is the path, nishkama karma is the principle of disinterestedness that drives it. Bal Gangadhar Tilak's Gita Rahasya (1915) read the whole text as a call to such selfless action. Indian psychologists discuss the idea as a buffer against outcome anxiety and burnout, close to Western ideas of process focus and intrinsic motivation. Option d is right because the stem asks for the specific principle of disinterested action, and that is nishkama karma, action without attachment to its fruits.","traps":{"a":"Bhakti yoga is the path of loving devotion to a personal God, developed in Gita chapter 12 and later in the bhakti movements. It is a path (marga), not the principle of acting without desire for results, though a devotee may offer every action to God. It tempts because the Gita's closing teaching (18.66) asks Arjuna to surrender to Krishna, and many readers see devotion as its main message.","b":"Jnana yoga is the path of knowledge: discriminating the eternal Self (Atman) from the changing body and mind, taught in chapter 2 and developed in Advaita Vedanta. It aims at liberation through insight, not at a rule for how to act. It tempts because the Gita's first teaching to Arjuna, that the Self cannot be killed, is a jnana argument, and nishkama karma is presented right after it.","c":"Karma yoga is the path of action, the discipline of doing one's duty as a spiritual practice, developed mainly in chapters 3 and 5. It is the broader path; nishkama karma is the specific attitude of disinterest in results that makes action into yoga. It tempts most strongly because the two are taught together and many textbooks use them as synonyms. A stem asking for the 'principle of disinterestedness' wants nishkama karma."}},{"q":"What is the focus of Mahayana Buddhism?","concept":"Buddhism began with Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in north India around the fifth century BCE. Its core teaching is the Four Noble Truths (suffering, its cause in craving, its cessation, and the Eightfold Path) and the Middle Way between indulgence and harsh asceticism. Within a few centuries of his death the tradition divided. The older stream, surviving today as Theravada ('teaching of the elders') in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, keeps the Pali canon and holds up the arhat, the person who reaches nirvana through his or her own effort. Mahayana ('great vehicle') arose around the start of the Common Era and spread to China, Korea, Japan and Tibet. Its defining ideal is the bodhisattva, a being who postpones final nirvana in order to free all sentient beings from suffering, guided by karuna (compassion) and prajna (wisdom). Mahayana followers called the older path Hinayana ('lesser vehicle'), a term now considered pejorative. Mahayana philosophy produced two major schools: Madhyamaka of Nagarjuna (around the second century CE), which teaches sunyata, the emptiness of all phenomena, and Yogacara, which holds that experience is 'mind only'. Vajrayana, the tantric Buddhism of Tibet, grew out of Mahayana and adds mantra, mandala and ritual as fast methods to enlightenment. Variants often ask about the bodhisattva, the arhat, or which school stresses sunyata, so link each term to its tradition. Option c is right because Mahayana's central focus is the bodhisattva aim of collective freedom from suffering and teaching all beings the way to enlightenment, rather than individual liberation alone.","traps":{"a":"Tantric rituals, mantras, mandalas and guru initiation are the marks of Vajrayana ('diamond vehicle'), the form of Buddhism dominant in Tibet. Vajrayana grew out of Mahayana and shares its bodhisattva ideal, but its distinctive ritual methods are not the focus of Mahayana as a whole. It tempts because Tibetan Buddhism is the most visible Mahayana-related tradition, so candidates assume its rituals define Mahayana.","b":"This describes a caricature of the older Theravada stream, which keeps the Pali Tripitaka as its canon and emphasises monastic discipline and individual liberation as an arhat. Even there, strict asceticism is not the goal: the Buddha himself rejected extreme self-mortification in favour of the Middle Way. It tempts because textbooks contrast Mahayana with a more conservative, monk-centred tradition, and this option sounds like that contrast.","d":"Voidness or emptiness (sunyata) of both inner and outer phenomena is a real Mahayana doctrine, taught by Nagarjuna's Madhyamaka school and the Perfection of Wisdom sutras: nothing has a fixed, independent essence. But it is a philosophical tenet within Mahayana, not its defining focus or aim. It tempts most because it is true of Mahayana, so you must choose the broader orientation (collective liberation) over a single doctrine."}},{"q":"Which pitaka in the Tripitaka is a rulebook for monks and nuns?","concept":"The Tripitaka (Pali Tipitaka, 'three baskets') is the canon of Buddhist scripture. Tradition says that soon after the Buddha's death the first council at Rajagriha fixed the teachings orally: the monk Upali recited the rules of discipline and Ananda, the Buddha's attendant, recited the discourses. The Pali version was first written down in Sri Lanka in the first century BCE. The first basket is the Vinaya Pitaka, the basket of discipline. It contains the monastic code (the Patimokkha), the story of how each rule arose, and procedures for ordination and community life; in Theravada the code has 227 rules for monks (bhikkhus) and more for nuns (bhikkhunis). The second is the Sutta Pitaka, the basket of discourses, holding the Buddha's sermons in five collections (nikayas): Digha, Majjhima, Samyutta, Anguttara and Khuddaka; the Dhammapada and the Jataka tales sit in the Khuddaka. The third is the Abhidhamma Pitaka, the 'higher teaching', a systematic analysis of mind and matter that lists moments of consciousness (citta) and mental factors (cetasika), which is why it is often called the first Buddhist psychology. Remember the pattern: Vinaya equals rules, Sutta equals sermons, Abhidhamma equals analysis of mind. Variants may ask which basket contains the Dhammapada (Sutta), which matters most for Buddhist psychology (Abhidhamma), or which was recited by Upali (Vinaya). Mahayana schools also accept further sutras outside the Pali canon. Option a is right because the Vinaya Pitaka is the disciplinary rulebook for monks and nuns, setting out the Patimokkha rules and monastic procedures.","traps":{"b":"The Sutta Pitaka is the basket of discourses: thousands of sermons and dialogues of the Buddha and his chief disciples, arranged in five nikayas, including the Dhammapada and the Jataka tales. It teaches doctrine (dhamma) such as the Four Noble Truths and dependent origination, not the monastic code. It tempts because it is the largest and best-known basket, and some suttas do describe how monks should behave.","c":"The Abhidhamma Pitaka, the 'higher teaching', is a later scholastic analysis that breaks experience into momentary states of consciousness (citta), mental factors (cetasika), matter and nirvana. It is the basket psychology students meet most often, as an early theory of mind. It is philosophy and psychology, not a rulebook. It tempts because its formal, list-heavy style looks like a code of rules.","d":"'None of the above' would be right only if no basket contained the monastic rules. The Vinaya Pitaka is exactly that rulebook, so this option fails. It tempts candidates who are unsure of the three names, or who think the rules for monks lie outside the canon in a separate code, since the Patimokkha is often recited and printed as a standalone text."}},{"q":"What are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology?","concept":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, inward tradition of Islam. Its psychology, built up by classical writers such as Abu Hamid al-Ghazali (1058 to 1111) and summarised for modern psychologists by Robert Frager in Heart, Self and Soul (1999), describes the person through three principal aspects. Nafs is the self or ego, the seat of desires, appetites and selfish drives; it is not evil in itself but must be trained. The tradition names its stages from the Quran: nafs al-ammara (the commanding self that urges wrongdoing), nafs al-lawwama (the self-reproaching self, close to conscience) and nafs al-mutmainna (the self at peace). Qalb, often written ghalb from its Persian pronunciation, is the heart: not the physical organ but the spiritual centre of perception, intuition and love, which can be veiled by the nafs or polished like a mirror to reflect the divine. Ruh is the spirit, the divine breath God blew into Adam, which longs to return to its source. Growth means purifying the nafs so that the heart opens and the ruh can guide the person. Do not confuse these inner aspects with the stages of the Sufi path, commonly listed as sharia (religious law), tariqa (the mystical way under a sheikh), haqiqa (inner truth) and marifa (direct knowledge of God). Sharia is an external code of conduct, not a part of the human being. Option a is right because nafs, qalb (ghalb) and ruh, the self, heart and spirit, are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps qalb (ghalb, the heart) and nafs (the self) but replaces ruh, the spirit, with sharia. Sharia is Islamic religious law, the external code of worship and conduct and the first stage of the Sufi path, not an inner aspect of the person. It tempts because two of the three terms are correct and sharia is the most familiar Islamic word, so partial recall picks it.","c":"This option keeps nafs (the self) and ruh (the spirit) but drops qalb, the heart, which is the centre of Sufi psychology, and adds sharia, the religious law. Sharia governs outward behaviour; the heart is where Sufis locate spiritual perception and love. It tempts because nafs and ruh are the two terms most often contrasted as lower and higher self, so the missing middle term is easy to forget.","d":"This option keeps ruh (spirit) and qalb (heart) but leaves out nafs, the ego or lower self whose training is the main work of the Sufi path, and inserts sharia, the religious law. Without nafs the model loses its account of desire and inner struggle. It tempts because it contains the two 'higher' aspects, and a candidate may wrongly think the law, not the ego, is the third element."}},{"q":"What is the innermost being in the concentric system of Integral Yoga?","concept":"Integral Yoga (Purna Yoga) is the system of Sri Aurobindo (1872 to 1950), the nationalist turned philosopher and yogi of Pondicherry, set out in The Life Divine and The Synthesis of Yoga. It aims not only at individual liberation but at transforming the whole of human nature, and it is a key source for Indian psychology. Aurobindo described the personality on two axes. The concentric system runs from outside to inside. The outermost ring is the outer or surface being: the physical (body), the vital (life-energy, desires, emotions, drives) and the mind (thought, will, reason). Behind it lies the inner or subliminal being, with an inner mind, inner vital and inner physical that are wider and subtler. At the very centre is the psychic being (chaitya purusha), the evolving soul, a spark of the divine that grows across lives and can guide the outer nature once it comes forward. The vertical system runs from below to above: the subconscient and inconscient lie below ordinary mind, while higher planes rise above it through higher mind, illumined mind, intuition and overmind to the supermind. Change follows a triple transformation: psychic (bringing the psychic being forward), spiritual (opening to higher planes) and supramental. A useful neighbour is the five koshas of the Taittiriya Upanishad (annamaya, pranamaya, manomaya, vijnanamaya, anandamaya), another layered model of the person that moves from body inwards to bliss. Option a is right because in Aurobindo's concentric system the psychic being, the inmost soul, lies at the centre, behind the outer layers of body, vital and mind.","traps":{"b":"In Integral Yoga the mind (thought, reason, will and perception) is one of the three layers of the outer or surface being, together with the vital and the physical. It is the highest part of ordinary nature but lies on the outer ring of the concentric system, not at its centre. It tempts because Western psychology often treats the mind as the core of the self, and Aurobindo's vertical system places higher mental planes above it.","c":"The vital is Aurobindo's term for the life-energy layer: desires, emotions, ambitions, drives and sensations, roughly matching prana in Indian thought. It sits between the mind and the body in the outer being and must be purified so that the psychic being can lead. It is not the innermost centre. It tempts because the vital feels intensely personal and close to who we are, so it can be mistaken for the core self.","d":"The physical or body is the most external layer of the outer being in the concentric system, the material base that Integral Yoga ultimately also seeks to transform. It is the outermost, not the innermost, part. It tempts candidates who reverse the scale, since layered models such as the five koshas start from the body (annamaya kosha) and list it first, which can be misread as most central."}},{"q":"When was the National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences established?","concept":"The National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru, is India's leading centre for psychiatry, clinical psychology, neurology and neurosurgery. Its roots lie in the Bangalore Lunatic Asylum of the mid nineteenth century, which later became the Mysore Government Mental Hospital. In the mid 1950s this hospital was developed into the All India Institute of Mental Health (AIIMH), a national teaching and research centre for psychiatry and clinical psychology. In 1974 the AIIMH and the mental hospital merged to form NIMHANS as an autonomous institute. It became a deemed university in 1994, and the NIMHANS Bengaluru Act 2012 declared it an Institute of National Importance. Sources differ on the 'established' year: NIMHANS histories count from 1954 (AIIMH) or 1974 (NIMHANS), and some question banks print 1955. Learn the parallel dates for the other entrance-exam institutes: the Central Institute of Psychiatry (CIP), Ranchi, was founded in 1918 as the Ranchi European Lunatic Asylum, and IHBAS, Delhi, grew out of the Hospital for Mental Diseases, Shahdara, and was set up as an institute in 1993. Common distractor years come from Indian psychology history: Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922, and the University of Calcutta opened India's first department of psychology in 1916. Today the RCI Act 1992 regulates the clinical psychology training these institutes offer. Option b is right because, among the years given, 1955 is the only one at the institutional origin of NIMHANS, the founding of the All India Institute of Mental Health in the mid 1950s (most official histories say 1954).","traps":{"a":"1922 is the year Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in Calcutta, one of the earliest landmarks of Indian psychology. It has nothing to do with NIMHANS or the All India Institute of Mental Health. It tempts because it sits in the cluster of early twentieth century history-of-psychology dates that candidates memorise together, so it feels familiar without being linked to any mental health institute.","c":"1924 is not a founding year for NIMHANS, the All India Institute of Mental Health or the other major mental health institutes in the syllabus: CIP Ranchi dates from 1918, and NIMHANS from 1954 and 1974. It works only as a filler year near other early twentieth century dates. It tempts candidates who vaguely remember that NIMHANS has roots in an older Mysore mental hospital and so guess a pre-independence date.","d":"1916 is when the University of Calcutta set up India's first department of psychology, which is why the year appears in Indian psychology history questions. It is unrelated to NIMHANS, which grew from a hospital in Bangalore, not a university department. It tempts because it is a genuine and frequently tested 'first' in Indian psychology, and candidates link any early landmark with the most famous institute."}},{"q":"What are the goodness or quality criteria for knowledge according to positivism?","concept":"This question comes from the paradigm tables of Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), 'Competing paradigms in qualitative research', in the Handbook of Qualitative Research. They compare four paradigms (positivism, postpositivism, critical theory and constructivism) first on three basic questions: ontology (what is real), epistemology (how the knower relates to the known) and methodology (how we find out). Positivism, named by Auguste Comte (1830s), holds a naive realist ontology (a real, knowable world), an objectivist epistemology (a detached observer can find value-free facts) and an experimental, mostly quantitative methodology that verifies hypotheses. Postpositivism keeps realism but makes it critical and stresses falsification, an idea from Karl Popper (1934). Guba and Lincoln then compare the paradigms on practical issues. For goodness or quality criteria, positivism and postpositivism use conventional benchmarks of rigour: internal validity, external validity, reliability and objectivity. Critical theory uses historical situatedness, erosion of ignorance and misapprehensions, and action stimulus. Constructivism uses trustworthiness and authenticity. Trustworthiness had been set out by Lincoln and Guba (1985) as parallel criteria: credibility (for internal validity), transferability (for external validity), dependability (for reliability) and confirmability (for objectivity). On knowledge accumulation, positivism sees accretion of 'building blocks' and cause-effect generalisations; critical theory, historical revisionism and generalisation by similarity; constructivism, more informed reconstructions and vicarious experience. Variant questions mix rows (aim, nature of knowledge, voice, values, ethics), so learn each row by paradigm. Option b is right because Guba and Lincoln list internal and external validity, reliability and objectivity as the positivist goodness or quality criteria.","traps":{"a":"Historical situatedness is the first goodness criterion of critical theory in Guba and Lincoln (1994): good inquiry must take account of the historical, social, economic and gender context of what it studies, and should also erode ignorance and stimulate action. Positivism, by contrast, seeks context-free, timeless laws. It tempts because it sounds like a scholarly quality standard and appears in the same row of the table.","c":"In Guba and Lincoln's (1994) table, 'generalization by similarity' sits in the knowledge-accumulation row under critical theory, beside historical revisionism: insights transfer to settings whose historical and structural conditions are similar. Positivist generalisation is instead statistical and law-like, building cause-effect linkages. It tempts because generalisation is a core positivist concern, and the phrase sounds like external validity.","d":"Vicarious experience belongs to constructivism in the knowledge-accumulation row of Guba and Lincoln (1994): knowledge grows as readers vicariously enter rich case descriptions and form more informed reconstructions, the logic of the naturalistic case study. It is a way of transferring understanding, not a positivist standard of rigour. It tempts because 'experience' sounds empirical, and empiricism is linked with positivism."}},{"q":"What is the voice in inquiry according to constructivism?","concept":"Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), in their chapter 'Competing paradigms in qualitative research', compared four inquiry paradigms: positivism, postpositivism, critical theory and constructivism. A paradigm, for them, is a basic belief system defined by its answers to three questions: ontological, epistemological and methodological. Constructivism holds a relativist ontology (realities are multiple, local, socially constructed), a transactional and subjectivist epistemology (findings are created through the interaction of inquirer and participants) and a hermeneutic, dialectical methodology (constructions are elicited, compared and refined through dialogue). Its aim is understanding and reconstruction, and its quality is judged by trustworthiness and authenticity. One row of their table, 'voice', asks what stance the inquirer takes. Positivism and postpositivism give the 'disinterested scientist' who informs decision makers, policy makers and change agents, standing apart from the values of the people studied. Critical theory gives the 'transformative intellectual', an advocate and activist who exposes oppression and works for emancipation, in the tradition of Paulo Freire (1970). Constructivism gives the 'passionate participant', a facilitator of multivoice reconstruction, who is personally involved and helps different stakeholders hear and revise each other's constructions. This links to the fourth generation evaluation of Guba and Lincoln (1989), where the evaluator negotiates among stakeholder claims and concerns. Variant questions ask the same row for another paradigm or swap in rows such as values (excluded versus formative) or ethics, so memorise the table by columns. Option c is right because in Guba and Lincoln's scheme the constructivist inquirer's voice is the 'passionate participant' who facilitates multivoice reconstruction.","traps":{"a":"The 'disinterested scientist' who informs decision makers, policy makers and change agents is the positivist and postpositivist voice in Guba and Lincoln (1994). The inquirer stands apart, keeps values out, and hands objective findings to those who act on them. Constructivists reject this detachment, since findings are co-created. It tempts because it is the default picture of a researcher in most psychology training.","b":"The 'transformative intellectual' as advocate and activist is the critical theory voice in Guba and Lincoln (1994), drawing on Frankfurt School critical theory, feminism and Paulo Freire's emancipatory pedagogy. The inquirer aims to expose structures of oppression and change them. Constructivists facilitate many voices rather than advocate one position. It tempts because constructivism and critical theory share a subjectivist epistemology and often appear together.","d":"'None of the above' would be right only if no option matched the constructivist voice. Choice c reproduces Guba and Lincoln's own wording for constructivism, the 'passionate participant' facilitating multivoice reconstruction, so this option fails. It tempts candidates who know constructivists reject the detached scientist but cannot recall the exact phrase, and who suspect the other labels are invented."}},{"q":"What was Socrates' method of gaining knowledge?","concept":"Socrates (c. 470 to 399 BCE) wrote nothing; we know his method mainly through the dialogues of his student Plato. His way of gaining knowledge was the elenchus (Greek for refutation or cross-examination), also called the Socratic method or Socratic dialectic. It works in steps. A person states a belief, often a definition, such as 'courage is a kind of endurance' in the Laches or 'piety is what the gods love' in the Euthyphro. Socrates then asks a series of questions that lead the person to accept further statements, and finally shows that these statements contradict the original claim. The result is aporia, a state of puzzlement in which the person realises he does not know what he thought he knew. Socrates claimed to know only that he knew nothing, and he called himself a midwife (maieutics) who helps others give birth to ideas already latent in them. Knowledge, for him, was reached by reason and dialogue, not by the senses, and his central concern was ethics: virtue is knowledge, and the unexamined life is not worth living. Examiners build variants around his successors. Plato developed the theory of Forms and of knowledge as recollection (anamnesis), a rationalist and nativist position. Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) turned to observation and classification of the natural world, the root of empiricism. Much later, Francis Bacon's Novum Organum (1620) promoted induction and experiment, which grew into experimental science. Option a is right because the elenchus, systematic questioning that exposes contradictions in a belief, was the defining Socratic method of seeking knowledge.","traps":{"b":"Observation and categorization describes the method of Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE), Plato's student, who collected and classified plants, animals and political constitutions and treated the senses as the starting point of knowledge. This is the root of empiricism and of later natural science, not of Socratic dialogue. It tempts because Socrates, Plato and Aristotle are taught as one chain, and examiners like to swap their methods.","c":"Intuition means grasping a truth directly, without step-by-step reasoning. In philosophy it is linked with later rationalists such as René Descartes (Meditations, 1641), who treated clear and distinct ideas as self-evident. Socrates did the opposite: he distrusted confident first impressions and tested them by slow questioning. It tempts because Socrates spoke of an inner divine voice (daimonion) that warned him, which sounds intuitive.","d":"Experimentation means deliberately manipulating a variable and measuring its effect under controlled conditions, the method that Francis Bacon (1620) championed and that psychology adopted when Wilhelm Wundt opened his Leipzig laboratory in 1879. Socrates never manipulated anything; his only tool was conversation. It tempts because his questioning is loosely described as 'testing' ideas, and because candidates assume any method of gaining knowledge must be scientific."}},{"q":"What did Descartes' dualism influence?","concept":"René Descartes (1596 to 1650) is the father of modern rationalism and the classic source of mind-body dualism, set out in his Meditations (1641) and The Passions of the Soul (1649). He divided reality into two substances: an unextended thinking mind (res cogitans) and an extended, mechanical body (res extensa). The two interact, he proposed, through the pineal gland. This split influenced psychology along two lines. First, because the body was a machine, its actions could be explained mechanically. Descartes described the reflex, in which an external stimulus moves 'animal spirits' through the nerves to produce a movement with no mind involved. This mechanistic, stimulus-response view of the body fed reflexology and later behaviourism, which John B. Watson (1913) launched by treating behaviour as mechanical responses to stimuli. Second, Descartes held that the mind has innate ideas (God, self, unity, the axioms of geometry) that are not learned from experience. This nativist strand passed through Immanuel Kant to the Gestalt psychologists, such as Max Wertheimer (1912), who argued that the mind organises perception by inborn principles. Variants may ask about interactionism versus psychophysical parallelism (Gottfried Leibniz), or about 'cogito ergo sum' from the Discourse on Method (1637). Remember that Descartes founded rationalism, and that empiricism, from John Locke (1690), was largely a reply to his innate ideas. Option a is right because Cartesian mechanism shaped behaviourism and Cartesian nativism shaped Gestalt psychology.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), centres on unconscious conflict, and humanistic psychology, led by Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers in the 1950s and 1960s, centres on growth and self-actualisation. Neither is built on the Cartesian picture of a conscious mind plus a machine body; Freud in fact challenged Descartes' equation of mind with consciousness. It tempts because both are major schools every candidate recognises.","c":"Existentialism (Martin Heidegger, Jean-Paul Sartre) and phenomenology (Edmund Husserl) are philosophical movements. Husserl did start from Descartes' method of doubt in his Cartesian Meditations (1931), but both movements tried to overcome the mind-body split by studying lived, embodied experience. They are not the psychological schools that inherited Cartesian mechanism and nativism. It tempts because Husserl openly named Descartes as his starting point.","d":"Rationalism (knowledge comes from reason and innate ideas) is the philosophical position Descartes himself founded, and empiricism (knowledge comes from sense experience), from John Locke (1690) onward, grew largely as a reply to his innate ideas. These are theories of knowledge that came before psychology, not schools that his dualism later shaped. It tempts because Descartes is the textbook rationalist and appears in every rationalism versus empiricism question."}},{"q":"What is the basic premise of Structuralism?","concept":"Structuralism was the first formal school of psychology. Its name came from Edward Bradford Titchener (1898), an Englishman who trained under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and then taught at Cornell University. Wundt had founded the first psychology laboratory in 1879 and called his own system voluntarism; Titchener narrowed it into structuralism. The subject matter was conscious experience, defined as experience dependent on the experiencing person. The aim was a kind of chemistry of the mind: break consciousness into its basic elements, then find out how they combine. Titchener stated three problems: reduce conscious processes to their simplest components, determine the laws by which these elements are combined and connected, and relate them to their physiological conditions. He named three elements: sensations (the basis of perception), images (the basis of ideas) and affective states or feelings (the basis of emotion). Each element had attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was analytic introspection by trained observers, who had to avoid the stimulus error, that is, describing the object ('a red apple') instead of the raw experience. The school faded after Titchener's death in 1927. Know the reactions to it: functionalism (William James, 1890; John Dewey) asked what consciousness is for; behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913) rejected introspection; Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) rejected elementism; psychoanalysis studied the unconscious. Option d is right because structuralism's stated premise was to describe the components of consciousness and how they are combined and connected.","traps":{"a":"Social structures and institutions (family, class, caste, economy) shaping behaviour is the subject of sociology, for instance the work of Émile Durkheim (The Division of Labour in Society, 1893), Talcott Parsons (The Social System, 1951), or the structural anthropology of Claude Lévi-Strauss (1958). These share only the word 'structure' with Titchener's school, which looked inward at the elements of individual consciousness. It tempts purely because of the name.","b":"The power of unconscious processes is the premise of psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (1900), who argued that most mental life lies outside awareness. Titchener's structuralism studied only conscious experience that a trained observer could report; it had no place for an unconscious. It tempts because psychoanalysis also describes a 'structure' of the mind, the id, ego and superego of Freud's structural model (1923).","c":"Free will and personal agency are the premise of humanistic psychology (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) and of existential psychology (Rollo May), the 'third force' of the 1950s and 1960s. Structuralism was strictly descriptive and elementistic; it did not deal with choice, values or the whole person. It tempts because Wundt called his own system voluntarism, a word that sounds as if it is about free will."}},{"q":"What is Sullivan's interpersonal theory?","concept":"Harry Stack Sullivan, an American psychiatrist, set out his interpersonal theory most fully in The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry (1953), published after his death in 1949. He belonged to the neo-Freudian, social-psychological group with Karen Horney (1945) and Erich Fromm (1941), who moved away from Sigmund Freud's biological drives. For Sullivan, personality cannot be studied in an isolated individual. He defined it as 'the relatively enduring pattern of recurrent interpersonal situations which characterize a human life'. A person exists only in a field of relations with others, real or imagined, so personality is an energy system in constant exchange with its social environment, an open system rather than a closed container of instincts. Key terms recur in exams. Tensions are of two kinds: needs (for tenderness, intimacy) and anxiety, which passes from mother to infant through empathy. Dynamisms are recurring patterns of behaviour; the self-system protects against anxiety using security operations such as selective inattention. Personifications are images of self ('good-me', 'bad-me', 'not-me') and of others. Experience develops through three modes: prototaxic (undifferentiated flow), parataxic (private, illogical links, the source of parataxic distortion, close to transference) and syntaxic (consensually validated, shared through language). He described developmental epochs from infancy to late adolescence and the therapist's role as participant observer. Contrast this with Freud's intrapsychic model and with the behaviourism of B. F. Skinner (1938). Option d is right because Sullivan saw personality as an open system that exists only in continuing interaction with its interpersonal environment.","traps":{"a":"The importance of unconscious processes is the defining claim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (1900): behaviour is driven by repressed, mainly sexual and aggressive, wishes within the individual's mind. Sullivan kept some dynamic ideas, such as selective inattention, but moved the focus from intrapsychic drives to relationships between people. It tempts because Sullivan trained in the psychoanalytic tradition and is grouped with the neo-Freudians.","b":"Social structures and institutions (class, economy, religion, the state) are the level of analysis of sociology and, within psychology, of writers such as Erich Fromm (1941), who linked character to the social and economic system. Sullivan worked at a smaller scale: the concrete relationship between two or more persons, starting with mother and infant. It tempts because both views are 'social' alternatives to Freud.","c":"Studying observable behaviour in social settings describes a behaviourist or social learning approach, for instance Albert Bandura's (1977) social learning theory or experimental social psychology. Sullivan studied inner experience too, including anxiety, personifications and modes of experience, inferred through the therapist's participant observation. It tempts because 'interpersonal' sounds like 'social behaviour', yet Sullivan's theory is about personality, not observable acts alone."}},{"q":"What are the five fundamental needs identified by Erich Fromm?","concept":"Erich Fromm, a German-born psychoanalyst and social philosopher, was one of the neo-Freudians who placed personality in its social and historical setting. In Escape from Freedom (1941) he argued that modern people, freed from medieval ties, feel isolated and may flee from freedom through authoritarianism, destructiveness or automaton conformity. In The Sane Society (1955) he proposed that humans, unlike animals, have lost instinctive harmony with nature; reason and self-awareness create an existential dilemma, and from it arise five distinctly human needs. Relatedness is the need to unite with others, best met through productive love rather than submission or domination. Transcendence is the need to rise above our passive, created status, met by creating or, failing that, by destroying. Rootedness is the need for belonging and a sense of home, met by brotherliness rather than fixation on mother, family or nation. Sense of identity is the need to experience oneself as a distinct individual, rather than through herd conformity. Frame of orientation is the need for a stable map of the world and an object of devotion, met best by reason rather than irrational belief. Fromm also described character orientations: four non-productive types (receptive, exploitative, hoarding and marketing) and the healthy productive orientation. Do not confuse these needs with the ranked hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943) or with self-determination theory, which also uses the word relatedness. Option d is right because relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, identity and orientation are exactly Fromm's five existential needs.","traps":{"a":"Safety, love and belonging, esteem and self-actualisation are levels of Abraham Maslow's (1943) hierarchy of needs; the full model starts with physiological needs at the base. Maslow ranked needs so that lower ones must be largely met before higher ones dominate. Fromm's needs are not ranked and arise from the human existential condition. It tempts because Maslow's list is the best-known five-item needs list in psychology.","b":"Competence, autonomy and relatedness are the three basic psychological needs of self-determination theory by Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), which explains intrinsic motivation. Self-esteem and purpose are padding. The option borrows 'relatedness', Fromm's first need, to look familiar. It tempts because it shares one term with the correct list and sounds like a modern humanistic account of human needs.","c":"Hunger, thirst, shelter, rest and reproduction are biological or physiological needs shared with other animals, the drives of Clark Hull's (1943) drive-reduction theory and the base of Maslow's pyramid. Fromm deliberately separated these animal needs from the distinctly human, existential needs that come from self-awareness and reason. It tempts because 'fundamental needs' sounds as if it should mean survival needs."}},{"q":"Who coined the term \"Mom-ism\" to describe the cultural idealization of motherhood?","concept":"Momism is a term from mid-twentieth-century American cultural criticism. The word itself first appeared in the writer Philip Wylie's best-selling polemic Generation of Vipers (1942), which mocked the sentimental cult of 'Mom' as a smothering, self-righteous figure who kept her sons dependent. The idea was taken into psychiatry by Edward A. Strecker, a professor of psychiatry at the University of Pennsylvania who advised the US armed forces during the Second World War. In Their Mothers' Sons (1946) he argued that many men rejected or discharged from military service for psychiatric reasons were the products of 'moms' who had failed to emotionally wean them. Strecker gave momism its clinical form, and he is the name this exam associates with the term. The concept belongs to a family of mother-blaming ideas that clinical psychology later rejected: the schizophrenogenic mother of Frieda Fromm-Reichmann (1948); the cold 'refrigerator' parent linked with Leo Kanner, who first described autism in 1943, and with Bruno Bettelheim (1967); and the double bind hypothesis of Gregory Bateson and colleagues (1956). Each blamed a parenting style for a severe disorder, and family and genetic research did not support them. Contrast these with Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), who proposed womb envy and challenged Freudian views of women, and with the mother archetype of Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961). Option d is right because, among the names listed, Edward Strecker is the one who brought momism into psychiatry, although strictly Philip Wylie coined the word.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) placed the mother at the centre of development through the Oedipus complex and the early mother-infant bond, but he did not coin momism. The word comes from American social criticism of the 1940s, after Freud's death. It tempts because Freud is the default answer for any question about mothers and sons, and Strecker's argument used loosely psychoanalytic language about dependency.","b":"Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) described the mother archetype, a universal image in the collective unconscious with both nourishing and devouring sides, and the mother complex. These are archetypal ideas, not cultural criticism of American mothers, and he did not use the term momism. It tempts because Jung's devouring or terrible mother sounds similar to the smothering 'mom' of Wylie and Strecker.","c":"Karen Horney (1885 to 1952) challenged Freud's view of women, proposed womb envy as a counterpart to penis envy, and explained neurosis through basic anxiety arising from parental indifference. She wrote about cultural influences on women but did not coin momism. It tempts because she is the only woman theorist in the list and is closely linked with motherhood, culture and feminine psychology."}},{"q":"Which of the following effects is characterized by a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lower ranks of the job scale?","concept":"Gender inequality at work is described through a set of glass and floor metaphors that examiners like to mix. The sticky floor refers to a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lowest-paid, low-status, low-mobility jobs at the bottom of the job scale, such as clerical, cleaning, care and retail work. The term is usually credited to the sociologist Catherine White Berheide (1992). It is about being stuck at the start: lack of training, part-time or informal contracts, no promotion ladders, and family responsibilities that limit mobility. The glass ceiling, a phrase popularised by a Wall Street Journal article in 1986, is the invisible barrier that stops qualified women and minorities from reaching top management even after they climb the middle ranks. The glass cliff, shown by Michelle Ryan and Alexander Haslam (2005) in their study of large UK firms, is the tendency to appoint women to leadership when an organisation is in crisis, so their position is precarious and failure more likely. The maternal wall, described by the legal scholar Joan C. Williams, is bias against workers who are mothers or become pregnant, who are assumed to be less committed. The glass escalator of Christine Williams (1992) is the reverse case: men in female-dominated jobs such as nursing are moved up quickly. Related ideas include the motherhood penalty and the leaky pipeline. Option c is right because the sticky floor is the pattern that holds women in the lower ranks rather than blocking them near the top.","traps":{"a":"The glass ceiling is an invisible but real barrier near the top of an organisation that blocks qualified women and minorities from senior management and boardroom posts. The metaphor was popularised by a 1986 Wall Street Journal article. It concerns the upper ranks, while this stem is about the lower ranks. It tempts because it is the most famous gender-discrimination metaphor and is often used loosely for any barrier.","b":"The maternal wall, a term from the legal scholar Joan C. Williams, is discrimination against employees who are pregnant, take maternity leave or have caregiving duties, based on the stereotype that mothers are less competent or committed. It can strike at any level of the job scale and is defined by parenthood, not rank. It tempts because it is a real women-specific barrier that also limits progress.","d":"The glass cliff, identified by Michelle Ryan and Alexander Haslam (2005), is the over-appointment of women to top leadership roles when a company is already in crisis or performing poorly, which makes those positions risky and failure likely. It concerns women who have reached the top, not those kept at the bottom. It tempts because it shares the 'glass' family name with the glass ceiling."}},{"q":"How is relative poverty measured?","concept":"Poverty is measured in two basic ways. Absolute poverty means lacking the income needed for a fixed minimum of food, shelter and other necessities, defined by a poverty line that does not move with the living standards of the society. The World Bank's international poverty line, a fixed number of dollars a day adjusted for purchasing power, and India's lines from the Tendulkar Committee (2009) and the Rangarajan Committee (2014) are absolute measures. Relative poverty means having so much less than others in one's own society that one cannot take part in its ordinary way of life. The sociologist Peter Townsend built this idea of relative deprivation in Poverty in the United Kingdom (1979). It is measured as the percentage of the population whose income falls below a fixed proportion of the median income: the OECD usually uses 50 percent of median household income, and the European Union uses 60 percent. The median is used because a few very rich households pull the mean upward. Because the line rises as the society gets richer, relative poverty is really a measure of inequality, and it may not fall during economic growth. Examiners also test types of poverty: situational poverty is caused by a specific event such as job loss, illness or divorce and is often temporary, while generational poverty lasts two or more generations, a distinction popularised by Ruby Payne (1996). Oscar Lewis proposed the culture of poverty (1959). Option b is right because relative poverty is the share of people below a fixed fraction of median income.","traps":{"a":"Situational poverty is poverty caused by a particular event, such as job loss, death of an earner, illness, divorce or a natural disaster, and it is often temporary; it is contrasted with generational poverty, as in Ruby Payne (1996). It is a type or cause of poverty, not a measurement method, and it says nothing about comparison with median income. It tempts because it sounds technical and 'relative to circumstances'.","c":"The percentage of the population living in rural areas is a measure of urbanisation (or its reverse), not of poverty. Rural areas often have higher poverty rates, and India has historically drawn separate rural and urban poverty lines, which is why the option feels plausible. But location alone does not define anyone as poor, absolutely or relatively. It tempts candidates who link poverty with villages.","d":"The percentage of the population living in urban areas is the urbanisation rate, a demographic indicator. Urban poverty exists, for example in slums, and is measured separately, but living in a city does not itself make a person poor. The option mirrors the rural one and is included as filler. It tempts only candidates who confuse where poverty is measured with how it is measured."}},{"q":"What are the three components of Deiner's tripartite model of subjective wellbeing?","concept":"Subjective well-being (SWB) is the scientific term for what people call happiness: how a person evaluates his or her own life, both in thought and in feeling. Ed Diener set out the tripartite model in a Psychological Bulletin review (1984), and it remains the standard definition. The three components are: life satisfaction, a cognitive, global judgement of one's life against one's own standards; frequent positive affect, such as joy, interest and contentment; and infrequent negative affect, such as sadness, anxiety and anger. High SWB means high life satisfaction, high positive affect and low negative affect. A key finding is that positive and negative affect are partly independent, as Norman Bradburn (1969) had shown, so reducing distress does not automatically create joy. Measurement follows the model: the five-item Satisfaction With Life Scale of Diener, Robert Emmons, Randy Larsen and Sharon Griffin (1985) for the cognitive part, and the PANAS of David Watson, Lee Anna Clark and Auke Tellegen (1988) for affect. Some writers add domain satisfactions (work, family, health). Contrast SWB, which is hedonic well-being, with eudaimonic models: the psychological well-being of Carol Ryff (1989), with six dimensions (self-acceptance, positive relations, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose in life, personal growth), and the PERMA model of Martin Seligman (2011). Related ideas include the hedonic treadmill of Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971). Option c is right because Diener's cognitive component is life satisfaction, joined by the presence of positive affect and the absence of negative affect.","traps":{"a":"Physical wellness (health, fitness, absence of illness) is a domain of life that people may be satisfied with, and it appears in multidimensional wellness models from health promotion. Diener's cognitive component is not a judgement about one particular domain; it is a global evaluation of life as a whole. It tempts because the two affect components are copied correctly, so only the first term differs.","b":"Spiritual wellness, a sense of meaning and connection with something larger, belongs to holistic wellness models and to research on spirituality and religiosity. It is not part of Diener's definition, which is deliberately content-free: people judge life by their own standards. It tempts because it keeps the two correct affect components and swaps only the cognitive part, and because meaning appears in eudaimonic models.","d":"Social wellness, or social well-being, is a separate model by Corey Keyes (1998) with five parts: social integration, acceptance, contribution, actualisation and coherence. Diener's cognitive component is global life satisfaction, not social functioning. It tempts because it keeps the two correct affect components and because positive relations with others appear in Carol Ryff's psychological well-being, a neighbouring model."}},{"q":"What is the set point theory of weight?","concept":"Set point theory holds that each person has a biologically regulated body weight, or more exactly a level of body fat, which the body defends much as a thermostat defends a temperature. Its roots are in the lipostatic hypothesis of Gordon Kennedy (1953), who proposed that the brain monitors fat stores and adjusts intake. Richard Nisbett (1972) used the idea to argue that many obese people are biologically underweight relative to their own higher set point. The set point is thought to be shaped by genes and early development. When weight falls below it, the body compensates: hunger rises, and energy expenditure drops more than the loss of tissue alone predicts. Rudolph Leibel, Michael Rosenbaum and Jules Hirsch (1995) showed that maintaining a 10 percent weight loss reduced daily energy expenditure beyond what was expected, while weight gain raised it. The discovery of leptin by Jeffrey Friedman's group (1994), a hormone released by fat cells that signals the hypothalamus, gave the theory a mechanism. This explains why most diets fail in the long run. Critics prefer a settling point model, in which weight drifts to a balance shaped by the food environment rather than a fixed target. Know the neighbouring ideas: restraint theory and disinhibition (C. Peter Herman and Janet Polivy, 1975), social contagion of obesity (Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler, 2007), and weight cycling. Option b is right because set point theory states that each person has a biological weight and resists weight loss by lowering energy expenditure.","traps":{"a":"Stress and anxiety disinhibiting eating comes from the restraint theory of C. Peter Herman and Janet Polivy (1975): chronic dieters hold back eating by cognitive control, and stress, alcohol or a broken diet rule releases that restraint, producing overeating (the 'what-the-hell effect'). This is a psychological account of eating, not a biologically defended weight. It tempts because it also explains why dieting often fails.","c":"Obesity spreading through social networks is the social contagion finding of Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler (2007), who analysed Framingham Heart Study data and found that a person's chance of becoming obese rose sharply if a friend, sibling or spouse became obese. It is a social and environmental explanation, not a biological set point. It tempts because it is a well-known modern theory of weight gain.","d":"Weight cycling, or yo-yo dieting, is repeated loss and regain. Animal work by Kelly Brownell and colleagues (1986) suggested that each cycle made later weight loss slower and regain faster, partly through lower metabolic rate; human evidence is mixed. It is a separate hypothesis about the effects of dieting history. It tempts because it resembles set point compensation, but it does not state the core claim of a biologically defended weight."}},{"q":"What is Buber's concept of the 'I-Thou' attitude?","concept":"Martin Buber, an Austrian-born Jewish philosopher, described two basic attitudes a person can take toward the world in his book I and Thou (Ich und Du, 1923). In the I-It attitude, the other is experienced as an object: something to be observed, measured, categorised, used or analysed into parts. Much of daily life, and all of science, properly runs on I-It. In the I-Thou (I-You) attitude, the other is met as a whole, unique person in a direct, mutual, present relationship, with no purpose beyond the meeting itself. Buber wrote that 'all real living is meeting', and held that the self becomes fully a self only through such relation. For psychology this became the basis of the dialogical and existential view of therapy: the therapist does not only diagnose the client but meets and confirms the client in the totality of his or her being, as a unique person embedded in relationships. Buber held a public dialogue with Carl Rogers in 1957 on whether the therapy relationship could be fully mutual; the core conditions Rogers described the same year (unconditional positive regard, empathy and congruence) are close to the I-Thou stance. The idea also shaped existential therapists such as Rollo May (1958) and Irvin Yalom (1980), and Gestalt therapy's emphasis on contact. Contrast it with the nomothetic approach, which seeks general laws and treats people as instances of categories, and with reductionism, which explains a person by breaking him into parts. Option b is right because I-Thou means validating the client as a whole, unique and socially embedded person.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing the person as an objective entity to be systematised and analysed is exactly Buber's I-It attitude, the opposite pole of I-Thou. In I-It the other becomes a thing to know or use, as in a purely diagnostic or nomothetic stance. Buber did not condemn I-It, which science needs, but it is not genuine meeting. It tempts because it is drawn from the same theory and uses Buber's own contrast.","c":"Breaking the individual down into universal parts is reductionism or elementism, the method of structuralism and of trait or factor approaches that describe people by common dimensions. It is closest to the I-It attitude, and the opposite of meeting a person as a unique whole. It tempts because it sounds academic and systematic, and candidates may think a philosophical concept must involve analysis into components.","d":"'None of the above' is a filler option. It would be right only if no listed choice captured I-Thou, but option b clearly states Buber's idea of meeting and confirming the whole, unique person. It tempts candidates who notice that b uses therapy language ('client') that Buber himself did not use in 1923, and who conclude that no option is faithful to the original philosophy."}},{"q":"What type of information is included in AXIS III of the DSM-IV-TR?","concept":"The multiaxial system was introduced by the American Psychiatric Association in DSM-III (1980) and kept in DSM-IV (1994) and its text revision, DSM-IV-TR (2000). It asked the clinician to record information on five axes, so that a diagnosis captured the whole clinical picture rather than a single label. Axis I: clinical disorders and other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention, such as schizophrenia, major depressive disorder, anxiety disorders and substance use disorders. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation, plus prominent maladaptive personality features and defence mechanisms. Axis III: general medical conditions potentially relevant to understanding or managing the mental disorder, such as hypothyroidism in a patient with depression, or a head injury. Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems, such as bereavement, unemployment, housing or legal problems. Axis V: Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a single rating on a 100-point scale. A memory aid: I clinical, II personality and retardation, III medical, IV stressors, V functioning. DSM-5 (2013) abolished the multiaxial system: Axes I to III were merged into one list of diagnoses, Axis IV became notes on psychosocial factors and Z codes, and the GAF was dropped, with the WHODAS 2.0 suggested instead. Mental retardation was renamed intellectual disability. ICD-10 (1992) and ICD-11 (adopted 2019, in force 2022) do not use these axes. Option c is right because Axis III of DSM-IV-TR records general medical conditions.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders and mental retardation were placed on Axis II, to make sure that long-standing patterns were not overlooked when an Axis I disorder drew attention. Examples are borderline personality disorder and mild mental retardation, now called intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) in DSM-5 (2013). It tempts because Axis II is the axis people remember best, and the order of axes is easy to mix up.","b":"Clinical psychiatric diagnoses, the main mental disorders such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, major depression and anxiety disorders, were recorded on Axis I. This was the primary diagnostic axis and listed everything except personality disorders and mental retardation. It tempts because 'clinical diagnosis' sounds like the most important information, and some candidates assume medical and psychiatric conditions shared an axis.","d":"Psychosocial and environmental problems, such as death of a family member, divorce, job loss, poverty, discrimination or problems with the legal system, were recorded on Axis IV. They are stressors that can influence diagnosis, treatment and prognosis. It tempts because Axes III and IV are adjacent and both describe context around the mental disorder; one records physical illness, the other records life stressors."}},{"q":"What is the term used to describe the inherited vulnerability?","concept":"The diathesis-stress model explains psychological disorders as the product of two things: a predisposition and a trigger. Diathesis comes from the Greek for disposition or arrangement; in older medicine it meant a constitutional tendency to a disease. In psychopathology it is the term for an inherited or early-acquired vulnerability, usually genetic or biological, though cognitive styles and early adversity can also count. Stress is the environmental or life event that acts on the diathesis to produce the disorder. Neither is enough alone: a person with a strong diathesis may need only mild stress, while a person with a weak diathesis can stay well unless stress is severe. Paul Meehl (1962) proposed an inherited neural defect, schizotaxia, which can develop into schizotypy and, under stress, into schizophrenia. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) restated this as the vulnerability-stress model, in which vulnerability is a relatively enduring trait while episodes come and go. Evidence includes identical-twin concordance for schizophrenia of roughly 40 to 50 percent, well short of 100, so environment matters. Cognitive diatheses are used for depression in the cognitive model of Aaron T. Beck (1967) and the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). A broader frame is the biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977). Distinguish the terms: diathesis is the technical name for the inherited predisposition, the stressor is the precipitating event, and vulnerability is the broader everyday word. Option c is right because diathesis is the technical term for an inherited vulnerability.","traps":{"a":"A stressor is the environmental event or demand that sets off a stress response, such as bereavement, job loss or conflict; Hans Selye (1956) and Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) built models of how people respond to it. In the diathesis-stress model the stressor is the precipitating, external half, not the inherited predisposition. It tempts because 'stressor' is the other half of the same model.","b":"Vulnerability is the general word for susceptibility, and Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) used it in their vulnerability-stress model. But it is the broader term, covering inherited and acquired weaknesses, and the stem asks for the technical name of an inherited vulnerability. It tempts strongly because it simply repeats a word from the stem; examiners often place the stem's own word as a decoy.","d":"'Enduring factor' is not a standard term in the model. Zubin and Spring did describe vulnerability as a relatively enduring trait, while stress and episodes are time-limited, and case formulation speaks of predisposing (enduring) versus precipitating and maintaining factors. It tempts because a diathesis is indeed enduring, but this descriptive phrase is not the name of the concept."}},{"q":"The greater the intensity of a stimulus, greater the probability that a learned response will be elicited. This is called","concept":"Clark L. Hull built the most formal learning theory of the neo-behaviourist period, a hypothetico-deductive drive-reduction theory, in Principles of Behavior (1943) and later in A Behavior System (1952). He wrote behaviour as equations linking stimulus inputs to response outputs through intervening variables. The core is reaction potential, sEr, the tendency of a stimulus to evoke a learned response. In the later version, sEr = sHr × D × V × K. Here sHr is habit strength, built up by the number of reinforced trials; D is drive, a state of need such as hours of food deprivation; V is stimulus-intensity dynamism, the principle that the more intense the stimulus, the greater the reaction potential and so the probability that the learned response is elicited; and K is incentive motivation, the size or quality of the reward, added after Leo Crespi (1942) showed that changing reward size changes performance at once. Because the terms multiply, if any one is zero, no response occurs. Reaction potential is then reduced by inhibition: reactive inhibition (Ir), a fatigue-like state from responding, and conditioned inhibition (sIr). Oscillation (sOr) adds random variation, and a response appears only if reaction potential exceeds the reaction threshold (sLr). Hull measured response strength by latency, amplitude and resistance to extinction. Kenneth Spence (1956) refined incentive motivation, and John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950) carried the theory into personality. Option a is right because Hull's name for the principle that a more intense stimulus raises the probability of the learned response is stimulus-intensity dynamism.","traps":{"b":"Incentive motivation (K) is Hull's term for the effect of the amount or quality of reward on reaction potential, added after Leo Crespi (1942) found that rats ran faster as soon as reward size was increased. It concerns the reinforcer that follows the response, not the strength of the stimulus that elicits it. It tempts because K and V sit side by side in the same Hullian equation.","c":"Response amplitude is one of Hull's measures of response strength, the magnitude of the response, such as the number of drops of saliva or the force of a lever press, alongside latency, probability and resistance to extinction. It is a dependent variable, an outcome, not the name of a principle relating stimulus intensity to responding. It tempts because a stronger stimulus may indeed produce a larger response.","d":"Reaction threshold (sLr) in Hull's system is the minimum reaction potential that must be exceeded before any response appears. It is a fixed cut-off, not a law about stimulus intensity. In sensory psychology a threshold is also the minimum stimulus intensity detected, as in the psychophysics of Gustav Fechner (1860), which makes the option sound related to stimulus intensity. That overlap is why it tempts."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning is learning that takes place without reinforcement and is not shown in behaviour until a reason to use it appears. It was demonstrated by Hugh Blodgett (1929) and most famously by Edward C. Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930) at Berkeley. Three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze. One group found food in the goal box every day and gradually made fewer errors. A second group was never rewarded and improved only a little. The third group was not rewarded for the first ten days and behaved like the unrewarded group; when food was introduced on day 11, its errors dropped sharply on the very next trials, matching or bettering the always-rewarded group. The sudden jump showed that the rats had been learning the maze all along; reinforcement affected performance, not learning. Tolman (1948) explained this with the cognitive map, an internal representation of the layout of the environment, and called his position purposive behaviourism. This challenged the reinforcement theories of Edward Thorndike (1898) and Clark Hull (1943), and was an early root of cognitive psychology. The learning-performance distinction also appears in Albert Bandura's (1965) Bobo doll study: children who saw a model punished imitated less, but when offered rewards they all reproduced the aggression. Other variants: place learning versus response learning, insight learning (Wolfgang Köhler, 1925) and incidental learning. Option d is right because a sudden rise in performance once reinforcement is introduced, after unreinforced trials, is the hallmark of latent learning.","traps":{"a":"Observational learning is learning by watching a model, described by Albert Bandura in social learning theory (1977) through four processes: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. The learner acquires behaviour without performing it or being reinforced directly. The stem, however, describes an organism's own unrewarded trials followed by reinforcement, with no model involved. It tempts because Bandura also separated learning from performance.","b":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond to one stimulus and not to a similar one, because only the first (the discriminative stimulus or S+) is reinforced, as when the dogs of Ivan Pavlov (1927) learned to salivate to one tone but not another. It needs differential reinforcement from the start, not unreinforced trials followed by reward. It tempts because it also involves trials, mazes and choices.","c":"Vicarious reinforcement means that seeing another person rewarded for a behaviour raises the observer's tendency to perform it, as in Albert Bandura's (1965) Bobo doll study, where children who saw the model rewarded copied more aggression. It requires a model who is reinforced. In the stem the organism itself had never been reinforced and then was. It tempts because it explains behaviour shown without direct reinforcement."}},{"q":"Cocktail party effect is better explained by","concept":"The cocktail party effect is the ability to follow one conversation in a noisy room while still noticing important material, such as your own name, in a conversation you were ignoring. Colin Cherry (1953) studied it with dichotic listening and shadowing: a different message goes to each ear, and the listener repeats one aloud. Listeners could report almost nothing of the meaning of the unattended message, not even a change of language, but noticed physical changes such as a male voice becoming female or a tone. Donald Broadbent (1958) explained this with his filter model, an early-selection theory: a selective filter acts like an all-or-none switch, passing one channel on the basis of physical features and blocking the rest before meaning is analysed. The problem was breakthrough. Neville Moray (1959) found that about a third of listeners heard their own name in the unattended ear, and Anne Treisman (1960) showed listeners following a story when it moved to the wrong ear. Treisman's attenuation model (1964) replaced the switch with an attenuator: unattended messages are turned down, not cut off, and words with low thresholds (one's name, danger words, expected words) can still reach awareness. J. Anthony Deutsch and Diana Deutsch (1963) proposed late selection, in which all input is analysed for meaning. Capacity theory (Daniel Kahneman, 1973) and automatic versus controlled processing (Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin, 1977) deal with resource limits. Option b is right because Treisman's attenuator model explains how one's name breaks through an unattended conversation.","traps":{"a":"The switch model is Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter theory, in which a selective filter acts like an all-or-none switch that passes one channel by physical features and blocks the others completely before meaning is processed. It explains why Cherry's listeners missed the unattended content, but it cannot explain breakthrough of one's own name, the core of the cocktail party effect. It tempts because it was the first formal model of selective attention.","c":"Automatic versus controlled processing is the dual-process account of Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin (1977): practised tasks become fast, parallel and effortless, while novel tasks need slow, serial, capacity-limited control. The Stroop effect (John Ridley Stroop, 1935) is the classic example. The model is about practice and effort, not about how an unattended channel is filtered. It tempts because detecting one's own name feels automatic.","d":"Capacity theory is Daniel Kahneman's (1973) resource model in Attention and Effort: attention is a limited pool of mental effort, shared among tasks by an allocation policy that depends on arousal, enduring dispositions and momentary intentions. It explains dual-task interference and divided attention rather than which item in an ignored message breaks through. It tempts because Kahneman's model is the standard alternative to filter theories."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a factor of the extent of lateral inhibition","concept":"Lateral inhibition is the process by which an excited neuron reduces the activity of its neighbours. It was first measured precisely by Haldan Keffer Hartline and Floyd Ratliff in the eye of the horseshoe crab, Limulus, in the 1950s; Hartline shared the 1967 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Limulus is useful because each ommatidium (a simple eye unit) has its own receptor with a large nerve fibre that can be recorded, and neighbouring receptors are linked by a lateral plexus that carries inhibition sideways. The classic experiment: light on receptor A alone produces a high firing rate in A's fibre. When light is also shone on a neighbouring receptor B, A's firing drops. The more intense the light on B, the stronger the inhibition of A. So the extent of lateral inhibition depends on how strongly the inhibiting cell is stimulated, and it also falls off with distance between the two cells. In the human retina the same job is done by horizontal cells and amacrine cells, which spread signals sideways between photoreceptors, bipolar cells and ganglion cells. Lateral inhibition creates centre-surround receptive fields, first described in the cat by Stephen Kuffler (1953). It explains Mach bands (Ernst Mach, 1865), the Hermann grid illusion and simultaneous contrast, all of which exaggerate brightness differences at borders. Variant questions ask what lateral inhibition does (enhances edges and contrast) or which cells carry it. Option b is right because the extent of lateral inhibition depends on the intensity of light falling on the inhibiting cell.","traps":{"a":"Time difference between two lights describes temporal factors studied in apparent motion, such as the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), and in visual masking, where a second stimulus shortly after the first reduces its visibility. In Hartline's Limulus experiments the lights were presented together, and the variable that changed inhibition was the intensity on the neighbouring receptor. It tempts because two-light procedures appear in both kinds of experiment.","c":"Wavelength determines the hue we see and which cone type responds most, the basis of the Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory (Thomas Young, 1802) and of colour-opponent cells. In Hartline's work the strength of lateral inhibition depended on how much light reached the inhibiting receptor, not on its colour. It tempts because wavelength is a basic property of light and the option copies the 'inhibitory cell' wording.","d":"Frequency of a light wave is the same physical property as wavelength seen the other way (frequency equals the speed of light divided by wavelength), so it again codes colour, not intensity. Do not confuse it with firing frequency, the rate at which a neuron fires, which is how inhibition is measured in Limulus recordings. It tempts because 'frequency' appears in nerve recordings and the option mimics the correct wording."}},{"q":"Lateral inhibition ________","concept":"Lateral inhibition means that when a neuron is excited, it suppresses the firing of its neighbours through sideways connections. In the vertebrate retina these connections are made by horizontal cells and amacrine cells; in the horseshoe crab, Limulus, Haldan Keffer Hartline and Floyd Ratliff measured the effect directly in the 1950s. Its main effect is to exaggerate differences. Consider a border between a light area and a dark area. A receptor just inside the light side receives strong inhibition from its bright neighbours on one side but weak inhibition from its dark neighbours on the other, so it fires more than receptors deeper in the light area. A receptor just inside the dark side gets extra inhibition from its bright neighbours, so it fires less than receptors deeper in the dark. The result is a bright stripe and a dark stripe along the border, the Mach bands described by Ernst Mach (1865). The edge is sharpened, which helps us find the boundaries of objects. The same mechanism is used to explain simultaneous contrast (a grey square looks darker on a white background than on a black one) and the grey spots of the Hermann grid (Ludimar Hermann, 1870). At the level of receptive fields, lateral inhibition builds the centre-surround organisation of retinal ganglion cells described by Stephen Kuffler (1953). Overall brightness is handled by light adaptation and lightness constancy, and colour by cone types and opponent processes (Ewald Hering, 1878). Option a is right because lateral inhibition increases contrast at borders, so it enhances edge perception.","traps":{"b":"Dulling edge perception is the exact reverse of what lateral inhibition does. Edges blur with optical problems such as refractive error or cataract, or when contrast is low, not through lateral inhibition, which sharpens borders by exaggerating differences in firing. It tempts because the word 'inhibition' suggests weakening or reducing, so candidates assume the effect on perception must be a loss.","c":"Lowering overall brightness is not the function of lateral inhibition. Overall sensitivity to light level is controlled by light and dark adaptation (pupil size, bleaching and regeneration of photopigment), and perceived lightness stays fairly constant through lightness constancy. Lateral inhibition does reduce firing in some cells, but its job is to change relative contrast at borders. It tempts because inhibition does lower absolute firing.","d":"Colour perception depends on three cone types (trichromatic theory, Thomas Young, 1802) and on opponent processing (red-green, blue-yellow) proposed by Ewald Hering (1878). Opponent cells do use a form of inhibition, which is why the option sounds possible, but the textbook function of lateral inhibition in brightness vision is to enhance edges and contrast, not colour. It tempts because inhibition appears in both explanations."}},{"q":"Very threatening or anxiety provoking material is often unable to gain access to conscious awareness. This is called","concept":"Repression is the central defence mechanism of psychoanalysis. Sigmund Freud developed it from his work with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and later called it the cornerstone on which the whole structure of psychoanalysis rests. In repression, thoughts, memories, wishes or feelings that would cause intolerable anxiety, guilt or shame are kept out of conscious awareness automatically and unconsciously. The person does not choose to forget and does not know that anything has been pushed away. The repressed material stays active in the unconscious and returns in disguised form: in dreams, slips of the tongue and neurotic symptoms. Freud distinguished primary repression, which keeps some content from ever becoming conscious, from repression proper, which pushes back material that was once conscious. Anna Freud (1936) placed repression first among the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. The key exam contrast is suppression: a conscious, deliberate decision to put a thought out of mind, which George Vaillant (1977) ranked among the mature defences. Denial refuses to accept an external reality; dissociation splits off whole parts of experience. In cognitive psychology, Michael Anderson and Collin Green (2001) showed with the think/no-think paradigm that deliberately suppressing retrieval reduces later recall, a laboratory form of motivated forgetting. Whether traumatic memories are truly repressed and later recovered remains debated, as in the false memory work of Elizabeth Loftus (1993). Option b is right because the automatic, unconscious exclusion of threatening material from awareness is repression.","traps":{"a":"Suppression (spelt 'supression' in the option) is the conscious, voluntary effort to push a disturbing thought out of mind, for example deciding not to think about an exam result until evening. George Vaillant (1977) counted it among the mature defences because the person knows what is set aside and can return to it. The stem describes material that cannot reach awareness, which is unconscious. It tempts because both words mean keeping things out of mind.","c":"Inhibition is a general term: in memory research it covers retroactive and proactive interference and retrieval inhibition, as in the think/no-think studies of Michael Anderson and Collin Green (2001); in psychoanalysis Freud wrote of inhibitions as restrictions of ego functions (1926). It is not the specific defence that keeps threatening material out of consciousness. It tempts because it sounds like a neutral scientific synonym for repression.","d":"Motivated forgetting is the broad umbrella term for forgetting driven by a wish to avoid unpleasant memories. It includes conscious suppression and laboratory retrieval inhibition as well as repression. The stem names the specific Freudian mechanism, defined by the unconscious, automatic exclusion of anxiety-provoking material. It tempts because repression is often given as an example of motivated forgetting, so the broader category looks correct."}},{"q":"Icon and echo are part of","concept":"Sensory memory, also called the sensory register, is the first stage in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). It holds a brief, literal copy of incoming sensory input, large in capacity but very short-lived, so that attention can select what goes on to short-term memory. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual and auditory forms: iconic memory (the icon) and echoic memory (the echo). George Sperling (1960) demonstrated iconic memory. He flashed a grid of 12 letters for 50 milliseconds. With whole report, people recalled only about four letters. With partial report, a tone after the display told them which row to recall, and they could report most letters of any cued row, so nearly the whole display had been available. If the cue was delayed by about a second, the advantage vanished, showing that the icon lasts roughly a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts longer, about two to four seconds, as Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972) showed with an auditory 'three-eared man' version of partial report. The echo explains why we can catch what someone just said when we were not listening. Compare the later stages: short-term memory holds about seven plus or minus two items (George A. Miller, 1956) for around 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal; working memory (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974) adds active processing; long-term memory is large and durable. Option d is right because the icon and the echo are the visual and auditory stores of sensory memory.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory is the relatively permanent store of very large capacity, divided by Endel Tulving (1972) into episodic and semantic memory and later contrasted with procedural or implicit memory. Information reaches it from short-term memory through rehearsal and deeper encoding (levels of processing, Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart, 1972). Icons and echoes fade within seconds and never reach it in raw form. It tempts only as filler.","b":"Working memory is the model of active short-term processing by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), with a central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad, plus the episodic buffer added in 2000. The phonological loop and sketchpad sound like auditory and visual stores, which is why the option tempts. But they hold coded, rehearsable information for active work, not a raw sensory trace.","c":"Short-term memory is the second store in the model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), holding about seven plus or minus two items (George A. Miller, 1956) for up to about 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal. Only attended information enters it from the sensory register. The icon and echo come before this stage. It tempts because both stores are brief and candidates confuse 'very brief' with 'short-term'."}},{"q":"REM sleep is NOT characterized by","concept":"Sleep is measured by polysomnography: EEG for brain waves, EOG for eye movements and EMG for muscle tone. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep when they saw bursts of rapid, jerky eye movements in sleeping subjects, and William Dement and Kleitman (1957) showed that people woken from REM usually report vivid dreams. Sleep has two kinds. NREM sleep has three stages in current scoring: N1 (light sleep, theta waves), N2 (sleep spindles and K-complexes) and N3 (slow-wave sleep with high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves). The deeper NREM gets, the more synchronised the EEG becomes: large groups of cortical neurons fire in unison, producing big, slow waves. REM sleep is different. Its EEG is desynchronised: low-voltage, fast, mixed-frequency activity with sawtooth waves that looks almost like waking. The body, however, is paralysed: atonia of skeletal muscles is produced by circuits in the pons acting through the medulla, which is why we do not act out dreams; loss of this atonia causes REM sleep behaviour disorder. Other REM signs are irregular breathing and heart rate, genital arousal and poor temperature control. Because the brain looks awake while the body is limp, Michel Jouvet (1959) called it paradoxical sleep. Cycles last about 90 minutes, and REM periods grow longer towards morning, taking up about 20 to 25 percent of adult sleep. Option d is right because EEG synchrony marks slow-wave NREM sleep, whereas REM shows a desynchronised, wake-like EEG, so synchrony is the feature REM lacks.","traps":{"a":"Lack of muscle tone (atonia) is a true REM feature: brainstem circuits in the pons, acting through the medulla, inhibit spinal motor neurons so skeletal muscles go limp, sparing the eyes and diaphragm. Its loss causes REM sleep behaviour disorder, in which people act out dreams. Since the stem asks what REM is NOT, a true feature is wrong. It tempts because 'lack' sounds like an absent quality.","b":"Dreaming is strongly tied to REM: in the work of William Dement and Nathaniel Kleitman (1957), most people woken from REM reported vivid, story-like dreams. Dreams also occur in NREM, but they are usually shorter and more thought-like. Because REM is the classic dream state, this is a true characteristic. It tempts candidates who know NREM dreaming exists and over-apply that fact.","c":"Rapid eye movements give REM its name: Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) saw bursts of darting, conjugate eye movements under closed lids, recorded by electro-oculogram. They are the defining sign of this stage, so they cannot be what REM lacks. It tempts only candidates who miss the capitalised NOT in the stem and choose the most obvious feature."}},{"q":"The chemical that is responsible for feeling drowsy is","concept":"Sleepiness is explained by the two-process model of Alexander Borbely (1982). Process S is homeostatic sleep pressure: it builds the longer you stay awake and falls during sleep, especially slow-wave sleep. Process C is the circadian rhythm, set by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which decides when in the 24-hour day sleep should occur. Adenosine is the best known chemical marker of Process S. Active neurons and glia use ATP for energy, and adenosine is a by-product. During long waking it accumulates outside cells, especially in the basal forebrain. It acts on A1 receptors to inhibit wake-promoting cholinergic neurons and on A2A receptors to excite sleep-promoting neurons, such as those of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO). The result is a growing feeling of drowsiness. During sleep adenosine is cleared, so you wake refreshed. Caffeine is the classic proof: it blocks adenosine receptors, so the pressure signal is masked, and drowsiness returns once caffeine wears off. Do not confuse adenosine with melatonin, the pineal hormone that signals darkness and the timing of sleep (Process C), not accumulated tiredness. The wake systems include orexin from the lateral hypothalamus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus, noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, serotonin from the raphe nuclei and acetylcholine from the basal forebrain and brainstem. The VLPO switches them off, a mutual inhibition that Clifford Saper and colleagues (2001) called the sleep flip-flop switch. Option a is right because adenosine accumulates with time awake and directly produces sleep pressure, the feeling of drowsiness.","traps":{"b":"Acetylcholine, released by neurons in the basal forebrain and the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei of the brainstem, promotes cortical arousal. It is high in waking and in REM sleep, where it drives the wake-like EEG, and low in slow-wave sleep. It produces alertness, not drowsiness. It tempts because it is linked to REM sleep, so candidates associate it with sleep in general.","c":"Serotonin from the raphe nuclei is most active in waking, lower in NREM and nearly silent in REM sleep, so it is a 'REM-off' signal, not a drowsiness chemical. It is also the precursor of melatonin in the pineal gland. It tempts because Michel Jouvet's research in the 1960s proposed a serotonin theory of sleep, and the melatonin link sounds sleep-related, but later work showed serotonin mainly supports waking.","d":"Orexin (hypocretin), discovered in 1998 by two independent teams, is made by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus and stabilises wakefulness by exciting the other arousal systems. Loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy type 1, with sleep attacks and cataplexy. So orexin opposes drowsiness; suvorexant, an orexin-receptor blocker, is even used as a sleeping pill. It tempts because it is clearly a sleep-wake chemical."}},{"q":"Raphe nuclei produces which neurotransmitter","concept":"The raphe nuclei are a chain of cell groups along the midline (raphe means 'seam') of the brainstem, running through the medulla, pons and midbrain. They are the main source of serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) in the brain. Annica Dahlstrom and Kjell Fuxe (1964) mapped the brain's monoamine neurons with a fluorescence method and labelled the serotonin groups B1 to B9; most lie in the raphe. The rostral groups, the dorsal and median raphe, send axons to almost the whole forebrain, including cortex, hippocampus, amygdala and hypothalamus; the caudal groups project to the spinal cord, where they modulate pain and motor output. Serotonin is made from the amino acid tryptophan via 5-HTP and broken down to 5-HIAA, which can be measured in cerebrospinal fluid. Its functions include mood, impulse control and aggression, appetite, pain, and sleep: raphe neurons fire fast in waking, slowly in NREM and almost not at all in REM. SSRIs such as fluoxetine act by blocking serotonin reuptake. Examiners pair each nucleus with its transmitter, so learn the list: locus coeruleus, norepinephrine; substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area, dopamine; lateral hypothalamus, orexin; tuberomammillary nucleus, histamine; basal forebrain (nucleus basalis of Meynert), acetylcholine; pineal gland, melatonin. A common twist is the reverse question: which nucleus is the main serotonin source, or which transmitter falls when the raphe is lesioned. Option a is right because the raphe nuclei are the brain's principal serotonergic nuclei, producing serotonin and distributing it throughout the brain and spinal cord.","traps":{"b":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) in the brain comes mainly from the locus coeruleus, a small blue-tinged nucleus in the dorsal pons, labelled A6 by Annica Dahlstrom and Kjell Fuxe (1964). It drives vigilance, attention and the stress response. It is not made by the raphe. It tempts because the locus coeruleus also sits in the brainstem, close to the raphe, and both are monoamine arousal systems with wide projections.","c":"Orexin (hypocretin) is a neuropeptide made only by a small group of neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. It excites the raphe, locus coeruleus and other arousal centres to stabilise wakefulness, and its loss causes narcolepsy type 1. So orexin acts on the raphe but is not produced there. It tempts because orexin and serotonin appear together in sleep-wake chapters, and the raphe is one of orexin's targets.","d":"Brain histamine is made by the tuberomammillary nucleus in the posterior hypothalamus, which promotes wakefulness. That is why older antihistamines that cross the blood-brain barrier, such as diphenhydramine, cause drowsiness. It is not a raphe product. It tempts because histamine is another monoamine arousal transmitter with wide projections, listed in the same sleep-wake tables as serotonin from the raphe."}},{"q":"a pictorial display of a person's family relationships and medical history is ___________","concept":"A genogram is a structured, multigenerational diagram of a family that records both who is related to whom and what has happened to them: births, deaths, marriages, divorces, illnesses, psychiatric disorders, substance use, migrations and the quality of relationships. Its theoretical base is the family systems theory of Murray Bowen (1978), whose idea of multigenerational transmission holds that patterns such as anxiety, emotional cut-offs and triangles pass down the generations. The standard method was set out by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985). A genogram usually covers at least three generations. Squares stand for males, circles for females, an X marks a death, a horizontal line joins partners and vertical lines lead to children; the index person (identified patient) is drawn with a double outline. Special lines show close, conflictual, distant, cut-off or fused relationships. Clinicians use it in family therapy, psychiatric history-taking, genetic counselling and general practice, to spot repeating patterns and to engage the family. Neighbouring tools appear as variants. A pedigree chart is the medical genetics version, tracing how one inherited trait passes through a family, without relational detail. An ecomap, created by Ann Hartman (1978), shows the family's links with outside systems such as school, work, health services and friends. A sociogram, from Jacob L. Moreno (1934), maps likes and choices within a group, not a family. A family tree lists ancestry only. Option a is right because a genogram is the pictorial display that combines family relationships with medical and psychological history across generations.","traps":{"b":"A family tree is a genealogical chart of descent: who was born to whom across generations, often kept for heritage or inheritance. It records names, dates and lineage but not illnesses, emotional relationships or patterns of conflict and closeness. It tempts because a genogram looks like a family tree at first glance, and in everyday speech people call any family diagram a family tree.","c":"Family history is the part of a clinical case history that records illnesses, psychiatric disorders, suicides and substance use among relatives, usually written as text or a list. It is information, not a standardised pictorial display; a genogram is one way to draw it. It tempts because the stem mentions medical history, and 'family history' is exactly what a clinician collects before drawing the diagram.","d":"A gene tree is a phylogenetic diagram in evolutionary biology showing how copies of a particular gene diverged across species or populations over time. It concerns molecular ancestry, not a person's family relationships or clinical history. It tempts because 'gene' echoes the 'gen' in genogram and suggests medical genetics, so candidates guessing from the word root may pick it."}},{"q":"Low levels of which neurotransmitter leads to aggression","concept":"The best supported neurochemical finding on aggression is that low serotonin (5-HT) activity goes with impulsive, reactive aggression. The evidence started with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) levels of 5-HIAA, the main breakdown product of serotonin. Marie Asberg and colleagues (1976) found low CSF 5-HIAA in depressed patients who made violent suicide attempts. Gerald Brown and colleagues (1979) found that military men with a history of aggression had lower CSF 5-HIAA, and Markku Linnoila and colleagues (1983) showed the drop was strongest in impulsive violent offenders rather than those who planned their crimes. Animal work agrees: monkeys with low serotonin are more aggressive and take more risks. The usual explanation is that serotonin, acting on the prefrontal cortex, supports impulse control, so low levels weaken the brakes on anger. This is why SSRIs are sometimes used for impulsive aggression and irritability. Keep the neighbours apart. Aggression is linked to high, not low, dopamine activity, and dopamine-blocking antipsychotics reduce it. Norepinephrine and epinephrine rise in hot, affective aggression, as part of the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). Testosterone is linked to dominance, and a low-activity MAOA gene interacts with childhood maltreatment to predict antisocial behaviour, as Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2002) showed. Separate impulsive (reactive) from premeditated (instrumental) aggression: the serotonin link holds mainly for the impulsive type. Option a is right because low serotonin is the neurotransmitter deficit most consistently linked to aggression, especially impulsive aggression, in human and animal research.","traps":{"b":"Dopamine, from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area, drives reward, motivation and movement. Where it is linked to aggression, the link runs the other way: increased dopamine activity, as with amphetamine or in mania and psychosis, raises aggression, and dopamine-blocking antipsychotics calm it. Low dopamine is linked to Parkinson's disease and anhedonia, not aggression. It tempts because dopamine is the best known 'behaviour' transmitter.","c":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla. It prepares the body for fight or flight, as described by Walter Cannon (1915), raising heart rate, blood pressure and blood glucose during anger and fear. Aggression goes with surges, not low levels, of epinephrine. It tempts because anger feels like an 'adrenaline rush', so candidates connect adrenaline with aggression without checking the direction.","d":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline), from the locus coeruleus and sympathetic nerves, raises arousal, vigilance and readiness for action. Affective aggression is associated with heightened, not low, noradrenergic activity, and beta-blockers such as propranolol are sometimes used to reduce aggression after brain injury. It tempts because it is a monoamine studied alongside serotonin in aggression research, so the candidate must check the direction of the link."}},{"q":"people with blindness caused by damage to the visual cortex can recognize facial expressions of emotion even though they have no conscious awareness of looking at a person’s face. This is called ________________","concept":"Blindsight is the ability of people with damage to the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex) to respond accurately to stimuli in the blind part of their visual field while denying that they see anything. Ernst Poppel, Richard Held and Douglas Frost (1973) first showed residual eye movements to unseen targets, and Lawrence Weiskrantz and colleagues (1974) named the effect after testing patient DB, who could point to and discriminate stimuli he insisted he could not see. Affective blindsight extends this to emotion. Beatrice de Gelder, Weiskrantz and colleagues (1999) showed that patient GY, blind in one half-field after V1 damage, could guess the emotional expression of faces shown to his blind field well above chance. Imaging suggests a subcortical route that bypasses V1: retina to superior colliculus to pulvinar of the thalamus to the amygdala, which responds to fearful faces even without awareness. This fits the 'low road' for fear described by Joseph LeDoux (1996). Separate it from neighbouring disorders. Hemispatial neglect, usually after right parietal damage, is a failure of attention to the left side of space, not blindness; yet neglected items can still be processed implicitly, as in the 'burning house' study of John Marshall and Peter Halligan (1988). Prosopagnosia is a failure to recognise familiar faces after fusiform damage, sometimes with covert recognition shown by skin conductance. Anton syndrome is the reverse of blindsight: cortically blind patients who deny their blindness. Option a is right because the stem describes non-conscious recognition of facial emotion after visual cortex damage, which is exactly affective blindsight.","traps":{"b":"'Affect neglect' is not an established neuropsychological term. It borrows the word neglect from hemispatial neglect, a disorder of attention after right parietal damage in which patients ignore the left side of space even though their vision is intact. In the stem the problem is cortical blindness, not inattention, and the preserved ability is emotion recognition. It tempts because neglect patients also process unseen information implicitly.","c":"Emotional neglect is a form of child maltreatment: caregivers persistently fail to meet a child's needs for warmth, attention and comfort. It is measured, for example, by a subscale of the Childhood Trauma Questionnaire and is a risk factor for later depression and attachment problems. It has nothing to do with brain damage or vision. It tempts because it pairs 'emotional' with a clinical word, matching the emotion theme of the stem.","d":"'Motivational blindsight' is an invented label. Blindsight itself was named by Lawrence Weiskrantz and colleagues (1974), and the version for facial emotion is affective blindsight; nothing in the stem concerns motivation, drives or goals. It tempts because it keeps the correct key word 'blindsight', so a candidate who remembers only that part may pick it. The emotional content of the faces points to 'affective'."}},{"q":"The smallest bone in the body is","concept":"The middle ear holds the three smallest bones of the body, the auditory ossicles, linked in a chain from the eardrum to the inner ear. The malleus (hammer) is attached to the tympanic membrane; the incus (anvil) sits in the middle; and the stapes (stirrup) has a footplate that fits into the oval window of the cochlea. The stapes is the smallest bone in the human body, about 3 millimetres long. The ossicles solve an impedance problem: sound travels in air but the cochlea is filled with fluid, so most sound energy would otherwise be reflected. The chain concentrates force from the large eardrum onto the small oval window and adds a small lever effect, raising pressure roughly twenty times. Two tiny muscles damp the chain in the acoustic reflex: the stapedius, the smallest skeletal muscle, supplied by the facial nerve, and the tensor tympani, supplied by the trigeminal nerve. Clinical links: otosclerosis fixes the stapes in the oval window and causes conductive hearing loss, treated by stapedectomy. Conductive loss arises in the outer or middle ear; sensorineural loss in the cochlea or auditory nerve. In the inner ear, hair cells on the basilar membrane turn vibration into nerve impulses. Know the other extreme too: the femur is the longest and strongest bone. Spelling matters in the distractor: carpals are wrist bones, while a carpel is part of a flower. Option c is right because the stapes, the stirrup-shaped ossicle that presses on the oval window, is the smallest bone in the human body.","traps":{"a":"The malleus (hammer) is the first and largest of the three ossicles. Its handle is attached to the inner surface of the eardrum, so it picks up the drum's vibrations and passes them to the incus; the tensor tympani muscle pulls on it. It is small but clearly larger than the stapes. It tempts because candidates remember that an ear ossicle is the smallest bone but not which one.","b":"The incus (anvil) is the middle ossicle, linking the malleus to the stapes through tiny joints. It is larger than the stapes, though smaller than the malleus. It tempts because candidates who know the answer is an ossicle often guess the middle one, and because many remember the order hammer, anvil, stirrup without recalling that size falls from the first bone to the last.","d":"Carpal bones are the eight small bones of the wrist (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform, trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate); the pea-shaped pisiform is the smallest of them, still far larger than the stapes. 'Carpel', as spelled here, is actually the female part of a flower. It tempts because the carpals are famous as small bones, and the misspelling hides the error."}},{"q":"The _______ is rich in _________ and is responsible for colour and detailed vision","concept":"The retina has two kinds of photoreceptor, the basis of the duplex theory of vision. Rods, about 120 million in each eye, contain rhodopsin and are extremely sensitive to light, so they serve night (scotopic) vision. Many rods converge onto one ganglion cell, which pools weak signals but blurs detail, and rods do not signal colour. Cones, about 6 million, work in bright (photopic) light. There are three types, sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths, and comparing their outputs gives colour vision. The fovea is a small pit at the centre of the macula, on the visual axis. Its centre is packed with cones and contains no rods, and the inner retinal layers are pushed aside so light reaches the cones directly. In the fovea each cone connects almost one-to-one with its own ganglion cell, so acuity is highest there; we move our eyes to put objects of interest on the fovea. Away from the fovea rods dominate, which is why a faint star is easier to see slightly off-centre. The blind spot (optic disc) is where ganglion cell axons leave the eye as the optic nerve; it has no rods or cones at all, and the brain fills in the gap. Exam variants include dark adaptation (cones adapt within minutes, rods take about 30 minutes), the shift of peak sensitivity towards blue at dusk described by Jan Evangelista Purkinje (1825), and macular degeneration, which destroys central detailed vision. Option c is right because the fovea is rich in cones, and cones provide colour and fine detailed vision.","traps":{"a":"The blind spot (optic disc) is where ganglion cell axons gather and leave the eye as the optic nerve, about 15 degrees to the nasal side of the fovea on the retina. It has no photoreceptors at all, neither cones nor rods, so it gives no vision, let alone colour or detail. The second half, cones, is correct. It tempts because candidates who know cones are right may grab the first option containing them.","b":"The fovea is the right place but the wrong cell. Rods, rich in rhodopsin, serve dim-light vision; they are colour-blind and, because many converge on one ganglion cell, give poor acuity. They are absent from the centre of the fovea and most dense about 20 degrees out in the periphery. It tempts because rods are the more numerous photoreceptor, so 'rich in rods' sounds plausible for any retinal area.","d":"Both halves are wrong. The blind spot contains no photoreceptors, so it cannot be rich in rods, and rods would not give colour or detail anyway, since they serve sensitive but low-acuity, colourless night vision. It tempts only by guesswork, because both words are familiar retina terms and a candidate unsure of the structure may pair them at random."}},{"q":"Which of the following follows the all or none law","concept":"The all-or-none law states that a response either occurs at full strength or does not occur at all; a stronger stimulus does not make it bigger. Henry Pickering Bowditch (1871) first described it for heart muscle, Keith Lucas (1909) showed it for skeletal muscle fibres, and Edgar Adrian (1914) showed it for nerve. The ionic basis was worked out by Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) on the squid giant axon. A neuron at rest sits at about minus 70 millivolts, held by the sodium-potassium pump and potassium leak channels. Inputs at the dendrites produce graded potentials: excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) depolarise, inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs) hyperpolarise, and their size varies with input strength. They spread passively, fade with distance and summate over space and time. If the summed input depolarises the axon hillock to threshold, about minus 55 millivolts, voltage-gated sodium channels open in a positive feedback loop and an action potential fires, peaking near plus 30 to 40 millivolts; potassium outflow then repolarises the membrane, often with a brief hyperpolarisation. Each spike has the same size and travels along the axon without decrement. Stimulus intensity is coded instead by firing rate and by the number of neurons recruited. The absolute refractory period blocks a second spike, while during the relative refractory period only a stronger stimulus can trigger one. Myelin speeds conduction by saltatory jumps between nodes of Ranvier. Option a is right because action potentials fire at full, fixed amplitude once threshold is reached and not at all below it, which is the all-or-none law.","traps":{"b":"An excitatory postsynaptic potential is a small, local depolarisation produced when a transmitter such as glutamate opens cation channels on the postsynaptic membrane. It is graded: more transmitter gives a bigger EPSP, and EPSPs summate in space and time. Only if their sum reaches threshold at the axon hillock does an all-or-none spike follow. It tempts because it is excitatory and can trigger an action potential.","c":"An inhibitory postsynaptic potential is a small, local hyperpolarisation, usually caused by GABA or glycine opening chloride or potassium channels. It moves the membrane away from threshold, making firing less likely. Its size is graded by the amount of transmitter, and it summates with EPSPs, so it does not obey the all-or-none law. It tempts because it is a named potential in the same chapter as the action potential.","d":"The resting membrane potential is the steady voltage of about minus 70 millivolts across an inactive neuron's membrane, kept by the sodium-potassium pump, selective potassium leak and trapped negative proteins inside. It is a baseline state, not a signal, so it has no 'all' or 'none'. It tempts because it is a fixed, stable value, and a fixed size is easily mistaken for the all-or-none property of spikes."}},{"q":"Schwan cells are found in","concept":"Glial cells support neurons, and two kinds make myelin, the fatty insulating sheath that lets impulses jump between gaps called nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction). Which cell does it depends on location. In the peripheral nervous system (PNS), meaning the cranial and spinal nerves and ganglia outside the brain and spinal cord, myelin is made by Schwann cells, named after Theodor Schwann (1839), who described them in his work on cell theory. Each myelinating Schwann cell wraps one segment of a single axon, while non-myelinating Schwann cells enclose bundles of thin unmyelinated axons. In the central nervous system (CNS), meaning the brain and spinal cord, myelin is made by oligodendrocytes, and one oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments of many different axons. Other glia complete the picture: in the CNS, astrocytes (blood-brain barrier, ion and transmitter balance), microglia (immune defence) and ependymal cells (lining the ventricles); in the PNS, satellite cells around neuron bodies in ganglia. Schwann cells guide the regrowth of cut peripheral axons, which is why peripheral nerves can regenerate while CNS damage usually does not. Disease contrasts are favourite questions: multiple sclerosis attacks CNS myelin, while Guillain-Barre syndrome and Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease affect peripheral nerves; a vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) is a benign tumour of Schwann cells on the eighth cranial nerve. One trap: the optic nerve is called a nerve but is CNS tissue, myelinated by oligodendrocytes. Option a is right because Schwann cells are found in the peripheral nerves outside the skull and spine, where they form the myelin sheath.","traps":{"b":"The brain is central nervous system tissue. Its axons are myelinated by oligodendrocytes, each of which sends processes to wrap segments of many axons, and its white matter owes its colour to that oligodendrocyte myelin. Schwann cells normally do not occur there. It tempts because myelin is discussed mainly in relation to brain white matter and diseases such as multiple sclerosis, so candidates link all myelin with the brain.","c":"The spinal cord is also part of the central nervous system, so its tracts are myelinated by oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells. The boundary lies where the nerve roots leave the cord: beyond that transition zone, the roots and spinal nerves are peripheral and do contain Schwann cells. It tempts because spinal nerves and the spinal cord sound alike, and candidates forget that the cord itself is CNS.","d":"The basal ganglia (caudate nucleus, putamen and globus pallidus, functionally linked with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra) are grey-matter nuclei deep in the brain, involved in movement and habit, and damaged in Parkinson's and Huntington's diseases. They lie inside the central nervous system, so any myelin there comes from oligodendrocytes. It tempts only as a specific-sounding brain structure for candidates unsure of the CNS and PNS split."}},{"q":"Group of cell bodies in the PNS is called_______","concept":"Neuroanatomy uses different words for the same kind of structure depending on whether it lies in the central nervous system (CNS: brain and spinal cord) or the peripheral nervous system (PNS: everything outside them, including cranial and spinal nerves and the autonomic system). Two kinds of structure matter. The first is a cluster of neuron cell bodies, which looks grey because cell bodies are unmyelinated. In the CNS such a cluster is a nucleus, for example the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus or the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. In the PNS it is a ganglion (plural ganglia), for example the dorsal root ganglia that hold the cell bodies of sensory neurons, or the sympathetic chain ganglia beside the spinal cord. The second kind is a bundle of axons, which looks white because of myelin. In the CNS it is a tract (the corticospinal tract, the optic tract). In the PNS it is a nerve (the sciatic nerve, the vagus nerve). A useful grid: CNS has nuclei and tracts, PNS has ganglia and nerves. One exception catches students: the basal ganglia are clusters of cell bodies deep inside the cerebrum, so strictly they are CNS nuclei, and many anatomists prefer the term basal nuclei; the old name survives by tradition. Examiners build variants by asking for the CNS equivalent (nucleus), the PNS axon bundle (nerve) or the CNS axon bundle (tract). Option b is right because a group of neuronal cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system is called a ganglion, so the plural answer is ganglia.","traps":{"a":"Nuclei (singular nucleus) are indeed clusters of neuronal cell bodies, but the term is reserved for the central nervous system, as in the hypothalamic or thalamic nuclei. The stem specifies the PNS, where the same structure is called a ganglion. It tempts because it describes the right kind of structure in the wrong location, and the misnamed basal ganglia blur the rule further.","c":"Tracts are bundles of myelinated axons running inside the central nervous system, such as the corticospinal or optic tract, and they form white matter. They contain no cell bodies and they are not in the PNS, so they fail the stem twice. They tempt because tract, nerve, nucleus and ganglion are taught together as one confusing set of terms.","d":"A nerve is a bundle of axons in the peripheral nervous system, such as the sciatic or vagus nerve, wrapped in connective tissue. It is the right location but the wrong component: nerves carry fibres, while the cell bodies of those fibres sit in ganglia or in the CNS. It tempts because 'nerve' is the most familiar PNS word for students."}},{"q":"Corpus callosum is a part of","concept":"The brain develops from the neural tube. Early in embryonic life its front end swells into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), the mesencephalon (midbrain) and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain). These then divide into five secondary vesicles, each with fixed adult derivatives that examiners love to test. The telencephalon becomes the two cerebral hemispheres: the cerebral cortex, the basal ganglia, the hippocampus and amygdala, the olfactory bulbs, the lateral ventricles and the white matter commissures that join the hemispheres, chiefly the corpus callosum and the anterior commissure. The diencephalon becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus (including the pineal gland) and subthalamus, around the third ventricle. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain: tectum (superior and inferior colliculi), tegmentum, substantia nigra and cerebral aqueduct. The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and the myelencephalon (medulla oblongata). The corpus callosum is the largest commissure in the brain, a broad band of roughly 200 million axons that lets the hemispheres share information. Its role became famous through the split-brain studies of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s, for which Sperry shared the 1981 Nobel Prize: when the callosum is cut to control epilepsy, information shown only to the right hemisphere cannot be named by the left-hemisphere language system. A quick rule: cerebrum means telencephalon, the 'thalamus' family means diencephalon. Option b is right because the corpus callosum connects the two cerebral hemispheres and develops with them from the telencephalon, the front part of the forebrain.","traps":{"a":"The diencephalon, the rear part of the forebrain, gives rise to the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus with the pineal gland, and subthalamus, arranged around the third ventricle. The corpus callosum arches directly above the thalamus, so they look like neighbours on a midline section, but it belongs to the hemispheres, not the diencephalon. It tempts because both are forebrain divisions lying close together.","c":"The mesencephalon is the midbrain. It contains the tectum (superior colliculi for visual reflexes, inferior colliculi for auditory relay), the tegmentum, the substantia nigra whose dopamine neurons degenerate in Parkinson's disease, and the cerebral aqueduct. It has no commissure joining the cerebral hemispheres. It tempts only because its name sounds similar to the other 'encephalon' options.","d":"The metencephalon is the front part of the hindbrain and develops into the pons and the cerebellum. The pons does carry large crossing fibre bundles, but these link the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum, not one hemisphere to the other. It tempts because crossing white matter in the pons can be confused with the callosum's role as the main bridge between hemispheres."}},{"q":"Which of the following is responsible for coordinating movement","concept":"Movement is produced by a team of structures, and exams test who does what. The primary motor cortex, in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe (Brodmann area 4), issues the command for voluntary movement; it is mapped as the motor homunculus described by Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950), and each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. The basal ganglia help select and initiate movements and suppress unwanted ones; damage gives Parkinson's disease (too little movement) or Huntington's disease (too much). The cerebellum, at the back of the brain under the occipital lobes, is the coordinator. It receives a copy of the motor command and continuous feedback from muscles, joints and the vestibular system, compares intended with actual movement, and corrects errors in timing, force, direction and balance. It is also central to motor learning, such as learning to ride a bicycle. It controls the same side of the body, so a right cerebellar lesion affects the right limbs. Cerebellar damage does not paralyse; it produces ataxia: a wide-based staggering gait, intention tremor, dysmetria (overshooting a target in the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia and nystagmus. Alcohol intoxication mimics this, which is why roadside sobriety tests use cerebellar tasks. Jeremy Schmahmann and Janet Sherman (1998) described the cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome, showing roles in planning, language and emotion too. Option a is right because the cerebellum is the structure that coordinates and smooths movement, integrating feedback to keep actions accurate and balanced.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus, Brodmann area 4) sends the commands that start voluntary movement down the corticospinal tract to the opposite side of the body. Damage causes weakness or paralysis, not clumsiness. It initiates movement, while the cerebellum coordinates it. It tempts because the word 'motor' makes it sound like the answer to any movement question.","c":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem and holds the cardiovascular and respiratory centres, plus reflexes such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting. It is also where most motor fibres cross over (the pyramidal decussation). It does not coordinate skilled movement. It tempts because it is a hindbrain structure next to the cerebellum and appears in the same brainstem list.","d":"The pons, meaning bridge, links the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum through the pontine nuclei and middle cerebellar peduncles, and contributes to breathing and REM sleep. It passes motor information to the cerebellum but does not do the coordinating itself. It tempts because it develops from the same metencephalon as the cerebellum and is directly wired to it."}},{"q":"Which is called the master gland","concept":"The endocrine system is a set of glands that release hormones into the blood. The pituitary gland (hypophysis) is a pea-sized gland that hangs from the base of the brain by a stalk (the infundibulum) and sits in a bony pocket called the sella turcica. It is classically called the master gland because many of its hormones are tropic: they control other endocrine glands. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) secretes thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH, acting on the thyroid), adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH, acting on the adrenal cortex), follicle-stimulating and luteinizing hormones (FSH and LH, acting on the gonads), plus growth hormone and prolactin. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) releases oxytocin and vasopressin. The subtlety examiners use is that the pituitary is itself controlled by the hypothalamus, which sends releasing hormones such as CRH and TRH through a portal blood system and sends nerve fibres directly into the posterior lobe. For that reason some books call the hypothalamus the 'master of the master gland'. Together they form axes such as the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, central to the stress response described by Hans Selye (1936). Keep the other options separate: the thalamus is a sensory relay, not a gland, and the pineal gland secretes melatonin for circadian rhythm. If a question asks which structure controls the pituitary, answer hypothalamus; if it asks for the master gland, answer pituitary. Option a is right because the pituitary is the conventional textbook master gland, since its tropic hormones regulate the thyroid, adrenals and gonads.","traps":{"b":"The hypothalamus is a small region of the diencephalon that regulates hunger, thirst, temperature and sexual behaviour, and controls the pituitary through releasing hormones and direct nerve fibres. That is why some texts call it the 'master of the master gland'. It is neural tissue and not the gland conventionally given the title. It tempts strongly because it truly sits above the pituitary in the chain of command.","c":"The thalamus is a paired relay station in the diencephalon that passes almost all sensory information except smell on to the cortex, for example vision via the lateral geniculate nucleus and hearing via the medial geniculate nucleus. It is not an endocrine gland and secretes no hormones. It tempts mainly because its name and location sound similar to the hypothalamus.","d":"The pineal gland, in the epithalamus, secretes melatonin in darkness and helps regulate the sleep-wake cycle and seasonal rhythms. It is a true endocrine gland but controls no other glands, so it cannot be the master gland. René Descartes (1649) famously called it the principal seat of the soul. It tempts because it is a well-known gland located inside the brain."}},{"q":"The posterior pituitary produces","concept":"The pituitary has two lobes with completely different origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is true glandular tissue that manufactures its own hormones under hypothalamic control. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is really an extension of the brain: it contains the axon terminals of large neurosecretory cells whose cell bodies lie in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. These neurons synthesise two small peptide hormones, transport them down their axons and store them in the posterior lobe, which releases them into the blood when the neurons fire. So, strictly, the posterior pituitary stores and releases rather than produces, although exam stems still say 'produces' or 'secretes'. The two hormones are oxytocin and vasopressin. Oxytocin causes uterine contractions during labour and milk ejection during breastfeeding, and in social neuroscience it is linked to bonding and trust. Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (ADH), makes the kidneys reabsorb water and, at high doses, constricts blood vessels; lack of it causes diabetes insipidus, and alcohol suppresses it, which is why drinking increases urination. Compare the anterior lobe's list (GH, prolactin, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH) so that variants asking which hormone is NOT posterior are easy. Distractor chemicals include orexin (hypocretin), a lateral hypothalamic peptide for wakefulness and appetite, and norepinephrine, a catecholamine from the locus coeruleus, sympathetic nerves and adrenal medulla. Option a is right because oxytocin and vasopressin are the only two hormones released by the posterior pituitary, even though the hypothalamus actually synthesises them.","traps":{"b":"Oxytocin is correct, but orexin (also called hypocretin) is a neuropeptide made by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. It promotes wakefulness and food seeking, and loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy with cataplexy. It acts within the brain and is not stored in or released by the posterior pituitary. It tempts because it pairs a right hormone with another hypothalamic peptide.","c":"Vasopressin is correct, but 'vasodilators' is not a hormone at all; it is a general label for any substance that widens blood vessels, such as nitric oxide. Vasopressin actually does the opposite, constricting vessels at high doses, which is where its name comes from. It tempts because the partner word sounds like vasopressin's family and half the pair is right.","d":"Vasopressin is correct, but norepinephrine is a catecholamine released by sympathetic nerve endings, by the adrenal medulla as a hormone, and by locus coeruleus neurons as a brain neurotransmitter. It has nothing to do with the posterior pituitary. It tempts because norepinephrine, like vasopressin, raises blood pressure, so the pair feels physiologically consistent."}},{"q":"Which of the following are directly involved in triggering eating","concept":"Eating is controlled by a balance between orexigenic signals, which start hunger and feeding, and anorexigenic signals, which produce satiety and stop it. Early research led to the dual-centre hypothesis of Eliot Stellar (1954): lesions of the lateral hypothalamus made rats stop eating (a hunger centre), while lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus made them overeat and become obese (a satiety centre). The modern picture centres on the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, which holds two opposing neuron groups. One group makes neuropeptide Y (NPY) and agouti-related peptide (AgRP); injecting NPY into the hypothalamus makes even well-fed animals eat heavily, so NPY is one of the most powerful feeding triggers known. The other group makes pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) and cocaine- and amphetamine-regulated transcript (CART), which suppress feeding. Body signals tune these groups. Ghrelin from the empty stomach is the main peripheral hunger hormone and excites NPY neurons. Leptin, released by fat cells in proportion to fat stores and identified by Jeffrey Friedman's laboratory (1994), inhibits NPY and stimulates POMC and CART, signalling long-term satiety; mice lacking leptin are massively obese. Cholecystokinin (CCK), released by the duodenum when fat and protein arrive, signals short-term fullness through the vagus nerve. A tidy exam list: triggers are NPY, AgRP, ghrelin and orexin; stoppers are leptin, CCK, CART, POMC, peptide YY and insulin. Option d is right because neuropeptide Y is the only listed signal that directly stimulates feeding; the other three are satiety signals.","traps":{"a":"Leptin is a hormone secreted by adipose (fat) cells in proportion to fat stores. It acts on the arcuate nucleus to inhibit NPY neurons and activate POMC and CART neurons, so it reduces appetite and signals long-term energy sufficiency. It suppresses rather than triggers eating. It tempts because it is the most famous appetite hormone, and students mix it up with ghrelin, the hunger hormone.","b":"CART, cocaine- and amphetamine-regulated transcript, is a peptide made alongside POMC in arcuate neurons. It is anorexigenic: it reduces food intake, which fits the appetite loss produced by stimulant drugs. It is activated by leptin and opposes NPY. It tempts because its name is unfamiliar, so a candidate who knows only that it is a hypothalamic feeding peptide may guess it promotes eating.","c":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when fats and proteins arrive after a meal. It slows stomach emptying, triggers bile and enzyme release, and signals fullness to the brainstem through the vagus nerve, ending the meal. It is a short-term satiety signal, so it stops eating. It tempts because it is directly involved in feeding control, just in the opposite direction."}},{"q":"What new method did Wechsler invent for obtaining the IQ score in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"Intelligence scores have been expressed in two ways. The first was the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision introduced mental age: the age level whose typical items a child can pass. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied by 100 in the Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100. A 10-year-old with a mental age of 12 scores 120. The ratio IQ breaks down for adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps increasing, so an unchanged adult would appear to decline every year. Its spread also varied across ages, so the same IQ meant different things at different ages. David Wechsler solved this in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale (1939) with the deviation IQ, which this item calls Standard IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the scores of people of the same age in the norm sample and converted to a standard score with a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. So 115 is one standard deviation above the mean (about the 84th percentile) and 130 is two (about the 98th). Wechsler also split the test into verbal and performance scales. The Stanford-Binet adopted the deviation IQ in 1960, and all modern scales such as the WAIS and WISC use it. Option a is right because Wechsler's innovation was the deviation, or standard score, IQ, which compares a person with same-age peers instead of using the mental age ratio.","traps":{"b":"An 'age-relative formula' IQ describes the older ratio approach, in which a score is computed by dividing mental age by chronological age. That formula was proposed by William Stern (1912) and used by Lewis Terman (1916) in the Stanford-Binet. Wechsler replaced it precisely because it fails for adults. It tempts because the deviation IQ is also age based, since it uses same-age norms.","c":"Mental age IQ is the ratio IQ: mental age, introduced in the 1908 Binet-Simon scale, divided by chronological age and multiplied by 100. It was the standard method before Wechsler and the very method he abandoned, because mental age plateaus in adolescence. It tempts because 'mental age' is the best-known historical term in intelligence testing and appears in every textbook account.","d":"'Physical age IQ' is not a recognised psychometric method. The nearest real term is chronological age, the denominator of the old ratio IQ, but nobody computes an IQ from physical age alone. It is a filler distractor. It tempts candidates who vaguely remember that some kind of age entered the old formula and cannot recall the exact term."}},{"q":"Which test can diagnose if an examinee consistently makes more errors on one side of the body than the other?","concept":"Neuropsychologists use lateralised testing to locate brain damage. The key principle is crossed control: touch, pain and position sense travel up pathways that cross the midline, so the left side of the body is represented in the right parietal lobe (the postcentral gyrus, primary somatosensory cortex) and vice versa. If a patient consistently makes more errors on one side of the body, the opposite hemisphere is suspected. The Finger Localization Test of Arthur Benton and colleagues (1983) applies this logic. The examiner touches one or two of the patient's fingers, with the hand first visible and then hidden, and the patient names or points to the fingers touched. Each hand is scored separately. Errors confined to one hand suggest a lesion in the opposite hemisphere, while bilateral finger agnosia is classically linked to dominant parietal damage and forms part of Gerstmann syndrome (finger agnosia, left-right confusion, agraphia and acalculia), described by Josef Gerstmann (1930). Similar side-by-side comparisons appear in the sensory-perceptual examination of the Halstead-Reitan Battery. Contrast this with attention tests, which measure a capacity rather than a side. The Continuous Performance Test of Haldor Rosvold and colleagues (1956) measures sustained attention and impulsivity, and the Test of Everyday Attention of Ian Robertson and colleagues (1994) measures selective, sustained and switching attention through everyday tasks. The attention disorder that is lateralised is unilateral neglect, assessed with cancellation and line bisection tasks. Option a is right because the Finger Localization Test scores each hand separately, so a consistent excess of errors on one side reveals lateralised sensory dysfunction.","traps":{"b":"The Continuous Performance Test, introduced by Haldor Rosvold and colleagues (1956), presents a long stream of stimuli and asks the person to respond only to a target. Omission errors index inattention and commission errors index impulsivity, which is why it is widely used in ADHD assessment. It measures vigilance over time, not differences between the two sides of the body. It tempts because it also counts errors.","c":"A sustained attention test measures the ability to keep focus on a dull task over a long period, as in the Mackworth Clock of Norman Mackworth (1948). Performance declines with time on task, called the vigilance decrement. Such tests give an overall attention score, not a left versus right comparison. It tempts because the stem's 'consistently makes more errors' sounds like a performance-over-time measure.","d":"The Test of Everyday Attention, by Ian Robertson and colleagues (1994), uses realistic tasks such as searching a map, searching a telephone directory and counting lift (elevator) tones to assess selective attention, sustained attention and attentional switching. It is ecologically valid but not designed to compare body sides. It tempts because it is a well-known named neuropsychological test and sounds comprehensive enough to detect anything."}},{"q":"What is NOT an example of executive function?","concept":"Executive functions are the higher-order control processes that manage other cognitive abilities when behaviour must be goal-directed, flexible and new rather than routine. They depend mainly on the prefrontal cortex, especially the dorsolateral region, and its loops with the basal ganglia and thalamus. Muriel Lezak (1982) grouped them into volition, planning, purposive action and effective performance, while Akira Miyake and colleagues (2000) identified three separable but related core functions: inhibition, shifting (cognitive flexibility) and updating of working memory. Higher abilities such as planning, abstract reasoning, concept formation and problem solving are built from these. Classic tests make the idea concrete: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test of David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) for concept formation and set shifting, the Tower of London of Tim Shallice (1982) for planning, the Stroop test of J. Ridley Stroop (1935) for inhibition, Trail Making Part B for switching, and verbal fluency for strategic retrieval. Damage produces the dysexecutive syndrome, famously seen in Phineas Gage after his 1848 accident: impulsivity, poor planning, concrete thinking and perseveration, with basic perception and memory often intact. That last point is the key to this item. Basic sensory and perceptual processes, such as hearing and recognising sounds, are lower-level, modality-specific functions handled by posterior and temporal cortex, for example the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus. They supply the information that executive processes act on, but they are not executive themselves. Option d is right because auditory processing is a basic perceptual function of the temporal lobe, while conceptualization, planning and logical analysis are all executive functions.","traps":{"a":"Conceptualization, the ability to form abstract concepts and see the rule behind examples, is a core executive ability. It is measured by the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, where the person must discover the sorting rule, and by similarities tasks. Frontal patients show concrete thinking here. It is not the exception the stem asks for. It tempts because it sounds like general thinking rather than control.","b":"Planning, which means formulating a sequence of steps toward a goal and holding it in mind, is the textbook example of an executive function. The Tower of London task of Tim Shallice (1982) was designed to measure it, and frontal lobe damage impairs it. It belongs inside the category, not outside. It tempts only through careless reading of the word NOT in the stem.","c":"Logical analysis, meaning reasoning, evaluating information and drawing valid inferences, is a higher-order process that relies on executive control and working memory in the prefrontal cortex. Neuropsychologists list it with abstraction and judgement among executive abilities. So it is an example, not the exception. It tempts because some students think of logic as general intelligence rather than an executive function."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the STAI?","concept":"Anxiety can be understood in two senses. State anxiety is a temporary emotional condition: tension, apprehension and worry with autonomic arousal, felt right now and changing with the situation, such as just before an exam. Trait anxiety is a relatively stable personality disposition: a general tendency to perceive situations as threatening and to respond with state anxiety more often and more strongly. Charles Spielberger built his state-trait theory of anxiety on this distinction and, with Richard Gorsuch and Robert Lushene (1970), published the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI); the revised Form Y appeared in 1983. It has 40 self-report items in two 20-item scales. The state scale asks how the person feels right now, at this moment, and the trait scale asks how they generally feel. Each item is rated on a four-point scale, so each scale ranges from 20 to 80. Because the state scale is sensitive to change, it is used to measure the effect of stress, therapy or relaxation, while the trait scale identifies anxiety-prone people. Examiners set the STAI against other inventories by purpose. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. Validity scales, such as the L, F and K scales of the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), check how honestly someone answered. Broad inventories measure normal or abnormal personality as a whole. Option c is right because the STAI was designed specifically to measure and separate state anxiety from trait anxiety.","traps":{"a":"Measuring both normal and abnormal personality describes broad multiscale inventories, for example combining a normal-range test such as the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell (1949) with a clinical test such as the MMPI. The STAI measures one construct, anxiety, in two forms. It tempts because trait anxiety is a personality variable, so the STAI can look like a personality test.","b":"Assessing the validity of responses is the job of validity scales, which detect lying, random answering, faking bad or defensiveness. The classic examples are the L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction) scales of the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). The STAI has no such purpose. It tempts because many self-report inventories include some check on response validity.","d":"Psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism are the three super-factors of the PEN model, measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975), which also has a lie scale. Neuroticism overlaps with trait anxiety, but the EPQ covers broad personality, not the state versus trait distinction. It tempts because neuroticism and trait anxiety correlate strongly."}},{"q":"Which strategy did Comrey pursue in developing his 180-item test?","concept":"Personality inventories are built by a few classic strategies, and exams ask you to match a test to its strategy. In the logical-content or rational strategy the author writes items that obviously reflect the trait, as in the Personal Data Sheet that Robert Woodworth (1917) built to screen soldiers. In the theoretical strategy items come from a theory, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule of Allen Edwards (1954), based on the need theory of Henry Murray (1938). In the criterion-group or empirical keying strategy, items are kept only if they separate a criterion group from controls, whatever their content, as in the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). In the factor-analytic strategy, factor analysis (introduced by Charles Spearman, 1904) is used to find clusters of items that correlate highly with each other and weakly with others; each cluster defines a factor, and scales are built around the factors. The Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell (1949) is the classic example. Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970) follow the same path. Comrey first grouped items into small factored homogeneous item dimensions (four items each, two positively and two negatively worded) and then factor analysed these dimensions. The result is a 180-item test answered on seven-point scales, with eight main personality scales, such as Trust versus Defensiveness and Extraversion versus Introversion, plus two validity scales. Option c is right because Comrey derived his 180-item scales by factor analysis of item and item-cluster intercorrelations, the defining feature of the factor-analytic strategy.","traps":{"a":"'Correlational' describes a research design that measures how variables co-vary without manipulating them, not a recognised test-construction strategy. Factor analysis does start from a matrix of correlations, which is why this tempts, but the named strategy is factor-analytic because it goes further and extracts latent factors that define the scales. Choosing 'correlational' confuses the raw input with the method used.","b":"Criterion-referencing means interpreting a score against a fixed standard of mastery (such as 80 percent correct on a driving test) rather than against other people, so it belongs to achievement testing. The personality strategy it resembles is criterion keying (empirical keying), used in the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). It tempts because the two 'criterion' terms sound alike.","d":"An experimental strategy means manipulating an independent variable and observing its effect on a dependent variable under controlled conditions. Personality inventories are not built that way; their items are selected by logic, theory, criterion groups or factor analysis. Experiments may later help validate a test, but they do not construct it. It tempts as a familiar research word for unsure candidates."}},{"q":"The tendency to explain others’ actions as stemming from dispositions even in the presence of situational causes is","concept":"Attribution theory, founded by Fritz Heider (1958), asks how people explain behaviour: by internal or dispositional causes (personality, attitudes, ability) or by external or situational causes (pressure, roles, luck). Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) proposed correspondent inference theory, describing when we infer that an act corresponds to an underlying disposition, for example when it is freely chosen and unexpected. In practice people over-infer. In the classic study of Edward Jones and Victor Harris (1967), students read essays praising or criticising Fidel Castro. Even when told the writer had been assigned the position, readers still judged that the writer held that attitude. Lee Ross (1977) named this the fundamental attribution error, and Daniel Gilbert and Patrick Malone (1995) used the label correspondence bias, explaining it by a two-step process: we automatically make a dispositional inference and correct for the situation only if we have time and mental resources. Most textbooks treat the two names as synonyms. The bias is weaker in collectivist cultures, where people attend more to context. Know the neighbours. The actor-observer bias of Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) says we blame situations for our own behaviour but dispositions for others'. The self-serving bias credits our successes to ourselves and blames our failures on circumstances. The ultimate attribution error of Thomas Pettigrew (1979) extends the error to whole groups. Option c is right because correspondence bias is exactly the tendency to explain others' actions by their dispositions even when clear situational causes are present.","traps":{"a":"Belief bias is a reasoning error in which people judge the logical validity of an argument by whether its conclusion is believable. For example, a valid syllogism with an unbelievable conclusion is often rejected, as shown by Jonathan Evans, Julie Barston and Paul Pollard (1983). It concerns deductive reasoning, not explaining other people's behaviour. It tempts because it is another named bias.","b":"The self-serving bias is the tendency to attribute our own successes to internal causes such as ability and effort and our own failures to external causes such as bad luck or an unfair test. It protects self-esteem and concerns explanations of our own outcomes, while the stem is about others' actions. It tempts because it also involves choosing between dispositional and situational causes.","d":"Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice and remember information that supports what we already believe and to ignore disconfirming evidence. Peter Wason (1960) demonstrated it with the 2-4-6 rule discovery task, where people tested only examples that fitted their hypothesis. It is about testing beliefs, not attributing behaviour. It tempts because it is the most famous cognitive bias."}},{"q":"Which of the following is false about social loafing","concept":"Social loafing is the tendency for people to exert less effort on a collective task, where individual contributions are pooled and cannot be identified, than when working alone. The first evidence came from Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that people pulling a rope in groups pulled less hard per person as group size grew. Part of this was coordination loss, but Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) showed real motivation loss: blindfolded participants who merely believed others were shouting with them shouted less loudly. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) reviewed many studies and proposed the collective effort model: people work hard only when they expect their effort to matter and to lead to outcomes they value. This predicts the conditions that reduce loafing, which are exactly what this question lists. Loafing drops when individual output is identifiable and evaluated, when the task is challenging, appealing or involving, when members believe high effort will be rewarded, when the group is cohesive or made of friends, and in collectivist cultures. The key link is evaluation apprehension, the concern about being judged, described by Nickolas Cottrell (1968). Loafing happens precisely because pooling removes evaluation apprehension; restoring it by making contributions identifiable cuts loafing. Contrast social facilitation, from Norman Triplett (1898) and Robert Zajonc (1965): when individual performance is observed, arousal rises and simple tasks improve. Related effects include the free-rider and sucker effects. Option c is right because it is the false statement: groups loaf less when evaluation apprehension is present, not absent.","traps":{"a":"This statement is true. When a task is challenging, appealing or personally involving, people care about the outcome and keep working hard even in a group, a finding consistent with the collective effort model of Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993). Since the stem asks for the false statement, this cannot be the answer. It tempts only candidates who miss the word 'false'.","b":"This statement is true. According to the collective effort model of Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993), effort depends on expecting that one's effort matters and leads to valued rewards. When members are convinced high effort will pay off for them or the group, loafing falls. It tempts because 'rewards' sounds like an extrinsic shortcut that might not apply to group work.","d":"This statement is true. Loafing is lower in cohesive groups, such as groups of friends, because members value the group and do not want to let each other down. Cross-cultural research also shows less loafing in collectivist cultures, where group identity is strong. It tempts because some students expect friends to relax together, but cohesion actually increases effort."}},{"q":"Persuasion though systematic processing is the __________________ and  involves _________________","concept":"Two dual-process models explain how persuasive messages change attitudes. The Elaboration Likelihood Model of Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) proposes two routes. On the central route, people think carefully about the message, scrutinise the strength and logic of the arguments and generate their own favourable or unfavourable thoughts; strong arguments persuade and weak ones can backfire. On the peripheral route, people do little thinking and are swayed by surface cues such as the attractiveness or fame of the source, the number of arguments, or a catchy slogan. Which route is taken depends on motivation (personal relevance, need for cognition) and ability (knowledge, time, freedom from distraction). Central-route change is more lasting, more resistant to counter-persuasion and more predictive of behaviour. The Heuristic-Systematic Model of Shelly Chaiken (1980) makes the same distinction in different words. Systematic processing is careful, effortful evaluation of message content and corresponds to the central route. Heuristic processing relies on simple decision rules or mental shortcuts, such as 'experts are usually right' or 'more arguments means a better case', and corresponds to the peripheral route. Both models build on the Yale communication research of Carl Hovland and colleagues (1953), which studied who says what to whom. A typical variant swaps the terms: if a stem mentions mental shortcuts or celebrity endorsement, the answer is the peripheral route or heuristic processing. Option a is right because systematic processing is the central route to persuasion, and it involves careful attention to the content and quality of the arguments.","traps":{"b":"Peripheral route with mental shortcuts is a correct pairing, but it describes heuristic processing, not systematic processing. On the peripheral route of the Elaboration Likelihood Model (Richard Petty and John Cacioppo, 1986), people rely on cues such as source attractiveness or the sheer number of arguments. It tempts because the pair is internally consistent, so a candidate who misreads 'systematic' picks it.","c":"This mixes the two routes. The central route is right for systematic processing, but it does not involve mental shortcuts; shortcuts, or heuristics, define the peripheral route and the heuristic mode of Shelly Chaiken (1980). On the central route people elaborate on argument quality. It tempts because one half is correct, so a candidate who checks only the first blank picks it.","d":"This also mixes the routes. Careful attention to arguments is the hallmark of the central route, while the peripheral route is defined by low elaboration and reliance on superficial cues. A peripheral route with careful attention is a contradiction in terms. It tempts because the second half is correct, so a candidate who checks only the second blank falls for it."}},{"q":"Trivialization and self-affirmation can reduce","concept":"Cognitive dissonance theory was proposed by Leon Festinger (1957). Dissonance is the unpleasant tension felt when two cognitions (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of one's own behaviour) are inconsistent, for example 'I smoke' and 'smoking kills'. Because the tension is aversive, people are motivated to reduce it. The classic demonstration is by Leon Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959): students paid only 1 dollar to tell the next participant that a boring task was interesting later rated the task as more enjoyable than those paid 20 dollars, because the small payment gave insufficient justification for the lie. Festinger listed direct routes: change the behaviour, change one of the cognitions, or add new consonant cognitions that justify the act. Later research added indirect routes that reduce the discomfort without resolving the inconsistency. Trivialization, studied by Linda Simon, Jeff Greenberg and Jack Brehm (1995), means deciding that the inconsistent attitudes or behaviours are unimportant, so the inconsistency does not matter. Self-affirmation, from the self-affirmation theory of Claude Steele (1988), means affirming some other valued part of the self (for example 'I am a caring parent'), so overall self-integrity is restored and the specific inconsistency stops hurting. Exam variants use neighbours: effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959), post-decision dissonance, the induced compliance paradigm, and the rival self-perception theory of Daryl Bem (1967), which explains the same findings without any tension. Option a is right because trivialization and self-affirmation are recognised indirect strategies people use to reduce cognitive dissonance.","traps":{"b":"Compliance is changing behaviour in response to a direct request, often won through techniques such as the foot-in-the-door of Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966). It is an outcome of social influence, not an aversive state that people try to reduce. Induced compliance can create dissonance, which then needs reducing. It tempts because compliance appears in classic dissonance experiments.","c":"Reactance, from Jack Brehm (1966), is the motivational state aroused when a person feels their freedom of choice is threatened, leading them to resist or do the opposite, as when a ban makes something more attractive. It is reduced by restoring freedom, not by trivialization or self-affirmation. It tempts because Jack Brehm also co-authored the trivialization research on dissonance.","d":"Self-regulation is the process of controlling one's own thoughts, emotions and behaviour in pursuit of goals, studied for example in the ego depletion work of Roy Baumeister and colleagues (1998). It is a capacity people exercise, not a tension they reduce. It tempts because self-affirmation contains 'self' and is sometimes discussed in self-regulation research on coping with threats."}},{"q":"What are the three components of empathy?","concept":"Empathy is the capacity to understand and share another person's emotional state, and most modern accounts treat it as having several parts rather than being one ability. The social psychology textbook of Robert A. Baron and Nyla R. Branscombe, widely used for Indian entrance exams, describes three components. The emotional aspect is feeling what the other person feels, a partly automatic sharing of their emotion. The cognitive component is understanding what the other person thinks and feels, also called perspective taking or empathic accuracy, close to the theory of mind idea of David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978). Empathic concern is the third part: other-oriented warmth and compassion for the person's welfare. The empathy-altruism hypothesis of C. Daniel Batson (1981) holds that empathic concern is what produces truly selfless helping. The Interpersonal Reactivity Index of Mark Davis (1980) measures four related facets: perspective taking, fantasy, empathic concern and personal distress. Counselling texts sometimes describe a different trio with a behavioural or communicative part, meaning conveying understanding back to the client, as in the accurate empathic understanding that Carl Rogers (1957) listed among the core conditions of therapy; that describes empathic skill in therapy, not the component model of empathy itself. Keep empathy separate from sympathy (feeling sorry for someone without sharing the feeling) and from personal distress (self-focused discomfort that makes people escape rather than help). Option c is right because the three components of empathy are the emotional aspect, the cognitive component and empathic concern.","traps":{"a":"This option includes 'positivity', which is not a component of empathy in any standard model. Positivity is a general tendency to evaluate things favourably, and cheerful positivity can even block attention to another's distress. Empathic concern and the emotional aspect are genuine elements, which makes the list look plausible. It tempts because two of its three terms are real empathy vocabulary.","d":"This list drops the emotional aspect, which every model treats as the core of empathy: sharing the other person's feeling. Without it, cognitive understanding plus concern plus behaviour could describe a skilled but emotionally detached helper. It tempts because each term on its own is real empathy vocabulary, and empathic concern is sometimes loosely treated as the emotional part.","b":"This trio (emotional, cognitive, behavioural) is the general affect, cognition and behaviour template applied to empathy, as in counselling texts where empathy has to be communicated back to the client, following Carl Rogers (1957). It is not the three-component model of empathy, where the third part is empathic concern, the compassion that C. Daniel Batson (1981) linked to altruistic helping. It tempts because the ABC triad fits almost any attitude question."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"Bystander intervention research began after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, reported (with exaggeration) as witnessed by 38 neighbours who did nothing. John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968) showed experimentally that the presence of others reduces helping, the bystander effect. Bibb Latané and John Darley (1970) then proposed a five-step decision model: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, assume personal responsibility, know how to help, and decide to help. Different mechanisms block different steps. Pluralistic ignorance blocks step two, interpretation. Emergencies are ambiguous, so each bystander looks at the others for clues; everyone tries to look calm, so each concludes nobody else is worried and that nothing serious is happening. In the smoke-filled room study of Latané and Darley (1968), 75 percent of participants who were alone reported the smoke, but only 10 percent did when sitting with two passive confederates. Diffusion of responsibility blocks step three: the more bystanders, the less personal responsibility each feels. In the seizure study, about 85 percent of participants who believed they were the only listener helped, against about 31 percent who believed four others could hear. A third mechanism is audience inhibition (evaluation apprehension), the fear of looking foolish by overreacting. Pluralistic ignorance also describes students who stay silent in a confusing lecture, each assuming the others understand. Option b is right because the stem describes bystanders hesitating because none of them knows for sure what is happening, which is pluralistic ignorance, a failure to interpret the situation as an emergency.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect is the overall finding, from John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968), that a person is less likely to help in an emergency when other people are present than when alone. It is the outcome, not the mechanism. The stem specifies a particular cause, uncertainty about what is happening, which names a specific explanation. It tempts because it is the umbrella term.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism in which each bystander feels less personally obliged to act because responsibility is shared among everyone present, so each assumes someone else will help. It blocks the step of taking responsibility, after the event is already seen as an emergency. In the stem the problem is uncertainty about what is happening. It tempts as the best-known bystander mechanism.","d":"'Amotivation to help' is not a recognised concept in bystander research. Amotivation is a term from the self-determination theory of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), meaning a lack of any intention to act, often because the activity seems pointless. Bystanders in emergencies are not unmotivated in this sense; they are uncertain or inhibited. It tempts as plausible-sounding filler."}},{"q":"choose the correct arrangement of events in group formation","concept":"Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed studies of small groups, including therapy and training groups, and proposed that groups develop through a predictable sequence. Forming is the orientation stage: members are polite, uncertain and dependent on the leader, testing what behaviour is acceptable. Storming is the conflict stage: members resist the task demands and each other, compete for roles and influence, and express disagreement. Norming follows when conflict is resolved: cohesion grows, roles and rules are accepted, and members share opinions openly. Performing is the stage of mature functioning: the structure now supports the task, energy goes into the work and problems are solved together. Bruce Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977) added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the task is complete and the group disbands; some writers call it mourning because members may feel loss. The order is easy to recall because of the rhyme: forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning. The logic is also intuitive: conflict must be worked through before norms settle, norms must exist before effective performance, and ending can only come last. Not every group passes neatly through every stage, and some cycle back to storming. An alternative is the punctuated equilibrium model of Connie Gersick (1988), in which project groups show long periods of inertia broken by a burst of change around the midpoint of their deadline. Variant questions ask which stage involves conflict (storming), cohesion and role acceptance (norming) or disbanding (adjourning). Option b is right because it is the only sequence that follows Tuckman's order of forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning.","traps":{"a":"This sequence places adjourning third, before norming and performing. Adjourning, added by Bruce Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977), is the termination stage when the group disbands after finishing its task, so it must come last. A group cannot end and then develop norms and perform. It tempts because the first two stages are correct, so a quick scan looks right.","c":"This sequence puts performing second and storming fourth, which reverses Tuckman's logic. Effective performance depends on settled norms, and norms emerge only after the conflict of storming has been resolved. A group cannot perform well before working through its disagreements. It tempts because it starts correctly with forming and ends correctly with adjourning, so only the middle looks scrambled.","d":"This sequence puts norming before storming and adjourning before performing. In the model of Bruce Tuckman (1965), conflict comes first and norms are agreed only after it is worked through, and adjourning is always the final stage. It also contains a spelling error ('stroming'), a hint it was carelessly built. It tempts because some real groups do agree on rules early."}},{"q":"The phenomenon in which groups make more extreme decisions than as individuals is called","concept":"Group polarization is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen the average initial leaning of members, so the group's decision is more extreme, in the same direction, than the average of their individual views. Its history begins with James Stoner (1961), whose master's thesis found that groups advised riskier courses of action on choice dilemmas than individuals did: the risky shift. Later studies found that groups whose members started out cautious became more cautious, so the shift was not always toward risk. Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni (1969) showed that French students mildly positive about their president and mildly negative about Americans became more extreme on both after discussion, and group polarization replaced risky shift as the general term. David Myers and George Bishop (1970) showed that discussion among like-minded students widened the gap in racial attitudes. Two explanations are standard. Informational influence (persuasive arguments): discussion exposes members to new arguments mostly supporting the shared view. Normative influence (social comparison): members want to be seen as good examples of the group's valued position, so they shift further toward it. Distinguish groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972) from the Bay of Pigs fiasco: a cohesive group seeking agreement suppresses dissent and decides badly, with symptoms such as an illusion of invulnerability and self-censorship. Groupthink is about poor decision quality; polarization is about extremity. Option a is right because group polarization is the established term for groups reaching more extreme decisions than their members would as individuals.","traps":{"b":"'Extremity' is an ordinary word describing how far an attitude is from neutral, and attitude extremity is a studied property of attitudes, but it is not the name of a group phenomenon. The process by which discussion makes group decisions more extreme is called group polarization. It tempts because the stem says 'more extreme', so the matching word feels like a direct echo.","c":"Groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), is a mode of thinking in highly cohesive, insulated groups with directive leaders, where the desire for consensus overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, as in the Bay of Pigs invasion. It concerns poor decision quality and suppressed dissent, not extremity as such. It tempts because both are group decision biases and groupthink can produce extreme decisions.","d":"Conformity is changing one's behaviour or beliefs to match a group, through normative or informational influence, as in the line-judgement studies of Solomon Asch (1951) and the autokinetic studies of Muzafer Sherif (1935). Conformity pulls people toward the group norm, which usually makes judgements converge rather than become extreme. It tempts because normative influence is also one explanation of polarization."}},{"q":"The research method that writes about people and cultures is called","concept":"Qualitative research has several traditions, each with its own focus. Ethnography, from the Greek for 'writing about people', is the study and written description of a culture or social group from the inside. It grew out of anthropology, especially the long-term fieldwork of Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) among the Trobriand Islanders. The ethnographer immerses in the setting for months or years, uses participant observation, informal interviews and field notes, and aims to capture the shared beliefs, rituals, language and everyday practices of the group. Clifford Geertz (1973) called the goal thick description: explaining not just what people do but what it means to them. Ethnographers distinguish the emic (insider) from the etic (outsider) view, terms coined by Kenneth Pike (1954). Psychology uses ethnography in cultural and community research, for example studying healing practices at a dargah or daily life on a psychiatric ward. Know the neighbours. Phenomenology, from the philosophy of Edmund Husserl (1913), describes the essence of individuals' lived experience of a phenomenon; interpretative phenomenological analysis was developed by Jonathan Smith (1996). Grounded theory, from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds a theory inductively from data through constant comparison and theoretical sampling. Narrative analysis studies the stories people tell about their lives, focusing on structure and meaning. A case study examines one person or unit in depth. A quick rule: culture means ethnography, experience means phenomenology, theory means grounded theory, story means narrative. Option b is right because ethnography is the method that literally writes about people and their culture, describing a group's shared way of life.","traps":{"a":"Narrative analysis studies the stories individuals tell about their experiences, examining plot, sequence and how people construct identity and meaning through storytelling. Its unit is the individual's story, often a life history or illness narrative, not a culture. It tempts because ethnographers also collect stories, and 'writes about people' sounds like storytelling. The defining feature here, writing about cultures, belongs to ethnography.","c":"Phenomenology, rooted in the philosophy of Edmund Husserl (1913), aims to describe the essence of how individuals experience a particular phenomenon, such as grief or chronic pain, by bracketing the researcher's assumptions. Its focus is lived subjective experience, not a culture or a group's shared practices. It tempts because both are qualitative, interview-based approaches that try to capture an insider's view.","d":"Grounded theory, developed by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds a new theory inductively from data, using coding, constant comparison and theoretical sampling until saturation. Its product is an explanatory theory, not a cultural description. It tempts because grounded theory studies often involve fieldwork and observation in social settings, much as ethnography does, and both are classic qualitative traditions."}},{"q":"Which of the sampling methods involves a two stage method","concept":"Sampling methods split into probability methods, where every unit has a known, non-zero chance of selection, and non-probability methods, where it does not. In simple random sampling each unit is drawn directly from a complete list. Stratified random sampling first divides the population into internally similar subgroups (strata) such as gender or class, then draws a random sample from every stratum. If each stratum's share of the sample matches its share of the population, it is proportionate; if small strata are oversampled so they can be analysed separately, it is disproportionate, and results are weighted later. Either way every stratum is sampled and individuals are picked in one round of selection. Cluster sampling works the other way round. The population is divided into naturally occurring groups (clusters) such as schools, villages or hospital wards, each ideally a mixed, miniature version of the population. Clusters, not individuals, are drawn at random. In one-stage cluster sampling everyone in the chosen clusters is studied; in two-stage cluster sampling a random sample of individuals is then drawn inside each chosen cluster. Adding more levels (districts, then villages, then households) gives multistage sampling, used in large Indian surveys such as the National Family Health Survey. Cluster sampling is cheap when no list of individuals exists, but less precise than a simple random sample of the same size. Memory aid: strata are all sampled from; clusters are themselves sampled. Option c is right because cluster sampling first selects groups and then, in its standard two-stage form, samples units within those chosen groups.","traps":{"a":"Proportionate stratified random sampling divides the population into internally similar strata (for example urban and rural) and draws a random sample from each stratum in proportion to its population share. Every stratum is represented, and individuals are chosen directly within strata in one round of random selection. It tempts because stratifying first and then sampling feels like two steps, but the strata themselves are never sampled.","b":"Disproportionate stratified random sampling also samples from every stratum, but deliberately takes a larger fraction from small strata (for example a minority group) so each can be analysed on its own; results are then weighted back to population proportions. Like the proportionate version, it selects individuals directly within all strata rather than sampling groups first. It tempts because examiners pair it with option a to offer two plausible stratified choices.","d":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method: the researcher fixes quotas for categories (say 50 men and 50 women) and interviewers fill them with whoever is convenient. It imitates stratified sampling without random selection, so sampling error cannot be estimated. It tempts because some marketing research texts (Naresh Malhotra) call it 'two-stage restricted judgmental sampling', but psychology exams key cluster sampling as the two-stage method."}},{"q":"A t-distribution is a similar to a normal distribution in the way, where ______________ .","concept":"The t-distribution was published in 1908 by William Sealy Gosset, a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, under the pen name 'Student', which is why it is called Student's t. He needed to test means from very small samples, where the population standard deviation is unknown and has to be estimated from the sample. That estimate adds extra uncertainty, and the t-distribution builds it in. Like the standard normal (z) distribution, t is continuous, bell-shaped, unimodal and perfectly symmetrical around a mean of zero, so mean, median and mode coincide and there is no skew. The differences lie in spread and tails. There is a family of t curves, one for each value of degrees of freedom (df, n minus 1 for a single sample). With few df the curve is flatter in the middle with heavier tails, so it is leptokurtic, and its variance, df divided by (df minus 2), is greater than 1, the variance of z. As df grows the curve tightens toward the normal; by about 30 df the two are close, and at infinite df they are identical. The practical effect is larger critical values: about 2.26 at 9 df (two-tailed, .05) against 1.96 for z, so small samples need bigger differences to reach significance. Contrast the chi-square and F distributions, which are positively skewed and never negative. Option b is right because, of the properties offered, symmetry about the mean is the one the t-distribution shares with the normal distribution.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' would be right only if the t and normal distributions shared none of the listed properties. They do share one: both are perfectly symmetrical, bell-shaped curves centred on zero. This option tempts candidates who remember that t differs from the normal in tails and spread, and then overgeneralise to 'they share nothing'. Always test each listed property before choosing 'none of the above'.","c":"Kurtosis describes how heavy the tails and how sharp the peak of a distribution are; the normal is mesokurtic, with excess kurtosis of zero. The t-distribution is leptokurtic, with heavier tails and an excess kurtosis of 6 divided by (df minus 4) when df exceeds 4, so kurtosis is not the same. It tempts because both curves look like bells at a glance, and the gap shrinks as df rises.","d":"Variability means spread, measured by variance or standard deviation. The standard normal has a variance of 1, while the t-distribution has a variance of df divided by (df minus 2), which is always larger than 1 when df exceeds 2. The extra spread reflects the added uncertainty of estimating the population standard deviation from a sample. It tempts because the difference becomes tiny once df is large."}},{"q":"the df for 'within groups' in one way ANOVA with three levels on intelligence and 10 samples per level is","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA), set out by Ronald A. Fisher (1925), tests whether three or more group means differ by splitting total variability into two parts. Between-groups variability reflects differences among the group means (any treatment effect plus error). Within-groups variability reflects how scores spread around their own group mean (error only). Each part has its own degrees of freedom (df), the number of values free to vary once a statistic is fixed. With k groups and N total scores the formulas are: df between = k minus 1; df within (also called df error or residual) = N minus k, because one df is lost for each group mean estimated; df total = N minus 1. The parts always add up: (k minus 1) plus (N minus k) equals N minus 1. In this stem the single independent variable has three levels, so k = 3, and 10 participants per level give N = 30. So df between = 2, df within = 30 minus 3 = 27, and df total = 29 (check: 2 + 27 = 29). The result is reported as F(2, 27), and the critical F at the .05 level is about 3.35. Examiners vary this item by asking for df between, df total, or the error df in a repeated-measures design, where the same people serve in every condition and error df becomes (k minus 1)(n minus 1). Option b is right because within-groups df equals N minus k, which here is 30 minus 3 = 27.","traps":{"a":"29 is the total degrees of freedom, N minus 1 = 30 minus 1. Total df treats all scores as one sample and equals the sum of between (2) and within (27) df. It tempts because N minus 1 is the most familiar df formula from one-sample t-tests, so candidates subtract one by habit instead of subtracting one for each group mean estimated.","c":"30 is simply N, the total number of observations (3 groups times 10). A sample size is never itself a df value in ANOVA, because at least one df is always used up when a mean is estimated from the data. It tempts candidates who multiply 3 by 10 correctly and then forget the subtraction step, especially under time pressure in a numerical item.","d":"31 does not match any ANOVA df in this design; it would require adding one to N, which no df formula does. Degrees of freedom are always reduced, never increased, by the parameters estimated from the data. It tempts only as an arithmetic slip, such as confusing N plus 1 with N minus 1, and is included mainly to catch guessing."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on an independent samples t-test with a total sample size of 25 in each group.","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of scores free to vary once certain statistics are fixed. If five scores must have a mean of 10, four can take any value, but the fifth is then determined, so df = 4. Each time a mean is estimated from a sample, one df is used up. This gives the df rules for the three t-tests, all based on the distribution published by William Sealy Gosset (1908) under the name 'Student'. In a one-sample t-test one mean is estimated, so df = n minus 1. In a paired (related or dependent) samples t-test the analysis runs on the difference scores, so df = number of pairs minus 1. In an independent samples t-test two separate group means are estimated, so df = (n1 minus 1) plus (n2 minus 1) = N minus 2, where N is the total of both groups. Here each group has 25 people, so N = 50 and df = 25 + 25 minus 2 = 48. Adding the df of each group gives the same result: 24 + 24 = 48. When group variances are unequal, the test proposed by Bernard L. Welch (1947) uses an adjusted, usually non-integer df. Watch the wording in variants: 25 matched pairs would give 24, and 25 people in total would give 23. Option a is right because an independent samples t-test with 25 in each group has df = n1 + n2 minus 2 = 50 minus 2 = 48.","traps":{"b":"24 is n minus 1 for a single group of 25. It would be the correct df for a one-sample t-test on 25 people, or for a paired samples t-test with 25 matched pairs, where the analysis runs on 25 difference scores. It tempts because the stem says '25 in each group', and candidates apply n minus 1 to one group instead of combining both groups.","c":"49 is N minus 1 for all 50 people treated as one sample. It subtracts only one df, as if a single mean had been estimated, whereas an independent samples t-test estimates two group means and so loses two df. It tempts because N minus 1 is the most memorised df formula, and 49 is close enough to 48 to look right.","d":"25 is the size of one group, not a degrees-of-freedom value. Degrees of freedom are always reduced by the number of means estimated, so a raw sample size cannot serve as df. It tempts because it is the only number printed in the stem, and a hurried reader may take 'total sample size of 25' as the answer itself."}},{"q":"F ratio in one-way ANOVA is given by","concept":"The F ratio is the test statistic of analysis of variance (ANOVA), the method Ronald A. Fisher developed for agricultural experiments and set out in 1925; George W. Snedecor named the statistic F in his honour in 1934. ANOVA partitions the total sum of squares (SS total) into SS between, the spread of group means around the grand mean, and SS within, the spread of individual scores around their own group mean. Each SS is divided by its degrees of freedom to give a mean square (MS), which is a variance estimate: MS between = SS between divided by (k minus 1), and MS within = SS within divided by (N minus k). The logic is signal over noise. MS within reflects only random error. MS between reflects that same error plus any real treatment effect. So F = MS between divided by MS within. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is close to 1; a real effect inflates the numerator and pushes F well above 1. F can never be negative because both terms are variances; its distribution is positively skewed and defined by two df values, written F(df between, df within). With only two groups, F equals t squared. Mean square total is not part of the ratio; SS total appears instead in effect sizes such as eta squared (SS between divided by SS total). Option a is right because F is defined as mean square between groups divided by mean square within groups.","traps":{"b":"MS total over MS within is not a standard statistic. Total variability is the starting point that ANOVA splits into between and within parts, so placing it in the numerator mixes error back into the signal and the comparison loses its logic. It tempts because SS total does appear in ANOVA tables and in eta squared (SS between divided by SS total).","c":"MS within over MS total also has no role in ANOVA. Dividing error by total variability resembles the proportion of unexplained variance (1 minus eta squared, which is computed from sums of squares, not mean squares). It tempts because it looks like a part-to-whole ratio, which is how effect sizes are built, but F compares two independent variance estimates rather than a part with its whole.","d":"MS within over MS between is the F ratio turned upside down. Putting error variance on top would make large treatment effects produce small values, the reverse of how F is read against its critical value. It tempts because candidates remember that both mean squares appear in the formula and simply forget which one is the numerator. Memory aid: signal (between) over noise (within)."}},{"q":"The most appropriate non-parametric alternative for a related samples t-test when using an ordinal level of variable with some magnitude is","concept":"Parametric tests such as the t-test assume interval or ratio data and roughly normal distributions. When those assumptions fail, or the data are ordinal, non-parametric (distribution-free) tests are used, usually working on ranks. Choosing one depends on two questions: are the samples independent or related, and how much information do the scores carry? For two related samples (the same people measured twice, or matched pairs) the parametric test is the paired samples t-test. Its non-parametric alternatives are the sign test and the Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). The sign test records only whether each difference is positive or negative and checks the split against the binomial distribution, so it discards size. The Wilcoxon test keeps it: find each pair's difference, drop zero differences, rank the absolute differences from smallest to largest, attach the original signs, and sum the positive and negative ranks separately. The smaller sum, T, is compared with a critical value. Because larger differences carry more weight, it is more powerful than the sign test. Sidney Siegel (1956) stressed that it needs data whose differences can be ordered by size, which is the 'ordinal with some magnitude' in the stem. Parallel tests for other designs: Kruskal-Wallis, from William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952), for three or more independent groups, and the Friedman test, from Milton Friedman (1937), for three or more related conditions. Option b is right because the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the related samples alternative that uses both the direction and the magnitude of differences.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test, from Henry B. Mann and Donald R. Whitney (1947), is the non-parametric alternative to the independent samples t-test. It pools scores from two separate groups, ranks them together, and checks whether one group's ranks are systematically higher. It ignores pairing, so it suits different people in each group, not matched or repeated measures. It tempts because it is the best-known rank test and is equivalent to Wilcoxon's rank-sum test.","c":"The chi-square test, from Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed and expected frequencies of nominal categories, either for goodness of fit or for independence in a contingency table. It works on counts, not ranked differences, and the standard version assumes independent observations, so it cannot handle paired ordinal magnitudes. It tempts because it is the most familiar non-parametric test and a common default guess for any non-parametric question.","d":"The sign test also handles two related samples, so it is the closest distractor. But it uses only the direction of each difference (plus or minus) and tests the split with the binomial distribution, ignoring how large each difference is. That wastes information and lowers power. It tempts because it is a genuine paired alternative; the phrase 'with some magnitude' in the stem is what rules it out in favour of Wilcoxon."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the second stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas, an English social psychologist and a co-founder of the London School of Economics, proposed the best-known stage model of creative thinking in The Art of Thought (1926). He built it from the self-reports of creative thinkers, especially the physicist Hermann von Helmholtz and the mathematician Henri Poincare, who described solutions arriving suddenly after a period away from the problem. The four stages, in order, are: (1) Preparation, the conscious, effortful work of defining the problem, gathering information and trying solutions, often ending in an impasse. (2) Incubation, setting the problem aside while the mind turns to other things; Wallas believed unconscious or fringe-conscious processing continued meanwhile. (3) Illumination, the sudden 'aha' or flash of insight when the idea enters awareness, sometimes announced by a vague feeling Wallas called intimation. (4) Verification, the conscious testing, checking and refining of the idea. A handy mnemonic is PIIV. Modern research supports a modest incubation effect, often explained by forgetting misleading cues or fading fixation rather than hidden full-scale problem solving. Neighbouring ideas used in variants: insight learning in the chimpanzee studies of Wolfgang Kohler (1917); divergent and convergent thinking from J. P. Guilford (1950); the five-stage version of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1996), which adds evaluation and elaboration; and functional fixedness, the block incubation can relieve. Exams also ask for the first stage, the last stage, or the stage matching a description. Option b is right because incubation is the second of Wallas's four stages, coming after preparation and before illumination.","traps":{"a":"Illumination is the third stage in Wallas (1926): the sudden flash of insight, the 'aha' moment, when the solution breaks into awareness after incubation. It is often preceded by intimation, a vague feeling that an idea is about to arrive. It tempts because illumination is the most dramatic and memorable stage, and candidates who remember insight following a break may misplace it as the second stage.","c":"Verification is the fourth and final stage in Wallas (1926): the conscious, deliberate testing, checking and refining of the idea produced by illumination, for example a mathematician working through the full proof. It brings back the effortful, logical thinking of preparation. It tempts because verification sits in the same list as the other stages, and a candidate unsure of the order can place it anywhere.","d":"Preparation is the first stage in Wallas (1926): defining the problem, collecting information and making conscious attempts at a solution, often until reaching a frustrating impasse. Incubation follows it. It tempts because preparation naturally feels like the start of the process, and candidates who know the stage names but not their order, or who misread 'second', pick it."}},{"q":"What is the illumination stage of Wallas' model of the creative process?","concept":"Graham Wallas (1926), in The Art of Thought, described creative thinking in four stages, drawing on reports by Hermann von Helmholtz and Henri Poincare of solutions that arrived suddenly after a rest from the problem. Preparation is conscious work: defining the problem, collecting information and trying solutions. Incubation is the period when the problem is set aside and attention turns elsewhere, while some unconscious processing is thought to continue. Illumination is the third stage: the sudden 'aha' or 'eureka' moment when a solution, or the key idea for one, appears in consciousness, often unexpectedly, as in Poincare's account of grasping a mathematical idea just as he stepped onto a bus. Wallas noted that illumination may be preceded by intimation, a fringe feeling that an answer is near. Verification is the final stage, in which the idea is tested, worked out and refined. Illumination is the experience psychologists study as insight. Wolfgang Kohler (1917) saw it when his chimpanzee Sultan suddenly joined two sticks to reach fruit, and Karl Duncker (1945) showed that insight comes from restructuring the problem. Insight problems are solved by such restructuring, unlike analytic problems solved step by step. Examiners build variants by giving a description and asking for the stage: initial attempts (preparation), leaving the problem aside (incubation), the flash of insight (illumination), testing the solution (verification). Option d is right because illumination is the stage at which insight into the problem is achieved and the solution suddenly becomes conscious.","traps":{"a":"Formulating the problem and making initial attempts to solve it describes preparation, the first stage in Wallas (1926). This is conscious, deliberate effort: gathering knowledge, defining the goal and trying approaches, often until the person gets stuck. It tempts because preparation is when the person works hardest on the problem, and candidates may wrongly link active effort with the arrival of the solution.","b":"Testing and carrying out the solution describes verification, the fourth and final stage in Wallas (1926). Here the insight produced during illumination is checked for accuracy, worked out in detail and applied, using deliberate, logical thinking. It tempts because verification deals with the solution and follows straight after illumination, so a candidate who thinks loosely of 'the solution stage' may choose it.","c":"Leaving the problem while considering other things describes incubation, the second stage in Wallas (1926). The problem is set aside, and unconscious processing, release from fixation or forgetting of misleading cues may prepare the ground for insight. It tempts because incubation leads directly into illumination, and many candidates merge the rest period and the sudden insight that follows it into a single stage."}},{"q":"According to Sternberg's investment theory, how many distinct but interrelated resources are required for creativity?","concept":"Robert J. Sternberg and Todd I. Lubart proposed the investment theory of creativity in 1991 and expanded it in their book Defying the Crowd (1995). Its central metaphor comes from finance: creative people 'buy low and sell high' in the world of ideas. They pursue ideas that are unknown or out of favour, persuade others of their worth despite resistance, then move on once the idea becomes popular. Sternberg and Lubart argue that creativity needs a confluence of six distinct but interrelated resources: (1) intellectual abilities, namely synthetic ability to see problems in new ways, analytic ability to judge which ideas are worth pursuing, and practical-contextual ability to sell ideas to others; (2) knowledge of the field, which helps but can also entrench old ways of thinking; (3) thinking styles, especially a legislative style that prefers making one's own rules; (4) personality, including willingness to overcome obstacles, take sensible risks and tolerate ambiguity; (5) motivation, mainly intrinsic and task-focused; and (6) environment, a setting that supports and rewards creative ideas. Confluence means the resources combine in complex ways: some need a minimum threshold and one can partly compensate for another. Distinguish neighbouring models: the componential model of Teresa Amabile (1983), with domain-relevant skills, creativity-relevant processes and intrinsic task motivation, later adding the social environment; the systems model of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1988), with person, domain and field; and the four stages of Graham Wallas (1926). Option d is right because the investment theory names exactly six resources: intellectual abilities, knowledge, thinking styles, personality, motivation and environment.","traps":{"a":"Five is not the count in the investment theory, which lists six resources. Five-part lists elsewhere cause the slip: the Big Five personality traits, in which openness to experience is the trait most linked to creativity, or the five-stage creative process of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1996). It tempts candidates who recall most of the list but forget one resource, usually environment or thinking styles.","b":"Four is not correct either. Four is the number of stages in the creative process of Graham Wallas (1926), namely preparation, incubation, illumination and verification, and of the components in the later version of Teresa Amabile's componential model, which added the social environment. It tempts because these four-part creativity models are taught in the same chapter, so the numbers are easily swapped.","c":"Seven overstates the count; the investment theory has six resources. Seven is the number of intelligences in the original multiple intelligences theory of Howard Gardner (1983), before he added naturalist intelligence. It tempts because Gardner and Sternberg are both taught as intelligence theorists who also wrote on creativity, so their lists are easily mixed up under exam pressure."}},{"q":"What is the difference between functional fixedness and mental set?","concept":"Both terms describe ways past experience blocks problem solving, a family often called fixation. Mental set is the broader idea: a tendency to keep using a problem-solving strategy that worked before, even when a simpler or better one is available. The classic demonstration is the water jar problem of Abraham S. Luchins (1942). Participants used three jars of given sizes to measure out exact amounts of water. The first several problems could only be solved with a long formula (B minus A minus 2C). Later problems could be solved with a simple one (A minus C), but most participants who had practised the long method kept using it, and some failed a problem only the simple method could solve. Luchins called this the Einstellung effect. Functional fixedness is a narrower block that concerns objects: the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: given a candle, matches and a box of tacks, people struggled to fix the candle to a wall because they saw the box only as a container, not a shelf, and did better when the box was presented empty. Norman Maier (1931) found a similar effect in the two-string problem, where pliers could serve as a pendulum weight. Some textbooks treat functional fixedness as a special case of mental set. Option c is right because functional fixedness concerns how we think about physical objects, while mental set concerns the rigid use of problem-solving strategies.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two definitions. It assigns problem-solving strategies to functional fixedness and physical objects to mental set, the opposite of the classic studies: the candle problem of Karl Duncker (1945) concerned an object's usual function, and the water jars of Abraham S. Luchins (1942) concerned a habitual method. It tempts because it uses the right two ideas, objects and strategies, and only careful reading reveals the swap.","b":"Saying there is no difference is wrong, though it holds a grain of truth. Both are forms of fixation in which past experience blocks a fresh approach, and some textbooks describe functional fixedness as a special type of mental set. But they are defined separately: one concerns the uses of objects, the other concerns strategies. It tempts candidates who remember only that both are obstacles to problem solving.","d":"This option borrows a perception term. Top-down processing means interpretation guided by prior knowledge and expectations, as in recognising a word from context; it is not the defining feature of functional fixedness, which is seeing an object only in its usual use. The second half, applying strategies too rigidly, does describe mental set. It tempts because one half is right and the jargon sounds sophisticated."}},{"q":"Why does functional fixedness occur?","concept":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function, which blocks you from noticing a new use that would solve the problem. Karl Duncker (1945) demonstrated it with the candle problem: given a candle, a box of tacks and some matches, people must fix the candle to a wall so that it burns without dripping. The solution is to empty the box, tack it to the wall and use it as a shelf. People solve it far less often when the box is presented full of tacks, because it is then encoded as a container, not a platform. Norman Maier (1931) found a similar block in the two-string problem, where a pair of pliers must be used as a pendulum weight. Functional fixedness is a special case of mental set, the readiness to attack a problem in the way that worked before, shown by Abraham Luchins (1942) with his water-jar problems. Both are failures caused by knowledge, not by its absence. In cognitive terms this is top-down processing: thinking and perception driven by stored concepts, schemas and expectations, as opposed to bottom-up processing, driven by the raw features of the stimulus. Tim German and Margaret Defeyter (2000) found that five-year-olds are less fixed than six- and seven-year-olds, who have formed firmer ideas of what objects are for. Option b is right because functional fixedness arises when prior knowledge of an object's usual use, a top-down influence, dominates and hides its other possible uses.","traps":{"a":"A lack of problem-solving skill would predict that experts and older children suffer least, but functional fixedness works the other way: it grows out of knowledge about how objects are normally used. German and Defeyter (2000) found older children more fixed than younger ones, and skilled adults routinely fail Duncker's candle problem. It tempts because any failure to solve a problem looks, on the surface, like a skills deficit.","c":"Underestimating the environment is not the mechanism. In the candle problem every needed object is in plain view and people do attend to it; the difficulty is that they encode the box only as a container. The obstacle is internal, a fixed mental representation, not neglect of the situation. It tempts because the solution does involve using the environment creatively, so the option sounds related to the outcome.","d":"Limited access to tools describes an external shortage of resources, not a cognitive bias. In the studies of Duncker (1945) and Maier (1931) all the necessary objects were provided; participants failed because they could not see a familiar object serving a new function. Functional fixedness is about how we represent what we have, not how much we have. It tempts because the classic examples all involve tools and objects."}},{"q":"What are heuristics in problem-solving?","concept":"In problem solving, a heuristic is a rule of thumb or mental shortcut that usually leads to a good answer quickly but carries no guarantee of success. The term was popularised by the mathematician George Polya (1945) in How to Solve It and formalised by Allen Newell and Herbert A. Simon (1972) in Human Problem Solving, who described problem solving as a search through a problem space from an initial state to a goal state. The contrast concept is the algorithm: a step-by-step procedure that, if followed correctly, always produces the solution, such as trying every combination of a lock or applying a formula. Algorithms are guaranteed but can be slow, or impossible when the problem space is huge; heuristics are fast and economical but can fail. Simon's idea of bounded rationality explains why people rely on heuristics: we have limited time, memory and information, so we satisfice rather than optimise. Common problem-solving heuristics are means-ends analysis, working backward, hill climbing, setting subgoals and analogy. In judgement and decision making, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) described the availability, representativeness, and anchoring and adjustment heuristics, and showed the systematic biases they produce. A variant question may ask which strategy guarantees a solution (algorithm) or which is a shortcut that may fail (heuristic). Neither is simply better: the choice depends on the cost of errors and of time. Option a is right because a heuristic is a general rule that is usually, but not always, right, trading certainty for speed.","traps":{"b":"A method that always produces a solution is the definition of an algorithm, the systematic procedure that exhausts possibilities or applies a fixed formula, such as checking every possible arrangement of an anagram. Heuristics carry no such guarantee; they can lead to dead ends or errors. It tempts because heuristics often do work, and candidates confuse 'usually works' with 'always works', which is exactly the distinction being tested.","c":"Neither strategy is better in general. Heuristics save time and effort, but algorithms guarantee a solution, so the right choice depends on the problem, the size of the search space and the cost of mistakes. Newell and Simon (1972) treated both as tools for searching a problem space. It tempts because textbooks praise heuristics for efficiency, which candidates misread as overall superiority.","d":"Accuracy is the algorithm's strength, not the heuristic's. A correctly applied algorithm reaches the right answer every time, while heuristics trade some accuracy for speed. Tversky and Kahneman (1974) showed that judgement heuristics such as availability and representativeness produce systematic biases, which is direct evidence that they are less accurate. It tempts because heuristics feel intuitive and are often good enough in daily life."}},{"q":"What is the hill-climbing heuristic in problem-solving?","concept":"Hill climbing is a problem-solving heuristic in which, at each step, you choose the move that seems to bring you closest to the goal state. It comes from the problem-space approach of Allen Newell and Herbert A. Simon (1972): every problem has an initial state, a goal state and operators that transform one state into another, and a heuristic is a rule for choosing among operators. The name is an analogy: a climber in fog who always steps uphill will usually reach the top. Hill climbing is fast and needs little memory, because you only compare the current state with the goal. Its weakness is that some problems require a move that temporarily takes you further from the goal. The classic illustration is the Hobbits and Orcs (missionaries and cannibals) river-crossing problem, where people get stuck at the step that requires carrying creatures back across the river. A hill climber can also be trapped on a local peak. Neighbouring heuristics that examiners swap in: means-ends analysis, in which you find the biggest difference between the current and goal states and set subgoals to reduce it, as in Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver; working backward from the goal; breaking a problem into subgoals; analogy, which reuses the solution to an earlier similar problem; and trial and error, which tries options with no guiding rule. Means-ends analysis tolerates detours that hill climbing avoids. Option d is right because hill climbing means choosing, at each point, the option that moves you closer to the goal state.","traps":{"a":"Using the solution of an earlier, similar problem is the analogy heuristic. Its success depends on spotting the deep structural similarity between problems, as Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak (1980) showed with Duncker's radiation problem: people rarely used a helpful military story unless given a hint. Hill climbing uses no earlier problem; it only compares the current state with the goal. It tempts because analogy is another familiar named heuristic.","b":"Breaking a problem into smaller parts and solving each separately is the subgoal strategy, also called problem decomposition, and it is the core of means-ends analysis described by Newell and Simon (1972). The Tower of Hanoi is the classic example, where you first aim to free and move the largest disc. Hill climbing sets no subgoals; it simply takes the locally best step. It tempts because both are goal-directed heuristics.","c":"Trying different solutions until one works is trial and error, the strategy Edward Thorndike (1898) observed in cats escaping from his puzzle boxes. It has no guiding rule for which attempt to make next, whereas hill climbing evaluates each possible move against the goal and picks the one that reduces the distance most. It tempts because hill climbing also proceeds step by step and sometimes fails."}},{"q":"What are the three aspects of intelligence in Sternberg's Triarchic Theory?","concept":"Robert J. Sternberg (1985) proposed the triarchic theory of intelligence in Beyond IQ, arguing that IQ tests measure only a narrow slice of intelligent behaviour. Triarchic means ruled by three, and the theory has three subtheories. The componential subtheory covers the internal mental mechanisms of thinking: metacomponents (planning, monitoring and evaluating problem solving), performance components (carrying out the strategy) and knowledge-acquisition components (learning new information). It yields analytical intelligence, the kind IQ tests and school examinations reward. The experiential subtheory covers how a person handles novelty and how well they automatise familiar tasks; it yields creative intelligence. The contextual subtheory covers adapting to, shaping, or selecting one's environment; it yields practical intelligence, close to street smarts or tacit knowledge. Sternberg later broadened this into the theory of successful intelligence, a balance of analytical, creative and practical abilities used to reach one's goals within a cultural context. Know the neighbouring theories used as distractors: Charles Spearman's (1904) general factor g; Louis L. Thurstone's (1938) primary mental abilities; Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences, which began with seven (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal) and later added naturalist; and Peter Salovey and John Mayer's (1990) emotional intelligence. Do not confuse the triarchic theory with Sternberg's separate triangular theory of love (1986), built from intimacy, passion and commitment. Option b is right because the three aspects of Sternberg's triarchic theory are componential, experiential and contextual.","traps":{"a":"Linguistic, logical-mathematical and spatial are three of the multiple intelligences of Howard Gardner (1983), whose original list also included musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal, with naturalist added later. Gardner argued for relatively independent intelligences, citing evidence such as brain damage and savant syndrome. It tempts because Gardner and Sternberg are the two best known challengers to g and are taught together, so their lists are easily swapped.","c":"Social, emotional and spiritual are separate constructs from different authors: social intelligence was described by Edward Thorndike (1920), emotional intelligence by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995), and spiritual intelligence is a later popular idea. None is a Sternberg subtheory. It tempts because Sternberg's practical intelligence sounds social, and these terms appear in the same chapter on intelligence beyond IQ.","d":"Physical, cognitive and emotional are broad domains of human development used in lifespan textbooks, not components of any theory of intelligence. Sternberg's three parts all concern mental functioning: internal mechanisms of thought, the handling of novelty, and adaptation to context. It tempts because it sounds like a complete, balanced list of human abilities and contains the word 'cognitive', which candidates link with intelligence."}},{"q":"What are the five major parts of emotional intelligence, according to Goleman?","concept":"Emotional intelligence (EI) was first defined by Peter Salovey and John D. Mayer (1990) as the ability to monitor one's own and others' feelings and emotions, to discriminate among them and to use this information to guide thinking and action. Daniel Goleman, a psychologist and science journalist, popularised the idea in his best seller Emotional Intelligence (1995), adapting Salovey's framework into five domains: knowing one's emotions (self-awareness, recognising a feeling as it happens), managing emotions (handling feelings appropriately, such as soothing anxiety or anger), motivating oneself (marshalling emotions toward a goal, delaying gratification and controlling impulses), recognising emotions in others (empathy) and handling relationships (managing emotions in others, social competence). In Working with Emotional Intelligence (1998) Goleman relabelled these for the workplace as self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skills, and he later merged them into four domains. Know the families of models. Ability models, such as Mayer and Salovey's (1997) four-branch model (perceiving, using, understanding and managing emotions), treat EI as a set of mental abilities measured by performance tests such as the MSCEIT. Mixed models, such as Goleman's and Reuven Bar-On's (1997) EQ-i, blend abilities with personality traits and motivation and are measured largely by self-report. The roots of the idea lie in Howard Gardner's (1983) interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences and Edward Thorndike's (1920) social intelligence. Option b is right because it reproduces Goleman's original 1995 list of five domains in his own wording: knowing our emotions, managing them, motivating ourselves, recognising others' emotions and handling relationships.","traps":{"a":"This list mixes Howard Gardner's (1983) interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences with generic labels such as cognitive and communication skills. Gardner's two personal intelligences are ancestors of emotional intelligence, but this is not Goleman's model, and cognitive skill belongs to IQ, the very thing EI was defined against. It tempts because interpersonal and intrapersonal sound like the self and other halves of Goleman's scheme.","c":"Self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skills is Goleman's own workplace relabelling in Working with Emotional Intelligence (1998), so it is not a false description of his ideas. The key prefers b because b reproduces the five domains in the exact wording of his 1995 book. It tempts strongly because this later version is the one used in most management and leadership training today.","d":"IQ, EQ, a social quotient, CQ and a spiritual quotient are popular quotient labels for separate constructs: general intelligence, emotional intelligence itself, social intelligence, cultural intelligence and spiritual intelligence. They are rival or related constructs, not five parts of EI, and the list even contains EQ, the very thing being divided. It tempts because it is a neat list of five that includes the right term."}},{"q":"What are the two basic mental operations that Piaget hypothesized to be involved in learning?","concept":"Jean Piaget (1936) explained cognitive growth as adaptation, the same principle that drives biological evolution. The basic unit of knowledge is the schema (or scheme): an organised pattern of action or thought, such as sucking or grasping in infancy, or a concept of dog later on. Adaptation works through two complementary processes. Assimilation means interpreting new experience in terms of an existing schema: a toddler who knows 'doggie' calls a cow 'doggie'. Accommodation means modifying an existing schema, or creating a new one, when the experience does not fit: the child learns that cows are different and forms a new category. When existing schemas handle experience well, the child is in equilibrium; a mismatch creates disequilibrium, and the drive to restore balance through assimilation and accommodation is called equilibration, the motor of development. Organisation is the related tendency to combine schemas into larger, coordinated systems. Together these processes produce the four stages: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11). Exam variants ask which process changes a schema (accommodation), which fits experience into an unchanged schema (assimilation), or which names the balancing process (equilibration). Piaget saw the child as a little scientist who constructs knowledge through action, which is why his view is called constructivism. Option c is right because Piaget proposed assimilation and accommodation as the two basic operations of adaptation through which learning and cognitive development occur.","traps":{"a":"Encoding and retrieval are stages of memory in the information-processing approach: encoding turns input into a storable form, storage holds it, and retrieval brings it back, as in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). They describe memory processes, not Piaget's mechanisms of schema change. It tempts because information processing is the main modern alternative to Piaget in cognitive development chapters.","b":"Attention and perception are cognitive processes that select and interpret sensory input. They matter in every theory of learning but are not Piaget's named mechanisms; in his theory, perception is itself organised by schemas through assimilation. Information-processing researchers, not Piaget, made attention central to development. It tempts because attention and perception sound like the fundamental mental operations any theory of learning must include.","d":"Sensory input and motor output describe the reflex arc and the content of Piaget's first stage, the sensorimotor period, in which infants know the world through senses and actions. They are the raw material of early schemas, not the two processes that change schemas. It tempts because the word sensorimotor is strongly linked with Piaget, so a sensory and motor pairing looks Piagetian."}},{"q":"What are parapraxes, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Parapraxes are the small errors of everyday life that Sigmund Freud (1901) analysed in The Psychopathology of Everyday Life: slips of the tongue, slips of the pen, misreadings, mishearings, forgetting of names or appointments, and mislaying objects. The English word parapraxis was introduced by Freud's translator James Strachey for the German Fehlleistung, literally faulty action. Freud's claim was that these errors are not random. They are compromise formations: an unconscious wish, impulse or thought that has been repressed slips past the ego's defences and interferes with what we consciously intend to say or do. A chairman who opens a meeting by declaring it closed reveals that he wishes it were over. Parapraxes therefore join dreams (the royal road to the unconscious in Freud's 1900 book on dreams), free association and neurotic symptoms as windows into unconscious content, and they illustrate psychic determinism, the principle that no mental event is accidental. The hidden content need not be sexual; it can be hostility, anxiety, rivalry or any other repressed wish. Neighbouring concepts used to build variants: the conscience and the ego-ideal, the two parts of the superego, formed through childhood punishment and praise respectively; repression, the defence that keeps material unconscious; and the topographic model of conscious, preconscious and unconscious. Modern cognitive psychologists explain many slips as ordinary errors in speech planning, so treat Freud's account as theory, not settled fact. Option b is right because parapraxes are mistakes that reveal the unconscious.","traps":{"a":"This option is half right: parapraxes do include errors in speaking and writing. But Freud did not define them as sexual in nature. The hidden motive can be any repressed wish, such as hostility, fear or rivalry, not only sexual content, so the added qualifier is the trap. It tempts because Freud's theory is popularly reduced to sex, and slips with sexual meaning are the best known jokes about Freudian slips.","c":"Good behaviours for which children have been praised build the ego-ideal, one of the two subsystems of the superego described by Sigmund Freud (1923). Rewarded behaviour becomes an internal standard that produces pride when we meet it. This concerns moral development, not everyday mistakes. It tempts because it comes from the same chapter on Freud's structural model and uses the language of childhood learning.","d":"Behaviours for which children are punished form the conscience, the second subsystem of the superego in Freud's (1923) structural model; violating it produces guilt. It is a moral structure created through punishment and identification with parents, not an error that leaks out of the unconscious. It tempts because guilt and the unconscious are related Freudian ideas, and the option sounds like a technical definition."}},{"q":"What are Freudian slips, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"A Freudian slip is the everyday name for what Sigmund Freud (1901) called a parapraxis, described in The Psychopathology of Everyday Life. The term covers slips of the tongue and pen, misreadings, forgetting a familiar name, losing objects and other small faulty actions. Freud's argument rests on psychic determinism: no mental act is accidental, so an apparently random error must have a cause. In his view the cause is a repressed wish or thought in the unconscious, which breaks through the ego's censorship and distorts the intended act. A student who writes that she looks forward to a long and fruitless collaboration may be revealing doubt about the project. The slip is a compromise between the conscious intention and the unconscious impulse. Freud placed slips beside dreams, jokes and neurotic symptoms as evidence for an active unconscious, and analysts used them clinically much as they used free association. The hidden content can be aggressive, competitive, anxious or sexual; sex is not part of the definition. Variants of this question swap in Freud's structural terms: the id, the pleasure-seeking reservoir of drives; the ego, which follows the reality principle; and the superego with its two parts, the conscience (formed by punishment) and the ego-ideal (formed by praise). They may also ask about the topographic model, in which the preconscious holds material that can easily become conscious. Critics note that many slips are explained by ordinary errors in speech planning. Option a is right because a Freudian slip is a mistake that reveals the unconscious.","traps":{"b":"Freudian slips can occur in speech or writing, so the first half fits, but the qualifier 'sexual in nature' makes it wrong. Freud (1901) held that the hidden motive can be any repressed content, such as hostility, jealousy, anxiety or ambition, as well as sexual wishes. It tempts because popular culture treats every Freudian slip as a sexual joke and because sexuality is central to Freud's drive theory.","c":"Behaviours that earned praise in childhood are internalised as the ego-ideal, the part of the superego that holds standards of what we should be and rewards us with pride, according to Sigmund Freud (1923). It is a moral structure, not an error that betrays hidden wishes. It tempts because it is drawn from the same Freud chapter and the wording sounds like a definition of a Freudian term.","d":"Behaviours punished in childhood form the conscience, the part of the superego that forbids and punishes with guilt, in Freud's (1923) structural model. A slip may reveal a guilty wish, but the conscience itself is a set of internal prohibitions, not a mistake. It tempts because guilt, punishment and unconscious conflict all appear in discussions of slips, and the option uses Freud's technical vocabulary."}},{"q":"What is the phallic stage of development, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Sigmund Freud set out the psychosexual stages in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905), naming the phallic stage in 1923. Each stage is defined by the erogenous zone through which the libido, the energy of the sexual instinct, seeks pleasure, and by a conflict that must be resolved; too much frustration or indulgence leaves a fixation that shapes adult personality. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth: sucking, biting and feeding. The anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years) centres on the anus, and its key conflict is toilet training. The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals. The child develops sexual feelings toward the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent: the Oedipus complex in boys, with castration anxiety, and in girls what Carl Jung (1913) called the Electra complex, a term Freud rejected, with penis envy. The complex is resolved by repressing the incestuous wishes and identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego and gender identity. The latency stage (about 6 years to puberty) is a quiet period in which sexual impulses are repressed and sublimated into schoolwork, friendships and play. The genital stage, from puberty, brings mature sexuality directed at partners outside the family. Exam variants match a stage to its zone, its conflict or its adult fixation, such as the oral character or the anal-retentive personality. Option c is right because the phallic stage is when incestuous Oedipal fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent arise.","traps":{"a":"The stage in which the mouth is the main erogenous zone is the oral stage, from birth to about 18 months, when pleasure comes from sucking, biting and feeding. Fixation here is linked with dependency and with oral habits such as smoking or overeating. It precedes the phallic stage by several years. It tempts because it is the first and most familiar stage, so candidates who only remember the stage list may guess it.","b":"Conflict over toilet training is the defining event of the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, when pleasure centres on retaining or expelling faeces. Harsh or lax training was thought to produce the anal-retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal-expulsive (messy, careless) character. It tempts because it is the stage immediately before the phallic stage, and both involve parental control and conflict.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct describes the latency stage, from about 6 years to puberty, when sexual impulses are repressed and channelled into school learning, hobbies and same-sex friendships. Sublimation is the defence mechanism that redirects drives into socially valued activity. It tempts because the latency stage follows directly from the resolution of the phallic stage, so the two are easily confused."}},{"q":"What are the two parts of the superego, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Sigmund Freud (1923) introduced the structural model of the mind in The Ego and the Id. The id, present at birth, is the reservoir of instinctual drives; it operates on the pleasure principle and seeks immediate gratification through primary process thinking. The ego develops to deal with reality; it operates on the reality principle and uses secondary process thinking, delaying gratification until a realistic outlet is found. The superego is the last structure to form, emerging at about age 5 to 6 as the child resolves the Oedipus complex by identifying with the same-sex parent and internalising parental and cultural standards. Freud described two subsystems of the superego. The conscience contains the prohibitions learned through punishment: what we must not do. When we violate it we feel guilt. The ego-ideal contains the positive standards learned through praise and reward: what we should strive to be. Living up to it brings pride and raised self-esteem. A harsh superego can be as troublesome as a strong id, producing excessive guilt and perfectionism, and the ego must mediate between id, superego and reality, using defence mechanisms when anxiety rises. Examiners build variants by asking which principle governs each structure, which structure forms last, or which part of the superego produces guilt. Remember the mapping: punishment builds the conscience and guilt; praise builds the ego-ideal and pride. Option d is right because Freud divided the superego into the conscience and the ego-ideal.","traps":{"a":"The id and the ego are the other two structures of Freud's (1923) tripartite model, not parts of the superego. The id is the inborn source of drives and the ego is the reality-oriented mediator; the superego stands beside them as the third structure. It tempts because the three are always taught together, so a candidate who remembers the model but not the superego's subdivisions picks the familiar pair.","b":"The pleasure principle and the reality principle are the operating rules of the id and the ego respectively, described by Sigmund Freud (1911): the id demands immediate gratification, while the ego delays it until reality allows. The superego follows neither; it operates on moral standards. It tempts because 'principle' sounds like a moral term, and these are among Freud's best known paired concepts.","c":"The ego and the unconscious mix Freud's structural model with his earlier topographic model of conscious, preconscious and unconscious. The ego is a separate structure, and the unconscious is a level of awareness, not a part of the superego. Large parts of both the ego and the superego themselves operate unconsciously. It tempts because the superego does work largely outside awareness, so 'unconscious' sounds plausibly related."}},{"q":"What is the anima, according to Jung's theory of archetypes?","concept":"Carl Gustav Jung, the Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, divided the psyche into the conscious ego, the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious. The collective unconscious is inherited and shared by all humans; it contains archetypes, universal predispositions to perceive and respond in certain ways, which appear as recurring images in myths, dreams, religions and art. The persona is the mask or socially acceptable front we present to others, named after the masks worn by actors in Roman theatre. The shadow is the dark, primitive and socially unacceptable side of personality, which we tend to deny and project onto others. The anima is the feminine side of the male psyche, and the animus is the masculine side of the female psyche. Jung held that each sex carries psychological qualities of the other, partly from the species' long experience of living with the other sex, and that a man projects his anima onto the women he becomes infatuated with. Other archetypes include the great mother, the wise old man, the hero, and the self, which represents wholeness and the drive toward unity and is symbolised by the mandala. Integrating these opposites is part of individuation, the lifelong process of becoming a complete self. Exam variants ask which archetype is the mask, the dark side, the feminine element in men or the masculine element in women. Option b is right because the anima is the female element of a man.","traps":{"a":"The male element of a woman is the animus, the counterpart of the anima in Carl Jung's theory of archetypes. It represents masculine qualities such as reason and assertiveness in the female psyche and shapes how a woman relates to men. It tempts because anima and animus look almost identical and are always taught as a pair, so swapping them is the most common error on this question.","c":"The socially acceptable front that we present to others is the persona, named after the mask of the Roman actor. Jung saw it as necessary for social life but harmful when a person over-identifies with the role and loses touch with the real self. It is an archetype, but it concerns public image, not the opposite sex. It tempts because the persona is the first archetype listed in most textbooks.","d":"The dark and unacceptable side of personality is the shadow, which holds primitive, animal instincts and qualities the ego refuses to acknowledge. Jung said we project the shadow onto others, for example onto disliked people or groups. It tempts because the anima is also hidden and unconscious, and all three archetypes in these options come from the same list, so a vague memory of the list is not enough."}},{"q":"What are the two attitudes in Jung's theory of personality?","concept":"In Psychological Types (1921), Carl Gustav Jung proposed a typology built from two attitudes and four functions. The attitudes describe the direction in which psychic energy (libido) flows. In extraversion it flows outward, toward people, objects and events; in introversion it flows inward, toward thoughts, feelings and the subjective world. Jung held that everyone has both attitudes, but one is dominant and conscious while the other is less developed and lies in the unconscious. The four functions describe how we take in and process experience. Thinking and feeling are the rational, or judging, functions: thinking evaluates by logic and truth, feeling by value, like or dislike. Sensing and intuiting are the irrational, or perceiving, functions: sensing registers what is actually present through the senses, while intuition grasps possibilities and hunches beyond the data. Combining the two attitudes with the four functions gives eight psychological types, such as the extraverted thinking type. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, developed by Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers from the 1940s, built on Jung and added a judging versus perceiving scale. Hans Eysenck (1947) later treated extraversion and introversion as a continuous trait dimension with a biological basis, and the dimension survives today in the Big Five. Examiners build variants by asking which pair are the rational functions, which are irrational, or what an attitude means. Option c is right because the two attitudes in Jung's theory are extraversion and introversion.","traps":{"a":"Rational and irrational are not attitudes but Jung's labels for groups of functions. Thinking and feeling are rational because they make judgements; sensing and intuiting are irrational because they simply perceive without evaluating. Irrational here means non-judging, not illogical. It tempts because it is a genuine Jungian pair from the same theory and sounds like a basic orientation of personality.","b":"Sensing and intuiting are two of Jung's four functions, the irrational or perceiving pair. Sensing takes in concrete facts through the senses, while intuition perceives possibilities and patterns. They describe how information is gathered, not the direction in which psychic energy flows. It tempts because these terms are familiar from the Myers-Briggs S and N scales and appear in the same chapter as the attitudes.","d":"Thinking and feeling are the rational, or judging, functions in Jung's (1921) typology: thinking judges by logic, feeling by personal value. They are functions, not attitudes, and each can combine with either attitude, giving types such as the extraverted thinker or the introverted feeler. It tempts because thinking versus feeling is a well known Myers-Briggs scale and looks like a fundamental personality split."}},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of Erikson's stages of psychosocial development?","concept":"Erik H. Erikson (1950) described eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan in Childhood and Society. Each stage is a crisis between two opposite tendencies, and a favourable balance yields a basic strength or virtue. The order matters, because each stage builds on those before it, following the epigenetic principle. Learn the full list with ages and virtues. First, basic trust versus mistrust (birth to about 1 year): hope. Second, autonomy versus shame and doubt (1 to 3 years): will. Third, initiative versus guilt (3 to 6 years): purpose. Fourth, industry versus inferiority (6 years to puberty): competence. Fifth, identity versus role confusion (adolescence): fidelity. Sixth, intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood): love. Seventh, generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood): care. Eighth, ego integrity versus despair (late adulthood): wisdom. The first four correspond roughly to Freud's oral, anal, phallic and latency stages and the fifth to the genital stage, but Erikson stressed social relationships and development after adolescence, which Freud did not. James Marcia (1966) later expanded the fifth stage into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Sequence questions list four stages and ask which list is in the right order; the safe method is to number each stage and check that the numbers rise. Here every list rises, so the item is flawed, and only the keyed list starts at stage 1 and includes its immediate successor. Option a is right because it lists trust, autonomy, identity and generativity in correct chronological order, beginning with the first stage.","traps":{"b":"This list begins with autonomy versus shame (stage 2), then industry versus inferiority (4), identity versus role confusion (5) and generativity versus stagnation (7). The numbers rise, so strictly it is also in correct order; the key rejects it presumably because it omits the first stage, trust versus mistrust. It tempts because it shares three stages with the keyed list and looks equally orderly.","c":"Initiative versus guilt (stage 3, about 3 to 6 years), industry versus inferiority (4), identity versus role confusion (5) and ego integrity versus despair (8) also run in rising order, so this list is not actually out of sequence. The key treats it as wrong, apparently because it starts midway rather than in infancy. It tempts because it spans early childhood to old age.","d":"Trust versus mistrust (stage 1), initiative versus guilt (3), intimacy versus isolation (6) and ego integrity versus despair (8) are also in rising order and even begin with the first stage. The key rejects it, perhaps because it skips autonomy, the stage that immediately follows trust. Strictly it is as correct as the keyed answer. It tempts because it opens with the first stage and ends with the last."}},{"q":"What are the four processes involved in observational learning, according to Bandura's social learning theory?","concept":"Observational learning, also called modelling or vicarious learning, is learning by watching others. Albert Bandura set it out in Social Learning Theory (1977), building on the Bobo doll experiments of Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), in which children who watched an adult hit and kick an inflatable doll later imitated the same aggressive acts. Bandura identified four sub-processes that must all occur. Attentional processes: the observer must notice the model's behaviour; attention is greater for models who are attractive, powerful, similar to us or rewarded. Retention processes: the behaviour must be stored in memory as images or verbal codes, often helped by mental rehearsal. Production (motor reproduction) processes: the observer must be physically and cognitively able to turn the stored representation into action, refining it through feedback. Motivational processes: the observer must have a reason to perform, which comes from external reinforcement, vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model rewarded) or self-reinforcement. Bandura (1965) separated learning from performance: children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet reproduced the aggression readily when offered a reward, showing they had learned it. Neighbouring ideas: self-efficacy (Bandura, 1977), the belief that one can perform a behaviour; reciprocal determinism, the mutual influence of person, behaviour and environment; and the later renaming of the approach as social cognitive theory (1986). Contrast classical and operant conditioning, which require direct experience of stimuli or consequences. Option b is right because Bandura's four processes are attentional, retention, production and motivational.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, perceptual, cognitive and affective are broad levels of psychological functioning, roughly the route from receiving input to interpreting it, thinking about it and feeling about it. They are not Bandura's named sub-processes, though attention and retention involve perception and cognition. It tempts because the list sounds like a sensible account of how we take in a model's behaviour, and it has four items like the answer.","c":"Encoding, storage and retrieval are the three stages of memory in information-processing models such as that of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), with evaluation added as filler. They describe only what happens inside Bandura's retention stage, not the whole sequence from attention to motivation. It tempts because retention is one of the four processes and memory vocabulary sounds technical and scientific.","d":"Stimulus, response, reinforcement and extinction are the vocabulary of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) and operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938), where learning depends on direct experience of stimuli and consequences. Bandura's point was that people can learn without performing a response or being reinforced themselves. It tempts because reinforcement also appears in Bandura's motivational stage, and both theories sit in the learning chapter."}},{"q":"What are cardinal traits in Allport's theory?","concept":"Gordon W. Allport (1937) built the first major trait theory in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation. With Henry Odbert (1936) he combed an unabridged English dictionary and found about 18,000 words describing personal characteristics, the starting point of the lexical approach. Allport distinguished common traits, shared by many people in a culture and useful for comparing individuals, from personal dispositions, the traits unique to one individual. He ranked personal dispositions by how pervasive they are. A cardinal trait is so dominant that it shapes almost everything a person does; few people have one, and those who do may become known by it, as when we call someone Machiavellian or Christ-like. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding characteristics you would mention in a letter of recommendation, such as honest, warm or ambitious. Secondary traits are the least important, appearing only in certain situations and inconsistently, such as a preference for a type of food or impatience in queues. Allport championed the idiographic approach, the intensive study of one individual (as in his case study Letters from Jenny), against the nomothetic approach of comparing many people on common dimensions. He also proposed the functional autonomy of motives: adult motives can become independent of the childhood needs from which they arose. Later trait theorists, Raymond B. Cattell (1949) and Hans Eysenck (1947), used factor analysis to reduce traits to a few dimensions, leading eventually to the Big Five. Option c is right because cardinal traits are the most pervasive and powerful traits, dominating the whole of a person's life.","traps":{"a":"The least important traits, displayed inconspicuously and inconsistently, are Allport's secondary traits, such as liking spicy food or becoming impatient only in queues. They appear only in specific situations and tell us little about the whole person, the opposite end of the hierarchy from cardinal traits. It tempts because it is a genuine level of Allport's hierarchy, so it looks familiar to anyone who remembers the three tiers.","b":"The handful of outstanding traits that describe a person's behaviour are central traits, the five to ten characteristics a friend would list in a letter of recommendation. Everyone has central traits, whereas few people have a cardinal trait. It tempts strongly because 'outstanding' sounds like 'most powerful', and central traits are the middle tier that candidates most often confuse with cardinal traits.","d":"Traits shared by a number of people, such as the members of a culture, are Allport's common traits. They allow people to be compared on the same dimension, for example assertiveness, and fit the nomothetic approach. Allport contrasted them with personal dispositions, the unique traits of one person, which are then ranked as cardinal, central or secondary. It tempts because common traits appear in the same section."}},{"q":"What are L-data in Cattell's theory?","concept":"Raymond B. Cattell, whose Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) appeared in 1949, built one of the most systematic trait theories using factor analysis, a statistical method that groups correlated variables into a smaller number of underlying factors. He argued that a theory of personality must be checked against three independent kinds of data, remembered as L, Q and T. L-data (life-record data) are information about a person's actual behaviour in everyday life, taken from records or from ratings by people who know the person well: school grades and absences, traffic offences, job history, or a teacher's ratings. Q-data (questionnaire data) are self-reports, in which people describe themselves on inventories such as the 16PF. T-data (objective test data) come from placing a person in a controlled, standardised situation and recording responses they cannot easily fake, such as reaction times or physiological measures. Cattell factor-analysed all three sources and found 16 source traits, the underlying stable structures (for example warmth, reasoning, emotional stability and dominance), which he contrasted with surface traits, clusters of observable behaviour that appear together. He also separated ability, temperament and dynamic (motivational) traits, and divided intelligence into fluid and crystallised forms (1963). His starting list of trait words came from the lexical work of Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936). Examiners usually test the letter codes, so remember: L for life record, Q for questionnaire, T for test. Option c is right because L-data are information gathered about a person's life, such as from school records.","traps":{"a":"Data gathered from self-reports and questionnaires are Q-data in Cattell's scheme, collected with inventories such as the 16PF (1949). They rely on people describing themselves, so they can be distorted by poor self-insight or by faking good. It tempts because questionnaires are the most familiar way of measuring personality, and the 16PF is Cattell's best known instrument, so candidates assume his data must be questionnaire data.","b":"Data collected by placing a person in a controlled test situation and noting or rating responses are T-data, objective test data. Examples include reaction time, physiological responses or behaviour in a miniature situation, where the person cannot easily tell what is being measured and so cannot fake. It tempts because L could be misread as laboratory, and this option describes the most scientific-sounding data source.","d":"Cattell's 16 basic personality traits are his source traits, the underlying factors found by factor analysis, such as warmth, dominance and emotional stability, measured by the 16PF. They are the results of the analysis, not one of the three kinds of data on which it is based. It tempts because the number 16 is the most memorable fact about Cattell, so any option mentioning it looks safe."}},{"q":"What is the need for affiliation (n Aff) in Murray's theory?","concept":"Henry A. Murray (1938) set out his theory, which he called personology, in Explorations in Personality, written with colleagues at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. He defined a need as a force in the brain that organises perception, thinking and action so as to change an unsatisfying situation. He distinguished viscerogenic (primary, physiological) needs such as food and water from psychogenic (secondary, psychological) needs, and listed about twenty psychogenic needs, each written with n for need. Examples: n Affiliation (to draw near to and enjoy cooperation with others, to form friendships and remain loyal), n Achievement (to accomplish something difficult, to master and to excel), n Dominance (to control and influence others), n Exhibition (to make an impression, to be seen and heard, to excite and amuse), n Recognition (to gain approval and social status), n Autonomy, n Aggression, n Nurturance and n Order. Needs interact with press, the environmental forces that help or hinder need satisfaction, and a need-press combination is called a thema. To measure needs, Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) developed the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures and project their needs into them. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT scoring to study n Achievement in depth, and his tradition focused on three needs: achievement, affiliation and power. Variants ask you to match a need to its description, or to separate needs from press. Option a is right because n Affiliation is the need for social interaction, friendship and companionship.","traps":{"b":"The need for power and control over others corresponds to Murray's n Dominance, the need to control, influence and direct others. David McClelland (1953) and his followers later studied it as the need for power (n Pow) in leadership. It is about controlling people, whereas affiliation is about being close to them. It tempts because affiliation, achievement and power form McClelland's famous trio, so the three are easily swapped.","c":"The need for recognition and success corresponds to two other Murray needs: n Achievement, the drive to accomplish difficult tasks and excel, and n Recognition, the desire for praise and social status. Both concern accomplishment and esteem, not warm relationships. It tempts because n Achievement is the most studied need in Murray's list, thanks to David McClelland (1953), and is often the first that candidates recall.","d":"The need for attention and excitement resembles Murray's n Exhibition, the need to make an impression, to be seen and heard, and to excite or amuse others, along with n Play. Exhibition uses people as an audience rather than seeking companionship with them. It tempts because attention-seeking involves other people, so it sounds social, like affiliation, even though the underlying motive is different."}},{"q":"What are the five needs in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, arranged in order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top?","concept":"Abraham H. Maslow (1943) proposed the hierarchy of needs in A Theory of Human Motivation and developed it in Motivation and Personality (1954). Needs are arranged by prepotency: lower needs are stronger and more urgent, so they dominate behaviour until they are reasonably satisfied, and only then do higher needs emerge. From the bottom: physiological needs (food, water, air, sleep, sex); safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear and danger); belongingness and love needs (affection, friendship, family, acceptance by a group); esteem needs (self-respect, competence and achievement, plus respect and recognition from others); and self-actualisation (becoming everything one is capable of becoming). Maslow called the first four deficiency needs (D-needs), because their absence creates tension, and self-actualisation a growth or being need (B-need). He studied people he considered self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described traits such as acceptance of self and others, spontaneity, autonomy and peak experiences. He did not insist that a lower need be completely satisfied; partial satisfaction is enough, and the order can vary for some people. Later he wrote of self-transcendence beyond self-actualisation. Neighbouring theories used in variants: Clayton Alderfer's (1969) ERG theory, which compresses the levels into existence, relatedness and growth and allows regression to lower needs; and Frederick Herzberg's (1959) two-factor theory of hygiene factors and motivators. The familiar pyramid drawing was popularised by others, not by Maslow himself. Option b is right because physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem and self-actualisation is the order from the strongest base to the weakest top.","traps":{"a":"Esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological, then self-actualisation scrambles the order: it runs the bottom four levels downward and then jumps to the top. In Maslow's (1943) hierarchy the base must be physiological needs, because they are the most prepotent. It tempts because it contains all five correct labels, so a candidate checking only for the right names, not the sequence, may choose it.","c":"Safety, physiological, esteem, belongingness and love, self-actualisation swaps two adjacent pairs: safety is placed below physiological needs, and esteem below belongingness. Maslow put physiological needs at the bottom because hunger and thirst override even safety, and he put love needs before esteem. It tempts because each swap is small and the first and last items look nearly right.","d":"Self-actualisation, esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological is the correct hierarchy read in reverse, from the top of the pyramid downward. The stem asks for the order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top, so the list must start with physiological needs. It tempts because the levels and their sequence are right, so only careful reading of the direction separates it from the answer."}},{"q":"What is learned helplessness, according to Seligman?","concept":"Learned helplessness was described by Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier (1967) in experiments with dogs. In a triadic design, one group received electric shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group received identical shocks they could not control, and a third group received no shock. Later all dogs were placed in a shuttle box where they could escape shock simply by jumping a low barrier. Dogs from the escapable and no-shock groups learned to escape quickly; most dogs from the inescapable group lay down and passively accepted the shocks. The key factor was not the shock but its uncontrollability: the animals had learned that their responses made no difference to outcomes. Seligman described three deficits: motivational (no attempt to escape), cognitive (difficulty learning that responses now work) and emotional (passivity and apparent distress). Donald Hiroto and Seligman (1975) produced similar effects in humans with uncontrollable noise and unsolvable problems. Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated the theory in attributional terms: helplessness and depression follow when people explain bad events as internal, stable and global. This fed into the hopelessness theory of depression and Seligman's later work on explanatory style and learned optimism. Neighbouring concepts used as distractors are escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus), avoidance learning (preventing it) and pain-elicited aggression. Maier and Seligman (2016) later revised the account, arguing that passivity is the default response and that what is learned is control. Option a is right because learned helplessness is the acceptance of avoidable punishment after repeated exposure to unavoidable punishment.","traps":{"b":"Learning to escape punishment through trial and error is escape learning, the kind of instrumental learning Edward Thorndike (1898) observed when cats tried responses until one opened the puzzle box. In Seligman's study, dogs given escapable shock did exactly this. Helpless dogs failed to do it, which is the whole point. It tempts because the shuttle-box procedure involves escape, so the vocabulary overlaps with the correct answer.","c":"Learning to avoid punishment through reinforcement is avoidance learning, in which a warning signal allows the animal to act before the aversive event, and the response is maintained by negative reinforcement. O. Hobart Mowrer (1947) explained it with his two-factor theory. Helpless animals show the opposite: they fail to act even when avoidance is possible. It tempts because the same shuttle-box apparatus was used in avoidance research.","d":"Becoming aggressive in response to punishment is pain-elicited aggression, studied by Roger Ulrich and Nathan Azrin (1962), who found that rats given shocks attacked each other. It also relates to the frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939). Learned helplessness produces passivity, not active attack. It tempts because both concern reactions to aversive stimulation, and both have been linked to clinical problems."}},{"q":"At which level of moral reasoning do people act under external controls and obey rules to avoid punishment or reap rewards?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) extended Jean Piaget's (1932) work on moral judgement by presenting boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the yes or no decision, and proposed three levels with two stages each. Level I, preconventional morality (typical of children aged about 4 to 10): people act under external controls, obeying rules to avoid punishment or gain rewards. Stage 1 is orientation toward punishment and obedience; Stage 2 is instrumental purpose and exchange, as in you scratch my back and I scratch yours. Level II, conventional morality (from about age 10, often lasting into adulthood): people have internalised the standards of authority figures. Stage 3 is maintaining mutual relations and the approval of others; Stage 4 is social concern and conscience, or law and order. Level III, postconventional morality (adolescence onward, if ever): people judge by abstract principles. Stage 5 is morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law; Stage 6 is morality of universal ethical principles. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory for neglecting an ethic of care, and later research found Stage 6 very rare. Variants describe a motive and ask for its level or stage; external rewards and punishments always point to Level I. Option a is right because obeying rules under external control to avoid punishment or gain reward defines Level I, preconventional morality.","traps":{"b":"Level II, conventional morality, describes people who have internalised the standards of authority figures. They follow rules because they want approval (Stage 3) or because doing one's duty and maintaining social order matter (Stage 4), not merely to avoid punishment. It tempts because conventional reasoners also obey rules, so the word 'obey' in the stem can mislead a candidate who ignores the phrase 'external controls'.","c":"Level III, postconventional morality, is reasoning by self-chosen principles: social contract and individual rights (Stage 5) and universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity (Stage 6). A postconventional person may break a law that violates these principles, the opposite of obeying for reward or punishment. It tempts only because it is a genuine Kohlberg level and sounds the most advanced.","d":"None of the above would be correct only if no option described Kohlberg's level for externally controlled, punishment-avoiding and reward-seeking morality. Kohlberg named that level explicitly as Level I, preconventional morality, so a correct option is present. It tempts candidates who confuse Kohlberg with Piaget's (1932) heteronomous morality and therefore doubt that any listed Kohlberg level fits."}},{"q":"At which stage of Level II of Kohlberg’s moral development are people concerned with doing their duty, showing respect for higher authority, and maintaining the social order?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg built his theory of moral development from his 1958 doctoral dissertation, extending the two-stage account in The Moral Judgment of the Child by Jean Piaget (1932). He gave boys moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma (should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife?) and scored the reasoning, not the yes or no answer. He described three levels, each with two stages, in an invariant sequence. Level I, preconventional: Stage 1, orientation toward punishment and obedience; Stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange. Level II, conventional, where people have internalised the standards of authority figures and society: Stage 3, maintaining mutual relations, approval of others and the golden rule, often called the good boy, good girl orientation; Stage 4, social concern and conscience, also called the law-and-order orientation, in which the person values doing one's duty, respecting higher authority and maintaining the social order, because laws hold society together. Level III, postconventional: Stage 5, morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law; Stage 6, morality of universal ethical principles. Most adolescents and adults reason at Stages 3 and 4; Stage 6 is rare. The option labels here follow the Human Development textbook of Diane E. Papalia and colleagues. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the scheme as biased toward a male ethic of justice and proposed an ethic of care. Option b is right because doing one's duty, respecting authority and maintaining social order define Stage 4, social concern and conscience, the second stage of Level II.","traps":{"a":"Maintaining mutual relations, approval of others and the golden rule describe Stage 3, the first stage of Level II, often called the good boy, good girl orientation. People here want to be seen as good by those close to them and judge acts by intentions and loyalty. It tempts because it is also conventional (Level II), so a candidate who knows the level but not the stage picks it.","c":"Morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law is Stage 5, the first stage of Level III (postconventional). People see laws as social agreements that can be changed democratically when they fail to protect rights and welfare. It tempts because it also mentions law, but Stage 4 obeys law to keep order, while Stage 5 treats law as a changeable contract serving people.","d":"Morality of universal ethical principles is Stage 6, the highest stage of Level III (postconventional). The person follows self-chosen principles of justice, equality and human dignity, even against the law. Kohlberg found it rare and later dropped it from his standard scoring. It tempts because 'conscience' in the correct option sounds like inner principle, which candidates link to the highest stage."}},{"q":"How does Vygotsky's theory differ from Piaget's theory of cognitive development?","concept":"Jean Piaget and Lev Vygotsky are the two classic theorists of cognitive development. Piaget, whose The Origins of Intelligence in Children first appeared in 1936, saw the child as a lone scientist who builds knowledge by acting on the physical world. Through assimilation, accommodation and equilibration, children pass through four stages in a fixed order: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onward). The stages describe general cognitive structures he believed appear in children everywhere. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist who died in 1934, proposed a sociocultural theory, best known in English through Mind in Society (1978). He argued that higher mental functions begin on the social plane, in interaction with parents, teachers and more skilled peers, and are then internalised. What children learn, and the tools they think with, such as language, counting systems and literacy, are shaped by their culture, so many cognitive skills are culture-specific rather than universal. His key ideas include the zone of proximal development, scaffolding and private speech. On private speech they disagreed directly: Piaget saw it as egocentric speech that fades, while Vygotsky saw it as self-guiding speech that turns into inner speech. Both were interactionists: Piaget stressed maturation plus experience, and Vygotsky wove a natural (biological) line of development with a cultural line. Neither held a strictly predetermined or solely environmental view. Option c is right because Vygotsky held that skills learned through interaction with more competent people are often culture-specific, while Piaget proposed universal cognitive structures.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the theorists. The claim that all children pass through the same stages in the same order belongs to Jean Piaget, with his four universal stages from sensorimotor to formal operational. Lev Vygotsky proposed no fixed universal stage sequence and stressed that development varies with culture and social interaction. It tempts because candidates remember that one theorist proposed universal stages but attach the claim to the wrong name.","b":"Lev Vygotsky did not think development was solely environmental. He described a natural, biological line of development interwoven with a cultural line, and saw the child as an active participant in social exchanges. Jean Piaget also combined maturation with experience. So this option misstates Vygotsky and blurs the real contrast. It tempts because Vygotsky is labelled sociocultural, which candidates overextend to mean environment only.","d":"Neither theorist saw development as purely predetermined. Lev Vygotsky saw it as shaped by social interaction and cultural tools, the opposite of a preset course. Jean Piaget's fixed stage order is the closer fit to 'predetermined', though he too gave experience a central role. The option therefore inverts the two. It tempts because both theorists discussed maturation and experience, so the terms sound plausible for either."}},{"q":"What is the zone of proximal development (ZPD)?","concept":"The zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the central idea in the sociocultural theory of Lev Vygotsky (1978). He defined it as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by what the child can do alone, and the level of potential development, shown by what the child can do under adult guidance or with more capable peers. Tasks below the zone are already mastered and need no help; tasks above it are too hard even with help; tasks inside it are too hard to manage alone but achievable with support. Vygotsky argued that instruction should target this zone, because learning that runs slightly ahead of development pulls development forward: what a child can do with help today, the child can do alone tomorrow. He also noted that two children with the same test score may have zones of different width, so the zone reveals learning potential that static tests miss. Related terms: scaffolding, introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976), means adjusting help to the learner's level and withdrawing it as competence grows; the helper is often called the more knowledgeable other; guided participation, from Barbara Rogoff (1990), extends the idea to everyday shared activity. In assessment the ZPD underpins dynamic assessment, such as the test-teach-retest approach of Reuven Feuerstein (1979). Contrast Jean Piaget (1936), who stressed readiness and the child's own discovery. Option b is right because the ZPD is the range of tasks too complex to master alone but achievable with guidance and encouragement from a more skilful partner.","traps":{"a":"Tasks simple enough to need no help lie below the ZPD, at the child's actual developmental level, the zone of independent performance. These skills are already mastered, so teaching aimed here produces no new development. The wording 'too simple to be mastered alone' is also self-contradictory. It tempts because it mentions doing tasks alone, which is part of the ZPD definition, and a hurried reader may miss 'simple'.","c":"Tasks too complex even with guidance lie above the ZPD, beyond the child's level of potential development. Help at this point cannot produce learning, because the child lacks the prerequisite skills to use it; frustration follows rather than progress. It tempts because it shares the phrase 'too complex to be mastered' and mentions a more skilful partner, so only the word 'even' separates it from the correct definition.","d":"Learning through experience rather than instruction is closer to the view of Jean Piaget, who saw children as independent explorers constructing knowledge through their own activity, and to discovery learning in general. Vygotsky's ZPD is defined by assisted performance with a more capable partner, the opposite emphasis. It tempts because both theorists are constructivists and the option reads like a harmless general statement about learning."}},{"q":"What is the first grasp that infants develop?","concept":"Fine motor development in infancy is mainly the story of reaching and grasping, and it follows the proximodistal trend: control spreads from the trunk and shoulder outward to the hands and fingers, and within the hand from the little-finger (ulnar) side to the thumb (radial) side. Newborns show the palmar grasp reflex, an automatic closing of the fingers when the palm is stroked; it is a primitive reflex, not a voluntary grasp, and fades over the first few months. When true reaching appears around 3 to 4 months, the first voluntary grip is the ulnar grasp: a clumsy, claw-like movement in which the fingers close against the palm, with the object held on the little-finger side and the thumb hardly used. Soon infants adjust their grip to an object's size and shape and begin passing objects from hand to hand. Grasping then shifts toward the thumb side, through palmar, radial-palmar and radial-digital grips, until around the end of the first year the pincer grasp appears: thumb and index finger meet to pick up tiny objects such as a raisin. Henry M. Halverson (1931) mapped this sequence from film records of infants, and child development textbooks such as Laura E. Berk's summarise it. Variants ask which grasp comes last (pincer), which is a reflex (the palmar grasp reflex), or which principle explains the order (proximodistal). Option c is right because the ulnar grasp is the first voluntary grasp infants develop, appearing once reaching begins and well before the pincer grasp.","traps":{"a":"The palmar grasp first appears as a newborn reflex: the fingers close automatically around anything that touches the palm. As a reflex it is not counted as a developed, voluntary grasp, and the voluntary whole-hand palmar grip comes after the ulnar grasp. It tempts because the palmar grasp reflex is present at birth, so a candidate thinking of reflexes may call it the first grasp.","b":"The pincer grasp is the precise grip in which the tip of the thumb meets the tip of the index finger, used to pick up small objects such as a raisin or a crumb. It appears around the end of the first year and is the last of the infant grasps, not the first. It tempts because it is the best-known grasp and the one most often named in milestone charts.","d":"The radial grasp uses the thumb side of the hand, with the thumb starting to oppose the fingers, in the second half of the first year. It comes after the ulnar grasp, because control within the hand develops from the little-finger side toward the thumb side. It tempts because ulnar and radial are paired anatomical terms, and a candidate unsure of the direction may reverse them."}},{"q":"What is the clumsy, claw-like grip that that permits little tactile exploration?","concept":"Infant grasping develops in a predictable sequence. At birth the palmar grasp reflex closes the fingers automatically around anything placed in the palm; it fades in the early months and gives way to voluntary grasping once true reaching begins around 3 to 4 months. The first voluntary grip is the ulnar grasp, named after the ulna, the forearm bone on the little-finger side. It is a clumsy, claw-like motion in which the fingers close against the palm and the thumb plays little part. Because the object is clamped against the palm rather than held at the fingertips, the infant can do little tactile exploration of its texture or shape; the grip mainly serves to hold objects and bring them to the mouth. Over the next months control shifts toward the thumb (radial) side, through radial-palmar and radial-digital grips, as the thumb begins to oppose the fingers. Around 4 to 5 months infants also transfer objects between hands, and by the end of the first year they achieve the pincer grasp, using thumb and index finger to pick up tiny objects with fine control and to explore them in detail. The order reflects the proximodistal principle. The dynamic systems theory of Esther Thelen (1995) adds that each new motor skill is assembled by the infant from maturation, body growth, motivation and practice, rather than simply unfolding. Option b is right because the ulnar grasp is the clumsy, claw-like grip, with fingers closing against the palm, that permits little tactile exploration.","traps":{"a":"The pincer grasp is the mature precision grip in which the thumb and index finger meet, appearing around the end of the first year. It allows fine manipulation and detailed exploration of small objects, the opposite of a clumsy grip that permits little exploration. It tempts because 'pincer' and 'claw' both suggest a gripping animal limb, and pincer is the best-known grasp term.","c":"The radial grasp uses the thumb side of the hand, with the thumb beginning to oppose the fingers, and emerges after the ulnar grasp in the second half of the first year. It is more controlled than the claw-like ulnar grip and allows more handling of objects. It tempts because ulnar and radial are paired anatomical terms, and candidates unsure which side comes first may swap them.","d":"Digital grasps use the fingertips rather than the palm. The radial-digital grasp appears late in the first year, and the digital pronate grasp is a toddler's way of holding a crayon. Both give more fingertip control than the ulnar grasp, not less. It tempts because 'digital' can sound like crude clawing with the fingers, but fingertip grips are actually the more refined ones."}},{"q":"What is the language-making capacity (LMC) proposed by Dan Slobin?","concept":"How children acquire grammar so quickly is explained by competing theories. B. F. Skinner (1957), in Verbal Behavior, treated language as behaviour learned through reinforcement and imitation. Noam Chomsky (1959) attacked that view and argued that children are born with a language acquisition device (LAD), an inborn linguistic processor activated by verbal input, containing universal grammar, knowledge of rules common to all languages. Hearing speech simply tunes that built-in grammar, which explains why children produce forms they never heard, such as the overregularisation 'goed'. Dan Slobin offered a softer nativism. From research on children learning very different languages, he proposed operating principles (1973) and later the language-making capacity (LMC), set out in his 1985 cross-linguistic volumes. The LMC is a set of inborn cognitive and perceptual abilities that are highly specialised for language learning: strategies for analysing speech, such as paying attention to the ends of words, noticing word order and avoiding exceptions. Unlike the LAD, the LMC contains no ready-made grammatical rules; children use these strategies to work out the grammar of whatever language they hear. Neighbouring ideas for variant questions: the language acquisition support system of Jerome Bruner (1983), the social scaffolding caregivers provide; the critical period hypothesis of Eric Lenneberg (1967); and interactionist views that combine biology with input such as child-directed speech. Option c is right because Slobin defined the language-making capacity as a set of cognitive and perceptual abilities that are highly specialised for language learning, not as built-in grammatical knowledge.","traps":{"a":"An inborn linguistic processor activated by verbal input is the textbook definition of the language acquisition device (LAD) of Noam Chomsky, who argued against B. F. Skinner (1957) that grammar is too complex to learn by reinforcement alone. Slobin's capacity is also inborn, but it is a set of analysing strategies, not a dedicated grammar processor. It tempts because both ideas are nativist and both are described as inborn.","b":"Knowledge of rules common to all languages is universal grammar, the content Noam Chomsky placed inside the language acquisition device. It claims children are born knowing the general principles of grammar and need input only to set language-specific parameters. Slobin rejected built-in rules and proposed general operating principles for discovering them. It tempts because Slobin also studied universals through cross-linguistic research.","d":"'All of the above' would require Slobin's capacity to be Chomsky's inborn processor and universal grammar as well as specialised abilities. Options a and b describe Chomsky's language acquisition device and universal grammar, which Slobin deliberately softened into learning strategies rather than innate rules. It tempts because all three options sound nativist and Slobin is often taught as a modified LAD theorist."}},{"q":"What is the receptive-expressive gap ?","concept":"Language has two sides. Receptive language is comprehension: understanding words, sentences and gestures. Expressive (productive) language is producing them in speech, sign or writing. In development comprehension comes first, and the lag between the two is the receptive-expressive gap. Infants respond to their own name by about 6 months and understand some familiar words before their first birthday, but usually say their first words only around 12 months. Helen Benedict (1979) followed children through the one-word period and found they understood about 50 words roughly five months before they could say 50. Expressive vocabulary then accelerates in the vocabulary spurt around 18 months, followed by two-word telegraphic speech, described by Roger Brown (1973). The gap never fully closes: adults also recognise far more words than they use. One reason is that understanding needs only recognition of a stored word, while speaking requires recall plus motor planning of the vocal tract. The concept has clinical uses. Receptive vocabulary is measured by pointing tasks such as the Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test. A late talker who understands well differs from a child whose comprehension is also delayed; DSM-5 (2013) groups both under language disorder, replacing the older expressive and mixed receptive-expressive types. In adults, expressive aphasia, described by Paul Broca (1861), gives halting speech with fair comprehension, while receptive aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), gives fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension. Option a is right because the receptive-expressive gap is the difference between what infants understand and what they can produce in language.","traps":{"b":"General cognitive abilities have no standard receptive versus expressive split; the term refers specifically to language. Receptive and expressive are labels for understanding and producing words, not for perception versus reasoning or memory. It tempts because comprehension is itself a cognitive process, and 'receptive' can sound like taking in any information, so the broader wording seems a reasonable paraphrase.","c":"Motor skills are described as gross and fine, not as receptive and expressive. Speech production does need motor control of the lips, tongue and breath, which is one reason expression lags behind comprehension, but the gap itself is defined by language, not by motor development. It tempts because the motor demands of speaking are part of the explanation for the gap.","d":"Social skills are not split into receptive and expressive in this concept. The gap concerns language comprehension versus language production. Social-communicative milestones such as social smiling, joint attention and pointing are related but separate. It tempts because early language develops inside social interaction, and clinicians do speak of social communication, as in the DSM-5 diagnosis of social (pragmatic) communication disorder."}},{"q":"What are the four skill modules of DBT?","concept":"Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha M. Linehan for chronically suicidal women who met criteria for borderline personality disorder, and set out in her treatment and skills training manuals (1993). It rests on a biosocial theory: an inborn emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, intense reactions, slow return to baseline) meets an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotional expression, producing pervasive emotion dysregulation. 'Dialectical' means balancing opposites, above all acceptance (drawn from Zen practice) and change (drawn from behaviour therapy). Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching between sessions, and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules. Two are acceptance skills: core mindfulness, including 'wise mind', the blend of emotion mind and reasonable mind; and distress tolerance, which teaches surviving crises without making them worse, plus radical acceptance. Two are change skills: emotion regulation, which teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability and acting opposite to an unhelpful urge; and interpersonal effectiveness, which teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect, using acronyms such as DEAR MAN. DBT is now also used for self-harm, substance use and eating disorders. Neighbouring therapies supply the distractors: cognitive restructuring from the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976), relaxation training from Edmund Jacobson (1929), and acceptance and commitment therapy from Steven C. Hayes (1999), which has six core processes rather than four modules. Option b is right because DBT's four skill modules are mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation and distress tolerance.","traps":{"a":"This list keeps mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness but swaps emotion regulation and distress tolerance for cognitive restructuring and relaxation. Cognitive restructuring, identifying and challenging distorted thoughts, is the core of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, and relaxation training comes from Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. DBT uses behavioural methods but does not name these as modules. It tempts because two of the four items are correct.","c":"This list keeps mindfulness and emotion regulation but adds cognitive restructuring and relaxation, dropping interpersonal effectiveness and distress tolerance. It reads like a generic cognitive behavioural package for anxiety: thought restructuring from Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy plus relaxation exercises. DBT is cognitive behavioural in origin, which is why the list sounds believable. It tempts because two items are genuine DBT modules and the others are common techniques.","d":"This list has three genuine DBT modules, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation and distress tolerance, but replaces mindfulness with cognitive restructuring. Mindfulness is the core DBT module, taught first and revisited between the others, because the other skills depend on observing and describing experience without judgement. It tempts because it is three-quarters right, making it the closest distractor for a candidate who recalls only the change skills."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic technique involves asking a person to play the role of an exhibitionist even if they claim to suffer from severe inhibitions and excessive timidity?","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding text, Gestalt Therapy, appeared in 1951. It is an existential, experiential therapy centred on the here and now: the client is helped to become aware of what they are doing, feeling and avoiding, rather than to analyse why. Unfinished business (unexpressed feelings from the past) and disowned parts of the self are brought into present awareness through experiments rather than interpretation. Gerald Corey's textbook Theory and Practice of Counseling and Psychotherapy lists the standard exercises. The empty chair or internal dialogue sets up a conversation between conflicting parts, such as the top dog and the underdog. The reversal exercise rests on the idea that symptoms often reverse hidden impulses, so the client acts out the opposite of their usual style; Corey's own example is asking a person who claims severe inhibitions and excessive timidity to play an exhibitionist. The rehearsal exercise voices aloud the silent rehearsing people do before acting. The exaggeration exercise asks a client to amplify a gesture, posture or phrase, such as trembling hands or a clenched fist, until the feeling behind it becomes clear. Staying with the feeling asks the client to remain with an unpleasant emotion instead of escaping it. Future projection, by contrast, comes from the psychodrama of Jacob L. Moreno (1946). Option a is right because asking a timid, inhibited person to play an exhibitionist means acting out the opposite of their usual style, which is the reversal exercise, and Corey uses this exact example for it.","traps":{"c":"The rehearsal exercise brings into the open the silent rehearsing people do before acting, as they prepare how to come across and win approval. Clients say their rehearsed thoughts aloud and notice how much energy goes into playing a role. It involves no switch to an extreme or opposite role. It tempts because both exercises involve acting out roles, and 'rehearsal' sounds like practising a new behaviour.","d":"The future projection technique comes from psychodrama, developed by Jacob L. Moreno, rather than from the core Gestalt exercises. The client acts out a feared or hoped-for future event, such as a job interview, in the here and now, to clarify goals and rehearse responses. It does not involve playing a trait opposite to one's own. It tempts because it is also an enactment technique used in experiential therapies.","b":"The exaggeration exercise asks a client to amplify a gesture, posture or phrase they already show, such as trembling legs or a tight smile, until the feeling behind it becomes clear. It intensifies existing behaviour; it does not switch to the opposite. A timid person playing an exhibitionist is acting against type, which is reversal. It tempts because playing an exhibitionist sounds like an over-the-top, exaggerated performance."}},{"q":"Which type of therapeutic technique in REBT involves using imagery?","concept":"Rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) was founded by Albert Ellis, who began practising it as rational therapy in 1955 and adopted the current name in 1993. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional and behavioural Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Irrational beliefs include rigid demands ('musts' and 'shoulds'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of self or others. Therapy adds D, Disputing those beliefs, and E, a new Effective philosophy. Ellis held that thinking, feeling and acting interact, so REBT is multimodal, and Gerald Corey's textbook groups its techniques into three families. Cognitive methods include disputing irrational beliefs, cognitive homework, changing one's language, and psychoeducational methods such as books and recordings. Emotive techniques work through feeling and vivid experience: rational emotive imagery, humour, role playing and shame-attacking exercises. Behavioural methods include in vivo desensitisation, reinforcement and penalties, and skill training. In rational emotive imagery, developed by Maxie C. Maultsby Jr. and adopted by Ellis, clients vividly imagine one of the worst things that could happen, let themselves feel the unhealthy emotion (such as panic), then change it into a healthy negative emotion (such as concern) by changing their belief. It counts as emotive because it works by arousing and transforming feeling, not by verbal argument. Do not confuse it with the imagery work in the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976) or with systematic desensitisation. Option b is right because, in REBT, imagery-based methods such as rational emotive imagery belong to the emotive techniques.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive methods in REBT work through reason and argument: disputing irrational beliefs (asking where the evidence is that you must succeed), cognitive homework such as ABC worksheets, changing self-talk, and reading. They target beliefs directly through verbal challenge. It tempts because imagery is a mental activity and, in other approaches such as the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976), imagery work is often classed as cognitive.","c":"Behavioural techniques in REBT are action-based: in vivo desensitisation, staying in feared situations, operant reinforcement and penalties for doing or skipping homework, and skill training such as assertiveness. They change beliefs by having clients act against them in real life. It tempts because rational emotive imagery is practised as homework and resembles the imaginal exposure used in systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958).","d":"Psychoeducational methods teach clients the REBT model through books, recordings, lectures and workshops, so they understand the ABC theory and can apply it themselves. In Gerald Corey's classification they sit within the cognitive methods, not as a separate imagery-based family. It tempts because it sounds like a distinct category and is often listed alongside the others, so candidates may treat it as the catch-all."}}],"pep-mock-2":[{"q":"How many General Principles are included in the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association (APA) adopted its first ethics code in 1953; the current Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct was adopted in 2002 and amended in 2010 and 2016. It has four parts: an Introduction and Applicability section, a Preamble, the General Principles, and the Ethical Standards. The General Principles are five aspirational ideals, lettered A to E: A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (benefit people, do no harm); B, Fidelity and Responsibility (trust and professional obligations to society); C, Integrity (accuracy, honesty, truthfulness); D, Justice (fair access to and equal quality of services); and E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (privacy, confidentiality, self-determination, cultural differences). They are not enforceable rules and do not by themselves form the basis for sanctions. The enforceable part is the ten Ethical Standards, for example Standard 2 Competence, 3 Human Relations, 4 Privacy and Confidentiality, 9 Assessment and 10 Therapy. Examiners build variants from neighbouring frameworks. The Belmont Report (1979) has three principles: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) set out four principles of biomedical ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five moral principles for psychologists: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice and fidelity. So remember: Belmont three, Beauchamp and Childress four, APA five principles plus ten standards. In India, clinical psychologists are registered by the Rehabilitation Council of India under the RCI Act 1992. Option c is right because the APA code contains exactly five General Principles, lettered A to E.","traps":{"a":"One would mean a single overarching rule, such as 'first, do no harm' (primum non nocere), which people often treat as the whole of medical ethics. The APA code instead separates its ideals into five lettered principles, and even nonmaleficence is paired with beneficence inside Principle A. It tempts candidates who remember that the code has a single Preamble, or who think of ethics as one master principle.","b":"Three is the number of principles in the Belmont Report (1979), the United States research ethics document: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Those three underlie informed consent, risk-benefit assessment and fair selection of research participants, but they are not the APA's General Principles. It tempts because Belmont is taught in the same research ethics lectures, and its principles overlap in name with APA Principles A and D.","d":"Seven matches no part of the APA code. The code has five General Principles and ten enforceable Ethical Standards, so seven is neither count. It tempts candidates who blur the principles with the standards, or who mix Karen Kitchener's (1984) five moral principles with other lists. Remember the pattern: Belmont three, Beauchamp and Childress four, APA five principles and ten standards."}},{"q":"According to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, what are children capable of?","concept":"The United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC) was adopted by the UN General Assembly on 20 November 1989, and India ratified it in 1992. It defines a child as every human being below 18 years. Its rights are often grouped as survival, development, protection and participation, and four articles act as general principles: non-discrimination (Article 2), best interests of the child (Article 3), the right to life, survival and development (Article 6), and respect for the views of the child (Article 12). Article 12 is the one examined here. It says a child who is capable of forming his or her own views has the right to express them freely in all matters affecting the child, and those views must be given due weight according to age and maturity. In clinical practice this is the basis of assent. Consent is a legal permission that requires capacity and, for minors, normally comes from a parent or guardian. Assent is the child's own agreement, and dissent is the child's objection, which the clinician must hear and weigh even when the guardian has consented. A neighbouring idea is Gillick competence, from the English case Gillick v West Norfolk (1985), under which a minor with sufficient understanding may consent. In India, the Juvenile Justice Act 2015 and the POCSO Act 2012 use the same under-18 definition. Option c is right because the Convention treats children as capable of forming and voicing views, that is, giving assent or dissent, not as legally competent decision makers.","traps":{"a":"Understanding complex legal terminology is a test of legal literacy, which the Convention never assumes. Article 12 asks only that the child can form a view, and Article 13 protects freedom of expression in any form the child can use. Explaining matters in child-friendly language is the adult's duty, not a capacity expected of the child. It tempts because the Convention is a legal treaty, so a legal-sounding option looks relevant.","b":"Providing consent for psychotherapy is a legal act that requires capacity, and for children this authority usually rests with a parent or guardian. Some systems allow mature minors to consent, as in Gillick competence (1985), but that is national case law, not what the Convention says. It tempts because the Convention stresses children's voices, and candidates slide from 'has a voice' to 'can consent'. The correct concept is assent.","d":"Making important life decisions overstates the child's role. The Convention gives children a right to be heard, with due weight according to age and maturity, while best interests (Article 3) and parental guidance in line with evolving capacities (Article 5) keep major decisions with adults. It tempts because participation rights sound like autonomy, but participation means being consulted and taken seriously, not having the final say."}},{"q":"Under what circumstances therapists may accept gifts?","concept":"Gifts from clients are a classic boundary question. Thomas Gutheil and Glen Gabbard (1993) separated boundary crossings, departures from strict therapeutic distance that can be harmless or even helpful, from boundary violations, crossings that exploit or harm the client. A small gift is usually a crossing. The ethical question is not 'gifts, yes or no' but 'what does accepting or refusing mean for this client and this therapy'. The American Counseling Association Code of Ethics (2014, Standard A.10.f) asks counsellors to consider the therapeutic relationship, the monetary value of the gift, the client's motivation for giving it, and the counsellor's motivation for accepting or declining it. It also notes that in some cultures small gifts are a token of respect and gratitude. The APA code (2002, amended 2016) has no specific gift rule, but its standards on multiple relationships (3.05) and exploitative relationships (3.08) apply. So a gift may be accepted when it is small, freely offered, and when accepting it serves the client's welfare, for example when refusal would feel like rejection to a client working on low self-worth, and acceptance affirms that what they offer has value. Large gifts, repeated gifts, gifts that create obligation, and gifts linked to fees are red flags. Related concepts are dual relationships, barter (APA Standard 6.05, allowed only when not clinically contraindicated and not exploitative) and countertransference, since the therapist's wish to receive can bias judgment. Option c is right because it gives a clinical, client-centred reason for acceptance, that the gift will help promote the client's self-acceptance.","traps":{"a":"Without any specific reason means accepting gifts casually, as a social habit. Every code that addresses gifts asks the therapist to examine the value of the gift, the client's motive and the therapist's own motive before accepting. Unreflective acceptance risks sliding from a boundary crossing into a boundary violation, in the terms of Thomas Gutheil and Glen Gabbard (1993). It tempts because most small gifts are harmless and refusing them can seem cold.","b":"Only on festive occasions turns a clinical decision into a calendar rule. Festival sweets at Diwali or Eid may well be acceptable in India, since codes recognise the cultural meaning of gifts, but the occasion is not what justifies acceptance; the clinical meaning for the client is. The word 'only' also makes it a rigid rule. It tempts because cultural gift-giving is familiar and feels like the safest exception.","d":"In exchange for reducing session fees makes the gift a payment, which is barter. APA Standard 6.05 allows barter only when it is not clinically contraindicated and not exploitative, and a gift traded for a fee cut creates a business relationship and obligations on both sides. That is a multiple relationship and a conflict of interest, not a therapeutic gift. It tempts because it sounds like a fair, practical arrangement."}},{"q":"What is the impact of the Criminal Law Amendment Act on the age of statutory rape in India?","concept":"Statutory rape means sexual intercourse with a person below the legal age of consent, where the minor's agreement is legally irrelevant. In India, rape was defined in Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code 1860, and before 2013 the age of consent there was 16 years. After the December 2012 Delhi gang rape, the Justice J. S. Verma Committee (2013) reviewed sexual offence law, and Parliament passed the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013. This Act widened the definition of rape, added offences such as stalking, voyeurism and acid attack, and raised the age of consent from 16 to 18 years. That brought the Indian Penal Code in line with the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act 2012, which already treated every person below 18 as a child and all sexual acts with them as offences. Neighbours an examiner may use: the marital rape exception, which the Supreme Court read down in Independent Thought v Union of India (2017) so that sex with a wife below 18 is rape; the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2018, which raised punishments for rape of girls below 12 and below 16; and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, which replaced the Indian Penal Code and keeps the age at 18. For clinical psychologists, POCSO also imposes mandatory reporting, so a therapist who learns of sexual activity involving a minor has a legal duty to report it. Option b is right because the 2013 amendment increased the age of consent, and therefore the age for statutory rape, from 16 to 18 years.","traps":{"a":"It lowered the age from 18 to 16 reverses the real direction. The age of consent in the Indian Penal Code was 16 before 2013, and the amendment raised it to 18. It tempts because public debate since then, including calls for a 'Romeo and Juliet' exception for consensual adolescent relationships, has discussed lowering the age, and a candidate may mix up the debate with the law itself.","c":"It criminalized all consensual sex among young people overstates the law. The amendment fixed an age threshold of 18; it did not ban sexual activity between adults of any age. A real criticism is that, together with POCSO 2012, the law makes consensual relationships between two 17-year-olds an offence, which courts have commented on. That criticism makes this option tempting, but the Act changed only the age threshold.","d":"It did not have any impact is simply false. The 2013 amendment rewrote Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code, and one explicit change was raising the age of consent from 16 to 18. It tempts candidates who assume the age was already 18, because the POCSO Act 2012 had defined a child as anyone below 18 a year earlier, so the change can look like no change at all."}},{"q":"What does ethical competency refer to?","concept":"Ethical competency is the capacity to practise ethically, and it is treated as a cluster of abilities rather than one skill. In the competency cube model of Emil Rodolfa and colleagues (2005), ethical and legal standards and policy is one of the foundational competencies that run through every functional activity such as assessment, intervention and supervision. Most frameworks describe three linked parts. The first is knowledge: knowing the relevant codes, laws and guidelines, such as the APA Ethics Code (2002, amended 2016), the RCI Act 1992 and the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (MHCA). The second is skill: recognising that a situation has an ethical dimension, analysing it, and resolving it, often through a step-wise decision-making model. The third is attitude and accountability: taking responsibility for one's own actions, seeking consultation, and correcting mistakes. James Rest (1986) gave the classic psychological account in his four-component model of moral behaviour: moral sensitivity (noticing the issue), moral judgment (deciding what is right), moral motivation (prioritising the ethical value) and moral character (having the persistence to act). Karen Kitchener (1984) added the distinction between intuitive and critical-evaluative levels of ethical reasoning. Knowledge without skill produces people who can quote the code but miss the dilemma; skill without responsibility produces rationalisation. Examiners also test the difference between ethical competence, a capacity, and ethical compliance, merely following rules to avoid sanction. Option d is right because ethical competency includes knowledge of codes, the ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations, and responsibility for one's own actions, so all three listed elements together define it.","traps":{"a":"Knowledge and understanding of ethics codes is the cognitive base of ethical competency, and it is genuinely part of it. On its own, though, it is incomplete: a clinician can know the APA code or RCI rules well and still fail to notice a dual relationship forming. It tempts because exam preparation stresses memorising codes, so knowledge feels like the whole of competence. In an 'all of the above' item it is only one piece.","b":"Ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations is the applied skill component, matching moral sensitivity and moral judgment in James Rest's (1986) four-component model. It is the heart of ethical decision making, but it leaves out knowledge of codes and personal accountability. It tempts because it is the longest and most complete-sounding option, which candidates often pick when they do not weigh the 'all of the above' choice.","c":"Responsibility for one's own actions is the accountability component: owning mistakes, seeking consultation and following through on decisions, close to moral character in James Rest's (1986) model. It is a real part of ethical competency but too narrow alone, since responsibility without knowledge or analysis can still produce poor decisions. It tempts because 'responsibility' is a common one-word definition of professional ethics."}},{"q":"According to Immanuel Kant's deontological approach, what are actions judged by?","concept":"Deontology is the family of ethical theories that judge an action by whether it conforms to a moral duty or rule, not by its results. Its classic form comes from Immanuel Kant in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785). Kant argued that the only thing good without qualification is a good will, that is, acting from duty. An action has moral worth because of the principle (maxim) behind it, so lying is wrong in itself even if a lie would produce a good outcome. His test is the categorical imperative. The universal law formulation says act only on a maxim you could will to become a universal law. The humanity formulation says treat persons always as ends in themselves and never merely as means. Kant contrasted categorical imperatives with hypothetical imperatives, which are conditional ('if you want X, do Y'). The main rival is consequentialism, especially the utilitarianism of Jeremy Bentham (1789) and John Stuart Mill (1863), which judges acts by outcomes, the greatest happiness of the greatest number, and uses cost-benefit reasoning. A third family is virtue ethics, from Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (fourth century BCE), which asks what a person of good character would do. W. D. Ross (1930) later softened Kant with prima facie duties, such as fidelity and non-maleficence, that can conflict. In clinical ethics, informed consent and respect for autonomy are Kantian ideas, while triage is utilitarian. Option b is right because Kant judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness, that is, their conformity to duty, not by their consequences.","traps":{"a":"Their consequences is the criterion of consequentialism, the opposite of Kant's view. Utilitarians such as Jeremy Bentham (1789) and John Stuart Mill (1863) judge an action right if it produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number. Kant held that a good outcome cannot make a wrong act right. It tempts because consequentialism is the most intuitive everyday way of judging actions, and the two theories are always taught as a pair.","c":"Cost-benefit analysis is a practical method of consequentialist reasoning: add up expected benefits and harms and choose the option with the best balance. Jeremy Bentham's (1789) felicific, or hedonic, calculus is its classic form. Kant rejected this kind of calculation because it can justify using a person merely as a means. It tempts because risk-benefit weighing is common in clinical and research ethics, so it sounds like a respectable ethical standard.","d":"None of the above would be correct only if no listed option captured Kant's criterion. 'Their intrinsic goodness or badness' does capture it: Kant judged acts by their inherent rightness, meaning conformity to duty and the categorical imperative, regardless of outcome. It tempts candidates who find 'intrinsic goodness' vague, or who know that Kant focused on the good will and motive and so doubt that any option fits."}},{"q":"What is a potential consequence of poor awareness of the culture and prevalent familial practices in family therapy?","concept":"Family therapy treats the family, not the individual, as the unit of assessment and change, and every family therapy model carries assumptions about what a healthy family looks like. The structural family therapy of Salvador Minuchin (1974), for example, judges boundaries as clear, rigid or diffuse and labels very close families as enmeshed. If a therapist applies such norms without cultural awareness, normal cultural patterns get read as pathology. In an Indian joint family, adult children living with parents, elders taking decisions, a mother-in-law's authority, arranged marriage or shared finances may be normative, not signs of enmeshment, poor differentiation of self in the sense of Murray Bowen (1978), or dependency. The result is wrong assessments and misinterpretations, then goals that clash with the family's values, poor engagement and dropout. Monica McGoldrick, John Pearce and Joseph Giordano's Ethnicity and Family Therapy (1982) made this a core training issue. Derald Wing Sue, Patricia Arredondo and Roderick McDavis (1992) defined multicultural competence in three parts: awareness of one's own cultural assumptions and biases, knowledge of the client's worldview, and culturally appropriate skills. Neighbouring terms examiners use: emic (culture-specific) versus etic (universal) approaches, cultural humility, and the Cultural Formulation Interview of DSM-5 (2013). In India, Vidya Sagar at Amritsar Mental Hospital in the 1950s pioneered keeping families with patients during inpatient care, an early sign that Indian practice must work with, not against, family systems. Option a is right because poor awareness of culture and familial practices leads the therapist to misjudge normal practices as dysfunction, producing wrong assessments and misinterpretations.","traps":{"b":"Accurate understanding of the family's dynamics is what good cultural awareness produces, so it is the opposite of the consequence asked for. A therapist who knows, for instance, that elders' involvement in decisions is normal in many Indian families can separate culture from dysfunction. It tempts only through careless reading of the stem, which asks for the result of poor awareness, a negative consequence, not a goal of therapy.","c":"Effective communication with the family depends on understanding its language, hierarchy and norms of respect, for example whom to address first. Poor cultural awareness damages communication, so this is a benefit of competence, not a consequence of its absence. It tempts because communication is a core family therapy skill, emphasised by Virginia Satir in Conjoint Family Therapy (1964), and it appears in every list of therapy outcomes.","d":"Development of a strong therapeutic alliance is a positive outcome that culturally insensitive work actively threatens. Edward Bordin (1979) defined the alliance as agreement on goals and tasks plus an emotional bond, and goals clash when the therapist pathologises the family's values. It tempts because the alliance is the most studied common factor in therapy, so it sounds like a safe answer, but the stem asks for a harmful consequence."}},{"q":"Which type of psychotherapy is NOT commonly used to treat survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"Survivors of sexual assault have high rates of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), depression and shame, and the treatments with the strongest evidence are trauma-focused, meaning they work directly on the memory of the assault and the beliefs it created. Prolonged exposure, by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998), was developed with rape survivors and uses imaginal and in vivo exposure. Cognitive processing therapy, by Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992), was also first tested with rape survivors and targets 'stuck points' such as self-blame. Trauma-focused cognitive-behavioural therapy (TF-CBT), developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger (2006), is the standard for children and adolescents after sexual abuse. Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR), by Francine Shapiro (1989), pairs recall of the trauma with bilateral stimulation and is recommended by the World Health Organization (2013) and NICE guidelines. Psychodynamic psychotherapy is widely used, especially for complex and long-standing effects of sexual trauma on relationships and identity, though its trial evidence is weaker. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT), by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), was built for depression and works on four problem areas: grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits. It is not trauma-focused and is not a standard treatment for assault survivors. One trial by John Markowitz and colleagues (2015) found IPT comparable to prolonged exposure for PTSD, so it may be treated as an emerging option in future. Option a is right because interpersonal psychotherapy is a depression-focused treatment that is not commonly used for sexual assault survivors, unlike TF-CBT, EMDR and psychodynamic therapy.","traps":{"b":"Psychodynamic psychotherapy explores how the assault connects with earlier relationships, unconscious conflict, shame and identity, and works these through within the therapeutic relationship. Its controlled-trial evidence for PTSD is weaker than that for trauma-focused CBT, but it is widely used with sexual trauma survivors, especially for long-term relational effects. It tempts because guidelines do not list it as first line, so candidates confuse 'not first line' with 'not commonly used'.","c":"EMDR, developed by Francine Shapiro (1989), asks the client to hold the trauma memory in mind while following bilateral stimulation such as side-to-side eye movements. It is recommended for PTSD by the World Health Organization (2013) and NICE, and is commonly used after rape and sexual abuse. It tempts because the eye movement procedure looks unusual and was once controversial, so candidates may assume it is a fringe therapy.","d":"TF-CBT, developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger (2006), combines psychoeducation, relaxation, affect regulation, cognitive coping, a trauma narrative and in vivo exposure, with caregiver sessions. It was designed for children and adolescents after sexual abuse and is a first-line, evidence-based treatment. It tempts only candidates who misread the NOT in the stem and choose the most familiar trauma treatment."}},{"q":"What is bilateral stimulation in EMDR?","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by the psychologist Francine Shapiro, who noticed in 1987 that moving her eyes from side to side while thinking about a disturbing thought reduced its emotional charge; her first controlled study with trauma survivors appeared in 1989. Bilateral stimulation (BLS) is the signature ingredient: rhythmic, alternating left-right stimulation that the client attends to while holding a target memory in mind. The classic form is saccadic eye movement, the client's eyes following the therapist's fingers back and forth across the visual field in short sets. Alternating hand taps, tones through headphones or light bars are accepted variants. The principle is dual attention: the client stays anchored in the safe present while briefly contacting the traumatic image, its negative belief (for example 'I am helpless') and its body sensations. Shapiro explained the effect with her Adaptive Information Processing (AIP) model: trauma is stored in an unprocessed form, and BLS helps link it to adaptive memory networks. A rival working memory account says the eye movements load working memory, so the recalled image becomes less vivid and less emotional. EMDR runs in eight phases (history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitization, installation, body scan, closure, reevaluation), with distress rated on the 0 to 10 Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale. The World Health Organization (2013) recommends EMDR, along with trauma-focused CBT, for PTSD, although dismantling studies question how much the eye movements add beyond exposure. Option b is right because bilateral stimulation means the back-and-forth eye movements the client makes while focusing on a disturbing memory.","traps":{"a":"This option garbles the technique. Alternating taps on the hands or knees are an accepted variant of bilateral stimulation, but the defining feature is rhythmic left-right alternation paired with a target memory, not 'physical touch' and 'thoughts' in general. Touch has no therapeutic role of its own in EMDR. It tempts because candidates remember that taps are allowed and that the client thinks of something during each set.","c":"Medication combined with behavioural techniques describes drug treatment plus behaviour therapy for anxiety, for example an SSRI with graded exposure or with the systematic desensitization of Joseph Wolpe (1958). EMDR is a stand-alone psychotherapy and uses no drugs. It tempts because the full name of EMDR contains 'desensitization', a word borrowed from behaviour therapy, and because EMDR treats anxiety-related conditions such as PTSD.","d":"Hypnosis induces a trance of focused attention and heightened suggestibility, and 'accessing repressed memories' recalls Freud's early cathartic method and the controversial recovered-memory therapies of the 1990s. EMDR works on memories the client already has, in a fully alert state of dual attention, and does not aim to uncover hidden memories. It tempts because both hypnosis and EMDR guide attention and are used for trauma."}},{"q":"What do researchers mean by pathologization and depathologization of homosexuality?","concept":"Pathologization means defining a way of being as a disease, so it becomes something to diagnose, explain and treat. Depathologization is the reverse: formally removing that diagnostic label. Homosexuality is the classic case. Nineteenth-century sexology, such as Richard von Krafft-Ebing's Psychopathia Sexualis (1886), framed it as degeneracy; DSM-I (1952) listed it under sociopathic personality disturbance, and DSM-II (1968) among the sexual deviations. The evidence turned against this view. Evelyn Hooker (1957) gave projective tests to matched gay and heterosexual men, and expert judges could not tell the groups apart, showing no inherent maladjustment. Alfred Kinsey's reports (1948, 1953) showed that same-sex behaviour was common. After protests by gay activists, the American Psychiatric Association voted in December 1973, with the psychiatrist Robert Spitzer central to the change, to remove homosexuality from DSM-II, keeping 'sexual orientation disturbance' only for people distressed by their orientation. This survived as 'ego-dystonic homosexuality' in DSM-III (1980) and was dropped in DSM-III-R (1987). The World Health Organization removed homosexuality from ICD-10, approved in 1990. In India, the Supreme Court decriminalised consensual same-sex relations in Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018), and the Indian Psychiatric Society stated the same year that homosexuality is not a mental illness. Keep three neighbours apart: depathologization concerns diagnostic status; conversion therapy tries to change orientation and is now rejected as harmful; coming out is personal disclosure of identity. Option b is right because pathologization is classifying homosexuality as a mental disorder and depathologization is removing that classification.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' is a catch-all distractor that is correct only when every listed choice is false. Here one listed choice states exactly what researchers mean: classifying homosexuality as a disorder, then removing that classification from DSM and ICD. It tempts candidates who find the long words 'pathologization' and 'depathologization' unfamiliar and assume the examiner is testing an obscure meaning that no option captures.","c":"Changing orientation from homosexual to heterosexual is the aim of conversion or 'reparative' therapy, which has used aversive conditioning, religious counselling and talk therapy. Professional bodies reject it as ineffective and harmful; in India the Madras High Court (2021) ordered action against it and the National Medical Commission (2022) declared it professional misconduct. It tempts because conversion therapy only made sense while homosexuality was pathologized.","d":"Coming out is the process of recognising one's own sexual orientation and disclosing it to others, described in stage models such as the homosexual identity formation model of Vivienne Cass (1979). It is a personal, developmental process, not a change in diagnostic manuals. It tempts because depathologization made coming out safer, and the two topics are discussed together in the LGBTQ+ mental health literature."}},{"q":"What are some defining features of minority stress?","concept":"Minority stress theory explains why lesbian, gay and bisexual (LGB) people show higher rates of depression, anxiety and substance use than heterosexual people. Virginia Brooks (1981) first used the term for lesbian women, and Ilan Meyer developed it, most influentially in his 1995 study of gay men and his 2003 review in Psychological Bulletin. It builds on social stress theory and on Erving Goffman's (1963) account of stigma. The core claim is that prejudice in the social environment creates extra stress, and that this excess stress, not sexual orientation itself, produces the health gap. Meyer gave minority stress three defining features: it is unique (added on top of the general stressors everyone faces), chronic (related to relatively stable underlying social and cultural structures), and socially based (stemming from social processes, institutions and structures beyond the individual, rather than from individual events or conditions). He arranged the stressors on a distal to proximal continuum. Distal stressors are objective events such as discrimination, harassment and violence; proximal stressors are internal, such as expecting rejection, concealing one's identity and internalized homophobia. Coping and community support can buffer these effects. Mark Hatzenbuehler (2009) added a psychological mediation framework linking stigma to emotion regulation and interpersonal problems. Contrast the transactional model of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), which locates stress in an individual's appraisal of a situation; minority stress locates the source in the social structure. Option b is right because Meyer defines minority stress as chronic and related to relatively stable underlying social and cultural structures.","traps":{"a":"This describes ordinary short-lived stress handled by individual coping, the territory of positive thinking and cognitive reframing. Minority stress is the opposite: chronic, because prejudice is built into lasting social structures, and not removable by changing one's thoughts alone. Meyer warned that treating it as a personal problem blames the victim. It tempts because coping and resilience do appear in the model, but only as buffers, not as a cure.","c":"Locating the stress in individual characteristics and personal choices is a person-centred view of stress, and it echoes the old pathologizing idea that something inside the minority person is faulty. Meyer's model says the source lies outside the individual, in stigma, prejudice and discrimination. It tempts because proximal stressors such as internalized homophobia and concealment do occur inside the person, but they are internalized forms of social stigma.","d":"This flatly negates Meyer's third defining feature, that minority stress is socially based, stemming from social processes, institutions and structures. Examiners build such options by adding a single 'not' to the textbook definition. It tempts careless readers who scan for the familiar phrase 'social processes, institutions, and structures', recognise it from their notes, and miss the negation at the start of the sentence."}},{"q":"Who is credited with coining the term \"clinical psychology\"?","concept":"Clinical psychology as a named discipline begins with Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956). Witmer studied with James McKeen Cattell and took his PhD under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig in 1892, so he was trained in the experimental laboratory tradition. In March 1896, at the University of Pennsylvania, he opened the first psychological clinic, after a teacher brought him a boy, Charles Gilman, with severe spelling problems. In December 1896 he described his clinical method to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose opening article, titled 'Clinical Psychology', put the term in print. For Witmer, clinical psychology meant applying scientific psychology to individual cases, mostly children with learning and behaviour difficulties, through assessment and remedial teaching. Examiners pair this with other firsts: Wundt's Leipzig laboratory (1879), Cattell's coining of 'mental test' (1890), the first practical intelligence scale by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), and the scientist-practitioner model set at the Boulder conference (1949). The names in the other options built different things: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951), and B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938). In India, the profession is regulated by the Rehabilitation Council of India under the RCI Act 1992; clinical psychologists must register with the RCI, which requires a recognised qualification such as the M.Phil in Clinical Psychology. Option c is right because Lightner Witmer coined the term 'clinical psychology' and founded the first psychological clinic in 1896.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) was a neurologist who founded psychoanalysis, with landmarks such as Studies on Hysteria (1895, with Josef Breuer) and The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He shaped psychotherapy and psychiatry, but he worked within a medical framework and never named clinical psychology. It tempts because Freud is the most famous figure in clinical work, and many students assume he founded everything clinical.","b":"Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) founded client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, built on empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness, and he pioneered recorded sessions and psychotherapy research. His key books appeared in 1942 and 1951, almost half a century after Witmer's clinic. It tempts because Rogers was a president of the American Psychological Association and a central figure in the growth of clinical and counselling psychology.","d":"'F. Skinner' is a slip for B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990), the radical behaviourist who developed operant conditioning, the Skinner box and schedules of reinforcement (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938). His work later fed behaviour modification and token economies in hospitals. It tempts because applied behaviour analysis is clinically important and Skinner is a giant of twentieth-century psychology, but he did not coin the term."}},{"q":"What is countertransference in therapy?","concept":"Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. Sigmund Freud introduced the term in 1910 for feelings aroused in the analyst by the patient's influence on the analyst's unconscious, and he treated it as an obstacle that the analyst's own analysis should remove. It is the mirror image of transference, which Freud analysed in the Dora case (1905): the client unconsciously redirects feelings and attitudes from significant early figures, such as parents, onto the therapist, for example treating a neutral therapist as a critical father. In countertransference the direction reverses: the therapist experiences similar unconscious feelings toward the client, such as irritation, rescue fantasies, boredom, attraction or overprotectiveness, often because the client resembles someone in the therapist's own life. Later writers turned the concept from a problem into a tool. Donald Winnicott (1949) wrote on 'hate in the counter-transference', and Paula Heimann (1950) argued that the analyst's feelings are a source of information about the client's inner world. This broader 'totalistic' view counts all of the therapist's reactions, while the classical view counts only those arising from the therapist's unresolved conflicts. Managing countertransference requires self-awareness, personal therapy and supervision. Neighbouring terms that examiners swap in are resistance (the client's unconscious opposition to change), the working alliance (the collaborative bond), boundary violations (ethical breaches that unmanaged countertransference can lead to), and projective identification, described by Melanie Klein (1946). Option c is right because countertransference is when the therapist experiences similar, transference-like feelings towards the client, the reverse of the client's transference.","traps":{"a":"Fear of the unknown, low motivation and feeling overwhelmed are common client barriers to starting or staying in therapy, and in psychodynamic terms some of them appear as resistance, the client's unconscious opposition to change that Freud described. They concern the client's state, not the therapist's feelings. It tempts because it reads like a list of therapy-process problems from the same textbook chapter on the therapeutic relationship.","b":"Maintaining appropriate boundaries is an ethical and professional duty covering time, fees, touch, gifts, self-disclosure and dual relationships, set out in professional codes of conduct. It is related, because unexamined countertransference can lead to boundary crossings and violations, but it is a rule of conduct, not an emotional process. It tempts because textbooks often discuss countertransference and boundaries in the same section.","d":"This is the definition of transference: the client unconsciously transfers feelings or attitudes toward significant people in their life onto the therapist. Freud first saw it as a form of resistance, then as the central vehicle of analytic work, handled through interpretation. Countertransference runs in the opposite direction, from therapist to client. It tempts because it is a perfectly correct definition of the paired concept, so a hurried reader picks it."}},{"q":"Which biological system is implicated in obsessive-compulsive disorder?","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is defined by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety) and compulsions (repetitive acts or mental rituals done to neutralise them). The serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) hypothesis is the classic biological account, and it rests on drug response. Clomipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant with strong serotonin reuptake blockade, reduced obsessions, while desipramine, a tricyclic that mainly blocks noradrenaline reuptake, did not. The selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), such as fluoxetine, fluvoxamine and sertraline, then proved effective and became first-line drugs. Two clinical facts tie OCD to serotonin specifically: SSRIs work in OCD whether or not the patient is depressed, and they need higher doses and longer trials (often 8 to 12 weeks) than in depression. Brain imaging adds the circuit level: PET studies show overactivity in the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop, especially the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate and caudate nucleus, and this activity falls after either SSRIs or exposure and response prevention. The other systems in the options matter, but less centrally. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter of the CSTC loop and a newer research target. Dopamine is relevant to OCD with tics, and low-dose antipsychotics are used to augment SSRIs in resistant cases. GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter, is the target of benzodiazepines, which ease general anxiety but not core obsessions. Examiners also link sudden childhood OCD to streptococcal infection (PANDAS, described by Susan Swedo, 1998). Option a is right because the central serotonergic (5-HT) system is the system most consistently implicated in OCD, shown by its selective response to serotonergic drugs.","traps":{"b":"The GABAergic system uses gamma-aminobutyric acid, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, acting on GABA-A receptors that benzodiazepines and barbiturates enhance. It is central to generalized anxiety, panic, alcohol withdrawal and sedation. Benzodiazepines may calm distress but do not reduce core obsessions and compulsions. It tempts because OCD was classed as an anxiety disorder until DSM-5 (2013) moved it to its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter.","c":"Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter and drives the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loop that is overactive in OCD. Raised glutamate in cerebrospinal fluid has been reported, and glutamate-modulating drugs such as memantine and N-acetylcysteine are being studied as add-ons. It tempts because it is a genuine, current research lead, but the established textbook system behind first-line treatment remains serotonin.","d":"Dopamine pathways underlie reward, movement and psychosis, and are the key system in schizophrenia and Tourette syndrome. In OCD, dopamine matters for the tic-related subtype and explains why low-dose antipsychotics such as risperidone or aripiprazole help when SSRIs fail. It tempts because compulsions look like habit-like behaviours of the basal ganglia, but dopamine is an augmentation target, not the primary system."}},{"q":"What are the clinical features of conversion disorder?","concept":"Conversion disorder is the modern name for what was once called hysteria: genuine loss or alteration of voluntary motor or sensory function, such as paralysis, tremor, non-epileptic seizures, blindness or numbness, not explained by neurological disease. Jean-Martin Charcot studied such patients at the Salpetriere in the 1870s and 1880s, and Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895) popularised 'conversion' for the idea that unacceptable psychic conflict is converted into a bodily symptom. Freud separated primary gain (anxiety kept out of awareness) from secondary gain (attention, or relief from duties). The classic picture, still examined through ICD-10 (F44, dissociative (conversion) disorders) and DSM-IV, is: symptoms affecting motor or sensory function, sudden onset, a close time link to a significant psychosocial stressor, no intentional production, and sometimes la belle indifference, a surprising calm about the disability. DSM-5 (2013) added the name functional neurological symptom disorder (made the main title in DSM-5-TR, 2022), required positive evidence that the symptom is incompatible with recognised neurological disease, such as Hoover's sign in functional leg weakness, and dropped the requirements for a stressor and for ruling out feigning. Tell the neighbours apart by intention and motive: conversion symptoms are not intentional; factitious disorder symptoms are deliberately produced to occupy the sick role without external reward; malingering symptoms are deliberately produced for external reward such as compensation. Somatic symptom disorder centres on excessive worry about real symptoms. Option b is right because it lists the defining clinical triad: altered motor or sensory function, sudden onset, and development after a significant psychosocial stressor.","traps":{"a":"Normal examination and investigations are true of conversion disorder, but they are equally true of factitious disorder, malingering, somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety, so this is not a defining feature. Modern practice also warns against diagnosis by exclusion alone; DSM-5 asks for positive signs of incompatibility with neurological disease. It tempts because it is a correct statement, yet it is incomplete and does not distinguish conversion from its neighbours.","c":"This is half right. DSM-IV did say conversion symptoms 'suggest a neurological or other general medical condition', but that phrase was its general definition of all somatoform disorders, so it does not single out conversion. The specific feature is that the symptoms affect voluntary motor or sensory function. It tempts because the rest of the option (sudden onset, psychosocial stressor) is word for word the same as the keyed answer.","d":"Intentional symptom production describes factitious disorder, where the person feigns or induces illness to occupy the sick role without external reward (its severe form named Munchausen syndrome by Richard Asher in 1951), and malingering, where the motive is external gain. Conversion symptoms are by definition not intentionally produced and typically follow a stressor closely. It tempts because 'no secondary gain' sounds like a familiar textbook phrase."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic belief associated with Koro?","concept":"Koro is a culture-bound syndrome, now called a cultural concept of distress, in which a person suddenly and intensely fears that the penis (in women, the vulva or nipples) is shrinking and retracting into the body, and that complete retraction will cause death. The name comes from the Malay and Indonesian region; in Chinese tradition it is suo yang ('shrinking of yang'), tied to beliefs about loss of vital essence. In the acute panic, the person or relatives may clamp or tie the organ to stop it disappearing. Koro occurs sporadically and also in epidemics spread by rumour, notably in Singapore (1967) and in north-eastern India and West Bengal, where Indian psychiatrists have documented outbreaks. DSM-IV listed it in its glossary of culture-bound syndromes, and DSM-5 (2013) gives it as an example of other specified obsessive-compulsive and related disorder, close to body dysmorphic disorder. Examiners rotate a set of culture-bound syndromes, so learn each by its core belief: Dhat syndrome (South Asia; anxiety about losing semen in urine, named by N. N. Wig in 1960); amok (Malaysia; brooding followed by a violent outburst and amnesia); latah (South-East Asia; startle-induced echolalia and echopraxia); taijin kyofusho (Japan; fear of offending others through one's body or gaze); windigo (Algonquian peoples; fear of becoming a cannibal monster); susto (Latin America; soul loss after a fright); and possession syndromes, now classed as dissociative trance or possession disorder. Option c is right because Koro is defined by the belief that the genitals are retracting into the body and that this may result in death.","traps":{"a":"Belief in possession by evil spirits describes possession syndromes, common in India as possession by a ghost, spirit or deity, and classed as trance and possession disorder (ICD-10 F44.3), a dissociative condition in which the person's usual identity is replaced by the possessing agent. It involves altered identity and trance, not fear of genital shrinkage. It tempts because both are culturally shaped conditions taught in the same lecture.","b":"A sense of impending doom tied to weather or atmospheric change is not a recognised culture-bound syndrome. It borrows the 'impending doom' of a panic attack and loosely echoes khyal cap ('wind attacks') among Cambodians, where a wind-like substance is believed to rise inside the body. Koro does involve panic and fear of death, which is why this tempts, but its specific content is genital retraction, not the environment.","d":"Believing that one is turning into a mythical creature or animal describes clinical lycanthropy (the delusion of becoming a wolf or other animal) or windigo among Algonquian peoples, the fear of being transformed into a cannibal monster. These concern transformation of identity, not shrinking organs. It tempts because koro, like these, involves a bizarre-sounding bodily belief rooted in cultural folklore."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic feature of Borderline Personality Disorder?","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a pervasive pattern, beginning by early adulthood, of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity. Adolph Stern (1938) coined 'borderline' for patients he saw as lying on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organization, marked by identity diffusion and primitive defences such as splitting. DSM-5 (2013) lists nine criteria, of which five are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealization and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substances, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability; chronic emptiness; intense, inappropriate anger; and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. ICD-10 calls it emotionally unstable personality disorder, borderline type (F60.31), alongside an impulsive type. Marsha Linehan (1993) explained it through her biosocial theory, emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment, and created dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), the best-supported treatment. To separate the personality disorders used as options, look for the core motive. BPD and dependent personality disorder both fear being left, but dependent patients cling submissively, whereas borderline patients swing into rage and self-harm. Histrionic patients seek attention through shallow, dramatic emotion; antisocial patients disregard others' rights; avoidant patients (Cluster C) shun people from fear of criticism while wanting acceptance. Borderline, histrionic, antisocial and narcissistic make up Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. Option d is right because unstable relationships, impulsive behaviour and fear of abandonment are the hallmark features of BPD.","traps":{"a":"Social inhibition, fear of rejection and desire for acceptance describe avoidant personality disorder, a Cluster C (anxious, fearful) disorder marked by feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation. The avoidant person withdraws from relationships to escape criticism, whereas the borderline person plunges into intense, stormy ones. It tempts because 'fear of rejection' sounds very like the borderline 'fear of abandonment'.","b":"Excessive attention-seeking, rapidly shifting but shallow emotion and theatrical behaviour describe histrionic personality disorder, another Cluster B disorder. The histrionic person needs to be the centre of attention and uses appearance and drama to get it, without the self-harm, emptiness and identity disturbance of BPD. It tempts because 'emotional volatility' overlaps with borderline affective instability and both belong to Cluster B.","c":"Disregard for and violation of others' rights, deceit and manipulation describe antisocial personality disorder, which DSM-5 diagnoses only from age 18 with evidence of conduct disorder before age 15. Hervey Cleckley (1941) described the related construct of psychopathy. It tempts because borderline patients can also be impulsive and are sometimes labelled manipulative, but their behaviour is driven by fear of abandonment, not callous exploitation."}},{"q":"What is Dysthymia?","concept":"Dysthymia (from Greek, 'bad state of mind') is chronic, low-grade depression. The term dysthymic disorder entered DSM-III (1980), replacing the older 'depressive neurosis', and DSM-5 (2013) merged it with chronic major depression under the name persistent depressive disorder (PDD). The DSM-5 criteria: depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years (one year in children and adolescents, where mood may be irritable), plus at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and hopelessness. The person is never free of symptoms for more than two months at a stretch, and there has never been a manic or hypomanic episode. ICD-10 codes dysthymia as F34.1 and places it, with cyclothymia (F34.0), among the persistent mood (affective) disorders. When a major depressive episode is superimposed on dysthymia, clinicians call it 'double depression'. The examiner's favourite trap is the set of chronic or recurrent mood conditions, which differ by polarity and severity: dysthymia is persistent mild depression only (unipolar); cyclothymia is at least two years of fluctuating mild depressive and hypomanic symptoms (bipolar spectrum, low severity); bipolar I disorder involves at least one manic episode; bipolar II disorder combines major depressive episodes with hypomania. Remember that the two-year rule applies to both dysthymia and cyclothymia, so duration alone cannot separate them; polarity does. Option c is right because dysthymia is defined as a persistent, chronic, mild depression without any manic or hypomanic phases.","traps":{"a":"Severe depression alternating with mania describes bipolar I disorder, the descendant of the 'manic-depressive insanity' of Emil Kraepelin (1899), in which at least one full manic episode occurs and major depressive episodes usually do too. Dysthymia is mild, chronic and purely depressive, with no manic phases. It tempts because 'dysthymia' sounds like a general term for disturbed mood, and candidates link any mood word with mood swings.","b":"Persistent instability of mood between mild depression and mild elation is cyclothymia (cyclothymic disorder), a bipolar spectrum condition lasting at least two years in adults, in which hypomanic and depressive symptoms never meet full episode criteria. It tempts because cyclothymia and dysthymia are both persistent mood disorders in ICD-10, share the two-year rule and are mild. The difference is polarity: cyclothymia goes up and down, dysthymia only down.","d":"Mild depression together with hypomania over two years is a bipolar spectrum picture, closest to cyclothymia, or to bipolar II disorder if full major depressive episodes occur. DSM-5 explicitly rules out persistent depressive disorder if there has ever been a manic or hypomanic episode. It tempts because it copies the correct 'persistent mild depression' and the two-year duration, then slips in hypomania."}},{"q":"According to Psychodynamic Theory, what is the primary defense mechanism used to deal with anxiety?","concept":"Defence mechanisms are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety arising from conflict between the id's impulses, the superego's demands and reality. Sigmund Freud put repression at the centre: in 1914 he called the theory of repression 'the corner-stone on which the whole structure of psycho-analysis rests', and his paper 'Repression' (1915) defined its essence as turning something away and keeping it at a distance from consciousness. Repression is motivated forgetting: threatening wishes, memories and feelings are pushed out of awareness, yet they stay active and return in disguised form as dreams, slips and symptoms. Freud separated primal repression, which keeps material from ever becoming conscious, from repression proper, which expels material that was once conscious. In Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) he revised the order: anxiety is a signal that sets repression in motion, not its product. Anna Freud (1936) systematised the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence and called repression the most efficacious and also the most dangerous of them. Contrast repression with suppression, the conscious, deliberate putting aside of thoughts, and with denial, the refusal to accept an external reality. George Vaillant (1977) ranked defences from psychotic and immature to neurotic and mature; repression sits at the neurotic level with displacement, isolation and reaction formation. Freud also tied specific defences to specific neuroses: displacement to phobias (Little Hans, 1909), isolation and undoing to obsessional neurosis, and conversion to hysteria. Option c is right because repression is the primary, foundational defence by which the ego keeps anxiety-provoking material out of consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Displacement shifts an impulse or emotion from its real, threatening target to a safer substitute, as when a man scolded by his boss shouts at his child. Freud used it to explain phobias: Little Hans (1909) displaced fear of his father onto horses. It is a real defence, but a secondary one that redirects material rather than barring it from awareness. It tempts because it is the textbook's most popular example of a defence.","b":"Conversion, in classical theory, is the mechanism of hysteria by which repressed conflict is transformed into a bodily symptom such as paralysis or blindness, as described by Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895). It is better seen as a form of symptom formation that follows repression, not as the basic defence against anxiety. It tempts because conversion disorder is well known and the word sounds like a defence.","d":"Isolation, or isolation of affect, separates an idea from its emotional charge, so a person can describe a traumatic event in a flat, detached way. Freud linked it, with undoing, to obsessional neurosis. It lets thoughts into consciousness stripped of feeling, so it is a later, partial defence, not the primary one. It tempts because 'isolation' sounds like shutting material away, which is what repression actually does."}},{"q":"What were the two clinical types differentiated by Emil Kraepelin?","concept":"Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926), a German psychiatrist who had trained in Wilhelm Wundt's laboratory, is called the father of modern psychiatric classification. Before him, diagnoses were mostly lists of symptoms. Kraepelin recorded thousands of cases on summary cards and followed patients over years, insisting that a disease is defined by its symptoms together with its course and outcome. On this basis, in the sixth edition of his textbook (1899), he divided the major psychoses into two groups, the Kraepelinian dichotomy. Dementia praecox (early dementia) began in youth and ran a chronic, deteriorating course; into it he merged hebephrenia, described by Ewald Hecker (1871), catatonia, described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874), and paranoid forms. Manic-depressive insanity ran in episodes with recovery in between and covered mania, melancholia and mixed states. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed dementia praecox 'schizophrenia', meaning a splitting of psychic functions, because many patients did not deteriorate and onset was not always early; he described the four A's (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism). Karl Leonhard (1957) later divided manic-depressive illness into unipolar and bipolar forms, a split that DSM-III (1980) adopted. The separation of schizophrenia spectrum disorders from mood disorders in DSM-5 and ICD-11 still follows Kraepelin, although schizoaffective disorder and shared genetic risk challenge the dichotomy. Variant questions ask who coined 'schizophrenia' (Bleuler), what criterion Kraepelin used (course and outcome), or who split unipolar from bipolar (Leonhard). Option c is right because the two clinical types Kraepelin differentiated were dementia praecox and manic-depressive illness.","traps":{"a":"Depression and anxiety are the two common non-psychotic conditions of modern psychiatry and primary care. Kraepelin's dichotomy concerned the major psychoses seen in asylums, and depression sat inside his manic-depressive insanity rather than being contrasted with anxiety. It tempts because depression and anxiety are the most familiar pair in present-day psychology, and the idea of depression also appears in the correct option.","b":"Schizophrenia is the 1911 name that Eugen Bleuler gave to Kraepelin's dementia praecox, and borderline personality disorder comes from Adolph Stern (1938) and Otto Kernberg (1967), entering DSM-III in 1980. Neither label belongs to Kraepelin's dichotomy, and a personality disorder is not a psychosis. It tempts because half of the option is the modern equivalent of one of Kraepelin's two categories.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder and bipolar disorder are modern DSM labels. Bipolar disorder descends from Kraepelin's manic-depressive insanity through the unipolar and bipolar split of Karl Leonhard (1957), but OCD is a neurosis, not a psychosis, and was never one of Kraepelin's two groups. It tempts because one half again matches a Kraepelinian category in modern dress, so candidates who spot 'bipolar' stop reading."}},{"q":"What is the sheath/kosh surrounding atman that is involved in perceptual processing","concept":"The Pancha Kosha (five sheaths) model comes from the Taittiriya Upanishad and was elaborated in Advaita Vedanta texts. It describes the human being as the atman (Self) covered by five layers, from gross to subtle, like the layers of an onion. Annamaya kosha is the food sheath, the physical body. Pranamaya kosha is the vital sheath of breath and life energy, the five pranas working with the five organs of action. Manomaya kosha is the mental sheath: manas (mind) together with the five organs of perception (jnanendriyas: eyes, ears, nose, tongue, skin). It receives sense impressions, organises them into percepts, and is the seat of desire, emotion, doubt and indecision. Vijnanamaya kosha is the intellect sheath: buddhi with the organs of perception, which discriminates, judges, decides and carries the sense of agency. Anandamaya kosha, the innermost, is the bliss sheath, experienced in deep dreamless sleep. The Self is none of the sheaths, and Vedanta teaches discrimination (viveka) to stop identifying with them. The five sheaths map onto three bodies (sharira): gross (annamaya), subtle (pranamaya, manomaya, vijnanamaya) and causal (anandamaya). In Indian psychology and yoga therapy the model serves as a holistic framework for health, with practices aimed at each level, such as asanas for the body, pranayama for the vital sheath and meditation for the mental sheath. The key exam distinction is manas versus buddhi: manas perceives and wavers, buddhi judges and decides. Option d is right because the manomaya kosha, formed of manas and the organs of perception, carries out perceptual processing.","traps":{"a":"Ananda-maya kosha is the bliss sheath, the innermost and subtlest layer, identified with the causal body and with the peace of deep dreamless sleep. It lies closest to the atman and involves no sensory activity at all. It tempts because it is the best-known kosha, and candidates sometimes assume that the deepest layer controls everything, including perception.","b":"Vijnana-maya kosha is the intellect or wisdom sheath, formed by buddhi with the organs of perception. It discriminates, judges, decides and holds the sense of 'I am the doer', working on what manas has already perceived. It tempts because 'vijnana' means knowledge or understanding and perception sounds cognitive, but in this model judgment belongs to buddhi and perception to manas.","c":"Prana-maya kosha is the vital or energy sheath, made of the five pranas (prana, apana, vyana, udana, samana) working with the five organs of action. It governs breathing, circulation, digestion and other physiological functions and links body to mind. It tempts because the sense organs are alive and energised by prana, but the processing of sense impressions is assigned to the mental sheath."}},{"q":"Which school of Buddhism focuses on nirvana via collective suffering?","concept":"Buddhism began with Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in north India around the fifth century BCE, and later divided into schools that differ in their ideal goal. Theravada ('teaching of the elders'), based on the Pali Canon and found today in Sri Lanka, Myanmar and Thailand, takes the arhat as its ideal: a person who has personally extinguished craving and reached nirvana through ethics, meditation and wisdom. Mahayana ('great vehicle') arose around the start of the Common Era, with texts such as the Perfection of Wisdom (Prajnaparamita) sutras and the Lotus Sutra, and philosophers such as Nagarjuna (around the second century CE). Its ideal is the bodhisattva, who vows to postpone final nirvana until all sentient beings are freed from suffering. Its central virtues are karuna (compassion) and prajna (wisdom), and its key doctrine is sunyata (emptiness). Mahayana writers called the earlier path Hinayana ('lesser vehicle'), a term now seen as pejorative. Vajrayana, the tantric form of Tibet, and Zen, which grew from Chinese Chan and stresses sudden insight through meditation, are later lineages that grew out of Mahayana and keep the bodhisattva ideal, but they are named for their methods. In psychology, Theravada vipassana and Zen fed mindfulness programmes such as Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction, founded by Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979), while Mahayana compassion practices inform compassion-focused interventions. The stem's 'collective suffering' means the shared suffering of all beings, from which Mahayana seeks collective liberation. Option b is right because Mahayana aims at nirvana for all beings together, through the bodhisattva who works for universal liberation.","traps":{"a":"Theravada, the 'teaching of the elders', is the oldest surviving school, based on the Pali Canon and followed in Sri Lanka, Myanmar and Thailand. Its ideal is the arhat, who reaches nirvana through his or her own practice of ethics, meditation and insight, so its focus is individual liberation. It tempts because it is the best-known 'original' Buddhism and is linked to the Four Noble Truths that every exam asks about.","c":"Vajrayana, the 'diamond' or 'thunderbolt vehicle', is tantric Buddhism, dominant in Tibet, Bhutan and Mongolia. It grew out of Mahayana and keeps the bodhisattva ideal, but it is defined by its esoteric methods: mantras, mandalas, visualisation, initiation by a guru and the aim of enlightenment within one lifetime. It tempts because it shares Mahayana compassion for all beings, yet the school identified with collective liberation is Mahayana itself.","d":"Zen is the Japanese form of Chinese Chan Buddhism, traditionally traced to Bodhidharma, which stresses seated meditation (zazen), koans and sudden, direct insight into one's true nature beyond scriptures. It is a Mahayana lineage and accepts the bodhisattva vow, but it is known for its meditative method rather than for the doctrine of collective salvation. It tempts because Zen is the most familiar Buddhist name in psychology."}},{"q":"The path to the cessation of suffering, as described in the fourth Noble Truth, is known as the _______.","concept":"The Four Noble Truths are the Buddha's first teaching, given in the Deer Park at Sarnath near Varanasi in the sermon called the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, 'setting in motion the wheel of the Dhamma'. They follow the logic of a physician: diagnosis, cause, prognosis and treatment. The First Truth is dukkha: life as ordinarily lived involves suffering or unsatisfactoriness, including birth, ageing, illness, death and separation from what we love. The Second is samudaya, the origin of suffering, which is tanha (craving or thirst) for sense pleasures, for existence and for non-existence. The Third is nirodha, the cessation of suffering when craving is fully extinguished, which is nirvana (Pali: nibbana). The Fourth is magga, the path leading to that cessation, the Noble Eightfold Path: right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness and right concentration. These eight are grouped into three trainings: wisdom (panna), ethical conduct (sila) and concentration (samadhi). The path is also called the Middle Way, avoiding both sensual indulgence and extreme asceticism. Do not mix the truths with the three marks of existence: anicca (impermanence), dukkha (suffering) and anatta (non-self). Examiners also test the order: dukkha, samudaya, nirodha, magga. Clinical psychology meets this framework in mindfulness-based therapies, which draw on right mindfulness, and in the idea that suffering is maintained by craving and clinging, which parallels experiential avoidance in the acceptance and commitment therapy of Steven Hayes (1999). Option b is right because magga, meaning 'path', is the Fourth Noble Truth, the way to the cessation of suffering.","traps":{"a":"Samudaya means 'arising' or 'origin' and is the Second Noble Truth: suffering arises from tanha, craving for sense pleasure, for existence and for non-existence. It explains why suffering exists, not how to end it. It tempts because candidates remember that one truth deals with what to do about craving, and samudaya is the truth that actually names craving.","c":"Nirodha means 'cessation' and is the Third Noble Truth: suffering ends when craving is completely extinguished, the state called nirvana. It is the goal, not the route. The stem asks for the path to cessation, which is the Fourth Truth. It tempts because the stem itself contains the word 'cessation', so a candidate matching words picks nirodha instead of the path that leads there.","d":"Anicca means impermanence, one of the three marks of existence along with dukkha (suffering) and anatta (non-self). All conditioned things arise and pass away, which is why clinging to them causes suffering. It is a characteristic of reality, not one of the Four Noble Truths at all. It tempts because it is a familiar Pali term from the same chapter on Buddhist psychology."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, the Trigunas belong to","concept":"The Bhagavad Gita, a 700-verse dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna in the Bhishma Parva of the Mahabharata, borrows much of its metaphysics from Samkhya, the dualist school traditionally founded by the sage Kapila and set out systematically in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika. Samkhya holds that reality has two independent principles. Purusha is pure consciousness: the passive witness, unchanging and without qualities (nirguna). Prakriti is primordial nature or matter: active, changing and made of the three gunas. Sattva is lightness, clarity and harmony; rajas is activity, passion and restlessness; tamas is heaviness, inertia and darkness. In the unmanifest state the gunas are in balance; when the balance is disturbed, Prakriti evolves into buddhi (mahat), ahamkara, manas, the senses and the elements, so even the mind belongs to Prakriti. The Gita states the point directly in chapter 14, verse 5: sattva, rajas and tamas are 'born of Prakriti' and bind the embodied self to the body. Chapters 14, 17 and 18 apply the gunas to food, faith, charity, action and personality, and the ideal person is gunatita, one who rises above all three. Freedom comes when the self recognises that the gunas act and it only witnesses. Indian psychologists have built triguna personality scales on this model. Variant questions ask which principle is free of the gunas (Purusha), what Prakriti evolves into, or how the Gita's field (kshetra, body and nature) differs from its knower (kshetrajna, consciousness) in chapter 13. Option c is right because the Gita describes the three gunas as born of, and belonging to, Prakriti.","traps":{"a":"Samadhi is meditative absorption, the eighth and final limb of the eight-limbed yoga in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, in which the mind becomes one with its object and ordinary distinctions dissolve. It is a state of consciousness reached by stilling the play of the gunas, not the principle to which they belong. It tempts because samadhi is a familiar Sanskrit term from the same Indian psychology unit.","b":"Purusha is pure consciousness in Samkhya and the Gita, the eternal witness that is nirguna, without qualities. The gunas belong to the other principle, and bondage arises only because Purusha wrongly identifies with their play. It tempts because Purusha and Prakriti are always taught as a pair, so a candidate who remembers the pair but not which side holds the gunas is left with a fifty-fifty guess.","d":"Samskaras are latent impressions left by past actions and experiences, stored in the mind (chitta) and shaping future tendencies, as described in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras. They are coloured by the gunas, but they are products of mental activity, not the ground to which the gunas belong. It tempts because samskaras, like the gunas, are used to explain personality and habitual behaviour in Indian psychology."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, what are the three elements of the mind?","concept":"Indian psychology describes the mind as an inner instrument, the antahkarana, distinct from the outer instruments, the senses. The Bhagavad Gita lists its parts when Krishna describes his lower nature in chapter 7, verse 4: earth, water, fire, air, space, manas, buddhi and ahamkara make up his eightfold Prakriti. So in the Gita the mind is not the true self; it is a subtle part of nature, while the self is the knower beyond it. Manas is the sensory mind: it receives input from the senses, attends, desires and wavers, the faculty of doubt and imagination (sankalpa and vikalpa). Buddhi is the intellect: it discriminates, judges and decides, and it is what yoga trains to be steady, as in the sthitaprajna, the person of steady wisdom, of chapter 2. Ahamkara is the ego or 'I-maker', which claims experiences and actions as 'mine' and creates the sense of being the doer. Chapter 3, verse 42 gives a hierarchy: the senses are high, the mind is higher than the senses, the intellect is higher than the mind, and the self is higher than the intellect. Samkhya also derives buddhi, ahamkara and manas from Prakriti, and later Vedanta adds a fourth part, chitta (the store of memory), giving a fourfold antahkarana. Examiners build distractors from other well-known Indian triads: the three gunas (qualities of nature), the purusharthas (goals of life) and metaphysical categories such as matter, consciousness and field. Option a is right because manas, buddhi and ahamkara are the three elements of the mind named in the Gita.","traps":{"b":"Prakriti (nature or matter) and purusha (consciousness) are the two ultimate principles of Samkhya, and kshetra (the field, meaning body and nature) is paired in Gita chapter 13 with kshetrajna, the knower of the field. These are metaphysical categories describing all of reality, not components of the mind. It tempts because the Gita discusses them at length and says the mind itself is part of prakriti and of the field.","c":"Dharma (duty, righteousness), artha (wealth) and kama (pleasure, desire) are three of the four purusharthas, the goals of human life, together called the trivarga; moksha (liberation) is the fourth. They describe what a person pursues, not what the mind is made of. It tempts because it is another famous Sanskrit triad and appears in the same syllabus unit on Indian psychology.","d":"Sattva, rajas and tamas are the three gunas, the qualities that make up Prakriti. They colour every mental state, so a mind can be called sattvic, rajasic or tamasic, but they are qualities operating in the mind, not its structural parts. It tempts because the gunas are the Gita's best-known psychological triad and are often presented as the basis of personality."}},{"q":"What are the four major goals of human life according to Indian culture?","concept":"The purusharthas, literally 'aims of a person', are the four goals that classical Hindu thought holds a full human life should pursue. Dharma is duty, righteousness and moral order, including the obligations of one's stage of life and social role. Artha is wealth, livelihood, power and material security. Kama is desire, pleasure, love and aesthetic enjoyment. Moksha is liberation from samsara, the cycle of birth and rebirth. The first three, called the trivarga, are worldly aims that must be balanced, with dharma regulating the pursuit of artha and kama; moksha is the transcendent fourth goal. Each has its classical texts: the Dharmashastras, such as the Manusmriti, for dharma; Kautilya's Arthashastra for artha; and Vatsyayana's Kamasutra for kama. The purusharthas are paired with the four ashramas, or stages of life: brahmacharya (student), grihastha (householder), vanaprastha (forest dweller, gradual withdrawal) and sannyasa (renunciant), with artha and kama most prominent for the householder and moksha for the renunciant. Psychologists often compare the scheme with the hierarchy of needs of Abraham Maslow (1943), likening moksha to the top of the hierarchy, self-actualisation and later self-transcendence, though the purusharthas are aims to be balanced rather than needs met in strict order. Distractors swap in plausible but non-canonical goals such as knowledge, fame, fitness or enlightenment; the test is to remember the Sanskrit four and translate them exactly. A simple memory aid is DAKM: dharma, artha, kama, moksha. Option c is right because duty, wealth, desires and liberation are the English equivalents of dharma, artha, kama and moksha.","traps":{"a":"Physical fitness and fame are modern-sounding substitutes, and neither is a purushartha. Fitness may be part of living dharmically, and fame loosely relates to artha, but the canonical list contains dharma and moksha, both missing here. Wealth and desires are correct, so the option looks half right. It tempts because it mixes two genuine goals with two everyday aims that many people actually pursue.","b":"Knowledge and enlightenment sound spiritual but are not the canonical terms. Knowledge (jnana) is a path to liberation, as in jnana yoga, not a separate goal, and while enlightenment loosely resembles moksha, the option drops dharma, the first and governing aim. It tempts because 'enlightenment' looks like a translation of moksha, and 'wealth, desires' are correct.","d":"This keeps dharma and kama but replaces artha with knowledge and moksha with fame or power. Liberation, the highest goal, is missing altogether, and wealth is missing too. It tempts because power is part of the broad meaning of artha (Kautilya's Arthashastra is a treatise on statecraft), so a candidate who half remembers artha as power may choose it."}},{"q":"Which of the following is an astik system in Indian thought?","concept":"Classical Indian philosophy divides its systems (darshanas, 'ways of seeing') into astika and nastika. The criterion is acceptance of the authority of the Vedas, not belief in God. The six astika (orthodox) schools come in three pairs: Nyaya (logic, attributed to Gautama) with Vaisheshika (atomism, Kanada); Samkhya (dualism of purusha and prakriti, Kapila) with Yoga (Patanjali's eight-limbed discipline of the mind); and Purva Mimamsa (ritual interpretation of the Vedas, Jaimini) with Uttara Mimamsa or Vedanta (Badarayana's Brahma Sutras). Vedanta interprets the Upanishads and has three main sub-schools. Advaita (non-dualism), systematised by Adi Shankaracharya around the eighth century CE, holds that Brahman alone is real, that the individual self (atman) is identical with Brahman, and that the world of multiplicity is maya. Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism) is associated with Ramanuja, and Dvaita (dualism) with Madhva. The nastika (heterodox) schools reject Vedic authority: Buddhism, Jainism, the materialist Charvaka or Lokayata, and the Ajivikas. A classic trap is that astika does not mean theist: classical Samkhya and Purva Mimamsa are astika although they need no creator God, while Jainism and Buddhism are nastika despite rich ethical and spiritual systems. Sufism, the mystical tradition of Islam brought to India by orders such as the Chishtis, stands outside this Vedic classification altogether. In Indian psychology, the Advaita model of the self and the Yoga model of the mind are the most cited sources. Option b is right because Advaita-Vedanta accepts the authority of the Vedas and is one of the six orthodox (astika) darshanas.","traps":{"a":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, devotional tradition within Islam, which reached India in the medieval period through orders such as the Chishti, Suhrawardi and Naqshbandi, and deeply influenced devotional poetry and music. It does not accept the Vedas and was never part of the astika and nastika classification, which applies only to Indian darshanas. It tempts because Sufi mysticism resembles Advaita in seeking union with the divine.","c":"Buddhism, founded by Siddhartha Gautama, rejects the authority of the Vedas, the Vedic ritual system and the idea of a permanent self (anatta), so it is classed as nastika (heterodox). It tempts because candidates wrongly equate astika with 'spiritual' or 'religious', and Buddhism is plainly both. It also arose in India, unlike Sufism, which makes it look like an orthodox Indian school.","d":"Jainism, whose twenty-fourth tirthankara Mahavira was a contemporary of the Buddha, rejects the authority of the Vedas and of a creator God, and teaches liberation through ahimsa, austerity and the three jewels (right faith, right knowledge, right conduct). It is therefore nastika. It tempts because Jainism accepts karma, rebirth and an eternal soul (jiva), ideas shared with the astika schools."}},{"q":"What are the three gunas in the Upanishads?","concept":"The three gunas, literally 'strands' or 'qualities', are the constituents of Prakriti, primordial nature, in Indian thought. The idea has roots in the Upanishads: the Chandogya Upanishad speaks of three colours (red, white and black) present in all things, the Shvetashvatara Upanishad describes an unborn nature of three colours, often read as the gunas, and later Upanishads such as the Maitri name sattva, rajas and tamas explicitly. Samkhya then systematised the theory, and the Bhagavad Gita gave it its best-known psychological form in chapters 14, 17 and 18. Sattva is purity, lightness, clarity, knowledge and harmony; its colour is white. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, ambition and restlessness; its colour is red. Tamas is inertia, darkness, dullness, ignorance and sloth; its colour is black. Every object, food, action and mental state contains all three in varying proportions, and personality is described by whichever guna dominates. The spiritual aim is to cultivate sattva and finally go beyond all three. Indian psychologists have built triguna personality inventories on this model. Do not confuse the gunas with the three doshas of Ayurveda (vata, pitta, kapha), which are bodily humours, although Ayurveda does treat rajas and tamas as the two mental doshas. Other triads that examiners use as distractors are purusha, prakriti and kshetra (metaphysical categories), atman, Brahman and maya (Vedantic ontology), and karma, dharma and moksha (action, duty and liberation). Option b is right because rajas, tamas and sattva are the three gunas, simply listed here in a different order.","traps":{"a":"Prakriti (nature), purusha (consciousness) and kshetra (the field, meaning body and nature as the arena of experience, from Gita chapter 13) are metaphysical categories, not qualities. The gunas are the stuff of which prakriti is made, so this option names their container rather than the gunas themselves. It tempts because prakriti and the gunas are taught together, and any list of Sanskrit metaphysical terms looks plausible.","c":"Atman (the individual self), Brahman (the absolute reality) and maya (the power by which the one appears as many) are the pillars of Vedantic, especially Advaita, ontology, and they come from the Upanishads themselves. They describe the nature of self and reality, not the qualities of nature. It tempts because the stem says 'in the Upanishads', and these three are the most famous Upanishadic concepts.","d":"Karma (action and its consequences), dharma (duty, moral order) and moksha (liberation from rebirth) are key ideas of Hindu ethics, with dharma and moksha also among the four purusharthas. They concern how to act and what to aim for, not the qualities that make up nature and mind. It tempts because this is another familiar Sanskrit triad with a spiritual tone."}},{"q":"Bavisha lost her job and has become so stressed that she cannot eat, has withdrawn, and cannot get herself to function, sunk in inertia. This dull, lethargic, cannot-act state reflects the dominance of which?","concept":"The three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas, come from Samkhya philosophy, the dualist school traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and systematised in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika. Samkhya separates purusha, pure consciousness, from prakriti, primordial nature, and holds that prakriti is woven from these three strands, so everything in nature, including mind and personality, is a mixture of them. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) applies them directly to human character. Sattva is lightness, clarity, calm, knowledge and balance. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, restlessness, ambition and anger. Tamas is heaviness, darkness, dullness, inertia, sloth, excess sleep, confusion and an inability to act. All three are always present; the dominant one colours a person's state at a given time, and the balance can shift. Ayurveda (Charaka Samhita) treats rajas and tamas as the two mental doshas, and modern Indian psychology uses the gunas as a personality typology with its own questionnaires. A useful clinical mapping for exams: withdrawal, psychomotor slowing, poor appetite and hopeless inactivity look tamasic; agitation, craving, irritability and frantic activity look rajasic; equanimity and insight look sattvic. Examiners build distractors from other Indian frameworks: the eight limbs (ashtanga) of Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, such as yama and pratyahara, which are practices, and the paths (margas) of the Gita, such as jnana, karma and bhakti, which are routes to liberation. A guna is a quality or state of mind; a limb or path is something you do. Option a is right because Bavisha's dull, lethargic, withdrawn, unable-to-act state is the textbook description of tamas dominance.","traps":{"b":"Yama is the first of the eight limbs (ashtanga) of Patanjali's Yoga Sutras: yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana and samadhi. The five yamas are ethical restraints: non-violence (ahimsa), truthfulness (satya), non-stealing (asteya), sexual restraint (brahmacharya) and non-possessiveness (aparigraha). Yama is a rule of conduct a person chooses to follow, not a mental state that overwhelms someone after a job loss. It tempts because it is a familiar Sanskrit term from the same Indian psychology unit.","c":"Pratyahara is the fifth limb of Patanjali's ashtanga yoga: deliberately withdrawing the senses from their objects so that attention turns inward, as preparation for dharana (concentration). It is a voluntary, trained discipline. Bavisha's withdrawal is involuntary, a collapse of energy and interest, not skilled sense control. It tempts because the stem uses the word 'withdrawn', and pratyahara is usually translated as 'withdrawal of the senses', so the surface match pulls candidates toward it.","d":"Jnana means knowledge, and jnana yoga is the path to liberation through discriminating the real from the unreal, one of the paths taught in the Bhagavad Gita alongside karma yoga (action) and bhakti yoga (devotion). Clear knowledge is a mark of sattva; tamas is exactly what clouds it. So jnana describes the opposite of Bavisha's state. It tempts as a well-known Indian psychology term for candidates who recognise the Sanskrit but not the guna framework."}},{"q":"What are the components of the Linga sharira (subtle body) according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"Indian psychology describes the person through two overlapping schemes. The first is the three bodies (sharira traya): the gross body (sthula sharira), the subtle body (sukshma or linga sharira) and the causal body (karana sharira). The second is the five sheaths (pancha kosha) of the Taittiriya Upanishad, layered from outer to inner: annamaya kosha (the food or physical sheath), pranamaya kosha (the vital sheath of breath and the five pranas), manomaya kosha (the mental sheath of manas, emotion and sense processing), vijnanamaya kosha (the intellect sheath of buddhi, judgement and doership) and anandamaya kosha (the bliss sheath). Advaita Vedanta texts such as Tattva Bodha and Vivekachudamani, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya (8th century CE), map one scheme onto the other: annamaya forms the gross body; pranamaya, manomaya and vijnanamaya together form the subtle body; anandamaya forms the causal body. The subtle body survives death and carries karmic impressions (samskaras) into the next birth. Atman, the Self, is not a sheath at all; it is pure consciousness, the witness that the five sheaths cover. The subtle body can also be listed by its 17 parts: five sense organs (jnanendriyas), five organs of action (karmendriyas), five pranas, manas and buddhi. Exams also use the antahkarana, the inner instrument: manas, buddhi, ahankara and chitta. Option a is right because it names the three middle koshas that make up the linga sharira; the added Atman is best read as the witnessing Self within, not as a fourth sheath.","traps":{"b":"This list adds annamaya kosha, the outermost food sheath, which is made of and sustained by food. It is the gross physical body (sthula sharira) that perishes at death, so it cannot be part of the subtle body, which begins with the pranamaya kosha. It tempts because three of its four items are correct and the list runs neatly from the outer sheath inward in textbook order, so it looks complete.","c":"Ahankara (the I-maker or ego), manas (the receiving, doubting mind), buddhi (the deciding intellect) and chitta (the store of memories and impressions) are the four functions of the antahkarana, the inner instrument of Vedanta psychology. The antahkarana does belong to the subtle body, but this is a list of mental functions, not of koshas, so it does not answer a question framed by the pancha kosha model. It tempts because all four are clearly subtle, mental entities.","d":"Jnanendriyas (five sense organs), karmendriyas (five organs of action), manas, buddhi and ahankara are tattvas, the evolutes of Samkhya, where the subtle body (linga) also includes the five subtle elements (tanmatras). The Vedanta version has 17 parts: ten indriyas, five pranas, manas and buddhi. Either way this is a list of elements, not of koshas. It tempts because it really does describe subtle body contents, but in a different classification from the one the stem asks for."}},{"q":"What are Samskara according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"The Pancha Kosha (five sheaths) model comes from the Taittiriya Upanishad, which describes the person as a set of nested sheaths around the Self (Atman). From outer to inner they are the annamaya kosha (food sheath, the physical body), pranamaya kosha (vital breath and energy), manomaya kosha (the mind that receives sense data and reacts), vijnanamaya kosha (intellect, discrimination, the sense of agency) and anandamaya kosha (the sheath of bliss). Vedanta maps these onto three bodies: the gross body (sthula sharira) is the food sheath; the subtle body (sukshma sharira) holds the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths; and the causal body (karana sharira) corresponds to the bliss sheath. Samskaras (from Sanskrit sam plus kr, 'to shape or put together') are the latent impressions that every action, thought and experience leaves behind. They lie dormant, then surface as habits, dispositions and automatic reactions. Clusters of samskaras form vasanas, enduring tendencies or desires. In the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, samskaras explain why patterns repeat and why practice and detachment are needed to weaken old impressions and plant new ones. Indian psychology texts therefore translate samskara as past tendencies or nature: the residue of the past that colours present personality, close to the modern ideas of habit, implicit memory and conditioned disposition. Keep three things apart: a sheath (a layer of the person), a body (gross, subtle or causal) and samskara (the impressions carried within those layers). Option b is right because samskaras are the accumulated past tendencies that shape a person's present nature.","traps":{"a":"The gross body (sthula sharira) is the annamaya kosha, the physical body built and maintained by food, which is born, grows and dies. It is the outermost layer of the person, not the store of past impressions. It tempts because the question names the kosha framework, and the gross body is the first and most familiar layer students memorise from it.","c":"The sense organs (jnanendriyas: ears, skin, eyes, tongue, nose) and organs of action (karmendriyas) are instruments of perception and action. In Vedanta these subtle faculties are grouped with the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths of the subtle body. They take in present experience; samskaras are what that experience leaves behind. It tempts because impressions begin as sense contact, so the two feel linked.","d":"The subtle body (sukshma sharira) combines the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths. In Vedanta it is the vehicle that carries samskaras from one life to the next, so it is closely linked to them. But a vehicle is not its contents: the question asks what samskaras are, not where they reside. That close link makes it the strongest distractor."}},{"q":"What is the process of psycho-spiritual transformation in Sufism?","concept":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, inward path within Islam. Indian psychology courses treat it as a psychology of transformation: a method for changing personality and consciousness, not just a set of beliefs. The central idea is the nafs, the lower or ego self. The Quran is read as describing stages of the nafs: the commanding self (nafs al-ammara) driven by impulse and desire, the blaming self (nafs al-lawwama) that notices its faults and feels remorse, and the tranquil self (nafs al-mutmainna) at peace with God. Later Sufi teachers added further stages. The seeker (salik) travels through stations (maqamat), stable qualities such as repentance, patience, trust and contentment that are earned by effort, and states (ahwal), passing experiences of grace such as longing or awe. Practices include dhikr (repeated remembrance of God), muraqaba (meditation), service, and guidance by a spiritual master (shaykh or pir), as in the Chishti order prominent in India. The goal is fana, the passing away of the ego self, followed by baqa, subsisting in God. A classic four-level map runs from shariah (law) to tariqah (path) to haqiqah (inner truth) to marifah (direct knowledge). Psychologically, this is a journey from ordinary surface awareness, dominated by desire and habit, toward deeper and higher levels of self-consciousness. Do not mix it with Buddhist psychology, whose path is built on the Four Noble Truths and the Noble Eightfold Path. Option b is right because it states the definition used in these texts: psycho-spiritual transformation as a journey from mundane surface awareness to the depths and heights of self-consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Guidelines and rules for the ordinary Muslim describe the shariah, the outward religious law covering prayer, fasting, charity and conduct. Sufis accept the shariah as the starting point, but the path of transformation begins where outward observance turns inward. The option describes rule-following, not a change in consciousness. It tempts because Sufism sits within Islam and any question on it brings Islamic law to mind.","c":"Moving from the outer layer of Islam to its inner dimension is a common general description of Sufism as the esoteric side of the faith, the step from shariah toward tariqah and haqiqah. It is not false, but it describes Sufism's place in religion, not the psychological process of transforming the self. It tempts because it sounds like a fair definition and is the closest rival to the keyed answer.","d":"The Four Noble Truths (suffering, its origin in craving, its cessation, and the path to cessation) are the core teaching of the Buddha, and the transformation they describe runs through the Noble Eightfold Path. They belong to Buddhist psychology, not Sufism. The option tempts because its first half, transformation of personality and consciousness, sounds exactly right before the wrong tradition is attached."}},{"q":"Which year saw the establishment of the first psychology department in India?","concept":"Academic psychology in India began at the University of Calcutta. An experimental psychology laboratory was set up there in 1915, and a separate Department of Psychology followed in 1916, the first in the country. The philosopher Brajendranath Seal drafted the 1916 syllabus on the model of the experimental psychology of Wilhelm Wundt, who had opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879. The department was led by Narendra Nath Sengupta (head from 1916), who had trained at Harvard under Hugo Munsterberg, a Wundt student brought to Harvard by William James in 1892. So early Indian psychology was laboratory based and Western in method, focused on perception, attention and psychophysics. Girindrasekhar Bose, a Calcutta physician who taught in the department and later headed it, founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922. The University of Mysore followed with psychology teaching in 1924 under M. V. Gopalaswamy, who had studied in London with Charles Spearman, known for his 1904 g factor. The Indian Journal of Psychology began in the mid-1920s. Variant questions are built from this timeline, so learn it as a chain: Wundt 1879, Calcutta laboratory 1915, Calcutta department 1916, Indian Psychoanalytical Society 1922, Mysore 1924. A later line of questions concerns indigenisation: Durganand Sinha (1986) argued that borrowed Western models fitted Indian social reality poorly. Option a is right because the University of Calcutta set up India's first psychology department in 1916.","traps":{"b":"1924 is usually cited as the year the University of Mysore began psychology teaching and a laboratory under M. V. Gopalaswamy, who had trained in London with Charles Spearman. That makes Mysore the second major centre, eight years after Calcutta. It tempts because it is a real founding date from the same early period, and candidates who remember 'a department in the 1920s' pick it.","c":"1905 predates academic psychology in India. In psychology it is best known as the year Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence scale in Paris; in Indian history it is the year of the partition of Bengal. It tempts because it is a famous psychology date and feels like a plausible 'early beginning' for the discipline in India.","d":"1938 is too late. By then Calcutta had taught psychology for over twenty years, Mysore had followed in 1924, and the Indian Psychoanalytical Society (1922) was well established. Psychology spread to many more universities in later decades, especially after independence. It tempts only as a guess for candidates who assume that academic psychology reached India late."}},{"q":"According to positivism, what is the nature of reality?","concept":"Research paradigms are sets of basic beliefs that answer three linked questions. Ontology asks what reality is. Epistemology asks what relationship the knower has with what can be known. Methodology asks how the inquirer can find it out. The standard comparison comes from Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), who set four paradigms side by side. Positivism, rooted in Auguste Comte's call in the 1830s for a science of society modelled on physics, holds naive realism: there is one real reality, driven by natural laws, and it can be apprehended as it is. Its epistemology is dualist and objectivist (the researcher stands apart and does not influence the object), and its findings are treated as true. Its method is experimental, verifying hypotheses with mainly quantitative data. Post-positivism holds critical realism: reality is real, but our grasp of it is imperfect and probabilistic, so findings are only probably true and hypotheses are tested by falsification, following Karl Popper (1934). Critical theory holds historical realism: a virtual reality shaped over time by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values, studied through dialogue to expose and change power. Constructivism holds relativism: multiple local and specific constructed realities, with findings co-created by researcher and participant through hermeneutic and dialectical methods. A fast way to sort options: 'apprehendable' means positivism, 'imperfectly and probabilistically' means post-positivism, 'shaped by values' means critical theory, 'constructed' means constructivism. Option c is right because positivism's naive realist ontology says a real reality exists and can be apprehended.","traps":{"a":"A virtual reality shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values, crystallised over time, is the historical realism of critical theory in Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994). It covers neo-Marxist, feminist and participatory inquiry aimed at exposing power. Positivism denies that values shape reality. It tempts because the option is long and detailed, which looks like a textbook definition.","b":"A real reality that can be apprehended only imperfectly and probabilistically is critical realism, the ontology of post-positivism. It keeps realism but admits that human observation is flawed, so findings are only probably true and are tested by falsification (Karl Popper, 1934). It tempts most because it differs from positivism by just one qualifier, 'imperfectly and probabilistically'.","d":"Only local and specific constructed realities is relativism, the ontology of constructivism, in which realities are mental constructions that differ across people and groups and are studied through hermeneutic and dialectical methods. It is the opposite end from positivism. It tempts because constructivism is the paradigm most often contrasted with positivism in qualitative research teaching."}},{"q":"What is the key goal of liberal feminism?","concept":"Feminist theories agree that women are disadvantaged but differ on the cause, and so on the cure. Exams test this by pairing each school with its goal. Liberal feminism is the oldest strand, growing from Enlightenment ideas of individual rights and reason. Mary Wollstonecraft (1792), in A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, argued that women appear inferior only because they are denied education. John Stuart Mill (1869), in The Subjection of Women, argued for legal equality. Betty Friedan (1963), in The Feminine Mystique, described the 'problem that has no name' among American housewives and helped found the National Organization for Women in 1966. The liberal diagnosis is that women are held back by unfair laws, customs and lack of opportunity, not by the system itself. So the cure is reform: equal rights, education, suffrage, equal pay, and access to jobs and public office, letting women enter and rise within mainstream institutions. Its rivals reject that strategy. Marxist feminism sees women's oppression as rooted in capitalism and class, and wants the system overthrown. Socialist feminism sees capitalism and patriarchy working together. Radical feminism sees patriarchy as the root and wants its basic assumptions challenged. Cultural feminism values traits linked with women and seeks to build a separate women's culture. The usual criticism is that liberal feminism mainly serves educated, middle-class women and leaves deeper structures untouched. Option c is right because liberal feminism's key goal is to integrate women into the existing structures of mainstream society through reform and equal opportunity.","traps":{"a":"Overthrowing the capitalist system is the aim of Marxist feminism, which follows Friedrich Engels (1884), The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State, in tracing women's subordination to private property and class society. On this view reform is not enough; the economic system must go. Liberal feminism wants change within existing institutions. It tempts because both are presented as equality movements.","b":"Building a women's culture is the aim of cultural feminism, which grew out of radical feminism in the 1970s. It celebrates qualities linked with women, such as care and cooperation, and builds women-centred spaces, art and communities rather than entering male institutions. That is the reverse of liberal feminism's integration. It tempts because it also sounds like empowerment.","d":"Recognising that women are oppressed by the capitalist system is the premise of Marxist and socialist feminism, which link women's unpaid domestic labour and low-paid work to the needs of capital. It is a diagnosis, not a liberal goal; liberals locate the problem in unequal laws and opportunity. It tempts because it sits beside 'overthrow capitalism' and splits candidates' attention."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of radical feminism?","concept":"Radical feminism arose in the late 1960s and 1970s within the women's liberation movement in the United States and Britain. 'Radical' means going to the root, and for radical feminists the root of women's oppression is patriarchy: a system of male power that runs through the family, sexuality, religion, law and culture, older and deeper than capitalism. Kate Millett (1970), in Sexual Politics, argued that the relationship between the sexes is a political relationship of domination. Shulamith Firestone (1970), in The Dialectic of Sex, located it in reproduction and the family. The slogan 'the personal is political', popularised by Carol Hanisch's 1970 essay, captures the method: problems women face in private life (housework, sexuality, violence at home) are products of power, not personal failings. Consciousness-raising groups, where women compared experiences, were the main tool. Because patriarchy lives in everyday assumptions, radical feminists aim to challenge its basic premises (that men naturally lead, that women belong in the home, that male sexuality defines the norm) rather than simply win equal access. Contrast the neighbours. Liberal feminism wants women integrated into existing institutions through reform. Marxist feminism blames capitalism and class. Socialist feminism combines capitalism and patriarchy. Cultural feminism, which grew out of radical feminism, focuses on building a separate women's culture. Option c is right because radical feminism's primary focus is challenging the fundamental assumptions of patriarchy, which it sees as the root of women's oppression.","traps":{"a":"Recognising that women are oppressed by the capitalist system is the starting point of Marxist and socialist feminism. Marxist feminists, following Friedrich Engels (1884), see women's subordination as a product of private property and class. Radical feminists reject this, arguing that patriarchy predates capitalism and survives under any economic system. It tempts because both schools are critical of the status quo.","b":"Building a women's culture is the aim of cultural feminism, which developed from radical feminism in the mid-1970s and shifted from fighting patriarchy to valuing female traits and creating women-centred communities. Because the two share roots, textbooks sometimes blur them. The question asks for the primary focus of radical feminism itself, which is patriarchy. It tempts as the closest neighbour.","d":"Integrating women into mainstream society is the goal of liberal feminism (Mary Wollstonecraft, 1792; Betty Friedan, 1963), which seeks equal rights and opportunity through reform of existing institutions. Radical feminists criticise this as insufficient, since entering a patriarchal structure leaves its assumptions intact. It tempts because integration is the best known feminist goal."}},{"q":"What is the primary characteristic of absolute poverty?","concept":"Poverty can be measured against a fixed standard or against the rest of society, and exams test which is which. Absolute poverty uses a fixed, minimum standard: a person is poor if they lack the resources to obtain the basic necessities for survival, such as food, safe water, shelter, clothing, sanitation and basic health care. Seebohm Rowntree's study of York (1901) set an early example with a subsistence line based on the cost of a minimum diet and essentials. The United Nations World Summit for Social Development in Copenhagen (1995) defined absolute poverty as severe deprivation of basic human needs, including food, safe drinking water, sanitation, health, shelter, education and information. Internationally it is tracked through the World Bank's international poverty line, a daily income measured in purchasing power parity dollars. In India, poverty lines were estimated by expert groups such as the Tendulkar Committee (2009) and the Rangarajan Committee (2014). Relative poverty, by contrast, is defined against the typical living standard of one's society, for example income below 60 percent of the median; Peter Townsend (1979) described it as being unable to take part in the customs and activities normal in that society. Relative poverty therefore rises with inequality, while absolute poverty can fall even as inequality grows. Two further types appear as distractors: situational (transient) poverty after a shock such as job loss, illness or disaster, and generational (chronic) poverty passed down across generations. Option b is right because the defining characteristic of absolute poverty is lack of access to basic necessities.","traps":{"a":"Income inequality is the uneven distribution of income across a population, measured with tools such as the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient (Corrado Gini, 1912). It is the basis of relative poverty, where poverty is judged against others. Absolute poverty uses a fixed survival threshold, so a country can reduce it while inequality rises. It tempts because both terms appear in every poverty chapter.","c":"Temporary deprivation after an adverse event, such as a job loss, illness, crop failure or natural disaster, is situational or transient poverty. It is defined by its cause and duration, and families often recover once the shock passes. Absolute poverty is defined by the level of deprivation, whatever its cause. It tempts because people in situational poverty may briefly fall below the absolute line.","d":"Poverty handed down through generations is generational or chronic poverty, in which low education, poor health, debt and lack of assets keep successive generations poor. Oscar Lewis (1959) linked it to a 'culture of poverty', a contested idea. It describes persistence over time, not the survival threshold that defines absolute poverty. It tempts because chronic poverty is often also absolute."}},{"q":"How does eudemonic wellbeing conceptualize wellbeing?","concept":"Positive psychology recognises two traditions of well-being, both traced to Greek philosophy. The hedonic tradition, from early Greek thinkers who equated the good life with pleasure, says well-being is pleasure and the absence of pain. In psychology it became subjective well-being, defined by Ed Diener (1984) as high life satisfaction, frequent positive affect and infrequent negative affect. The eudaimonic tradition comes from the Nicomachean Ethics of Aristotle (4th century BCE), where eudaimonia means living well by realising one's daimon or true potential through virtuous activity. Feeling good is not the test; living in line with one's best capacities is. Carol Ryff (1989) turned this into six dimensions of psychological well-being: self-acceptance, positive relations with others, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose in life and personal growth. Richard Ryan and Edward Deci (2001) linked eudaimonia to self-determination theory, in which well-being follows satisfaction of the needs for autonomy, competence and relatedness. Martin Seligman's authentic happiness model (2002) separated the pleasant life from the engaged and meaningful lives, and Christopher Peterson and Seligman (2004) classified 24 character strengths under six virtues, giving eudaimonic work its tools. The meaningful life, in Seligman's terms, means using one's strengths in service of something larger than oneself. For exam sorting: pleasure, relaxation, positive mood and satisfaction point to hedonic well-being; growth, meaning, virtue, strengths, purpose and contribution point to eudaimonic well-being. Income and possessions belong to neither; they are objective or economic indicators. Option c is right because eudaimonic well-being is about cultivating personal strengths and contributing to the greater good.","traps":{"a":"Measuring well-being by income and material possessions is an economic or objective-indicator approach, such as using GDP per head. Richard Easterlin (1974) showed that rising national income does not reliably raise reported happiness, the Easterlin paradox. Neither hedonic nor eudaimonic psychology defines well-being as wealth. It tempts because income is the most common everyday proxy for a good life.","b":"Focusing on pleasure and relaxation is the hedonic view, which treats well-being as feeling good: positive affect, comfort, enjoyment and freedom from distress. The subjective well-being model of Ed Diener (1984) belongs to this tradition. Eudaimonic theorists accept that pleasure matters but treat it as a by-product, not the definition. It tempts because relaxation sounds healthy and close to wellness.","d":"Equating well-being with pleasure and happiness is the core hedonic definition, the direct opposite of eudaimonia in every positive psychology text. Richard Ryan and Edward Deci (2001) frame the field around exactly this contrast. A person can be pleased yet not flourishing, or struggling yet growing. It tempts because 'eudaimonia' is often loosely translated as 'happiness', which makes the hedonic option look right."}},{"q":"What are some areas of post-traumatic growth?","concept":"Post-traumatic growth (PTG) is positive psychological change experienced as a result of struggling with a highly challenging life crisis. The term was introduced by Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun in the mid-1990s, and their Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (1996) has 21 items grouped into five domains. Relating to others: closer relationships, more compassion, more willingness to accept help. New possibilities: new interests, roles or life paths. Personal strength: 'if I survived that, I can handle anything'. Spiritual change: deeper engagement with spiritual or existential questions. Appreciation of life: changed priorities and valuing everyday things. Growth comes not from the trauma itself but from the struggle with it, especially deliberate rumination that rebuilds a shattered set of core beliefs. Distress and growth can coexist; PTG is not the absence of PTSD. Know the neighbours. Resilience means bouncing back to the previous level of functioning; growth means going beyond it. Benefit finding is seeing positives in adversity, often studied in illness. Stress-related growth, measured by Crystal Park and colleagues (1996), is a similar idea for less extreme stress. Viktor Frankl (1946), in Man's Search for Meaning, is the classic forerunner, arguing that meaning can be found even in suffering. Critics note that self-reported growth may partly be a coping illusion. Exams usually test recall of the five domains, so learn them as a list. Option d is right because appreciation of life, relating to others, personal strength and spiritual change are all PTGI domains, making it the fullest correct list offered.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' would be right only if no option listed real growth domains. Both b and d list genuine domains from the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory of Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun (1996), so this is plainly wrong. It tempts candidates who notice that neither option lists all five domains and conclude that no option is complete enough to accept.","b":"Appreciation of life, personal strength and new possibilities are all genuine domains of the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun, 1996), so this option is not false. It is simply less complete: it gives three of the five, while d gives four, adding relating to others and spiritual change. In single-best-answer format the fuller correct list wins. It tempts because every item in it is right.","c":"Material possessions, financial success and personal weakness are not growth domains; the inventory measures increased perceived strength, not weakness, and growth is psychological, not material. Slipping 'new possibilities' (a real domain) into the list makes it look plausible. It tempts candidates who skim, recognise one familiar term and miss the plainly wrong ones beside it."}},{"q":"How have physician recommendations and increased awareness contributed to higher exercise participation rates?","concept":"Health psychology explains why people take up exercise using models of health behaviour. The Health Belief Model, developed in the 1950s by US Public Health Service researchers including Godfrey Hochbaum and Irwin Rosenstock, says action depends on perceived susceptibility to a disease, perceived severity, perceived benefits of acting, perceived barriers, cues to action and, in later versions, self-efficacy. A doctor's recommendation is a classic cue to action, and information on how exercise lowers the risk of heart disease, diabetes, obesity and depression raises perceived benefits. Public-health campaigns work the same way at scale. The Theory of Planned Behaviour of Icek Ajzen (1991) adds that attitudes, subjective norms (what important others, including one's doctor, expect) and perceived behavioural control shape intention. Self-efficacy, from Albert Bandura (1977), is the belief that one can carry out the behaviour. The Transtheoretical Model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983) adds stages: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance. Awareness moves people from precontemplation to contemplation. Knowledge alone rarely sustains change, because the gap between intention and action is large, so programmes add goal setting, social support and feedback. For this item, though, the mechanism is simple: physician advice and campaigns make people aware of the benefits of exercise, and greater awareness of benefits raises participation. The other choices either reverse the effect or deny that any answer applies. Option c is right because physician recommendations and awareness campaigns raise participation by increasing knowledge of the benefits of exercise.","traps":{"a":"Creating barriers would lower participation, the reverse of what the stem describes. In the Health Belief Model (1950s), perceived barriers such as lack of time, cost, fatigue or fear of injury are exactly what reduce health behaviour, and good advice aims to lower them. It tempts only candidates who recognise 'barriers' as a model term and attach it to the wrong effect.","b":"Discouraging people from physical activity contradicts both the content of medical advice and the observed rise in participation. Doctors may restrict exercise for a few medical conditions, but recommendations as a whole push people toward activity. This option is a reversal distractor. It tempts only through careless reading, since the stem already says that participation rose.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because option c states the actual mechanism, greater awareness of the benefits of exercise. This catch-all is right only when every listed option fails. It tempts candidates who think awareness is too simple an explanation, knowing that knowledge alone rarely changes behaviour, and so reject all the listed options."}},{"q":"What eating disorder involves cycles of binge eating and purging through various methods?","concept":"Bulimia nervosa was first described as a separate syndrome by Gerald Russell (1979), who called it an ominous variant of anorexia nervosa. DSM-III (1980) listed 'bulimia', and DSM-III-R (1987) renamed it bulimia nervosa. In DSM-5 (2013) the diagnosis needs: recurrent binge eating, meaning eating within a discrete period an amount clearly larger than most people would, with a sense of loss of control; recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour to prevent weight gain, such as self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives, diuretics or other medicines, fasting or excessive exercise; both occurring on average at least once a week for three months; self-evaluation unduly influenced by body shape and weight; and the episodes not occurring only during anorexia nervosa. Severity is rated by compensatory episodes per week: mild 1 to 3, moderate 4 to 7, severe 8 to 13, extreme 14 or more. Body weight is usually normal or above, which separates it from anorexia. Signs of repeated vomiting include Russell's sign (calluses on the knuckles), swollen parotid glands, dental enamel erosion and low potassium. Three neighbours build variants. Anorexia nervosa needs restriction leading to significantly low body weight, fear of weight gain and body image disturbance; its binge-eating/purging subtype can purge, but low weight decides the diagnosis. Binge-eating disorder has binges with distress but no regular compensation. Purging disorder, under other specified feeding or eating disorders, has purging without binges. Option b is right because recurrent cycles of binge eating followed by purging define bulimia nervosa.","traps":{"a":"Anorexia nervosa is defined by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a disturbed experience of body shape (DSM-5, 2013). It has a binge-eating/purging subtype, so purging can occur, but the defining feature is low weight, not a binge and purge cycle. It tempts because the two disorders share body image concerns and can shade into each other.","c":"Binge-eating disorder, a full DSM-5 (2013) diagnosis, involves recurrent binges with loss of control and marked distress, at least once a week for three months, but without regular compensatory behaviour such as vomiting or laxatives. Many sufferers are overweight. It tempts because 'binge eating' is in its name and the stem begins with bingeing, yet it fails the purging half of the stem.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because bulimia nervosa exactly matches cycles of binge eating and purging. It might tempt a candidate who notices that the option says 'Bulimia' rather than the full 'bulimia nervosa', or who knows that purging also appears in anorexia, and so doubts every option. The short name still refers to the same disorder."}},{"q":"According to Guthrie, which of the following is NOT improvable by practice?","concept":"Edwin R. Guthrie set out the simplest learning theory of the behaviourist era in The Psychology of Learning (1935). It rests on one law of contiguity: a combination of stimuli that has accompanied a movement will, on its recurrence, tend to be followed by that movement. No reinforcement is needed; pairing in time is enough. From this follows one-trial learning: the stimulus-movement bond reaches full strength on its first pairing. Practice does not strengthen a bond, and reward matters only because it changes the situation, so the last response made is preserved (the recency principle). To explain why performance still improves with practice, Guthrie separated three levels. Movements are specific muscle contractions, learned completely in one trial. Acts are classes of movements defined by their outcome, such as opening a door or typing a word, which can be achieved by many different movements in many situations. Skills are collections of acts, such as driving or playing cricket. Acts and skills improve with practice because each repetition attaches the needed movements to more of the stimulus combinations in which they must occur. Guthrie and George Horton (1946) supported this with cats escaping a puzzle box, whose escape movements became highly stereotyped. William Estes (1950) later formalised the idea in stimulus sampling theory. Contrast incremental theories: the law of effect of Edward Thorndike (1911) and the habit strength of Clark Hull (1943) both grow gradually with reinforced trials. Option c is right because Guthrie held that movements are learned fully in one trial, so practice cannot improve them.","traps":{"a":"Skills, in the scheme of Edwin Guthrie (1935), are large collections of acts, such as playing a musical instrument or driving a car. Because a skill must work across a huge range of stimulus situations, practice keeps attaching the right movements to new cues, so skills clearly improve with practice. It tempts because 'skill' sounds like a fixed, already learned unit.","b":"Acts are outcome-defined classes of movements, such as catching a ball or writing a name. The same act can be done with different movements in different situations, so practice improves it by connecting the right movement to each situation. Guthrie used this to reconcile one-trial learning with gradual learning curves. It tempts because acts sit close to movements in his hierarchy.","d":"'None of the above' would mean that everything improves with practice. That is the incremental view of Edward Thorndike (1911) and Clark Hull (1943), in which each reinforced trial strengthens a bond. Guthrie rejected it for movements, which are learned completely in one pairing. It tempts candidates who apply the everyday rule that practice improves everything."}},{"q":"The view of 'observational learning' where reinforcement is not required for learning a behaviour is given by","concept":"Observational learning, or modelling, is learning by watching another person (the model) and the consequences of their behaviour. Albert Bandura developed it into social learning theory (1977) and later social cognitive theory (1986). His key claim is that acquisition and performance are separate. A person can learn a behaviour simply by observing it, with no reinforcement for themselves; reinforcement, direct or vicarious, only affects whether the learned behaviour is later performed. The Bobo doll studies show this. Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961) found that children who watched an adult attack an inflatable doll copied the specific aggressive acts. In Bandura (1965), children saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Those who saw punishment imitated less, but when every child was then offered an incentive to show what they remembered, all groups reproduced the behaviour about equally. Learning had happened without reinforcement. Bandura listed four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. Related ideas include vicarious reinforcement (seeing a model rewarded raises our tendency to copy) and self-efficacy (1977). Compare the neighbours. Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941), in Social Learning and Imitation, explained imitation by drive reduction: imitating was learned because it was reinforced. B. F. Skinner (1938) treated learning as shaped by reinforcement of the learner's own responses. Edward Tolman (1930) also showed learning without reward, but in latent maze learning, not imitation of a model. Option b is right because Bandura held that observational learning occurs without reinforcement, which affects performance rather than acquisition.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner (1938, 1953) explained behaviour through operant conditioning, in which a response is learned because it is followed by reinforcement. In his view imitation is just another operant, strengthened when copying is rewarded. That is the opposite of reinforcement-free learning. It tempts because Skinner is the default name for any question that mentions reinforcement.","c":"Edward Tolman, with Charles Honzik (1930), showed latent learning: rats explored a maze without reward and formed a cognitive map, revealed only when food was introduced. That is also learning without reinforcement, but it comes from the animal's own exploration, not from watching a model. It tempts because 'no reinforcement needed' is Tolman's signature idea too.","d":"Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) offered the first social learning theory, but in Hullian drive-reduction terms: imitation (matched-dependent behaviour) is learned because the imitator is reinforced for copying. So reinforcement is required. Bandura's departure from them is exactly this point. It tempts because their book title, Social Learning and Imitation, sounds like the right answer."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning is learning that takes place without reinforcement and stays hidden (latent) until an incentive gives the organism a reason to show it. Early evidence came from Hugh Blodgett (1929), and the classic study is Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930). Three groups of rats ran a complex maze once a day. One group found food in the goal box every day; one never found food; the third found none for the first ten days and food from day eleven. The never-rewarded rats improved little, as did the third group before food appeared. Yet once rewarded, that group's errors dropped sharply on the very next day, matching or even beating the always-rewarded rats. They must have learned the maze layout during the unrewarded trials. Tolman (1948) called this internal representation a cognitive map, and the study supports the distinction between learning and performance: reinforcement drives performance, not learning itself. This challenged Clark Hull (1943) and others who held reinforcement necessary for learning. The signature in a stem is a sudden jump in performance once reward is introduced, after earlier unrewarded trials. Separate it from insight learning (Wolfgang Kohler, 1917), a sudden solution through restructuring a problem, and from the Crespi effect (Leo Crespi, 1942), a change in running speed when the size of an existing reward is shifted. The observational learning of Albert Bandura (1977) also separates learning from performance, but it involves a model. Option d is right because a sudden improvement once reinforcement arrives, after unrewarded trials, is the defining sign of latent learning.","traps":{"a":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond to one stimulus and not to another, because responses to one are reinforced and responses to the other are not; Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed it with dogs that learned to separate similar tones. It depends on differential reinforcement throughout, not on learning that appears only once reward begins. It tempts as a standard term from the same chapter.","b":"Vicarious reinforcement, from Albert Bandura (1965), is the effect of seeing a model rewarded: the observer becomes more likely to perform the modelled behaviour. The stem describes the organism's own trials and its own first reward, with no model at all. It tempts because it also involves reinforcement changing performance, and Bandura's work is famous for separating learning from performance.","c":"Observational learning, in the social learning theory of Albert Bandura (1977), is learning by watching a model, as in the Bobo doll studies. It shares the idea that learning can occur without reinforcement, but it needs another individual to observe. The stem describes a single organism's unrewarded practice followed by a sudden jump when rewarded. It tempts because both concepts separate learning from performance."}},{"q":"According to Skinner, the response rate _________ after extinction","concept":"In operant conditioning, as set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, extinction is the procedure of withholding reinforcement for a response that was previously reinforced. Before conditioning, every response already has a baseline frequency called the operant level: a rat in a Skinner box presses the lever now and then by chance. Reinforcement raises the rate far above that level. When reinforcement stops, the rate declines, often after a brief extinction burst (a temporary rise in rate and variability, sometimes with emotional behaviour), and eventually returns to roughly the operant level, its earlier pre-conditioning rate. On a cumulative record this appears as a curve that gradually flattens. Extinction is not the same as forgetting or erasing the response. Spontaneous recovery, first noted by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in classical conditioning, shows that responding can reappear after a rest. Work on renewal by Mark Bouton (2004) shows that an extinguished response can return when the context changes, which suggests extinction is new learning layered over the old. Extinction is also slower after intermittent than after continuous reinforcement, the partial reinforcement extinction effect, first shown by Lloyd Humphreys (1939). Contrast punishment, which adds an aversive consequence to suppress behaviour, and negative reinforcement, which strengthens behaviour by removing something unpleasant. Option c is right because Skinner described extinction as bringing the response rate back down to its earlier, pre-conditioning operant level, not to zero.","traps":{"a":"An increase in responding happens only briefly at the start of extinction, the extinction burst, when the expected reinforcer fails to arrive, as when people press a lift button repeatedly. It is a transient reaction, not the outcome of extinction. Over time the rate falls. It tempts because candidates who know about the burst mistake it for the end state.","b":"If the response rate stayed the same, reinforcement would not be controlling the behaviour at all. In the data of B. F. Skinner (1938), rate falls steadily once reinforcement stops, faster after continuous reinforcement and more slowly after partial reinforcement. Persistence after partial reinforcement can look like 'staying the same' in the short run, which is why this option tempts.","d":"Zero responding is the popular image of extinction, and many introductory books say the behaviour 'disappears'. Skinner's point was that the rate returns to the operant level, the low chance rate that existed before conditioning, since the organism emitted the response occasionally even then. Spontaneous recovery also shows the response is not erased. It tempts as the textbook shorthand."}},{"q":"The allocation policy in Kahneman's model includes","concept":"Daniel Kahneman (1973), in his book Attention and Effort, proposed the capacity or resource model of attention. Earlier filter models, from Donald Broadbent (1958) to the attenuation model of Anne Treisman (1964), treated attention as a bottleneck at one stage of processing. Kahneman instead treated attention as a single limited pool of mental effort that can be spread across several tasks at once, as long as total demand does not exceed supply. The size of the pool is not fixed: it rises and falls with arousal, so a moderately aroused person has more capacity than a drowsy one, while very high arousal impairs control. Capacity is distributed by the allocation policy, which has three inputs. Enduring dispositions are involuntary, long-standing rules of attention, such as automatic orienting to a sudden movement, a loud noise or one's own name. Momentary intentions are current, deliberate goals, such as 'listen to the left ear'. Evaluation of demands on capacity is a running check of how much effort each activity needs; if demand is too high, one task is dropped or done poorly, and rising demand also feeds back to raise arousal. Arousal mainly sets the total supply rather than being one of the three listed inputs. The model explains dual-task interference and why effort shows up as pupil dilation. Christopher Wickens (1984) later proposed multiple resource pools for different modalities, a common contrast question. Option d is right because Kahneman's allocation policy is governed by enduring dispositions, momentary intentions and the evaluation of demands.","traps":{"a":"This option keeps 'evaluation of demands' but changes the other two terms. 'Enduring temperament' borrows a personality word, whereas Kahneman meant enduring dispositions, automatic attention rules such as orienting to novelty or to your own name. 'Momentary distractions' reverses the idea: the policy follows momentary intentions, which are deliberate current goals, not passing distractions. It tempts because one term is exactly right and the other two sound very close.","b":"'Enduring temperament; momentary distractions; environmental demands' changes all three of Kahneman's terms. Temperament is a personality construct, distractions are what the policy guards against rather than a determinant of it, and 'environmental demands' points outward, whereas Kahneman meant the person's own evaluation of how much capacity a task requires. It tempts because each phrase sounds attention-related and the option keeps the same enduring, momentary, demands structure as the real list.","c":"This option gets 'momentary intentions' right but replaces the other two terms. 'Enduring temperament' should be enduring dispositions, and 'environmental demands' should be the evaluation of demands on capacity, an internal appraisal of the effort a task needs rather than a description of the surroundings. It tempts because it contains the correct middle term, which candidates often remember most clearly, and the substitutes look like harmless synonyms."}},{"q":"An idealized representative object of a category of objects is","concept":"Concepts let us treat different objects as members of one category. The classical view, going back to Aristotle, said a category is defined by necessary and sufficient features: a bachelor is an unmarried adult male. Eleanor Rosch (1973, 1975) showed that natural categories do not work like that. Membership is graded: people rate a robin as a better example of 'bird' than a penguin or an ostrich, verify 'a robin is a bird' faster, and list it first. Rosch explained this with the prototype, an idealised, abstract representative of the category that captures its central tendency, the average or most typical combination of features. A new item is categorised by its similarity to the prototype; typical members share many features with other members, which Rosch and Carolyn Mervis (1975) called family resemblance, a term borrowed from Ludwig Wittgenstein (1953). The prototype need not be any real member you have seen. A rival is exemplar theory (Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer, 1978), which says we store many concrete instances and compare new items with all of them. Rosch also described levels of categories: superordinate (furniture), basic (chair) and subordinate (kitchen chair), with the basic level learned first and used most. In pattern recognition the same word appears beside templates, features and geons, which is how examiners build distractors: a template is an exact stored copy, a feature is one component, a geon is a three-dimensional building block. Option a is right because an idealised representative of a category, its best example, is the definition of a prototype.","traps":{"b":"A template is a stored, exact copy of a pattern. Template-matching theory says we recognise input by checking it against these copies for an exact fit, like a machine reading the printed numbers on a cheque. It fails because one object appears in endless sizes, angles and handwriting styles, which would need countless templates. A template is literal and specific; a prototype is abstract and averaged. It tempts because both are stored mental standards used for comparison.","c":"A feature is a single component attribute, such as a horizontal line, a curve or an angle. Feature-analysis models, such as the Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959), recognise patterns by detecting features, supported by the feature-detector cells found by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962). A feature is a part, not a whole representative of a category. It tempts because a prototype is described as the most typical combination of features.","d":"Geons (geometric ions) are about 36 simple three-dimensional shapes, such as cylinders, cones, blocks and wedges, proposed by Irving Biederman (1987) in recognition-by-components theory. Objects are recognised by identifying their geons and how they are joined, much as letters combine into words. Geons are building blocks of individual object shapes, not idealised representatives of a category. It tempts because recognition by components sits in the same object-recognition chapter as prototypes."}},{"q":"In which of the following cases, would the sensitivity 'd'  be highest in a signal detection task?","concept":"Signal detection theory (SDT), brought into psychology by Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) and set out fully by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates two things that classical threshold methods mixed together: how well an observer can tell a signal from noise (sensitivity) and how willing they are to say 'yes' (response bias or criterion). On each trial the signal is present or absent and the observer says yes or no, giving four outcomes: hit (present, yes), miss (present, no), false alarm (absent, yes) and correct rejection (absent, no). The theory pictures two overlapping normal distributions of internal activity, one for noise alone and one for signal plus noise. Sensitivity, d' (d-prime), is the distance between their means in standard-deviation units, computed as z(hit rate) minus z(false-alarm rate). When hits greatly exceed false alarms, the distributions are far apart and d' is large. When hits equal false alarms, d' is zero: the observer is guessing. When false alarms exceed hits, d' is negative, which suggests confusion or reversed responding. Bias is a separate quantity, beta or c: a liberal observer says yes often, raising both hits and false alarms, so d' can stay unchanged. Examiners also ask about the ROC (receiver operating characteristic) curve, which plots hit rate against false-alarm rate; the more it bows toward the top left, the greater the sensitivity, while the diagonal means d' of zero. Option c is right because, with equal numbers of signal and noise trials, d' is positive and grows only when hits exceed false alarms.","traps":{"a":"If false alarms are slightly more than hits, the false-alarm rate is a little above the hit rate, so z(hit rate) minus z(false-alarm rate) is slightly negative. The observer is doing no better than guessing, and d' is near zero or just below it. It tempts because 'slightly' makes the difference sound trivial, and candidates who equate lots of 'yes' responding with alertness may think the observer is detecting well.","b":"When false alarms exceed hits, the observer says 'yes' more often on noise-only trials than on signal trials. That gives a negative d', meaning performance is worse than chance, usually from misunderstanding the task or reversing responses. Sensitivity is lowest here, not highest. It tempts because a high false-alarm count can look like an eager, responsive observer, but that eagerness reflects a liberal criterion (bias), not sensitivity.","d":"When hits equal false alarms, the hit rate equals the false-alarm rate, the two internal distributions overlap completely, and d' equals zero. The point lies on the diagonal of the ROC curve: the observer cannot tell signal from noise at all. It tempts because equality sounds balanced or ideal, and candidates may confuse a neutral response bias with good sensitivity, although bias and sensitivity are separate measures in SDT."}},{"q":"A Prototype is a/an _________ and ________ member of a category","concept":"This item tests the two defining properties of a prototype in the prototype theory of categorisation of Eleanor Rosch (1975). First, a prototype is abstract: it is a mental summary built from experience, not a specific concrete object you have met. Your prototype of 'dog' is not your neighbour's dog, and it may not match any real dog at all. Second, it is averaged: it represents the central tendency of the category, the most typical value on each feature, formed by pooling across the members encountered. Classic evidence comes from Michael Posner and Steven Keele (1968), who showed people distorted versions of dot patterns; later, participants classified the never-seen undistorted prototype as accurately as the patterns they had actually studied. That is only possible if the mind abstracted an average. Prototype theory predicts typicality effects: items close to the average (a robin for bird) are judged faster and more confidently than atypical items (a penguin). Contrast this with exemplar theory (Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer, 1978), in which categories are stored as many concrete individual instances and their variability is kept rather than averaged away; exemplar models explain why we know that some categories are more variable than others. The classical definitional view has neither, only necessary and sufficient rules. So when a question pairs abstract versus concrete with averaged versus varied, abstract and averaged describes the prototype, while concrete and varied describes stored exemplars. Option d is right because a prototype is an abstract and averaged representation of the members of a category.","traps":{"a":"'Abstract; varied' gets the first half right, since a prototype is abstract, but 'varied' is wrong. A prototype compresses the variability among members into a single central-tendency summary; it does not preserve the variety. Keeping the spread of individual instances is a feature of the exemplar theory of Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer (1978). It tempts because categories themselves are varied, so candidates attach the word to the representation instead of to the members.","b":"'Concrete; averaged' gets 'averaged' right but 'concrete' wrong. A prototype is not a particular, physically experienced member; it is an abstraction that may correspond to no real object, as the never-seen dot-pattern prototypes of Michael Posner and Steven Keele (1968) showed. It tempts because people often name a concrete best example, such as a robin, when asked for a typical bird, which blurs a typical exemplar with the abstract prototype.","c":"'Concrete; varied' describes the exemplar view, not prototype theory. In the exemplar approach of Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer (1978), memory holds many specific, concrete instances along with their full variability, and a new item is compared with all of them. It tempts because it is the exact mirror image of the correct answer, so candidates who mix up prototype theory and exemplar theory choose it."}},{"q":"For perception of a contour, _________ and __________ are required","concept":"A contour is the boundary we see where one region of the visual field changes abruptly into another in brightness, colour or texture. Contours give objects their shape and allow figure-ground segregation, described by Edgar Rubin (1915). Without contours, form vanishes: in a Ganzfeld, a completely uniform field studied by Wolfgang Metzger (1930), observers see only a featureless fog. Two stimulus conditions must be met for a contour to register. First, the difference in stimulation must reach a minimal intensity, so the change crosses the detection threshold. The retina helps through lateral inhibition, which sharpens edges and produces Mach bands, described by Ernst Mach (1865). Second, the stimulus must be exposed for a minimum time, because contour formation takes time. Heinz Werner (1935) showed this with metacontrast masking: a black disc was briefly flashed and then followed by a ring whose inner edge matched the disc's outline, and the disc often was not seen, because the later contour disrupted the one still forming. The trade-off between intensity and duration is expressed in Bloch's law (Adolphe-Moise Bloch, 1885): for flashes shorter than about a tenth of a second, threshold depends on intensity multiplied by duration, so a weaker stimulus needs a longer exposure. Examiners contrast this with reaction time, which is the speed of the observer's response, not a property of the stimulus, and with illusory contours, such as the triangle of Gaetano Kanizsa (1955), seen where no physical edge exists. Option a is right because contour perception needs both a minimal intensity of stimulation and a minimum exposure time.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps the correct first condition, minimal stimulus intensity, but replaces exposure time with reaction time. Reaction time is the interval between stimulus onset and the observer's response, measured since Franciscus Donders (1868) with his subtraction method. It describes how fast a person responds, not what a stimulus needs in order to be seen, so it cannot be a condition for perceiving an edge. It tempts because it is half right.","c":"Minimum exposure time is a genuine requirement, but this option drops stimulus intensity and adds reaction time. A very faint edge would not be seen however long it was shown, so intensity cannot be left out, and reaction time is a measure of the response, not a condition of the stimulus. It tempts because exposure time is correct and the word 'time' appears twice, which makes the pair look like a matched set.","d":"Neither term is a requirement for contour perception. Reaction time is the speed of the observer's response, and movement is not needed because stationary edges are seen perfectly well. Movement matters for motion perception, as in the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), not for seeing a boundary. It tempts because 'minimum movement' sounds like the tiny eye movements discussed in the same perception chapter, and 'minimum' echoes the wording of the stem."}},{"q":"the theory which assumes that perceiving an object is by due to 'geons' is","concept":"Recognition by components (RBC) is the theory of object recognition proposed by Irving Biederman (1987). It says that the visual system recognises a three-dimensional object by breaking it into a small set of simple volumetric parts called geons, short for geometric ions. Biederman proposed about 36 geons, such as cylinders, cones, bricks and wedges, defined by non-accidental properties: features of edges that stay the same from almost any viewpoint, such as straightness, curvature, parallelism, symmetry and edges meeting at a vertex. A mug is a cylinder with a curved handle-shaped geon on its side; a pail is the same two geons with the handle attached on top. The arrangement matters as much as the parts, just as a few letters form many words. RBC explains viewpoint invariance: we recognise objects from new angles because the geons remain identifiable. In Biederman's degraded-picture studies, line drawings with segments deleted from the middle of contours were still recognised, but when the deletions removed the vertices and junctions that define geons, recognition failed. The main criticism is that RBC handles basic-level recognition (a chair) better than fine distinctions within a category (one face versus another), and it struggles with the view-dependent effects reported by Michael Tarr and Steven Pinker (1989). Examiners pair RBC with older pattern-recognition models: template matching (exact stored copies), feature analysis such as the Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959), and prototype theory (Eleanor Rosch, 1975). Option b is right because recognition by components is the theory that explains object perception through geons.","traps":{"a":"The Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959) is a feature-analysis model of pattern recognition, usually explained with letters. Image demons record the input, feature demons detect lines and angles, cognitive demons 'shout' for the letters matching those features, and a decision demon picks the loudest. It works on two-dimensional features, not three-dimensional volumetric parts. It tempts because it is also a parts-based model taught in the same chapter as geons.","c":"The prototype model of Eleanor Rosch (1975) recognises an item by comparing it with an abstract, averaged best example of its category, so a robin is recognised as a bird because it is close to the bird prototype. It is a theory of categorisation by similarity to a central tendency, not of building objects out of geons. It tempts because both theories explain how we identify objects as members of a type.","d":"Template matching proposes that the mind stores exact copies of patterns and recognises input when it fits one, as a machine reads the numbers printed on cheques. Its well-known weakness is that it needs a separate template for every size, orientation and style of an object. RBC was partly a solution to this problem, since a few geons combine to describe countless objects. It tempts because it is the most familiar recognition theory."}},{"q":"Which of the following depth perception cues requires both eyes?","concept":"Depth perception means seeing the world in three dimensions and judging distance, although each retinal image is flat. Cues are grouped as binocular (needing both eyes) or monocular (available to one eye). There are two binocular cues. Retinal or binocular disparity is the slight difference between the images on the two retinas, because the eyes are about six centimetres apart; the brain fuses them into one image with depth, a process called stereopsis, first demonstrated with the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone (1838). Convergence is the inward turning of both eyes to fixate a near object; the brain uses the feedback from the eye muscles (an oculomotor, kinaesthetic cue) to judge distance, and it is useful only up to a few metres. Monocular cues include the oculomotor cue of accommodation, the change in the curvature of the lens to focus on near objects; pictorial cues, such as linear perspective, relative size, interposition (occlusion), texture gradient (James J. Gibson, 1950), aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field; and movement-produced cues, such as motion parallax. A frequent trap is that convergence and accommodation are both oculomotor cues sensed through muscle feedback, so they are learned as a pair, yet accommodation happens within a single eye. Another variant asks which cues artists use: pictorial cues, because they work on a flat canvas. Option c is right because convergence depends on the angle between the lines of sight of the two eyes, so it requires both eyes.","traps":{"a":"Motion parallax is a monocular, movement-produced cue. When you move, for example while looking out of a train window, near objects seem to sweep past quickly in the opposite direction while distant objects move slowly or seem to travel with you. It works perfectly well with one eye closed. It tempts because 'parallax' sounds like a difference between two viewpoints, which is how binocular disparity is often described.","b":"Aerial or atmospheric perspective is a monocular pictorial cue: distant objects look hazier, less detailed and bluer because light is scattered by the air, dust and moisture between them and the viewer. Painters use it on a flat canvas, which shows that it needs only one eye. It tempts because the word 'perspective' sounds technical, and candidates may link it with stereoscopic viewing.","d":"Accommodation is a monocular oculomotor cue: the ciliary muscles change the curvature of the lens to focus on near or far objects, and the muscle feedback signals distance. It occurs within each eye separately and is useful only at short range. It tempts because it is taught alongside convergence as a pair of muscle-based cues, so candidates assume both of them need two eyes."}},{"q":"The process of conversion of stimulus input into electrical signal by the receptors is called","concept":"Sensation begins when a physical stimulus reaches a sense organ, but the brain cannot use light, sound waves or pressure directly; it works only with electrochemical neural signals. Transduction is the conversion of stimulus energy into these signals by specialised receptor cells. In the eye, rods and cones contain photopigments (rhodopsin in rods) that change shape when they absorb light, altering the receptor's membrane potential. In the ear, hair cells in the cochlea bend as the basilar membrane vibrates, opening ion channels. In the skin, mechanoreceptors such as Pacinian corpuscles respond to pressure and vibration; in taste and smell, chemoreceptors respond to molecules. The receptor first produces a graded receptor (generator) potential, which, if large enough, triggers action potentials in the sensory neurons that carry the message to the brain. Transduction is one link in a chain: detection by receptors, transduction, then coding, the way features such as intensity and pitch are represented in the rate and pattern of neural firing. Examiners pair it with neighbouring terms: sensation (detecting and encoding stimuli) versus perception (organising and interpreting them); the absolute threshold and the difference threshold, studied by Ernst Weber (1834) and Gustav Fechner (1860); and sensory adaptation, the reduced response to an unchanging stimulus. Distractors are usually words that sound similar, so learn the exact technical meaning of each. Option a is right because transduction is precisely the conversion of stimulus input into electrical signals by the receptors.","traps":{"b":"Transference is a psychoanalytic concept from Sigmund Freud, discussed in his paper 'The Dynamics of Transference' (1912): the client redirects feelings and attitudes from important early figures, such as parents, onto the therapist. Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. It belongs to psychotherapy, not to sensory physiology. It tempts because it begins with 'trans' and means carrying something across, which sounds like passing a signal along.","c":"Transformation means a change of form in general. In psychology it is best known from the transformational grammar of Noam Chomsky (1957), in which rules convert one sentence structure into another, such as active into passive, and in statistics, where raw scores are transformed into z scores. It is not the technical name for receptor conversion of energy. It tempts because transduction does involve changing one form of energy into another.","d":"Transfusion is a medical term for transferring blood or blood products from a donor into a patient's circulation. It has no meaning in sensory psychology at all. It tempts only because it shares the prefix 'trans' with the correct answer, a common trick in vocabulary-based items, so a candidate relying on a vague memory of 'trans-something' may pick it."}},{"q":"_________ are tiny rapid movements of the eyes to ensure ___________","concept":"Our eyes are never completely still. Saccades are fast, jerky, ballistic movements that jump the gaze from one point to another, about three or four times a second while reading or scanning a scene, and vision is suppressed during each jump (saccadic suppression). Between saccades the eyes rest in fixations, which last a few hundred milliseconds and are when most visual information is taken in. Even during fixation, the eyes make tiny involuntary movements: microsaccades, slow drift and high-frequency tremor, together called fixational eye movements or physiological nystagmus. Their job is to keep the retinal image moving slightly. This matters because receptors show sensory adaptation: a perfectly unchanging stimulus produces a declining response. Stabilised-image experiments, such as those of Lorrin Riggs and colleagues (1953) and Roy Pritchard (1961), used contact-lens devices so that the image moved with the eye and stayed on the same receptors. The image soon faded and disappeared, often in meaningful parts. Troxler fading, described by Ignaz Troxler (1804), is a related effect: a faint peripheral spot fades away when the gaze is held steady. So tiny rapid eye movements prevent adaptation and keep perception constant. Other eye movements used as distractors are smooth pursuit (tracking a moving target), vergence (convergence and divergence for depth) and the vestibulo-ocular reflex (stabilising gaze when the head moves). Option a is right because saccades, in their tiny form as microsaccades, are rapid eye movements that refresh the retinal image so that perception remains constant instead of fading through adaptation.","traps":{"b":"Fixation is the pause between saccades, when the gaze is held on one point for roughly a quarter of a second and most visual information is taken in. It is a relatively stable state, not a rapid movement, so it cannot fill the first blank. The tiny movements that occur during fixation are microsaccades, drift and tremor. It tempts because the second half of the option, constant perception, is correct.","c":"Saccades is correct for the first blank, but sensory adaptation is the wrong purpose. Sensory adaptation is the decline in receptor response to an unchanging stimulus, as when you stop noticing a smell after a few minutes. Tiny eye movements exist to prevent adaptation; stabilised retinal images fade precisely because adaptation is allowed to happen. It tempts because the term is closely tied to the correct explanation, only in reverse.","d":"This option is wrong on both counts. Fixation is the steady holding of gaze, not a rapid movement, and sensory adaptation is the fading of response to an unchanging stimulus, which eye movements work against rather than ensure. If the eyes truly fixated without any movement, adaptation would occur and the image would fade. It tempts because fixation and adaptation are both genuine terms from the same topic."}},{"q":"the component of working memory that is capable of mentally rotating an object is","concept":"Working memory is the small, active workspace in which information is held and manipulated during ongoing tasks. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) replaced the single short-term store of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) with a multi-component model. The central executive is a limited-capacity attentional controller: it focuses, divides and switches attention and coordinates the subsystems, but stores nothing itself. The phonological loop holds speech-based information through a passive phonological store and an articulatory rehearsal process, the inner voice; it explains the word-length effect and the phonological similarity effect. The visuospatial sketchpad holds and manipulates visual and spatial information, the inner eye: imagining a route, counting the windows of your house, or mentally rotating a shape. Robert Logie (1995) divided it into a passive visual cache (form and colour) and an active inner scribe (spatial and movement processes). Baddeley (2000) later added the episodic buffer, which binds information from the subsystems and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Evidence for separate subsystems comes from dual-task studies: two verbal tasks interfere with each other, while a verbal and a spatial task interfere much less. Mental rotation itself was shown by Roger Shepard and Jacqueline Metzler (1971): the time taken to judge whether two three-dimensional figures were identical increased linearly with the angle between them, as if the image were turned at a steady rate. Option c is right because mentally rotating an object is visuospatial manipulation, the job of the visuospatial sketchpad.","traps":{"a":"The phonological loop is the verbal subsystem of working memory. Its phonological store holds speech-based material for about two seconds, and articulatory rehearsal refreshes it, as when you repeat a phone number to yourself. It explains the word-length effect and is disrupted by articulatory suppression, such as repeating 'the the the'. It handles sounds, not images, so it cannot rotate an object. It tempts because it is the best-known component.","b":"The central executive is the supervisory, attention-controlling component of the model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974). It allocates attention, switches between tasks and coordinates the phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad, but it has no storage of its own and does not hold the image being rotated. It tempts because mental rotation is effortful and clearly needs executive control, so candidates credit the manager instead of the specialised workspace.","d":"Executive functioning is a broad neuropsychological umbrella term for frontal-lobe control processes, such as planning, inhibition, set-shifting and updating, assessed by tests like the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. It is not a named component of the Baddeley and Hitch working memory model at all. It tempts because it sounds like the central executive, and spatial reasoning tasks sometimes appear in executive function test batteries."}},{"q":"The mnemonic where a sentence is framed using first letter of items on a list is called","concept":"Mnemonics are strategies that make material easier to remember by adding organisation, imagery or meaning. Two first-letter techniques are often confused. An acronym takes the first letter of each item and forms a single word or pronounceable string: VIBGYOR for the colours of the rainbow, or OCEAN for the Big Five personality traits. An acrostic takes the same first letters but builds a sentence or phrase in which each word begins with the cue letter: 'Every Good Boy Deserves Fruit' for the musical notes E, G, B, D, F, or 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nachos' for the planets. Acrostics are useful when the first letters do not make a word. Both work as retrieval cues and by chunking, but they help only if the items themselves are already familiar. Imagery-based mnemonics form the other family. The method of loci, credited in legend to the Greek poet Simonides of Ceos, places items at points along a familiar route. The pegword system uses a memorised rhyme (one is a bun, two is a shoe) and links each item to a peg by an image. The keyword method, studied by Richard Atkinson and Michael Raugh (1975), links a foreign word to a similar-sounding native word and an interactive image. The dual-coding theory of Allan Paivio (1971) explains why imagery helps: concrete items can be stored in both verbal and visual codes. Option b is right because a sentence built from the first letters of the items on a list is, by definition, an acrostic.","traps":{"a":"An acronym is formed when the first letters of the items create a single word or pronounceable letter string, such as VIBGYOR for the rainbow colours or OCEAN for the Big Five traits. It uses first letters exactly as the stem describes, but it produces a word, not a sentence. It tempts because acronym and acrostic both begin with 'acro' and both rely on first-letter cues.","c":"The pegword system is an imagery mnemonic. The learner first memorises a fixed rhyme of number pegs, such as one is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, and then links each list item to its peg with a vivid interactive image. It suits ordered lists and allows recall by number. It uses rhymes and images, not first-letter sentences. It tempts because it is another named mnemonic for lists.","d":"The keyword method, studied by Richard Atkinson and Michael Raugh (1975), is used mainly for foreign vocabulary. The learner picks a native-language keyword that sounds like part of the foreign word and forms an image linking it to the meaning, for example linking the Spanish 'carta' (letter) to a cart carrying a letter. It relies on sound similarity and imagery, not first letters. It tempts because 'keyword' sounds like a word built from key letters."}},{"q":"In short term memory encoding is primarily ________","concept":"Encoding is the form in which information is stored. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), material passes from sensory memory to short-term memory (STM) and then to long-term memory (LTM), and each store tends to use a different code. Sensory memory keeps the raw, modality-specific form: iconic for vision and echoic for sound. STM is mainly acoustic. R. Conrad (1964) showed letters visually and found that recall errors were acoustic confusions, such as writing B for V or P for T, letters that sound alike rather than look alike. Alan Baddeley (1966) gave the classic contrast: immediate recall of word lists was much worse for acoustically similar words (cat, cap, can) than for dissimilar ones, while semantic similarity (big, large, huge) hardly mattered; when recall was delayed, testing LTM, the pattern reversed and semantic similarity caused the problems. So STM is primarily acoustic and LTM primarily semantic. These are tendencies, not absolutes: STM can hold visual codes, as when rotating a shape in the mind, and semantic codes for meaningful material. Other STM facts examiners ask about: a capacity of about seven plus or minus two items (George Miller, 1956), revised by Nelson Cowan (2001) to about four chunks; a duration of roughly 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal, from the Brown-Peterson technique (John Brown, 1958; Lloyd and Margaret Peterson, 1959); and forgetting through decay and interference. Option a is right because the dominant code in short-term memory is acoustic, the sound of the material, as acoustic confusion errors show.","traps":{"b":"Visual encoding is used in sensory memory (iconic memory) and in the visuospatial sketchpad of working memory, and STM can hold visual images briefly. But it is not the primary code: even when letters are presented visually, R. Conrad (1964) found that people confuse letters that sound alike, not those that look alike. It tempts because many STM experiments present material visually, so candidates assume the stored code matches the input.","c":"Haptic encoding is coding through active touch, the sense we use to recognise objects by handling them. There is a brief haptic sensory store, and touch information can enter STM, but it is a minor, modality-specific code, not the primary form of short-term encoding for the verbal material used in memory research. It tempts mainly as an unfamiliar, technical-sounding term that candidates sometimes pick when unsure.","d":"Semantic encoding, storing material by its meaning, is the primary code of long-term memory. Alan Baddeley (1966) showed that semantically similar words such as big, large and huge disrupted delayed recall from LTM but barely affected immediate recall from STM. Meaning is also the deep level in the levels-of-processing framework of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972). It tempts because meaning-based processing is linked with good memory in general."}},{"q":"Which sampling technique is most likely to result in a representative sample?","concept":"A sample is representative when its characteristics, such as age, gender, class and symptom severity, mirror those of the population it is drawn from, so that results can be generalised. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling, every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and selection is random. The main types are simple random sampling (lottery or random-number table), systematic sampling (every kth name after a random start), stratified random sampling (dividing the population into strata, such as urban and rural, and sampling randomly within each, often in proportion), cluster sampling (randomly selecting whole groups, such as schools or villages) and multistage sampling. Because chance alone decides who is chosen, systematic selection bias is removed, sampling error can be estimated, and inferential statistics such as confidence intervals rest on this assumption. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on the researcher's convenience or judgement, so some people have no chance of inclusion. Types include convenience or accidental sampling, purposive or judgement sampling, quota sampling (filling set numbers per subgroup, but non-randomly) and snowball sampling. These are cheaper and suit pilot work, qualitative studies and hidden populations, but they cannot guarantee representativeness. A common variant asks which method is best when the population has distinct subgroups: stratified random sampling. Another trap is quota sampling, which resembles stratified sampling but lacks random selection. Larger samples reduce random error but do not fix biased selection. Option c is right because random selection with known probabilities gives probability sampling the best chance of producing a representative sample.","traps":{"a":"Non-probability sampling is the umbrella term for methods in which selection is not random, so the probability of any person being chosen is unknown and some people have no chance at all. It includes convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. These methods are practical and common in clinical research, but they are open to selection bias and do not support confident generalisation. It tempts because it is the broad category paired with the correct answer.","b":"Convenience or accidental sampling recruits whoever is easiest to reach, such as students in the researcher's own class or patients attending one hospital outpatient department on a given day. It is quick and cheap but systematically over-represents accessible, willing people, so results may not generalise. Many published psychology studies use convenience samples, which is exactly why it tempts: it is common, but common does not mean representative.","d":"Snowball sampling starts with a few participants who then refer others from their networks, so the sample grows like a rolling snowball. It is useful for hidden or hard-to-reach populations, such as drug users or sex workers, for whom no sampling frame exists. But referrals stay within connected social circles, so the sample is biased toward well-connected people. It tempts because it can produce large samples quickly."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the third stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas (1926), an English social psychologist and political scientist, described the creative process in his book The Art of Thought, drawing on the self-reports of famous scientists about how their discoveries came to them. He proposed four stages. Preparation is the first: consciously studying the problem, gathering information and trying solutions, usually without success. Incubation is the second: the person stops working on the problem consciously, and processing is thought to continue outside awareness. Illumination is the third: the sudden 'aha' moment when the solution appears, often unexpectedly, as in the story of Archimedes in his bath. Verification is the fourth: the idea is consciously tested, elaborated and put into usable form. Wallas also described intimation, a fringe feeling that a solution is about to arrive, as part of the shift into illumination, and some textbooks list it as a separate stage, making five; in that version illumination becomes the fourth stage, so read the options carefully. A simple mnemonic is PIIV: prepare, incubate, illuminate, verify. Related concepts used in variants include insight learning in the chimpanzee studies of Wolfgang Kohler (1925), the call for creativity research by J. P. Guilford (1950), who later distinguished divergent from convergent thinking, and functional fixedness from Karl Duncker (1945), which incubation is thought to help break by letting misleading approaches fade. Option b is right because illumination is the third of the four stages of Wallas, coming after preparation and incubation and before verification.","traps":{"a":"Incubation is the second stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926). After intense conscious preparation, the person sets the problem aside, and unconscious processing, rest, or the fading of fixed wrong approaches is thought to prepare the ground for insight. It comes before illumination, not third. It tempts because candidates often remember incubation as the middle of the process and lose track of its exact position.","c":"Preparation is the first stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926): defining the problem, gathering information, learning the relevant skills and making conscious attempts at a solution. It lays the groundwork without which incubation and illumination cannot happen, since insight comes to a prepared mind. It tempts because some candidates start counting from incubation, forgetting that preparation is the opening stage rather than a later one.","d":"Verification is the fourth and final stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926): the insight is checked, tested, refined and put into communicable form, for example by proving a theorem or building a working version of an invention. It is conscious and effortful, like preparation. It tempts because candidates who misremember the order and put the 'aha' moment at the very end place verification third."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, how does cognitive development occur?","concept":"Jean Piaget (1936), the Swiss developmental psychologist, held a constructivist and interactionist view: children actively build their own understanding, and development comes from the interaction between biological maturation and experience with the environment. He named four factors: maturation of the nervous system, which sets the limits of what is possible at each age; experience, both physical (acting on objects) and logico-mathematical (reflecting on one's own actions); social transmission, meaning teaching and language; and equilibration, the self-regulating process that coordinates the other three. The mechanism works through schemas, organised patterns of action or thought. Assimilation fits new experience into an existing schema; accommodation changes the schema when the experience does not fit. A mismatch creates disequilibrium, which motivates the child to restore equilibrium at a higher level. This produces four invariant stages: sensorimotor (0 to 2 years), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). Neighbouring theories supply the distractors. Arnold Gesell (1928) stressed maturation alone, development unfolding on a biological timetable. Strict behaviourists such as John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1938) stressed learning alone. Lev Vygotsky, whose work appeared in English as Mind in Society (1978), stressed social interaction and culture, with the zone of proximal development and scaffolding, a term introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976). Piaget saw children as little scientists working largely on their own; Vygotsky saw them as apprentices. Option c is right because Piaget explained cognitive development as the product of maturation and learning through experience together, with neither acting alone.","traps":{"a":"'Solely through maturation' is the maturationist position of Arnold Gesell (1928), who held that development unfolds on a genetic timetable with little role for experience. Piaget did think maturation sets limits on what a child can do at each stage, but he insisted that the child's own activity and experience are essential. It tempts because Piaget's fixed, age-linked sequence of stages looks biologically driven.","b":"'Solely through learning' is the strict empiricist or behaviourist view of John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1938): the child is shaped by conditioning and reinforcement, and development is the accumulation of learned responses. Piaget rejected this, arguing that children can learn only what their current cognitive structures allow, so maturation and readiness matter. It tempts because Piaget gave experience a central role, which can sound like learning theory.","d":"Development through socialisation and cultural practices is the sociocultural theory of Lev Vygotsky, published in English as Mind in Society (1978). He argued that higher mental functions begin as social interaction and are then internalised, with learning guided by more skilled others within the zone of proximal development. Piaget listed social transmission as one factor but saw it as secondary to the child's own activity. It tempts because both theorists are constructivists."}},{"q":"What are phonemes?","concept":"Psycholinguistics studies language at several levels, and examiners test whether you can match each term to its level. Phonology is the sound system. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning in a particular language: /b/ and /p/ are different phonemes in English because 'bat' and 'pat' are different words. Phonemes have no meaning of their own. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set; Japanese speakers, for example, do not treat English /r/ and /l/ as separate phonemes. Infants can at first discriminate speech sounds from all languages but by about 10 to 12 months tune in to the phonemes of their native language, as shown by Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984). Morphology deals with morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness), and grammatical endings such as the plural -s are bound morphemes. Syntax is the set of rules for combining words and grammatical markers into sentences; Noam Chomsky (1957) made it central to linguistics. Semantics concerns the meaning of words and sentences. Pragmatics covers the social rules of language use, such as taking turns, politeness and adjusting speech to the listener, and it varies across cultures. A useful ladder: phonemes combine into morphemes, morphemes into words, and words into phrases and sentences under syntax, all used socially under pragmatics. Option b is right because phonemes are the basic units of sound used in a spoken language.","traps":{"a":"The smallest meaningful units of language are morphemes, not phonemes. A free morpheme can stand alone as a word, such as 'book', while a bound morpheme must attach to another, such as the plural -s or the prefix un-. 'Books' therefore has two morphemes but four phonemes. It tempts because both definitions use the word 'smallest', and candidates mix up the smallest sound unit with the smallest meaningful unit.","c":"Rules for combining words and grammatical markers into meaningful sentences define syntax, the grammar of word order and sentence structure. Syntax explains why 'the dog bit the man' and 'the man bit the dog' mean different things although they contain the same words. Noam Chomsky (1957) made syntax central to modern linguistics. It tempts because it is a correct definition from the same language chapter, only for a different level.","d":"Culturally specific rules for how language is used in social contexts define pragmatics: knowing how to take turns, be polite, make requests indirectly and adjust speech for a child versus an elder. Pragmatic deficits are prominent in autism spectrum disorder and define social (pragmatic) communication disorder in DSM-5 (2013). It tempts because it is a valid language definition placed beside the correct answer to test level matching."}},{"q":"which of the following F ratio values will always be insignificant despite the degrees of freedom","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA), popularised by Ronald A. Fisher (1925), tests whether two or more group means differ; the letter F was named in his honour by George Snedecor (1934). ANOVA splits total variability into between-groups variance, reflecting any treatment effect plus chance, and within-groups variance, reflecting chance or error alone. Each is turned into a mean square by dividing its sum of squares by its degrees of freedom. The F ratio is mean square between divided by mean square within. If the null hypothesis is true, both mean squares estimate the same error variance, so F should be close to 1. A real effect inflates the numerator, so F rises above 1. Significance is judged by comparing the obtained F with the critical F from tables for the given numerator and denominator degrees of freedom. Because the test looks only at the upper tail and the critical value at conventional levels is always greater than 1, an F below 1 can never be significant, whatever the degrees of freedom. An F below 1 simply means that group differences are smaller than the variation within groups. Whether F values above 1, such as 1.2, 1.55 or 2, are significant depends on the degrees of freedom and alpha: with very large samples even a small F can exceed the critical value. Related facts: F cannot be negative, since variances are squared quantities, and with two groups F equals t squared. Option b is right because 0.99 is less than 1, so it can never exceed the critical F value.","traps":{"a":"An F of 2 means the between-groups variance is twice the within-groups variance. Whether that is significant depends on the degrees of freedom: with 2 and 12 degrees of freedom the critical F at .05 is about 3.89, so it fails, but with many groups and large samples the critical value falls below 2 and it passes. It tempts because 2 looks small and unimpressive, yet it is not always insignificant.","c":"An F of 1.55 is above 1, so the between-groups variance exceeds the error variance. With small samples it will not be significant, but critical values shrink toward 1 as both degrees of freedom grow, so with large enough numerator and denominator degrees of freedom, 1.55 can be significant. It tempts because candidates who know only the common critical values, usually between about 2 and 5, assume such a small F can never pass.","d":"An F of 1.2 is close to 1 and in ordinary studies will almost never be significant. Yet it is above 1, and with very large numerator and denominator degrees of freedom the critical F approaches 1, so 1.2 can reach significance. 'Always insignificant' must hold for every possible degrees of freedom, which only an F below 1 satisfies. It tempts because it is the smallest value above 1 and looks practically equal to chance."}},{"q":"Lack of organization and planning of dreams is due to low activation of ________","concept":"Dreams, especially in REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, are vivid, emotional and visual, yet bizarre, poorly organised, short on logic and planning, and accepted uncritically while they happen. Brain imaging explains this pattern. PET studies by Pierre Maquet and colleagues (1996) and Allen Braun and colleagues (1997) found that in REM sleep the limbic system, especially the amygdala, along with the brainstem and visual association areas, is highly active, while the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is markedly deactivated. The prefrontal cortex supports executive functions: planning, working memory, logical sequencing, self-reflection and reality testing. With it offline, dream scenes jump without transition, impossible events go unquestioned, and we rarely recognise that we are dreaming. Lucid dreaming, in which the dreamer knows they are dreaming, is linked to partial reactivation of frontal areas. Braun and colleagues (1998) also showed a split within vision: the primary visual (striate) cortex was relatively deactivated while extrastriate cortex was active, fitting imagery generated internally rather than from the eyes. Theories built on this include the activation-synthesis hypothesis of J. Allan Hobson and Robert McCarley (1977), which says the forebrain makes a story out of random brainstem (pontine) activation, and the later AIM model of Hobson and colleagues (2000), describing brain states by activation, input source and neuromodulation. Mark Solms (2000) argued that dreaming depends on forebrain dopamine circuits, not just the pons. Option c is right because the lack of organisation and planning in dreams reflects low activation of the prefrontal cortex during REM sleep.","traps":{"a":"The striate cortex is the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe, named for its striped appearance. It receives input from the lateral geniculate nucleus and handles basic features such as edges and orientation. In REM sleep it is relatively deactivated because no input arrives from the eyes, but its role is vision, not organising or planning dream narratives. It tempts because dreams are so strongly visual.","b":"The somatosensory cortex, in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, processes touch, temperature, pain and body position, laid out as the sensory homunculus mapped by Wilder Penfield and Edwin Boldrey (1937). It has no special role in the logic or planning of dreams. It tempts as a filler because dreams can include bodily sensations such as falling or flying, which seem to call for a body-sense area.","d":"The extrastriate cortex comprises the visual association areas beyond V1, such as V4 for colour and V5 (MT) for motion, which build complex images and recognise objects. Allen Braun and colleagues (1998) found it active during REM sleep, which helps explain vivid dream imagery. So it is highly activated, not low, and it serves imagery rather than planning. It tempts because its REM activity is often cited alongside the prefrontal finding."}},{"q":"Slow wave sleep occurs when neurons in the ____________________ become active","concept":"Sleep and waking are controlled by mutually inhibiting groups of neurons, described by Clifford Saper and colleagues (2001) as a 'flip-flop switch'. On the sleep side is the ventrolateral preoptic area (VLPO) of the anterior hypothalamus, identified as sleep-active by Saper's group in 1996. Its neurons release the inhibitory transmitters GABA and galanin onto the arousal systems: the histaminergic tuberomammillary nucleus, the noradrenergic locus coeruleus, the serotonergic raphe nuclei, cholinergic brainstem neurons, and the orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus. When VLPO neurons fire, arousal is shut down and slow wave sleep (NREM stage N3, with high-amplitude delta waves) begins; when the arousal systems fire, they inhibit the VLPO and we wake. Because each side suppresses the other, transitions are quick, and orexin neurons stabilise the switch, which is why their loss produces narcolepsy. Earlier, Constantin von Economo, studying encephalitis lethargica from 1917, noticed that anterior hypothalamic damage caused insomnia while posterior damage caused excessive sleepiness. What pushes the switch toward sleep? Adenosine builds up with time awake (the homeostatic drive, Process S in the two-process model of Alexander Borbely, 1982) and promotes VLPO activity, while the suprachiasmatic nucleus supplies circadian timing (Process C). REM sleep has its own switch in the brainstem: REM-on neurons of the sublaterodorsal nucleus versus REM-off neurons in the ventrolateral periaqueductal gray. Option b is right because slow wave sleep starts when the GABAergic neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic area become active and inhibit the arousal centres.","traps":{"a":"The ventrolateral periaqueductal gray (vlPAG), in the midbrain around the cerebral aqueduct, contains GABAergic REM-off neurons. Together with the lateral pontine tegmentum they inhibit REM-on cells, so when they fall silent REM sleep can begin (Jun Lu and colleagues, 2006). The wider periaqueductal gray also controls pain modulation and defensive behaviour. It tempts because 'ventrolateral' appears in both this option and the correct one, but this region governs REM, not slow wave sleep.","c":"The sublaterodorsal nucleus (SLD) in the dorsal pons holds glutamatergic REM-on neurons. When they fire, REM sleep begins, and their projections to the medulla and spinal cord produce the muscle atonia of REM. Damage here releases movement during REM, a model for REM sleep behaviour disorder. It tempts because it is a genuine sleep-switch nucleus described in the same textbook passage, but it switches on REM sleep, not slow wave sleep.","d":"The suprachiasmatic nuclei (SCN) of the hypothalamus, sitting just above the optic chiasm, are the master circadian clock. They receive light input from melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells and set daily rhythms, including melatonin release by the pineal gland. They time when sleep is likely (Process C), but SCN activity does not itself generate slow wave sleep. It tempts because the SCN is the best known hypothalamic structure linked to sleep."}},{"q":"Study of monozygotic and dizygotic twins who are separated at  birth and reared apart","concept":"Behaviour genetics asks how much of the variation in a trait across people comes from genetic differences (heritability) and how much from environment. Since human breeding experiments are impossible, it relies on natural experiments in which genetic relatedness varies. Francis Galton (1875) first proposed comparing twins. Monozygotic (MZ, identical) twins come from one fertilised egg and share virtually all their genes; dizygotic (DZ, fraternal) twins come from two eggs and share about 50 percent of segregating genes, like ordinary siblings. In the classic twin study, if MZ pairs are more alike (more concordant) than DZ pairs, genes are implicated; Douglas Falconer (1960) estimated heritability as twice the difference between the MZ and DZ correlations. The strongest version studies twins separated early and reared apart, because their similarity cannot come from a shared home. The best known example is the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart led by Thomas Bouchard from 1979, which found MZ twins reared apart correlated about .70 on IQ. Examiners build variants from the neighbouring designs. Family studies check whether a disorder runs in families; risk rises with relatedness, but genes and environment are confounded. Adoption studies compare adoptees with their biological and adoptive parents, as in Leonard Heston's (1966) study of children of mothers with schizophrenia. Linkage and association studies, including genome-wide association studies, locate specific genes. Twins reared apart are, strictly, a hybrid twin and adoption design. Option d is right because a study comparing MZ and DZ twins, including those reared apart, is by definition a twin study.","traps":{"a":"Family studies compare the rate of a trait or disorder in relatives of affected people with the rate in the general population. For schizophrenia, Irving Gottesman (1991) showed risk rising with genetic closeness, from about 1 percent in the population to about 10 percent in first-degree relatives. But families share homes as well as genes, so the two cannot be separated. It tempts because twins are relatives too, yet the stem specifies MZ and DZ twins.","b":"Adoption studies compare adopted children with their biological parents (who gave the genes) and adoptive parents (who gave the environment). Leonard Heston (1966) and the Danish studies of Seymour Kety (1968) found schizophrenia more common in biological relatives. This tempts strongly because twins 'separated at birth and reared apart' were in effect adopted, making such work a twin and adoption hybrid. But the stem's defining feature is the MZ versus DZ comparison, which marks a twin study.","c":"Linkage studies track whether a disorder is inherited together with a known DNA marker across generations of large families with several affected members. Co-inheritance suggests the responsible gene lies close to that marker on the same chromosome, expressed as a LOD score. They locate genes rather than estimate heritability. It tempts because it is another behaviour-genetic method taught alongside family, twin and adoption designs, but it does not involve twins reared apart."}},{"q":"Which of the following mechanoreceptors is involved in pain perception?","concept":"The skin contains several kinds of receptors, each tuned to a type of stimulus. Mechanoreceptors respond to physical deformation of tissue. Four specialised endings handle harmless touch: Meissner's corpuscles (light touch, flutter and low-frequency vibration; fast adapting), Merkel's discs (steady pressure, fine form and texture; slow adapting), Ruffini endings (skin stretch; slow adapting) and Pacinian corpuscles (deep pressure and high-frequency vibration; fast adapting). Fast adapting receptors signal change, slow adapting ones signal a continuing stimulus. Pain is handled differently. Nociceptors are free nerve endings: bare, unencapsulated branches of sensory neurons. They detect tissue-damaging mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli, and other free endings signal temperature and itch. Many are polymodal, so a free ending that responds to crushing or pinching is both a mechanoreceptor and a nociceptor, which is why the stem can loosely call them mechanoreceptors. Two fibre types carry pain: thin myelinated A-delta fibres give fast, sharp first pain, and unmyelinated C fibres give slow, dull, burning second pain. Heat and capsaicin act on the TRPV1 channel in these endings. Pain is then modulated centrally, as in the gate control theory of Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall (1965), in which activity in large touch fibres can close a spinal gate, explaining why rubbing an injury eases it. Exams vary this item by asking which receptor signals vibration (Pacinian), stretch (Ruffini) or texture (Merkel). Option c is right because free nerve endings are the receptors that signal pain.","traps":{"a":"Merkel's discs (Merkel cell-neurite complexes), described by Friedrich Merkel (1875), lie at the base of the epidermis, densely in the fingertips. They are slowly adapting, so they keep firing during steady pressure and signal fine form, edges and texture, as in reading Braille. They are low-threshold receptors that respond to harmless pressure. It tempts because they are a well known mechanoreceptor and hard pressure can hurt, but that pain is carried by free endings.","b":"Meissner's corpuscles are encapsulated receptors in the dermal papillae of hairless (glabrous) skin such as fingertips, palms and lips. They adapt rapidly, so they signal change: light stroking touch, flutter, low-frequency vibration and an object slipping in the hand. They respond to gentle stimulation and play no role in signalling tissue damage. They tempt because they are among the most sensitive touch receptors, and 'sensitive' is easily confused with 'painful'.","d":"Ruffini endings (Ruffini corpuscles) are spindle-shaped encapsulated receptors deep in the dermis and in joint capsules. They are slowly adapting and respond to skin stretch and sustained pressure, helping to sense finger position and the shape of grasped objects. Like the other corpuscles, they have a low threshold for harmless mechanical change. They tempt because older textbooks linked Ruffini endings with warmth, and candidates stretch that to pain, but neither is their job."}},{"q":"Norman Triplett observed _______________ tended to race _________ when racing in the presence of others","concept":"Social facilitation is the effect of the mere presence of others, as co-actors or as an audience, on individual performance. Its starting point is Norman Triplett (1898), often credited with the first social psychology experiment. Studying racing records of the League of American Wheelmen, Triplett noticed that cyclists rode faster when racing against others or with a pacer than when riding alone against the clock. He proposed 'dynamogenic' factors: the presence of a rival releases latent energy. He then tested this in the laboratory with children winding fishing reels, alone or in pairs; many wound faster in pairs, though some did worse. Floyd Allport later named the effect social facilitation. The mixed results were explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, and arousal strengthens the dominant response. On simple or well-learned tasks the dominant response is correct, so performance improves; on complex or new tasks it is often wrong, so performance worsens (social inhibition). Zajonc and colleagues (1969) even showed this in cockroaches running simple versus complex runways. Nickolas Cottrell (1968) argued the arousal comes from evaluation apprehension, and Robert Baron (1986) proposed distraction-conflict theory. Do not confuse this with social loafing (Bibb Latane and colleagues, 1979), where people working on a pooled group task, as in Max Ringelmann's rope pulling, exert less effort because individual output cannot be identified. Option c is right because Triplett observed that cyclists raced faster in the presence of others than alone.","traps":{"a":"This keeps the correct subjects but reverses the direction. A slowing effect of others, called social inhibition, does occur, but on complex or poorly learned tasks, as Robert Zajonc (1965) explained through arousal strengthening the dominant response. Racing a bicycle is a simple, well-practised task, so arousal helped. It tempts because some of Triplett's own reel-winding children did worse in pairs, and because social loafing also describes reduced effort in groups.","b":"Both parts are wrong. Rats were not Triplett's subjects; his data came from human cyclists and then children winding fishing reels. Animal work on social facilitation came later, most famously the 1969 cockroach runway study by Robert Zajonc and colleagues, and the effect on a simple task was faster, not slower. It tempts because rats dominate early learning research, from Edward Tolman's (1948) maze studies to B. F. Skinner's (1938) operant chamber.","d":"This has the direction right but the subjects wrong. Faster performance in company was later shown in animals such as ants and cockroaches, the latter in the 1969 study by Robert Zajonc and colleagues, but Triplett (1898) worked with human cyclists' racing records and children's reel winding. It tempts because candidates remember 'faster' and that social facilitation was later demonstrated in animals, then attach the wrong species to Triplett."}},{"q":"What is systematic measurement error?","concept":"Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman (1904), says every observed score is the sum of a true score and error (X = T + E). Error comes in two kinds, and examiners like to contrast them. Random error is unpredictable: it pushes scores up on one occasion and down on another, through things like momentary fatigue, guessing, noise or lapses of attention. Because it is equally likely in either direction, it tends to cancel out across many measurements, and its main effect is to lower reliability (precision). Systematic error, also called bias or constant error, pushes scores in the same direction every time. A weighing scale that always reads 2 kg heavy, a translated questionnaire whose items are harder for one group, a lenient examiner, and social desirability responding are all examples. Because it repeats consistently, systematic error does not lower reliability; a biased scale can give the same wrong reading every time. Instead it threatens validity and accuracy, because the scores are consistently off target. The dartboard analogy helps: darts tightly grouped away from the bullseye show high reliability with systematic error, while darts scattered around the bullseye show random error. Systematic error can arise from the instrument, the observer, the procedure or the participant, and it cannot be removed simply by averaging more measurements; it needs calibration, standardisation, blinding or counterbalancing. Option b is right because systematic error is defined by being consistent in the same direction across measurements.","traps":{"a":"Error from random fluctuations is random (unsystematic) error. It arises from chance factors such as momentary fatigue, mood, distraction or guessing, and it varies from one measurement to the next. In classical test theory it is the E that lowers reliability and averages toward zero over many trials. It tempts because 'error' in everyday speech means random mistakes, and because both kinds of error are defined together in the same textbook section.","c":"Error that is inconsistent and changes direction across measurements is again random error, here defined by its behaviour rather than its cause. It is the direct opposite of systematic error, which keeps the same direction. Random error reduces reliability, whereas systematic error biases scores and reduces validity. It tempts because it mirrors the correct option word for word with one key change, so a reader who skims picks the wrong half of the pair.","d":"Participant bias, such as social desirability, acquiescence or reacting to demand characteristics as described by Martin Orne (1962), can indeed produce systematic error. But it is only one possible source, alongside the instrument, the observer and the procedure. The question asks what systematic error is, its defining property, not one of its causes. It tempts because it names a real bias and sounds more specific, which many candidates mistake for more correct."}},{"q":"Which of the following were flaws that David Wechsler aimed to rectify with his Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"David Wechsler, chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, published the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale in 1939 because the leading test of the day, the Stanford-Binet of Lewis Terman (1916, revised with Maud Merrill in 1937), served adults poorly. His criticisms form a favourite exam list. First, the Binet was built for children: its items and norms did not suit or appeal to adults. Second, it leaned heavily on verbal material and word manipulation, penalising people with limited schooling or language. Third, it gave weight to speed, with timed items scattered through the scale, which handicapped older adults whose accuracy was intact. Fourth, it relied on mental age and the ratio IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) proposed by William Stern (1912); since mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, a ratio IQ makes little sense for adults. Wechsler's solutions became standard. He replaced the age scale, in which items are grouped by the age at which most children pass them, with a point scale, in which items of one type form a subtest and earn points. He added a Performance scale of nonverbal tasks such as Block Design and Picture Completion, drawing on the Army Beta of World War I. He replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ, mean 100 and standard deviation 15, comparing each person with his or her own age group. These ideas carried into the WISC (1949) and WAIS (1955). Option a is right because it lists exactly the four flaws Wechsler set out to correct.","traps":{"b":"This reverses the first flaw. The Stanford-Binet descends from the 1905 scale of Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon, designed for schoolchildren, so appeal to children was never its weakness. It also substitutes 'math questions' for the real complaint about verbal emphasis, and 'physical age' for mental age, a term no IQ formula uses. Only 'speed over accuracy' is correct. It tempts because that one true phrase makes the whole option look partly right.","c":"This gets 'no appeal for adults' right, and vocabulary is at least a word task, but the last two parts are wrong. The Binet valued speed over accuracy, the reverse of what is written here, and it relied on mental age, not 'physical age'. It tempts because the first phrase matches the real list exactly and vocabulary items did feature in the Binet, so a reader who checks only the opening words is caught.","d":"This contains only one real flaw, reliance on mental age. The Binet did appeal to children, it did not emphasise history questions, and its timed items favoured speed rather than accuracy. Wechsler's concern was adults, which is why his first scale targeted them; his children's scale, the WISC, came only in 1949. It tempts because mental age is the most famous Binet concept, and seeing it named makes the option feel authoritative."}},{"q":"What is the formula for Wechsler's new method for obtaining the IQ score?","concept":"Two ways of expressing IQ appear in exams. The ratio IQ, proposed by William Stern (1912) and adopted by Lewis Terman for the 1916 Stanford-Binet, divides mental age by chronological age and multiplies by 100, so a ten-year-old performing like a typical twelve-year-old scores 120. It fails for adults, because mental age levels off in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps rising, so a ratio IQ would fall steadily with age. David Wechsler, building the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), dropped mental age entirely. He described the IQ as the relation between the score a person actually attains and the mean score expected for people of the same age, which is the formula in this question: IQ equals actual score over expected mean score for age. A person scoring exactly at the age-group mean therefore sits at the centre of the scale. In practice the comparison is carried out with standard scores, the deviation IQ: the raw score is converted to a z score within the person's age group, z = (X minus age-group mean) divided by the age-group standard deviation, and then IQ = 100 + 15z. So an IQ of 115 is one standard deviation above age peers, and 130 is two above, roughly the top 2 percent. The Stanford-Binet adopted the deviation IQ in 1960 (with SD 16 until the fifth edition moved to 15). Option a is right because Wechsler's IQ divides the person's actual score by the expected mean score for his or her age group.","traps":{"b":"This inverts Wechsler's ratio by putting the expected mean on top. A person who outperformed age peers would then get a value below 1, so the brightest people would receive the lowest IQs. It mirrors a common slip with the ratio IQ of William Stern (1912), writing chronological age over mental age instead of mental age over chronological age. It tempts because it uses the same two terms and only their order changes.","c":"Multiplying the actual score by the age-expected mean has no psychometric meaning. The product grows with both terms, so a person in an age group with a high mean would gain IQ points simply for being that age, the opposite of comparing someone with peers. Every IQ formula compares two quantities, by division or in standard deviation units. It tempts only because the familiar ratio IQ formula ends in 'times 100'.","d":"Adding the expected mean for age to the actual score has no meaning either, because it does not compare the person with anyone. Two people of the same age would differ only by their raw scores, and the age norms would play no real role. It tempts because the modern deviation IQ formula does contain an addition, IQ = 100 + 15z, so a candidate who remembers a plus sign without the logic may pick it."}},{"q":"Which cognitive theories were used to derive the five factors of intelligence assessed in the SB5?","concept":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, Fifth Edition (SB5), developed by Gale Roid (2003), covers ages 2 to 85 and above. It measures five cognitive factors, each through a verbal and a nonverbal subtest, giving ten subtests in all: Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing and Working Memory. These combine into a Full Scale IQ, a Verbal IQ and a Nonverbal IQ, each with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Two routing subtests, Nonverbal Fluid Reasoning and Verbal Knowledge, set the starting level for the rest. The factor structure was built on two cognitive theories. The first is the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which joins Raymond Cattell's (1963) distinction between fluid and crystallized intelligence, extended by John Horn, with the three-stratum theory of John B. Carroll (1993), drawn from his reanalysis of more than 460 factor-analytic data sets: general ability (g) at stratum III, broad abilities such as fluid reasoning, crystallized knowledge, quantitative knowledge and visual processing at stratum II, and narrow abilities at stratum I. Four SB5 factors map onto these broad abilities. The fifth, Working Memory, draws on the model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), in which a central executive directs a phonological loop and a visuospatial sketchpad; this is why the SB5 tests working memory in both verbal and nonverbal forms. Variants ask how many factors the SB5 has, its routing subtests, or the theory behind the KABC-II or Woodcock-Johnson batteries. Option c is right because the SB5 factors were derived from Carroll's hierarchical model of abilities and Baddeley's working memory model.","traps":{"a":"Erik Erikson (1950) proposed eight psychosocial stages, each a crisis such as trust versus mistrust, and Lev Vygotsky (Mind in Society, 1978 in English) stressed that thinking develops through social interaction, introducing the zone of proximal development. Both are developmental theories about how people change, not factor models of ability structure, so neither supplied the SB5 factors. It tempts because both names are famous and Vygotsky's work is sometimes classed as cognitive.","b":"Jean Piaget, working from the 1920s onward, described four stages of cognitive development (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, formal operational), and Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed a hierarchy of needs rising to self-actualisation. Piaget explains how thinking changes qualitatively with age, and Maslow addresses motivation, not ability. Neither yields measurable factors like quantitative reasoning or working memory. It tempts because Piaget is the most famous 'cognitive' theorist and the Binet tradition tests children.","d":"B. F. Skinner (1938) developed operant conditioning, explaining behaviour through reinforcement history without reference to mental structures, and Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) founded psychoanalysis, explaining behaviour through unconscious conflict. One is behaviourist and the other psychodynamic, so neither is a cognitive theory at all, and neither proposes ability factors. It tempts only as a pair of famous names for candidates who are guessing."}},{"q":"What are the two scales included in the KBIT-2?","concept":"The Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, Second Edition (KBIT-2), by Alan S. Kaufman and Nadeen L. Kaufman (2004), is a short screening test of verbal and nonverbal intelligence for ages 4 to 90 that takes about 20 minutes. It revised the original K-BIT (1990). Its two scales follow the fluid and crystallized distinction of Raymond Cattell (1963) and John Horn. The Verbal or Crystallized scale has two subtests, Verbal Knowledge (pointing to the picture that matches a word or answers a question) and Riddles (naming a concept from verbal clues), which tap vocabulary, general information and verbal reasoning learned through schooling and culture. The Nonverbal or Fluid scale is Matrices, in which the examinee completes a visual pattern or analogy, tapping reasoning with new material that depends little on prior learning. The two scales combine into an IQ Composite with mean 100 and SD 15. Because the nonverbal task needs minimal language, a large gap between the scales can flag language difficulty or cultural disadvantage. Brief tests like this, or the Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence (1999), are used for screening and research, not for diagnosing intellectual disability, which needs a full battery. Keep the Kaufman family straight: the K-ABC (1983) measured sequential versus simultaneous processing based on the neuropsychology of Alexander Luria (The Working Brain, 1973), the KABC-II (2004) added a CHC interpretation, and the KTEA is their achievement test of reading, mathematics and writing. Option d is right because the KBIT-2 consists of a Verbal or Crystallized scale and a Nonverbal or Fluid scale.","traps":{"a":"Reading comprehension and mathematics are school achievement areas, measured by achievement tests such as the Kaufman Test of Educational Achievement (KTEA) or the Wechsler Individual Achievement Test. Achievement tests measure what has been taught, whereas intelligence tests aim at reasoning capacity. It tempts because the Kaufmans did publish an achievement test, and because the gap between ability and achievement was long used to identify specific learning disorders.","b":"Quantitative and analytical sections recall aptitude tests like the GRE, whose older General Test had verbal, quantitative and analytical parts, and the triarchic theory of Robert Sternberg (1985) with its analytical intelligence. Quantitative Reasoning is also one of the five SB5 factors. The KBIT-2, however, has no quantitative or analytical scale. It tempts because both words sound like standard intelligence subscales from other well known tests.","c":"EQ, or emotional intelligence, was defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995). It is measured with separate instruments such as the Mayer-Salovey-Caruso Emotional Intelligence Test (MSCEIT) or the Bar-On EQ-i, not by brief cognitive screeners, which measure only cognitive ability. It tempts because 'IQ and EQ' is a familiar pairing in popular psychology and sounds like a neat two-scale structure."}},{"q":"What was developed by Alan Kaufman to measure intelligence?","concept":"Alan S. Kaufman is an American psychometrician who, with his wife Nadeen L. Kaufman, produced a whole family of individually administered tests. Before that he worked with David Wechsler on the WISC-R (1974), and his book Intelligent Testing with the WISC-R (1979) taught clinicians to interpret patterns of subtest scores in the light of theory, not just the Full Scale IQ. The Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test (K-BIT, 1990; KBIT-2, 2004) is a quick screener of verbal (crystallized) and nonverbal (fluid) ability. The Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children (K-ABC, 1983) was built on the sequential versus simultaneous processing distinction from Alexander Luria (1973) and aimed to reduce cultural loading; the KABC-II (2004) offers both a Luria and a Cattell-Horn-Carroll interpretation. The Kaufman Adolescent and Adult Intelligence Test (KAIT, 1993) covers ages 11 and above, and the Kaufman Test of Educational Achievement (KTEA) measures academic skills. Distinguish these clinical, one-to-one tests from large-scale group admission tests. The SAT, first given in 1926 and developed for the College Board by Carl Brigham, and the ACT, founded by E. F. Lindquist in 1959, are United States college admission tests. The GRE, run by Educational Testing Service, is used for graduate admission. These are group aptitude or achievement tests used for selection, not intelligence tests authored by Kaufman. Variants ask who developed the K-ABC, which theory it rests on, or what the KBIT-2 scales are. Option b is right because the K-BIT, the Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, was developed by Alan Kaufman with Nadeen Kaufman to measure intelligence.","traps":{"a":"The ACT is an American college admission test introduced in 1959 by E. F. Lindquist of the University of Iowa. It covers English, mathematics, reading and science, so it is closer to an achievement test of school learning than to an intelligence test, and it is given to large groups at once. It has no connection with Kaufman. It tempts because, like the SAT and GRE, it is a famous standardised test.","c":"The SAT was first administered in 1926 for the College Board, adapted by Carl Brigham from the Army Alpha tests of World War I. It is a group admission test of reading, writing and mathematics used by United States colleges, originally called the Scholastic Aptitude Test. Although it correlates with IQ, it is a selection test, not a Kaufman instrument. It tempts because its original name contained 'aptitude', which sounds like intelligence.","d":"The GRE (Graduate Record Examinations) is a group admission test for graduate school, run by Educational Testing Service, with verbal reasoning, quantitative reasoning and analytical writing sections. It assesses developed academic skills for selection and was not authored by any single psychologist, certainly not by Kaufman. It tempts because its 'reasoning' sections sound like intelligence subtests, and because it is the test most familiar to students planning graduate study abroad."}},{"q":"What is the RAT?","concept":"The RAT is the Remote Associates Test, devised by Sarnoff Mednick (1962) to measure creativity from his associative theory. Mednick defined creative thinking as forming associative elements into new combinations that are useful or meet some requirement, and argued that creative people have a flat associative hierarchy: besides the obvious, strong associations to a word, they can reach weak, remote ones. People with a steep hierarchy produce only the common responses and stop. Each RAT item gives three words that seem unrelated, and the testtaker must find a fourth word that links all three; for example, cottage, Swiss and cake are linked by cheese. The original college form was a timed, 40-minute paper-and-pencil test of about 30 items, scored as the number solved. Note the paradox examiners like: although it targets creativity, each RAT item has a single correct answer, so it relies on convergent thinking. Contrast it with divergent thinking tests, based on the distinction drawn by J. P. Guilford (1950, 1967), such as the Alternate Uses Task (list many uses for a brick) and the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking by E. Paul Torrance (1966), scored for fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration. Modern compound remote associate items are used in insight research to compare insight with step-by-step analytic solutions. Another classic creativity framework is the four-stage model of Graham Wallas (1926): preparation, incubation, illumination and verification. Option c is right because the RAT is a timed, 40-minute paper-and-pencil test.","traps":{"a":"'Rapid Action Team' is an invented expansion with no place in psychological testing. In India the similar-sounding Rapid Action Force is a riot-control wing of the Central Reserve Police Force, which may make the phrase feel familiar. In psychology, RAT stands for the Remote Associates Test of Sarnoff Mednick (1962). It tempts because acronym questions train candidates to look for an expansion, and this is the only option that offers one.","b":"This plays on the literal word. Rats are the classic laboratory animal of learning psychology, from the cognitive map studies of Edward Tolman (1948) to the operant chamber of B. F. Skinner (1938), so 'rat' feels at home in a psychology paper. But the capitalised RAT here is an acronym for a creativity test, the Remote Associates Test. It tempts candidates who do not recognise the acronym and fall back on the literal meaning.","d":"The RAT is not a device; it is a paper-and-pencil test made of printed word triads, although researchers now often present the items on computers. In computing, RAT means remote access trojan, a type of malware, which may explain the lure. Electronic recording tools in psychology include the polygraph and the EEG. It tempts because modern testing is often computerised, so 'electronic' sounds plausible for almost any test."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the MMPI-2?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota to help diagnose psychiatric patients. Its key innovation was empirical criterion keying: items were kept only if they statistically separated diagnosed patient groups from normal controls, whatever the item content seemed to mean. The MMPI-2 (1989, James Butcher and colleagues) restandardised it on a more representative US sample and has 567 true/false items for adults. It yields ten clinical scales: Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Masculinity-Femininity, Paranoia, Psychasthenia, Schizophrenia, Hypomania and Social Introversion, scored as T-scores (mean 50, SD 10), with T of 65 or more treated as clinically significant. Validity scales check test-taking attitude: L (lie), F (infrequency or faking bad), K (defensiveness), plus VRIN and TRIN for inconsistent responding. Clinicians interpret code types, the two highest clinical scales, such as 2-7 for anxious depression. Later versions are the MMPI-A (1992) for adolescents, the shorter MMPI-2-RF (2008) and the MMPI-3 (2020). Neighbours to separate: the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977) targets personality disorders; the 16PF of Raymond Cattell (1949) and the NEO-PI-R of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) measure normal personality; interest inventories guide careers. The MMPI is also used in personnel screening, custody and forensic work, but its core purpose remains clinical. Option a is right because the MMPI-2 was designed to assess psychopathology and so aid psychiatric diagnosis.","traps":{"b":"Educational assessment measures learning and school readiness, using achievement tests (for example the Wechsler Individual Achievement Test), aptitude tests and diagnostic tests for specific learning disorders. The MMPI-2 measures none of these; it is for adults 18 and over, and even the adolescent version, the MMPI-A, targets psychopathology, not learning. It tempts because school counsellors and educational psychologists also use personality tests, so candidates link testing in general with education.","c":"Physical health diagnosis is made through medical examination and laboratory tests, not a personality inventory. The MMPI-2 does have a Hypochondriasis scale (scale 1) and a Health Concerns content scale, but these measure psychological preoccupation with bodily symptoms, not actual disease. It tempts because those health-related scales, and the use of the MMPI in medical settings to screen for psychological factors in pain or illness, suggest a medical purpose.","d":"Career assessment uses interest inventories such as the Strong Interest Inventory, begun by Edward K. Strong (1927), and the Self-Directed Search based on the RIASEC theory of John Holland (1959), which match a person's interests to occupations. The MMPI-2 contains no occupational scales. It tempts because the MMPI is used in personnel screening for high-risk jobs such as police and pilots, but there it screens out psychopathology rather than guiding career choice."}},{"q":"What does answering 'false' to L Scale items in the MMPI-2 assert?","concept":"The MMPI-2 has validity scales that show how a person approached the test before any clinical scale is interpreted. The L (Lie) scale has 15 items, chosen rationally rather than by empirical keying, modelled on the character studies of Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928). Each item describes a small fault or socially disapproved act that almost everyone will admit, such as occasionally putting off until tomorrow what ought to be done today, or sometimes getting angry. All L items are scored when answered 'false'. Answering 'false' therefore denies ordinary human weaknesses, which claims a level of virtue and self-control rarely seen in the culture. A few such answers are normal, but a high L score (T of 65 or more) points to a naive, unsophisticated attempt to look good; problems may be underreported and clinical scales falsely low. High L is common in custody evaluations, job applicants, and very conventional or devout people. Contrast the other validity scales: K measures subtler, more sophisticated defensiveness and is used to correct five clinical scales; S (Superlative Self-Presentation, James Butcher and Yossef Han, 1995) also detects over-favourable reporting; F (Infrequency) detects rare, extreme answers, suggesting faking bad, severe distress or random responding; VRIN and TRIN detect inconsistent and yea-saying or nay-saying responding; and the Cannot Say count flags omitted items. Option c is right because denying the common faults in L items asserts a degree of personal virtue rarely observed in the culture.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the meaning. Lacking virtue would be shown by admitting faults, that is, answering 'true' to L items, and doing so is the normal, honest pattern that produces a low or average L score. A low L does not mean a person lacks virtue; it suggests frank, sometimes self-critical responding. It tempts because 'false' sounds like a negative statement about the self, so candidates link it with something negative.","b":"Careless responding from not reading items properly is detected by the VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) scale, which counts contradictory answers to pairs of similar items, and by an elevated F scale. Random answering raises both, and L only by chance. Consistently denying the specific faults in the L items is a deliberate pattern of looking good, not carelessness. It tempts because any invalid profile is easy to blame on careless reading.","d":"Poor comprehension, from low reading ability, limited language or confusion, usually shows up as many omitted items (the Cannot Say count), inconsistent answers on VRIN, or a raised F scale. The L items are short and simple, so denying them reflects motive, not misunderstanding. It tempts because high L scores are more common in less educated, unsophisticated respondents, which candidates mistake for poor understanding rather than naive defensiveness."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not a factor that contributes to consistency in measurement?","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of measurement: whether a test gives the same result for the same person under the same conditions. In classical test theory (Charles Spearman, 1904), an observed score is a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of observed score variance that is true score variance. Anything that reduces error variance makes scores more consistent. Three classic contributors appear in this question. First, the stability of the construct itself: a stable trait such as intelligence gives consistent scores over time, while a fluctuating state such as mood or state anxiety changes even if the test is perfect, which is why the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory of Charles Spielberger (1970) measures the two separately. Second, consistency of administration: standard instructions, time limits, setting and examiner behaviour, which is the point of standardisation. Third, consistency of scoring: objective keys, clear rubrics and trained scorers, checked by inter-rater statistics such as Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960). Other factors that raise reliability, and often appear in variant questions, include test length (more items, as the Spearman-Brown formula shows), a heterogeneous sample with a wide score range, items of moderate difficulty, and homogeneous item content. Complexity of a measure is not a recognised source of consistency: a complex test can be highly reliable and a simple one unreliable, depending on how it is built, given and scored. Option d is right because the complexity of the measure is the only choice that is not a standard contributor to consistency.","traps":{"a":"Stability of the construct is a genuine contributor. If the trait itself changes between occasions, scores change and test-retest reliability falls, even with a perfect test. Traits such as intelligence are stable; states such as mood, fatigue or state anxiety fluctuate. It tempts in a 'not' question because candidates think of reliability as a property of the test alone, forgetting that the thing being measured must also hold still.","b":"Consistent administration is a genuine contributor. Standardised instructions, time limits, testing conditions and examiner behaviour remove variation that has nothing to do with the testtaker, which is the whole purpose of a standardised test. Differences in rapport or in how instructions are read add error variance. It tempts because it seems procedural rather than psychometric, so some candidates wrongly assume it does not affect reliability.","c":"Consistent scoring is a genuine contributor. When scoring is objective, as with multiple-choice keys, scorers agree; when it needs judgement, as with essays or projective tests like the Rorschach, scorer differences add error. That is why John Exner (1974) built the Comprehensive System for the Rorschach, and why inter-rater agreement is checked with Cohen's kappa. It tempts because scoring happens after testing and seems separate from measurement itself."}},{"q":"Which method of estimating reliability involves administering the same test twice to the same group of people and correlating their scores?","concept":"Reliability can be estimated in several ways, and each targets a different source of error. The test-retest method gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the two sets of scores with Pearson's r; the result is the coefficient of stability, and its error source is time sampling. Its problems are practice and memory (carryover) effects if the interval is short, and genuine change in the trait if it is long, so it suits stable traits such as intelligence rather than moods. The alternate or parallel forms method gives two equivalent versions of the test and yields a coefficient of equivalence, controlling memory for specific items. Internal consistency methods need only one administration: split-half reliability correlates two halves and corrects with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910); Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937) handles right-or-wrong items; and coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) generalises KR-20 to any item format. Inter-rater reliability checks agreement between scorers. Item response theory, developed by Frederic Lord (1952) and by Georg Rasch (1960), is a different framework: it models the probability of each response from item difficulty, discrimination and person ability, and replaces a single reliability coefficient with a test information function. A useful memory hook is that only test-retest uses the identical test twice, separated by time. Option b is right because administering the same test twice to the same group and correlating the scores is the test-retest method.","traps":{"a":"The split-half method gives the test once, divides it into two halves (often odd versus even items), correlates the halves, and corrects the result upward with the Spearman-Brown formula because each half is only half as long. It measures internal consistency, so its error source is content sampling, not time. It tempts because it also involves correlating two sets of scores obtained from the same people.","c":"Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is an internal consistency coefficient computed from a single administration, using the number of items, the item variances and the total score variance. It equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients and generalises KR-20 to items with more than two response options. It tempts because it is the most widely reported reliability statistic, so candidates choose it for any reliability question.","d":"Item response theory (Frederic Lord, 1952; Georg Rasch, 1960) is a modern measurement framework, not a reliability procedure. It models the probability of a given response from item parameters (difficulty, discrimination, guessing) and the person's latent ability, and it underpins computerised adaptive testing. It does not need two administrations. It tempts because it is a sophisticated, current-sounding approach that candidates feel must be relevant to any measurement question."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a potential limitation of the internal consistency method of estimating reliability?","concept":"Internal consistency reliability estimates how well the items of a test hang together, using one administration only. Methods include split-half reliability with the Spearman-Brown correction, Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937) for right-or-wrong items, coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) for any item format, and omega (Roderick McDonald, 1999). The error source these methods capture is content sampling: whether the particular items chosen measure the same thing. Their main limitation follows directly from the design. Because all the data come from a single sitting, internal consistency says nothing about stability across time; a mood questionnaire can have an alpha of .90 and still give very different scores next week. Time sampling error is the job of test-retest reliability, and differences between versions are the job of alternate forms reliability. Other cautions are worth knowing for variant questions. Alpha rises with the number of items, so a long test can show high alpha even when items are only modestly related. Alpha is lowered when a test is heterogeneous or multidimensional, yet a high alpha does not prove that a test measures a single dimension. Internal consistency should not be used for speeded tests, because unreached items at the end inflate the estimate. A one-item measure cannot be assessed this way. The keyed answer is the standard textbook limitation; strictly, the content and multiple-item statements also describe real properties, which makes 'All of the above' arguable. Option a is right because internal consistency is based on a single administration and therefore cannot show consistency over time.","traps":{"b":"Internal consistency is indeed affected by content: if the items sample different domains, inter-item correlations fall and alpha drops. But sensitivity to content heterogeneity is exactly what the method is designed to detect, so textbooks treat it as what internal consistency measures rather than as its limitation. Differences between versions of a test are the concern of alternate forms reliability. It tempts because the statement is true, which is why this key is debatable.","c":"Needing multiple items is literally true: with one item there is nothing to correlate, and alpha needs at least two. But almost every psychological test has many items, so this is a trivial condition of use rather than the recognised limitation. The textbook answer focuses on the lack of any information about stability over time. It tempts because the statement is factually correct, and factual correctness pulls candidates toward it.","d":"'All of the above' would be right only if every statement were accepted as a limitation. The key treats the single-administration problem as the one real limitation, and regards content sensitivity and the need for several items as properties rather than flaws. It tempts strongly here because the other two statements are technically true, so a careful reader may feel forced to include them; this is the arguable point in the item."}},{"q":"What is the major challenge with split-half reliability?","concept":"Split-half reliability is an internal consistency method: the test is given once, scored as two halves, and the two half-scores are correlated. Because each half is only half as long as the whole test, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation underestimates full-test reliability and is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910), 2r / (1 + r). The central practical problem is how to split. The two halves must be as nearly equivalent as possible in content, difficulty and position, or the correlation will be lowered by differences between the halves rather than by unreliability. Splitting first half against second half is the worst choice, because items usually get harder, testtakers tire or warm up, and in a timed test many never reach the later items. Accepted solutions are the odd-even split, random assignment of items to halves, or matching items for content and difficulty. Different splits of the same test give different coefficients; a 10-item test can be split 126 ways. Lee Cronbach (1951) dealt with this by showing that coefficient alpha equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients. The formulas of Phillip Rulon (1939) and Louis Guttman (1945) estimate split-half reliability without assuming equal half variances. Keep the other designs separate: test-retest gives the same test twice across a time interval, and alternate forms gives two different versions of the test. Option b is right because dividing the test into two nearly equivalent halves is the major challenge of split-half reliability.","traps":{"a":"The Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) corrects the half-test correlation to estimate full-length reliability: full r = 2r / (1 + r), where r is the correlation between halves. Once the halves are correlated, this is a simple calculation, a routine step rather than a challenge. It tempts because it is the most technical-sounding part of split-half reliability, and candidates assume the hard part must be the formula.","c":"Giving the same test to the same group twice, with a time interval between, is the test-retest method, which yields a coefficient of stability. Its own challenges are choosing the interval, practice and memory effects, and real change in the trait. Split-half needs only one administration, which is its main convenience. It tempts because the option accurately describes a real reliability procedure, just not the one in the stem.","d":"Giving two different versions of a test to the same group is the alternate (parallel) forms method, which yields a coefficient of equivalence. Its challenge is building two forms that are truly equal in content, difficulty and variance, which is costly. It tempts because that problem, constructing equivalent forms, closely resembles the split-half problem of constructing equivalent halves; split-half is essentially alternate forms built within one test."}},{"q":"If the half-test reliability coefficient is .60, what is the estimated reliability of the full test using the Spearman-Brown formula?","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula, published separately by Charles Spearman and William Brown in 1910, predicts how reliability changes when a test is lengthened or shortened with comparable items. The general form is new r = kr / (1 + (k minus 1)r), where r is the current reliability and k is the factor by which length changes. In split-half reliability each half is half the length of the whole test, so the correlation between halves underestimates full-test reliability; doubling (k = 2) simplifies the formula to full r = 2r / (1 + r). Here: 2 times .60 = 1.20; 1 + .60 = 1.60; 1.20 divided by 1.60 = .75. So a half-test correlation of .60 implies a full-test reliability of .75. Notice the pattern: lengthening raises reliability, but with diminishing returns, and the result can never exceed 1. Exam variants use other values of k. Tripling a test with reliability .60 gives 1.80 / 2.20 = .82. Halving it (k = 0.5) gives .30 / .70 = .43. You can also solve for k: to raise reliability from .60 to .80, k = (.80 times .40) / (.60 times .20) = 2.67, so the test must be about 2.7 times as long. The formula assumes the added items are parallel to the existing ones and that the test is not speeded. For whole tests, coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) avoids depending on a single split. Option a is right because 2(.60) / (1 + .60) = 1.20 / 1.60 = .75.","traps":{"b":".82 is what Spearman-Brown gives if the test is tripled rather than doubled: 3(.60) / (1 + 2(.60)) = 1.80 / 2.20 = .82. Split-half correction always involves doubling, because each half is half the full test, so k is 2, not 3. It tempts because it is a genuine Spearman-Brown result and lies in the plausible range above .60, so candidates who apply the wrong k land on it.","c":".30 is simply .60 divided by 2. It moves the coefficient in the wrong direction: the full test is longer than either half, so its reliability must be higher than .60, not lower. Even shortening a .60 test to half length by Spearman-Brown would give .43, not .30. It tempts candidates who see the word 'half-test' and mechanically halve the number they are given.","d":".60 is the uncorrected correlation between the two halves, which is the input to the formula, not the answer. Leaving it uncorrected underestimates the reliability of the full-length test, because each half has only half the items and shorter tests are less reliable. It tempts candidates who forget that split-half coefficients must always be stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula before being reported."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of averaging several splits in estimating the reliability of a psychological test according to Cronbach?","concept":"Split-half reliability depends on how the test is split: odd versus even items, first versus second half, or a random division can each give a different coefficient, and even a 10-item test can be split 126 ways. Any one split is therefore partly arbitrary. Lee J. Cronbach (1951), in his paper on coefficient alpha and the internal structure of tests, showed that alpha equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients (computed with the formula of Phillip Rulon, 1939). Averaging across every split removes the luck of a particular division, so the estimate is more accurate and stable than any single split-half value. Alpha is calculated without actually splitting: alpha = (k / (k minus 1)) times (1 minus the sum of item variances divided by total score variance), where k is the number of items. For right-or-wrong items it reduces to Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937). Points examiners use in variants: alpha rises with more items and with higher average inter-item correlation; values around .70 are often treated as acceptable for research and about .90 for individual clinical decisions; alpha is a lower bound to true reliability unless items are essentially tau-equivalent; and a high alpha does not prove the test measures a single dimension. Cronbach and colleagues (1972) later broadened reliability into generalizability theory, which separates several sources of error at once, such as raters, occasions and items. Option d is right because averaging several splits increases the accuracy of the reliability estimate.","traps":{"a":"Averaging split-half coefficients does not add dimensions. Dimensions are the underlying constructs a test measures, identified by factor analysis, as in the g factor of Charles Spearman (1904) or the primary mental abilities of Louis Thurstone (1938). Adding dimensions would actually lower alpha, which is highest when items measure one construct. It tempts because Cronbach's 1951 paper mentions the 'internal structure of tests', which sounds like dimensionality.","b":"This is the exact opposite of the purpose. Averaging many estimates reduces the random variation attached to any single estimate, the same logic as averaging several measurements of a length, so accuracy goes up, not down. No psychometrician would set out to make an estimate less accurate. It tempts because it mirrors the correct option with one word reversed, catching candidates who read quickly or misremember the direction.","c":"Averaging splits leaves the test untouched; every item is used in every split. Reducing items is a separate decision made during item analysis, when weak items with low item-total correlations are dropped, and shortening a test usually lowers reliability, as the Spearman-Brown formula shows. It tempts because item analysis and reliability are taught together, and candidates confuse refining a test with estimating its reliability."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"The bystander effect is the finding that a person is less likely to help in an emergency when other people are present. It was studied by John Darley and Bibb Latane after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, reported at the time as witnessed by 38 neighbours, a figure later shown to be exaggerated. In their decision model (Latane and Darley, 1970), a bystander must notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, accept personal responsibility, decide how to help, and then act. Others can block help at different steps through distinct processes. Pluralistic ignorance works at the interpretation step. When a situation is ambiguous, each person looks to others for cues; everyone is trying to look calm, so each concludes from the others' calm that nothing is wrong. In the smoke-filled room study (Latane and Darley, 1968), about 75 percent of people alone reported the smoke, but only 10 percent did so when sitting with two passive confederates. The term itself comes from Daniel Katz and Floyd Allport (1931). Diffusion of responsibility works at the responsibility step, when the emergency is clear but each person feels less personally obliged; in the seizure study (Darley and Latane, 1968), 85 percent of lone participants helped before the seizure ended, against 31 percent who believed four others could hear. A third process, audience inhibition, is fear of looking foolish in front of others. Option b is right because the stem describes not helping because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening, which is pluralistic ignorance.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect, studied by John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968), is the general finding that the presence of others reduces helping. It is the outcome, an umbrella term, while pluralistic ignorance and diffusion of responsibility are the processes that explain it. The stem asks for the specific mechanism, uncertainty about what is happening. It tempts because it is the most familiar term and is not exactly wrong, only less precise.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the process in which each bystander feels less personal obligation because responsibility is shared among everyone present. It operates after the event is recognised as an emergency, as in the 1968 seizure study of Darley and Latane, where the need was clear. The stem stresses that no one knows what is happening, a problem of interpretation, not of responsibility. It tempts because it is the best known explanation of bystander apathy.","d":"Amotivation is a term from self-determination theory (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, 1985), meaning a complete lack of motivation, where a person sees no reason or value in acting. 'Amotivation to help' is not a recognised bystander process and says nothing about uncertainty or the presence of others. It tempts because 'not helping' seems to imply low motivation, and the technical-sounding word suggests an established concept."}},{"q":"Which of the following are powerful sources of provocation","concept":"Provocation means words or actions by another person that we perceive as deliberate attacks on us, and it is one of the strongest social triggers of human aggression. Social psychology textbooks, notably Robert A. Baron and Donn Byrne's Social Psychology, describe direct provocation as producing retaliation: people usually answer aggression with matching or slightly stronger aggression. Two forms are singled out as especially powerful. Condescension is an expression of arrogance or disdain, being talked down to or treated as inferior. Harsh, unjustified criticism, especially criticism that attacks the person rather than the behaviour, is the second. Teasing and sneering also provoke, but the pair the textbook labels powerful is condescension plus unjustified criticism. Whether provocation leads to aggression depends on attribution: if we judge the harm intentional we retaliate, while a credible excuse or apology reduces anger. Kenneth Dodge (1980) showed that aggressive children carry a hostile attribution bias, reading ambiguous acts as hostile. Examiners build variants from neighbouring causes of aggression. The frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939) said frustration always leads to aggression; Leonard Berkowitz (1989) revised it, arguing that frustration produces aggression mainly when it creates negative affect. Dolf Zillmann's excitation transfer theory (1971) explains how leftover arousal, for example from exercise, intensifies anger at a later provocation. The General Aggression Model of Craig Anderson and Brad Bushman (2002) combines person factors, situational factors such as provocation, and appraisal. Option c is right because condescension and harsh, unjustified criticism are the two provocations the social psychology literature names as especially powerful triggers of aggression.","traps":{"a":"Teasing means remarks that call attention to a person's flaws or mistakes, and research does show it can provoke aggression, especially when it is seen as hostile rather than playful. However, this pair drops unjustified criticism, which the textbook names alongside condescension as the especially powerful provocations. It tempts because condescension is correct and teasing is a real, familiar provocation.","b":"Harsh, unjustified criticism is one of the two powerful provocations, so half of this pair is right. The pair leaves out condescension, the expression of arrogance or disdain that the textbook lists first. Teasing does provoke, but it is not one of the two forms highlighted as especially powerful. It tempts because it contains one correct element and one plausible element.","d":"'None of the above' would be correct only if no listed pair matched the textbook. Choice c gives exactly the pair named as powerful sources of provocation, so this choice fails. It tempts candidates who know that teasing also provokes aggression, feel unable to choose between three pairs that each contain a real provocation, and retreat to the escape option."}},{"q":"splintering of a group into distinct factions is called","concept":"A schism is the splintering of a group into distinct factions, a term used in group dynamics textbooks. The word comes from the Greek for 'split' and was first used for religious divisions, such as the Great Schism of 1054 between the Eastern and Western churches. In a schism a group does not just argue: it divides into rival subgroups, each with its own identity, and these often end up as separate groups. Fabio Sani and Stephen Reicher (1998), studying the split of the Italian Communist Party, showed that schism follows when members believe a change has subverted the group's core identity; Sani's later work added that schism is more likely when dissenters feel they have no voice. Schism is the end point of intragroup conflict. Neighbouring terms an examiner may substitute: a faction or coalition is a subgroup inside a group that has not broken away; group polarization, shown by Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni (1969), is the shift of a whole group's opinion toward a more extreme position after discussion; groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), is the opposite problem, excessive cohesion that suppresses dissent; and deviance means one member departing from group norms. Bruce Tuckman (1965) placed conflict in the storming stage of group development, which a group may or may not survive. Intergroup conflict, as in Muzafer Sherif's Robbers Cave study (1954), is conflict between two groups rather than division inside one. Option a is right because schism is the technical name for a group splintering into distinct factions.","traps":{"b":"A chapter is a local branch of a larger organisation, such as a city chapter of a professional association. Chapters are created by the parent body and remain loyal parts of it; they involve no conflict and no breakaway. It tempts because a chapter is a smaller unit carved out of a larger whole, which superficially resembles a group being split into parts.","c":"'Division' is an everyday word for any part or section, or for the act of dividing, as in a company's sales division. It has no technical meaning in group dynamics and does not imply factions, rival identities or conflict. It tempts because a schism is literally a kind of division, so the general word looks like a safe synonym, but the stem asks for the specific term.","d":"Deviation, or deviance, is one member's departure from the norms of a group. The group responds by pressuring, persuading and finally rejecting the deviate, as Stanley Schachter (1951) showed in his studies of opinion deviates. It concerns an individual, not the splintering of the whole group into factions. It tempts because deviance is a familiar group dynamics term that is linked with conflict."}},{"q":"Fiedler's contingency theory does NOT include which of the following as dimensions","concept":"Fred Fiedler's contingency model of leadership (first proposed in 1964 and set out in his 1967 book A Theory of Leadership Effectiveness) was the first major theory to say that no single leadership style is best: effectiveness depends on the match between the leader's style and the situation. Style is measured by the Least Preferred Co-worker (LPC) scale. A leader who describes their least preferred co-worker harshly (low LPC) is task-oriented; one who still describes that person favourably (high LPC) is relationship-oriented. Fiedler treated style as a fairly fixed trait, so he advised changing the situation rather than the leader. Situational favourableness, or situational control, has exactly three dimensions, in order of importance: leader-member relations (trust and loyalty between leader and group), task structure (how clearly goals and procedures are defined), and position power (the formal authority to reward, punish, hire and fire). Combining these gives eight octants. Task-oriented leaders do best when the situation is very favourable or very unfavourable; relationship-oriented leaders do best in moderately favourable situations. Variant questions use neighbouring models. The path-goal theory of Robert House (1971) does make follower motivation central, since the leader clarifies paths to rewards. The situational leadership model of Paul Hersey and Kenneth Blanchard (1969) matches style to follower readiness or maturity. The model of Victor Vroom and Philip Yetton (1973) concerns how far subordinates should share in decisions. Option a is right because employee motivation is not one of Fiedler's three situational dimensions, while the other three options name them.","traps":{"b":"Leader-member relations is the first and most important of Fiedler's three dimensions. It refers to the degree of trust, respect and loyalty the group gives the leader, rated simply as good or poor. Because it is a genuine dimension of the model, it cannot be the answer to a NOT question. It tempts candidates who skim the stem and miss the word NOT.","c":"Task structure is Fiedler's second dimension: how clearly the goals, procedures and success criteria of the task are spelled out. A highly structured task, like assembling a standard product, gives the leader more control than a vague, creative one. It is part of the model, so it does not answer this NOT question. It tempts because 'structure of the task' is worded differently from the textbook term.","d":"Position power is Fiedler's third dimension: the formal authority that comes with the leader's role, including the power to reward, punish, hire and fire. The wording 'intrinsic power and authority' is an unusual paraphrase, since this power belongs to the position rather than the person. It is still a genuine dimension, so it is not the exception. It tempts because the odd wording sounds unfamiliar."}},{"q":"Identify the correct order of cellular layers to the retinal receptors","concept":"The vertebrate retina is built 'inside out'. The photoreceptors, rods and cones, lie at the very back of the eye against the pigment epithelium, so light entering through the pupil must first pass through several layers of neurons before it reaches them. Travelling from the vitreous humour toward the receptors, light meets the retinal ganglion cells, then the amacrine cells, then the bipolar cells, then the horizontal cells, and finally the rods and cones. Neural signals flow the opposite way: receptors pass the signal to bipolar cells, which pass it to ganglion cells, whose axons gather at the optic disc and leave the eye as the optic nerve. Because the axons exit there, the optic disc has no receptors and forms the blind spot, which the brain fills in by completion. Horizontal and amacrine cells are the lateral neurons: horizontal cells link receptors and bipolar cells sideways, and amacrine cells link bipolar and ganglion cells sideways, which supports lateral inhibition and contrast enhancement. At the fovea the inner layers are thinned out so that light reaches the densely packed cones with little distortion, which is why acuity is highest there. Stephen Kuffler (1953) recorded the centre-surround receptive fields of ganglion cells that this circuitry produces. The duplex theory adds that rods (about 120 million) serve dim, scotopic vision and cones (about 6 million) serve colour and detail in photopic vision. Option d is right because ganglion, amacrine, bipolar and horizontal cells is the order of the layers that light crosses on its way to the receptors.","traps":{"a":"This sequence starts with horizontal cells, which actually lie deepest among the retinal neurons, right next to the receptors. It then places ganglion cells second and bipolar cells last, mixing inner and outer layers. Horizontal cells link receptors to bipolar cells sideways, so they cannot be the first layer light meets. It tempts because horizontal cells are often described first when textbooks explain receptor-level circuitry.","b":"This sequence begins with bipolar cells and puts amacrine cells last, so it does not follow the physical layering at all. Bipolar cells sit in the middle of the retina, between the receptor-level horizontal cells and the ganglion-level amacrine cells. It tempts because bipolar cells are the direct link in the main receptor, bipolar, ganglion pathway, so candidates thinking of signal flow put them first.","c":"This sequence starts with amacrine cells and ends with ganglion cells, close to the reverse of the true arrangement. Ganglion cells form the innermost layer, the first that light meets, and their axons run across the retinal surface to the optic disc. Placing them next to the receptors is wrong. It tempts candidates who picture the retina the 'right way round', with receptors facing the light."}},{"q":"Tendency of acoustic stimuli of different frequencies (of sound) to cause different parts of the basilar membrane to flex back and forth is","concept":"Place coding, the basis of place theory, holds that the pitch of a sound is signalled by where along the basilar membrane the vibration peaks. The idea goes back to Hermann von Helmholtz (1863), who pictured the membrane as a set of tuned resonators. Georg von Békésy, who received the Nobel Prize in 1961, showed that sound sets up a travelling wave along the basilar membrane inside the cochlea. The membrane is narrow and stiff at the base, near the oval window, and wide and flexible at the apex. High frequencies produce maximum flexing near the base; low frequencies peak toward the apex. Hair cells of the organ of Corti at the point of greatest flexing fire most, so frequency is mapped onto place. This tonotopic layout is kept all the way to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. The rival frequency theory (temporal coding), proposed by William Rutherford (1886), says the auditory nerve fires in step with the sound wave, so the timing of firing signals pitch. Single neurons cannot fire much faster than about 1000 times per second, so Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray (1930) proposed the volley principle: groups of neurons fire in staggered turns. The modern view combines both: temporal coding handles low frequencies, place coding handles high frequencies, and both operate in the middle range. Loudness is coded mainly by the rate and number of firing neurons. Option b is right because place coding is exactly the tendency of different frequencies to flex different parts of the basilar membrane.","traps":{"a":"Rate coding means that stimulus intensity is signalled by how fast neurons fire: a stronger stimulus produces a higher firing rate. In hearing, louder sounds increase firing rate and recruit more neurons. It describes a property of neural firing, not the location on the basilar membrane that vibrates most. It tempts because rate coding also appears in hearing chapters and sounds like a general answer to any coding question.","c":"Frequency coding, or temporal coding, is the claim of frequency theory, first put forward by William Rutherford (1886): auditory nerve fibres fire in time with the sound wave, so firing frequency signals pitch. It was extended by the volley principle of Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray (1930). It concerns timing, not place on the membrane. It tempts because the stem mentions different frequencies of sound.","d":"'Pitch coding' is not a standard technical term. Pitch is the perceived quality that corresponds mainly to frequency, and both place coding and temporal coding are theories of how pitch is coded. Choosing this option names the problem, not the mechanism the stem describes. It tempts because sounds of different frequencies are heard as different pitches, so the label feels intuitively right."}},{"q":"__________ layer is sensitive to color and ________ layer is sensitive to movement","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus, the main relay between retina and primary visual cortex, has six layers, and each layer receives input from only one eye. The bottom two layers are magnocellular (large cell bodies) and the top four are parvocellular (small cell bodies). Thin koniocellular (very small cell) layers lie between them. These layers begin parallel channels that stay partly separate up to the cortex. The parvocellular channel takes its input mainly from cones, has small receptive fields, and is sensitive to colour, fine pattern detail and stationary or slowly moving objects. The magnocellular channel takes much of its input from rods and is sensitive to movement, flicker and small differences in brightness, but is essentially colour blind. The koniocellular channel carries signals including those from short-wavelength (blue) cones; its full role is less well understood. Margaret Livingstone and David Hubel (1988) proposed that these channels segregate the processing of form, colour, movement and depth. Their outputs feed two cortical streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): the dorsal stream to parietal cortex, the 'where' pathway dominated by magnocellular input, and the ventral stream to inferotemporal cortex, the 'what' pathway with heavy parvocellular input. Melvyn Goodale and David Milner (1992) later recast them as vision for action versus vision for perception. A memory hook: parvo for particulars such as colour and detail, magno for motion. Option d is right because the parvocellular layers are sensitive to colour and the magnocellular layers are sensitive to movement.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the true pairing. Magnocellular neurons have large cell bodies and large receptive fields and are sensitive to movement but essentially colour blind; parvocellular neurons are sensitive to colour and fine detail. So magnocellular cannot fill the colour blank. It tempts because both correct terms are present, and a candidate who has memorised the two names but not their functions has a fifty-fifty chance.","b":"Parvocellular for colour is correct, but koniocellular is wrong for movement. Koniocellular cells form thin layers between the main LGN layers and carry signals including those from short-wavelength (blue) cones, so if anything they relate to colour, not motion. Movement belongs to the magnocellular layers, which this option leaves out. It tempts because the first half is right and koniocellular is an unfamiliar term.","c":"Both halves fail. Magnocellular is the motion channel, not the colour channel, and the koniocellular layers do not specialise in movement; they carry signals including those from blue cones. The option puts a real term in the wrong blank and adds a distractor in the other. It tempts because magnocellular is genuinely linked to one of the two functions in the stem, so it looks familiar."}},{"q":"Correct order to the auditory receptor is","concept":"Sound is a wave of air pressure, and the ear converts it into neural signals through a fixed sequence. The pinna (outer ear) collects sound and funnels it down the external auditory canal to the tympanic membrane (eardrum), which vibrates. In the middle ear three tiny bones, the ossicles, carry the vibration: the malleus (hammer) is attached to the eardrum, the incus (anvil) sits in the middle, and the stapes (stirrup) presses on the oval window, a membrane opening into the cochlea of the inner ear. Because the eardrum is many times larger than the oval window and the ossicles act as levers, pressure is amplified; this impedance matching lets airborne sound move the cochlear fluid. Vibration of the oval window sets up a travelling wave in that fluid, the round window bulges to release pressure, and the basilar membrane flexes. On the basilar membrane sits the organ of Corti, the auditory receptor organ: its hair cells are bent against the tectorial membrane and transduce motion into signals carried by the auditory nerve (cranial nerve VIII). The central pathway then runs through the cochlear nuclei, superior olives, inferior colliculi and medial geniculate nuclei to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. Damage before the cochlea causes conductive hearing loss; damage to hair cells or the auditory nerve causes sensorineural loss. Georg von Békésy (Nobel Prize 1961) described the travelling wave. Option a is right because it gives the true outside-to-inside order: pinna, tympanic membrane, ossicles, oval window, cochlea, organ of Corti.","traps":{"b":"This sequence puts the cochlea second, straight after the pinna, and the ossicles after the oval window. The cochlea is the snail-shaped inner ear structure deep in the temporal bone; sound can reach it only after the eardrum and the middle ear bones have passed on the vibration. It tempts because the cochlea is the best-known hearing structure, so candidates who think of it as central may place it too early.","c":"This sequence places the oval window before the tympanic membrane and the cochlea before the ossicles. The oval window is the membrane between the middle and inner ear, pushed by the stapes, so it must come after both the eardrum and the ossicles. It tempts because all six structures are real and the pinna and organ of Corti are correctly placed at the ends, so only careful ordering of the middle exposes the error.","d":"This is the closest distractor: it only swaps the ossicles and the oval window. The ossicles (malleus, incus and stapes) must come before the oval window, because the footplate of the stapes is what pushes on the oval window. Only then does the motion pass into the cochlear fluid. It tempts because candidates often remember the oval window as part of the middle ear chain and misplace it."}},{"q":"The Anterolateral system system in somatosensory pathway carries ___________ information","concept":"Somatosensation, the body senses, reaches the brain through two main ascending systems. The dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system carries fine discriminative touch, vibration and proprioception (the sense of limb position). Its large, fast fibres enter the spinal cord and ascend on the same side in the dorsal columns, synapse in the dorsal column nuclei of the medulla, cross the midline, and travel in the medial lemniscus to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus and on to primary somatosensory cortex. The anterolateral system carries nociceptive information (pain) and temperature, plus some crude touch. Its small fibres, the faster A-delta fibres for sharp pain and the slow C fibres for dull aching pain, synapse soon after entering the cord; the second neurons cross over at once and ascend in the anterolateral part of the cord. It has three tracts: the spinothalamic tract to the thalamus, the spinoreticular tract to the reticular formation, and the spinotectal tract to the tectum. Because the two systems cross at different levels, damage to one half of the spinal cord (Brown-Séquard syndrome) causes loss of touch and proprioception on the same side but loss of pain and temperature on the opposite side, a classic exam item. Pain is also modulated on its way up: the gate control theory of Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall (1965) proposed that touch input and descending signals from the brain can close a spinal gate on pain. Option d is right because the anterolateral system is the pathway for nociceptive (pain) and temperature information.","traps":{"a":"Proprioception is the sense of the position and movement of the limbs, coming from receptors in muscles, tendons and joints. It travels in the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system, on large myelinated fibres that ascend on the same side and cross in the medulla. It is not an anterolateral modality. It tempts because the two systems are taught together in the same somatosensory chapter, so candidates often swap their contents.","b":"Vibration is detected mainly by Pacinian corpuscles, fast-adapting receptors deep in the skin, and travels in the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system along with fine touch and proprioception. Clinically it is tested with a tuning fork to check dorsal-column function. It is not carried by the anterolateral system. It tempts because vibration feels like a basic, crude sensation that a candidate might wrongly group with pain and temperature.","c":"Touch, especially fine discriminative touch such as two-point discrimination, is carried by the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system. The anterolateral system carries only some crude, poorly localised touch, a minor part of its function. It tempts because that minor role is real, so a candidate who remembers crude touch in the spinothalamic tract may pick this, but the defining content of the anterolateral system is pain and temperature."}},{"q":"The part of the nervous system responsible for conservation of energy resources is ___________ and that responsible of allocation of resources for expenditure is _________","concept":"The peripheral nervous system has two parts. The somatic nervous system interacts with the external environment: its afferent nerves carry signals from the skin, skeletal muscles, joints, eyes and ears, and its efferent nerves move skeletal muscles under voluntary control. The autonomic nervous system regulates the internal environment, serving internal organs, smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands. Its efferent nerves form two divisions with largely opposite effects. Sympathetic nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord and stimulate, organise and mobilise energy resources in threatening situations, the 'fight or flight' response named by Walter Cannon (1915): heart rate and blood pressure rise, pupils dilate, glucose is released and digestion slows. Parasympathetic nerves leave the brain (for example the vagus nerve) and the sacral cord and act to conserve energy, the 'rest and digest' pattern: heart rate slows and digestion and salivation increase. Most organs receive both inputs, so autonomic control works by balance between opposing divisions. A structural detail examiners like: sympathetic preganglionic neurons are short and postganglionic neurons long, mostly releasing norepinephrine at target organs, while parasympathetic preganglionic neurons are long and postganglionic neurons short, releasing acetylcholine. Hans Selye (1936) later described the general adaptation syndrome, in which sustained sympathetic and hormonal arousal under chronic stress wears the body down. Option a is right because the parasympathetic system conserves energy resources and the sympathetic system allocates and mobilises them for expenditure, in that order.","traps":{"b":"This choice reverses the pairing. The sympathetic system does not conserve energy; it spends it, mobilising glucose, raising heart rate and diverting blood to the muscles for fight or flight. The parasympathetic system does the conserving, slowing the heart and promoting digestion and storage. It tempts because both correct terms appear and the two blanks invite an order mistake, especially under time pressure.","c":"The somatic nervous system carries sensory signals from the skin, muscles and sense organs and controls voluntary skeletal muscle; it is not the energy-conserving system. The autonomic system regulates internal organs, but as a whole it both conserves and spends energy through its two divisions. So neither term fits its blank. It tempts because somatic versus autonomic is the first split of the peripheral nervous system.","d":"This reverses the previous pair and is still wrong. The autonomic nervous system is the umbrella that contains both the conserving parasympathetic and the spending sympathetic divisions, so it cannot be one side of the contrast. The somatic system controls voluntary skeletal muscle and has no special role in energy expenditure. It tempts because autonomic is correctly linked with internal energy regulation in general."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter undergoes recycling by enzymatic degradation exclusively","concept":"After a neurotransmitter is released and binds to receptors, its action must be ended quickly, or the next signal would be blurred. There are two deactivation mechanisms. Reuptake is the most common: transporter proteins in the presynaptic membrane, and sometimes in glial cells, draw the transmitter back out of the synaptic cleft so it can be repackaged and reused. Enzymatic degradation is the second: an enzyme breaks the transmitter down in the cleft. Acetylcholine is the classic transmitter deactivated this way. The enzyme acetylcholinesterase, present in the cleft, splits acetylcholine into choline and acetate within milliseconds; the choline is then taken back into the presynaptic terminal and recycled to make new acetylcholine. That is why textbooks describe acetylcholine as recycled by way of enzymatic degradation. The monoamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin) and the amino acids (glutamate, GABA) are deactivated mainly by reuptake; enzymes such as monoamine oxidase (MAO) and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) also break down monoamines, but reuptake is their main route of clearance. Acetylcholine was the first neurotransmitter identified, through Otto Loewi's (1921) frog heart experiment, in which fluid from a vagus-stimulated heart slowed a second heart. Clinical links follow: acetylcholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil are used in Alzheimer's disease and related drugs treat myasthenia gravis, while organophosphate nerve gases and pesticides block the enzyme irreversibly. Reuptake blockers such as SSRIs and cocaine act on the other mechanism. Option c is right because acetylcholine is the transmitter cleared by enzymatic degradation, through acetylcholinesterase, rather than by reuptake.","traps":{"a":"Dopamine is a catecholamine whose synaptic action is ended mainly by reuptake through the dopamine transporter (DAT). Cocaine and amphetamine produce their effects by blocking or reversing this transporter. Dopamine is also broken down by MAO and COMT, but that degradation is not the main way its signal ends. It tempts because candidates know MAO inhibitors exist and assume enzymes are the main route.","b":"GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter of the brain, is cleared from the synapse by GABA transporters into neurons and nearby astrocytes. Only inside cells is it broken down by GABA transaminase, and the antiepileptic drug tiagabine works by blocking GABA reuptake. So its action ends through reuptake, not enzymes in the cleft. It tempts because GABA is partly recycled through glial cells, which sounds like the stem.","d":"Glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter, is cleared mainly by excitatory amino acid transporters, largely into astrocytes. There it is converted to glutamine, which returns to neurons and is turned back into glutamate (the glutamate-glutamine cycle). Enzymes are involved, but inside cells after reuptake, not in the cleft. It tempts because the word 'recycling' in the stem fits this well-known glial cycle."}},{"q":"Which of the following are not catecholamines?","concept":"Catecholamines are a family of monoamine neurotransmitters that share a catechol nucleus (a benzene ring carrying two hydroxyl groups) and are made from the amino acid tyrosine in a single chain. Tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, converts tyrosine to L-DOPA; DOPA decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine; dopamine beta-hydroxylase converts dopamine to norepinephrine (noradrenaline); and in the adrenal medulla and a few neurons, norepinephrine is converted to epinephrine (adrenaline). So the three catecholamines are dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. The monoamines are a larger class that also includes the indolamines, chiefly serotonin, made from tryptophan. Small-molecule transmitters are usually grouped into four classes: amino acids (glutamate, aspartate, glycine, GABA), monoamines, acetylcholine, and unconventional transmitters such as nitric oxide and the endocannabinoids. Neuropeptides form a separate class of large-molecule transmitters. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is an amino acid transmitter, made from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase; it is the main inhibitory transmitter of the brain and has no catechol ring. The synthesis chain explains a clinical fact: L-DOPA is given in Parkinson's disease because, unlike dopamine, it crosses the blood-brain barrier and is then converted to dopamine. This treatment grew from the work of Arvid Carlsson (1958), who showed that dopamine is a transmitter in its own right and later shared the 2000 Nobel Prize. Monoamine oxidase and COMT break catecholamines down. Option a is right because GABA is an amino acid neurotransmitter, not a catecholamine, while the other three are the catecholamine family.","traps":{"b":"Dopamine is the first catecholamine in the synthesis chain, made from L-DOPA, which is made from tyrosine. It acts in the nigrostriatal pathway, whose degeneration causes Parkinson's disease, and in the mesolimbic reward pathway. Since it is a catecholamine, it is not the answer to a 'not' question. It tempts candidates who misread the stem as asking which ones are catecholamines.","c":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a catecholamine made from dopamine by dopamine beta-hydroxylase. It is the transmitter of the locus coeruleus in the brainstem and of most sympathetic postganglionic neurons, and is linked to arousal and vigilance. Because it belongs to the catecholamine family, it does not answer the stem. It tempts candidates who file it only under the sympathetic nervous system and forget its chemical class.","d":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the last catecholamine in the chain, made from norepinephrine mainly in the adrenal medulla. It acts chiefly as a hormone in the stress response, and only a few brain neurons use it as a transmitter. It is still chemically a catecholamine. It tempts because its role as a circulating hormone makes some candidates think it cannot belong to a neurotransmitter family."}},{"q":"According to existentialists, what is the basic unity of person and environment expressed in?","concept":"Existential psychology applies the philosophy of Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) to the study of persons. Heidegger's key term is Dasein, literally 'being there', which he described as being-in-the-world. The hyphens matter: person and world are not two separate things that later interact; they form one indivisible unity, and each exists only through the other. This rejects the subject-object split of Cartesian thinking and of much scientific psychology. The Swiss psychiatrist Ludwig Binswanger (1942) built his Daseinsanalysis on this idea, and Rollo May, with Ernest Angel and Henri Ellenberger, brought it to English-speaking psychologists in the book Existence (1958). Binswanger distinguished three simultaneous modes of being-in-the-world. Umwelt is the 'around world' of biological needs, drives and the physical environment. Mitwelt is the 'with world' of relationships with other people. Eigenwelt is the 'own world' of self-awareness and the relationship to oneself. Some later writers add a fourth, Uberwelt, the spiritual world of ideals and beliefs. Examiners often ask which mode matches a description, so learn the three by their prefixes: Um for around, Mit for with, Eigen for own. Related existential themes include thrownness (being born into circumstances one did not choose), authenticity, freedom and responsibility, and the four ultimate concerns of Irvin Yalom (1980): death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Option b is right because Dasein, being-in-the-world, is the term that expresses the basic unity of person and environment, while the other three are only modes within it.","traps":{"a":"Umwelt, literally the 'around world', is the biological and physical environment: bodily needs, drives, instincts and natural surroundings. It is one of Ludwig Binswanger's three modes of being-in-the-world, the mode that behaviourism and biology study best. It is only one aspect of existence, not the basic unity of person and world. It tempts because 'environment' in the stem sounds like a direct translation of Umwelt.","c":"Mitwelt, the 'with world', is the mode of being-in-the-world made up of relationships with other people: love, community, social roles and shared meaning. It is one of Ludwig Binswanger's three modes, not the overall unity of person and world. It tempts because existentialists stress that a person always exists in relation to others, so the social world can seem to capture that unity.","d":"Eigenwelt, the 'own world', is the mode of being-in-the-world made up of the relationship to oneself: self-awareness, self-reflection and the personal meaning things have. Rollo May argued it was the mode most neglected by modern psychology. It is still only one of three modes, not the overall unity. It tempts because it sounds the most personal and existential of the three German terms."}},{"q":"What is the third level of moral reasoning according to Kohlberg?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (doctoral dissertation, 1958) extended the work of Jean Piaget (1932) on moral judgement by presenting boys, and later adults, with moral dilemmas. The best known is the Heinz dilemma, in which a man considers stealing an overpriced drug to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasoning behind an answer, not the answer itself, and described three levels, each with two stages. Level I, preconventional morality: stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation; stage 2, individualism and exchange, where right is what serves one's own interests. Level II, conventional morality: stage 3, good interpersonal relationships, the 'good boy, nice girl' orientation; stage 4, maintaining social order, the 'law and order' orientation. Level III, postconventional morality: stage 5, social contract and individual rights, where laws are seen as agreements that can be changed; stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity. Kohlberg sometimes described Level III as the postconventional, autonomous or principled level. The stages are claimed to be invariant in sequence and universal, and only a minority of adults reach Level III; stage 6 was later treated as a theoretical ideal. Piaget's earlier two-stage scheme, heteronomous morality (moral realism) followed by autonomous morality, is a common source of distractors. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised Kohlberg's justice orientation as male-biased and proposed an ethic of care. Option d is right because postconventional morality is the standard name of the third and highest level in Kohlberg's theory.","traps":{"a":"Preconventional morality is Kohlberg's first level, typical of most children up to about nine. At stage 1 actions are judged by whether they are punished; at stage 2 by whether they serve one's own interests through fair exchange. Morality is external, based on consequences imposed by authority. It is the first level, not the third. It tempts candidates who count the levels in the wrong direction.","b":"Conventional morality is Kohlberg's second level, typical of adolescents and many adults. At stage 3 people seek approval and want to be seen as good; at stage 4 they uphold laws and social order because society needs them. The rules of the group are accepted as right. It is the middle level, not the third. It tempts because it is the level most adults actually reach.","c":"'Autonomous moral principles' is not the standard name of a Kohlberg level. Kohlberg did describe Level III as autonomous or principled, but its accepted name is postconventional. The word 'autonomous' also recalls the autonomous morality of Jean Piaget (1932), the second of his two stages, where rules are seen as changeable agreements. It tempts because it describes the third level's content and borrows Piaget's terminology."}},{"q":"Which style of contact in gestalt therapy is characterized by a high need for acceptance and liking, resulting in enmeshment?","concept":"Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (key text Gestalt Therapy, 1951, co-written with Ralph Hefferline), sees healthy functioning as good contact at the boundary between self and environment. Contact means meeting the world with full awareness while keeping a sense of separateness. When contact is avoided or distorted, Gestalt therapists speak of resistances, interruptions or styles of contact, which Erving and Miriam Polster (1973) set out systematically. Introjection is uncritically swallowing beliefs and standards from others, such as parental 'shoulds', without making them one's own. Projection is disowning aspects of oneself and attributing them to other people or the environment. Retroflection is turning back onto oneself what one would like to do to others, or doing for oneself what one wants from others, as in self-blame or psychosomatic tension. Deflection is avoiding direct contact through humour, vagueness or changing the subject. Confluence is the blurring of the boundary between self and others, so the person no longer experiences where they end and others begin. It is driven by a high need for acceptance and to be liked, so the person avoids conflict, hides different feelings and agrees in order to keep the relationship safe. The result is enmeshment, and the therapist counters it with questions such as 'What do you want right now?' Other examined Gestalt ideas are here-and-now awareness, unfinished business and the empty chair. Option b is right because confluence is the contact style marked by a high need for acceptance and liking that dissolves boundaries and produces enmeshment.","traps":{"a":"Projection, in Gestalt therapy, is disowning parts of oneself, such as anger or attraction, and seeing them in other people or the environment, so the person avoids responsibility for those feelings. It keeps self and other apart rather than merging them. It tempts because projection is a familiar psychoanalytic defence mechanism, so it is often chosen whenever a question mentions a disturbance in relationships.","c":"Introjection is passively swallowing the values, rules and beliefs of others, typically parents, without examining or digesting them, so they remain foreign 'shoulds' inside the person. It concerns what is taken in, not the dissolving of the boundary in close relationships. It tempts because introjection also involves adopting other people's views, which can look like the agreeable, conforming behaviour of confluence.","d":"Retroflection is turning back onto oneself what one would like to do to another person, for example directing anger inward as self-criticism, or doing for oneself what one wants from others. Its signs include guilt, self-harm and physical tension. It turns energy inward rather than merging with others. It tempts because it also arises from avoiding conflict with people, which a need for acceptance can motivate."}},{"q":"Which step in systematic desensitization involves gradually exposing clients to anxiety-arousing situations?","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). It rests on reciprocal inhibition: a response incompatible with anxiety, usually deep muscle relaxation, is paired with anxiety cues so that the anxiety is gradually weakened. This is a form of counterconditioning, foreshadowed by Mary Cover Jones (1924), who reduced a boy's fear of a rabbit by pairing it with eating. The procedure has three steps. First, relaxation training, usually a shortened form of the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938), taught over several sessions. Second, construction of the anxiety hierarchy: client and therapist list roughly ten to twenty feared situations and rank them with the Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale from 0 to 100. Third, systematic desensitization proper: while deeply relaxed, the client imagines the lowest item; if anxiety appears they signal, stop and relax again, and only when an item can be imagined calmly do they move up the hierarchy. Exposure can also be in vivo (in real life). Variant questions contrast this graded method with flooding, prolonged exposure to the most feared situation at once without escape, and with the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), which floods in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Exposure and response prevention for OCD and the participant modelling of Albert Bandura (1969) are related techniques. Option c is right because systematic desensitization proper is the step in which the relaxed client is gradually exposed to the anxiety-arousing situations of the hierarchy.","traps":{"a":"Relaxation training is the first step of systematic desensitization. The client learns to relax deeply, usually by tensing and releasing muscle groups in the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938), and practises at home. It supplies the response that will compete with anxiety, but no feared material is presented yet. It tempts because relaxation runs through the whole procedure, so it can seem like the active step.","b":"Developing the anxiety hierarchy is the second step. Client and therapist list feared situations and rank them from least to most disturbing, often with Subjective Units of Disturbance ratings from 0 to 100. This is planning, not exposure: nothing is yet imagined while relaxed. It tempts because the hierarchy is a graded list of anxiety-arousing situations, which closely echoes the wording of the stem.","d":"Cognitive restructuring is a technique from cognitive therapy, associated with Aaron T. Beck (1976) and the rational emotive approach of Albert Ellis (1962), in which the client identifies and challenges irrational or distorted thoughts. It is not a step in Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitization, which is a purely behavioural counterconditioning method. It tempts because modern cognitive behavioural treatment of anxiety often combines both."}},{"q":"Which stage is characterized by the development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships?","concept":"Sigmund Freud (Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, 1905) proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each defined by the body zone through which the libido, the sexual life energy, seeks gratification. Oral stage (birth to about 18 months): pleasure from sucking, biting and swallowing; fixation is linked to dependency, overeating or smoking. Anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years): pleasure from retaining or expelling faeces, with toilet training as the key conflict; fixation produces anal retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal expulsive (messy) traits. Phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years): the genitals become the focus, the Oedipus complex arises, and the child resolves it by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. Latency period (about 6 years to puberty): sexual impulses are repressed or sublimated into school, sport and same-sex friendships; Freud did not treat it as a true stage. Genital stage (puberty onward): sexual energy returns and is now directed toward peers outside the family, and the person develops a mature adult sex-role identity and the capacity for adult social, sexual and working relationships, summed up in the phrase attributed to Freud that the healthy adult can love and work. Fixation at an earlier stage weakens this outcome. Erik Erikson (1950) recast the stages as psychosocial crises; his adolescent crisis of identity versus role confusion and young adult crisis of intimacy versus isolation overlap with the genital stage. Option d is right because the genital stage is the stage marked by adult sex-role identity and adult social relationships.","traps":{"a":"The oral stage runs from birth to about 18 months. The mouth is the erogenous zone, and the infant gains pleasure from sucking, feeding and later biting. The central issue is dependency on the caregiver, and fixation is linked to traits such as dependence, overeating, smoking or verbal aggression. It has nothing to do with adult sex-role identity. It tempts only as the first, most familiar stage.","b":"The anal stage runs from about 18 months to 3 years. The anus is the erogenous zone, and the key conflict is toilet training, the child's first experience of external rules imposed on a bodily pleasure. Fixation produces anal retentive traits (orderliness, stubbornness, stinginess) or anal expulsive traits (messiness). It concerns control, not adult relationships. It tempts because it is about learning social rules.","c":"The phallic stage runs from about 3 to 6 years. The genitals become the erogenous zone, and the child experiences the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, a term from Carl Jung, 1913). Resolving it by identifying with the same-sex parent gives an early gender identification and the superego. It tempts because of this early gender link, but mature sex-role identity and adult relationships come later."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of the phallic stage of psychosexual development?","concept":"The phallic stage is the third of the psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, 1905), lasting from about 3 to 6 years. The genitals become the main erogenous zone, and the child notices the anatomical difference between the sexes. Its central event is the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek king who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother: the child develops incestuous fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent and sees the same-sex parent as a rival. In boys, fear that the father will punish these wishes produces castration anxiety. In girls, Freud described penis envy and a turn toward the father; Carl Jung (1913) called the girl's version the Electra complex, a term Freud did not adopt. The complex is resolved by identification with the same-sex parent, a form of identification with the aggressor, which leads to internalising that parent's values, forming the superego, and to an early sense of gender. Unresolved conflict here was thought to underlie later neurosis and phallic character traits such as vanity or recklessness. Examiners build variants by attaching each stage to its focus: oral (feeding and dependency), anal (toilet training and control), phallic (Oedipus and Electra complexes), latency (repression and sublimation of sexual impulses into school and friendships), genital (mature sexuality and adult relationships). Karen Horney (1926) criticised penis envy as a male-centred idea and proposed womb envy. Option c is right because incestuous fantasies and the Oedipus/Electra complex are the primary focus of the phallic stage.","traps":{"a":"Toilet training and gratification from defecation describe the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, which comes before the phallic stage. The anus is the erogenous zone, and the child faces the first major demand from society to control a bodily pleasure. Fixation leads to anal retentive or anal expulsive traits. It tempts because it is the stage immediately before the phallic stage.","b":"Development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships describes the genital stage, from puberty onward, when sexual energy is directed toward peers outside the family and mature love and work relationships become possible. The phallic stage only lays down early gender identification. It tempts because identification with the same-sex parent in the phallic stage does begin gender identity, so candidates link sex-role identity to it.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct describes the latency period, from about 6 years to puberty, after the Oedipus complex is resolved. Sexual impulses are repressed or channelled into socially valued activities such as schoolwork, sport and same-sex friendships. Sublimation is the defence mechanism of redirecting impulses into acceptable outlets. It tempts because latency directly follows the phallic stage and involves the same sexual energy."}},{"q":"Who developed the basic behavioral procedure of systematic desensitization?","concept":"Joseph Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist, developed systematic desensitization and described it in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He first studied cats that developed 'experimental neuroses' after receiving shocks in a cage; their fear faded when they were fed in settings progressively more similar to the original cage. From this he formed the principle of reciprocal inhibition, a term borrowed from the reflex physiology of Charles Sherrington (1906): if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of anxiety cues, the bond between those cues and anxiety weakens. The procedure has three steps: relaxation training, building an anxiety hierarchy rated in Subjective Units of Disturbance (0 to 100), and graded exposure to the hierarchy items, usually in imagination, while relaxed. It became a founding technique of behaviour therapy, a label popularised by Hans Eysenck (1959). Earlier, Mary Cover Jones (1924) had reduced a boy's fear of a rabbit by gradual exposure paired with food, so she is often called the mother of behaviour therapy, and John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) had shown with Little Albert that fear can be conditioned. Neighbouring exposure methods with other founders are common distractors: flooding, the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), and the participant modelling of Albert Bandura (1969). Option a is right because Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitization as the basic behavioural procedure built on reciprocal inhibition.","traps":{"b":"Carl Rogers founded client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, set out in Client-Centered Therapy (1951). It is a humanistic, non-directive approach in which the therapist offers unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness, trusting the client's actualising tendency. It uses no hierarchies or conditioning. It tempts because Rogers is among the most famous psychotherapists of the same era, and candidates may link any well-known therapy to him.","c":"Albert Bandura developed social learning theory, with the Bobo doll studies (1961) on imitation of aggression and the concept of self-efficacy (1977). His participant modelling, in which the therapist models handling the feared object and then guides the client to do the same, treats phobias and resembles graded exposure. It tempts because Bandura is a learning theorist who also worked on phobia treatment.","d":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and explained phobias as products of unconscious conflict, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of horses he traced to the Oedipus complex. Treatment works through free association and interpretation, not conditioning. Behaviour therapists, Wolpe included, explicitly rejected this model. It tempts because Freud is the most famous name in the list and wrote at length on phobias."}},{"q":"Ionotropic receptors bind to","concept":"Receptors are protein molecules in the postsynaptic membrane that bind neurotransmitters, and they come in two broad types. Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels: the binding site and the channel are parts of one protein, so when the transmitter binds, the channel opens at once and ions flow, producing a fast postsynaptic potential lasting milliseconds. Examples are the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, the AMPA, kainate and NMDA glutamate receptors, the GABA-A receptor, the glycine receptor and the 5-HT3 serotonin receptor. Metabotropic receptors are G-protein-coupled: binding activates a G protein, which may open a nearby channel or trigger a second messenger such as cyclic AMP, producing slower, longer-lasting and more widespread effects, including changes in gene expression. Neurotransmitters themselves come in two sizes. Small-molecule transmitters (amino acids, monoamines and acetylcholine) are made in the terminal and released quickly. Large-molecule transmitters, the neuropeptides such as endorphins, substance P and oxytocin, are short chains of amino acids made in the cell body and packaged in large vesicles. Small-molecule transmitters can act on either ionotropic or metabotropic receptors, but neuropeptides act through metabotropic receptors. So, in the standard textbook account, only small-molecule transmitters bind ionotropic receptors. Autoreceptors on the presynaptic neuron, which monitor and reduce release, are typically metabotropic too. Many drugs act at ionotropic sites: benzodiazepines and alcohol enhance GABA-A receptors, while ketamine and phencyclidine block NMDA receptors. Option a is right because ionotropic receptors are bound by small-molecule transmitters only, while large neuropeptides work through metabotropic receptors.","traps":{"b":"Large-molecule transmitters, the neuropeptides such as endorphins, substance P, oxytocin and neuropeptide Y, act on metabotropic, G-protein-coupled receptors, not on ligand-gated ion channels. So ionotropic receptors do not bind both sizes. It tempts because small transmitters do bind both receptor types, and a candidate can easily turn that fact around into 'both sizes of transmitter bind ionotropic receptors'.","c":"This reverses the truth. Large transmitters (neuropeptides) work through metabotropic receptors, which act slowly through G proteins and second messengers. Fast ionotropic receptors such as the nicotinic, AMPA, NMDA and GABA-A receptors are bound by small-molecule transmitters. It tempts candidates who associate 'large' with powerful, fast effects, when in fact neuropeptide effects are slow, diffuse and long-lasting.","d":"'None of the above' would be correct only if every listed statement were false. Small-molecule transmitters only is the accepted textbook answer, so this escape option fails. It tempts candidates who know that rare exceptions exist, such as a peptide-gated channel found in snails, or who are unsure about receptor classes and fall back on the safe-sounding choice."}},{"q":"Varicosities are ________ synapses and they release  ___________ neurotransmitters","concept":"Synapses can be classified by how precisely release is targeted. At directed synapses the site of release and the site of reception are very close, separated only by a narrow synaptic cleft a few tens of nanometres wide, as at classic axodendritic and axosomatic synapses. Fast small-molecule transmitters such as glutamate, GABA and acetylcholine typically act this way, point to point. At nondirected synapses the release site is some distance from the receptors. Transmitter is released from varicosities, bead-like swellings spaced along an axon and its branches, so the axon looks like a string of beads; these are also called string-of-beads or en passant synapses. The transmitter diffuses through the extracellular fluid and affects many cells over a wide area, a pattern known as volume transmission. Monoamines are the classic nondirected transmitters. Norepinephrine neurons of the locus coeruleus, serotonin neurons of the raphe nuclei and dopamine neurons of the midbrain have few cell bodies, clustered in the brainstem, but highly branched varicose axons that spread through much of the brain, which suits their modulatory roles in arousal, mood and reward. In the periphery, sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine from varicosities onto smooth muscle. Neuropeptides are also often released diffusely. Examiners pair this topic with other synapse types: axoaxonic synapses, which mediate presynaptic inhibition or facilitation, dendrodendritic synapses, and electrical synapses formed by gap junctions. Option a is right because varicosities are nondirected synapses and the transmitters they classically release are monoamines.","traps":{"b":"Both halves are wrong. Directed synapses have release and receptor sites close together across a narrow cleft, and they are where fast amino acid transmitters such as glutamate and GABA usually act. Varicosities are the opposite case, releasing transmitter at a distance from their targets. It tempts because 'directed; amino acid' is itself a correct description of ordinary fast synapses, just not of varicosities.","c":"'Non-directed' is correct, but 'amino acid' is not. Amino acid transmitters (glutamate, GABA, glycine, aspartate) mostly act at directed synapses where fast, precise point-to-point signalling is needed. The varicose, string-of-beads release pattern is typical of monoamines such as norepinephrine and serotonin. It tempts because the first half is right, so a candidate who knows only the synapse type may stop there.","d":"'Monoamine' is correct, but 'directed' is wrong. Varicosities release transmitter into the extracellular fluid at some distance from target receptors, so they are nondirected by definition. A directed synapse needs the release site to face the receptor site across a narrow cleft. It tempts candidates who remember the monoamine half correctly but assume every chemical synapse is directed, as in the standard textbook diagram."}},{"q":"What is Error degrees of freedom for a randomized block design with 4 treatments and 5 blocks (1 sample per block)","concept":"The randomized block design (RBD) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher and set out in The Design of Experiments (1935). Similar units (plots of similar soil, participants matched on age or IQ, or the same person tested repeatedly) are grouped into blocks, and every treatment is randomly assigned once within each block. Blocking pulls a known nuisance source of variance out of the error term, so the F test becomes more powerful. The data are analysed with a two-way ANOVA without replication, and the degrees of freedom (df) are partitioned as: total = N - 1; treatments = t - 1; blocks = b - 1; error (residual) = (t - 1)(b - 1). With one score per cell, the treatment by block interaction serves as the error term. Here N = 20, so total df = 19, treatment df = 3, block df = 4, and error df = 19 - 3 - 4 = 12. Neighbouring designs: a completely randomized one-way ANOVA with the same 20 scores in 4 groups has error df = N - k = 16, because block variance stays in the error. A repeated-measures ANOVA is an RBD in which each subject is a block, so error df = (k - 1)(n - 1). A Latin square with t treatments has error df = (t - 1)(t - 2). Option a is right because the error df of a randomized block design is (t - 1)(b - 1) = (4 - 1)(5 - 1) = 3 x 4 = 12.","traps":{"b":"20 is N, the total number of observations (4 treatments x 5 blocks, one score per cell). Degrees of freedom are always fewer than N because each estimated mean uses up information: even the total df is N - 1 = 19, and the error df is what remains after treatment and block df are removed. It tempts because 4 x 5 is the first multiplication a student does with these numbers.","c":"15 is what you get from N - b (20 - 5), or from total df minus block df (19 - 4), which ignores the 3 treatment df. It would be the within-groups df if the five blocks were treated as the only grouping factor. It tempts because it looks like a proper subtraction from N, but in a randomized block design both the treatment df and the block df must be removed.","d":"16 is N - k = 20 - 4, the error (within-groups) df of a completely randomized one-way ANOVA with 4 groups of 5. That design does not remove block variance, so its error term keeps the 4 block df. It tempts because the one-way ANOVA formula is the one most students memorise first, and it gives a believable number close to the correct 12."}},{"q":"how many pairwise comparisons will have to be made post-hoc for an Independent variable with 4 levels?","concept":"A significant omnibus ANOVA F test says only that at least one group mean differs from another; post hoc (a posteriori) tests find which pairs differ. The number of distinct pairs among k means is the combination k(k - 1)/2, also written kC2. With k = 4 levels (call them A, B, C, D) the pairs are AB, AC, AD, BC, BD and CD: 4 x 3 / 2 = 6. Order does not matter, since comparing A with B is the same test as comparing B with A, which is why k(k - 1) is divided by 2. Quick values: 3 levels give 3 pairs, 5 give 10, 6 give 15. The count matters because of familywise error. If each test uses alpha = .05, the chance of at least one Type I error across c tests is roughly 1 - (1 - .05)^c, about .26 for 6 tests. The Bonferroni correction (from Carlo Emilio Bonferroni's inequality, applied to multiple comparisons by Olive Jean Dunn in 1961) divides alpha by c, so .05/6 = .0083 per comparison. Common post hoc tests are John W. Tukey's honestly significant difference test (all pairwise comparisons), Henry Scheffe's (1953) test (all possible contrasts, the most conservative), Ronald A. Fisher's least significant difference (the least conservative) and Charles Dunnett's (1955) test, which compares each treatment only with one control group, giving k - 1 = 3 comparisons. Option d is right because an independent variable with 4 levels yields 4 x 3 / 2 = 6 unique pairwise comparisons.","traps":{"a":"8 is 2 x k, a number with no basis in counting pairs. It may come from confusing the 4 levels with a 2 x 4 factorial layout or from adding comparisons carelessly. The correct count is the combination kC2 = k(k - 1)/2. It tempts because it is a round, plausible-looking number that sits between the true answer of 6 and the double count of 12.","b":"12 is k(k - 1) = 4 x 3, the number of ordered pairs. It counts A versus B and B versus A as two different comparisons, which is a permutation, not a combination. In post hoc testing the direction of a comparison does not change the test, so the product must be divided by 2. It tempts because students remember the numerator of the formula and forget the halving.","c":"4 is simply the number of levels, k. It may also be confused with the k - 1 = 3 comparisons in Dunnett's test, where each treatment is compared only with a control, or with the k - 1 degrees of freedom available for planned orthogonal contrasts. It tempts because it repeats a number from the stem, but testing all pairs after the ANOVA needs k(k - 1)/2 = 6 comparisons."}},{"q":"Which of the following rotation methods can be used for second order factor analysis","concept":"Factor analysis first extracts factors, for example by principal axis factoring, and then rotates them to make the loadings easier to interpret. Louis L. Thurstone (1947) set the goal of simple structure: each variable should load highly on one factor and near zero on the rest. Rotations come in two families. Orthogonal rotations keep the factor axes at 90 degrees, so the factors are uncorrelated. Varimax, from Henry F. Kaiser (1958), simplifies the columns of the loading matrix so that each factor has a few high loadings; Quartimax simplifies the rows so that each variable loads on as few factors as possible, often leaving a general factor; Equimax is a compromise between the two. Oblique rotations let the axes take any angle, so the factors may correlate. Promax, from Alan Hendrickson and Paul White (1964), starts from a Varimax solution and raises the loadings to a power to relax orthogonality; Direct Oblimin is the other common choice. An oblique solution gives a pattern matrix, a structure matrix and a factor correlation matrix. Second-order (higher-order) factor analysis factors that correlation matrix among the first-order factors to find broader factors above them. Classic examples are the global factors of Raymond B. Cattell's 16PF (1949) and the general factor g found above Thurstone's primary mental abilities. If the first-order factors are forced to be uncorrelated, their correlation matrix is an identity matrix and there is nothing left to factor. Option a is right because Promax is the only oblique rotation listed, and only correlated first-order factors can yield second-order factors.","traps":{"b":"Equimax is an orthogonal rotation proposed as a compromise between Varimax and Quartimax, balancing simplicity of the factors (columns) and of the variables (rows). Because it keeps the axes at right angles, the factors it produces are uncorrelated, so there is no factor correlation matrix to analyse at a second order. It tempts because its name is less familiar, which makes students guess it is the special one.","c":"Varimax, developed by Henry F. Kaiser (1958), is the most widely used orthogonal rotation. It maximises the variance of the squared loadings within each factor, so every factor has a few high and many near-zero loadings. Its factors are uncorrelated by design, which rules out higher-order factors. It tempts because it is the rotation students know best and the one reported in most published factor analyses.","d":"Quartimax is an orthogonal rotation that simplifies the rows of the loading matrix, trying to make each variable load on as few factors as possible. It often produces one large general factor plus smaller ones. Even so, its factors stay uncorrelated, so no second-order analysis is possible. It tempts precisely because its general-factor tendency resembles the idea of a higher-order g."}},{"q":"An uninvolved objective observation is done in ___________ approach of phenomenology; while an involved observation is done in ____________ approach of phenomenology","concept":"Phenomenology, founded by the philosopher Edmund Husserl (Logical Investigations, 1900-1901), is the study of experience as it appears to the person who lives it. Its rule is to suspend prior theory (epoche or bracketing) and describe the phenomenon itself. Karl Jaspers brought it into psychiatry in General Psychopathology (1913): the clinician tries to grasp the patient's inner experience through empathy and careful description, separating the form of a symptom (for example, a hallucination) from its content (what the voice says). The classification in this question sorts phenomenological observation by the observer's position. In the direct approach the observer is involved: he enters or shares the experience, as in first-person description of one's own experience or empathic, participant-style understanding of another person. In the indirect approach the observer stays uninvolved and records the person's reports, expressions and behaviour objectively from outside, as a third party. 'Semi-direct' is not a standard category. Related distinctions an examiner may swap in: participant versus non-participant observation in field research; introspection, which in the laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (1879) was trained self-observation of sensations, versus phenomenological description, which keeps meaning intact; and the descriptive phenomenological method of Amedeo Giorgi (1970) versus the interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA) of Jonathan A. Smith (1996). A memory aid: involvement means you are directly inside the experience; detachment means you see it indirectly from outside. Option a is right because uninvolved, objective observation belongs to the indirect approach and involved observation belongs to the direct approach.","traps":{"b":"This puts the uninvolved observation under the direct approach, which reverses the logic: direct means the observer is inside the experience, involved, not standing apart. It then adds 'semi-direct', which is not a recognised approach in phenomenology. It tempts because 'direct observation' in general research methods means watching behaviour as it happens, which can sound objective and uninvolved.","c":"The first half is correct: uninvolved, objective observation is the indirect approach. The second half fails because involved observation is the direct approach, and 'semi-direct' is an invented middle category, not a phenomenological term. It tempts because candidates who know only the first half pick any option starting with 'indirect', and 'semi-direct' sounds like a cautious compromise between involvement and detachment.","d":"This is the exact reversal of the key. It pairs detached, objective observation with the direct approach and involved observation with the indirect one. It tempts because in everyday research language 'direct observation' means an observer watching behaviour objectively, as in naturalistic observation, so students carry that meaning over. In this phenomenological classification, direct refers to being inside the experience, which is involvement."}},{"q":"Survey research does NOT","concept":"Survey research collects information from a sample, usually through questionnaires or interviews, in order to describe a larger population. The classic definition comes from Fred N. Kerlinger in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first published 1964): survey research studies large and small populations by selecting and studying samples drawn from them to discover the relative incidence, distribution and interrelations of sociological and psychological variables. Those three words map onto three of the options here. Relative incidence is how common something is, for example the prevalence of depression among college students. Distribution is how a trait spreads across age, gender or region. Interrelations are correlations between variables such as income and stress. Kerlinger classed surveys as non-experimental, ex post facto research: the researcher measures variables as they already exist and manipulates nothing. That is why survey data cannot isolate the factors that cause an event. Establishing causal factors needs an experiment, in which the independent variable is manipulated, participants are randomly assigned and extraneous variables are controlled, or at least a strong quasi-experimental design. Know the survey types an examiner may use: cross-sectional surveys (one point in time), longitudinal surveys (panel or trend studies over time), and the census versus the sample survey. Typical weaknesses are self-report bias, social desirability, non-response and sampling error. Option c is right because identifying the causal factors of an event lies outside survey research, while incidence, distribution and relationships between variables are exactly what Kerlinger's definition says surveys study.","traps":{"a":"Studying the distribution of a trait, for example how anxiety scores spread across age groups, states or income levels, is one of the three aims in Fred N. Kerlinger's definition of survey research. Surveys describe a population as it is, and distribution is a purely descriptive question that needs no manipulation. It tempts because NOT questions make candidates hunt for anything that sounds too simple, but this is a core survey function.","b":"Surveys routinely study relationships between variables, the 'interrelations' in Kerlinger's definition, using correlation and regression on the data collected, such as the link between screen time and sleep quality. What surveys cannot do is show that one variable causes the other. It tempts because students remember 'surveys cannot show causation' and wrongly stretch it to 'surveys cannot study relationships'.","d":"Relative incidence means how often a phenomenon occurs in one group compared with another, such as the rate of substance use among urban versus rural adolescents. Epidemiological prevalence surveys, including India's National Mental Health Survey, exist to answer exactly this kind of question. It tempts because 'incidence' sounds like a technical epidemiology term, but it appears word for word in the classic definition of survey research."}},{"q":"An exploratory research study is also a","concept":"Research designs are often classified by purpose, a scheme set out by Claire Selltiz, Marie Jahoda, Morton Deutsch and Stuart Cook in Research Methods in Social Relations (1959) and repeated in Indian research methodology texts. Exploratory, also called formulative, research is used when a problem is new or poorly understood. Its aim is to become familiar with the phenomenon, clarify concepts, set priorities and formulate precise questions and hypotheses for later work. It uses flexible methods: review of the literature, the experience survey (interviewing people with practical knowledge of the problem), analysis of insight-stimulating cases, focus groups and pilot studies. Because it starts without a firm hypothesis, it is hypothesis-generating. Descriptive research portrays the characteristics of a group or situation accurately, for example the prevalence of a disorder, and diagnostic research asks how often something occurs together with something else; both need more structured designs. Hypothesis-testing (experimental, confirmatory) research tests a causal hypothesis stated in advance, which normally means manipulating an independent variable, randomising and controlling extraneous variables. The same contrast appears in qualitative versus quantitative work: the grounded theory of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967) is inductive and builds theory from data, while a randomised experiment is deductive and checks a prediction. A useful sequence to remember is explore, then describe, then test. Option b is right because exploratory research is done to discover insights and produce hypotheses, not to manipulate variables or to test hypotheses that already exist.","traps":{"a":"Variable manipulation is the defining feature of experimental research, where the researcher changes an independent variable and measures its effect on a dependent variable under controlled conditions. Exploratory work comes before that stage: it uses flexible, open methods such as literature review, experience surveys and case analysis, and usually manipulates nothing. It tempts because many students treat the experiment as the standard model of all research.","c":"'All of the above' would require exploratory research to manipulate variables, generate hypotheses and test hypotheses at the same time. Manipulation and hypothesis testing belong to experimental, confirmatory research, so only one listed option fits. It tempts because exploratory work is flexible and may borrow many techniques, which makes 'all' feel safe, and because the option sits third rather than last, so it is easy to misread.","d":"Hypothesis-testing (confirmatory) research starts with a specific prediction, derived from theory or from earlier exploratory work, and tests it, usually through an experiment with manipulation, random assignment and significance testing. It is the stage that follows exploratory research, not a description of it. It tempts because the word 'hypothesis' appears in both b and d, so a candidate reading quickly may pick the more familiar phrase."}},{"q":"In grounded theory, the process of weaving the data back together around groups of concepts is known as","concept":"Grounded theory was introduced by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss in The Discovery of Grounded Theory (1967). It builds theory inductively from qualitative data, such as interview transcripts, rather than testing a theory chosen in advance. Its core tools are constant comparison (each new piece of data is compared with earlier codes), theoretical sampling (choosing the next cases to develop emerging categories), memo writing, and theoretical saturation (stopping when new data add no new properties). The three-step coding scheme most exams ask about comes from Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin in Basics of Qualitative Research (1990). Open coding breaks the data apart: lines and incidents are labelled with concepts, and similar concepts are grouped into categories with properties and dimensions. Axial coding then puts the data back together in new ways by making connections between a category and its subcategories, around an 'axis'. Its coding paradigm asks about causal conditions, context, intervening conditions, action and interaction strategies, and consequences. Selective coding finally chooses one core category and integrates all the others around it to tell the storyline of the theory. Versions differ: Glaser later rejected axial coding as forcing the data and kept substantive and theoretical coding, while Kathy Charmaz (2006), in constructivist grounded theory, uses initial coding and focused coding. Option b is right because axial coding is the stage that weaves the fragmented data back together by relating groups of concepts, the categories and subcategories, to one another.","traps":{"a":"'Early coding' is not a named stage in any major version of grounded theory. The first stage, which breaks the data into labelled concepts, is open coding in Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) and initial coding in Kathy Charmaz (2006). It tempts because it sounds like a plain-English description of the first step, and candidates who forget the technical terms may choose a word that feels chronological.","c":"Open coding is the first analytic step in Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin's (1990) scheme. The researcher reads the data line by line, fractures it into discrete incidents, labels them with concepts and groups those concepts into tentative categories. It takes the data apart rather than weaving it back together. It tempts because open coding does involve grouping concepts into categories, which looks close to the stem's wording.","d":"'Core coding' is not a standard grounded theory term. What exists is the core category, the central concept chosen during selective coding, around which all the other categories are integrated into a single theory. Selective coding integrates the whole theory around one idea, while axial coding links categories with their subcategories. It tempts because 'core' is a real grounded theory word, borrowed here into a fake label."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 30","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain quantities have been fixed. The one-sample t-test, introduced by William Sealy Gosset writing as 'Student' (1908), compares a sample mean with a known or hypothesised population mean: t = (sample mean - population mean) / (s / square root of n). The sample standard deviation s is calculated around the sample mean, and fixing that mean uses up one piece of information: if you know the mean of 30 scores and any 29 of the scores, the 30th is determined. So df = n - 1. This is also why the sample variance divides by n - 1 rather than n, which gives an unbiased estimate of the population variance. As df increase, the t distribution, which has heavier tails than the normal curve, approaches the z distribution; at df = 29 the two-tailed .05 critical t is about 2.045, compared with 1.96 for z. Learn the neighbouring formulas: the paired-samples t uses n - 1 where n is the number of pairs; the independent-samples t uses n1 + n2 - 2; a Pearson r is tested with n - 2; chi-square goodness of fit uses k - 1 and the contingency test uses (r - 1)(c - 1); one-way ANOVA uses k - 1 between groups and N - k within groups. Option c is right because a one-sample t-test with n = 30 has df = 30 - 1 = 29.","traps":{"a":"30 is the sample size n itself. Degrees of freedom are not the number of scores but the number of independent pieces of information left after estimating parameters, and estimating the sample mean costs one. It tempts because 30 is also the sample size at which many textbooks say the t distribution becomes close to normal, so the number feels meaningful in this stem.","b":"28 is n - 2, the df for an independent-samples t-test with two groups of 15 (n1 + n2 - 2), for testing a Pearson correlation, or for a simple regression slope, where two parameters are estimated. A one-sample t estimates only one parameter, the mean, so only one df is lost. It tempts because n - 2 is a real and frequently tested formula, just for a different test.","d":"31 is n + 1, which has no statistical basis: estimating a parameter removes degrees of freedom and never adds them, so df cannot exceed n here. A student may reach this number by misremembering the sign in n - 1. It tempts mainly as a careless slip under exam pressure, sitting symmetrically on the other side of the sample size from the correct answer."}},{"q":"Which of the following measures of variability is affected by extreme scores in the sample","concept":"Measures of variability describe how spread out scores are, and they differ in how much an extreme score (an outlier) affects them. The range, highest minus lowest score, depends entirely on the two extremes, so it is the most affected. The variance and the standard deviation (SD), a term introduced by Karl Pearson (1894), are computed from every score's deviation from the mean, and those deviations are squared, so one very large deviation adds a disproportionately large amount. Example: the scores 4, 5, 5, 6, 5 have an SD of about 0.7; replace one 5 with 25 and the SD jumps to about 9, while the median stays at 5. The mean, on which the SD is built, is pulled the same way. Resistant (robust) measures ignore the tails. The interquartile range (IQR) is Q3 minus Q1, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores, and the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range is (Q3 - Q1)/2. Because quartiles depend on rank position, not on how far out an extreme score lies, outliers barely move them. The same logic applies to central tendency: the median resists outliers, the mean does not. So for skewed data or data with outliers, texts recommend the median with the IQR or quartile deviation, and for symmetric data the mean with the SD. The mean (average) deviation is also affected, though less than the SD because it does not square. Option c is right because the standard deviation uses every score's squared distance from the mean, so extreme scores inflate it.","traps":{"a":"The interquartile range is Q3 minus Q1, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores. It ignores the lowest and highest quarters entirely, so an extreme score in either tail does not change it; the boxplot of John W. Tukey (1977) uses it to flag outliers. It tempts because it is a measure of variability students know well, but it is the classic resistant measure, the opposite of what the stem asks.","b":"The quartile deviation, also called the semi-interquartile range, is (Q3 - Q1)/2, half the interquartile range. Like the IQR it depends only on the positions of the quartiles, so extreme scores in the tails leave it unchanged. Indian statistics texts pair it with the median for skewed or open-ended distributions for exactly this reason. It tempts because its name contains 'deviation', which sounds like the standard deviation.","d":"'None of the above' would mean that no listed measure is affected by extreme scores. The standard deviation clearly is, because squaring the deviations magnifies large distances from the mean. It tempts candidates who know that two of the three listed measures are resistant and wrongly generalise that all measures of spread are, or who over-think the item because the answer feels too obvious."}},{"q":"On a Binet-Simon type test (mean IQ= 100, sd =16), a person has secured an IQ of 136, what percentage of people fall below him?","concept":"Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence scale in France in 1905. Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) reported a ratio IQ, mental age divided by chronological age times 100, building on William Stern's (1912) mental quotient. Ratio IQs fail for adults, so David Wechsler (1939) introduced the deviation IQ: a standard score showing where a person stands within his own age group's normal distribution. Wechsler scales use mean 100 and SD 15. The 1960 Stanford-Binet (Form L-M) used mean 100 and SD 16, which is why the stem says SD 16; the fifth edition (2003) uses 15. To find a percentile, convert to z: z = (X - mean)/SD = (136 - 100)/16 = 36/16 = 2.25. Then look up the area under the normal curve below that z. Benchmarks to memorise: z = 0 gives 50 percent, z = 1 about 84 percent, z = 1.28 about 90 percent, z = 1.645 about 95 percent, z = 1.96 about 97.5 percent, z = 2 about 97.7 percent, z = 2.25 about 98.8 percent, and z = 3 about 99.9 percent. The 68-95-99.7 rule gives the areas within 1, 2 and 3 SDs of the mean. Variants change the SD: the same IQ of 136 on a Wechsler scale (SD 15) gives z = 2.4, about the 99th percentile. Option c is right because z = 2.25 leaves about 98.8 percent of the normal curve below it, and 98.7 percent is the nearest option.","traps":{"a":"97.5 percent is the area below z = 1.96, the familiar two-tailed .05 critical value, which on this scale is an IQ of about 131 (100 + 1.96 x 16). An IQ of 136 lies further out, at z = 2.25, so more people fall below it. It tempts because 1.96 and 97.5 percent are the most memorised normal-curve pair, and 136 looks roughly like 'two SDs above the mean'.","b":"90 percent is the area below z = 1.28, which on this scale is an IQ of about 120. That is far too low for a score 36 points, or 2.25 SDs, above the mean. It tempts candidates who estimate by feel rather than computing z, or who mix up the 'top 10 percent' cut-offs used in gifted-education screening with the precise percentile for this score.","d":"84 percent is the area below z = 1, one SD above the mean, which here would be an IQ of 116. It is the most familiar benchmark (50 percent below the mean plus 34 percent between the mean and +1 SD). It tempts students who forget to divide by the SD and simply see 136 as 'one step above average', or who use the 68 percent rule without converting the score."}},{"q":"Ordered variables are considered to __________, while nominal variables are considered to be ___________","concept":"Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) defined four levels of measurement. Nominal scales only name or classify (gender, religion, diagnosis); numbers are labels and the only meaningful operation is counting frequencies. Ordinal scales rank (class rank, socioeconomic grade, Likert agreement); the order is meaningful but the gaps between ranks are not known to be equal. Interval scales have equal units but no true zero (Celsius temperature, standard IQ scores), and ratio scales have a true zero (reaction time, weight). A second, cruder division used in statistics and software is categorical versus continuous. Categorical (qualitative) variables place cases in groups with no inherent quantity; nominal variables are always categorical. Continuous (quantitative) variables can in principle take any value along a dimension. Ordered variables sit in between. Because ranks reflect an underlying dimension, many psychology texts treat ordered variables, especially summed rating scales, as continuous for analysis, and that convention is what this item tests. Strictly, though, ordinal data are discrete, and categorical-data books such as that of Alan Agresti (1990) call them ordered categorical variables. A related distinction: discrete (discontinuous) variables take only separate values, such as number of children, while continuous ones can take fractions, such as time. Examiners also ask which statistics suit each level: mode and chi-square for nominal; median, percentiles and Spearman's rho for ordinal; mean, SD, Pearson r and t-tests for interval and ratio. Option b is right because the item contrasts ordered variables, treated as lying on a continuum, with nominal variables, which are purely categorical labels.","traps":{"a":"'Categorical, Continuous' reverses the key. Calling ordered variables categorical has some technical support, since ordinal data are ordered categories, but calling nominal variables continuous is impossible: nominal numbers are labels such as 1 = male and 2 = female, with no quantity or order. It tempts because the first half sounds right to students who have read that ordinal data are 'ordered categorical'.","c":"'Discontinuous, Categorical' calls ordered variables discontinuous (discrete) and nominal variables categorical. Strictly, ordinal ranks are discrete, so this is not absurd; but nominal variables are also discontinuous, so the option sets up no real contrast between the two blanks. The item follows the common psychology convention of treating ordered variables as continuous. It tempts because 'ranks are discrete' is a true textbook statement.","d":"'Categorical; Discontinuous' calls ordered variables categorical and nominal variables discontinuous. Both labels describe the same broad family of non-continuous data, so the option does not separate the two types the stem contrasts. Nominal variables are best described as categorical; 'discontinuous' is the usual label for counts such as number of siblings. It tempts because both words can technically be applied to non-quantitative data."}},{"q":"Which of the following can NOT be used a test of homogeneity of variance before regression","concept":"Most parametric tests assume homogeneity of variance. In the t-test and ANOVA this means the groups have equal population variances. In regression the matching assumption is homoscedasticity: the spread of the residuals is the same at every level of the predicted value. When it fails (heteroscedasticity), the regression coefficients stay unbiased but their standard errors, and so the t and F tests, become unreliable. Tests of equal variances include Maurice Bartlett's (1937) test, which is powerful but very sensitive to non-normality, so a significant result may only reflect skewed data; Howard Levene's (1960) test, which runs an ANOVA on the absolute deviations of scores from their group mean and is robust to non-normality; the version by Morton Brown and Alan Forsythe (1974), which uses deviations from the median; and Herman Otto Hartley's (1950) Fmax, the largest group variance divided by the smallest. For regression residuals specifically, analysts also plot residuals against fitted values or use the test of Trevor Breusch and Adrian Pagan (1979). The test of Samuel Shapiro and Martin Wilk (1965) answers a different question: whether a sample comes from a normally distributed population. A significant W means the data depart from normality. Q-Q plots and skewness and kurtosis values serve the same purpose. Keep the assumption checks apart: normality (Shapiro-Wilk), equal variance (Levene, Bartlett), independence of errors (the statistic of James Durbin and Geoffrey Watson, 1950) and multicollinearity (variance inflation factor). Option c is right because Shapiro-Wilk tests normality, not equality of variances, so it cannot serve as a homogeneity-of-variance test.","traps":{"a":"Levene's test, from Howard Levene (1960), checks whether several groups have equal variances by running an ANOVA on each score's absolute deviation from its group mean. A non-significant result supports homogeneity. It is robust to non-normality and is printed automatically with the independent-samples t-test in SPSS. It tempts because in a NOT question a hurried candidate may simply pick the most familiar name.","b":"Bartlett's test, from Maurice Bartlett (1937), is a chi-square based test of equal variances across groups. It is powerful when the data are normal but very sensitive to departures from normality, so Levene's test is usually preferred. It is still a genuine homogeneity-of-variance test, so it can be used. It tempts because candidates remember its sensitivity to non-normality and confuse that with being a normality test.","d":"'None of the above' would mean all three listed tests can check homogeneity of variance. That fails because the Shapiro-Wilk test, by Samuel Shapiro and Martin Wilk (1965), tests the normality of a distribution, a separate assumption. It tempts because regression assumption checks are usually taught together in one list, and students blur normality, homoscedasticity and linearity into a single 'assumption testing' step."}},{"q":"The correlation between two variable where the common overlap with a third variable is statistically controlled is called","concept":"Correlation coefficients describe how two variables vary together, and Karl Pearson's product-moment r (1896) is the basic index for two continuous variables. Often, though, the link between X and Y partly reflects a third variable Z related to both: height and vocabulary are correlated in children because both rise with age. Partial correlation, written r12.3, removes the influence of the third variable from both X and Y and correlates what is left (the residuals). The first-order formula is r12.3 = (r12 - r13 x r23) / square root of [(1 - r13 squared)(1 - r23 squared)]. Controlling one variable gives a first-order partial, controlling two gives a second-order partial, and the plain r is a zero-order correlation. If the partial correlation drops near zero, the original relationship was spurious, explained by the third variable. Neighbouring indices an examiner may swap in: the semipartial or part correlation, r1(2.3), removes the third variable from only one of the two variables, and its square shows the unique variance a predictor adds in multiple regression; multiple correlation, R, is the correlation between one criterion and the best weighted combination of several predictors, and R squared is the variance they explain together; Charles Spearman's (1904) rho correlates ranks; the phi coefficient is used when both variables are true dichotomies (a 2 x 2 table); point-biserial r suits one true dichotomy and one continuous variable. Option b is right because partial correlation is precisely the correlation between two variables with their common overlap with a third variable statistically held constant.","traps":{"a":"Multiple correlation (R) measures the relationship between one criterion variable and a weighted combination of two or more predictors, as in multiple regression; R squared gives the variance they jointly explain. It combines variables rather than removing a third variable's influence from a two-variable relationship. It tempts because it also involves three or more variables, so it seems to fit a stem that mentions a third variable.","c":"Spearman's rank-order correlation, rho, from Charles Spearman (1904), applies Pearson's formula to ranks, so it suits ordinal data or monotonic relationships with outliers. It measures a simple two-variable relationship and does not control any third variable. It tempts because Spearman is the most famous name on the list and because his work on g and factor analysis involved correlating many variables at once.","d":"The phi coefficient measures association between two variables that are both genuine dichotomies, arranged in a 2 x 2 table, such as gender and pass or fail. It is a Pearson r computed on 0 and 1 codes and equals the square root of chi-square divided by N. It does not partial out a third variable. It tempts because it is another named special correlation taught in the same chapter."}},{"q":"Social loafing is also called __________","concept":"Social loafing is the reduction of individual effort when people pool their efforts toward a group goal, compared with working alone. Its roots lie in the work of the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann, whose rope-pulling studies (published 1913) showed that as group size grew, total force rose but force per person fell. This decline is called the Ringelmann effect. Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) separated its two causes, coordination loss (people pulling at different moments) and motivation loss: blindfolded participants who only believed others were pulling with them still pulled less, showing a real loss of motivation. Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named this motivation loss social loafing in their clapping and shouting studies. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard only when they expect their effort to matter and value the outcome. Loafing decreases when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful, the group is cohesive, and in collectivist cultures. Contrast social facilitation, the improvement in performance when others are present, first shown by Norman Triplett (1898) with cyclists and children winding fishing reels, and explained by Robert Zajonc (1965) as arousal strengthening dominant responses. Related group effects are the free-rider effect and the sucker effect described by Norbert Kerr (1983). Option a is right because social loafing is also known as the Ringelmann effect, after Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling findings.","traps":{"b":"The 'Triplett effect' is an informal name for social facilitation, from Norman Triplett (1898), who found that cyclists rode faster when racing others or with a pacer than alone, and that children wound fishing reels faster in pairs. That is an increase in performance in the presence of others, the opposite direction to loafing. It tempts because both Triplett and Ringelmann are classic early studies of performance in groups.","c":"The McGurk effect, reported by Harry McGurk and John MacDonald (1976), is an audiovisual speech illusion: when a video of a face saying 'ga' is dubbed with the sound 'ba', viewers often hear 'da'. It shows that vision changes what we hear and belongs to perception, not group behaviour. It tempts only because it is an eponymous 'effect' and fits the pattern of the other options.","d":"The free-rider effect, studied by Norbert Kerr and Steven Bruun (1983), is reduced effort because a member feels his contribution is dispensable, since others will achieve the goal anyway. It is one cause or form of motivation loss in groups, closely linked to loafing, but it is not the accepted alternative name for social loafing. It tempts because the meaning is close and the term sounds like everyday group-work slang."}},{"q":"What is the distinct type of motivation proposed by Maslow for self-actualizers?","concept":"Abraham Maslow set out his hierarchy of needs in 'A Theory of Human Motivation' (1943): physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation. He later grouped the first four as deficiency needs, or D-needs. A D-need is felt as a lack; satisfying it removes tension, and once satisfied it stops motivating. Behaviour driven by these needs is D-motivation (deficiency motivation). Self-actualisation is different: it is a growth need, and satisfying it does not end the motive but strengthens it. In 'A Theory of Metamotivation' (1967), Maslow argued that self-actualising people, whose basic needs are largely met, are moved by metamotivation, also called being-motivation, B-motivation or growth motivation. They are drawn to B-values (metaneeds) such as truth, goodness, beauty, wholeness, justice, aliveness, uniqueness and simplicity. When B-values are blocked, metapathologies such as apathy, cynicism and alienation result. Maslow drew the same D versus B contrast elsewhere: D-love (needing love from others) versus B-love (non-possessive love for another person's being), and D-cognition versus B-cognition, the latter typical of peak experiences. His self-actualisers, studied through figures such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, showed accurate perception of reality, acceptance, spontaneity, problem-centredness and autonomy. Metamotivation overlaps with, but is not the same as, intrinsic motivation in the self-determination theory of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985). Option d is right because Maslow named metamotivation as the distinct motivation of self-actualisers, in contrast to the deficiency motivation of people still meeting basic needs.","traps":{"a":"D-motivation, or deficiency motivation, is Maslow's term for behaviour driven by unmet lower needs: hunger, safety, belonging and esteem. It aims at reducing tension caused by a lack, and it stops once the need is met. Self-actualisers have largely moved beyond it, so it is the contrast to their distinctive motivation, not the answer. It tempts because it is a genuine Maslow term with the same letter-plus-motivation format.","b":"'E-motivation' is not a term in Maslow's theory or in mainstream motivation psychology. It may be built to suggest 'esteem', the fourth level of the hierarchy, but esteem is a deficiency need and its motivation is D-motivation. It tempts because it copies the format of the real term D-motivation, and a candidate who half-remembers 'letter motivations' may think there is a whole series of them.","c":"'F-motivation' is also not a Maslowian or standard psychological term. It may be designed to suggest 'fulfilment', which sounds like self-actualisation, but Maslow's word for the growth-driven motivation of self-actualisers is metamotivation, or B-motivation (being-motivation). It tempts because 'fulfilment' and 'self-actualisation' feel like synonyms, and the letter pattern of the options invites guessing."}},{"q":"According to self-determination theory, what are the three basic needs that lead to a state of well-being?","concept":"Self-determination theory (SDT) was developed by Edward L. Deci and Richard M. Ryan, whose main early statement was Intrinsic Motivation and Self-Determination in Human Behavior (1985). It grew from Deci's (1971) finding that paying students for solving puzzles reduced their later free-time interest in the task, an undermining effect also shown by Mark Lepper, David Greene and Richard Nisbett (1973) as the overjustification effect in children's drawing. SDT holds that people have three innate, universal psychological needs, and that well-being, intrinsic motivation and growth depend on satisfying them: competence (feeling effective and able to master challenges), autonomy (feeling that one's actions are self-chosen and endorsed, not controlled), and relatedness (feeling connected to and cared for by others). Autonomy here means volition, not independence: a person can autonomously choose to rely on others. SDT also places motivation on a continuum from amotivation through external, introjected, identified and integrated regulation to intrinsic motivation (organismic integration theory), and its cognitive evaluation theory explains how rewards and feedback raise or lower intrinsic motivation by affecting perceived competence and autonomy. Do not mix SDT needs with the needs for achievement, affiliation and power of David McClelland (1961), with Alfred Adler's striving for superiority, or with the hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943). Clinically, SDT informs motivational interviewing and autonomy-supportive therapy and teaching. Option b is right because SDT's three basic psychological needs are competence, autonomy and relatedness.","traps":{"a":"This option swaps competence for achievement. The need for achievement (nAch) comes from David McClelland (1961), building on Henry Murray's (1938) list of needs, and is measured with the Thematic Apperception Test. It is a learned motive to excel against standards of excellence, not one of SDT's innate needs. It tempts because autonomy and relatedness are correct, so two-thirds of the option matches.","c":"'Independence and superiority' replace autonomy and relatedness. Striving for superiority is Alfred Adler's master motive in Individual Psychology, and independence is not the same as SDT's autonomy, which means acting from one's own will, even while depending on others. Relatedness is missing entirely. It tempts because competence is right and 'independence' sounds like a synonym for autonomy.","d":"This option keeps competence and autonomy but replaces relatedness with excellence. SDT has no need for excellence; striving to meet standards of excellence is closer to McClelland's achievement motive. Leaving out relatedness removes SDT's social need, the sense of being connected to and cared for by others. It tempts because two of the three needs are correct and 'excellence' sounds like a positive growth term."}},{"q":"Gratitude increases prosocial behavior, primarily by enhancing helpers’ feelings of","concept":"Gratitude is the positive emotion felt when someone has intentionally done us good. Michael McCullough and colleagues (2001) described it as a moral affect: it registers kindness, rewards the helper and motivates the receiver to act prosocially, and Robert Emmons and Michael McCullough (2003) showed that keeping a weekly gratitude list improved well-being. But why does being thanked make the helper help again? Adam Grant and Francesca Gino (2010), in four experiments reported in 'A Little Thanks Goes a Long Way', proposed two routes. The agentic route is self-efficacy, Albert Bandura's (1977) term for belief in one's ability to succeed at a task: being thanked tells the helper that the help worked. The communal route is self-worth, or social worth: being thanked tells the helper that he is valued by others. In one study, participants who received a thank-you note from a student they had helped were about twice as likely to help him again as those who got a neutral reply, and thanked helpers also helped a different person more. Gratitude raised both feelings, but in their mediation analyses social worth carried the effect, not self-efficacy. Keep neighbouring terms apart: self-esteem is a global evaluation of oneself, measured by the scale of Morris Rosenberg (1965); self-confidence is a loose everyday word without a specific theory; reciprocity norms and the empathy-altruism hypothesis of C. Daniel Batson (1991) are other explanations of helping. Option a is right because it pairs self-worth, the feeling that carried the effect, with self-efficacy, the other mechanism Grant and Gino tested and textbooks report.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps self-worth, which is correct, but replaces self-efficacy with self-confidence. Self-confidence is a general, everyday term for trusting oneself, with no specific theory behind it. Self-efficacy, from Albert Bandura (1977), is a precise, task-specific belief that one can perform an action successfully, and it is the agentic mechanism Grant and Gino tested. It tempts because confidence and efficacy sound interchangeable.","c":"This option keeps self-efficacy but swaps self-worth for self-esteem. Self-esteem is a global, fairly stable evaluation of oneself as worthy, as measured by Morris Rosenberg's (1965) scale. The gratitude research measured social worth, the specific feeling of being valued by others in that interaction. It tempts because self-esteem and self-worth are often used as synonyms in everyday speech and in some textbooks.","d":"This option replaces both researched constructs with broader, everyday ones: self-esteem (a global self-evaluation) instead of self-worth (feeling valued by others), and self-confidence instead of Albert Bandura's (1977) task-specific self-efficacy. Neither was a mechanism tested in the gratitude studies. It tempts because it sounds like the most natural description of how being thanked makes someone feel good about themselves."}},{"q":"In a POX relationship triad, balance exists when there are","concept":"Fritz Heider set out balance theory in 1946 and developed it in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958). It is a cognitive consistency theory: people prefer their attitudes and relationships to fit together. The P-O-X triad has three elements: P, the perceiver; O, another person; and X, an object, idea or third person. Each of the three relations (P to O, P to X, O to X) is either positive (liking, agreeing, belonging) or negative (disliking, disagreeing). The rule is to multiply the three signs. If the product is positive the triad is balanced; if negative it is imbalanced. So balanced triads have all three positive (I like you, we both like cricket) or exactly one positive and two negatives (I like you and we both dislike a politician; or I dislike you, you dislike X, and I like X). Imbalanced triads have two positives and one negative (I like you, I like X, you hate X) or three negatives. Imbalance creates tension, and P restores balance by changing the weakest relation, for example liking O less or changing his view of X. Dorwin Cartwright and Frank Harary (1956) generalised the rule to larger networks using graph theory. Neighbouring theories: the A-B-X model of communication of Theodore Newcomb (1953); the congruity theory of Charles Osgood and Percy Tannenbaum (1955), which adds degrees of attitude; and the cognitive dissonance theory of Leon Festinger (1957). Option b is right because two negatives and one positive multiply to a positive product, which makes the triad balanced.","traps":{"a":"One negative and two positives gives a negative product, so the triad is imbalanced. Example: P likes O and P likes X, but O dislikes X. P feels strain, because a friend rejects something P values, and is pushed to change one relation. It tempts because two positives out of three looks 'mostly positive', and students assume that more liking means more balance, ignoring Fritz Heider's (1946) sign-multiplication rule.","c":"Three negatives multiply to a negative product, so the rule classes the triad as imbalanced. In the all-negative case the 'enemy of my enemy is my friend' logic predicts pressure for two members to become allies, which is why it is not stable. It tempts because complete mutual dislike sounds consistent in everyday terms, and Fritz Heider (1946) himself treated this case as less clear-cut than the others.","d":"'No positives' means all three relations are negative, which is the same triad as option c, and its product is negative, so it is imbalanced. Since c and d describe one situation, neither could be the single correct answer. Balance depends on the product of the three signs, not on the count of positives or negatives. It tempts because 'no positives' sounds like consistent agreement in dislike."}}],"met-mock-1":[{"q":"The all-or-none law of action potentials means that:","concept":"The all-or-none law says that a neuron either fires a complete action potential or does not fire at all; there is no half spike. It was first described for heart muscle by Henry Pickering Bowditch (1871) and later shown for nerve fibres. A neuron at rest sits at about -70 mV. Its inputs produce graded potentials (excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials, receptor potentials) whose size varies with stimulus strength, which summate spatially and temporally, and which fade with distance (decremental conduction). If the summed depolarisation at the axon hillock reaches threshold, about -55 mV, voltage-gated sodium channels open in a positive feedback loop and the membrane shoots to a fixed peak of about +30 to +40 mV. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out this ionic mechanism in the squid giant axon. Because each patch of axon regenerates the spike, it travels without decrement and keeps the same amplitude from hillock to terminal. In myelinated axons it jumps between the nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction), which changes speed, not size. How, then, does the brain tell a strong stimulus from a weak one? Edgar Adrian and Yngve Zotterman (1926) showed that intensity is coded by firing frequency (rate coding) and by the number of neurons recruited (population coding). Examiners build variants by swapping in graded potentials, which do vary in size, or the refractory periods, which cap the firing rate. Option d is right because once threshold is reached the spike fires in full and propagates along the axon at a constant amplitude, whatever the stimulus strength.","traps":{"a":"This describes amplitude coding, the exact idea the all-or-none law rejects. Once threshold is crossed, a strong stimulus and a just-sufficient stimulus produce spikes of the same height. A stronger stimulus instead raises the firing rate and recruits more neurons, as Edgar Adrian and Yngve Zotterman (1926) showed. It tempts because 'stronger cause, bigger effect' feels natural, and graded potentials really do grow with stimulus strength.","b":"Sub-threshold stimuli do produce something, but it is a local graded potential, not a small action potential. Such depolarisations spread passively, shrink with distance and die out unless they summate to threshold at the axon hillock. There is no 'miniature action potential'. The option tempts because the phrase echoes miniature end-plate potentials, described by Paul Fatt and Bernard Katz (1952), which are tiny graded responses to single packets of acetylcholine.","c":"Grading by stimulus intensity is the defining property of graded potentials: receptor potentials and excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials. Their size rises with stimulus strength, and they summate in space and time. The axonal action potential is the opposite kind of signal, fixed in size and self-regenerating. The option tempts because the full chain of neural signalling does contain graded steps, so a candidate who mixes up the two signal types will pick it."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of major depressive episode is:","concept":"A major depressive episode is the building block of major depressive disorder and of the depressive phase of bipolar disorder. The criteria come from the American Psychiatric Association's DSM-5 (2013) and were carried into its text revision, DSM-5-TR (2022). Five or more of nine symptoms must be present during the same 2-week period, nearly every day, and at least one must be depressed mood or markedly diminished interest or pleasure (anhedonia). The other seven are significant weight or appetite change, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or retardation, fatigue, worthlessness or excessive guilt, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and recurrent thoughts of death or suicide. The symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment and must not be due to a substance or a medical condition. The 2-week floor separates an episode from ordinary low days. Examiners love duration questions, so learn the neighbours as a set: manic episode at least 1 week (any duration if hospitalised); hypomanic episode at least 4 consecutive days; persistent depressive disorder at least 2 years in adults and 1 year in children; schizophrenia at least 6 months including 1 month of active symptoms; generalised anxiety disorder 6 months; post-traumatic stress disorder more than 1 month. ICD-11, adopted by the World Health Organization in 2019, also sets a 2-week minimum for a depressive episode. DSM-5 also removed the bereavement exclusion, so an episode can be diagnosed after a loss. Option a is right because DSM-5-TR requires the symptoms to persist for at least 2 weeks.","traps":{"b":"One week is the minimum duration for a manic episode in DSM-5-TR: abnormally elevated, expansive or irritable mood with increased energy, lasting at least 1 week, or any duration if hospitalisation is needed. A hypomanic episode needs only 4 consecutive days. It tempts because mood-episode durations are taught side by side, and candidates swap the depressive and manic numbers under time pressure.","c":"One month appears elsewhere in DSM-5-TR: post-traumatic stress disorder needs symptoms for more than 1 month, acute stress disorder lasts from 3 days to 1 month, schizophrenia needs 1 month of active-phase symptoms, and brief psychotic disorder lasts less than 1 month. None of these concerns depression. It tempts because one month sounds like a sensible clinical waiting period before calling low mood an illness.","d":"Six months is the duration for generalised anxiety disorder (excessive worry more days than not), for the total course of schizophrenia including prodromal and residual phases, and for social anxiety disorder and specific phobia, which typically last 6 months or more. Depression needs only 2 weeks. It tempts because six months is one of the most frequently repeated durations in DSM-5-TR, so it feels like a safe default."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, the ability to mentally reverse operations first appears in which stage?","concept":"Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) described four stages of cognitive development, each a qualitatively different way of thinking. In the sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) the infant knows the world through action and gains object permanence. In the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) symbolic thought and language appear, but thinking is egocentric, centred on one striking feature (centration) and irreversible. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) the child acquires operations, which Piaget defined as internalised mental actions that can be reversed. Reversibility has two forms: inversion or negation (pouring the water back would restore the original level) and reciprocity or compensation (the glass is taller but also thinner). Together with decentration, reversibility makes conservation possible, the understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in appearance. Conservation is the classic test: a preoperational child says the tall thin glass has more water; a concrete operational child says it is the same because you can pour it back. Conservation does not arrive all at once: number and liquid come first, weight later and volume last, a lag Piaget called horizontal decalage. Seriation, classification and transitive inference also appear now, but only for concrete, visible objects. In the formal operational stage (about 11 onwards) the adolescent reasons abstractly and hypothetically, testing variables systematically, as in the pendulum task of Barbel Inhelder and Piaget (1958). Option d is right because reversible mental operations first emerge in the concrete operational stage, which is why conservation is mastered then.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is the period of reflexes, circular reactions and the slow building of object permanence, the knowledge that objects exist when out of sight. Thought here is action, not mental representation, so there are no operations to reverse. It tempts because infants do learn that actions can be undone in practice, for example by finding a hidden toy, which looks like an early form of reversal.","b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is defined by the absence of reversibility. The child centres on one dimension, such as the height of the water, and cannot mentally undo the pouring, so fails conservation. Egocentrism, shown in the three mountains task of Piaget and Inhelder (1956), and animism also belong here. It tempts because conservation is most often discussed at this stage, but as a failure, not a success.","c":"The formal operational stage (about 11 years onwards) adds abstract, hypothetical and propositional reasoning: thinking about possibilities, testing variables one at a time and reasoning about ideas rather than objects. Formal operations build on reversibility, which is already present from the concrete stage. It tempts because formal thought uses reversibility in a richer form, but the stem asks where the ability first appears, not where it is most developed."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability r_xx = 0.81, the maximum possible validity coefficient with any other measure is:","concept":"This item rests on classical test theory, in which every observed score is a true score plus random error (X = T + E). Reliability, written r_xx, is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance. Error is random, so it cannot correlate with anything. A test can therefore correlate with an outside criterion only through its true-score part. The correlation between observed scores and true scores, called the index of reliability, equals the square root of r_xx, and this sets the upper limit for any validity coefficient. The general rule is that r_xy can be no larger than the square root of (r_xx multiplied by r_yy), where r_yy is the reliability of the criterion. If the criterion is perfectly reliable (r_yy = 1), the ceiling reduces to the square root of r_xx. Here the square root of 0.81 is 0.90. If the criterion had r_yy = 0.64, the ceiling would fall to the square root of (0.81 x 0.64), which is 0.72. Charles Spearman (1904) used the same logic in his correction for attenuation, which estimates the correlation between true scores by dividing r_xy by the square root of (r_xx x r_yy). Keep the neighbours apart: the coefficient of determination is r squared (the share of variance two measures have in common); the standard error of measurement is SD multiplied by the square root of (1 minus r_xx); and reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Option c is right because the maximum validity equals the square root of 0.81, which is 0.90.","traps":{"a":"0.81 is the reliability coefficient itself, not the validity ceiling. Reliability is a proportion of variance, while a validity coefficient is a correlation, so the two are put on the same scale by taking the square root. Because the square root of a number between 0 and 1 is larger than the number, the ceiling (0.90) is higher than 0.81. It tempts because many students assume validity can never exceed reliability.","b":"A validity coefficient of 1.00 would need both the test and the criterion to be perfectly reliable, with no measurement error at all. With r_xx = 0.81, 19 percent of the test's variance is error, and error cannot correlate with anything, so a perfect correlation is impossible. It tempts candidates who think of validity as a separate property, unconnected to how consistently the test measures.","d":"0.95 is the square root of about 0.90 (0.95 x 0.95 = 0.9025), not of 0.81. The square root of 0.81 is exactly 0.90, because 0.9 x 0.9 = 0.81. A candidate who estimates the root by eye, or who treats 0.90 as if it were the reliability, lands here. It tempts because it looks like a plausible 'high but not perfect' ceiling and sits just above the correct value."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which approach?","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its key text, Gestalt Therapy, written by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman, appeared in 1951. Perls trained as a psychoanalyst but turned to an experiential, here-and-now approach, borrowing from Gestalt psychology the idea that people organise experience into wholes. Therapy aims at awareness and integration. Problems are seen as 'unfinished business': feelings and needs from the past, often grief, anger or resentment toward someone, that were never completed and keep intruding into the present. Contact with the environment is disturbed by processes such as introjection, projection, retroflection, deflection and confluence. The empty chair technique makes the unfinished business present. The client imagines a person, often a dead parent or an absent partner, sitting in an empty chair and speaks to them directly, in the present tense, instead of talking about them. In the related two-chair technique the client moves between chairs to voice two parts of the self, such as Perls's 'topdog' (the demanding inner critic) and 'underdog' (the passive resister), until the split is integrated. Perls drew on the role-play methods of psychodrama, founded by Jacob L. Moreno in the 1920s, and Leslie Greenberg, Laura Rice and Robert Elliott (1993) later built chair work into emotion-focused therapy. Other Gestalt methods include dream work, in which every dream element is treated as a projected part of the self, and the instruction to use 'I' statements. Option b is right because the empty chair is the signature technique of Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive Behaviour Therapy grew from Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy of the 1960s and Albert Ellis's rational therapy (1955). It works through thought records, Socratic questioning, cognitive restructuring, exposure and behavioural experiments, treating feelings as products of thoughts to be tested, not unfinished experiences to be enacted. It tempts because some later variants, especially schema therapy, borrowed chair work, so candidates may have seen chairs used in a cognitive setting.","c":"Person-Centred Therapy, set out by Carl Rogers in Client-Centered Therapy (1951), is non-directive. The therapist offers empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence, trusts the client's actualising tendency, and avoids structured exercises. Rogers would not ask a client to address a chair. It tempts because Gestalt and person-centred therapy are both humanistic and present-focused, and they are taught together in the same 'third force' chapter.","d":"Psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria with Josef Breuer, 1895), uses free association, dream interpretation and the analysis of transference and resistance, with the analyst largely interpretive. The past is understood through talk, not acted out in the room. It tempts because Fritz Perls was himself trained as a psychoanalyst and Gestalt therapy keeps ideas such as projection, but Perls rejected interpretation in favour of direct experience."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"This item tests the punctuation of relative clauses, the clauses introduced by who, whom, whose, which or that. English has two kinds. A restrictive (defining) relative clause identifies which person or thing is meant and takes no commas: 'The patient who was anxious refused medication' implies several patients, and it was the anxious one who refused. A non-restrictive (non-defining) clause adds extra information about someone already identified and must be enclosed by a pair of commas, one before and one after: 'The patient, who was anxious, refused medication' means there is one patient, who happened to be anxious. A quick test is deletion: if you can remove the clause and the sentence still tells you who is meant, it is non-restrictive and needs commas. Commas around such a clause act like brackets, so they come in pairs unless the clause ends the sentence, where the full stop closes it. Three linked rules are often examined. First, 'that' introduces only restrictive clauses, so 'The patient, that was anxious,' is wrong. Second, never put a single comma between a subject and its verb. Third, a semicolon joins two independent clauses and cannot close a clause opened by a comma. In this item only one version encloses the clause in a matching pair of commas; the others mix marks, leave the clause open, or split the subject from its verb. Option b is right because the non-restrictive clause 'who was anxious' is correctly enclosed by a matching pair of commas.","traps":{"a":"This mixes two marks: a comma opens the clause and a semicolon closes it. Paired marks around a parenthetical element must match, comma with comma or bracket with bracket. A semicolon has a different job, joining two independent clauses that could each stand alone, as in 'The patient was anxious; she refused medication.' Here 'refused medication' is not an independent clause. It tempts because a semicolon looks like a stronger, more formal pause.","c":"This opens the non-restrictive clause with a comma but never closes it. Commas around a parenthetical clause work like brackets and must come in a pair, so a second comma is needed after 'anxious'. As written, the clause runs straight into the verb and the sentence reads as if 'anxious refused medication' were one unit. It tempts because the opening comma looks right and the missing one is easy to overlook.","d":"This places one comma after the clause and none before it. The result separates the subject ('The patient who was anxious') from its verb ('refused') with a single comma, a classic error in standard English. A non-restrictive clause needs a comma on both sides; a restrictive clause needs none. It tempts because speakers often pause after a long subject, and writers wrongly mark that pause with a comma."}},{"q":"A Type I error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"Null hypothesis significance testing, in the decision framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), treats every test as a choice made under uncertainty. The null hypothesis (H0) says there is no effect or no difference; the alternative (H1) says there is. The truth is unknown, so four outcomes are possible. If H0 is true and you retain it, you are correct. If H0 is true and you reject it, you have made a Type I error, a false positive: you claim an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level set in advance, conventionally 0.05 following Ronald Fisher (1925). If H0 is false and you retain it, you have made a Type II error, a false negative: you miss a real effect. Its probability is beta. If H0 is false and you reject it, you are correct, and the probability of doing so is power, 1 minus beta; Jacob Cohen (1988) recommended power of at least 0.80. A courtroom analogy helps: convicting an innocent person is a Type I error; acquitting a guilty one is a Type II error. For a fixed sample size, lowering alpha reduces Type I errors but raises beta. Running many tests inflates the overall Type I error rate, which is why corrections divide alpha by the number of tests. Sample size, effect size and alpha all change power, but none of them defines an error type. Option d is right because a Type I error is rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true.","traps":{"a":"Correctly retaining a true null hypothesis is not an error at all. It is one of the two correct decisions in the decision table, and its probability is 1 minus alpha (for example 0.95 when alpha is 0.05). It tempts because the word 'retained' appears in the definition of a Type II error, so a candidate scanning for keywords may grab it without checking whether the decision was right or wrong.","b":"A small sample reduces statistical power, which raises the risk of a Type II error (missing a real effect). It is a cause that changes error rates, not the definition of any error type, and the Type I error rate stays fixed at alpha whatever the sample size. It tempts because 'sample size too small' is the standard answer to many research-design questions about why a study went wrong.","c":"Wrongly retaining a null hypothesis that is false is the definition of a Type II error (beta), a false negative: a real effect exists but the study fails to detect it. Its complement, 1 minus beta, is power. It tempts because this option and the keyed one are mirror images, and candidates who learned the two errors as a pair often swap them. Remember: Type I is a false alarm, Type II is a miss."}},{"q":"During the absolute refractory period of a neuron:","concept":"After a neuron fires, it cannot fire again straight away. This recovery time is the refractory period, and it has two parts. The reason lies in the voltage-gated sodium channel, which has two gates: an activation gate that opens quickly at threshold and an inactivation gate that closes a moment later and plugs the channel. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) described these gating processes in the squid giant axon. During the absolute refractory period, which runs from the start of the spike through most of repolarisation and lasts about 1 millisecond, the sodium channels are either already open or inactivated. An inactivated channel cannot reopen until the membrane has repolarised and the inactivation gate has reset, so no stimulus, however strong, can produce a second spike. Then comes the relative refractory period, lasting a few milliseconds. Some sodium channels have recovered, but voltage-gated potassium channels are still open and the membrane is briefly hyperpolarised below resting potential (the after-hyperpolarisation). A second spike is possible now, but only with a stronger-than-normal stimulus. Refractory periods have two key effects. They cap the firing rate: with about 1 millisecond of absolute refractoriness, a neuron cannot exceed roughly 1000 spikes per second. They also make the action potential travel in one direction only, because the patch just behind the spike cannot be re-excited. Examiners swap the two periods, so link 'no stimulus' with absolute and 'stronger stimulus' with relative. Option d is right because during the absolute refractory period the sodium channels are inactivated and no stimulus can produce another spike.","traps":{"a":"A stronger-than-normal stimulus producing another spike describes the relative refractory period, which follows the absolute one. By then some sodium channels have recovered from inactivation, so a large enough depolarisation can trigger a spike despite the lingering potassium outflow. During the absolute period no stimulus works at all. It tempts because the two periods are taught together and differ by exactly this detail, so swapping them is the commonest error.","b":"Hyperpolarisation below the resting potential is the after-hyperpolarisation (undershoot), caused by voltage-gated potassium channels closing slowly so that potassium keeps leaving the cell. It occurs during the relative refractory period, after the absolute period has ended, and it is one reason a stronger stimulus is needed then. It tempts because hyperpolarisation does make firing harder, so it sounds like the reason the neuron cannot fire.","c":"Voltage-gated potassium channels open more slowly than sodium channels, and the outflow of potassium drives repolarisation, beginning around the peak of the spike. That moment does fall inside the absolute refractory period, but it describes only one instant and is not what makes the period absolute; sodium channel inactivation is. It tempts because potassium efflux is part of the falling phase and is easily mistaken for the cause of refractoriness."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms are most associated with:","concept":"Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist, listed symptoms that he considered highly suggestive of schizophrenia when organic brain disease is absent; his list became widely known in English through the 1959 translation of his Clinical Psychopathology. He called them first-rank symptoms. There are eleven, in four groups. Auditory hallucinations: audible thoughts (thought echo), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts and somatic passivity, all felt as imposed by an outside agency. Delusional perception: a normal perception given a sudden, unrelated delusional meaning. The common thread is loss of the boundary between self and outside world. Schneider also described second-rank symptoms, such as other hallucinations, perplexity, mood changes and sudden delusional ideas, which occur in many disorders. Compare Eugen Bleuler, whose 1911 monograph on schizophrenia stressed the four As: loosening of associations, blunted affect, ambivalence and autism. First-rank symptoms are not pathognomonic, since they also appear in mania and other states. DSM-IV allowed a single Schneiderian hallucination to satisfy criterion A, but DSM-5 (2013) removed that special weight, while ICD-10 and ICD-11 still describe passivity and thought alienation prominently. A key distinction is ownership: in thought insertion the thought feels alien, while an obsessional thought is recognised as one's own. Option d is right because Schneider's first-rank symptoms were devised as markers of schizophrenia and remain most closely associated with it.","traps":{"a":"Bipolar disorder is defined by manic or hypomanic episodes, usually with depressive episodes, and is diagnosed on mood, not on Schneiderian phenomena. However, first-rank symptoms do occur in some patients with psychotic mania, which is the main reason Schneider's list is no longer treated as specific to schizophrenia. It tempts because a well-read candidate knows this overlap and may overcorrect, but the stem asks what the symptoms are most associated with.","b":"Borderline personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of unstable relationships, self-image and affect, with marked impulsivity, fear of abandonment and recurrent self-harm. Under stress, patients may have brief paranoid ideas or dissociation, and some report voices, but these are transient and not the core of the disorder. It tempts because the word 'borderline' originally referred to the border between neurosis and psychosis, suggesting psychotic features.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves intrusive, unwanted thoughts and repetitive acts done to reduce anxiety. The crucial difference is ownership: an obsession is recognised as a product of one's own mind, even though it is unwanted, whereas in thought insertion, a first-rank symptom, the thought is felt to come from outside. It tempts because both involve thoughts that seem to intrude, and the phrase 'intrusive thoughts' blurs the line."}},{"q":"In Erikson's psychosocial theory, the crisis of middle adulthood is:","concept":"Erik Erikson set out his psychosocial theory in Childhood and Society (1950). He accepted Sigmund Freud's view that development unfolds in stages but shifted the focus from sexual drives to social relationships and extended it across the whole life span. Each of eight stages poses a crisis, a turning point between two opposite tendencies; a favourable balance yields a basic virtue (ego strength). Development follows the epigenetic principle: each stage has its own time of special importance, and earlier outcomes shape later ones. The stages, with approximate periods and virtues, are: trust versus mistrust (infancy, hope); autonomy versus shame and doubt (early childhood, will); initiative versus guilt (preschool, purpose); industry versus inferiority (school age, competence); identity versus role confusion (adolescence, fidelity); intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood, roughly 40 to 65, care); and integrity versus despair (late adulthood, wisdom). Generativity means concern for establishing and guiding the next generation, through parenting, teaching, mentoring, productive work or creative contribution. Its failure is stagnation, a sense of self-absorption and personal impoverishment. The first five stages roughly parallel Freud's oral, anal, phallic, latency and genital stages; the last three are Erikson's own. James Marcia (1966) later turned the adolescent stage into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Exams often give a description, such as a 50-year-old who feels his life has no purpose beyond himself, and ask for the stage. Option a is right because generativity versus stagnation is the crisis Erikson placed in middle adulthood.","traps":{"b":"Intimacy versus isolation is Erikson's sixth stage, in young adulthood (roughly the twenties and thirties). The task is to form close, committed relationships, including love and deep friendship, without losing one's newly formed identity; the virtue is love. Failure brings isolation and self-absorbed loneliness. It tempts because young and middle adulthood are adjacent stages, and both concern relationships, so the labels are easily swapped.","c":"Integrity versus despair is Erikson's eighth and final stage, in late adulthood (roughly 65 onwards). The older person looks back on life and either accepts it as meaningful (integrity, with the virtue of wisdom) or feels regret and fear of death because it is too late to start again (despair). It tempts because both later stages involve taking stock of one's life, and 'middle age' and 'old age' are often blurred.","d":"Identity versus role confusion is Erikson's fifth stage, in adolescence. The young person tries out roles, values and occupations to build a stable sense of who they are; the virtue is fidelity. James Marcia (1966) extended it into four identity statuses. It tempts because the popular idea of a 'midlife crisis' sounds like an identity crisis, but Erikson placed the identity crisis in adolescence, not middle age."}},{"q":"A T-score of 70 on the MMPI corresponds to a deviation of:","concept":"Raw test scores mean little until they are placed on a common scale, so tests convert them to standard scores. The basic one is the z-score: the raw score minus the mean, divided by the standard deviation, giving a mean of 0 and an SD of 1. Because z-scores include negatives and decimals, they are often linearly transformed. The T-score is one such scale, with a mean of 50 and an SD of 10, so T = 50 + 10z. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI), published by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), reports its clinical and validity scales as T-scores based on its normative sample. A T of 70 is (70 minus 50) divided by 10, that is z = +2, two standard deviations above the mean. On a normal curve about 97.7 percent of people fall below this point, so only about 2.3 percent score higher. On the original MMPI, a T of 70 or above marked a clinically significant elevation. The MMPI-2 (1989) introduced uniform T-scores and lowered the cut-off to 65, a frequent trap in variant questions. Other standard-score scales follow the same logic: deviation IQ with mean 100 and SD 15 (so +2 SD is 130); Wechsler subtest scaled scores with mean 10 and SD 3; stanines with mean 5 and SD about 2; stens with mean 5.5 and SD 2. To convert between scales, always go back to z first. Option a is right because a T-score of 70 lies exactly 2 standard deviations above the T mean of 50.","traps":{"b":"A deviation of 1.5 SD above the mean corresponds to T = 50 + (10 x 1.5) = 65. That value matters, because a T of 65 is the clinical cut-off on the MMPI-2 (1989), so a candidate who remembers '65' as the clinical line may link 70 with a smaller deviation. It tempts as a midway guess, but T-score arithmetic is exact: every 10 points equals one standard deviation.","c":"One SD above the mean corresponds to T = 60, around the 84th percentile on a normal curve. A T of 60 is mildly above average and is not usually interpreted as clinically elevated on the MMPI. It tempts candidates who mistakenly think the T-score SD is 20, or who confuse the T scale with deviation IQ, where 115 marks one SD above the mean of 100.","d":"Three SD above the mean corresponds to T = 80, a score that only about 0.1 percent of a normal population would exceed. It tempts candidates who count 50, 60 and 70 as three steps, or who confuse T-scores with deviation IQ, where 70 is 2 SD below the mean of 100, an important but different cut-off used in diagnosing intellectual disability."}},{"q":"Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:","concept":"Carl Rogers founded client-centred, later called person-centred, therapy, set out in Client-Centered Therapy (1951). His theory holds that every person has an actualising tendency, an inborn drive toward growth. Distress arises from incongruence, a gap between the self-concept and actual experience, created when a child learns conditions of worth: the sense of being valued only when meeting others' expectations. In a famous 1957 paper Rogers named six necessary and sufficient conditions for therapeutic personality change: two persons in psychological contact; a client in a state of incongruence, vulnerable or anxious; a therapist who is congruent in the relationship; a therapist who experiences unconditional positive regard for the client; a therapist who experiences empathic understanding of the client's internal frame of reference; and a client who perceives, at least minimally, the therapist's empathy and regard. Conditions three to five are the therapist-offered core conditions. Congruence (genuineness) means the therapist is real, not hiding behind a professional facade. Unconditional positive regard means non-judgemental warmth and acceptance of the whole person. Accurate empathy means sensing the client's world 'as if' it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and reflecting it back. Rogers argued that techniques matter less than this relationship. Do not confuse these with the working alliance of Edward Bordin (1979), defined by agreement on goals, agreement on tasks, and the bond, which is a pan-theoretical concept. Option c is right because unconditional positive regard, congruence and accurate empathy are exactly Rogers's three core conditions.","traps":{"a":"Transference, free association and interpretation are the core tools of psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, with Josef Breuer, 1895). The patient says whatever comes to mind, feelings toward early figures are displaced onto the analyst, and the analyst interprets them. These belong to an expert-led, interpretive method, the opposite of Rogers's non-directive stance. It tempts because it is a familiar set of three terms from a 'major schools' chapter.","b":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation and exposure are techniques of Cognitive Behaviour Therapy. Restructuring comes from Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy of the 1960s and 1970s, behavioural activation targets depressive withdrawal through planned activity, and exposure treats anxiety by confronting feared stimuli. These are therapist-structured methods, not relational conditions. It tempts because CBT is the most widely taught modern therapy, and its three named techniques sound like 'core' elements.","d":"Working alliance, transference and resistance interpretation belong to psychodynamic practice. The working alliance was named by Ralph Greenson (1965) and generalised by Edward Bordin (1979) as goals, tasks and bond; resistance interpretation addresses the patient's unconscious opposition to change. It tempts because the alliance overlaps with Rogers's emphasis on the relationship and common-factors research cites both, but Rogers's specific list is regard, congruence and empathy."}},{"q":"Identify the antonym of 'GREGARIOUS':","concept":"'Gregarious' comes from the Latin grex, gregis, meaning a flock or herd. A gregarious person is fond of company, sociable and happiest in groups; in biology a gregarious animal lives in flocks or herds. The same root gives congregate (gather together), aggregate (collect into a mass), segregate (set apart from the flock) and egregious (literally standing out from the flock, now meaning shockingly bad). Learning roots this way lets you decode unfamiliar words in a vocabulary section. Synonyms of gregarious include sociable, outgoing, convivial (cheerfully fond of feasting and company), affable (friendly and easy to talk to), companionable and extroverted. Antonyms include reclusive (living apart from others; a recluse avoids society), solitary, unsociable, withdrawn, introverted and misanthropic (actively disliking people). A useful strategy for antonym items: if three options mean roughly the same thing as the key word, they are synonyms planted to trap a careless reader, and the odd one out is the antonym. Here sociable, affable and convivial all lie on the same side as gregarious. The word also has a psychology link. In the NEO Personality Inventory, Revised, of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992), gregariousness is one of the six facets of extraversion, alongside warmth, assertiveness, activity, excitement-seeking and positive emotions. At the clinical extreme, schizoid personality disorder involves a lack of desire for close relationships and an almost constant choice of solitary activities. Option b is right because reclusive, meaning withdrawn and avoiding company, is the opposite of gregarious.","traps":{"a":"Sociable means liking to meet and spend time with other people. It is the closest everyday synonym of gregarious, so it is the opposite of what the item asks. It tempts because the stem asks for an antonym, and a reader who skims the instruction, or who simply recognises the meaning and matches it, picks the first familiar equivalent. Always re-read whether the item asks for a synonym or an antonym.","c":"Affable means friendly, good-natured and easy to talk to, from the Latin affabilis, 'easy to speak to'. An affable person is approachable and pleasant in conversation, which places the word on the same side as gregarious, as a near-synonym. It tempts because affable is less common, and a candidate unsure of its meaning may guess that the unfamiliar word is the opposite. Knowing roots prevents this.","d":"Convivial means cheerful, friendly and fond of feasting and good company, from the Latin convivium, a feast (literally a 'living together'). It describes both people and atmospheres, as in a convivial evening. It is a synonym of gregarious, not an antonym. It tempts because it is a less familiar, formal word, and candidates often assume the hardest-looking option must be the answer in a vocabulary item."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the:","concept":"Parametric tests such as the t-test and ANOVA compare means and assume interval or ratio data, roughly normal distributions and, for some tests, equal variances. When those assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or heavily skewed scores, researchers use non-parametric (distribution-free) tests, which usually work on ranks. The Mann-Whitney U test, published by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), compares two independent groups. All scores from both groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest, the ranks are summed for each group, and U is calculated from the rank sums. If one group's scores tend to be higher, its ranks cluster at the top and U becomes extreme. It is often described as a test of medians, though strictly it tests whether one distribution tends to produce larger values. It is mathematically equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). Learn the full matching table, because examiners rotate it: independent t-test to Mann-Whitney U; paired (dependent) t-test to Wilcoxon signed-rank test; one-way ANOVA to the Kruskal-Wallis H test of William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952); repeated-measures ANOVA to the Friedman test of Milton Friedman (1937); Pearson correlation to Spearman's rho (Charles Spearman, 1904) or Kendall's tau (Maurice Kendall, 1938). Non-parametric tests are somewhat less powerful when parametric assumptions actually hold. Option a is right because the Mann-Whitney U test is the rank-based alternative to the independent samples t-test, comparing two separate groups.","traps":{"b":"The paired (dependent-samples) t-test compares two related sets of scores, such as the same patients before and after therapy. Its non-parametric equivalent is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945), which ranks the differences within each pair; the simpler sign test uses only the direction of each difference. It tempts because Wilcoxon also gave the rank-sum test, equivalent to Mann-Whitney U, so the two Wilcoxon tests are easily confused.","c":"One-way ANOVA compares the means of three or more independent groups. Its non-parametric equivalent is the Kruskal-Wallis H test of William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952), which extends the rank-sum logic of Mann-Whitney U to more than two groups. It tempts because Kruskal-Wallis is literally a generalisation of Mann-Whitney U, so both sit in the same row of the textbook table, differing only in the number of groups.","d":"The chi-square test of Karl Pearson (1900) is already a non-parametric test. It compares observed with expected frequencies in categories, either for goodness of fit or for independence in a contingency table. It works on counts, not on ranks or means, so it is not the rank-based equivalent of any t-test. It tempts because it is the best-known non-parametric test, and candidates link 'non-parametric' straight to chi-square."}},{"q":"The corticospinal tract decussates primarily at the:","concept":"The corticospinal (pyramidal) tract is the main pathway for voluntary, skilled movement. Its fibres start mainly in the primary motor cortex (Brodmann area 4 in the precentral gyrus), with contributions from premotor and somatosensory areas. They descend through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, the cerebral peduncles of the midbrain and the basilar pons, and then form two ridges on the front of the medulla called the pyramids, which give the tract its name. At the lower end of the medulla, near the junction with the spinal cord, about 85 to 90 percent of the fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal (motor) decussation and descend as the lateral corticospinal tract. The remaining fibres stay on the same side as the anterior (ventral) corticospinal tract and mostly cross at the spinal segment where they end. This crossing is why the left motor cortex controls the right side of the body. It also explains a clinical rule: an upper motor neuron lesion above the decussation, such as a stroke in the internal capsule, causes weakness on the opposite side, whereas a spinal cord lesion causes weakness on the same side below the lesion. Upper motor neuron signs include spasticity, brisk reflexes and the extensor plantar response described by Joseph Babinski (1896). Compare the sensory tracts: the dorsal column pathway also crosses in the medulla, at the sensory decussation, while the spinothalamic tract crosses within a segment or two of entering the cord. Option d is right because most corticospinal fibres decussate at the pyramids of the lower medulla.","traps":{"a":"The optic chiasm, just above the pituitary gland, is where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross, so each hemisphere receives the opposite visual field. It is a sensory crossing in the visual system and has nothing to do with motor fibres; a pituitary tumour pressing on it causes bitemporal hemianopia. It tempts because it is the most famous crossing in neuroscience, so the idea of 'crossing' cues the word 'chiasm'.","b":"Crossing near the level of entry describes the spinothalamic (anterolateral) tract for pain and temperature, whose fibres cross within a segment or two of entering the cord. Among motor fibres, only the small anterior corticospinal tract crosses in the spinal cord, near the segment where it ends. It tempts because it mixes up ascending sensory and descending motor pathways, and 'level of entry' sounds anatomically precise.","c":"The pons carries corticospinal fibres through its basilar part, where pontine nuclei split them into bundles, but the tract does not cross there. Corticopontine fibres relay through pontine nuclei to the opposite cerebellum, and the trapezoid body carries crossing auditory fibres, so crossings do occur in the pons, just not the corticospinal one. It tempts because the pons lies on the route, between the midbrain and the medulla."}},{"q":"In DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder was created in DSM-5 (2013) by merging two DSM-IV diagnoses, dysthymic disorder and chronic major depressive disorder, and it is retained in DSM-5-TR (2022). The core criterion is depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults. In children and adolescents the minimum is 1 year, and the mood may be irritable rather than sad. At least two of six further symptoms must be present: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy or fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration or difficulty making decisions, and feelings of hopelessness. During that period the person must never have been free of symptoms for more than 2 months at a time. Criteria for major depressive disorder may be met continuously, and specifiers record whether full major depressive episodes occur and whether onset was early (before age 21) or late. When a major depressive episode is superimposed on long-standing dysthymia, clinicians informally call it double depression. The 2-year adult and 1-year child pattern is shared with cyclothymic disorder, the chronic, milder relative of bipolar disorder, a pairing examiners like. Compare other durations: major depressive episode 2 weeks, disruptive mood dysregulation disorder 12 months, generalised anxiety disorder 6 months. ICD-11, adopted by the World Health Organization in 2019, also uses a 2-year minimum for dysthymic disorder. Option b is right because DSM-5-TR requires at least 2 years of depressed mood in adults and 1 year in children and adolescents.","traps":{"a":"Six months is the duration for generalised anxiety disorder and for the total course of schizophrenia, among others, but it is far too short for a chronic mood disorder. Persistent depressive disorder is defined by its long course, a minimum of 2 years in adults. It tempts because six months is the most common 'chronic' threshold in DSM-5-TR, so candidates reach for it when they remember only that the disorder is long-lasting.","c":"One year is only half the right answer: it is the minimum for children and adolescents, not for adults. Twelve months also appears in disruptive mood dysregulation disorder. The keyed answer covers both age groups, while this option gives the wrong figure for adults. It tempts because a candidate who remembers '1 year' from the child criterion, or from cyclothymic disorder in children, may apply it to adults.","d":"Five years has no role in the DSM-5-TR criteria for persistent depressive disorder. Many patients do have the disorder for far longer, often from adolescence, which is why early onset (before age 21) is a specifier, but the diagnostic minimum is 2 years. It tempts because chronic depression is often described as lasting 'for years', and a long, round number seems to fit a disorder whose name stresses persistence."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be comforted on reunion and may resist contact is classified as:","concept":"Attachment theory began with John Bowlby (1969), who argued that infants are biologically prepared to seek closeness to a caregiver who serves as a safe haven in danger and a secure base for exploration. Mary Ainsworth turned this into a measurable procedure, the Strange Situation, reported fully in Patterns of Attachment (Ainsworth, Blehar, Waters and Wall, 1978). Infants aged about 12 to 18 months go through eight short episodes in an unfamiliar playroom: mother and infant explore, a stranger enters, the mother leaves twice and returns twice, and the infant is briefly left alone. Observers code exploration, stranger anxiety, separation distress and, most importantly, reunion behaviour. Ainsworth described three patterns. Secure (B) infants use the mother as a base, may cry when she leaves, greet her actively on return, are soothed quickly and go back to play; most infants in her sample were secure. Insecure-avoidant (A) infants show little distress and ignore or turn away from the mother on reunion. Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (C) infants are wary even before separation, become very upset when she leaves, and on reunion mix contact-seeking with angry resistance, such as clinging then pushing away, and cannot be settled. Ainsworth linked the patterns to caregiver sensitivity: sensitive care with B, rejecting care with A, inconsistent care with C. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990) later added disorganised (D) infants. Option a is right because distress on separation combined with inability to be comforted and resistance to contact on reunion defines the insecure-resistant, ambivalent (C) pattern.","traps":{"b":"Insecure-avoidant (Type A) infants show little visible distress when the mother leaves and actively ignore or turn away from her when she returns, often treating the stranger much like the mother. Ainsworth linked this to caregivers who rebuff bids for comfort. Heart-rate studies suggest these infants are aroused even though they look calm. It tempts because both A and C are insecure, but avoidant infants avoid contact rather than seek and resist it.","c":"Secure (Type B) infants may cry on separation, but on reunion they greet or approach the mother, are comforted quickly and return to exploring. This is the most common pattern and reflects sensitive, responsive caregiving. The stem describes an infant who cannot be comforted and resists contact, the opposite of the secure pattern. It tempts because distress on separation is part of secure behaviour too, so reading only the first half of the stem misleads.","d":"Disorganised (Type D) infants, described by Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990), lack a coherent strategy: they may freeze, approach with the head turned away, rock, or appear dazed and fearful of the caregiver. The pattern is linked with frightened or frightening parenting and with maltreatment. It tempts because resistant infants also look chaotic, but Type C behaviour is an organised, if anxious, strategy, whereas Type D shows a breakdown of strategy."}},{"q":"In Wechsler's classification, an FSIQ in the range 70–84 falls under:","concept":"David Wechsler built the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939) and replaced the old ratio IQ with the deviation IQ: a person's score is compared with others of the same age and expressed on a scale with a mean of 100 and an SD of 15. Each descriptive band therefore corresponds to a distance from the mean. On the WAIS-IV (2008), the commonly taught classification is: 130 and above Very Superior; 120 to 129 Superior; 110 to 119 High Average; 90 to 109 Average; 80 to 89 Low Average; 70 to 79 Borderline; 69 and below Extremely Low. Labels change between editions: Wechsler's older manuals called 80 to 89 'Dull Normal', and the WISC-V (2014) relabels 70 to 79 as 'Very Low'. A span of 70 to 84 therefore does not fit one Wechsler band. Scores from 70 to 79 are Borderline, while 80 to 84 are Low Average, so the honest description is low average to borderline. Note a source of confusion: DSM-IV-TR listed borderline intellectual functioning for IQ 71 to 84, roughly 1 to 2 SD below the mean, which is a clinical category, not a Wechsler band. Intellectual disability needs more than a score: DSM-5 requires deficits in intellectual and adaptive functioning beginning in the developmental period, with IQ about 2 SD below the mean (roughly 65 to 75, allowing for measurement error), and grades severity by adaptive functioning. Option a is right because the 70 to 84 span covers both the Borderline (70 to 79) and Low Average (80 to 89) Wechsler bands, so no single label fits it.","traps":{"b":"Borderline is the Wechsler label for 70 to 79 only. Calling the whole 70 to 84 span Borderline ignores that 80 to 84 falls in the Low Average band. The range 71 to 84 does appear in DSM-IV-TR as borderline intellectual functioning, a clinical category roughly 1 to 2 SD below the mean. It tempts strongly because that DSM range nearly matches the stem, but the stem asks for Wechsler's classification.","c":"Average on the Wechsler scales is 90 to 109, the middle band, which holds about half of the population. A score of 70 to 84 lies between 1 and 2 SD below the mean, so it is clearly below average. It tempts candidates who loosely think of anything above the intellectual disability line of 70 as 'normal' or average, but the Wechsler bands make a finer distinction than normal versus disabled.","d":"Mild intellectual disability corresponded to IQ 50 to 55 up to about 70 in DSM-IV, and ICD-10 gives 50 to 69. DSM-5 requires deficits in both intellectual and adaptive functioning beginning in the developmental period, and grades severity by adaptive functioning, not by IQ alone. A score of 70 to 84 is above this range. It tempts because 70 is the familiar cut-off, and a range starting at 70 looks as if it belongs there."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, developed cognitive therapy after noticing that depressed patients had streams of negative automatic thoughts. In his 1967 book on depression he described the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless, defective, unlovable'), of the world or ongoing experience ('Everything goes wrong, people reject me'), and of the future ('It will never get better'). The third element, hopelessness, later proved in Beck's research to be a strong predictor of suicide. The triad sits on a three-level model. Core beliefs or schemas, formed early in life, are deep negative assumptions about the self. Intermediate beliefs are rules and conditional assumptions ('If I am not perfect, I am a failure'). Automatic thoughts are fast, situation-specific thoughts that pop up. Systematic cognitive distortions keep the triad alive: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Beck, Rush, Shaw and Emery set out the treatment in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979), and the Beck Depression Inventory (1961) measures severity. Examiners build variants from neighbouring models: the learned helplessness of Martin Seligman (1975); the reformulated attributional model of Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978), in which internal, stable and global explanations for failure predict depression; and the ABC model of Albert Ellis, who began rational therapy in 1955. Option d is right because Beck's cognitive triad is a negative view of the self, the world and the future.","traps":{"a":"Id, ego and superego are the three structures of personality in Sigmund Freud's structural model, set out in The Ego and the Id (1923). The id seeks pleasure, the superego carries moral standards, and the ego mediates with reality. Freud explained depression through loss and anger turned inward in Mourning and Melancholia (1917). It tempts because it is the best-known triad in psychology, and Beck himself trained as a psychoanalyst before breaking away.","b":"Past, present and future is a time-based triad that sounds plausible, because depressed people do ruminate about past failures and Beck's triad does include the future. But Beck's three targets are the self, the world and the future, defined by what is being judged, not by time. It tempts because 'future' is shared with the real triad, and candidates who half-remember the list fill in the other two with time words.","c":"Mother, father and self evokes family and psychodynamic ideas, such as the Oedipal triangle that Sigmund Freud first discussed in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900), or triangulation in family systems therapy, where a child is drawn into conflict between parents. These concern early relationships, not the content of depressive thinking. It tempts because early family experience does shape Beck's core beliefs, so the option borrows a true idea and attaches it to the wrong concept."}},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'","concept":"English verbs are often followed by a fixed preposition, called a dependent preposition, and the pairing (a collocation) must simply be learned. 'Insist' takes 'on', or the more formal 'upon', when followed by the thing demanded: 'The therapist insisted on a follow-up after two weeks.' The same pattern takes a gerund: 'She insisted on seeing the patient alone.' 'Insist' can also take a that-clause with no preposition, often with the subjunctive in formal English: 'The therapist insisted that the patient return after two weeks.' 'To' can follow 'insist' only to mark the listener, as in 'He insisted to the nurse that he had taken his tablets', never to introduce the thing demanded. Learners of Indian English often write 'insist for', 'discuss about' or 'request for' (as a verb), which are frequent exam traps. Learn the common pairs as a set: depend on, rely on, comment on, concentrate on, persist in, succeed in, consist of, accuse someone of, deprive someone of, comply with, cope with, adhere to, object to, refrain from, prevent someone from, abide by. Some words change preposition with meaning: 'agree with' a person, 'agree to' a proposal, 'agree on' a decision. Nouns may differ from their verbs: the noun 'request' takes 'for' ('a request for leave'), but the verb does not ('he requested leave'). Option c is right because 'insist' takes the preposition 'on' before the thing demanded, giving 'insisted on a follow-up'.","traps":{"a":"'Insist for' is not standard English, though it is heard in Indian English, as in the error 'He insisted for a refund.' The standard form is 'insist on' or the formal 'insist upon'. Examiners include it because it sounds natural to many Indian candidates. It tempts because it feels logical by analogy with verbs that do take 'for', such as 'ask for', 'press for' and 'call for'.","b":"'Insist with' does not introduce the thing demanded in standard English. 'With' follows verbs such as comply with, cope with, agree with (a person) and interfere with, but 'insisted with a follow-up' has no clear meaning. It tempts because the therapist and patient are interacting, so 'with' suggests a person-to-person exchange, and because 'persist with' is correct and sounds very close to 'insist'.","d":"'To' after 'insist' can only mark the person spoken to, as in 'He insisted to the doctor that he felt fine', and it is usually followed by a that-clause. It cannot introduce the thing demanded, so 'insisted to a follow-up' is wrong. It tempts because many verbs of wanting take 'to' plus an infinitive ('wanted to', 'asked to'), and candidates may confuse 'insisted to' with 'insisted on'."}},{"q":"If the p-value of a study is 0.03 and α was set at 0.05:","concept":"A significance test asks how surprising the data would be if the null hypothesis (H0) were true. The p-value is the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the one observed, assuming H0 is true. Alpha is the threshold, chosen before the study, for how much risk of a false positive (Type I error) the researcher will accept; 0.05 has been the convention since Ronald Fisher (1925). The decision rule: if p is less than or equal to alpha, reject H0 and call the result statistically significant; if p is greater than alpha, retain (fail to reject) H0. Here p = 0.03 is below 0.05, so H0 is rejected. Had alpha been set at 0.01, the same p of 0.03 would not be significant. Three misreadings are examined often. First, p is not the probability that H0 is true; it is the probability of the data given H0. Second, p is not the size or importance of an effect: a tiny, clinically trivial effect can be highly significant in a very large sample, which is why effect sizes such as Cohen's d (Jacob Cohen, 1988) and confidence intervals should be reported. Third, a significant result says nothing directly about power, which depends on alpha, sample size and effect size and should be planned in advance. A 2016 statement of the American Statistical Association, by Ronald Wasserstein and Nicole Lazar, warned against treating 0.05 as a bright line. Option b is right because p (0.03) is smaller than alpha (0.05), so the null hypothesis is rejected.","traps":{"a":"Retaining the null hypothesis is the correct decision only when p is greater than alpha, for example p = 0.08 with alpha = 0.05. Here p = 0.03 falls below 0.05, so the data are surprising enough under H0 to reject it. It tempts candidates who reverse the rule, thinking that a smaller p means weaker evidence, or who link the word 'less' with 'not enough' evidence.","c":"Calling the result not significant reverses the decision rule. A result is statistically significant when p is at or below alpha, and 0.03 is below 0.05. It would be non-significant only if alpha had been set at a stricter level such as 0.01, which is not the case here. It tempts because 0.03 is close to 0.05, and some candidates think a significant result needs p far below alpha.","d":"Power is the probability of rejecting H0 when it is actually false, equal to 1 minus beta. It depends on alpha, sample size and the true effect size, and should be planned before the study, for example at the 0.80 level recommended by Jacob Cohen (1988). A single significant p-value does not make power high; underpowered studies can still give significant results by chance. It tempts because significance and power both concern detecting effects."}},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is purely motor and supplies most extraocular muscles?","concept":"There are twelve pairs of cranial nerves, and exams test both their numbers and their function type. A classic mnemonic for function is 'Some Say Marry Money, But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More': S for sensory, M for motor and B for both, for nerves I to XII in order. Purely sensory are I (olfactory), II (optic) and VIII (vestibulocochlear). Motor are III (oculomotor), IV (trochlear), VI (abducens), XI (accessory) and XII (hypoglossal). Mixed are V (trigeminal), VII (facial), IX (glossopharyngeal) and X (vagus). Six extraocular muscles move each eye, and their nerve supply is summarised as LR6 SO4: the lateral rectus is supplied by the abducens (VI), the superior oblique by the trochlear (IV), and all the rest by the oculomotor (III). The oculomotor nerve therefore supplies four of the six: superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus and inferior oblique. It also supplies the levator palpebrae superioris, which raises the upper eyelid, and it carries parasympathetic fibres, via the ciliary ganglion, to the pupillary sphincter and ciliary muscle, controlling pupil constriction and accommodation. Those parasympathetic fibres are also outgoing (efferent), so III counts as a motor nerve with no sensory function. A complete third nerve palsy leaves the eye 'down and out', with ptosis and a dilated pupil, and compression by an aneurysm often affects the pupil early. Option c is right because the oculomotor nerve is a motor nerve that supplies most of the extraocular muscles, four of the six.","traps":{"a":"The optic nerve (II) is purely sensory: it carries visual information from the retina to the optic chiasm and on to the lateral geniculate nucleus and visual cortex. It moves no muscles, although it forms the input side of the pupillary light reflex, whose output runs through the oculomotor nerve. It tempts because it is the best-known 'eye' nerve, and a candidate who reads 'eye' and stops thinking will grab it.","b":"The trochlear nerve (IV) is motor, but it supplies only one extraocular muscle, the superior oblique (the SO4 of LR6 SO4), which mainly rotates the eye inward and depresses it when it is turned toward the nose. It is the only cranial nerve that leaves the back of the brainstem. Damage causes vertical double vision, often worse going downstairs. It tempts because it is a purely motor eye nerve, matching half the stem.","d":"The abducens nerve (VI) is motor but supplies only the lateral rectus (the LR6 of LR6 SO4), which abducts the eye, turning it outward. Palsy causes horizontal double vision and an eye that cannot look outward. Its long course along the base of the skull makes it sensitive to raised intracranial pressure, so it can be a false localising sign. It tempts because it too is a purely motor eye nerve."}},{"q":"The cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is defined in DSM-5 (2013), and in essentially the same way in DSM-5-TR (2022), by the presence of obsessions, compulsions, or both. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, urges or images experienced as intrusive and unwanted, which usually cause marked anxiety or distress; the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, praying, repeating words) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules. They aim to reduce distress or prevent a feared event, but are not realistically connected to it or are clearly excessive. For diagnosis, the obsessions or compulsions must be time-consuming (for example, more than 1 hour a day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment, and must not be due to a substance, a medical condition or another disorder. Insight specifiers range from good or fair, through poor, to absent insight or delusional beliefs, and there is a tic-related specifier. DSM-5 moved OCD out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania and excoriation disorder. Distinguish OCD from obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, a lifelong, ego-syntonic pattern of perfectionism and control without true obsessions or compulsions. Exposure and response prevention, pioneered by Victor Meyer (1966), and serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs and clomipramine are first-line treatments. Option a is right because obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming or distressing are the defining feature of OCD.","traps":{"b":"Persistent depressive symptoms define persistent depressive disorder, a chronic depressed mood lasting at least 2 years in adults. Depression is a very common complication of OCD, and many patients become demoralised by their symptoms, but low mood is not the defining feature. It tempts because comorbidity is so high that OCD case vignettes often mention depression, and 'persistent' echoes the persistent, repetitive nature of obsessions.","c":"A pervasive pattern of grandiosity, need for admiration and lack of empathy is the DSM-5-TR definition of narcissistic personality disorder, a Cluster B personality disorder. It concerns the regulation of self-esteem, not intrusive thoughts or rituals. It tempts because 'pervasive pattern' is the standard opening of personality disorder criteria, and candidates who confuse OCD with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, a Cluster C condition, may drift toward any personality disorder option.","d":"Recurrent unexpected panic attacks, followed by at least a month of worry about further attacks or maladaptive change in behaviour, define panic disorder. A panic attack is an abrupt surge of intense fear that peaks within minutes. OCD can involve intense anxiety, but it is triggered by obsessions, not unexpected. It tempts because OCD was classed among the anxiety disorders until DSM-5 (2013), so the two are still taught together."}},{"q":"The zone of proximal development is a concept proposed by:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky (1896 to 1934), a Soviet psychologist, built a sociocultural theory of development in which higher mental functions arise first between people and only later within the individual. In his words, every function in the child's cultural development appears twice, first on the social level and then on the individual level. The zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the distance between the child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the level of potential development, shown by problem solving under adult guidance or in collaboration with more capable peers. Teaching works best when aimed inside this zone: tasks the child can already do alone teach little, and tasks beyond the zone cannot be done even with help. The helper is often called the more knowledgeable other. The related term scaffolding was introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) for the temporary, adjustable support an adult gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows. Vygotsky also stressed cultural tools, especially language, and saw private speech (children talking to themselves) as thought being internalised, whereas Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) called it egocentric speech that simply fades away. The ZPD also underlies dynamic assessment, which measures how much a child gains with help rather than only what the child already knows. Much of Vygotsky's work reached English readers late, notably in Mind in Society (1978). Option c is right because the zone of proximal development is Lev Vygotsky's concept.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980) proposed four universal stages of cognitive development driven by the child's own activity, through assimilation, accommodation and equilibration. He saw the child as a lone scientist, treated social input as secondary, and held that development comes before learning, while Vygotsky argued that learning leads development. It tempts because Piaget is the first name linked with cognitive development, and the two theorists are always taught side by side.","b":"Urie Bronfenbrenner set out ecological systems theory in The Ecology of Human Development (1979). It places the child within nested environments: the microsystem (family, school), mesosystem (links between them), exosystem (settings that affect the child indirectly), macrosystem (culture) and chronosystem (change over time). It tempts because, like Vygotsky, Bronfenbrenner stressed social context, but he described environmental layers, not the gap between independent and assisted performance.","d":"Lawrence Kohlberg extended Piaget's work to moral reasoning, beginning with his 1958 doctoral research using dilemmas such as Heinz and the drug. He proposed three levels, preconventional, conventional and postconventional, each with two stages. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory for neglecting an ethic of care. It tempts because Kohlberg is another stage theorist from the same developmental chapter, but his work concerns moral judgement, not assisted learning."}},{"q":"Which projective test uses incomplete cartoon-style frames in which the respondent fills in what the depicted character is saying in a frustrating situation?","concept":"Projective tests present ambiguous or incomplete material on the assumption, called the projective hypothesis, that people reveal their needs, conflicts and ways of coping when they fill in the gaps. The Rosenzweig Picture-Frustration Study, developed by Saul Rosenzweig (1945), is a semi-structured projective test grounded in his theory of frustration and aggression. It presents 24 cartoon-like pictures, each showing two people in a mildly frustrating everyday situation, such as a pedestrian being splashed by a passing car. One character says something frustrating or blaming, shown in a speech balloon; the second character's balloon is empty, and the respondent writes what that person would reply. Each response is scored on two dimensions. Direction of aggression: extrapunitive (blaming others or the environment), intropunitive (blaming oneself) or impunitive (blaming no one and glossing over the problem). Type of reaction: obstacle-dominance (focus on the barrier), ego-defence (protecting the self) or need-persistence (seeking a solution). A group conformity rating compares the person's answers with typical answers, and there are adult and children's forms. Compare the other projective tests: the Rorschach inkblots (Hermann Rorschach, 1921); the Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935), which asks for whole stories about pictures; and sentence completion tests such as the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank (Julian Rotter, 1950). Projective tests differ in structure: the P-F Study and sentence completion are far more structured than the Rorschach. Option b is right because the Rosenzweig P-F Study uses cartoon frames in which the respondent supplies what a frustrated character says.","traps":{"a":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray (1935), presents ambiguous drawings of people in uncertain situations. The respondent tells a whole story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and how it ends. Stories are analysed for needs and environmental presses. It tempts because both tests use pictures of people and rely on identification with a character, but the TAT has no speech balloons.","c":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test, published by Hermann Rorschach (1921), presents ten symmetrical inkblots, five containing colour, and asks 'What might this be?' Responses are scored for location, determinants such as form, colour and shading, and content, often through the Comprehensive System of John Exner (1974) or newer systems. It tempts because it is the most famous projective test, but its stimuli are abstract blots, not cartoon scenes of frustration.","d":"Sentence completion tests present verbal stems such as 'My mother...' or 'I am afraid of...' that the respondent finishes in writing. The best known is the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank (Julian Rotter, 1950), scored for conflict and adjustment. It tempts because, as in the P-F Study, the respondent completes something in their own words and the format is semi-structured, but the stimuli are written phrases, not cartoon pictures of frustrating situations."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:","concept":"Systematic desensitisation was developed by the South African psychiatrist Joseph Wolpe and set out in his book Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He borrowed the term reciprocal inhibition from the neurophysiology of Charles Sherrington (early 1900s), where activating one muscle inhibits its antagonist. Wolpe's version is psychological: two incompatible responses cannot occur at the same time, so if a response that is antagonistic to anxiety is made to occur in the presence of a feared stimulus, the anxiety is suppressed and the bond between stimulus and anxiety weakens. This is counterconditioning, a classical (respondent) conditioning method. Wolpe first showed it with cats given an experimental neurosis through shock; he reduced their fear by feeding them in settings progressively closer to the original cage. Mary Cover Jones (1924) had done something similar with a boy called Peter, feeding him while a rabbit was moved gradually nearer. The clinical procedure has three steps: training in deep muscle relaxation (adapted from the progressive relaxation of Edmund Jacobson, 1929), building an anxiety hierarchy ranked in subjective units of disturbance (SUDs, 0 to 100), and pairing relaxation with each hierarchy item in imagination, moving up only when the current item no longer provokes anxiety. Wolpe also used assertive and sexual responses as anxiety antagonists. Contrast flooding and implosive therapy, which give intense exposure without relaxation and rely on extinction; aversion therapy, which pairs an unwanted behaviour with discomfort; and participant modelling. Option b is right because Wolpe explicitly grounded systematic desensitisation in reciprocal inhibition: relaxation is incompatible with anxiety and so inhibits it.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction is the decline of a response when the reinforcement that maintained it is withheld, a principle from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning; ignoring a child's tantrums is the classic example. Systematic desensitisation does not target a reinforced behaviour; it changes a classically conditioned fear by attaching a new, incompatible response. It tempts because exposure therapies are often explained today in terms of extinction, and 'extinction' sounds like a fear reduction mechanism.","c":"Modelling is observational learning, described by Albert Bandura in social learning theory, as in the Bobo doll studies of 1961. In therapy, participant modelling has the client watch a model handle the feared object and then copy each step. It reduces fear through vicarious learning and self-efficacy, not by pairing relaxation with a hierarchy. It tempts because Bandura also treated phobias, so both methods appear in the same behaviour therapy chapter.","d":"Habituation is the simplest form of non-associative learning: a response weakens when the same harmless stimulus is repeated, like no longer noticing a ticking clock. Some later researchers argued that exposure works partly through habituation, but Wolpe did not base his technique on it, and the word 'only' makes the option extreme. It tempts because anxiety in desensitisation does fade over repeated presentations, which looks like habituation."}},{"q":"Identify the synonym of 'EPHEMERAL':","concept":"Ephemeral comes from the Greek ephemeros, 'lasting only a day', built from epi (on) and hemera (day). It was first used for fevers that lasted a day and for insects such as mayflies, whose order is still called Ephemeroptera because the adults live only a few hours to a day or two. In modern English it describes anything short-lived: ephemeral fame, an ephemeral mood, ephemeral social media posts. The core meaning is about duration, not about quality, brightness or age. Its synonym cluster is transient, transitory, fleeting, evanescent, momentary, short-lived and passing. Its antonym cluster is permanent, enduring, lasting, perpetual, everlasting and eternal. Learn the two clusters as a pair, because exams flip the same word into an antonym question. Watch the look-alikes: ethereal means delicate or heavenly, and an ephemeris is an astronomical table of positions. Psychology uses the same idea. Sensory memory is ephemeral, since George Sperling (1960) showed that iconic traces fade within about a second, and transient global amnesia and a transient ischaemic attack are named for being temporary. A reliable method for synonym items is to define the word in your own words before reading the options, then remove the antonym, then remove options that describe a different dimension (intensity, age, size). Whatever survives and matches your definition is the answer. Option c is right because transient means lasting only a short time, which is exactly what ephemeral means.","traps":{"a":"Vivid means bright, intense or strikingly clear, as in vivid colours or a vivid memory. It describes intensity and clarity, not how long something lasts. It tempts because short-lived experiences such as dreams are often described as vivid, so the two words turn up in the same sentences. In psychology, Roger Brown and James Kulik (1977) called vivid recollections of shocking events flashbulb memories, which feel anything but short-lived.","b":"Permanent means lasting indefinitely without change, the exact opposite of ephemeral. It is the antonym trap: examiners place the opposite word among the options because a candidate who senses that ephemeral is about duration, but is unsure of its direction, may pick it. If the question asked for the antonym, permanent (or enduring, perpetual) would be correct. Always check whether the stem asks for a synonym or an antonym.","d":"Ancient means belonging to the very distant past, or very old, as in ancient civilisations. It concerns age, how long ago something began, not how briefly it lasts; an ancient monument has in fact endured. It tempts because ephemeral sounds learned and Greek, and its root is about time, so a candidate guessing from the 'old' feel of the word may link it with antiquity."}},{"q":"A Solomon four-group design is used to control for:","concept":"The Solomon four-group design was proposed by Richard L. Solomon (1949) and later listed by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley (1963) among the true experimental designs. It solves a specific problem of the ordinary pretest-posttest control group design: the pretest itself may change how participants react to the treatment. Someone who has just filled in a prejudice questionnaire may watch an anti-prejudice film more attentively, so the film appears to work partly because the pretest primed people. Campbell and Stanley called this the reactive or interaction effect of testing, a threat to external validity, because the results may not generalise to people who are never pretested. Solomon's answer was four randomly assigned groups: group 1 is pretested, treated and posttested; group 2 is pretested and posttested without treatment; group 3 is treated and posttested with no pretest; group 4 receives only the posttest. Posttest scores are then analysed as a 2 x 2 factorial (pretest yes or no by treatment yes or no). The treatment main effect gives the effect of the intervention, the pretest main effect shows the simple testing effect, and the interaction shows pretest sensitisation. Its costs are a doubled sample and a more complex analysis. Neighbouring answers: the posttest-only control group design avoids sensitisation but cannot measure it; random assignment handles selection; any control group handles history and maturation; blinding handles experimenter bias. Option b is right because the two extra unpretested groups exist precisely to detect and control pretest sensitisation.","traps":{"a":"Maturation is a threat to internal validity listed by Campbell and Stanley (1963): natural changes inside participants over time, such as growing older, tired or hungry, that could mimic a treatment effect. It is controlled by any randomly assigned control group, which matures at the same rate, so it needs no special four-group structure. The word 'only' also makes the option wrong. It tempts because maturation is one of the first threats students memorise.","c":"Selection bias occurs when groups differ before treatment because of how participants were chosen or assigned, for example volunteers ending up in the treatment group. It is controlled by random assignment, which the Solomon design uses but shares with every true experiment, so it is not the design's distinctive purpose. The word 'only' makes the option doubly wrong. It tempts because selection is the best-known threat to internal validity.","d":"Researcher or experimenter bias is the influence of the investigator's expectations on results, shown in Robert Rosenthal's experimenter expectancy studies of the 1960s. It is controlled by double-blind procedures, standardised instructions and objective scoring, not by adding unpretested groups. The word 'only' also makes the option extreme. It tempts because bias of every kind is a familiar target in research design questions, and 'bias' sounds like what a clever design should control."}},{"q":"Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus typically produces:","concept":"The hypothalamus regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, sleep and hormones, and hunger questions rest on the dual-centre model summarised by Eliot Stellar (1954). Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940) found that bilateral lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) in rats produced massive overeating (hyperphagia) and obesity, so the VMH was called the satiety or 'stop eating' centre. Bal K. Anand and John Brobeck (1951) then showed that lesions of the lateral hypothalamus (LH) caused aphagia (refusal to eat) and adipsia, with often fatal weight loss, so the LH was called the feeding centre. The VMH syndrome has two phases: a dynamic phase of rapid weight gain, then a static phase in which weight levels off at a new, higher point that the animal defends, often while becoming finicky about taste. Later work complicated the picture. VMH lesions raise insulin release, which pushes the body to store calories as fat, and damage to nearby fibres, including those linked to the paraventricular nucleus, contributes. Today the arcuate nucleus, which responds to leptin and ghrelin, is seen as a key integrator. Neighbouring hypothalamic functions that examiners mix in: the preoptic and anterior hypothalamus handle heat loss and promote sleep, the posterior hypothalamus conserves heat, the suprachiasmatic nucleus sets circadian rhythms, and orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus maintain wakefulness, their loss causing narcolepsy. Option c is right because removing the VMH satiety signal produces hyperphagia and obesity.","traps":{"a":"Aphagia (refusal to eat) and weight loss follow lesions of the lateral hypothalamus, the so-called feeding centre, as shown by Bal K. Anand and John Brobeck (1951). It is the mirror image of the VMH syndrome, so it is the classic reversal trap: the right symptoms for the wrong nucleus. Candidates who remember that hypothalamic damage disturbs eating, but mix up 'lateral' and 'ventromedial', choose it.","b":"Insomnia after hypothalamic damage is linked to the preoptic area, especially the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus, whose sleep-promoting neurons inhibit the arousal systems; lesions there leave animals sleeping very little. The VMH is concerned with feeding and some aspects of aggression, not sleep. It tempts because the hypothalamus does control sleep and waking, and Constantin von Economo described hypothalamic lesions causing both insomnia and excessive sleep.","d":"Thermoregulation is controlled mainly by the preoptic area and anterior hypothalamus, which detect warming and trigger heat loss such as sweating and vasodilation, and by the posterior hypothalamus, which triggers heat conservation such as shivering. Damage there disturbs body temperature. The VMH is not the temperature centre. It tempts because temperature control is a textbook hypothalamic function, and a candidate may assume any hypothalamic lesion affects it."}},{"q":"A patient presents with sudden inability to recall important autobiographical information, beyond ordinary forgetting, with no medical cause. The most likely diagnosis is:","concept":"Dissociative amnesia is a dissociative disorder in DSM-5 (2013) and DSM-5-TR (2022), and it is also listed in ICD-11. DSM-III called it psychogenic amnesia. The DSM criteria are: an inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is inconsistent with ordinary forgetting; clinically significant distress or impairment; and no better explanation by a substance, a neurological or other medical condition (head injury, seizures, transient global amnesia), or another mental disorder such as dissociative identity disorder or PTSD. The loss is mainly retrograde and personal (episodic); general knowledge, language and skills usually stay intact, and the person can learn new information. Forms include localised amnesia for a specific period (the most common), selective amnesia for parts of that period, and generalised amnesia for the whole life history, which is rare. A specifier, with dissociative fugue, covers purposeful travel or bewildered wandering linked to the amnesia. Organic amnesias look different. They usually impair new learning (anterograde amnesia), have a demonstrable medical cause, and rarely remove personal identity while sparing other memory. Korsakoff syndrome, transient global amnesia and Alzheimer's disease are the standard contrasts, and the clues that separate them are sudden versus gradual onset, anterograde versus autobiographical loss, and organic versus stress-linked cause. Option d is right because the stem gives the three defining features of dissociative amnesia: loss of important autobiographical memory, loss too great for normal forgetting, and no medical cause.","traps":{"a":"Transient global amnesia is a sudden episode of mainly anterograde amnesia, usually in middle-aged or older adults, lasting hours and resolving within 24 hours. The person repeatedly asks the same questions but keeps personal identity. It is presumed neurological, sometimes triggered by exertion, emotional stress or cold water, and DSM lists it as an exclusion for dissociative amnesia. It tempts because 'sudden' appears in the stem, but the stem specifies autobiographical loss.","b":"Korsakoff syndrome, described by Sergei Korsakoff in the 1880s, is a chronic amnestic disorder caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, usually from alcohol use, often after Wernicke encephalopathy (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia). Damage to the mammillary bodies and thalamus causes severe anterograde amnesia, some retrograde loss and confabulation. It has a clear medical cause, which the stem rules out. It tempts as the best-known amnesia in exam lists.","c":"Alzheimer's disease, first described by Alois Alzheimer in 1906, is a progressive neurodegenerative dementia with amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles. Onset is gradual, recent memory fails first, and other domains such as language and orientation decline over years. It is a medical cause by definition and is neither sudden nor limited to personal history. It tempts because memory loss is its best-known symptom and it is the commonest cause of dementia."}},{"q":"Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the immediate family in the:","concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner set out ecological systems theory in The Ecology of Human Development (1979), picturing the child at the centre of nested environments, like a set of Russian dolls. The microsystem is the innermost layer: the settings in which the child personally takes part in face-to-face relationships and activities, such as the family, the classroom, the peer group, the neighbourhood and day care. In an Indian joint family, grandparents living in the household belong here too. Influence is bidirectional: a fussy infant shapes the parents' behaviour as much as they shape the infant. The mesosystem is the set of links between microsystems, for example parent-teacher contact, or how home and peer group pull together or apart. The exosystem includes settings the child does not enter but which still affect them, such as a parent's workplace, the school board or local health services. The macrosystem is the overarching culture: values, laws, religion, economic conditions and ideology. Later he added the chronosystem, the dimension of time, covering transitions such as a divorce or a move and wider historical events. In his later bioecological model, developed with Pamela Morris (1998), he stressed proximal processes (regular, increasingly complex interactions with people and objects) and the Process-Person-Context-Time framework, which adds the child's own biological characteristics. Examiners usually test the layers by example, so practise placing each: parent's office (exosystem), parent-teacher meeting (mesosystem), cultural norms on gender (macrosystem). Option d is right because the immediate family is the closest setting of direct, daily, face-to-face interaction, which defines the microsystem.","traps":{"a":"The macrosystem is the outermost layer in Urie Bronfenbrenner's (1979) model: the culture's values, laws, customs, religion, economic system and ideology, for example norms about caste, gender roles or collectivism. It shapes the family indirectly by setting the expectations that all microsystems operate within, but it is not the family itself. It tempts because family values come from culture, so candidates may confuse the family with the cultural norms it carries.","b":"The mesosystem is the network of connections between two or more microsystems in which the child takes part, such as the relationship between parents and teachers, or between the family and the peer group. The family on its own is a single microsystem; the mesosystem appears only when it interacts with another setting. It tempts because 'meso' (middle) sounds close to the child, and because the family appears in most mesosystem examples.","c":"The exosystem contains settings that the child does not directly participate in but that still affect them, such as a parent's workplace, the extended family's social network, the local government or the school management. A parent's job stress, for example, can change how the parent treats the child at home. The family itself is a direct setting, not an indirect one. It tempts because exosystem examples usually mention parents."}},{"q":"An item discrimination index (D) of −0.20 indicates that the item:","concept":"Item analysis in classical test theory judges each item by two numbers. The difficulty index (p) is the proportion of examinees who answer correctly, so a high p means an easy item. The discrimination index (D) shows whether the item separates strong from weak examinees. To compute it, rank everyone by total score and take upper and lower groups, conventionally the top and bottom 27 percent, a figure Truman Kelley (1939) showed to be efficient. Then D = (U - L) / n, where U and L are the numbers answering correctly in the upper and lower groups and n is the size of one group. D runs from -1 to +1. Widely used rules of thumb read: 0.40 and above very good, 0.30 to 0.39 reasonably good, 0.20 to 0.29 marginal and in need of improvement, below 0.20 poor. A value near zero means the item does not discriminate. A negative value means more low scorers than high scorers got it right. For example, with 40 people per group, if 10 in the upper group and 18 in the lower group answer correctly, D = (10 - 18) / 40 = -0.20. Usual causes are a wrong key, ambiguous wording that able students over-read, or an arguably correct distractor. Distractor analysis helps: a good distractor attracts more lower-group than upper-group examinees. The point-biserial correlation between item and total score is an alternative discrimination statistic. Option d is right because a negative D signals a malfunctioning, probably miskeyed or ambiguous item that must be revised or discarded.","traps":{"a":"A well-functioning item has a clearly positive D, ideally 0.30 or more, meaning high scorers succeed on it more often than low scorers, so it agrees with the test as a whole. A negative D is the opposite: the item works against the total score and lowers reliability. Retaining it would penalise able examinees. It tempts candidates who read the absolute value, 0.20, and recall it as the 'acceptable' cut-off.","b":"Ease is shown by the difficulty index p, the proportion answering correctly; a very easy item has p near 1.0, often above about 0.90. Very easy items do tend to have low D, because nearly everyone in both groups passes, but then D approaches zero rather than turning negative. Difficulty and discrimination are separate indices. It tempts because candidates confuse the two indices or assume any low D means 'too easy'.","c":"Ideal difficulty refers to p, not D. For maximum discrimination p is around 0.50; for a four-option multiple-choice item, allowing for guessing, the target is about midway between chance (0.25) and 1.0, roughly 0.62. A discrimination value says nothing about whether difficulty is ideal. It tempts because 0.20 looks like a plausible proportion and the option uses the familiar phrase 'ideal difficulty'."}},{"q":"The ABC model in Ellis' REBT stands for:","concept":"Rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) was founded by Albert Ellis, who began practising it as Rational Therapy in 1955; it was later called rational emotive therapy and, from 1993, REBT. It is generally counted as the first of the modern cognitive-behavioural therapies. Its central idea comes from the Stoic philosopher Epictetus: people are disturbed not by things but by the views they take of them. The ABC model expresses this. A is the activating event (or adversity), such as failing an exam. B is the belief system about A, which may be rational (flexible preferences: 'I would like to pass') or irrational ('I must pass, or I am worthless'). C is the emotional and behavioural consequence, such as depression and avoidance, or healthy disappointment and renewed effort. The key teaching point is that A does not cause C directly; B does. Ellis grouped irrational beliefs into demandingness (rigid musts and shoulds, which he mocked as 'musturbation'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance ('I can't stand it') and global rating of self or others. Therapy adds D, disputing, in which the therapist actively challenges beliefs on empirical, logical and pragmatic grounds, and E, the effective new philosophy that results. Contrast the cognitive therapy that Aaron T. Beck developed in the 1960s, which targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through collaborative empiricism and Socratic questioning rather than forceful disputation. Also separate the behavioural ABC of applied behaviour analysis (antecedent, behaviour, consequence), used in functional analysis. Option c is right because in REBT the letters stand for Activating event, Beliefs and Consequences.","traps":{"a":"This version keeps A (activating event) and C (consequence) but replaces Beliefs with Behaviour, deleting the cognitive core of Albert Ellis's model. In REBT, behaviour is part of C, one of the consequences produced by beliefs, not the middle link. The whole point of the model is that beliefs mediate between events and reactions. It tempts because it is two-thirds correct and 'behaviour' appears in the therapy's name.","b":"Antecedent, Behaviour, Consequence is the ABC of applied behaviour analysis, built on B. F. Skinner's three-term contingency: a discriminative stimulus or antecedent, the response, and the reinforcing or punishing consequence. Clinicians use it in functional analysis to find what triggers and maintains a problem behaviour. It contains no beliefs, so it is not Ellis's model. It tempts because both are called the ABC model and share the letters A and C.","d":"Affect, behaviour and cognition are the three components of an attitude in the tripartite or ABC model of attitudes used in social psychology, associated with Milton Rosenberg and Carl Hovland (1960). They describe parts of an attitude, not a causal sequence from event to reaction. It tempts because it is another well-known 'ABC' acronym and includes cognition, which sounds like REBT's beliefs."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with the correct subject-verb agreement:","concept":"Subject-verb agreement means a verb must match the number of its grammatical subject: singular subject, singular verb; plural subject, plural verb. The commonest trap, called agreement by attraction or the proximity error, is a plural noun sitting between the subject and the verb. In 'Each of the participants was given a consent form', the subject is the pronoun each, which is distributive and singular: it picks out the participants one at a time. The phrase 'of the participants' is a prepositional phrase that modifies each, and a noun inside a prepositional phrase never controls the verb. Strip it out mentally and you get 'Each was given a consent form', which is clearly correct. The same rule applies to every, either, neither, one, everyone, everybody, anyone, someone, somebody and nobody. Phrases like 'as well as', 'along with' and 'together with' also do not change the number: 'The researcher, along with her assistants, was present'. Note the reverse case: when each follows a plural subject, the verb stays plural, as in 'The participants each have a form'. Other high-yield rules: 'a number of' takes a plural verb, while 'the number of' takes a singular one; with 'either/or' and 'neither/nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject; and in formal and APA-style writing 'data' is plural ('the data were'). Option d is right because the singular subject each requires the singular passive verb 'was given'.","traps":{"a":"'Each of the participants were given a consent form' makes the verb agree with participants, the plural noun nearest to it, instead of with the true subject each. This is the classic attraction error, and it is very common in speech, which is why it sounds acceptable. The passive structure 'were given' is fine; only the number is wrong. It tempts because the ear hears 'participants were' as a natural pair.","b":"'Each of the participants are given a consent form' has the same attraction error in the present tense: are is plural, while each is singular. The correct present-tense version would be 'Each of the participants is given a consent form'. It tempts because a candidate may think the problem in the other sentences is tense rather than number, and so picks the one with a different tense.","c":"'Each of the participants have given a consent form' has two faults. The verb have is plural, so it fails agreement with the singular each; the correct form would be has. It also switches to the active present perfect, which changes the meaning: the participants did the giving, rather than receiving the form. It tempts because it looks different from the other wrong options and the error is easy to miss when reading quickly."}},{"q":"Cluster sampling differs from stratified sampling primarily in that:","concept":"Stratified and cluster sampling are both probability methods: every unit has a known, non-zero chance of selection. Both start by dividing the population into groups, which is why they are confused, but they use the groups in opposite ways. In stratified sampling, the researcher divides the population into strata on a variable that matters, such as gender, caste, or urban versus rural residence, and then draws a random sample from every stratum, either in proportion to its size (proportionate) or deliberately oversampling small strata (disproportionate). Strata should be internally similar and different from each other; the method improves precision and guarantees that small subgroups are represented. In cluster sampling, the population is already divided into naturally occurring groups, such as schools, villages, hospital wards or city blocks. The researcher randomly selects some clusters and then studies all units inside them (one-stage) or a random subsample inside them (two-stage). Adding further levels gives multistage sampling, as when a survey selects districts, then villages, then households. The main advantages are cost and convenience, and a full list is needed only for the selected clusters, not for every individual. The weakness is lower precision, because people within a cluster resemble each other; Leslie Kish (1965) called the resulting loss the design effect. Memory aid: stratified means all groups, some members; cluster means some groups, all members. Option c is right because cluster sampling randomly selects whole natural groups and then usually includes every unit within the chosen groups.","traps":{"a":"This option simply swaps the two definitions. In stratified sampling every stratum is included and individuals are randomly chosen within each; in cluster sampling whole groups are chosen at random. Reversal options are the commonest way examiners test this pair, and they catch candidates who remember that 'one method picks groups, the other picks within groups' but not which is which. Use the memory aid: stratified, all groups; cluster, all members.","b":"Non-probability methods, such as convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling, do not give each unit a known chance of selection. Stratified and cluster sampling are both probability methods because random selection operates at some stage. The confusion usually comes from quota sampling, which resembles stratified sampling in dividing people into subgroups but fills each quota non-randomly, with whoever is available. That resemblance is why this option tempts.","d":"A complete list of every individual, the sampling frame, is a requirement of simple random and systematic sampling, and stratified sampling needs individuals identified within each stratum. Cluster sampling is used precisely when such a list is unavailable or too costly: one needs only a list of clusters, then a list of members of the chosen clusters. The option states the reverse of cluster sampling's main advantage. It tempts because sampling frames are a familiar textbook requirement."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a basal ganglia structure?","concept":"The basal ganglia are a group of interconnected nuclei deep in the forebrain and midbrain that work with the cortex and thalamus to select and start wanted movements and suppress unwanted ones; they also support habit and procedural learning and reward. The core structures are the striatum, made of the caudate nucleus and putamen (the ventral striatum includes the nucleus accumbens), the globus pallidus with external and internal segments (putamen plus globus pallidus form the lentiform nucleus), the subthalamic nucleus, and the substantia nigra. The last two lie outside the cerebral hemispheres, in the diencephalon and midbrain, yet are counted as basal ganglia because they belong to the same circuits. Two pathways matter for exams. The direct pathway, from striatum to the internal globus pallidus and substantia nigra pars reticulata, releases the thalamus and facilitates movement; the indirect pathway, through the external pallidum and subthalamic nucleus, suppresses movement. Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta boosts the direct pathway and dampens the indirect one. Clinical links follow: loss of nigral dopamine neurons causes Parkinson's disease (resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia); degeneration of the caudate causes Huntington's disease with chorea; a subthalamic lesion causes contralateral hemiballismus. The red nucleus also lies in the midbrain, but it belongs to a different motor system: it receives input from the cerebellum and motor cortex and sends the rubrospinal tract to the spinal cord. Option c is right because the red nucleus is a midbrain tegmental nucleus of the cerebellar and rubrospinal system, not a basal ganglia structure.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus is a C-shaped nucleus curving alongside the lateral ventricle. With the putamen it forms the striatum, the main input station of the basal ganglia, receiving cortical and dopaminergic input and taking part in movement, learning and goal-directed behaviour. Its degeneration is the hallmark of Huntington's disease. It is clearly a basal ganglia structure, so it cannot answer a NOT question; it tempts only if the 'NOT' is overlooked.","b":"The globus pallidus lies medial to the putamen and has external and internal segments. The internal segment, with the substantia nigra pars reticulata, is the main output nucleus of the basal ganglia, inhibiting the thalamus. With the putamen it forms the lentiform nucleus, and it is a deep brain stimulation target in Parkinson's disease. It is a core basal ganglia structure; it tempts only candidates who think of the striatum alone as the basal ganglia.","d":"The subthalamic nucleus is a small lens-shaped nucleus in the diencephalon, below the thalamus. It is part of the indirect pathway, exciting the internal globus pallidus to brake movement. Damage causes hemiballismus, violent flinging of the opposite limbs, and it is the commonest target of deep brain stimulation for Parkinson's disease. It tempts because its location outside the cerebrum makes it look like an outsider, but functionally it is a basal ganglia nucleus."}},{"q":"ICD-11 places 'gaming disorder' under:","concept":"ICD-11 was adopted by the World Health Assembly in 2019 and came into effect on 1 January 2022. In its chapter on mental, behavioural or neurodevelopmental disorders, one grouping is titled 'Disorders due to substance use or addictive behaviours'. It has two parts: disorders due to substance use (alcohol, cannabis, opioids and so on) and disorders due to addictive behaviours, which contains gambling disorder and gaming disorder, each specified as predominantly online or predominantly offline. Strictly, gaming disorder was new in ICD-11; ICD-10 had no such diagnosis. It requires a persistent pattern of gaming with three features: impaired control over gaming (when to start, how often, how long, when to stop), increasing priority given to gaming over other interests and daily activities, and continuation or escalation despite negative consequences, with significant impairment in personal, family, social, educational or occupational life. The pattern is normally evident for at least 12 months, though a shorter period is acceptable if all features are present and severe. A related code, hazardous gaming, sits in the chapter on factors influencing health status, for risky gaming that falls short of the disorder. Gambling also moved: ICD-10 listed pathological gambling under habit and impulse disorders. Compare DSM-5 (2013), which placed gambling disorder in 'Substance-Related and Addictive Disorders' but left internet gaming disorder in Section III as a condition for further study. Compulsive sexual behaviour disorder, by contrast, went to impulse control disorders. Option a is right because ICD-11 classifies gaming disorder under disorders due to substance use or addictive behaviours.","traps":{"b":"'Disorders specifically associated with stress' is the ICD-11 grouping that holds post-traumatic stress disorder, complex PTSD (new in ICD-11), prolonged grief disorder (also new), adjustment disorder, reactive attachment disorder and disinhibited social engagement disorder. Each requires exposure to a stressful or traumatic event as a defining feature. Gaming disorder needs no such stressor. It tempts because heavy gaming is often described as a way of coping with stress.","c":"Mood disorders in ICD-11 cover depressive disorders (single episode, recurrent, dysthymic, mixed depressive and anxiety disorder) and bipolar or related disorders (bipolar type I, bipolar type II, cyclothymic disorder). They are defined by disturbances of mood, not by a behaviour pattern with impaired control. Gaming disorder may co-occur with depression, which is why this distractor seems plausible, but comorbidity does not decide classification.","d":"Impulse control disorders in ICD-11 include pyromania, kleptomania, compulsive sexual behaviour disorder and intermittent explosive disorder, all marked by repeated failure to resist an impulse or urge. Gaming disorder was deliberately placed with addictive behaviours instead, alongside gambling disorder. The word 'only' adds a second error. It tempts because ICD-10 kept pathological gambling among habit and impulse disorders, so older textbooks link behavioural addictions with impulse control."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's preconventional level of moral reasoning is dominated by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg built his theory in his 1958 doctoral dissertation, extending Jean Piaget's The Moral Judgment of the Child (1932). He presented boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the yes or no choice. He described three levels, each with two stages, passed through in an invariant sequence. Preconventional (Level I, typical of most children under about 9): Stage 1, punishment and obedience orientation, in which right is whatever avoids punishment by powerful authorities; Stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange (instrumental hedonism), in which right is what serves one's own interests, with fair deals of the 'you scratch my back, I'll scratch yours' kind. Conventional (Level II, most adolescents and adults): Stage 3, good boy and good girl orientation, seeking approval and keeping relationships; Stage 4, law and order, maintaining the social system and doing one's duty. Postconventional (Level III, a minority of adults): Stage 5, social contract and individual rights; Stage 6, universal ethical principles, so rare that it was later dropped from the scoring manual. Main critics: Carol Gilligan (1982), who argued that the scheme favours a male ethic of justice over an ethic of care, and cross-cultural researchers who found that it undervalues collectivist reasoning. Option a is right because the preconventional level combines Stage 1 punishment avoidance with Stage 2 instrumental exchange.","traps":{"b":"Maintaining laws and social order is Stage 4, the law and order or social system orientation, the higher stage of Kohlberg's conventional level. The person reasons that rules must be obeyed because society would collapse if everyone broke them, and duty matters more than personal gain. It belongs to Level II, not Level I. It tempts because Stage 1 also involves obeying rules, but there the motive is avoiding punishment, not upholding the system.","c":"Social contract (Stage 5) and universal ethical principles (Stage 6) make up the postconventional or principled level, the highest in Kohlberg's scheme. Here laws are seen as agreements that can be changed when they fail to protect rights, and conscience follows self-chosen principles such as justice and human dignity. It is the opposite end of the scale from preconventional reasoning. It tempts candidates who mix up the order of the level names.","d":"Conformity to peer and group expectations is Stage 3, the 'good boy, good girl' or interpersonal concordance orientation, the first stage of the conventional level. The person wants approval and to be seen as nice, loyal and trustworthy by family and friends. It tempts because adolescent peer conformity seems immature, yet in Kohlberg's scheme it is already a step above the self-interest of the preconventional level."}},{"q":"Cattell's distinction between fluid and crystallised intelligence is operationalised in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is best measured by:","concept":"Raymond B. Cattell (1943) split general intelligence into two broad abilities. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve new problems that cannot be solved by drawing on what you already know: seeing relationships, inductive and deductive reasoning, working with unfamiliar patterns. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the knowledge and skill acquired through education and culture: vocabulary, general information, verbal comprehension. Cattell's image was that fluid ability 'crystallises' into knowledge through learning. John Horn (Horn and Cattell, 1966) expanded the theory with further broad abilities, and John B. Carroll (1993), after re-analysing more than 460 data sets, proposed a three-stratum hierarchy with g at the top. In the late 1990s Kevin McGrew combined the two into the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, now the main framework for building and interpreting IQ tests. The two abilities develop differently: Gf peaks in early adulthood and declines with age, while Gc is maintained or keeps rising into late adulthood. Gc is also more culture-loaded, so it varies with schooling and language. On the Wechsler scales, Vocabulary, Information and Similarities load on Gc and form the Verbal Comprehension Index. Matrix Reasoning and Figure Weights tap Gf; Raven's Progressive Matrices and Cattell's own Culture Fair Intelligence Test are classic Gf measures. Block Design and Visual Puzzles mainly measure visual processing (Gv), and Coding and Symbol Search measure processing speed (Gs). Option d is right because vocabulary and information subtests directly sample knowledge acquired through education and culture, which is crystallised intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Block Design asks the examinee to reproduce red-and-white patterns with blocks under time pressure. In the CHC model it mainly measures visual processing (Gv), with some fluid reasoning and speed, and on the WAIS-IV it falls in the Perceptual Reasoning Index. It needs little acquired verbal knowledge, so it is a poor Gc measure. It tempts because it is one of the most famous Wechsler subtests, and candidates link any well-known subtest with intelligence in general.","b":"Matrix Reasoning presents an incomplete pattern grid and asks which option completes it, modelled on John C. Raven's Progressive Matrices (1938). It is the prototype of fluid intelligence (Gf), demanding inductive reasoning with novel, non-verbal material and minimal prior knowledge. That makes it the opposite of crystallised ability. It tempts because it is the most famous 'pure intelligence' task, and candidates who reverse Gf and Gc choose it.","c":"Coding (digit-symbol substitution) requires copying symbols paired with numbers as fast as possible within a time limit. In CHC terms it measures processing speed (Gs), along with visual-motor coordination and attention, and on the Wechsler scales it forms the Processing Speed Index with Symbol Search. It is sensitive to brain injury and ageing but samples little stored knowledge. It tempts because it uses digits, which may seem academic or learned."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha M. Linehan at the University of Washington for chronically suicidal, self-harming women who met criteria for borderline personality disorder (BPD). Standard cognitive behaviour therapy had struggled with them, because a constant push for change felt invalidating and led to dropout. Her first randomised trial was published in 1991 and the treatment manuals in 1993. DBT rests on biosocial theory: BPD develops when a biological emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, intense reactions, slow return to baseline) transacts with an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotional expression. The 'dialectic' is the balance of acceptance and change: validation and mindfulness, drawn from Zen practice, are held together with behavioural problem solving. Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, group skills training, telephone coaching and a therapist consultation team. The skills group teaches four modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Sessions follow a target hierarchy (life-threatening behaviour first, then therapy-interfering behaviour, then quality-of-life problems) and use diary cards and chain analysis of each episode of self-harm. DBT is a 'third wave' behaviour therapy, alongside acceptance and commitment therapy and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy. Other evidence-based BPD treatments include mentalization-based treatment, transference-focused psychotherapy and schema therapy, but each grew from a different model of the disorder. DBT has since been adapted for adolescents, eating disorders and substance use. Option a is right because DBT was created for borderline personality disorder with chronic suicidal and self-injurious behaviour.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated primarily with antipsychotic medication, supported by psychosocial work such as family psychoeducation, social skills training, cognitive remediation and cognitive behaviour therapy for psychosis (CBTp). DBT was not designed for psychotic disorders, and active psychosis usually needs stabilising before skills groups can help. It tempts because schizophrenia is the most prominent severe mental illness, and candidates assume a well-known therapy must have been designed for it.","c":"The first-line psychological treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder is exposure and response prevention (ERP), developed from Victor Meyer's work in the 1960s and often combined with SSRIs. The person faces obsessional triggers while blocking rituals, so anxiety falls without compulsions. DBT targets emotional dysregulation and self-harm, not obsessions. It tempts because both are structured cognitive-behavioural treatments, and DBT's mindfulness resembles acceptance-based work in OCD.","d":"Specific phobias respond best to exposure therapy: graded in vivo exposure, systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) or one-session treatment developed by Lars-Göran Öst. These work by confronting the feared object until fear subsides. DBT's four skills modules are far broader than a single phobia requires. It tempts because DBT is a behaviour therapy, and phobias are the classic target of behaviour therapy."}},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'The data were INCONCLUSIVE.'","concept":"Parts of speech are judged by the job a word does in its sentence, not just by its look. In 'The data were inconclusive', the subject is 'the data' and the verb is were, a form of be. Be is a linking (copular) verb: it does not describe an action but links the subject to a subject complement that renames or describes it. Other linking verbs are seem, appear, become, remain, look, sound and feel. A subject complement is either a predicate noun ('The data were a mess') or a predicate adjective ('The data were inconclusive'). Inconclusive describes a quality of the data, namely that they do not lead to a firm conclusion, so it is a predicate adjective. Two quick tests confirm this: it can be placed before a noun (inconclusive data, the attributive position), and it can be graded with words like 'largely' or 'somewhat'. Morphology helps too. Inconclusive is built from in- (not) plus conclusive, which comes from the verb conclude plus the adjective-forming suffix -ive. Other adjective suffixes are -ous, -ful, -less, -able, -al and -ic. The word family shows each part of speech: conclude (verb), conclusion and inconclusiveness (nouns), inconclusive (adjective), inconclusively (adverb). Note also that formal and APA-style writing treats data as plural (singular datum), which is why the sentence correctly says 'were'. Option d is right because inconclusive follows the linking verb were and describes the subject, which makes it a predicate adjective.","traps":{"a":"A noun names a person, place, thing or idea and can follow a determiner, as in 'the conclusion'. Inconclusive cannot take 'the' or a plural ending; the noun forms of this family are conclusion and inconclusiveness. A noun could follow were ('The data were a disappointment'), which is why the option tempts: after a linking verb, a candidate may expect a predicate noun. The -ive ending marks an adjective.","b":"A verb expresses an action or state and carries tense, as in conclude, concluded, concludes. In this sentence the only verb is were, the past plural form of be, acting as a linking verb. Inconclusive cannot be put into the past tense or stand as a verb with its own subject. It tempts because the word is derived from the verb conclude, and because candidates sometimes treat everything after the subject as part of the verb.","c":"An adverb modifies a verb, adjective or another adverb, telling how, when, where or to what degree; many end in -ly. The adverb in this family is inconclusively, as in 'The trial ended inconclusively'. After a linking verb, English uses an adjective, not an adverb, to describe the subject, which is why 'I feel bad', not 'I feel badly', is standard. It tempts because the word comes after the verb, a typical adverb position."}},{"q":"In a normal distribution, approximately what percentage of cases falls within ±1 SD of the mean?","concept":"The normal distribution is the symmetric, bell-shaped curve first derived by Abraham de Moivre (1733) as an approximation to the binomial and developed by Carl Friedrich Gauss (1809) in his theory of measurement errors, hence the name Gaussian curve. Its mean, median and mode coincide at the centre, its tails approach but never touch the baseline (asymptotic), and its exact shape is fixed by two parameters, the mean and the standard deviation (SD). Because of this, fixed proportions of cases always lie between given SD distances from the mean, summarised as the empirical or 68-95-99.7 rule: about 68.26 percent within plus or minus 1 SD, about 95.44 percent within 2 SD, and about 99.73 percent within 3 SD. Split by halves: 34.13 percent lies between the mean and 1 SD on each side, 13.59 percent between 1 and 2 SD, 2.14 percent between 2 and 3 SD, and about 0.13 percent beyond 3 SD. Exactly 95 percent lies within 1.96 SD and 99 percent within 2.58 SD, the values behind 95 and 99 percent confidence intervals. Applied to IQ (mean 100, SD 15), about 68 percent of people score 85 to 115, about 95 percent score 70 to 130, and roughly 2 percent fall below 70. A z score of +1 sits at about the 84th percentile, and -1 at about the 16th. Option c is right because about 68 percent of cases fall within one standard deviation either side of the mean.","traps":{"a":"Fifty percent is the proportion of cases on each side of the mean in a normal curve, because the mean equals the median. The middle 50 percent lies within about 0.67 SD either side of the mean, the span between the first and third quartiles, called the interquartile range. It is not the figure for 1 SD. It tempts because 'half' is the most intuitive guess and the median splits the curve there.","b":"About 95 percent (95.44) of cases fall within plus or minus 2 SD of the mean, and exactly 95 percent within 1.96 SD, the basis of the 95 percent confidence interval and the 0.05 significance level. It is the second figure of the 68-95-99.7 rule, not the first. It tempts because 95 percent is the most frequently used number in statistics, so candidates who remember the rule only vaguely choose it.","d":"About 99.7 percent (99.73) of cases fall within plus or minus 3 SD of the mean, leaving only about 0.27 percent in both tails together. That is why scores beyond 3 SD are often treated as outliers and why quality control uses 'three sigma' limits. It is the third figure of the 68-95-99.7 rule. It tempts candidates who remember the three numbers but not which SD distance each belongs to."}},{"q":"The cones of the human retina are MOST concentrated in the:","concept":"The human retina has two kinds of photoreceptor. Rods, about 120 million, contain rhodopsin, work in dim light (scotopic vision) and do not signal colour. Cones, about 6 million, work in bright light (photopic vision) and, with three types tuned to short, medium and long wavelengths, give colour vision and fine detail. This division of labour is called the duplex or duplicity theory of vision. The two are distributed very unevenly. Near the centre of the retina lies the macula lutea, a yellowish area about 5 mm across, and at its centre the fovea centralis, a small pit where the inner retinal layers are pushed aside so that light reaches the receptors directly. Cone density is highest in the fovea, and its central part, the foveola, has no rods at all. Foveal cones also show almost no convergence: each connects to roughly one bipolar and one ganglion cell, which preserves detail and gives maximum visual acuity. That is why we move our eyes to bring objects onto the fovea when reading. Rods peak in density about 20 degrees from the fovea and pool their signals heavily, so peripheral vision is sensitive but blurred; astronomers use averted vision to see faint stars. The optic disc, where ganglion cell axons leave the eye, has no receptors and creates the blind spot. Clinically, macular degeneration destroys central, cone-based vision, while retinitis pigmentosa usually begins with rod loss, causing night blindness and tunnel vision. Option c is right because the fovea centralis has the highest concentration of cones in the retina.","traps":{"a":"The optic disc is where the ganglion cell axons gather to leave the eye as the optic nerve and where the central retinal vessels enter. It contains no rods or cones at all, which is why it produces the blind spot, about 15 degrees to the side of fixation, normally filled in by the brain. It tempts because it is a named landmark close to the macula, and 'disc' sounds like a sensory centre.","b":"The peripheral retina is dominated by rods, whose density peaks about 20 degrees from the fovea. Cones are present there but sparse, and many receptors converge on each ganglion cell, so peripheral vision is good at detecting dim light and motion but poor at detail and colour. It tempts because there are far more rods than cones overall, and candidates may misremember which receptor fills which region.","d":"The macula lutea is the yellowish central area of the retina, and the fovea lies at its centre. Cone density rises steeply towards the fovea and is highest in the foveola, so the surrounding macula has fewer cones and more rods than the fovea itself. Excluding the fovea removes the peak. It tempts because the macula is correctly the cone-rich central region, and macular degeneration is known to destroy detailed vision."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa is differentiated from bulimia nervosa primarily by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa share a core psychopathology: self-evaluation unduly driven by body shape and weight, and fear of weight gain. They also share behaviours, since both can include bingeing and purging. The feature that separates them in DSM-5 (2013) is body weight. Anorexia nervosa requires restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, developmental trajectory and physical health, together with intense fear of gaining weight (or behaviour that blocks weight gain) and disturbed experience of one's weight or shape, or denial of the seriousness of the low weight. Adult severity is graded by body mass index: mild 17 or more, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme below 15. DSM-5 dropped the old amenorrhoea criterion. There are two subtypes, restricting and binge-eating/purging. Bulimia nervosa, first described by Gerald Russell (1979) as an 'ominous variant' of anorexia, requires recurrent binge eating with loss of control and recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour (vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting, excessive exercise), both at least once a week for three months, with weight usually in the normal or overweight range. Its severity is graded by the weekly number of compensatory episodes. The hierarchy rule matters: if a person binges and purges while significantly underweight, the diagnosis is anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type, not bulimia. Binge-eating disorder has binges without regular compensation, and avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID) has restriction without body-image concern. Option b is right because significantly low body weight is required for anorexia nervosa and is the main feature distinguishing it from bulimia.","traps":{"a":"Body image disturbance is present in both disorders. In bulimia nervosa, DSM-5 requires that self-evaluation is unduly influenced by body shape and weight, the same overvaluation seen in anorexia. What differs is weight, not body image. It tempts because seeing oneself as fat despite emaciation is the most vivid image of anorexia, so candidates assume bulimia lacks it. The disorder of restriction without body-image concern is ARFID.","c":"Compensatory behaviours, such as self-induced vomiting, laxative or diuretic misuse, fasting and excessive exercise, are a required criterion of bulimia nervosa, so they cannot be present 'only in anorexia'. Anorexia can include them too, in the binge-eating/purging subtype. The option reverses the facts. It tempts because 'compensatory behaviour' is a key diagnostic term, and candidates who remember it from the criteria may misplace which disorder requires it.","d":"Purging is not absent in anorexia nervosa. DSM-5 has a binge-eating/purging subtype for people with anorexia who regularly binge, vomit, or misuse laxatives, diuretics or enemas. Many people with the restricting subtype later develop purging, and some move between diagnoses over time. It tempts because the stereotype of anorexia is pure starvation while purging is linked with bulimia, so the contrast looks clean but is false."}},{"q":"The Visual Cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated that depth perception emerges:","concept":"Eleanor J. Gibson and Richard D. Walk (1960) built the visual cliff to ask whether depth perception is present before much visual experience. The apparatus is a glass-topped table with a centre board. On the shallow side a checkered pattern lies directly under the glass; on the deep side the same pattern lies on the floor some distance below, so the glass looks like a drop. They tested 36 infants aged 6 to 14 months. Of the 27 who left the centre board, all crawled onto the shallow side at least once, but only 3 ventured onto the deep side, even when their mothers called them; many cried or crawled away. Gibson and Walk concluded that most infants can perceive depth by the time they can crawl. Day-old chicks, and goats and lambs as soon as they could stand, also avoided the deep side, suggesting an innate basis in species that move from birth. The method's limit is that it cannot test babies who cannot crawl. Joseph Campos and colleagues (1970) solved this with heart rate. Infants of about 2 months lowered onto the deep side showed heart-rate slowing, a sign of interest rather than fear, so they detected depth; fear (heart-rate acceleration) appeared later, linked to crawling experience. James Sorce and colleagues (1985) used a shallower cliff to study social referencing: infants often crossed when mothers looked happy, but none crossed when mothers showed fear. Option b is right because the visual cliff showed depth perception by the time infants crawl, around 6 to 8 months.","traps":{"a":"'Only after age 2' contradicts the data. Gibson and Walk's infants were 6 to 14 months old, and most already avoided the deep side, while heart-rate studies by Joseph Campos and colleagues (1970) showed depth detection at around 2 months. Age 2 is roughly when Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage ends, which may be why the figure sounds familiar. It tempts candidates who assume perceptual skills must wait for language.","c":"'At birth, fully developed' overstates the findings. The visual cliff cannot test newborns, who cannot crawl, and later work showed that depth perception develops in steps: sensitivity to motion cues comes first, binocular cues around 4 months and pictorial cues around 6 to 7 months. Fear of heights also depends on crawling experience. It tempts because chicks, goats and lambs avoided the cliff from birth, which supports a nativist reading.","d":"Explicit verbal teaching is irrelevant, because the infants tested were 6 to 14 months old, too young to understand instructions about heights. Avoidance of the deep side appeared without any teaching, and newborn animals avoided it too. The only adult influence shown on a cliff is nonverbal social referencing, when infants read a mother's facial expression, as in James Sorce and colleagues (1985). It tempts as a learning-theory distractor for candidates who assume all fears are taught."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:","concept":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test, usually called the Bender-Gestalt, was published by the American child neuropsychiatrist Lauretta Bender (1938). It uses nine simple geometric designs, a card labelled A and cards 1 to 8, taken from figures Max Wertheimer (1923) had used to demonstrate the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation. The examinee copies each design onto a blank sheet with no time limit; some versions add a recall phase. Bender's idea was that copying requires the brain to perceive a whole pattern and reproduce it through coordinated movement, so the test samples visual-motor integration, which matures with age in children and breaks down with brain dysfunction in adults. Errors linked to organicity include rotation of a figure, perseveration (repeating dots or circles beyond what is needed), fragmentation, overlapping difficulty, simplification and problems with angles and closure. Several scoring systems exist: Elizabeth Koppitz (1963) developed developmental scoring for children, used to estimate visual-motor maturity and screen for learning problems, and Gerald Pascal and Barbara Suttell (1951) produced an adult system. The Bender-Gestalt II (Gary Brannigan and Scott Decker, 2003) added new designs and a recall phase and was normed from early childhood to old age. It is a quick screen, not a diagnostic test: a poor record calls for fuller neuropsychological assessment. Neighbouring screens include the Benton Visual Retention Test and the Trail Making Test; in India the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction is a common fuller battery. Option b is right because the Bender-Gestalt is primarily a screen for organic brain damage and visual-motor functioning.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with structured interviews such as the SCID-5-PD and with inventories such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. C. McKinley, 1943). Some clinicians used the Bender designs projectively, reading personality into size, placement and pressure, but that use is weakly supported and is not the test's main purpose. It tempts because the Bender is often grouped with projective-looking drawing tests such as Draw-a-Person.","c":"Schizophrenia is diagnosed through clinical interview against DSM-5 or ICD-11 criteria, with severity rated on scales such as the PANSS or the BPRS. Bender records of psychotic patients can be disorganised, and early clinicians tried to use the test to separate psychosis from organic states, but it cannot diagnose schizophrenia. It tempts because Indian clinical batteries often include the Bender when psychiatric inpatients are assessed, so it seems linked to psychosis.","d":"ADHD is assessed through clinical history and rating scales completed by parents and teachers, such as the Conners or Vanderbilt scales, often supplemented by continuous performance tests of attention. Koppitz scoring of the Bender can flag immature visual-motor skills in children, which sometimes accompany ADHD or learning disorders, but the test does not screen for attention problems as such. It tempts because the Bender is widely used with children referred for school difficulties."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, transference refers to:","concept":"Transference is the process in which a patient unconsciously redirects feelings, wishes and expectations originally attached to important figures from the past, especially parents, onto the therapist. Sigmund Freud first noticed it in Studies on Hysteria (1895, with Josef Breuer) and recognised its full importance after his patient Dora broke off treatment; in the published case (1905) he concluded that he had failed to notice and interpret her transference. He elaborated the idea in 'The Dynamics of Transference' (1912). Transference can be positive (affection, idealisation, erotic attachment) or negative (hostility, suspicion, rivalry). A client who expects the therapist to be critical, as her father was, and bristles at neutral comments is showing transference. Freud saw it first as an obstacle, then as the main vehicle of cure: when the patient's old conflicts are revived in the relationship with the analyst, sometimes as a full transference neurosis, they can be interpreted and worked through in the present. The analyst's relative anonymity and neutrality, the 'blank screen', encourages this. Neighbouring terms that examiners swap in: countertransference, the analyst's own emotional reactions to the patient, which Freud mentioned in 1910 as something to be overcome and Paula Heimann (1950) reframed as a useful source of information; free association, the fundamental rule of saying whatever comes to mind; resistance, any unconscious opposition to treatment such as silence or missed sessions; and interpretation and working through. Option c is right because transference is the client redirecting feelings about significant figures onto the therapist.","traps":{"a":"The therapist's emotional reaction to the client is countertransference. Sigmund Freud (1910) first treated it as the analyst's own unresolved conflicts, to be controlled through self-analysis, and Paula Heimann (1950) later argued that it can be a useful clue to the patient's inner world. It is the mirror image of transference, flowing from therapist to client. It tempts because the two terms differ only by a prefix and are always taught together.","b":"Free association is the fundamental rule of psychoanalysis, which Sigmund Freud adopted in the 1890s in place of hypnosis: the patient says whatever comes to mind without censoring it, so that unconscious material can emerge. It is a technique for reaching the unconscious, not a process of redirecting feelings toward the therapist. It tempts because transference often shows itself during free association, and both are core psychoanalytic terms.","d":"Resistance is any opposition, usually unconscious, to the work of therapy, such as forgetting appointments, long silences, changing the topic or rejecting interpretations. Sigmund Freud saw it as the defences that keep painful material out of awareness. Transference can serve resistance, as when feelings for the analyst become a reason to stop exploring, but the terms are distinct. It tempts because 'resistance to interpretations' sounds like a technical feature of the analytic relationship."}},{"q":"Choose the best one-word substitution for 'a person who is fluent in many languages':","concept":"Polyglot comes from the Greek polys (many) and glotta, a form of glossa (tongue, language), so it literally means 'many-tongued'. As a noun it names a person who knows and can use several languages fluently; as an adjective it describes anything containing many languages, as in a polyglot city or a polyglot Bible printed in parallel languages. Someone who speaks an unusually large number is sometimes called a hyperpolyglot. Learn it with its number family: monolingual or monoglot (one language), bilingual (two), trilingual (three), multilingual (several, often used for whole societies, as in India's three-language formula, adopted in the National Policy on Education of 1968). Polyglot stresses one person's command of many languages. Other one-word substitutions examiners place nearby: linguist (a scientist who studies the structure of language), philologist (one who studies language in historical texts), lexicographer (a compiler of dictionaries), etymologist (a student of word origins), interpreter (one who translates speech orally), translator (one who translates written text), orator (an eloquent public speaker), and polymath (a person learned in many fields, not languages specifically). Psychology has its own interest here: Elizabeth Peal and Wallace Lambert (1962) found that bilingual Montreal children outperformed monolinguals on intelligence tests, overturning earlier claims that bilingualism harmed cognition. A sound method for one-word substitutions is to break the phrase into its key ideas (person, fluent, many languages) and pick the word that captures all of them. Option a is right because a polyglot is a person fluent in many languages.","traps":{"b":"Bilingual means able to use two languages (Latin bi, two, and lingua, tongue), as in a child raised speaking Kannada and English. It names a specific number, two, while the stem asks for many languages. If the stem said 'two languages', bilingual would be right; for three it would be trilingual. It tempts because bilingualism is the most familiar term in language psychology, and every polyglot is also, trivially, bilingual.","c":"A linguist is a scientist who studies language: its sounds (phonology), word structure (morphology), sentence structure (syntax) and meaning (semantics), as Noam Chomsky did in Syntactic Structures (1957). A linguist need not speak many languages, and a polyglot need not study language scientifically. Everyday usage sometimes applies 'linguist' loosely to someone who knows many languages, which is exactly why it tempts, but the precise exam answer is polyglot.","d":"An orator is a skilled, eloquent public speaker who delivers formal speeches that move an audience; the art is oratory. Swami Vivekananda's address at the 1893 World's Parliament of Religions in Chicago is a famous Indian example. Oratory concerns how well one speaks, not how many languages one speaks. It tempts because both words describe ability with spoken language, and fluency is a quality of every good orator."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8 are conventionally interpreted as:","concept":"Cohen's d is the most common effect size for the difference between two means. Jacob Cohen set it out in Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences (1969; second edition 1988). It is the difference between the two group means divided by the pooled standard deviation, so it expresses the gap in standard deviation units: d = 0.5 means the groups differ by half an SD, whatever the original scale. Cohen offered rough benchmarks: 0.2 small, 0.5 medium and 0.8 large. He illustrated them with height: a small effect resembles the difference between 15 and 16 year old girls, a medium one is 'visible to the naked eye', and a large one resembles the difference between 13 and 18 year old girls. He warned that these are conventions for use only when no field-specific standard exists. Parallel benchmarks exist for other indices: correlation r of 0.10, 0.30 and 0.50, and eta squared of 0.01, 0.06 and 0.14. Effect size differs from significance: a p value depends heavily on sample size, so a trivial effect can be significant in a huge sample, while d describes magnitude. Effect sizes also drive power analysis: with alpha 0.05 and power 0.80, a two-group t test needs about 26 per group for a large effect, 64 for a medium effect and 393 for a small one. Related measures include Glass's delta, which uses the control group SD, and Hedges' g, which corrects small-sample bias. Option a is right because Cohen labelled d values of 0.2, 0.5 and 0.8 small, medium and large.","traps":{"b":"'Trivial, weak, moderate' shifts every label down one step. In Cohen's scheme an effect below 0.2 is often called trivial or negligible, 0.2 is already small, and 0.5 is medium, not weak. Labels such as 'weak, moderate, strong' are used for correlation coefficients in some textbooks, which feeds the confusion. It tempts candidates who think any effect below 1.0 must be modest, since d values can exceed 1.","c":"'Medium, large, very large' shifts every label up one step. A d of 0.8 is Cohen's large effect; 'very large' (1.2) and 'huge' (2.0) were proposed later by Shlomo Sawilowsky (2009) as an extension of Cohen's scheme, not part of it. A d of 0.2 is small, not medium. It tempts candidates who know that 0.8 is impressive and overcorrect upward.","d":"'Negligible at all three values' is false. A d of 0.2 means the group means differ by a fifth of a standard deviation, which Cohen called small but real, and 0.8 means almost a full SD, a large difference. Effects of this size can matter a great deal in clinical or educational practice. It tempts candidates who see decimals below 1 and wrongly assume that effect sizes must exceed 1 to mean anything."}},{"q":"Place theory of hearing (Békésy) best explains the perception of:","concept":"Pitch perception has two classic explanations. Place theory, proposed by Hermann von Helmholtz (1863) as a resonance theory and revised by Georg von Békésy, who won the 1961 Nobel Prize for showing how the cochlea actually moves, holds that different frequencies stimulate different places along the basilar membrane, and the brain reads pitch from which hair cells are most active. Békésy showed that sound sets up a travelling wave that moves along the membrane from the base, near the oval window, toward the apex, and peaks at a point that depends on frequency. The base is narrow and stiff, so high frequencies peak there; the apex is wide and floppy, so low frequencies peak there. This tonotopic map continues up to the auditory cortex. Frequency theory, William Rutherford's telephone theory (1886), says the auditory nerve fires at the same rate as the sound wave, so firing rate codes pitch. It works for low tones, but a single neuron cannot fire more than about 1,000 times a second. Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray added the volley principle: groups of neurons fire in staggered turns, extending rate coding up to about 4,000 to 5,000 Hz. The modern synthesis is that low frequencies are coded mainly by timing, high frequencies mainly by place, and the middle range by both. Place theory's weak point is low tones, which produce broad, poorly localised waves near the apex. Option a is right because place theory best explains high-frequency pitch through the location of maximum basilar membrane displacement.","traps":{"b":"Coding low-frequency sounds by firing rate is frequency theory, William Rutherford's telephone theory (1886), extended by the volley principle of Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray, in which neurons fire in staggered groups. Low tones create broad waves near the apex that place coding handles poorly, so timing does this job. It tempts because it correctly describes the rival theory, which appears in the same textbook paragraph.","c":"Localising sounds in the horizontal plane depends on binaural cues, explained by Lord Rayleigh's duplex theory (1907): interaural time differences for low frequencies and interaural level differences for high frequencies, where the head casts a sound shadow. These are compared in the superior olivary complex of the brainstem. This concerns where a sound is, not its pitch. It tempts because place theory has 'place' in its name, suggesting spatial location.","d":"Loudness is coded mainly by how many auditory neurons fire and how fast they fire: a louder sound produces larger basilar membrane vibration, recruiting more hair cells and raising firing rates. Physical intensity is measured in decibels, and perceived loudness in sones, a scale proposed by Stanley Smith Stevens (1936). Place theory addresses pitch, not intensity. It tempts because both loudness and pitch are coded in the cochlea."}},{"q":"A patient firmly believes that a close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor. This is:","concept":"Capgras delusion is the best known of the delusional misidentification syndromes. It was described by the French psychiatrists Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923), who called it 'l'illusion des sosies' (the illusion of doubles) in a woman convinced that her husband, children and others had been replaced by look-alikes. The patient agrees that the person looks exactly the same, yet insists it is an impostor, usually a spouse, parent or other close relative. It occurs in schizophrenia, in dementia (especially dementia with Lewy bodies) and after brain injury, often to the right hemisphere. Hadyn Ellis and Andrew Young (1990) explained it as the mirror image of prosopagnosia: visual recognition of the face is intact, but the normal emotional response to a familiar face is missing, so the mind concludes the person must be a double. Know the neighbours, because examiners swap them. In Fregoli delusion, described by Paul Courbon and Gabriel Fail (1927), different strangers are believed to be one familiar person, usually a persecutor, in disguise. In intermetamorphosis, people are believed to exchange identities with each other. In the syndrome of subjective doubles, the patient believes he has a double living an independent life. Cotard delusion, described by Jules Cotard (1880), is a nihilistic belief that one is dead or has lost one's organs. A simple rule: in Capgras the familiar becomes a stranger; in Fregoli strangers become familiar. Option d is right because the belief that a close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor is exactly Capgras delusion.","traps":{"a":"Cotard delusion, described by Jules Cotard (1880), is a nihilistic delusion: the patient believes he is dead, rotting, has no blood or internal organs, or does not exist at all. It is seen mainly in severe psychotic depression, especially in older people, and also in schizophrenia. It concerns the patient's own body and existence, not the identity of other people. It tempts because it is another eponymous French delusion taught in the same list.","b":"Folie à deux, described by Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret (1877), is a shared delusion: a dominant person with a psychotic disorder transmits the belief to a close, often isolated and dependent partner, and separating them often helps the secondary partner. It names how a delusion spreads, not what the delusion is about. It tempts because the stem mentions a family member, and shared delusions typically occur within families.","c":"Fregoli delusion, described by Paul Courbon and Gabriel Fail (1927) and named after the Italian quick-change actor Leopoldo Fregoli, is the belief that different strangers are really one familiar person, usually a persecutor, changing disguises. It is the reverse of Capgras: here strangers become familiar, whereas in Capgras a familiar person becomes a stranger. It tempts because both are misidentification syndromes built around disguise and impostors."}},{"q":"According to Baumrind, the parenting style with high warmth and high demandingness is:","concept":"Diana Baumrind (1966, 1967) observed preschool children and their parents in California and described parenting styles using two dimensions: warmth or responsiveness (affection, acceptance, attention to the child's needs) and demandingness or control (rules, supervision, expectations for mature behaviour). Crossing the two gives four styles. Authoritative parents are high on both: warm and firm, they set clear limits but explain the reasons, listen to the child and encourage independence. Authoritarian parents are high on demandingness but low on warmth: strict, obedience-oriented, relying on punishment and 'because I said so'. Permissive (indulgent) parents are high on warmth but low on demandingness: loving but with few rules. Baumrind's original scheme had three styles; Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) added the fourth, uninvolved or neglectful, low on both dimensions. Typical outcomes asked in exams: authoritative parenting is linked to the best results, including self-reliance, social competence and school achievement; authoritarian to anxious, withdrawn, less self-confident children; permissive to impulsive, less self-controlled children; uninvolved to the poorest outcomes, including behaviour problems. Some cross-cultural research, including studies in collectivist societies, finds that strict parenting does not always carry the same negative effects, so these outcome links are strongest in Western samples. The usual trap is spelling: authoritative (warm and firm) versus authoritarian (cold and firm). Option b is right because the authoritative style is the one that combines high warmth with high demandingness.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high in demandingness but low in warmth: strict rules, a demand for obedience without explanation, and reliance on punishment. Baumrind linked it to children who are more anxious, withdrawn and less socially confident. It tempts mainly because the words authoritarian and authoritative look and sound almost identical, and both share the 'high demandingness' half of the stem. The missing ingredient is warmth.","c":"Permissive or indulgent parenting is high in warmth but low in demandingness: affectionate, accepting parents who set few rules, rarely enforce limits and let the child regulate himself. Baumrind associated it with impulsive, less self-controlled and less mature children. It tempts because it matches the 'high warmth' half of the stem, but it lacks the firm expectations and control that the stem also requires.","d":"Uninvolved or neglectful parenting is low in both warmth and demandingness: parents are emotionally detached and provide little supervision or structure. It was not among Baumrind's original three styles; Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) added it when they crossed the two dimensions into a two-by-two grid. It is linked to the poorest child outcomes. It fits neither half of the stem and tempts only as a guess."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales assess all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"Adaptive behaviour means the everyday conceptual, social and practical skills a person actually uses to meet the demands of daily life, as opposed to intelligence, the capacity to reason and learn. The Vineland tradition began with Edgar Doll (1935), who built the Vineland Social Maturity Scale at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey; its Indian adaptation by A. J. Malin is still used in India to give a Social Quotient. The modern Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales were produced by Sara Sparrow, David Balla and Domenic Cicchetti (1984), followed by Vineland-II and Vineland-3. They are completed mainly as a semi-structured interview or rating form with a parent, caregiver or teacher, not by testing the person directly. The core domains are Communication (receptive, expressive, written), Daily Living Skills (personal, domestic, community) and Socialisation (interpersonal relationships, play and leisure, coping skills), with a Motor Skills domain for young children and an optional Maladaptive Behaviour section. Domain scores combine into an Adaptive Behaviour Composite. Clinical use: DSM-5 (2013) defines intellectual disability by deficits in both intellectual and adaptive functioning, and its severity levels (mild to profound) are set by adaptive functioning, not by IQ. So the Vineland is paired with an IQ test, such as the Wechsler scales or, in India, MISIC or the Binet-Kamat Test, rather than replacing it. Option b is right because verbal IQ is a cognitive score from intelligence tests, not an adaptive behaviour domain of the Vineland.","traps":{"a":"Communication is a core Vineland domain with three subdomains: receptive (what the person understands), expressive (what the person says) and written (reading and writing). It rates how a person uses language in everyday life, reported by an informant, not language ability on a formal test. It tempts in an EXCEPT question because 'communication' sounds like a verbal ability that an intelligence test would measure.","c":"Socialisation is a core Vineland domain, the one most directly descended from Edgar Doll's (1935) Social Maturity Scale. Its subdomains are interpersonal relationships, play and leisure, and coping skills. It is especially useful in autism assessment, where social adaptive skills often lag far behind IQ. It tempts only if the candidate thinks the Vineland measures self-care alone and forgets its social roots.","d":"Daily Living Skills is a core Vineland domain covering personal care (eating, dressing, hygiene), domestic skills (household tasks) and community skills (using money, time, the telephone, road safety). It is the most practical of the domains and maps onto the practical domain of adaptive functioning in DSM-5. It tempts only if the candidate equates 'adaptive behaviour' with social behaviour and forgets self-care."}},{"q":"Token economy is an example of:","concept":"A token economy is a behaviour-modification system in which people earn tokens (chips, points, stars, stamps) for specified target behaviours and later exchange them for backup reinforcers such as snacks, outings or privileges. It was developed with chronic psychiatric inpatients by Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, whose book The Token Economy appeared in 1968. It rests on operant conditioning as set out by B. F. Skinner (1938): behaviour is shaped by its consequences, and a consequence that increases a behaviour is a reinforcer. The key idea is the type of reinforcer. A primary reinforcer satisfies a biological need (food, water, warmth). A secondary or conditioned reinforcer has no value of its own but acquires it by being paired with, or exchanged for, primary reinforcers. Tokens, like money, are generalised conditioned reinforcers because they buy many different things, so they resist satiation, and they can be given immediately after the behaviour, bridging the delay before the backup reward. Taking tokens away for problem behaviour is response cost, a form of negative punishment. The main criticism is poor generalisation once the programme ends, so tokens are gradually faded and replaced by social praise. Token economies are used in hospitals, classrooms, homes for children with ADHD or intellectual disability, and rehabilitation units. Do not confuse them with the Premack principle of David Premack (1959), where a preferred activity serves as the reinforcer. Option b is right because tokens are secondary reinforcers given contingent on voluntary behaviour, which is operant conditioning.","traps":{"a":"Classical conditioning, studied by Ivan Pavlov (1927), pairs a neutral stimulus with an unconditioned stimulus until the neutral stimulus alone elicits a reflexive, involuntary response, like salivation to a bell. Nothing the learner does earns anything. A token economy rewards voluntary behaviour after it occurs. It tempts because tokens acquire their value by association with backup rewards, a classical process, but the system as a whole is operant.","c":"Cognitive restructuring is the core technique of cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1979) and of rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955): the client identifies automatic thoughts or irrational beliefs, tests the evidence and replaces them with more balanced ones. It changes thoughts, not the consequences of behaviour. It tempts because token economies are often taught in the same CBT and behaviour therapy chapters.","d":"Insight-oriented therapy covers psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams), and later psychodynamic therapies. The aim is for the client to understand unconscious conflicts, defences and the childhood roots of symptoms through interpretation, free association and transference, with change expected to follow understanding. A token economy ignores inner causes and changes behaviour directly through consequences. It tempts only as a contrasting school."}},{"q":"Identify the correct meaning of the idiom 'to bury the hatchet':","concept":"An idiom is a fixed expression whose meaning cannot be worked out from the literal meaning of its words, so it must be learned as a whole. 'To bury the hatchet' means to make peace: to stop quarrelling and put an old disagreement aside. A hatchet is a small axe, used here as a symbol of war. The phrase comes from a custom among some Native American peoples of the north-east, such as the Haudenosaunee (Iroquois), of burying weapons when peace was agreed; English colonists recorded the practice and the phrase passed into English. The opposite image existed too: to take up the hatchet meant to go to war. Example: 'After years of not speaking, the two brothers finally buried the hatchet at their father's birthday.' Exam variants test idioms of conflict and reconciliation, so learn them in families. Making peace: extend an olive branch (offer peace), let bygones be bygones (forget past wrongs), make amends (repair harm), patch things up. Conflict: cross swords (argue), at loggerheads (in strong disagreement), have a bone to pick with someone (have a complaint), add fuel to the fire (make a quarrel worse). Reviving the past: open old wounds, rake up the past. Concealment: keep one's cards close to one's chest (hide one's plans). A reliable method is to picture the literal action: burying a weapon takes it out of use, which points to ending a fight, not starting or reviving one. Option a is right because 'to bury the hatchet' means to make peace and end a dispute.","traps":{"b":"To start a quarrel is the opposite meaning. Idioms that do mean this include 'pick a fight', 'cross swords' and, historically, 'take up the hatchet', which meant going to war. Burying a weapon puts it out of use, so the idiom points to ending hostility, not beginning it. It tempts because the word 'hatchet' suggests violence, and a candidate who reacts to that single word picks a conflict meaning.","c":"To hide one's intentions is expressed by idioms such as 'keep one's cards close to one's chest'. The tempting logic is that burying something hides it, so 'bury' is misread as 'conceal'. But what is buried here is a weapon, and the purpose is to end hostility, not to keep a plan secret. The idiom is about reconciliation, not concealment.","d":"To dig up an old issue is expressed by idioms such as 'rake up the past' or 'open old wounds'. It is the reverse image of the stem: digging up is the opposite of burying. The option tempts because it uses the same digging and burying imagery, so a hurried reader matches the picture instead of the direction of the action. Burying the hatchet closes an issue; it does not reopen it."}},{"q":"An ANOVA F-test is significant. To determine which specific group means differ, you would conduct:","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA), developed by Ronald A. Fisher (1925), compares the means of three or more groups in a single test. It splits total variability into between-groups variance and within-groups (error) variance; the F ratio is the between-groups mean square divided by the within-groups mean square. If the null hypothesis that all population means are equal is true, F is close to 1. A significant F is an omnibus result: it says at least one mean differs somewhere, but not which. Why not simply run t tests on every pair? Each test carries a 5 percent chance of a Type I error, so with many comparisons the familywise error rate climbs; with three independent tests it is 1 minus 0.95 cubed, about 14 percent. Post-hoc (a posteriori) tests make the pairwise comparisons while holding familywise error at the chosen alpha. Common ones: Tukey's honestly significant difference (HSD), devised by John Tukey, the standard choice for all pairwise comparisons; the Bonferroni correction, which divides alpha by the number of comparisons; the Scheffe test, the most conservative, which also allows complex contrasts; Dunnett's test, for comparing each group only with a control; and Games-Howell when variances are unequal. Planned (a priori) contrasts, in contrast, are specified before seeing the data. With only two groups no post-hoc test is needed, because the F test already shows which differs. The non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis test is followed by Dunn's test. Option a is right because post-hoc tests such as Tukey HSD identify which specific group means differ after a significant F.","traps":{"b":"Running another ANOVA on the same groups simply repeats the omnibus test and gives the same F, still without saying which pair differs. Running a series of small two-group ANOVAs is equivalent to multiple t tests and inflates the familywise Type I error rate, which is exactly what post-hoc procedures are designed to control. It tempts because a further analysis of the same kind feels like the natural next step.","c":"The chi-square test, introduced by Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed with expected frequencies in categorical data, as in a goodness-of-fit test or a test of independence in a contingency table, for example gender by treatment preference. It does not compare means of a continuous outcome. It tempts because it is another familiar significance test, and candidates sometimes pick any named test when they forget the post-hoc logic.","d":"Pearson's product-moment correlation, developed by Karl Pearson, measures the strength and direction of the linear relationship between two continuous variables measured on the same people, ranging from minus 1 to plus 1. It describes association, not differences between group means. It tempts because ANOVA and correlation both belong to the general linear model and appear in the same statistics chapter, so the names feel interchangeable."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is most directly implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (mesolimbic pathway)?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis is the oldest neurochemical theory of schizophrenia. It grew from two observations. First, antipsychotics block dopamine receptors: Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) proposed that chlorpromazine and haloperidol act on dopamine systems, and in 1976 Philip Seeman and, independently, Solomon Snyder's group showed that the clinical dose of each antipsychotic tracks how strongly it binds D2 receptors. Second, drugs that raise dopamine, such as amphetamine, can produce a paranoid psychosis resembling schizophrenia. The refined version separates four dopamine pathways, a favourite exam table. Mesolimbic (ventral tegmental area to nucleus accumbens and limbic areas): excess dopamine, producing positive symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions; antipsychotics work here. Mesocortical (ventral tegmental area to prefrontal cortex): too little dopamine, linked to negative and cognitive symptoms. Nigrostriatal (substantia nigra to striatum): blocking it causes extrapyramidal side effects such as parkinsonism and tardive dyskinesia. Tuberoinfundibular (hypothalamus to pituitary): dopamine normally inhibits prolactin, so blocking it causes hyperprolactinaemia. Oliver Howes and Shitij Kapur (2009) described dopamine dysregulation as the final common pathway to psychosis. Other transmitters matter but are secondary for this question: glutamate (NMDA receptor hypofunction, since ketamine and phencyclidine mimic both positive and negative symptoms) and serotonin (5-HT2A blockade by atypical antipsychotics such as clozapine). Option c is right because excess dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway is the neurotransmitter change most directly tied to positive symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Serotonin enters schizophrenia mainly through drugs. LSD, a 5-HT2A agonist, produces mostly visual hallucinations, and atypical antipsychotics such as clozapine and risperidone block 5-HT2A as well as D2 receptors, which is thought to reduce extrapyramidal effects. But serotonin modulates dopamine here; it is not the direct cause of mesolimbic positive symptoms. It tempts because atypicals are described as serotonin-dopamine antagonists.","b":"GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. It is the target of benzodiazepines and barbiturates and is central to anxiety, sleep, alcohol withdrawal and seizure control. Reduced function of GABA interneurons has been reported in schizophrenia research, but GABA is not the transmitter of the mesolimbic pathway. It tempts because benzodiazepines are often given alongside antipsychotics to calm acutely agitated psychotic patients.","d":"Acetylcholine is the transmitter of the neuromuscular junction and of basal forebrain memory pathways (nucleus basalis of Meynert), whose loss underlies the cholinergic hypothesis of Alzheimer's disease and the use of donepezil. In schizophrenia it appears mainly through side effects: anticholinergics such as trihexyphenidyl treat antipsychotic-induced parkinsonism. It tempts because many antipsychotics have anticholinergic actions, so acetylcholine keeps appearing in the same pharmacology chapter."}},{"q":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:","concept":"Schizophrenia symptoms are grouped into positive, negative and disorganised dimensions. Positive symptoms are additions to or distortions of normal experience: hallucinations (most often auditory, such as voices commenting or conversing), delusions and passivity experiences. Negative symptoms are losses or reductions of normal function. Timothy Crow (1980) proposed Type I schizophrenia, with acute positive symptoms, good antipsychotic response and a dopamine basis, versus Type II, with negative symptoms, enlarged ventricles, poor drug response and a worse course. Nancy Andreasen (1982) built the Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms (SANS), later paired with the SAPS for positive symptoms; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler (1987) combined both in the PANSS. Negative symptoms are remembered as the five As: affective flattening or blunting (reduced facial and vocal expression), alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (loss of motivation and goal-directed activity), anhedonia (reduced pleasure) and asociality (withdrawal from relationships). DSM-5 (2013) lists diminished emotional expression and avolition among its Criterion A symptoms. Negative symptoms are more persistent, predict poorer functioning and respond poorly to typical antipsychotics. Secondary negative symptoms must be ruled out, since they can result from depression, drug-induced parkinsonism or an understimulating institution. Do not confuse the five As with the four As of Eugen Bleuler (1911): loosened associations, affective disturbance, ambivalence and autism. Option b is right because auditory hallucinations add something abnormal to experience, so they are a positive symptom, not a negative one.","traps":{"a":"Avolition is a reduced ability to start and keep up purposeful, goal-directed activity: the patient may sit for hours, neglect hygiene, or drop work and study. It is one of the two negative symptoms named in DSM-5 Criterion A and one of the five As. It tempts in an EXCEPT question only if it is confused with depression, where loss of drive also occurs but comes with low mood.","c":"Affective flattening, also called blunted affect or diminished emotional expression, is a reduction in the outward expression of emotion: an unchanging face, poor eye contact, a monotonous voice and few gestures. It is a core negative symptom, rated on the SANS and named in DSM-5 Criterion A. It tempts if the candidate confuses it with inappropriate (incongruent) affect, which belongs to the disorganised dimension instead.","d":"Alogia is poverty of speech: brief, empty replies and little spontaneous speech, sometimes with poverty of content, where the amount of speech is adequate but it conveys little. It is one of the five negative-symptom As on the SANS. It tempts because candidates mix it up with disorganised speech, such as derailment or word salad, which is a symptom of excess and disorganisation, not of deficit."}},{"q":"Object permanence, in Piaget's theory, is fully achieved by approximately:","concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist when they cannot be seen, heard or touched. Jean Piaget, in The Construction of Reality in the Child (1937; English 1954), traced it through the six substages of the sensorimotor period, birth to about 2 years, by watching how infants search for hidden toys. In substages 1 and 2 (0 to 4 months) there is no search: out of sight is out of mind. In substage 3 (4 to 8 months) the infant retrieves a partly hidden toy but gives up if it is fully covered. In substage 4 (8 to 12 months) the infant finds a fully hidden toy but makes the A-not-B error: after finding it repeatedly under cloth A, the infant keeps searching at A even after watching it hidden at B. In substage 5 (12 to 18 months) the infant follows visible displacements but fails invisible ones, when the toy is moved while hidden. In substage 6 (18 to 24 months) the child can mentally represent the unseen object and solve invisible displacements; this is full object permanence, arriving with symbolic thought and deferred imitation. Critics argued that Piaget underestimated infants because searching needs motor skill. Renee Baillargeon (1987), using the violation-of-expectation method in her drawbridge study, found that infants of about 3.5 to 4.5 months looked longer at physically impossible events, suggesting early object knowledge. Option b is right because, in Piaget's account, object permanence is fully achieved only at the end of the sensorimotor stage, around 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"a":"At about 2 months the infant is in Piaget's second substage, primary circular reactions (1 to 4 months), repeating pleasurable actions centred on the body, such as thumb-sucking. There is no search for hidden objects: when a toy disappears, the infant looks briefly where it vanished and then loses interest. It tempts only as the earliest age offered, but nothing about object permanence is achieved at this point.","c":"8 to 12 months is Piaget's fourth substage, coordination of secondary circular reactions, when infants first search for a fully hidden object, so object permanence is beginning. But they still make the A-not-B error, searching where the toy was found before rather than where it was last hidden, which shows the concept is incomplete. It tempts because visible searching starts here, and the option itself names the error.","d":"4 months is the boundary between substages 2 and 3, when infants begin reaching for partly hidden toys but not fully hidden ones. It tempts because Renee Baillargeon's (1987) violation-of-expectation studies showed that infants of about 3.5 to 4.5 months look longer at impossible events, implying some early object knowledge. That finding challenges Piaget; it is not Piaget's own age for full object permanence."}},{"q":"The Indian adaptation of the WAIS used commonly in clinical practice in India is the:","concept":"Intelligence testing in India relies heavily on adaptations of Western tests, and exams expect you to match each Indian test to its source test and age group. The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) was published by David Wechsler (1955), succeeding his Wechsler-Bellevue scale of 1939; Wechsler introduced the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15) and separate verbal and performance scores. The WAIS-IV (2008) dropped the verbal and performance IQ split in favour of four indices, Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed, plus a Full Scale IQ. The WAIS-IV India edition is the version adapted for Indian adults, and it is the WAIS adaptation the question intends. Older Indian adult tools still met in clinics include P. Ramalingaswamy's adaptation of the WAIS performance subtests (WAPIS) and C. M. Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests. For children, the main tools are Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC), Arthur J. Malin's adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, and the Binet-Kamat Test (BKT), V. V. Kamat's adaptation of the Stanford-Binet scale of Lewis Terman (1916), which in turn descends from the scale of Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905). A quick rule: Wechsler adaptations are point scales organised into subtests; Binet adaptations are age scales that yield a mental age. Remember too that diagnosing intellectual disability needs an adaptive behaviour measure as well as IQ. Option a is right because the WAIS-IV India edition is the only option that is an Indian adaptation of the adult Wechsler scale.","traps":{"b":"The Binet-Kamat Test is V. V. Kamat's Indian adaptation of the Stanford-Binet scale of Lewis Terman (1916). It is an age scale that yields a mental age and IQ, used mainly with children and widely used in India to assess intellectual disability. It is a Binet test, not a Wechsler test, so it cannot be an adaptation of the WAIS. It tempts because it is one of the best known Indian intelligence tests.","c":"MISIC, Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children by Arthur J. Malin, is the Indian adaptation of the Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC), with verbal and performance subtests and Indian norms for school-age children. It is a Wechsler adaptation, which makes it tempting, but it is the child scale, not the adult WAIS that the stem asks about. Watch for the C, for Children, in WISC and MISIC.","d":"'All of the above' fails because only one option is an adaptation of the WAIS. The BKT is a Binet-type age scale and MISIC adapts the children's Wechsler scale. Both are genuinely common in Indian clinics, which is what makes this option tempting: all three tests are used, but the stem asks specifically for the Indian adaptation of the adult Wechsler scale."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) uses the acronym OARS to refer to:","concept":"Motivational Interviewing (MI) was first described by William R. Miller (1983) in work with problem drinkers and developed with Stephen Rollnick in their book of 1991. It is a collaborative, person-centred way of talking about change that strengthens the person's own motivation, built on the client-centred stance of Carl Rogers (1951) but more goal-directed. MI assumes that ambivalence, wanting and not wanting to change at the same time, is normal, and that arguing for change pushes the client to defend the status quo. The counsellor therefore resists the 'righting reflex', the urge to fix and persuade. The core communication micro-skills are OARS: Open questions, which invite elaboration rather than yes or no; Affirmations, which recognise strengths and efforts; Reflective listening, simple or complex reflections of what the person means, the central skill; and Summaries, which collect and link what has been said. These skills are used to evoke change talk, the client's own statements of desire, ability, reasons and need to change (DARN), which lead toward commitment. The spirit of MI is partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation, and its four processes are engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. Earlier editions listed principles such as expressing empathy, developing discrepancy, rolling with resistance and supporting self-efficacy. MI is often paired with the stages of change model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983), but the two are distinct. Option a is right because OARS stands for Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening and Summaries, the core skills of MI.","traps":{"b":"Outcomes, Assessment, Rewards, Sanctions sounds like a contingency management or behaviour-contract framework, in which behaviour is monitored and then followed by rewards or penalties. MI does the opposite: it avoids external pressure and evokes the client's own reasons for change. Sanctions in particular clash with its non-confrontational spirit. It tempts because MI is widely used in addiction settings, where contingency management is also common.","c":"Observation, Analysis, Reframing, Suggestion is an invented expansion that sounds clinical. Reframing is a genuine counselling technique and can appear within MI reflections, which adds to the temptation. But suggestion and unrequested advice represent the expert, directive style that MI deliberately limits, because pushing advice expresses the counsellor's righting reflex and tends to provoke resistance. None of these four words is part of OARS.","d":"Openness, Acceptance, Responsibility, Support lists therapeutic attitudes rather than the specific communication skills of OARS. It tempts because it begins with O and A like OARS, and because acceptance genuinely belongs to the spirit of MI (partnership, acceptance, compassion, evocation). But the acronym OARS names concrete skills the counsellor performs in the conversation, not attitudes the counsellor holds."}},{"q":"Read the passage and answer:\n\n'Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) rests on the premise that distorted thinking patterns sustain emotional distress. Identifying and modifying these patterns can therefore alleviate the distress, even when the original triggers cannot be changed. Empirical trials over five decades support its effectiveness across a range of disorders.'\n\nThe central idea of the passage is:","concept":"A central-idea (main-idea) question asks for the one claim that the whole passage exists to support, not a single detail, an example or an outside opinion. A reliable method is to summarise each sentence in a few words, then ask which statement the others serve. Here sentence one gives the premise: distorted thinking patterns sustain emotional distress. Sentence two gives the consequence: modifying these patterns can relieve distress even when the original triggers cannot be changed. Sentence three gives the evidence: five decades of trials support CBT across a range of disorders. Premise plus consequence is the mechanism of CBT, and the evidence backs it, so the main idea is that CBT works by changing the distorted thoughts that maintain distress. The content matches the cognitive model of Aaron T. Beck (1976), in which the interpretation of an event, not the event itself, drives emotion, an idea Albert Ellis (1962) expressed in his ABC model. Wrong options in such questions fall into predictable types. Extreme or overgeneralised options use words like 'every', 'all', 'always' or 'never' when the passage is hedged. Contradictions state the opposite of what the passage says. Distortions twist a detail into a false claim. Out-of-scope options bring in ideas the passage never discusses. Too-narrow options repeat a true detail but miss the overall point. Judge each option against the text, not against your own beliefs about therapy. Option b is right because it restates the passage's core mechanism: CBT works by changing distorted thoughts that keep distress going.","traps":{"a":"'CBT cures every psychological disorder' is an extreme overgeneralisation. The passage says trials support effectiveness 'across a range of disorders', a limited claim, and effectiveness is not the same as a cure. Words like 'every', 'all' and 'always' are warning signs when the passage itself is hedged. It tempts because it agrees with the passage's positive attitude toward CBT, only in an exaggerated form.","c":"'Triggers of distress are easy to remove' contradicts the passage. The text says CBT can relieve distress 'even when the original triggers cannot be changed', implying that triggers are often impossible to change and that this is why changing thinking matters. It tempts because the word 'triggers' appears in the passage, so a reader scanning for matching words finds it familiar without checking what is actually claimed.","d":"'Empirical trials are unnecessary in psychotherapy' is the opposite of the passage, which cites five decades of trials as its evidence for CBT. The author treats research evidence as the support for the claim, not as something to dispense with. It tempts only a careless reader who notices the word 'empirical' and matches it without checking whether the passage approves of trials or dismisses them."}},{"q":"A correlation of r = +0.90 between hours studied and exam scores indicates that hours studied:","concept":"Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient, r, developed by Karl Pearson (1896), summarises the linear relationship between two continuous variables. Its sign gives direction: positive means high scores on one go with high scores on the other; negative means high goes with low. Its absolute size, from 0 to 1, gives strength. By the conventions of Jacob Cohen (1988), about 0.10 is small, 0.30 medium and 0.50 or more large, so +0.90 is a very strong positive linear association. Two classic errors are tested. First, correlation does not establish causation. Even a strong r can arise from a third variable (motivation could raise both study time and marks), from reverse direction (students who already do well may enjoy studying more), or from chance; only an experiment with random assignment supports a causal claim. Second, r is not a percentage of variance. The proportion of variance in one variable shared with, or predictable from, the other is the coefficient of determination, r squared: 0.90 times 0.90 is 0.81, so 81 percent, leaving 19 percent unexplained (1 minus r squared, the coefficient of non-determination). Further cautions: r captures only linear relationships, so a strong curved relationship can give r near zero; restriction of range lowers r; outliers can inflate or deflate it. For ranked data, use Spearman's rho, from Charles Spearman (1904). Always inspect the scatterplot. Option c is right because r = +0.90 describes only a strong positive linear association between hours studied and exam scores.","traps":{"a":"'Cause higher scores' commits the correlation-causation fallacy. A correlational design measures variables as they occur, so it cannot rule out a third variable, such as motivation driving both study hours and marks, or reverse causation. A causal claim needs an experiment that manipulates study time with random assignment. It tempts because studying plausibly does raise marks, so common sense supplies a causal story the data cannot prove.","b":"'Account for 90 percent of the variance' confuses r with the coefficient of determination, r squared. Variance explained is r squared, so 0.90 squared is 0.81, or 81 percent, not 90 percent. Only when r is plus or minus 1 do the two numbers coincide. It tempts because 0.90 looks like 90 percent, and examiners deliberately offer the unsquared value to catch candidates who skip the squaring step.","d":"'Causally unrelated' overcorrects the rule that correlation does not prove causation. A correlation cannot prove a causal link, but it cannot rule one out either; a strong association is consistent with causation and often prompts experiments to test it. It tempts candidates who have memorised the slogan and wrongly conclude that a correlation shows the variables have no causal connection at all."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus is critically dependent on which receptor?","concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) is a lasting increase in the strength of a synapse after brief, high-frequency stimulation. Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) first described it in the rabbit hippocampus, stimulating the perforant path and recording in the dentate gyrus. It is the best studied cellular model of learning and memory and fits the rule of Donald O. Hebb (1949) that cells that fire together wire together. At the classic CA3 to CA1 (Schaffer collateral) synapse the transmitter is glutamate, acting on two ionotropic receptors. AMPA receptors carry ordinary fast excitatory transmission. NMDA receptors are special: at resting potential their channel is blocked by a magnesium ion, so glutamate binding alone is not enough. Only when the postsynaptic cell is already strongly depolarised does the magnesium leave, letting calcium flow in. The NMDA receptor is therefore a coincidence detector, needing presynaptic release and postsynaptic depolarisation together, which is exactly Hebb's condition. The calcium activates enzymes such as CaMKII, leading to insertion of more AMPA receptors into the membrane, so later signals produce bigger responses; this is the expression of LTP. Blocking NMDA receptors with the drug AP5 prevents LTP, and Richard Morris and colleagues (1986) showed it also impairs spatial learning in the water maze. Long-term depression (LTD) is the weakening counterpart. Clinical links: ketamine and phencyclidine are NMDA antagonists, and memantine, used in Alzheimer's disease, also blocks NMDA receptors. Option d is right because the induction of hippocampal LTP critically depends on NMDA receptor activation and the calcium entry it allows.","traps":{"a":"AMPA receptors are glutamate-gated channels that carry most fast excitatory transmission. They matter for LTP because they depolarise the cell enough to expel magnesium from NMDA channels, and because extra AMPA receptors are inserted at the synapse when LTP is expressed. But they lack the magnesium block and calcium-based coincidence detection that trigger LTP. It tempts because AMPA receptors really do change during LTP, so they seem central.","b":"Muscarinic M1 receptors are G-protein-coupled (metabotropic) acetylcholine receptors, common in the cortex and hippocampus. Cholinergic input modulates attention and can make LTP easier, and anticholinergic drugs such as scopolamine impair memory, which is why acetylcholine matters in Alzheimer's disease. But M1 receptors only modulate plasticity; they are not the receptor required to induce LTP. It tempts through the familiar link between acetylcholine and memory.","c":"GABA-A receptors are ionotropic chloride channels that mediate fast inhibition, and benzodiazepines, barbiturates and alcohol enhance them. Inhibition works against LTP, because it keeps the postsynaptic membrane from depolarising enough to unblock NMDA channels; blocking GABA-A receptors experimentally makes LTP easier to induce. It tempts because GABA-A drugs such as benzodiazepines do affect memory, causing anterograde amnesia, so the receptor seems linked to learning."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder is characterised primarily by:","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a Cluster B personality disorder in DSM-5 (2013). The term goes back to Adolph Stern (1938), who described patients on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) later described a 'borderline personality organisation'. DSM-5 defines BPD as a pervasive pattern of instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and shown by at least five of nine criteria: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships alternating between idealisation and devaluation (splitting); identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas, such as spending, sex, substances or binge eating; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability from marked mood reactivity, usually lasting hours; chronic emptiness; inappropriate, intense anger; and transient, stress-related paranoid ideas or severe dissociation. Know the clusters. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal. Cluster B, dramatic or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic, narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive. BPD differs from bipolar disorder because its mood shifts are brief and triggered by interpersonal events rather than forming sustained episodes. ICD-11 (2022) replaced named types with a severity rating plus trait domains, keeping an optional borderline pattern specifier. The best-supported treatment is dialectical behaviour therapy, developed by Marsha Linehan (1993) from her biosocial theory. Option a is right because instability in relationships, self-image and affect, with marked impulsivity, is the defining core of BPD.","traps":{"b":"Pervasive grandiosity, a need for admiration and lack of empathy define narcissistic personality disorder, another Cluster B disorder. Narcissistic patients are vulnerable to criticism and can react with rage, and the two disorders often co-occur, but the narcissistic self-image is inflated and stable rather than unstable. It tempts because both are dramatic Cluster B conditions involving stormy relationships.","c":"Eccentric beliefs, magical thinking, odd speech and ideas of reference without frank psychosis describe schizotypal personality disorder, a Cluster A disorder genetically related to schizophrenia; ICD classifies schizotypal disorder with schizophrenia rather than with personality disorders. It tempts because BPD can also show transient stress-related paranoid ideas, but in schizotypal disorder the oddness is persistent, not a brief reaction to stress.","d":"Detachment from social relationships with a restricted range of emotional expression describes schizoid personality disorder, a Cluster A disorder. The schizoid person prefers solitude, shows little desire for close relationships and appears cold or indifferent. This is the opposite of BPD, where relationships are intense and emotions overflow. It tempts because the chronic emptiness of BPD can be confused with schizoid emotional coldness."}},{"q":"Harlow's surrogate mother experiments with rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow (1958), in his paper 'The Nature of Love', tested what bonds an infant to its mother. The dominant view, from psychoanalysis and from the drive-reduction learning theory of Clark Hull (1943), was the 'cupboard love' or secondary drive account: the infant loves the mother because she feeds it, so she becomes a learned reinforcer. Harlow separated newborn rhesus monkeys from their mothers and gave them two surrogates: a bare wire frame and a similar frame covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother held the milk bottle. The infants fed from the wire mother but spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother. When frightened by a strange mechanical toy they ran to the cloth mother, and in an unfamiliar room they used her as a base from which to explore. Harlow concluded that contact comfort, not feeding, is the basis of attachment. Later work showed that monkeys reared in total social isolation developed severe social, sexual and emotional abnormalities, and that females raised without mothers often became neglectful or abusive mothers. The studies supported the ethological attachment theory of John Bowlby (1958, 1969), which sees attachment as an evolved system for seeking proximity and security, and are now criticised on ethical grounds. Neighbouring findings: Konrad Lorenz (1935) on imprinting in goslings, and Mary Ainsworth (1978) on attachment types in the Strange Situation. Option c is right because the infants preferred the comforting cloth mother to the feeding wire mother, showing that contact comfort matters more than feeding for attachment.","traps":{"a":"'Feeding is the primary basis of attachment' is the cupboard love or secondary drive theory, drawn from Sigmund Freud's oral stage and from drive-reduction learning theory, in which the mother is loved because she reduces hunger. Harlow's study was designed to test exactly this, and the infants clung to the cloth mother that gave no milk. It tempts because it is the theory most candidates meet first, and it sounds intuitive.","b":"'Imprinting occurs only in birds' refers to the work of Konrad Lorenz (1935), who showed that goslings follow the first moving object they see during a brief critical period after hatching. Harlow's monkey study did not address whether imprinting is limited to birds. It tempts because Lorenz and Harlow are taught together as the two classic animal studies behind John Bowlby's attachment theory, so their findings blur in memory.","d":"'Attachment is solely instinctive and does not require contact' contradicts Harlow's central finding that physical contact comfort is what the infants sought and what bonded them to the surrogate. His isolation studies also showed that social contact in early development is necessary for normal emotional and social growth. It tempts because John Bowlby described attachment as an innate, evolved system, but innate does not mean independent of contact."}},{"q":"A test–retest reliability coefficient is most appropriate when assessing:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measurement. In classical test theory an observed score equals a true score plus random error, and reliability is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance, ranging from 0 to 1. Each type of reliability targets a different source of error, so match the coefficient to the question. Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the scores; the result is the coefficient of stability, telling you whether scores stay steady over time. It suits traits expected to be stable, such as intelligence or trait anxiety, and is misleading for states that genuinely change, such as the state anxiety scale of Charles Spielberger (1970). The interval matters: too short and memory and practice effects inflate the coefficient; too long and real change lowers it. Parallel or alternate forms reliability correlates two equivalent versions of a test, giving the coefficient of equivalence. Split-half reliability correlates two halves of one test and is corrected for length with the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910). Internal consistency across all items is estimated by Cronbach's alpha, from Lee Cronbach (1951), or by the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 of G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) for items scored right or wrong. Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between observers, using Cohen's kappa, from Jacob Cohen (1960), or the intraclass correlation. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. Option d is right because a test-retest coefficient indexes the stability of scores over time.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency asks whether the items of a single test measure the same construct, using only one administration. It is estimated by Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951), by KR-20 for dichotomous items, or by split-half reliability with the Spearman-Brown correction. No second occasion is involved, so it says nothing about stability over time. It tempts because alpha is the most commonly reported reliability coefficient in research papers.","b":"Inter-rater (inter-scorer) reliability asks whether different observers scoring the same behaviour or responses agree, as in scoring a Rorschach protocol or rating a diagnostic interview. It is measured with percentage agreement, with Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects for chance agreement, or with the intraclass correlation coefficient. The source of error is the rater, not time. It tempts because both methods compare two sets of scores for the same people.","c":"Parallel or alternate forms reliability gives two different but equivalent versions of a test, matched in content, difficulty and score distribution, and correlates them, yielding the coefficient of equivalence. It reduces the memory effect that inflates test-retest coefficients. If the two forms are given on different days, the coefficient reflects both equivalence and stability. It tempts because it also involves two administrations and a correlation, just like test-retest."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims primarily to increase:","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT, said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes, with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson, whose treatment manual appeared in 1999. It belongs to the 'third wave' of behaviour therapy, alongside dialectical behaviour therapy and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy. Its philosophy is functional contextualism, and its theory of language and cognition is relational frame theory, which explains how humans become entangled in their own verbal thoughts. ACT's goal is psychological flexibility: the ability to contact the present moment fully, as a conscious human being, and to persist in or change behaviour in the service of chosen values. The model is drawn as a hexagon, the 'hexaflex', of six core processes: acceptance (willingness to have unwanted feelings instead of fighting them); cognitive defusion (stepping back from thoughts and seeing them as words, not truths); contact with the present moment (mindful awareness); self-as-context (an observing self distinct from passing content); values (what matters to the person); and committed action (value-guided behaviour change). Its opposite, psychological inflexibility, arises from experiential avoidance and cognitive fusion. A key exam contrast: the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1979) and the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955) change the content of thoughts by testing or disputing them; ACT changes the person's relationship with thoughts and leaves their content alone. Symptom reduction often happens but is not the target. Option b is right because ACT's primary aim is to increase psychological flexibility through its six core processes.","traps":{"a":"Symptom elimination is the goal ACT explicitly rejects. ACT holds that struggling to control or remove unwanted thoughts and feelings, called experiential avoidance, often makes suffering worse and narrows life. Clients are invited to make room for symptoms while acting on their values, and any reduction in symptoms is treated as a by-product. It tempts because most therapies, and most patients, do aim at removing symptoms.","c":"Insight into childhood conflicts is the aim of psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams), and of psychodynamic therapy, which use free association, interpretation and analysis of the transference to make unconscious conflicts conscious. ACT is present-focused and behavioural and does not treat uncovering the past as the route to change. It tempts as a familiar therapy goal that sounds deep and transformative.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is the method of rational emotive behaviour therapy, developed by Albert Ellis (1955), in which the therapist actively challenges beliefs such as 'I must be approved of by everyone' within the ABCDE model. ACT deliberately does not dispute thought content; it uses defusion to change how one relates to thoughts. It tempts because ACT is classed among the cognitive-behavioural therapies."}},{"q":"Per the same passage, the author's tone toward CBT is best described as:","concept":"Tone questions ask for the author's attitude toward the subject, shown through word choice, claims and qualifications, not for the topic itself. The passage (from the previous question) says CBT rests on the premise that distorted thinking sustains distress, that modifying these patterns 'can therefore alleviate' distress even when triggers cannot be changed, and that trials over five decades 'support its effectiveness across a range of disorders'. Two features decide the tone. First, the attitude is positive: the author explains CBT's logic sympathetically and backs it with evidence, with no criticism or irony. Second, the claims are hedged: 'can' rather than 'will', 'support' rather than 'prove', and 'a range of disorders' rather than all disorders. Hedges are qualifiers that limit the strength of a claim, typical of careful academic writing. Positive plus hedged gives supportive but qualified. Learn the common tone words. Laudatory: openly praising. Supportive: approving. Objective or neutral: reporting without judgement. Ambivalent: mixed feelings. Sceptical: doubtful, demanding proof. Critical: finding fault. Dismissive: treating the subject as not worth attention. Sarcastic: mocking by saying the opposite of what is meant. Cynical: distrusting motives. Polemical: aggressively arguing one side against opponents. Didactic: instructive. A useful test is to imagine the author reading the passage aloud: here the voice sounds measured and favourable, never mocking or combative. Option c is right because the author endorses CBT with evidence while carefully qualifying the strength of its claims.","traps":{"a":"A dismissive tone treats the subject as unimportant or not worth serious consideration, typically through brushing-off phrases such as 'merely', 'so-called' or 'hardly worth mentioning'. The passage does the opposite: it explains CBT's rationale and cites five decades of supporting trials. It tempts only a reader who mistakes the hedged words 'can' and 'a range of' for doubt about CBT, when they simply express careful, limited claims.","b":"A sarcastic tone mocks its subject by saying the opposite of what is meant, often with exaggerated praise, for example 'Of course, talking about thoughts cures everything.' Nothing in the passage is ironic; its statements are literal, measured and evidence-based. Sarcasm needs an obvious gap between the words and the intended meaning. It tempts only a candidate who guesses among unfamiliar tone words or reads irony into plain text.","d":"A polemical tone is aggressively argumentative: it attacks opposing views, uses loaded language and presents one side as obviously right. The passage argues for CBT, but calmly, without opponents, attacks or emotional language, and it limits its own claims. Supporting a view is not the same as being polemical. It tempts because the passage does take a position, and candidates confuse having a position with polemic."}},{"q":"The CONSORT statement provides reporting guidelines for:","concept":"Reporting guidelines are checklists that tell authors what to report so that readers can judge a study's methods and risk of bias. Each major study design has its own guideline, and most are collected by the EQUATOR Network. CONSORT, the Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials, covers randomised controlled trials (RCTs). It was first published in 1996 and has been revised several times since. Its two core tools are a checklist (items on the title, trial design, eligibility, interventions, outcomes, sample size, how the random sequence was generated, allocation concealment, blinding, statistical methods, results, harms and registration) and a flow diagram that tracks participants through enrolment, allocation, follow-up and analysis, showing how many were randomised, excluded and lost. Extensions cover cluster, non-inferiority and pragmatic trials and non-drug interventions such as psychotherapy. Its companion SPIRIT covers trial protocols. Learn the matching set, since examiners rotate them. PRISMA: systematic reviews and meta-analyses, with its own flow diagram of records screened. STROBE: observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional). STARD: diagnostic accuracy studies. COREQ and SRQR: qualitative research. CARE: case reports. ARRIVE: animal research. Do not confuse reporting guidelines with tools for judging quality: the Cochrane risk-of-bias tool appraises trials, and the GRADE approach rates the certainty of a body of evidence. CONSORT improves the transparency of reporting; it does not by itself make a trial well designed. Option d is right because CONSORT is the reporting guideline for randomised controlled trials.","traps":{"a":"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are reported using PRISMA, the Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses, first published in 2009 and updated in 2020. Like CONSORT it has a checklist and a flow diagram, but its diagram tracks records identified, screened and included rather than participants. It tempts because systematic reviews often pool RCTs, and both guidelines belong to the top of the evidence hierarchy.","b":"Qualitative research is reported using COREQ, the Consolidated Criteria for Reporting Qualitative Research (2007), designed for interviews and focus groups, or SRQR, the Standards for Reporting Qualitative Research. These cover the research team's reflexivity, sampling, data collection and thematic analysis, not randomisation. It tempts because COREQ also begins with 'Co' and the word 'Consolidated', so the two acronyms are easy to swap.","c":"Diagnostic accuracy studies, which compare a test against a reference standard to estimate sensitivity, specificity and predictive values, are reported using STARD, the Standards for Reporting of Diagnostic Accuracy Studies, first published in 2003 and updated in 2015. It tempts because diagnostic tests in psychology, such as screening questionnaires, are evaluated in clinical studies that can look superficially like trials."}},{"q":"The hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary that promotes water reabsorption is:","concept":"The pituitary gland has two parts with different origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is true glandular tissue that makes its own hormones under the control of releasing hormones from the hypothalamus: growth hormone, prolactin, ACTH, TSH, FSH and LH. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is an extension of the brain and makes nothing itself. Its two hormones, vasopressin and oxytocin, are synthesised by large neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, carried down their axons, stored in the posterior pituitary and released into the blood. Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (ADH), is released when the blood becomes too concentrated, as sensed by hypothalamic osmoreceptors, or when blood volume or pressure falls. It acts on the collecting ducts of the kidney, inserting water channels (aquaporins) so that more water returns to the blood and urine becomes concentrated; at high levels it also constricts blood vessels, hence the name vasopressin. Lack of ADH, or kidneys that fail to respond to it, causes diabetes insipidus, with large volumes of dilute urine and great thirst. Two psychology links are often examined. Alcohol suppresses ADH release, which is why drinking increases urination. Long-term lithium treatment can make the kidney unresponsive to ADH, producing nephrogenic diabetes insipidus with polyuria and polydipsia. Vasopressin also acts in the brain on social behaviour, including pair bonding in prairie voles. Option c is right because vasopressin (ADH) is the posterior pituitary hormone that promotes water reabsorption by the kidney.","traps":{"a":"Oxytocin is the other posterior pituitary hormone, also made in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei. It causes uterine contractions in labour and the milk let-down (ejection) reflex in breastfeeding, and in the brain it is linked to bonding, trust and maternal behaviour, hence the popular label 'love hormone'. It does not regulate water balance. It tempts because it shares the same origin as vasopressin and differs from it by only two amino acids.","b":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid made by the adrenal cortex, not the pituitary. It is the end product of the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: the hypothalamus releases CRH, the anterior pituitary releases ACTH, and the adrenal cortex releases cortisol, which raises blood sugar and dampens inflammation during stress. The adrenal hormone that does affect water balance is aldosterone. It tempts because the pituitary drives cortisol release, though only indirectly.","d":"Prolactin is made by the anterior pituitary, not the posterior, and stimulates milk production after childbirth. Its release is held in check mainly by dopamine from the tuberoinfundibular pathway, so dopamine-blocking antipsychotics such as haloperidol and risperidone raise prolactin, causing galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea and sexual dysfunction. It tempts because prolactin and oxytocin both concern breastfeeding, and candidates often misplace prolactin in the posterior lobe."}},{"q":"The most common comorbidity with autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5 is:","concept":"Autism spectrum disorder (ASD) was first described by Leo Kanner (1943) as 'autistic disturbances of affective contact' and, independently, by Hans Asperger (1944). DSM-5 (2013) merged autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder, childhood disintegrative disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into one spectrum defined by two domains: persistent deficits in social communication and interaction, and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, including sensory over- or under-reactivity. Severity is rated at three levels according to the support needed. Specifiers record whether the condition comes with or without accompanying intellectual impairment, with or without language impairment, and whether it is associated with a known genetic or medical condition, such as fragile X syndrome. Comorbidity is the rule rather than the exception: DSM-5 notes that about 70 percent of autistic people may have one comorbid mental disorder and about 40 percent two or more. Among the most frequent are intellectual disability and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD); anxiety disorders, depression and medical problems such as epilepsy and sleep difficulties are also common. An exam favourite is the ADHD rule change: DSM-IV did not allow ADHD to be diagnosed alongside a pervasive developmental disorder, but DSM-5 permits both diagnoses together. Intellectual disability is diagnosed alongside ASD only when social communication is below what the person's general developmental level would predict. Schizophrenia is uncommon in autism, though the word 'autism', coined by Eugen Bleuler (1911) for schizophrenic withdrawal, once blurred the two. Option d is right because intellectual disability and ADHD are among the commonest co-occurring conditions in ASD, unlike the other options.","traps":{"a":"Conduct disorder is a repetitive pattern of violating others' rights or major social norms: aggression, destruction of property, deceit and serious rule-breaking. Autistic children can show aggression or meltdowns, usually linked to distress, sensory overload or communication difficulty, but this is not deliberate antisocial conduct, and conduct disorder is far less frequent in ASD than intellectual disability or ADHD. It tempts because both are childhood-onset disorders with challenging behaviour.","b":"Schizophrenia is uncommon in autism. The confusion is historical: Eugen Bleuler (1911) coined 'autism' for the withdrawn inner life of schizophrenic patients, and autistic children were once labelled as having childhood schizophrenia. Later research separated the two by age of onset and symptoms: ASD begins in early development and lacks the hallucinations and delusions of psychosis. It tempts through that shared word and the shared history of the labels.","c":"Bipolar I disorder requires at least one manic episode. It can occur in autistic people and is sometimes over-diagnosed when irritability or poor sleep is mistaken for mania, but it is much less common in ASD than intellectual disability, ADHD or anxiety, and it typically begins in late adolescence or adulthood. It tempts because mood swings and irritability are frequent reasons why autistic young people are referred for psychiatric review."}},{"q":"Telegraphic speech, characterised by two-word utterances such as 'mommy go', typically appears around:","concept":"Children's expressive language follows a predictable sequence, and exams ask for the ages. Crying is present from birth; cooing, vowel-like sounds such as 'ooo', starts around 2 months; babbling, consonant-vowel syllables like 'ba-ba', appears around 6 months and becomes repetitive by about 7 to 10 months; the first true words arrive around 12 months. From about 12 to 18 months children are in the holophrastic or one-word stage, where a single word carries a whole sentence's meaning ('milk' meaning 'I want milk'). Around 18 months vocabulary grows quickly, the vocabulary spurt, often after about 50 words. Between about 18 and 24 months children begin combining two words: 'mommy go', 'more juice', 'daddy car'. This is telegraphic speech, so called because, like an old telegram where every word cost money, it keeps content words (nouns, verbs, adjectives) and leaves out function words and grammatical endings (articles, prepositions, auxiliaries, plural and tense markers). Word order is usually correct, which shows early grammar. Roger Brown (1973), in A First Language, described these stages and used mean length of utterance (MLU) in morphemes, rather than age, as the better index of grammatical development. After 24 months, utterances lengthen, grammatical morphemes are added in a fairly fixed order, and overregularisation errors such as 'goed' and 'mouses' appear, showing that the child is applying rules. By about 3 years children speak in simple sentences of three or more words. Option a is right because the two-word telegraphic stage typically appears at around 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"b":"12 months is when most children say their first words and enter the holophrastic or one-word stage, in which a single word such as 'up' or 'milk' expresses a whole idea. Combining two words needs a larger vocabulary and early grammar, which usually come several months later. It tempts because the first birthday is the best-known language milestone, and candidates attach any early grammar step to it.","c":"Around 6 months infants babble, producing repeated consonant-vowel syllables such as 'ba-ba' or 'da-da' that are not yet words. Deaf infants exposed to sign language babble with their hands, which suggests babbling is a general stage of language development rather than a purely vocal one. It tempts because babbles such as 'ma-ma' sound like real words to parents, but no meaning or word combination is present.","d":"By 36 months most children have moved beyond telegraphic speech to simple sentences of three or more words that include function words and grammatical endings, such as the plural -s and the past tense -ed. Overregularisation errors such as 'goed' and 'foots' are typical at this age, because the child applies rules too widely. It tempts because some telegraphic features persist into the third year, especially in children with language delay."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality based on:","concept":"The NEO Personality Inventory, Revised (NEO-PI-R) was published by Paul T. Costa Jr. and Robert R. McCrae (1992) as the standard measure of the Five-Factor Model (FFM) of personality. The Big Five grew out of the lexical hypothesis: important personality differences become encoded as words in everyday language, so factor analysing trait words should reveal the basic dimensions. This line of work runs from Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936), who listed about 18,000 trait words, to Lewis Goldberg (1981), who coined the term 'Big Five'. The five domains, remembered as OCEAN, are Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism. The name NEO comes from the original 1985 inventory, which measured facets only for Neuroticism, Extraversion and Openness; facets for Agreeableness and Conscientiousness were added in the revision. The NEO-PI-R has 240 items rated on a five-point scale and measures 30 facets, six per domain; for example, Neuroticism includes anxiety, angry hostility, depression, self-consciousness, impulsiveness and vulnerability. A short 60-item version, the NEO-FFI, gives the five domain scores only, and the NEO-PI-3 is a later update. There are self-report (Form S) and observer-rating (Form R) versions. Compare the neighbouring models: the 16 factors of Raymond Cattell (16PF, 1949), the three-factor PEN model of Hans Eysenck, measured by his 1975 EPQ, and the needs theory of Henry Murray (1938). The HEXACO model adds a sixth factor, Honesty-Humility. Option c is right because the NEO-PI-R is built on, and measures, the Five-Factor Model of Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness and Neuroticism.","traps":{"a":"Raymond Cattell (1949) factor-analysed trait descriptions and questionnaire data to identify 16 source traits, such as warmth, dominance and emotional stability, measured by the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF). Second-order factor analysis of the 16 factors yields broad global factors that resemble the Big Five, which is why this option tempts. But the NEO-PI-R was written for the Five-Factor Model directly, not for Cattell's 16 factors.","b":"Hans Eysenck's PEN model has three broad, biologically based dimensions: Psychoticism, Extraversion and Neuroticism. It is measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans and Sybil Eysenck (1975), and earlier by the Eysenck Personality Inventory, which lacked P. It tempts because Extraversion and Neuroticism appear in both PEN and the Big Five, and the NEO name itself contains N and E. But PEN has three factors, not five.","d":"Henry Murray (1938), in Explorations in Personality, proposed that behaviour reflects internal needs (such as achievement, affiliation and dominance) interacting with environmental press. His theory underlies the Thematic Apperception Test, developed with Christiana Morgan, and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule. It is a needs-based, partly projective tradition, separate from factor-analytic trait models. It tempts as another classic personality theory with its own well-known tests."}},{"q":"EMDR was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by the American psychologist Francine Shapiro, who reported in 1987 noticing that her own disturbing thoughts lost their intensity as her eyes moved rapidly from side to side during a walk; her first controlled study followed in 1989. EMDR is a structured, eight-phase therapy for traumatic memories: history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitisation, installation, body scan, closure and re-evaluation. In the core phases the client briefly holds the target memory in mind, with its image, negative belief and body sensations, while attending to bilateral stimulation, usually following the therapist's fingers from side to side, or alternating taps or tones. Shapiro's explanatory model is the Adaptive Information Processing model: trauma memories are stored in an unprocessed form, and EMDR helps link them to adaptive memory networks so they lose their emotional charge. Effectiveness for post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) is well supported, and the World Health Organization (2013) recommends EMDR, alongside trauma-focused CBT, for PTSD. The controversy is about mechanism: dismantling studies suggest the eye movements may add little beyond imaginal exposure, so critics describe EMDR as exposure therapy with extras, while supporters argue that the dual-attention task taxes working memory and makes the memory less vivid. Know the other trauma treatments by founder: prolonged exposure by Edna Foa, built on emotional processing theory of Edna Foa and Michael Kozak (1986), and cognitive processing therapy by Patricia Resick (1992). Option a is right because EMDR was developed by Francine Shapiro.","traps":{"b":"Marsha Linehan developed dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), set out in her 1993 treatment manual, for chronically suicidal women with borderline personality disorder. DBT combines behavioural problem-solving with acceptance and mindfulness drawn from Zen practice, and teaches mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness skills. It tempts because DBT, like EMDR, is a newer evidence-based therapy often listed alongside trauma-informed approaches.","c":"Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy in the 1960s, first for depression, and with colleagues published the classic manual Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979). It targets automatic thoughts, the cognitive triad and underlying schemas through collaborative empiricism; Beck also created the Beck Depression Inventory. Cognitive therapy ideas feed into trauma treatments such as cognitive processing therapy, but Beck did not develop EMDR. It tempts as the most famous name in evidence-based psychotherapy.","d":"Edna Foa developed prolonged exposure therapy for PTSD, grounded in the emotional processing theory she set out with Michael Kozak (1986): fear memories change when they are fully activated and corrected by new information, through repeated imaginal and in vivo exposure. EMDR also asks clients to recall the trauma, so critics see it as a form of exposure. It tempts because Foa is a leading figure in trauma therapy, and both treatments target PTSD."}},{"q":"Find the error: 'Neither the patient nor the family members was informed about the diagnosis.'","concept":"This is an error-spotting item on subject-verb agreement, one of the most tested rules in the English sections of entrance exams. The basic rule is that a verb agrees in number with its subject: a singular subject takes 'is' or 'was', and a plural subject takes 'are' or 'were'. Compound subjects cause the trouble. Two subjects joined by 'and', or by 'both ... and', make a plural subject ('The patient and his family were informed'). Two subjects joined by the correlative pairs 'either ... or', 'neither ... nor', 'not only ... but also' or 'not ... but' follow the rule of proximity: the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. So 'Neither the nurses nor the doctor was present' is correct, and so is 'Neither the doctor nor the nurses were present'. Good style places the plural subject second, so that the plural verb sounds natural. Examiners build variants from neighbouring rules. Phrases such as 'as well as', 'along with', 'together with' and 'in addition to' do not create a compound subject, so the verb follows the first noun ('The doctor, as well as the nurses, was present'). 'Neither of the reports' and 'each of the patients' take a singular verb in formal usage, because 'neither' and 'each' are the real subjects. A quick test is to join the nearest subject directly to the verb and say it aloud: 'the family members was informed' clearly fails. Option d is right because the nearer subject, 'the family members', is plural, so 'was' must be corrected to 'were'.","traps":{"a":"'Neither' correctly opens the correlative pair and is properly matched by 'nor' later in the sentence. Errors with this word usually involve pairing it with 'or' ('neither ... or') or using it for more than two items, and neither problem occurs here. It tempts candidates who sense that something is wrong in a negative sentence and blame the first word they see.","b":"'The patient' is a correctly formed singular noun phrase and the first half of the compound subject. It would control the verb only if it stood nearer to it, as in 'Neither the family members nor the patient was informed'. It tempts because many learners wrongly make the verb agree with the first subject, and the singular 'patient' seems to justify the singular 'was'.","c":"'No error' is the right choice only when every part of the sentence follows standard grammar. Here the plural noun phrase 'the family members' sits directly before the verb, and under the proximity rule it demands 'were'. It tempts because 'neither ... was' sounds familiar from sentences such as 'Neither of them was ready', where a singular verb is correct, so the sentence feels acceptable on a quick reading."}},{"q":"REM sleep is characterised by all EXCEPT:","concept":"Sleep is divided into rapid eye movement (REM) sleep and non-REM (NREM) sleep. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) first described REM sleep after noticing bursts of rapid eye movement in sleeping subjects, linked with vivid dream reports on waking. Current scoring by the American Academy of Sleep Medicine uses stages N1, N2, N3 and REM, replacing the older four NREM stages of Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968). N1 is light sleep with theta waves; N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes; N3, slow-wave or deep sleep, is dominated by high-amplitude delta waves (about 0.5 to 2 Hz). REM sleep has a very different profile. Its EEG is low-voltage, mixed-frequency and fast, much like waking, with sawtooth waves, which is why it is called paradoxical sleep. Other features are bursts of conjugate rapid eye movements, atonia of almost all skeletal muscles (the diaphragm and eye muscles are spared), irregular heart rate and breathing, and penile or clitoral tumescence. A sleep cycle lasts about 90 minutes and repeats four or five times a night; N3 dominates the early cycles, while REM periods lengthen towards morning. REM is about 20 to 25 percent of adult sleep and about half of newborn sleep. Clinical links are common exam material: short REM latency in depression, sleep-onset REM periods in narcolepsy, and loss of atonia in REM sleep behaviour disorder, whereas sleepwalking and night terrors arise from N3. Option b is right because delta-wave activity marks NREM stage N3, not REM sleep.","traps":{"a":"Rapid eye movements are the defining sign that gave REM sleep its name: bursts of quick, conjugate eye movements under closed lids, first recorded by Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953). They are a true feature of REM, so this cannot be the exception. It tempts only candidates who miss the word EXCEPT in the stem and pick the first familiar, correct-sounding statement.","c":"During REM, circuits in the pons and medulla actively inhibit spinal motor neurons, so almost all skeletal muscles go limp; only the diaphragm and eye muscles keep working. This atonia stops us acting out dreams, and its loss produces REM sleep behaviour disorder. It is a true REM feature. It tempts because paralysis sounds like 'deep' sleep, which candidates wrongly link with delta waves.","d":"Penile erection and clitoral engorgement occur during REM periods regardless of dream content. This is the basis of nocturnal penile tumescence testing, which helps separate organic from psychogenic erectile dysfunction: normal night-time erections point to a psychological cause. It is a true REM feature. It tempts because it is less often taught and sounds unlikely, so unsure candidates guess it is the exception."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many symptom clusters?","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) was moved by DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders, and DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps the same criteria structure. Criterion A is the gateway, not a symptom cluster: exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it, witnessing it, learning that it happened to a close family member or friend, or repeated exposure to aversive details. Then come four symptom clusters with 20 symptoms in all. Criterion B, intrusion (at least 1 of 5): memories, nightmares, flashbacks, and psychological distress or physiological reactions to cues. Criterion C, avoidance (at least 1 of 2): of internal reminders or of external reminders. Criterion D, negative alterations in cognitions and mood (at least 2 of 7), such as distorted blame, persistent negative beliefs and inability to feel positive emotions. Criterion E, alterations in arousal and reactivity (at least 2 of 6), such as hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability and poor sleep. Criteria F to H require duration of more than one month, significant distress or impairment, and exclusion of substances or medical conditions. Specifiers are 'with dissociative symptoms' and 'with delayed expression' (full criteria met only 6 months or more after the event). DSM-IV had three clusters (re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, hyperarousal); DSM-5 split numbing off into the new cluster D. ICD-11 PTSD uses three core elements, and complex PTSD adds disturbances in self-organisation. Option a is right because DSM-5-TR PTSD requires symptoms from four clusters, B to E.","traps":{"b":"Three is the DSM-IV (1994) structure: re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, and hyperarousal, with 17 symptoms. ICD-11 also describes PTSD through three core elements (re-experiencing in the present, avoidance, and a sense of current threat). DSM-5 separated active avoidance from numbing and expanded the latter into a fourth cluster. It tempts because older textbooks and ICD-11 both teach a three-part model.","c":"No edition of DSM or ICD describes PTSD with two symptom clusters. The figure comes from confusing the number of clusters with the number of symptoms needed within a cluster: DSM-5-TR asks for at least two symptoms from cluster D and two from cluster E. It tempts candidates who remember the 'two' in the scoring rule but not the overall structure of the criteria.","d":"Five usually comes from counting Criterion A, the traumatic exposure, as a cluster along with B to E, since the letters A to E run in sequence. Criterion A is the gateway event, not a group of symptoms. Five is also the number of intrusion symptoms listed under Criterion B. It tempts because the lettered criteria look like five parallel symptom groups at a glance."}},{"q":"In Bowlby's theory, the phase during which an infant displays clear attachment behaviour to one specific caregiver and shows separation anxiety is:","concept":"John Bowlby (1969), a British psychiatrist and psychoanalyst, set out ethological attachment theory in the first volume of Attachment and Loss. He argued that attachment is an evolved behavioural system that keeps the infant close to a caregiver for protection, drawing on Konrad Lorenz's (1935) work on imprinting and Harry Harlow's (1958) studies of infant monkeys. Bowlby described four phases (ages are approximate). Pre-attachment, birth to about 6 weeks: built-in signals such as grasping, smiling, crying and gazing bring adults near, but the baby does not mind being left with an unfamiliar adult. Attachment-in-the-making, about 6 weeks to 6 to 8 months: the infant responds differently to a familiar caregiver and develops a sense of trust, but does not yet protest separation. Clear-cut attachment, about 6 to 8 months to 18 months or 2 years: the infant shows separation anxiety, follows the caregiver, uses him or her as a secure base for exploring, and often becomes wary of strangers. Formation of a reciprocal relationship, from about 18 months to 2 years onward: language and mental representation let the toddler understand the parent's comings and goings and negotiate, so separation protest declines. The outcome is an internal working model of self and others. Neighbouring ideas include the stages of Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964), namely asocial, indiscriminate, specific and multiple attachments, and Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation (1978), which classifies attachment quality rather than phase. Option b is right because focused attachment to one caregiver, with separation anxiety, defines Bowlby's clear-cut attachment phase.","traps":{"a":"Pre-attachment runs from birth to about 6 weeks. Built-in signals such as grasping, smiling, crying and gazing bring adults close, and the newborn can recognise the mother's smell and voice, but there is no attachment yet: the baby does not mind being left with an unfamiliar adult, so there is no separation anxiety. It tempts because it is the first phase listed and contains the word 'attachment'.","c":"Attachment-in-the-making runs from about 6 weeks to 6 to 8 months. The infant now responds differently to a familiar caregiver, smiling and settling more readily, and learns to expect that the caregiver will respond. However, the baby does not yet protest when separated. It tempts because a preference for one person begins here, so it seems to fit 'one specific caregiver'.","d":"Formation of a reciprocal relationship begins at about 18 months to 2 years. Language and mental representation let the toddler understand when and why the parent leaves and predict the return, so separation protest declines and the child negotiates with the caregiver instead. It tempts because 'relationship' sounds like the most developed and specific bond, but the stem's separation anxiety peaks in the earlier phase."}},{"q":"If a raw score lies at the 84th percentile on a normally distributed test, the corresponding z-score is approximately:","concept":"A z-score tells you how many standard deviations a raw score lies above or below the mean: z equals the raw score minus the mean, divided by the standard deviation. A percentile rank tells you the percentage of the norm group scoring below a given score. On a normal curve the two are linked by fixed areas, summarised in the 68-95-99.7 rule: about 68 percent of scores fall within one standard deviation of the mean, about 95 percent within two, and about 99.7 percent within three. The mean, median and mode coincide at the 50th percentile, where z is 0. Half of the 68 percent, about 34 percent, lies between the mean and +1, so a z of +1 sits at about the 84th percentile (50 plus 34), and a z of minus 1 at about the 16th. A z of +2 sits near the 98th percentile (97.7), a z of minus 2 near the 2nd, and a z of +1.5 near the 93rd. In other standard scores, T-scores (mean 50, SD 10) put z of +1 at T of 60; deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15) put it at 115; stanines have a mean of 5 and an SD of 2. Percentiles are ordinal, not interval, units: equal percentile gaps near the middle of the curve stand for much smaller raw-score differences than equal gaps in the tails. Option b is right because the 84th percentile lies one standard deviation above the mean, which is a z-score of about +1.","traps":{"a":"A z-score of 0 means the raw score equals the mean, which in a normal distribution coincides with the median and the 50th percentile. A score at the 84th percentile beats 84 percent of the group, so it must lie above the mean and have a positive z. It tempts candidates who confuse percentile with z or who think of 84 as merely 'average to good'.","c":"A z-score of +1.5 lies one and a half standard deviations above the mean, where about 93 percent of scores fall below, so it matches roughly the 93rd percentile. It is too high for the 84th. It tempts as a halfway guess between +1 and +2 by candidates who know the score is above average but cannot recall the areas under the normal curve.","d":"A z-score of +2 corresponds to roughly the 98th percentile (97.7), because about 95 percent of scores lie within two standard deviations, leaving about 2.3 percent above +2. On the IQ scale this is 130, a common cut-off for giftedness. It tempts when candidates map the '95' of the 68-95-99.7 rule onto the stem or simply pick the familiar number."}},{"q":"In family therapy, the structural approach is associated with:","concept":"Family therapy treats the family, not the individual, as the unit of change, and several rival schools grew up between the 1950s and 1970s. Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin, first with delinquent boys from poor families at the Wiltwyck School and then at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic; its key text is Families and Family Therapy (1974). Minuchin held that symptoms are maintained by the family's structure, the invisible set of rules organising who interacts with whom. Families contain subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling) separated by boundaries. Clear boundaries are healthy; diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment, where members are over-involved; rigid boundaries produce disengagement, where members are isolated. Problems also come from a confused hierarchy, such as a child holding more power than the parents, and from cross-generational coalitions. The therapist joins the family, draws a structural map, and then restructures it through enactments, boundary making, unbalancing and reframing. Minuchin applied this to anorexia nervosa in Psychosomatic Families (1978). Learn the neighbouring schools as pairs: Murray Bowen (1978), intergenerational family systems theory with differentiation of self and triangles; Virginia Satir (1964), the experiential and communication approach with communication stances and family sculpting; Jay Haley (1976), strategic therapy with directives and paradox; Michael White and David Epston (1990), narrative therapy and externalising the problem. Option a is right because Salvador Minuchin founded structural family therapy, built on subsystems, boundaries and hierarchy.","traps":{"b":"Murray Bowen developed family systems theory (Family Therapy in Clinical Practice, 1978), an intergenerational approach built on differentiation of self, triangles, the multigenerational transmission process and emotional cutoff, and mapped with the genogram. The therapist acts as a calm coach rather than restructuring interactions in the session. It tempts because Bowen also uses 'systems' language, and his idea of fusion resembles Minuchin's enmeshment.","c":"Virginia Satir led the experiential and communication approach (Conjoint Family Therapy, 1964), focusing on self-esteem and honest, congruent communication. She described four stances people adopt under stress, placating, blaming, super-reasonable and irrelevant, and used family sculpting to show relationships physically. It tempts because she is one of the best-known founders of family therapy and is often listed beside Minuchin in the same chapter.","d":"Jay Haley developed strategic family therapy (Problem-Solving Therapy, 1976), in which the therapist designs directives, often paradoxical, to break the interaction sequence that maintains a symptom; he drew on Milton Erickson's hypnotherapy and the Palo Alto communication group. It tempts strongly because Haley worked alongside Minuchin at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic and also stressed hierarchy, so the two approaches are often blended."}},{"q":"Choose the correct passive form of: 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow.'","concept":"Voice tells us whether the subject does the action (active) or receives it (passive). To change active to passive, the object of the active sentence becomes the new subject, the verb becomes a form of 'be' plus the past participle, and the original subject, if kept, moves into a 'by' phrase. The key rule is that only the voice changes, not the tense, so the form of 'be' must carry the original tense. Simple present 'announces' becomes 'is announced'; present continuous 'is announcing' becomes 'is being announced'; simple past 'announced' becomes 'was announced'; past continuous 'was announcing' becomes 'was being announced'; present perfect 'has announced' becomes 'has been announced'; past perfect 'had announced' becomes 'had been announced'; simple future 'will announce' becomes 'will be announced'; future perfect 'will have announced' becomes 'will have been announced'. Modals follow the same pattern ('can be announced', 'must be announced'). Perfect continuous and future continuous forms are rarely put into the passive. Adverbs of time and place, such as 'tomorrow', stay unchanged, and they give a quick check, because 'tomorrow' cannot sit with a past or present perfect verb. Only transitive verbs, which take an object, have a passive, so 'The patient slept' cannot be changed. Here the subject is 'the committee', the verb 'will announce' is simple future, and the object is 'the results'. Option a is right because simple future active 'will announce' becomes simple future passive 'will be announced', with the object as the new subject.","traps":{"b":"'Are being announced' is the present continuous passive, the passive of 'The committee is announcing the results tomorrow'. English does use the present continuous for fixed future arrangements, so the sentence is grammatical, but it changes the tense of the given sentence, and voice change must keep the tense. It tempts because it sits naturally with 'tomorrow' and contains no obvious grammatical error.","c":"'Have been announced' is the present perfect passive, the passive of 'The committee has announced the results'. The present perfect describes a completed action with present relevance, so it cannot combine with the future adverb 'tomorrow', and the sentence is ungrammatical. It tempts because 'have been' plus a participle looks like a typical passive, and candidates match the word 'been' with passive voice.","d":"'Were announced' is the simple past passive, the passive of 'The committee announced the results'. It moves the event into the past and clashes with 'tomorrow', so the sentence is ungrammatical as well as the wrong tense. It tempts because 'was' or 'were' plus a past participle is the passive pattern most often drilled in school exercises, so it looks familiar at first glance."}},{"q":"A patient with damage to the right parietal lobe is most likely to show:","concept":"The parietal lobe lies behind the central sulcus. Its front strip, the postcentral gyrus, is the primary somatosensory cortex; the posterior parietal cortex combines vision, touch and body position into a map of space and directs attention within it. This is the dorsal 'where' visual stream, distinct from the ventral 'what' stream in the temporal lobe, a division proposed by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982). Spatial attention is lateralised unevenly: the right hemisphere attends to both left and right space, while the left hemisphere attends mainly to the right. So damage to the right parietal lobe, especially the inferior parietal lobule and temporoparietal junction, leaves the left side of space unattended, producing left hemispatial neglect; left-sided damage causes milder right neglect because the right hemisphere compensates. Patients eat food from only the right half of the plate, shave or dress only the right side, bisect lines to the right of centre, miss left-side targets on cancellation tests, and crowd all the numbers onto the right of a clock face. Neglect is not blindness but a failure of attention, often with extinction to double simultaneous stimulation and anosognosia, denial of the left-sided paralysis. Edoardo Bisiach and Claudio Luzzatti (1978) showed it can affect imagined scenes too. Neighbouring pairings: left angular gyrus damage gives Gerstmann syndrome (finger agnosia, left-right confusion, agraphia, acalculia), and bilateral parieto-occipital damage gives Balint syndrome. Option c is right because right parietal damage classically produces neglect of the left side of space.","traps":{"a":"Broca's aphasia is a non-fluent, expressive aphasia: slow, effortful, telegraphic speech with relatively preserved comprehension. It follows damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus (Broca's area), described by Paul Broca (1861) in his patient 'Tan'. That is the wrong lobe and usually the wrong hemisphere. It tempts because it is the most famous lesion-deficit pairing in any biopsychology chapter.","b":"Anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new long-term declarative memories after the damage, while older memories are relatively spared. It follows damage to the medial temporal lobes, especially the hippocampus, as in patient H.M. (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), or to the diencephalon in Korsakoff syndrome. It tempts because it is a classic neuropsychological syndrome, but it is not a parietal one.","d":"Prosopagnosia is face blindness: inability to recognise familiar faces despite adequate vision, so patients identify people by voice, gait or clothing. It follows damage to the fusiform gyrus in the ventral occipitotemporal cortex, often right-sided or bilateral. It tempts because it is also linked to the right hemisphere, but it belongs to the ventral 'what' stream, whereas neglect is a disorder of the dorsal parietal 'where' stream."}},{"q":"A delirium differs from a major neurocognitive disorder primarily in:","concept":"Delirium and major neurocognitive disorder (NCD) both impair cognition, so examiners test what separates them. DSM-5 (2013) introduced 'major neurocognitive disorder' for what was called dementia, and DSM-5-TR (2022) keeps the criteria. Delirium is defined by: (A) a disturbance in attention (reduced ability to direct, focus, sustain and shift it) and awareness (reduced orientation to the environment); (B) development over a short period, usually hours to a few days, as a change from baseline, with a tendency to fluctuate during the day; (C) an additional cognitive disturbance such as memory deficit, disorientation, or language or perceptual disturbance; (D) not better explained by a pre-existing NCD and not occurring in coma; and (E) evidence of a medical cause, substance intoxication or withdrawal, or multiple causes. It may be hyperactive, hypoactive (easily missed) or mixed. Major NCD is a significant decline from a previous level in one or more of six domains (complex attention, executive function, learning and memory, language, perceptual-motor, social cognition) that interferes with independence; onset is usually insidious, the course gradual, and consciousness clear until late stages. Mild NCD is a modest decline with independence preserved. The two often coexist, and dementia is a major risk factor for delirium. The Confusion Assessment Method of Sharon Inouye and colleagues (1990) captures the distinction: acute onset and fluctuating course plus inattention, together with either disorganised thinking or an altered level of consciousness. Option c is right because acute onset, fluctuating course and a primary disturbance of attention and awareness mark delirium off from major NCD.","traps":{"a":"Hallucinations, usually visual, are common in delirium but not unique to it. Recurrent well-formed visual hallucinations are a core feature of dementia with Lewy bodies, and they also occur in Parkinson's disease dementia and later Alzheimer's disease. Psychotic features are associated with delirium, not defining of it. It tempts because the picture of a hallucinating, agitated delirious patient is vivid and widely taught.","b":"Memory impairment occurs in both conditions. It is the hallmark of major NCD due to Alzheimer's disease, where learning and memory is often the first domain affected, and delirium also disrupts memory, largely because attention fails. Saying 'only in delirium' reverses the usual picture. It tempts because delirious patients often cannot recall the episode afterwards, which looks like a memory disorder.","d":"This reverses the facts. Delirium is usually reversible when its cause is treated, though it can leave lasting decline and carries high mortality in the elderly. Most major NCDs (Alzheimer's, vascular, frontotemporal) are progressive and irreversible; only a minority due to treatable causes, such as vitamin B12 deficiency, hypothyroidism or normal pressure hydrocephalus, improve. It tempts because reversibility is a real difference, stated the wrong way round."}},{"q":"Theory of mind is most commonly assessed in 4–5 year olds using the:","concept":"Theory of mind is the understanding that people have mental states, such as beliefs, desires and intentions, that guide their behaviour and can differ from one's own and from reality. The term comes from David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978), who asked whether chimpanzees have one. The acid test is false belief: can a child predict what someone will do when that person believes something untrue? Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner (1983) built the first false-belief task, in which Maxi's chocolate is moved while he is away. Simon Baron-Cohen, Alan Leslie and Uta Frith (1985) simplified it into the Sally-Anne task. Sally puts her marble in a basket and leaves; Anne moves it to a box; the child is asked where Sally will look for her marble. Passing means saying 'the basket', the place Sally falsely believes it to be. Most typically developing 4-year-olds and children with Down syndrome passed, while about 80 percent of the autistic children failed despite adequate verbal ability, which led to the 'mindblindness' account of autism. Children usually pass first-order false-belief tasks at about 4 to 5 years and second-order tasks ('John thinks that Mary thinks') at about 6 to 7. Do not confuse theory of mind with egocentrism in the three mountains task of Jean Piaget and Barbel Inhelder (1956), which concerns visual perspective. Option c is right because the false-belief (Sally-Anne) task is the standard test of theory of mind at 4 to 5 years.","traps":{"a":"The A-not-B task comes from Jean Piaget's study of object permanence (The Construction of Reality in the Child, 1954 in English). An infant of about 8 to 12 months who has repeatedly found a toy at place A keeps searching at A even after watching it hidden at B. It measures object permanence and inhibitory control, not understanding of others' beliefs. It tempts because, like Sally-Anne, it involves a hidden object moved to a new place.","b":"The conservation of liquid task is Jean Piaget's test of whether a child knows that quantity stays the same despite a change in appearance. Water from one of two equal glasses is poured into a tall, thin glass; preoperational children (about 2 to 7) say it now has more, because of centration and lack of reversibility. It tests reasoning about physical quantity, not mental states. It tempts as the best-known Piagetian task for preschoolers.","d":"The Strange Situation, devised by Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978), is a laboratory procedure of eight short episodes of separation from and reunion with the caregiver, with a stranger present. Used with infants of about 12 to 18 months, it classifies attachment as secure, avoidant or resistant, with disorganised added later by Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1990). It measures attachment quality, not belief understanding, at a younger age."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Battery is used for:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery (HRNB) is the classic fixed battery for detecting and describing brain damage. Ward Halstead (1947), at the University of Chicago, built tests of what he called 'biological intelligence', and his student Ralph Reitan extended and validated them from the 1950s onward. The core adult battery, for ages 15 and above, includes the Category Test (abstract reasoning and concept formation), the Tactual Performance Test (placing blocks in a form-board while blindfolded, then drawing it from memory), the Seashore Rhythm Test, the Speech-Sounds Perception Test, the Finger Tapping Test (motor speed), and the Trail Making Test, Parts A and B (visual scanning, speed and set-shifting). It is usually supplemented by an aphasia screening test, a sensory-perceptual examination and grip strength, often with a Wechsler intelligence scale and the MMPI. Key scores feed the Halstead Impairment Index, the proportion of scores in the impaired range, where 0.5 or more suggests brain dysfunction. Separate versions exist for older children (9 to 14) and younger children (the Reitan-Indiana battery, 5 to 8). Reitan's interpretation combines level of performance, pathognomonic signs, patterns across tests and right-left differences. Contrast this fixed-battery approach with the Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery of Charles Golden and colleagues (1980), based on the qualitative methods of Alexander Luria, and with Edith Kaplan's flexible Boston process approach. Indian clinicians also use the NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery and the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction. Option c is right because the Halstead-Reitan Battery is a comprehensive neuropsychological assessment of brain function.","traps":{"a":"Personality assessment uses tools such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) or Raymond Cattell's 16PF (1949) to measure enduring traits and psychopathology. The MMPI is often given alongside the Halstead-Reitan to judge emotional status, but it is not part of what the battery measures, which is brain-behaviour functioning. It tempts because neuropsychological reports commonly include both kinds of test.","b":"Vocational interest assessment uses inventories such as the Strong Vocational Interest Blank (Edward K. Strong, 1927), the Kuder Preference Record and John L. Holland's RIASEC-based Self-Directed Search. They match a person's likes to occupations and say nothing about brain integrity. It tempts only because the word 'battery' also describes multi-test aptitude batteries, such as the Differential Aptitude Tests, used in career guidance.","d":"Projective assessment of children uses ambiguous stimuli to reveal needs and conflicts, for example the Children's Apperception Test (Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak, 1949), the House-Tree-Person test (John Buck, 1948) and the Rorschach. The Halstead-Reitan uses objective, standardised performance tests scored against norms, and its core form is for ages 15 and over. It tempts because children's versions exist and the Tactual Performance Test looks like play."}},{"q":"The therapeutic factor in group therapy described by Yalom as 'the realisation that one is not alone in their suffering' is:","concept":"Irvin D. Yalom set out the therapeutic factors of group psychotherapy in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy (first edition 1970), building on an earlier list by Raymond Corsini and Bina Rosenberg (1955). He named eleven factors: instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, the corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, interpersonal learning, group cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors. Universality is the discovery that others share similar problems, thoughts and feelings: 'we are all in the same boat'. Many people enter therapy believing that their fears, fantasies or shameful secrets are unique, and hearing others voice the same experience brings strong relief and reduces isolation and shame. It is especially powerful early in a group and in groups built around stigmatised problems, such as survivors of abuse, people with addictions in Alcoholics Anonymous, or carers of people with dementia. Examiners test the factors by one-line definitions, so learn them in contrast. Universality: I am not alone. Instillation of hope: seeing others improve shows that I can too. Altruism: helping others raises my self-worth. Cohesiveness: I belong and am accepted, the group analogue of the therapeutic alliance. Catharsis: releasing strong emotion. Interpersonal learning: discovering how I affect others and correcting distorted perceptions. Imitative behaviour: copying the therapist or other members. Corrective recapitulation: reliving and correcting early family conflicts within the group. Existential factors: accepting responsibility, mortality and aloneness. Option b is right because Yalom's universality is precisely the realisation that one is not alone in one's suffering.","traps":{"a":"Catharsis is the open expression of strong emotion, getting things off one's chest, an idea that goes back to Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's Studies on Hysteria (1895). Yalom stresses that catharsis helps only when combined with cognitive learning and group support. It concerns emotional release, not the discovery of shared suffering. It tempts because pouring out painful feelings is often the moment when members discover they are not alone.","c":"Altruism is the factor in which members help one another through support, reassurance, suggestions and insight. Discovering that one can be useful to others raises self-esteem and draws people out of morbid self-absorption. It is about giving, not about realising that others suffer in the same way. It tempts because both factors arise from members sharing their problems supportively within the same session.","d":"Instillation of hope is faith that treatment can work, gained especially by seeing members who have improved or coped with similar problems. It keeps clients in therapy and is used deliberately in recovery groups, where senior members tell their stories. It concerns optimism about change rather than relief at not being alone. It tempts because both factors come from contact with people who share one's problem."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'AMBIVALENT':","concept":"'Ambivalent' describes holding opposite feelings or attitudes towards the same person, object or idea at the same time, such as love and resentment towards a parent, or both wanting and fearing a promotion. The word is built from Latin 'ambi' (both) and 'valentia' (strength), so it literally means strong pulls in two directions. It entered the language through psychiatry: the Swiss psychiatrist Eugen Bleuler (1911) treated ambivalence as one of the fundamental symptoms of schizophrenia, later summarised as the 'four A's' (loosening of associations, affective flattening, ambivalence and autism). Sigmund Freud took up the term for the love-hate conflict of the Oedipus complex. In motivational interviewing, William R. Miller and Stephen Rollnick (1991) treat ambivalence about change as normal and work by eliciting change talk. The 'anxious-ambivalent' or resistant infants of Mary Ainsworth (1978) both seek and resist the mother at reunion. For synonym questions, first remove antonyms, then near-misses that share a feature but not the core meaning. The usual near-misses are indifferent (no feeling at all), apathetic (lacking interest or emotion), ambiguous (open to two interpretations, used for statements rather than feelings) and equivocal (deliberately vague). Antonyms include decisive, resolute, certain and wholehearted. A good synonym must capture both parts of the meaning, the two feelings and their coexistence, which is why 'torn' and 'conflicted' are also accepted. Option a is right because 'having mixed feelings' exactly matches ambivalent, holding opposing feelings at once.","traps":{"b":"'Indifferent' means having no particular interest, feeling or concern about something; it is close to apathetic or unconcerned. Indifference is the absence of feeling, while ambivalence is too much feeling pulling in opposite directions. It tempts because an ambivalent person often looks indifferent from the outside: torn between two pulls, he fails to act or commit, which an observer can mistake for not caring.","c":"'Decisive' means able to make choices quickly and firmly, or settling an issue conclusively, as in 'a decisive victory'. It is close to an antonym of ambivalent, since mixed feelings usually leave a person hesitant and undecided. It tempts candidates who link the '-valent' ending with 'valiant' or strength and read the word as strong-minded, and antonyms are a deliberate trap in synonym questions.","d":"'Hostile' means unfriendly, antagonistic or showing ill will. Hostility is a single, consistently negative attitude, whereas ambivalence combines positive and negative feelings towards the same target. It tempts because ambivalence is usually taught through the love-hate example, so candidates who remember only the 'hate' half pick the negative word, and because ambivalent relationships can contain open conflict."}},{"q":"The autosomal dominant disorder caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion on chromosome 4 is:","concept":"Huntington's disease was described by the American physician George Huntington (1872) in his paper 'On Chorea'. It is autosomal dominant: one copy of the faulty gene is enough, so each child of an affected parent has a 50 percent risk, and males and females are equally affected. In 1983 James Gusella and colleagues mapped the gene to chromosome 4, and in 1993 the Huntington's Disease Collaborative Research Group identified it: the HTT gene on the short arm (4p16.3), containing a CAG trinucleotide repeat. CAG codes for glutamine, so the expanded repeat produces a huntingtin protein with an abnormally long polyglutamine stretch. Up to 26 repeats is normal, 27 to 35 is intermediate, 36 to 39 gives reduced penetrance, and 40 or more causes the disease with full penetrance. The repeat tends to grow across generations, especially through the father, causing earlier onset (anticipation); juvenile cases usually carry very long repeats. Medium spiny GABAergic neurons of the striatum degenerate, with visible atrophy of the caudate nucleus. The clinical triad is chorea and other movement problems, a subcortical dementia with executive dysfunction, and psychiatric symptoms such as depression, irritability, apathy and raised suicide risk, typically starting between 30 and 50. Tetrabenazine is used for chorea. Other repeat disorders make good variant questions: fragile X syndrome (CGG, X chromosome), myotonic dystrophy (CTG, chromosome 19), Friedreich's ataxia (GAA, chromosome 9, autosomal recessive) and several spinocerebellar ataxias (CAG). Option d is right because Huntington's disease is the autosomal dominant CAG repeat disorder on chromosome 4.","traps":{"a":"Down syndrome is usually caused by trisomy 21, an extra copy of chromosome 21 (most often from nondisjunction, less often translocation or mosaicism). It was described by John Langdon Down (1866), and its chromosomal cause was found by Jerome Lejeune (1959). It causes intellectual disability and early-onset Alzheimer pathology. It is a numerical chromosome error, not a repeat expansion. It tempts as the most famous genetic syndrome in psychology syllabi.","b":"Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal recessive inborn error of metabolism, discovered by Asbjorn Folling (1934). Mutations in the phenylalanine hydroxylase gene on chromosome 12 block conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, and the build-up causes intellectual disability unless a low-phenylalanine diet starts early, which is why newborns are screened. It tempts as the classic gene-environment example, but it is recessive and not a repeat disorder.","c":"Fragile X syndrome is the commonest inherited cause of intellectual disability. It is X-linked and caused by a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene: over 200 repeats is a full mutation that silences the gene, and 55 to 200 is a premutation. Features include a long face, large ears and autism-like behaviour. It tempts strongly because it is also a repeat disorder with anticipation, but the repeat is CGG on the X chromosome."}},{"q":"The biopsychosocial model in psychopathology is most associated with:","concept":"The biopsychosocial model was proposed by the American psychiatrist George L. Engel (1977) in a Science paper titled 'The need for a new medical model: a challenge for biomedicine', and applied to clinical practice in a 1980 paper. Engel attacked the biomedical model, which treats disease as a deviation from measurable biological norms, rests on mind-body dualism, and reduces illness to biochemistry. He argued that it cannot explain why people with the same lesion experience illness so differently, or why some patients with no lesion feel ill. Drawing on general systems theory, he described a hierarchy of nested systems, from molecules and cells through organs and the person to family, community and culture, each affecting the others. Illness therefore emerges from the interaction of biological factors (genes, neurochemistry, physical health), psychological factors (beliefs, coping, emotions, learning) and social factors (relationships, poverty, culture, stigma). In clinical formulation this becomes a grid of predisposing, precipitating, perpetuating and protective factors in each of the three domains. A close relative is the vulnerability-stress (diathesis-stress) model of schizophrenia of Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977). Contrast it with the distractor names: Emil Kraepelin (1899), who built a biological, course-based classification; Eugen Bleuler (1911), who renamed dementia praecox schizophrenia and described its fundamental symptoms; and Aaron T. Beck (1967), whose cognitive model of depression is one psychological strand that the biopsychosocial model would integrate. Option d is right because George L. Engel introduced the biopsychosocial model as an alternative to biomedical reductionism.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin was the German psychiatrist who founded modern psychiatric classification. In the sixth edition of his textbook (1899) he separated dementia praecox from manic-depressive insanity on the basis of course and outcome, and he saw mental illness mainly as brain disease with hereditary causes. His descriptive approach underlies the DSM, whose authors were called neo-Kraepelinians. It tempts because he is a foundational name in psychopathology chapters.","b":"Aaron T. Beck developed the cognitive model of depression (1967) and cognitive therapy, built on the cognitive triad (negative views of self, world and future), dysfunctional schemas and cognitive distortions. This is a psychological model, one of the three strands that the biopsychosocial model brings together, not the integrative model itself. It tempts because Beck's model includes a diathesis-stress element in which life stress activates latent schemas.","c":"Eugen Bleuler, the Swiss psychiatrist, coined the term 'schizophrenia' (in a 1908 lecture, developed in his 1911 monograph) to replace dementia praecox, and described its fundamental symptoms, later summarised as the four A's: associations, affect, ambivalence and autism. He also stressed psychological understanding of symptoms, but he did not propose a biopsychosocial model. It tempts because he is a major historical name in descriptive psychopathology."}},{"q":"The temperamental category in the New York Longitudinal Study (Thomas & Chess) characterised by withdrawal from new stimuli but gradual adaptation is:","concept":"Temperament refers to early-appearing, biologically based individual differences in emotional reactivity and self-regulation. The New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), begun in 1956 by Alexander Thomas, Stella Chess and Herbert Birch, followed children from infancy into adulthood using detailed parent interviews. They rated nine dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity (regularity of eating and sleeping), approach or withdrawal to new stimuli, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span and persistence. From these they described three types. Easy children (about 40 percent) quickly establish regular routines, are generally cheerful and adapt easily. Difficult children (about 10 percent) have irregular routines, are slow to accept new experiences and react negatively and intensely. Slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent) are inactive, show mild, low-key reactions, are often negative in mood, and withdraw at first from new people and situations, but adapt gradually with repeated, unpressured exposure. About 35 percent fitted no category. The crucial distinction is between difficult and slow-to-warm-up: both withdraw from novelty, but the difficult child reacts intensely, while the slow-to-warm-up child reacts mildly and adjusts slowly. Thomas and Chess (1977) also proposed goodness of fit: outcomes depend on how well parenting matches the child's temperament. Related models include Jerome Kagan's behavioural inhibition, studied from the 1980s, which separates shy, fearful 'inhibited' children from bold 'uninhibited' ones, and Mary Rothbart's dimensions of surgency, negative affectivity and effortful control. Option b is right because initial withdrawal from new stimuli followed by gradual adaptation defines the slow-to-warm-up child.","traps":{"a":"Easy children, about 40 percent of the NYLS sample, quickly establish regular routines in infancy, are generally cheerful, and approach and adapt to new experiences easily. They show approach rather than withdrawal. It tempts only because 'gradual adaptation' in the stem resembles adaptability, an easy-child trait, but easy children adapt quickly and without the initial withdrawal the stem describes.","c":"Difficult children, about 10 percent of the NYLS sample, have irregular daily routines, are slow to accept new experiences, and tend to react negatively and intensely, with loud crying and tantrums; they are at higher risk of later adjustment problems. They also withdraw from novelty, which is why this tempts. The key difference is intensity: difficult children react strongly, while slow-to-warm-up children react mildly and gradually adapt.","d":"'Inhibited' is Jerome Kagan's term for children high in behavioural inhibition, who react to unfamiliar people, objects and situations with shyness, vigilance and fearful withdrawal, as opposed to bold 'uninhibited' children. It comes from a separate research programme and is not one of the Thomas and Chess categories, and 'only' makes the option extreme. It tempts because inhibition sounds very close to slow-to-warm-up."}},{"q":"Standardisation in test construction primarily refers to:","concept":"Anne Anastasi (1954) defined a psychological test as an objective and standardised measure of a sample of behaviour. Standardisation has two linked meanings. First, uniformity of procedure: the same materials, instructions, time limits, demonstrations, ways of answering queries, testing conditions and scoring rules for everyone, all laid down in the test manual. Without this, a score difference could reflect the examiner rather than the examinee. Second, norms: the test is given to a large standardisation (normative) sample that represents the population for which it is meant, by age, sex, region, education and similar variables. A raw score has no meaning on its own; it becomes interpretable only when converted into a derived score relative to these norms, such as a percentile rank, z-score, T-score, stanine or deviation IQ, or into age and grade equivalents. Tests used in India often need fresh Indian norms, because foreign norms may misplace Indian examinees. In the sequence of test construction, standardisation follows planning, item writing, tryout and item analysis, and runs alongside reliability and validity studies. Keep the neighbouring properties distinct. Reliability is consistency of scores (test-retest, split-half, internal consistency, inter-rater). Validity is whether the test measures what it claims (content, criterion-related and construct validity). Objectivity is agreement among scorers. Fairness concerns freedom from bias across groups. Standardisation supports all of these but is not the same as any of them. Option c is right because standardisation means fixed procedures for administration and scoring, together with norms for interpreting scores.","traps":{"a":"Validity is the degree to which a test measures what it claims to measure, established through content, criterion-related (concurrent and predictive) and construct validity, the last set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955). Standardisation is a precondition for meaningful scores, but a fully standardised test can still be invalid. It tempts because validity is the most valued property of any test and seems like the ultimate aim.","b":"Eliminating cultural bias is the concern of test fairness. Culture-fair or culture-reduced tests, such as Raven's Progressive Matrices (John C. Raven, 1938) and Raymond Cattell's Culture Fair Intelligence Test, cut down language and cultural content. Standardisation alone does not remove bias: a test standardised on one group can still be unfair to another. It tempts because a representative norm group sounds like a guarantee of fairness.","d":"Computer administration is only a mode of delivery. It can increase uniformity, and computerised adaptive tests are standardised, but a paper-and-pencil test given by a trained examiner who follows the manual is just as standardised. Standardisation is about fixed procedures and norms, not the medium. It tempts because computers seem to guarantee identical conditions for every examinee and sound modern and objective."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression focuses primarily on:","concept":"Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald L. Klerman, Myrna M. Weissman and colleagues in the 1970s and manualised in Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression (1984). Its roots are the interpersonal school of Harry Stack Sullivan (1953) and John Bowlby's (1969) attachment theory, plus research linking depression to life events and social support. IPT does not claim that relationships alone cause depression; it holds that depression occurs in an interpersonal context and that mood and relationships influence each other. It is time-limited, usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions, in three phases. The initial phase gives the diagnosis, assigns the 'sick role', takes an interpersonal inventory of key relationships and picks a focal problem area. The middle phase works on that area. The final phase consolidates gains and prepares for ending. There are four problem areas: grief (complicated bereavement after a death), interpersonal role disputes (non-reciprocal expectations with a significant other), role transitions (life changes such as marriage, divorce, a new job, retirement or childbirth) and interpersonal deficits (social isolation or lack of lasting relationships). The therapist is active and supportive and focuses on present relationships, not on childhood or the transference. In the NIMH Treatment of Depression Collaborative Research Program (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989), IPT performed comparably to cognitive behaviour therapy and imipramine. Adaptations exist for adolescents, groups, perinatal depression and bipolar disorder. Option b is right because IPT targets current interpersonal problem areas: grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits.","traps":{"a":"Childhood unconscious conflicts are the focus of psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy from Sigmund Freud onward, which works through free association and the interpretation of transference and resistance. IPT does not interpret unconscious or childhood material; it stays with current relationships. It tempts because IPT grew from Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal psychiatry, which had psychodynamic roots, and 'interpersonal' sounds like relational psychodynamic work.","c":"Cognitive distortions, such as overgeneralisation and personalisation, are the target of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979), which teaches patients to identify and test automatic thoughts. IPT may notice negative thinking but does not make it the target, and 'only' makes the option extreme. It tempts because CBT and IPT are the two best-supported brief therapies for depression and are compared in trials.","d":"Reinforcement schedules come from B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning (Charles Ferster and B. F. Skinner, Schedules of Reinforcement, 1957): fixed and variable ratio and interval schedules. Behavioural treatments of depression, such as behavioural activation, try to increase rewarding activity, but that is not IPT. It tempts as a filler for candidates who know behavioural models of depression also stress reduced positive reinforcement from other people."}},{"q":"Identify the correctly spelt word:","concept":"'Conscientious' means careful, thorough and diligent, guided by a sense of duty or conscience: a conscientious nurse checks every dose twice. The spelling is easiest to fix through its root. It comes from 'conscience', from Latin 'conscientia' (con, 'with', plus scire, 'to know'), the same 'sci' root found in science, conscious and omniscient. So the word is built as con + sci + en + tious: c-o-n-s-c-i-e-n-t-i-o-u-s, thirteen letters. Take 'conscience', drop the final 'ce', and add 'tious', the ending also seen in cautious, ambitious and nutritious. The 'sci' is pronounced like 'shi', as in conscious, which is why the 's', the 'c' or the 'i' gets lost in common misspellings. Keep four look-alike words apart: conscious (aware or awake), conscience (the inner moral sense), conscientious (diligent and dutiful) and conscientiousness (the quality of being diligent). A 'conscientious objector' refuses military service on moral grounds. In personality psychology, conscientiousness is one of the Big Five traits, measured by the NEO Personality Inventory of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (revised 1992). It covers orderliness, dutifulness, achievement striving, self-discipline and deliberation, and it is the Big Five trait that most consistently predicts academic and job performance. Spelling items usually present one correct form among three that each drop or swap a single letter, so check each option against the root letter by letter rather than by overall look. Option d is right because 'conscientious' is the only option that keeps the full 'consci' root followed by 'entious'.","traps":{"a":"'Conscentious' keeps 'cons' but drops the 'i' after 'sc', giving 'scen' instead of 'scien'. That breaks the link with 'conscience' and 'science', which both carry 'sci'. It tempts because in speech the middle of the word blurs into 'shen', so the 'i' is easy to lose, and the first part looks like a real word, close to 'consent'.","b":"'Concientious' drops the 's' of 'cons', so 'consci' becomes 'conci'. Since 'c' before 'i' already sounds soft, writers often leave out the 's', the same slip that produces 'concious' for 'conscious'. It tempts because the word still looks balanced and is pronounced the same way, and Spanish spellings such as 'conciencia' make the 'conci' form look familiar.","c":"'Consientious' drops the 'c' of 'sci', so 'consci' becomes 'consi'. It is a phonetic spelling, written as the word sounds, and the silent 'c' after 's' is the letter most often lost, as in 'sissors' for 'scissors' or 'sience' for 'science'. It tempts because it looks plausible at a glance and only one letter is missing, which is exactly how spelling distractors are built."}},{"q":"The Yerkes–Dodson law predicts that performance on a complex task is best at:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law comes from Robert M. Yerkes and John D. Dodson (1908), who trained 'dancing mice' to choose between a white and a black box, giving electric shocks of different strengths for wrong choices. When the discrimination was easy, learning improved as shock strength increased; when it was difficult, learning was fastest at an intermediate shock and worse with strong shock. The finding was later generalised into a law about arousal, especially after Donald Hebb (1955) linked it to arousal theory. The law has two parts. First, the relation between arousal and performance is an inverted U: performance rises with arousal up to an optimum, then falls as arousal becomes excessive. Second, the optimum depends on task difficulty: simple or well-learned tasks are performed best at relatively high arousal, while complex or novel tasks are performed best at lower, moderate arousal. A common explanation is the cue-utilisation hypothesis of James A. Easterbrook (1959): high arousal narrows attention, cutting out irrelevant cues, which helps simple tasks, but also relevant ones, which hurts complex tasks. Neighbouring ideas make good variants. Clark Hull's (1943) drive theory predicts that more drive always strengthens the dominant response. Robert Zajonc's (1965) social facilitation theory says the presence of others raises arousal, improving simple tasks and impairing complex ones. In clinical and educational settings the law explains why moderate anxiety can help exam performance while severe test anxiety disrupts it. Option d is right because complex tasks peak at moderate arousal, with the optimum lower than for simple tasks.","traps":{"a":"On the inverted U, very high arousal impairs performance on nearly all tasks, and complex tasks suffer most, through narrowed attention (James A. Easterbrook, 1959), anxiety and intrusive worry. Only simple, well-practised tasks tolerate fairly high arousal, and even they decline at extreme levels. It tempts because of Clark Hull's (1943) drive theory and the everyday belief that more pressure always produces better effort.","b":"Very low arousal is the left tail of the inverted U: the person is drowsy, bored and inattentive, and performance is poor on every kind of task. The law says the optimum for complex tasks is lower than for simple ones, not that it is very low. It tempts candidates who remember 'lower arousal for complex tasks' and push the idea to the extreme.","c":"An optimum independent of task difficulty contradicts the second half of the law. The shift of the optimum with difficulty was the core finding of Yerkes and Dodson's 1908 mouse experiments, where strong shock helped easy discriminations but hurt hard ones. It tempts because many textbooks draw a single inverted-U curve and omit the task-difficulty interaction, so candidates think of one fixed optimum."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a Cluster A personality disorder?","concept":"A personality disorder is an enduring pattern of inner experience and behaviour that deviates markedly from the expectations of the person's culture, is pervasive and inflexible, begins by adolescence or early adulthood, is stable over time and causes distress or impairment. DSM-III (1980) placed personality disorders on Axis II and grouped them into three clusters; DSM-5 (2013) dropped the multiaxial system but kept the ten disorders and the clusters, and DSM-5-TR (2022) retains them. Cluster A, odd or eccentric: paranoid (pervasive distrust and suspiciousness), schizoid (detachment from relationships and restricted emotional expression) and schizotypal (discomfort in close relationships, cognitive or perceptual distortions and eccentric behaviour). Cluster A has a genetic link to the schizophrenia spectrum. Cluster B, dramatic, emotional or erratic: antisocial, borderline, histrionic and narcissistic. Cluster C, anxious or fearful: avoidant, dependent and obsessive-compulsive. A common memory aid is weird, wild and worried. The most tested contrast is schizoid versus avoidant: both people are socially isolated, but the schizoid person neither desires nor enjoys relationships and is indifferent to praise or criticism, whereas the avoidant person longs for relationships but avoids them through fear of criticism, rejection and inadequacy. Another variant asks which disorder ICD treats differently: ICD-10 and ICD-11 place schizotypal disorder with schizophrenia, not among personality disorders. ICD-11 (in effect from 2022) replaced the categories with severity levels and trait domain qualifiers (negative affectivity, detachment, dissociality, disinhibition, anankastia) plus a borderline pattern specifier. Option b is right because avoidant personality disorder belongs to Cluster C, the anxious or fearful cluster, not Cluster A.","traps":{"a":"Paranoid personality disorder is a Cluster A condition: pervasive distrust and suspiciousness from early adulthood. The person reads hidden demeaning meanings into harmless remarks, bears grudges, suspects a partner's fidelity without reason and is reluctant to confide, without being psychotic. As a Cluster A disorder it cannot be the answer. It tempts because guarded, suspicious people can look anxious and fearful, which pulls the mind toward Cluster C.","c":"Schizotypal personality disorder is in DSM Cluster A: acute discomfort in close relationships, cognitive or perceptual distortions (ideas of reference, odd beliefs, magical thinking, unusual perceptions) and eccentric behaviour. It is genetically linked to schizophrenia, and ICD-10 and ICD-11 place schizotypal disorder in the schizophrenia chapter rather than among personality disorders. It tempts because of this ICD difference, which makes candidates think it falls outside the cluster.","d":"Schizoid personality disorder is in Cluster A: detachment from social relationships and a restricted range of emotional expression. The person neither desires nor enjoys close relationships, chooses solitary activities, seems indifferent to praise or criticism and appears cold. It tempts most because, like the avoidant person, the schizoid person is isolated; the difference is that the schizoid person does not want closeness, while the avoidant person wants it but fears rejection."}},{"q":"In Freud's psychosexual theory, fixation at the anal stage is hypothesised to produce:","concept":"Sigmund Freud (1905) set out his psychosexual stages in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality. Libido, the sexual or life energy, is focused on a different erogenous zone at each stage: oral (birth to about 18 months), anal (about 18 months to 3 years), phallic (3 to 6 years, the stage of the Oedipus complex), latency (6 years to puberty) and genital (puberty onward). Too much frustration or too much gratification can cause fixation, in which part of the libido stays invested in that stage and shapes adult character. In the anal stage pleasure centres on holding in and expelling faeces, and the key conflict is toilet training, the child's first major clash with parental control. In his paper 'Character and Anal Erotism' (1908), Freud described the anal character triad of orderliness, parsimony (stinginess) and obstinacy. Harsh, early or punitive toilet training is said to produce the anal-retentive personality: neat, rigid, controlling, stingy and stubborn, close to obsessive-compulsive traits. Lax or overindulgent training is said to produce the anal-expulsive personality: messy, disorganised, careless, defiant and sometimes over-generous. Erik Erikson (1950) recast the same period as autonomy versus shame and doubt. Variants test the other stages: oral fixation with dependency, smoking or sarcasm; phallic fixation with vanity or problems with authority; and the Oedipus complex as the phallic conflict, not a fixation outcome. These ideas are historically central but have little empirical support. Option c is right because anal fixation is held to produce the anal-retentive (orderly, stingy) or anal-expulsive personality.","traps":{"a":"Oral aggression belongs to fixation at the oral stage (birth to about 18 months), when pleasure centres on sucking, biting and swallowing. Fixation in the later, teething part of this stage is linked to oral-aggressive traits such as sarcasm, verbal hostility and nail biting, while earlier fixation is linked to dependency and gullibility. It is the wrong stage. It tempts because aggression resembles the defiance and stubbornness of the anal character.","b":"The Oedipus complex is the central conflict of the phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years): the boy's desire for the mother and rivalry with the father, resolved through castration anxiety and identification with the father, which forms the superego. Carl Jung (1913) proposed the term Electra complex for girls, which Freud rejected. It belongs to a different stage and is a conflict, not a fixation outcome. It tempts as the most famous Freudian term.","d":"'Genital identity confusion' is not a Freudian concept. The genital stage begins at puberty and aims at mature love and work; as the final stage, it is not linked to a fixation character. The phrase blends Freud's genital stage with Erik Erikson's adolescent crisis of identity versus role confusion (Childhood and Society, 1950). It tempts because it sounds technical and mixes terms from two familiar stage theories."}},{"q":"Item response theory (IRT) differs from classical test theory primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Classical test theory (CTT), going back to Charles Spearman (1904), says that an observed score equals a true score plus random error (X = T + E). Its item statistics are the difficulty index p (proportion answering correctly) and a discrimination index such as the item-total correlation. These are sample-dependent: the same item looks easy in a bright group and hard in a weak one, and a person's score depends on which items were given. CTT also gives a single standard error of measurement for everyone. Item response theory (IRT), developed through the work of Georg Rasch (1960) and of Frederic Lord and Melvin Novick (1968), models each item separately. It states the probability of a correct response as a function, usually logistic, of the person's latent trait level (theta) and the item's parameters. The one-parameter or Rasch model uses difficulty (b) only; the two-parameter model adds discrimination (a), the slope of the curve; the three-parameter model of Allan Birnbaum (1968) adds a pseudo-guessing parameter (c), the lower asymptote. Polytomous models, such as the graded response model of Fumiko Samejima (1969), handle rating scales. IRT assumes unidimensionality and local independence. If the model fits, item parameters are invariant across samples, ability estimates are invariant across item sets, and precision varies with theta through information functions. These properties make IRT the basis of computerised adaptive testing, item banking, test equating and analysis of differential item functioning. Option d is right because IRT models response probability as a function of the latent trait and item parameters.","traps":{"a":"This describes classical test theory, not IRT. In CTT the difficulty index (proportion passing) and discrimination index change with the sample: an item looks easy in a bright group and hard in a weak one. IRT's main selling point is parameter invariance: if the model fits, item parameters estimated in different samples should agree, up to a linear transformation. It tempts because it is a true statement about the wrong theory.","b":"A single difficulty parameter describes only the one-parameter logistic or Rasch model (Georg Rasch, 1960). IRT also includes the two-parameter model, which adds discrimination, the three-parameter model of Allan Birnbaum (1968), which adds guessing, and polytomous models for rating scales. The words 'always' and 'only' make the option false. It tempts because the Rasch model is usually the first IRT model taught.","c":"IRT is in fact the foundation of computerised adaptive testing. Because items and persons sit on the same theta scale, the computer picks each next item to match the current ability estimate, where it gives the most information, so precise scores come from fewer items. It tempts because adaptive tests give different examinees different items, which seems to make scores incomparable, a problem that IRT itself solves."}},{"q":"In CBT, a 'downward arrow' technique is used to:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck's cognitive model, set out in Cognitive Therapy of Depression (1979) with John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery, describes three levels of cognition. Automatic thoughts are quick, situation-specific thoughts at the surface ('She didn't reply to my message'). Intermediate beliefs are rules, attitudes and assumptions ('If people don't respond, they don't like me'). Core beliefs are deep, global, absolute beliefs about oneself, others or the world, usually grouped as helpless, unlovable or worthless. The downward arrow (or vertical arrow) technique, popularised by David D. Burns in Feeling Good (1980) and taught in Judith S. Beck's Cognitive Therapy: Basics and Beyond (1995), moves from the surface level to the deepest. Instead of disputing an automatic thought, the therapist temporarily accepts it as true and asks, 'If that were true, what would it mean to you? What would be so bad about that?' Each answer is written below the previous one with an arrow, and the questioning continues until a core belief appears, for example: 'I made an error in the report', then 'My boss will think I'm careless', then 'I'll lose my job', then 'I'm a failure'. The belief is then tested with evidence and behavioural experiments. Neighbouring techniques: Socratic questioning (guided discovery), the thought record, functional ABC analysis, subjective distress ratings and exposure hierarchies, the last three drawn from behaviour therapy. Option a is right because the downward arrow uncovers the core belief beneath an automatic thought by repeatedly asking what it would mean if the thought were true.","traps":{"b":"Identifying behavioural antecedents is functional or ABC analysis from behaviour therapy: Antecedent (trigger), Behaviour, and Consequence that maintains it. It is used to plan interventions, for example for self-injury or compulsions, and looks at external triggers and reinforcers rather than deeper layers of meaning. It tempts because CBT assessment links situations, thoughts, feelings and behaviour, and Albert Ellis's (1962) ABC model uses the same letters.","c":"Rating symptom intensity from 0 to 10 is a subjective rating scale, like the Subjective Units of Distress used in behaviour therapy, often scored 0 to 100, or the belief and mood ratings in a thought record. It measures how strong an emotion or belief is and tracks change, but it does not uncover meaning. It tempts because therapists sometimes rate belief strength along a downward arrow, yet rating is not its purpose.","d":"An exposure hierarchy is a graded list of feared situations, ranked from least to most anxiety-provoking, used in systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), graded exposure and exposure and response prevention. The client works up it step by step. It is a behavioural method for reducing fear, not a cognitive method for uncovering beliefs. It tempts because both techniques involve a stepwise vertical list that looks similar on paper."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: 'Had she taken the medication, she ___ recovered sooner.'","concept":"English has four main conditional patterns, and exams test which result clause goes with which condition. Zero conditional, for general truths: 'If + present, present' ('If you heat ice, it melts'). First conditional, for a real future possibility: 'If + present, will + base verb' ('If she takes the medication, she will recover'). Second conditional, for an unreal or unlikely present or future: 'If + past simple, would + base verb' ('If she took the medication, she would recover'). Third conditional, for an unreal past, something that did not happen: 'If + past perfect, would have + past participle' ('If she had taken the medication, she would have recovered sooner'). Mixed conditionals combine a past condition with a present result ('If she had taken the medication, she would be well now'). In formal English the 'if' can be dropped and the auxiliary moved before the subject, a pattern called inversion: 'Had she taken' means 'If she had taken'; 'Were I you' means 'If I were you'; 'Should you need help' means 'If you should need help'. So a sentence opening with 'Had' plus a past participle signals the third conditional, and its result clause needs 'would have' (or could have, might have) plus a past participle. A common error is to put 'would have' into the if-clause ('If she would have taken'), which is non-standard. Option d is right because the inverted past perfect condition 'Had she taken' requires the third-conditional result 'would have recovered'.","traps":{"a":"'Would recover' is the result clause of the second conditional (would + base verb), which pairs with a past simple condition such as 'If she took the medication', for an unreal present or future. It cannot follow the past perfect 'Had she taken', which places the condition in the past. It tempts because 'would' is correct in itself; only the missing 'have' and past participle make it wrong.","b":"'Had recovered' is the past perfect, the form that belongs in the if-clause of a third conditional, not in the result clause. 'Had she taken the medication, she had recovered sooner' repeats the condition form and lacks the modal 'would'. It tempts because it echoes the opening 'Had', and since both clauses refer to the past, matching their tenses seems logical.","c":"'Will recover' is the result clause of the first conditional (will + base verb), which pairs with 'If she takes the medication' for a real future possibility. It cannot follow a past counterfactual condition, so 'Had she taken ..., she will recover' is ungrammatical. It tempts because 'recover sooner' sounds like a future outcome, and 'will' is the default way to talk about the future."}}],"met-mock-3":[{"q":"During an action potential, the absolute refractory period exists primarily because:","concept":"An action potential is a brief, all-or-none reversal of the membrane potential that travels down an axon. Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) worked out its ionic basis in the squid giant axon. At rest the inside sits near -70 mV, a gradient kept up by the Na+/K+ ATPase and by K+ leak channels. When depolarisation reaches threshold (about -55 mV), voltage-gated Na+ channels open, Na+ rushes in and the membrane swings positive. Each Na+ channel has two gates: a fast activation gate and a slower inactivation gate, often pictured as a hinged lid or 'ball and chain' that soon plugs the pore. An inactivated channel is not simply closed; no voltage can open it until the membrane has repolarised, which removes the inactivation and returns it to the closed, ready state. Meanwhile voltage-gated K+ channels open more slowly, K+ leaves, and the membrane repolarises and briefly overshoots into after-hyperpolarisation. The absolute refractory period (roughly 1 to 2 ms) is the window when too many Na+ channels are inactivated for any stimulus, however strong, to fire a second spike. The relative refractory period follows: some Na+ channels have recovered, but open K+ channels and hyperpolarisation mean only a stronger than normal stimulus works. Refractoriness keeps spikes separate, caps the firing rate, and makes conduction one-way, since the patch just behind the spike cannot refire. Stimulus intensity is therefore coded by firing frequency, not spike size. Option a is right because Na+ channel inactivation, reversed only by repolarisation, is the direct cause of the absolute refractory period.","traps":{"b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase pumps three Na+ out and two K+ in, using ATP, to maintain the concentration gradients that make spikes possible. It runs continuously and slowly; a single action potential moves only a tiny fraction of the ions, so the pump does not shut down after a spike and does not set the refractory window. It tempts because it is the best-known ion-transport mechanism in the neuron chapter.","c":"Voltage-gated K+ channels open slowly during the spike and stay open into the after-hyperpolarisation, which is why they contribute to the relative refractory period. They are never permanently inactivated; if they were, the neuron could not repolarise or fire again. The word 'permanently' makes the option impossible. It tempts because K+ channels really do matter for refractoriness, just for the later, relative phase.","d":"Cl- influx, for example through GABA-A receptors, makes the inside more negative or holds it near rest, producing inhibitory postsynaptic potentials at synapses on dendrites and the cell body. That is synaptic inhibition imposed by another neuron, not an intrinsic property of an axon that has just fired. It tempts because it also makes firing harder, which sounds like refractoriness."}},{"q":"The release of neurotransmitter at the synaptic terminal is most directly triggered by:","concept":"Most neurons communicate chemically, and the sequence at the presynaptic terminal is a favourite exam chain. The action potential travels down the axon and depolarises the terminal button. That depolarisation opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels clustered at the active zones, where synaptic vesicles are docked. Ca2+ is roughly ten thousand times more concentrated outside the cell than inside, so it rushes in, and this local Ca2+ rise is the direct trigger for release. Ca2+ binds synaptotagmin, the calcium sensor on the vesicle, which lets the SNARE proteins (synaptobrevin on the vesicle, syntaxin and SNAP-25 on the membrane) zip together and fuse the vesicle with the membrane. Transmitter spills into the cleft by exocytosis, binds ionotropic or metabotropic receptors, and is cleared by reuptake, by enzymes such as acetylcholinesterase, or by diffusion. Jose del Castillo and Bernard Katz (1954) showed at the frog neuromuscular junction that transmitter is released in packets, or quanta, and Katz's group later showed that release fails without external Ca2+. Clinical links make good variant questions. Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins and blocks acetylcholine release, causing flaccid paralysis. In Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome, antibodies attack the presynaptic voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, so less acetylcholine is released; contrast myasthenia gravis, where antibodies attack the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptors. Keep the ions straight: Na+ influx drives the spike, K+ efflux ends it, and Cl- influx is a postsynaptic inhibitory event. Option d is right because Ca2+ entry through voltage-gated Ca2+ channels is the immediate signal that makes vesicles fuse and release transmitter.","traps":{"a":"Delayed rectifier K+ channels open late in the action potential, let K+ flow out, and repolarise the membrane. In the terminal this shortens depolarisation and so limits Ca2+ entry; drugs that block K+ channels, such as 4-aminopyridine, actually prolong the spike and increase release. K+ efflux ends the signal rather than triggering it. It tempts because it is a genuine step in every action potential.","b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase is a pump, not a gated channel: it constantly moves three Na+ out and two K+ in, using ATP, to maintain the resting gradients. It does not open or close with each spike, and stopping it (as with ouabain) only slowly runs the gradients down. Vesicle fusion does not depend on its moment-to-moment activity. It tempts because it is the best-known ion mechanism in the neuron chapter.","c":"Cl- influx through GABA-A receptors happens on the postsynaptic neuron after GABA has already been released, producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential that moves the cell away from threshold. Benzodiazepines and barbiturates enhance this current. It is a consequence of release at an inhibitory synapse, not the presynaptic trigger for it. It tempts because it is a receptor-linked ion flow that happens at the synapse."}},{"q":"The mesocortical dopamine pathway, when underactive, is most associated with:","concept":"The brain has four main dopamine pathways, and exams routinely ask you to match each to a symptom or side effect. The mesolimbic pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) in the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens and limbic structures; it carries reward and salience, and its overactivity is linked to positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions). The mesocortical pathway also starts in the VTA but projects to the prefrontal cortex, especially dorsolateral and ventromedial regions; underactivity here is linked to negative symptoms (avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality, blunted affect) and to cognitive deficits in working memory, attention and executive function. The nigrostriatal pathway runs from the substantia nigra pars compacta to the dorsal striatum and controls movement; its loss causes Parkinson's disease, and D2 blockade here causes extrapyramidal side effects. The tuberoinfundibular pathway runs from the hypothalamus to the pituitary, where dopamine inhibits prolactin; blockade causes hyperprolactinaemia. Kenneth Davis and colleagues (1991) revised the dopamine hypothesis into this two-part form: too much subcortical dopamine, too little prefrontal dopamine. Earlier, Timothy Crow (1980) had split schizophrenia into Type I (positive symptoms, good drug response) and Type II (negative symptoms, poorer response, structural brain changes). The model explains a clinical puzzle: typical antipsychotics block D2 receptors in every pathway, so they calm positive symptoms but can worsen negative and cognitive ones. Option d is right because an underactive mesocortical projection deprives the prefrontal cortex of dopamine, producing the negative and cognitive symptoms of schizophrenia.","traps":{"a":"Positive symptoms such as hallucinations, delusions and disorganised speech are attributed to excess dopamine in the mesolimbic pathway, from the VTA to the nucleus accumbens. This is why D2-blocking antipsychotics reduce them. The stem asks about an underactive mesocortical pathway, the opposite direction and a different target. It tempts because both pathways start in the VTA and both are tied to schizophrenia.","b":"Parkinsonian rigidity, tremor and bradykinesia come from dopamine loss in the nigrostriatal pathway, from the substantia nigra pars compacta to the dorsal striatum. Antipsychotics that block D2 receptors there cause drug-induced parkinsonism, one of the extrapyramidal side effects. This is a motor circuit, not the cortical projection in the stem. It tempts because it is also a dopamine-deficiency state, so 'underactive' points students toward it.","c":"Hyperprolactinaemia arises when dopamine stops acting on the pituitary through the tuberoinfundibular pathway, because dopamine normally inhibits prolactin secretion. Antipsychotic D2 blockade is the classic cause, producing galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea and sexual dysfunction. This pathway runs from the hypothalamus to the pituitary, not to the cortex. It tempts because it is a genuine result of reduced dopamine signalling, just in a different pathway."}},{"q":"Which structure is the principal source of acetylcholine projections to the cortex and is severely depleted in Alzheimer's disease?","concept":"Each major neuromodulator in the brain comes from a small cluster of neurons with very wide projections, so if you know the transmitter you can name the source. Acetylcholine reaches the cortex mainly from the basal forebrain. The nucleus basalis of Meynert, named after the 19th-century neuroanatomist Theodor Meynert, lies in the substantia innominata beneath the basal ganglia and sends cholinergic fibres to almost the entire neocortex and the amygdala. The nearby medial septal nucleus and diagonal band of Broca supply the hippocampus. Brainstem cholinergic cells in the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei project mainly to the thalamus and help control arousal and REM sleep. In Alzheimer's disease the nucleus basalis loses many of its neurons and cortical choline acetyltransferase falls sharply. This led to the cholinergic hypothesis of Raymond Bartus and colleagues (1982), which linked age-related and Alzheimer memory decline to cholinergic loss. The hypothesis underlies the cholinesterase inhibitors donepezil, rivastigmine and galantamine, which slow the breakdown of the acetylcholine that remains; memantine, by contrast, is an NMDA glutamate antagonist. Anticholinergic drugs such as scopolamine impair memory, which supports the same link. Learn the other source nuclei together: locus coeruleus in the pons for noradrenaline, raphe nuclei in the midline brainstem for serotonin, ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra in the midbrain for dopamine, and the tuberomammillary nucleus of the hypothalamus for histamine. Option c is right because the nucleus basalis of Meynert is the main cholinergic source for the cortex and degenerates severely in Alzheimer's disease.","traps":{"a":"The locus coeruleus, a small blue-tinged nucleus in the dorsal pons, is the main source of noradrenaline for the cortex, hippocampus and cerebellum. It regulates arousal, vigilance and the stress response. It is affected early in Alzheimer's disease, which makes it tempting, but its transmitter is noradrenaline, not acetylcholine. Remember: coeruleus means blue, and its signature transmitter is noradrenaline.","b":"The raphe nuclei lie along the midline of the brainstem and are the main source of serotonin (5-HT) for the forebrain and spinal cord. They are involved in mood, sleep, appetite and impulse control, and their system is the target of SSRIs. They tempt because they form another diffuse modulatory system with cortex-wide projections, but the transmitter is serotonin, not acetylcholine.","d":"The ventral tegmental area in the midbrain is a dopamine source. It gives rise to the mesolimbic pathway to the nucleus accumbens (reward, positive symptoms) and the mesocortical pathway to the prefrontal cortex (cognition, negative symptoms). Its cortical projection makes it look plausible, but the transmitter is dopamine, and its key disease links are addiction and schizophrenia, not Alzheimer's disease."}},{"q":"The blood–brain barrier is formed primarily by:","concept":"The blood-brain barrier (BBB) keeps most substances in the blood out of brain tissue, protecting neurons from toxins, pathogens and swings in blood chemistry. Paul Ehrlich (1885) found that dyes injected into the bloodstream stained body organs but not the brain, and his student Edwin Goldmann (1913) showed the reverse: dye injected into the cerebrospinal fluid stained the brain but not the body. The barrier lies in the capillary wall itself. Brain endothelial cells are joined by tight junctions (built from proteins such as claudins and occludin), lack the fenestrations (pores) found in many other capillaries, and do very little vesicle transport, so molecules cannot slip between or through them. Astrocyte end-feet wrap the capillaries and, with pericytes, induce and maintain these junctions; together they form the neurovascular unit. Small lipid-soluble molecules cross easily (oxygen, alcohol, nicotine, anaesthetics; heroin enters faster than morphine), and nutrients cross on specific transporters, such as glucose via GLUT1. This is why Parkinson's disease is treated with L-DOPA, which uses an amino acid carrier, rather than dopamine, which cannot cross. A few circumventricular organs lack the barrier so they can sense blood chemistry: the area postrema (chemoreceptor trigger zone for vomiting), subfornical organ, OVLT, median eminence and posterior pituitary. Do not confuse the BBB with the blood-CSF barrier at the choroid plexus, formed by tight junctions between epithelial cells. Option a is right because the barrier is formed by tight junctions between capillary endothelial cells, supported by astrocyte end-feet.","traps":{"b":"The pia mater is the thin, innermost meningeal layer that clings to the brain surface, with the CSF-filled subarachnoid space just outside it. It does not form a selective barrier, and fenestrations (pores) would make vessels leakier, not tighter. Fenestrated capillaries are exactly what the circumventricular organs have, where the barrier is absent. It tempts because the meninges sound like a protective covering.","c":"Microglia are the brain's resident immune cells, related to macrophages. They patrol the tissue, engulf debris and dead cells, prune synapses during development, and become activated in inflammation and neurodegeneration. Their phagocytic activity is an immune defence inside the brain, not a physical seal on the capillary wall. It tempts because 'protecting the brain' sounds like a barrier function.","d":"Oligodendrocytes wrap segments of central nervous system axons in myelin (Schwann cells do this in the peripheral system), allowing fast saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier. They do not wrap capillaries, and their loss causes demyelinating disease such as multiple sclerosis, not barrier failure. It tempts because they are another glial cell and 'sheath' suggests a sealing layer."}},{"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced primarily by the:","concept":"Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) is the clear, colourless fluid that fills the ventricles of the brain, the central canal of the spinal cord and the subarachnoid space. It is secreted mainly by the choroid plexus, tufts of capillaries covered by a specialised epithelium (modified ependymal cells) that project into all four ventricles, most of all the two lateral ventricles. The epithelium actively transports ions and water from blood plasma, and tight junctions between its cells form the blood-CSF barrier. An adult has about 150 mL of CSF at any moment but makes about 500 mL a day, so the whole volume turns over several times daily. Learn the flow route in order: lateral ventricles, interventricular foramina (of Monro), third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius), fourth ventricle, then out through the median foramen of Magendie and the two lateral foramina of Luschka into the subarachnoid space. CSF is finally absorbed through the arachnoid granulations (villi) into the dural venous sinuses, mainly the superior sagittal sinus. Its functions are buoyancy, which greatly reduces the brain's effective weight, cushioning against blows, a stable chemical environment and waste removal. If flow or absorption fails, hydrocephalus results: obstructive (non-communicating) when a channel such as the aqueduct is blocked, communicating when absorption is impaired. Normal pressure hydrocephalus in older adults causes a potentially reversible dementia with the triad of gait disturbance, urinary incontinence and cognitive decline. The meninges, from outside in, are dura, arachnoid and pia. Option d is right because the choroid plexus of the ventricles is the main site where CSF is produced.","traps":{"a":"Arachnoid granulations (arachnoid villi) are small outpouchings of the arachnoid membrane that push into the dural venous sinuses, especially the superior sagittal sinus. They are where CSF is absorbed back into the blood, the end of the circuit rather than its start. Scarring of the granulations after meningitis or subarachnoid haemorrhage causes communicating hydrocephalus. It tempts because production and absorption are taught together.","b":"The subarachnoid space lies between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater. It is where CSF circulates around the brain and spinal cord, and it widens into cisterns; the lumbar cistern is where CSF is sampled in a lumbar puncture. It is a space that holds CSF, not tissue that secretes it. It tempts because it is the place most associated with CSF in clinical practice.","c":"The dura mater is the tough, outermost meningeal layer. Its two layers split to enclose the dural venous sinuses, which receive CSF from the arachnoid granulations, and its folds (falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli) support the brain. It protects and drains, but it does not secrete CSF. It tempts because of its link with the venous sinuses where CSF ends up, and because the 'mater' terms are easy to mix up."}},{"q":"Lesions of the suprachiasmatic nucleus most directly disrupt:","concept":"Circadian rhythms are roughly 24-hour cycles in sleep and waking, body temperature, hormone release and alertness. The master clock is the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), a pair of tiny nuclei in the anterior hypothalamus sitting just above the optic chiasm. Its cells keep time on their own through a molecular feedback loop: the CLOCK and BMAL1 proteins switch on the Period and Cryptochrome genes, whose proteins build up and then switch off their own production, a cycle of about a day. Jeffrey Hall, Michael Rosbash and Michael Young received the 2017 Nobel Prize for this mechanism. Lesion studies by Robert Moore and Victor Eichler (1972) and by Friedrich Stephan and Irving Zucker (1972) showed that destroying the SCN in rats abolishes these rhythms. The animals still sleep about the same total amount, but in scattered bouts across day and night. Martin Ralph and colleagues (1990) then transplanted SCN tissue into lesioned hamsters and found that rhythms returned with the donor's period, proving the SCN generates them. Without cues the human clock free-runs at slightly longer than 24 hours, so it is reset daily by zeitgebers, mainly light. Light reaches the SCN through the retinohypothalamic tract from melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells. The SCN signals the pineal gland to release melatonin at night. Learn the neighbouring hypothalamic regions: preoptic area for temperature and sleep onset, lateral hypothalamus for hunger, ventromedial hypothalamus for satiety, and the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei for pituitary hormones. Option d is right because the SCN is the master circadian pacemaker, so its loss most directly disrupts circadian rhythms.","traps":{"a":"Thermoregulation is controlled mainly by the preoptic area and anterior hypothalamus, which sense blood temperature and trigger sweating, shivering or changes in skin blood flow. The SCN only times the daily rise and fall of body temperature; it does not set the temperature itself. SCN lesions flatten the daily temperature rhythm without destroying temperature control. It tempts because the preoptic area sits right next to the SCN.","b":"Anterior pituitary hormones are controlled by releasing and inhibiting hormones from hypothalamic nuclei such as the arcuate and paraventricular nuclei, carried through the hypophyseal portal system. The SCN only sets the daily timing of some of them, for example the morning peak of cortisol driven by ACTH. A lesion disturbs when hormones peak, not whether they are released. It tempts because the hypothalamus controls the pituitary.","c":"Hunger and satiety are classically linked to the lateral hypothalamus (lesions cause aphagia, a refusal to eat) and the ventromedial hypothalamus (lesions cause overeating and obesity); modern accounts add the arcuate nucleus with its leptin and ghrelin signals. These are separate from the SCN. Meal timing is entrained by the clock, but appetite itself is controlled elsewhere. It tempts because it is the other hypothalamic function every textbook lists."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes the cerebellum's role in motor learning from that of the basal ganglia?","concept":"The cerebellum and basal ganglia are both motor structures, but neither issues the main movement command. Each works as a side loop that receives input from the cortex and returns its output to the motor cortex through the thalamus; the descending command itself travels from primary motor cortex down the corticospinal tract. Their kinds of learning differ. The cerebellum does error-based, supervised learning. Mossy fibres bring it information about the intended movement and the body's current state, and climbing fibres from the inferior olive signal errors to the Purkinje cells, the only output neurons of the cerebellar cortex. David Marr (1969) and James Albus (1971) proposed that these error signals adjust parallel fibre synapses, and Masao Ito and colleagues (1982) demonstrated the resulting cerebellar long-term depression. The cerebellum therefore calibrates timing, coordination and adaptation, as in prism adaptation and eyeblink conditioning. Damage produces ipsilateral ataxia, intention tremor, dysmetria and dysdiadochokinesia. The basal ganglia (striatum, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, substantia nigra) do reward-based reinforcement learning. Through direct ('go') and indirect ('no-go') pathways they select one action and suppress competitors, while dopamine bursts from the substantia nigra act as reward prediction errors (Wolfram Schultz and colleagues, 1997). Repeated rewarded actions become habits. Barbara Knowlton and colleagues (1996) found that people with Parkinson's disease failed a probabilistic habit-learning task that amnesic patients learned normally. Damage produces Parkinson's disease or Huntington's chorea. Option b is right because it correctly pairs the cerebellum with error correction and coordination, and the basal ganglia with action selection and habit or procedural learning.","traps":{"a":"Climbing fibres belong to the cerebellum, not the basal ganglia. They arise in the inferior olive and wrap around Purkinje cells, carrying the error signals that drive cerebellar motor learning. This option simply transplants a cerebellar mechanism into the basal ganglia. It tempts because the jargon is real and 'error signal' sounds like motor learning; spotting the wrong structure is the whole test.","c":"Habit learning and reward-based action selection are the basal ganglia's job: striatal circuits, shaped by dopamine prediction-error signals, choose among competing actions and turn repeated rewarded actions into habits. This option attributes them to the cerebellum, reversing the two structures. It tempts because both structures do 'motor learning', and students who remember the list of functions but not which goes where fall for the swap.","d":"The primary descending command comes from motor cortex, mainly through the corticospinal (pyramidal) tract, plus brainstem pathways. The cerebellum and basal ganglia project mostly back to the cortex through the thalamus and shape the command rather than issue it; older texts grouped them as 'extrapyramidal'. Neither issues the primary command to muscles. It tempts because damage to either produces dramatic movement disorders."}},{"q":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is most directly involved in:","concept":"The thalamus is the relay station for almost every sense on its way to the cortex, and each sense has its own nucleus. The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN), a knee-shaped bump on the back of the thalamus ('geniculate' means knee-like), relays vision. The route runs: retina, optic nerve, optic chiasm (where fibres from the nasal half of each retina cross), optic tract, LGN, then the optic radiation to the primary visual cortex (V1, Brodmann area 17, the striate cortex around the calcarine sulcus of the occipital lobe). The LGN has six layers. Layers 1 and 2 are magnocellular, carrying motion and coarse, fast information; layers 3 to 6 are parvocellular, carrying colour and fine detail; thin koniocellular layers lie between them. Each layer receives input from only one eye: the contralateral eye feeds layers 1, 4 and 6, and the ipsilateral eye feeds layers 2, 3 and 5. The LGN also receives heavy feedback from the cortex, so it filters as well as relays. Some retinal fibres bypass it: to the superior colliculus for orienting eye movements, to the pretectum for the pupillary light reflex, and to the suprachiasmatic nucleus for circadian timing. Learn the partner nuclei: medial geniculate nucleus for hearing (memory aid: lateral for light, medial for music), ventral posterolateral nucleus for body touch and pain, and ventral posteromedial nucleus for the face and taste. Smell is the one sense that reaches its primary cortex without a thalamic relay. Option c is right because the lateral geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for vision.","traps":{"a":"Audition relays through the medial geniculate nucleus (MGN) of the thalamus, which receives input from the inferior colliculus and projects to the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus of the temporal lobe. The MGN and LGN are neighbours on the back of the thalamus and have nearly identical names. That near-identity is exactly why the option tempts. A memory aid helps: lateral for light, medial for music.","b":"Somatosensation (touch, pain, temperature, body position) relays through the ventral posterolateral nucleus for the body and the ventral posteromedial nucleus for the face, both projecting to the primary somatosensory cortex in the postcentral gyrus. These ventral posterior nuclei are separate from the geniculate bodies. It tempts because all are thalamic sensory relays, and a student who knows only that the thalamus relays the senses must guess.","d":"Olfaction is the only sense whose signals reach primary cortex without first relaying in the thalamus. Olfactory receptor neurons project to the olfactory bulb, then through the olfactory tract to the piriform cortex and amygdala; a later route reaches the orbitofrontal cortex partly via the thalamus. It tempts because examiners like to test this exception, but the LGN plays no part in smell."}},{"q":"Damage to which structure produces a contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing?","concept":"Visual field defects let a clinician pinpoint a lesion, because the visual pathway reorganises at the optic chiasm. Each retina's nasal half sees the temporal (outer) visual field, and its temporal half sees the nasal field. At the chiasm, fibres from the nasal halves cross, so from the optic tract onwards each side of the brain carries the opposite visual field from both eyes. An optic nerve lesion causes blindness in that one eye. A chiasm lesion, classically from a pituitary adenoma pressing from below, causes bitemporal hemianopia, loss of both outer fields. Any lesion behind the chiasm causes a contralateral homonymous hemianopia, loss of the same half of the field in both eyes. Optic tract lesions give an incongruous field cut without macular sparing. Lesions of Meyer's loop in the temporal lobe give a contralateral upper quadrantanopia ('pie in the sky'); parietal radiation lesions give a lower quadrantanopia. Lesions of the primary visual cortex, usually a posterior cerebral artery stroke, give a congruous homonymous hemianopia that often spares the macula, the central field. The usual explanation is blood supply: the occipital pole, where the macula is represented over a large area, also receives collateral supply from the middle cerebral artery, so it survives. Some patients with V1 damage show blindsight, responding to stimuli they deny seeing (Lawrence Weiskrantz and colleagues, 1974). Do not confuse hemianopia with hemispatial neglect, an attentional disorder after right parietal damage. Option b is right because macular sparing is the signature of a primary visual cortex infarct that spares the occipital pole.","traps":{"a":"An optic chiasm lesion damages the crossing fibres from both nasal retinas, which carry both temporal visual fields. The result is bitemporal hemianopia, a heteronymous defect in which each eye loses its outer half of the field. The classic cause is a pituitary adenoma or craniopharyngioma pressing from below. It is neither homonymous nor macula-sparing. It tempts because the chiasm is the most famous site in visual pathway questions.","c":"An optic tract lesion lies behind the chiasm, so it does cause a contralateral homonymous hemianopia, which makes this the strongest distractor. But tract lesions typically produce an incongruous defect (unequal in the two eyes) and do not spare the macula, because the macular fibres run through the same damaged tract. Macular sparing points instead to the occipital cortex with its dual blood supply at the pole.","d":"An optic nerve lesion in front of the chiasm affects only one eye, causing monocular blindness or a field defect in that eye, as in optic neuritis linked with multiple sclerosis. Because the fibres have not yet crossed, it cannot produce a homonymous defect affecting the same half-field in both eyes. It tempts students who know a nerve lesion causes blindness but not which pattern it produces."}},{"q":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was supported originally by which observation?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis states that schizophrenia, especially its positive symptoms, involves excess dopamine activity. It grew out of drug evidence rather than brain studies. Jean Delay and Pierre Deniker (1952) showed that chlorpromazine calmed psychotic patients. Arvid Carlsson and Margit Lindqvist (1963) found that antipsychotics raised dopamine metabolites, which they read as a response to blocked monoamine receptors. The classic support came from Philip Seeman and colleagues (1976) and, independently, Ian Creese, David Burt and Solomon Snyder (1976). They showed that the average clinical dose of typical antipsychotics tracks their affinity for the D2 receptor across a very wide range: haloperidol binds D2 tightly and works at a few milligrams, while chlorpromazine binds weakly and needs hundreds. Binding to other receptors showed no such match. Amphetamine, which releases dopamine, can cause a paranoid psychosis resembling schizophrenia, and L-dopa can trigger psychotic symptoms in Parkinson's disease. Later revisions: Kenneth Davis and colleagues (1991) proposed subcortical excess with prefrontal deficit, and Oliver Howes and Shitij Kapur (2009) framed dopamine dysregulation as a final common pathway that makes ordinary events feel abnormally salient. Rival models include the glutamate (NMDA hypofunction) hypothesis, based on ketamine and phencyclidine, and serotonin models, based on 5-HT2A blockade by atypicals such as clozapine, whose D2 affinity is low. Twin studies show heritability but not total genetic determination. Option b is right because the correlation between the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics and their D2 receptor affinity was the classic evidence behind the dopamine hypothesis.","traps":{"a":"Serotonin entered the schizophrenia story through LSD, whose hallucinogenic effects act on 5-HT2A receptors, and through atypical antipsychotics such as clozapine and risperidone, which block 5-HT2A as well as D2 receptors. Antipsychotics do not all elevate serotonin, and the dopamine hypothesis rests on dopamine evidence, not serotonin evidence. It tempts because serotonin is the best-known rival neurotransmitter in schizophrenia research.","c":"Twin concordance for schizophrenia is far below 100 percent: Irving Gottesman's (1991) pooled figures were about 48 percent for identical twins and 17 percent for fraternal twins. Identical twins share essentially all their genes, so incomplete concordance shows that environment matters. Even a perfect figure would support a genetic cause, not a dopamine mechanism. It tempts because twin studies are the most quoted evidence about schizophrenia's causes.","d":"Levodopa raises brain dopamine, so if the dopamine hypothesis is right it should worsen psychosis, and in Parkinson's disease it can in fact trigger hallucinations and paranoid ideas. It does not reliably improve negative symptoms in schizophrenia. The option turns real evidence upside down. It tempts because the revised hypothesis links negative symptoms to low prefrontal dopamine, which suggests that adding dopamine might help."}},{"q":"The 'fight or flight' response is mediated primarily by:","concept":"The fight-or-flight response is the body's rapid preparation to confront or escape a threat. Walter B. Cannon described it in Bodily Changes in Pain, Hunger, Fear and Rage (1915), and later coined the term homeostasis. When the hypothalamus detects danger it triggers the sympathetic division of the autonomic nervous system, whose outflow leaves the thoracic and lumbar spinal cord. Sympathetic nerves release noradrenaline onto target organs within seconds. At the same time, preganglionic sympathetic fibres stimulate the adrenal medulla, which is effectively a modified sympathetic ganglion, to release adrenaline (most of its output) and some noradrenaline into the blood. This sympatho-adrenomedullary (SAM) system raises heart rate and blood pressure, dilates the bronchi and pupils, releases glucose from the liver, shifts blood to the muscles, and slows digestion. The parasympathetic division, with cranial and sacral outflow and the vagus nerve as its main pathway, does the opposite: 'rest and digest', slowing the heart and promoting digestion. Contrast the slower hormonal stress route, the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis: CRH from the hypothalamus releases ACTH from the pituitary, which releases cortisol from the adrenal cortex over minutes to hours. Hans Selye (1936) built his general adaptation syndrome, with stages of alarm, resistance and exhaustion, around this sustained response. Shelley Taylor and colleagues (2000) proposed 'tend and befriend' as a common female pattern, linked to oxytocin. Freezing is also recognised as an early defensive response. Option c is right because fight or flight is driven by the sympathetic nervous system together with adrenaline release from the adrenal medulla.","traps":{"a":"The parasympathetic nervous system, mainly through the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X), produces the 'rest and digest' state: slower heart rate, constricted pupils, more salivation and digestion. It restores the body after a threat rather than preparing it for action. Strong vagal activation can even cause fainting. It tempts because it is the other half of the autonomic nervous system and students mix up the two divisions.","b":"The enteric nervous system is the network of neurons in the wall of the gut, sometimes called the 'second brain', which can control peristalsis and secretion on its own. It is influenced by both autonomic divisions, and sympathetic activity during stress inhibits digestion. It is a target of fight or flight, not its driver. It tempts because stress so obviously affects the stomach, as in 'butterflies'.","d":"The pituitary-thyroid axis runs from TRH in the hypothalamus to TSH from the anterior pituitary to thyroxine (T4) and T3 from the thyroid. It sets the basal metabolic rate over days and weeks, which is far too slow for an emergency response. Hyperthyroidism causes anxiety-like symptoms, which is why it tempts, but the acute stress reaction is sympathetic and adrenal."}},{"q":"Substance abuse disorders are most consistently associated with structural and functional changes in which neural circuit?","concept":"Addiction research centres on the brain's reward circuit. James Olds and Peter Milner (1954) found that rats would press a lever repeatedly for electrical stimulation of certain brain sites, revealing a reward system. Its core is the mesolimbic dopamine pathway, from the ventral tegmental area (VTA) in the midbrain to the nucleus accumbens in the ventral striatum. Gaetano Di Chiara and Assunta Imperato (1988) showed that drugs abused by humans raise dopamine in the nucleus accumbens, though by different routes: cocaine blocks dopamine reuptake, amphetamine reverses the dopamine transporter, opioids disinhibit VTA neurons, and nicotine stimulates nicotinic receptors on them. With repeated use the circuit changes. PET studies show reduced striatal D2 receptors and lower activity in the orbitofrontal and anterior cingulate cortex, which weakens control over impulses. Barry Everitt and Trevor Robbins (2005) described a shift from the ventral to the dorsal striatum, so drug-taking moves from goal-directed action to habit and then compulsion. Terry Robinson and Kent Berridge (1993) proposed incentive sensitisation: dopamine drives 'wanting' (craving), which grows with use, while 'liking' (pleasure) does not grow and may decline. Other models stress the extended amygdala and stress systems that maintain use through negative reinforcement during withdrawal. DSM-5 groups these conditions as substance use disorders, rated on eleven criteria covering impaired control, social impairment, risky use, tolerance and withdrawal. Option b is right because the mesolimbic dopamine system and ventral striatum are the circuit most consistently altered, structurally and functionally, in substance use disorders.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellar vermis, the midline strip of the cerebellum, controls posture, gait and trunk balance. It is damaged in chronic alcohol use, where degeneration of the anterior vermis produces a wide-based, unsteady gait. That is a toxic effect of one drug, not the circuit shared by all addictions. It tempts because alcohol-related brain damage is a common exam topic and alcohol is the commonest substance problem.","c":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is the thalamic relay for vision, carrying signals from the retina to the primary visual cortex through the optic radiation. It has no role in reward, motivation or craving. Hallucinogens such as LSD alter visual experience, but through cortical serotonin 5-HT2A receptors, not through changes in the geniculate. It tempts only as a recognisable brain name placed among the options.","d":"The vestibular nuclei in the brainstem process balance signals from the semicircular canals and otolith organs of the inner ear and control eye and posture reflexes. Alcohol intoxication produces vertigo and nystagmus through vestibular and cerebellar effects, but these are acute drug effects, not the circuit underlying addiction. It tempts because intoxication so visibly disturbs balance."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is classically used to detect dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) was developed by David A. Grant and Esta A. Berg (1948) to study how flexibly people shift between rules. Four key cards are laid out (one red triangle, two green stars, three yellow crosses, four blue circles), and the person sorts response cards by matching each to a key card. The examiner never states the rule (colour, form or number) and says only 'right' or 'wrong'. After ten consecutive correct sorts the rule changes without warning, so the person must notice the negative feedback, drop the old rule and find the new one. Main scores are categories completed, perseverative errors (continuing to sort by the old rule despite being told it is wrong) and failure to maintain set. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal excisions made many perseverative errors, while patients with temporal or posterior lesions largely did not. The WCST became the classic test of dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) function: set shifting, abstract reasoning, hypothesis testing and using feedback. Daniel Weinberger, Karen Berman and Ronald Zec (1986) found that people with schizophrenia failed to activate the DLPFC during the WCST, a 'hypofrontality' finding. The test is sensitive but not perfectly specific, since diffuse damage also impairs it. Other executive tests: Stroop (inhibition), Trail Making Part B (switching), Tower of London (planning, Tim Shallice, 1982) and verbal fluency. Memory tests (hippocampus), finger tapping (motor cortex) and finger-nose testing (cerebellum) target other regions. Option a is right because the WCST is classically used to detect dorsolateral prefrontal dysfunction, shown by perseveration.","traps":{"b":"The hippocampus supports forming new declarative memories, the lesson of patient H.M. after bilateral medial temporal removal (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957). It is assessed with learning and delayed-recall tests such as word-list learning or figure recall. Patients with hippocampal amnesia can sort cards and shift rules normally within a session. It tempts because the WCST involves learning from feedback, which sounds like memory.","c":"The primary motor cortex in the precentral gyrus (Brodmann area 4) executes voluntary movement, organised as the motor homunculus. Damage causes contralateral weakness, assessed with tests such as finger tapping or grip strength. The WCST needs only a simple placing movement, so motor cortex damage would not produce rule perseveration. It tempts because it is a frontal lobe structure and 'frontal' is associated with the WCST.","d":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and, in modern accounts, contributes to some cognitive functions, as in the cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome. Its classic bedside tests are finger-nose, heel-shin and rapid alternating movements, which detect ataxia and dysmetria. It is not what the WCST was designed or classically used to detect. It tempts because cerebellar cognition is a newer and much discussed topic."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) of synaptic strength typically depends on:","concept":"Synaptic plasticity is the change in strength of a synapse with use, and it is the main cellular model of learning. Donald O. Hebb (1949) proposed that when one neuron repeatedly helps fire another, the connection between them strengthens. Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973) found such long-term potentiation (LTP) in the rabbit hippocampus: a brief, high-frequency burst (tetanus) produced a lasting increase in response. Long-term depression (LTD) is the reverse, a lasting weakening. Serena Dudek and Mark Bear (1992) showed that in hippocampal area CA1, prolonged low-frequency stimulation (typically 900 pulses at 1 Hz, about 15 minutes) produces LTD, and that blocking NMDA receptors prevents it. The key is calcium. At rest the NMDA receptor channel is plugged by Mg2+; when glutamate binds and the membrane is depolarised, the plug leaves and Ca2+ enters, making the receptor a coincidence detector. A large, rapid Ca2+ rise activates kinases such as CaMKII, which phosphorylate AMPA receptors and insert more of them into the membrane, giving LTP. A modest, prolonged Ca2+ rise preferentially activates phosphatases such as calcineurin and protein phosphatase 1, which dephosphorylate AMPA receptors and remove them, giving LTD. A second classic form is cerebellar LTD at parallel fibre to Purkinje cell synapses, which underlies motor learning. LTD is not simply forgetting; it keeps synapses from saturating and sharpens stored patterns. Classic LTP properties are input specificity, cooperativity and associativity. Option d is right because LTD is typically induced by prolonged low-frequency stimulation that produces only a modest postsynaptic Ca2+ rise.","traps":{"a":"Brief, high-frequency stimulation (a tetanus, for example 100 Hz for one second) and a large, fast postsynaptic Ca2+ rise are the conditions for long-term potentiation (LTP), the strengthening described by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo (1973). The large Ca2+ signal activates kinases such as CaMKII and adds AMPA receptors. This is the mirror image of LTD. It tempts because students memorise the LTP recipe and pick it whenever plasticity is mentioned.","b":"Presynaptic GABA release produces fast inhibition through GABA-A receptors, making postsynaptic neurons harder to depolarise. In hippocampal slice work, GABA-A blockers are often added to make plasticity easier to induce, which shows that GABA is not what LTD depends on. Inhibition is a moment-to-moment reduction in firing, not a lasting change in synaptic strength. It tempts because 'depression' sounds like inhibition.","c":"Mg2+ blocks the NMDA receptor, not the AMPA receptor. At resting potential a Mg2+ ion plugs the NMDA channel, and depolarisation expels it so Ca2+ can enter; this unblocking is needed for both NMDA-dependent LTP and LTD, so it is not specific to LTD. AMPA receptors carry the fast Na+ current and are what LTD removes from the synapse. It tempts by mixing two real receptor names."}},{"q":"Which event-related potential component is most commonly elevated in oddball paradigms and reduced in schizophrenia?","concept":"Event-related potentials (ERPs) are small voltage changes in the EEG that are time-locked to a stimulus or response and extracted by averaging many trials. Components are named by polarity (P for positive, N for negative) and by latency in milliseconds or by order. The P300, or P3, was reported by Samuel Sutton and colleagues (1965), who found a large positive wave about 300 ms after stimuli that resolved the subject's uncertainty. It is classically elicited in the oddball paradigm: a series of frequent standard tones is interrupted by rare deviant targets (for example 80 percent standard, 20 percent target) that the person must count or respond to. The rare target produces a large positive deflection, usually 250 to 500 ms after onset and maximal over central and parietal sites. Its amplitude grows as targets become rarer and more task-relevant, and is thought to index attention allocation and the updating of working memory (the context-updating account of Emanuel Donchin and Michael Coles, 1988). Its latency reflects stimulus evaluation time and lengthens with age and dementia. A frontal P3a follows unexpected novel stimuli; the parietal P3b follows attended targets. In schizophrenia, auditory P300 amplitude is reliably reduced, a candidate endophenotype; Henri Begleiter and colleagues (1984) also found reduced P300 in sons of alcoholic fathers. Neighbours: N100 is an early sensory response, mismatch negativity reflects pre-attentive change detection, P50 measures sensory gating with paired clicks, and the Bereitschaftspotential precedes voluntary movement. Option b is right because the P300 is the component enlarged by rare oddball targets and reduced in schizophrenia.","traps":{"a":"The N100 (N1) is a negative wave about 100 ms after a sound or other stimulus, generated largely in sensory cortex and reflecting early sensory registration. Attention can enlarge it, and it is somewhat reduced in schizophrenia. But it is an early, largely obligatory response present for standards and deviants alike; the large oddball effect belongs to the later P300. It tempts as the other well-known auditory component.","c":"The P50 is a small positive wave about 50 ms after a click, measured in a paired-click paradigm: normally the response to the second click is strongly suppressed, showing sensory gating. People with schizophrenia often fail to suppress it, a finding linked to the alpha-7 nicotinic receptor. It tempts because it is a real schizophrenia marker, but it is a gating ratio measured with paired clicks, not an oddball response.","d":"The Bereitschaftspotential, or readiness potential, is a slow negative shift over motor areas that begins before a voluntary movement. Hans Helmut Kornhuber and Luder Deecke (1965) described it, and Benjamin Libet and colleagues (1983) used it in their free-will timing experiments. It is tied to movement preparation, not to rare stimuli. It tempts because its German name makes it look like an advanced answer."}},{"q":"Anterograde amnesia following bilateral mammillary body / dorsomedial thalamus damage with confabulation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Korsakoff's syndrome is a chronic amnestic disorder caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, most often in long-term heavy drinkers with poor nutrition, though it also follows severe vomiting (hyperemesis gravidarum), bariatric surgery or starvation. It usually follows Wernicke's encephalopathy, the acute stage described by Carl Wernicke (1881), classically a triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia (eye-movement palsy, nystagmus) and ataxia, though few patients show all three. It is a medical emergency treated with thiamine, given before glucose. If untreated, many patients are left with the chronic amnesia described by Sergei Korsakoff (1887); together these are called Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome. The damage falls on the mammillary bodies and the dorsomedial (mediodorsal) and anterior thalamus. These lie on the circuit proposed by James Papez (1937): hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, mammillothalamic tract, anterior thalamus, cingulate gyrus and back. The core picture is severe anterograde amnesia, variable retrograde amnesia with a temporal gradient (older memories better kept), confabulation (honest but false filling of memory gaps, most marked early), apathy and poor insight, with relatively preserved intelligence and procedural memory. DSM-5 classifies it as alcohol-induced major neurocognitive disorder, amnestic-confabulatory type. Compare the distractors: Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a rapidly progressive prion dementia with myoclonus; Pick's disease is a frontotemporal dementia with early personality change; Huntington's disease is an inherited disorder with chorea and caudate atrophy. Option c is right because anterograde amnesia with confabulation after mammillary body and dorsomedial thalamic damage is the hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a prion disease in which misfolded prion protein spreads through the brain, causing spongiform change. It produces a rapidly progressive dementia over months, with myoclonus, ataxia and visual problems, periodic sharp-wave complexes on EEG, and death usually within a year. Variant CJD is linked to bovine spongiform encephalopathy. It tempts as another dementia, but it is global and rapid, not a confabulating amnesia.","b":"Pick's disease is a form of frontotemporal dementia with tau-positive Pick bodies in neurons and severe frontal and temporal atrophy. It often begins before 65 with personality change, disinhibition, apathy, loss of social awareness or language decline, while memory is relatively spared early. It tempts because it is another named dementia, but its damage is lobar cortical atrophy, not the mammillary-thalamic system.","d":"Huntington's chorea (Huntington's disease) is an autosomal dominant disorder caused by an expanded CAG repeat in the huntingtin gene on chromosome 4. Degeneration begins in the caudate nucleus and putamen, producing chorea, psychiatric symptoms such as depression and irritability, and a subcortical dementia with slowed thinking. It tempts because it is a subcortical disorder, but its lesion is striatal, not mammillary or thalamic."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter binds the receptor that benzodiazepines positively modulate?","concept":"Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators of the GABA-A receptor. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. Its GABA-A receptor is ionotropic: a ligand-gated chloride channel made of five subunits, usually two alpha, two beta and one gamma. When GABA binds at the alpha-beta interfaces, the channel opens, Cl- flows in, and the neuron is pushed away from firing threshold. Benzodiazepines bind a different site, between an alpha and a gamma2 subunit. They cannot open the channel alone; they increase GABA's effect by making the channel open more frequently. Barbiturates bind yet another site and lengthen each opening, and at high doses open the channel directly, which is why barbiturate overdose is far more dangerous than benzodiazepine overdose. Alcohol also enhances GABA-A function, explaining cross-tolerance and the use of benzodiazepines such as chlordiazepoxide or lorazepam in alcohol withdrawal. Flumazenil is the antagonist that reverses benzodiazepine overdose. Z-drugs such as zolpidem act at the same site but prefer alpha1-containing receptors and are used mainly for sleep. Clinical effects are anxiolytic, sedative-hypnotic, anticonvulsant, muscle relaxant and amnestic. Chlordiazepoxide (Librium), synthesised by Leo Sternbach, reached the market in 1960 and diazepam (Valium) in 1963. Do not confuse GABA-B, a metabotropic receptor targeted by baclofen. Neighbouring transmitters: glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter, and glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter of the spinal cord and brainstem, blocked by strychnine. Option c is right because benzodiazepines bind GABA-A receptors and enhance the action of GABA, the neurotransmitter that binds those receptors.","traps":{"a":"Glutamate is the brain's main excitatory transmitter, acting on ionotropic AMPA, NMDA and kainate receptors and on metabotropic receptors. Drugs at its receptors include ketamine and phencyclidine, which block NMDA receptors, and memantine, used in Alzheimer's disease. Benzodiazepines do not act on glutamate receptors. It tempts because calming the brain could be imagined as reducing excitation, but benzodiazepines boost inhibition instead.","b":"Acetylcholine acts on nicotinic receptors (ionotropic, at the neuromuscular junction and in the brain) and muscarinic receptors (metabotropic). Drugs acting here include donepezil, which prevents acetylcholine breakdown, and scopolamine, which blocks muscarinic receptors. Benzodiazepines have no direct cholinergic action. It tempts because benzodiazepines impair memory, and memory problems are classically linked with acetylcholine.","d":"Glycine is the main inhibitory transmitter in the spinal cord and brainstem. Its receptor is also a ligand-gated chloride channel, which makes this the closest distractor. But benzodiazepines do not modulate glycine receptors; strychnine blocks them, causing convulsions. Glycine also acts as a required co-agonist at NMDA receptors. It tempts because it shares the 'inhibitory chloride channel' description with GABA-A."}},{"q":"Erikson's stage of 'autonomy vs shame and doubt' is associated with which age range and developmental task?","concept":"Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages across the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two poles, and a good resolution yields a virtue (basic strength). He drew on Freud's psychosexual stages but stressed social relationships and identity, and his epigenetic principle holds that each stage builds on earlier ones. The stages with approximate ages: trust vs mistrust (0 to 1 year, hope); autonomy vs shame and doubt (1 to 3 years, will); initiative vs guilt (3 to 6 years, purpose); industry vs inferiority (6 to 12 years, competence); identity vs role confusion (adolescence, fidelity); intimacy vs isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity vs stagnation (middle adulthood, care); and integrity vs despair (late adulthood, wisdom). The second stage covers the toddler years, matching Freud's anal stage. Newly able to walk, talk and control their bodies, toddlers want to do things themselves: feeding, dressing, choosing, saying 'no', and toilet training, the classic arena. Parents who allow reasonable choices within firm, supportive limits foster autonomy and the virtue of will. Overcontrol, harsh criticism or shaming over accidents breeds shame and self-doubt. Examiners build variants by swapping ages, crises or virtues, so learn them as a set. James Marcia (1966) extended the fifth stage into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Option c is right because autonomy vs shame and doubt belongs to roughly 1 to 3 years, when toilet training and the exercise of will and independence are the central tasks.","traps":{"a":"Ages 0 to 1 and learning trust through consistent caregiving describe Erikson's first stage, trust vs mistrust, which matches Freud's oral stage. A reliable, responsive caregiver lets the infant expect that the world will meet its needs, producing the virtue of hope. It tempts because it is the stage just before autonomy, and both involve the caregiver, but its crisis is trust, not independence.","b":"Ages 3 to 6, initiating play and tolerating guilt describe the third stage, initiative vs guilt, roughly Freud's phallic stage. Preschoolers plan activities, take on roles in play and ask endless questions; support builds the virtue of purpose, while excessive punishment of their initiative creates guilt. It tempts because it immediately follows autonomy and also concerns the child acting on its own.","d":"Ages 6 to 12, comparing oneself with peers and developing competence describe the fourth stage, industry vs inferiority, which matches Freud's latency period. School-age children master academic and social skills and judge themselves against classmates; success brings the virtue of competence, repeated failure brings feelings of inferiority. It tempts because 'competence' and 'independence' both sound like autonomy."}},{"q":"Schaffer and Emerson's longitudinal study identified four phases of attachment. The 'specific attachment' phase typically begins around:","concept":"H. Rudolph Schaffer and Peggy Emerson (1964) followed 60 infants from working-class families in Glasgow, visiting them at home monthly in the first year and again at 18 months. Mothers reported separation protest (distress when a particular person left) in everyday situations, and the researchers measured stranger anxiety. From these data they described four phases. Asocial phase (birth to about 6 weeks): babies respond similarly to social and non-social stimuli, though they prefer faces. Indiscriminate attachment (about 6 weeks to 7 months): babies prefer people to objects and enjoy company, but accept comfort from anyone and do not protest when a particular person leaves. Specific attachment (from about 7 months): separation protest appears toward one particular person, usually the primary attachment figure, and stranger anxiety begins. Multiple attachments (from about 10 months): attachments to others such as father, siblings and grandparents follow quickly, and by 18 months most infants had several. A key finding was that the main attachment figure was the person who responded most sensitively to the baby's signals, not necessarily the one who fed and changed it, which argued against learning-theory 'cupboard love' explanations. Compare the phases of John Bowlby (1969): pre-attachment (0 to 6 weeks), attachment in the making (6 weeks to 6 to 8 months), clear-cut attachment (6 to 8 months to about 2 years) and goal-corrected partnership. Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978) assessed attachment quality at 12 to 18 months with the Strange Situation. Option a is right because Schaffer and Emerson placed the onset of specific attachment at about 7 months.","traps":{"b":"Five months falls within the indiscriminate attachment phase (about 6 weeks to 7 months). Babies of this age clearly prefer people to objects, smile more at familiar faces and can tell familiar from unfamiliar people, but they still accept comfort from anyone and show no consistent separation protest or stranger anxiety. It tempts because growing recognition of the mother at this age looks like a specific bond.","c":"Three months is early in the indiscriminate phase. The social smile has appeared by about 6 to 8 weeks, and babies enjoy social interaction with anyone, but they do not yet single out one person whose departure they protest. In John Bowlby's (1969) scheme this is 'attachment in the making'. It tempts students who confuse the first social responsiveness with the first specific attachment.","d":"Twelve months is after specific attachment has formed; by then most infants are entering the multiple attachments phase, which begins at about 10 months. Twelve months is also the youngest age tested in the Strange Situation of Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978), which assesses an attachment that already exists. It tempts because one year is a familiar milestone strongly associated with attachment research."}},{"q":"Vygotsky considered private (self-directed) speech in young children to be:","concept":"Private speech is the audible talk young children direct at themselves while playing or solving problems ('now the red one goes here'). Jean Piaget (1923), in The Language and Thought of the Child, called it egocentric speech: a sign that the young child cannot yet take the listener's view, which simply fades as the child becomes socialised, around age 7. Lev Vygotsky, in Thinking and Speech (1934, translated as Thought and Language), reversed this reading. For him speech begins as social communication with others, then turns back on the self as private speech that plans and guides action, and is finally internalised as silent inner speech, which becomes verbal thought. Private speech does not vanish; it goes underground. Vygotsky noted that private speech increases when tasks become harder, for example when a needed material is suddenly missing, which shows that it serves problem solving. Later research supports him: children use more private speech on difficult tasks, and those who do often perform better and show better self-control. Adults, too, talk to themselves under difficulty. This fits Vygotsky's wider sociocultural theory, in which higher mental functions start between people and are then internalised. Related ideas: the zone of proximal development (the gap between what a child can do alone and with help), the more knowledgeable other, and scaffolding, a term from David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976). Option c is right because Vygotsky saw private speech as a self-regulatory tool that is internalised as inner speech and verbal thought.","traps":{"a":"'Autistic thinking' was the phrase of Eugen Bleuler, who introduced the word autism in 1911, for private, undirected, wish-driven thought as opposed to directed, logical thought; Piaget borrowed it. It has nothing to do with autism as a diagnosis. Vygotsky did not see private speech as a regression and never advised discouraging it; he treated it as a step forward in self-regulation. It tempts because the phrase appears in the Piaget and Vygotsky debate.","b":"Treating private speech as mere imitation with no function matches neither theorist. Behaviourist accounts stress imitation in language learning, but Vygotsky argued the opposite: private speech is functional, rising when tasks get harder and guiding attention and action. Even Piaget gave it meaning, as a window on egocentric thought. It tempts because children often echo adult phrases while playing, so self-talk can look like copying.","d":"This is close to the view of Jean Piaget (1923): private speech, which he called egocentric speech, reflects the child's inability to take another's perspective and declines as social cooperation grows, around age 7. The words 'only with formal schooling' add a distortion, but the main point is that this is the theory Vygotsky argued against. It tempts because it is the textbook rival position in the same debate."}},{"q":"Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory places 'rapprochement' at approximately what age?","concept":"Margaret Mahler, with Fred Pine and Anni Bergman (1975), described in The Psychological Birth of the Human Infant how a baby gradually becomes a separate self. The account comes from psychoanalytic ego psychology and object relations and rests on observing mothers and infants together in a nursery setting. She first proposed two early phases. Normal autism (0 to about 2 months) is a near-closed state focused on physiological needs. Normal symbiosis (about 2 to 5 months) is a sense of fusion with the mother as one unit. Mahler herself later softened the 'autistic' phase after infant research, notably by Daniel Stern (1985), showed that newborns are socially responsive. Separation-individuation then runs through four subphases. Differentiation or 'hatching' (about 5 to 10 months): the infant becomes more alert, explores the mother's face, and stranger reactions appear. Practising (about 10 to 15 months): crawling and walking bring a 'love affair with the world' and elated exploration, with brief returns for 'emotional refuelling'. Rapprochement (about 15 to 24 months): the toddler realises its separateness and vulnerability and swings between clinging and pushing away, shadowing the mother and darting off, which can peak in a 'rapprochement crisis'. On the way to object constancy (from about 24 to 36 months onward): a stable inner image of the mother lets the child tolerate separation. Later object relations writers linked borderline personality to unresolved rapprochement conflicts. Option b is right because Mahler placed rapprochement at roughly 15 to 24 months.","traps":{"a":"Birth to 2 months is Mahler's normal autistic phase, when the newborn was thought to be largely closed off from the outside world and focused on sleep, feeding and physiological balance. It comes before symbiosis and long before separation-individuation begins. Infant research has since shown much more early social responsiveness. It tempts because it is the first phase in Mahler's list and has a memorable name.","c":"Five to 10 months is the differentiation, or 'hatching', subphase, the first subphase of separation-individuation. The infant becomes more alert and outward-looking, explores the mother's face, hair and clothes, compares her with others, and begins to show stranger reactions. Rapprochement comes later, after practising, once the toddler can walk away and grasp its own separateness. It tempts because differentiation is also a 'separation' step.","d":"Three to 5 years lies beyond Mahler's sequence, which ends with the open-ended move toward object constancy from about the third year. This age range instead matches Freud's phallic and Oedipal stage and the initiative vs guilt stage of Erik Erikson (1950). Rapprochement belongs to toddlerhood, when walking has just made real separation possible. It tempts students who misplace rapprochement by mixing it with later psychoanalytic stages."}},{"q":"Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese is best characterised as:","concept":"Imprinting is a form of early learning in which young animals form a bond with a caregiver during a short window after hatching or birth. Douglas Spalding (1873) first noted that newly hatched chicks follow the first moving object they see, but Konrad Lorenz (1935) made the concept famous in ethology. Lorenz divided a clutch of greylag goose eggs: half hatched with their mother, half in an incubator where Lorenz was the first moving thing they saw. The incubator goslings followed Lorenz everywhere, and when the groups were mixed they sorted themselves back to their own 'parent'. Imprinting has four classic features. It is rapid, needing only brief exposure and no reward. It occurs in a critical period soon after hatching; later work on ducklings placed peak sensitivity at roughly 13 to 16 hours, and following becomes difficult after a few days as fear of novel objects grows. It was claimed to be irreversible. It shapes later sexual preferences: imprinted birds may court the species they imprinted on (sexual imprinting, as distinct from filial imprinting). Later research showed imprinting can be modified, so most psychologists now prefer the term sensitive period. It occurs in precocial species such as geese, ducks and chickens, which are mobile soon after hatching. Lorenz shared the 1973 Nobel Prize with Niko Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch. John Bowlby (1969) borrowed this ethological thinking for human attachment, though human attachment forms gradually over months. Option b is right because imprinting is rapid, largely irreversible and confined to a narrow critical period.","traps":{"a":"Slow, reversible learning across the lifespan describes ordinary associative learning such as classical and operant conditioning, which can be acquired at any age and later extinguished. Imprinting is the opposite on each point: it is fast, tied to a brief window soon after hatching, and was held to be hard or impossible to reverse. It tempts only students who assume all learning is gradual and flexible.","c":"Operant conditioning, described by B. F. Skinner (1938), changes behaviour through its consequences, such as food reward. Imprinting needs no reward: goslings follow the first moving object even when it never feeds them, and the monkeys of Harry Harlow (1958) likewise preferred a soft, food-free cloth mother. Such findings helped overturn 'cupboard love' theories of attachment. It tempts because learning theorists explained early bonding through feeding.","d":"Imprinting is not a purely visual recognition system: sounds also matter (ducklings respond to maternal calls, even before hatching), and the whole point of imprinting is that it produces attachment behaviour, following the mother, staying close and later choosing mates. Lorenz's work is cited precisely because it links early learning to attachment. It tempts because the classic demonstrations stress the first moving object the young bird sees."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind (1966, 1967) observed preschool children in Berkeley, California, and interviewed and watched their parents at home. She found that children's competence went with distinct patterns of parental authority and described three styles. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) later reorganised her work into two dimensions: demandingness (control, maturity demands, supervision) and responsiveness (warmth, acceptance, sensitivity). Crossing them gives four styles. Authoritative: high demands and high warmth. Parents set clear, firm rules but explain the reasons, listen to the child's view, encourage independence and adjust as the child matures. Children tend to be self-reliant, socially competent, self-controlled and academically successful, the most consistently positive outcomes. Authoritarian: high demands, low warmth. Rules are absolute, obedience is valued for its own sake ('because I said so') and punishment is common; children tend to be obedient but anxious, withdrawn and lower in self-esteem. Permissive (indulgent): low demands, high warmth, few rules; children tend to be impulsive, immature and low in self-control. Uninvolved (neglectful): low on both, added by Maccoby and Martin; it has the poorest outcomes. A memory aid: authoritative means authority with reasons; authoritarian means authority alone. Culture matters. Ruth Chao (1994) argued that Chinese parenting labelled authoritarian often reflects 'training' within a warm, devoted relationship and does not carry the same costs, so style labels should not be applied mechanically across cultures. Option d is right because authoritative parenting combines high demands with high warmth and reasoned discipline that supports autonomy.","traps":{"a":"High demands, low warmth and strict obedience describe the authoritarian style. Such parents value control and conformity, rarely explain rules and often rely on punishment. Baumrind found their children tended to be obedient but anxious, unhappy and lower in self-esteem and social competence. It tempts because the two names differ by only a few letters; remember that authoritarian means authority without reasons.","b":"Low demands and low warmth describe the uninvolved or neglectful style, which Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) added to Baumrind's three. These parents are disengaged, offering little supervision, guidance or affection, sometimes because of their own stress or depression. It is linked to the poorest outcomes, including low achievement, poor self-control and higher risk of delinquency. It tempts mainly as the fourth cell of the grid.","c":"Low demands, high warmth and few rules describe the permissive, or indulgent, style. These parents are loving and accepting but avoid setting limits or enforcing rules and let the child regulate itself. Their children tend to be impulsive, demanding and lower in self-control and persistence. It tempts because warmth is part of authoritative parenting too; the difference is that authoritative parents combine warmth with firm demands."}},{"q":"The 'still face' paradigm developed by Edward Tronick demonstrates that infants:","concept":"The still-face paradigm was introduced by Edward Tronick and colleagues (1978), including T. Berry Brazelton, to test what infants expect from face-to-face interaction. It has three short episodes, each usually about two minutes. First, the parent plays normally with the infant. Second, the parent keeps looking at the infant but adopts a neutral, expressionless face and stops responding to anything the baby does. Third, in the reunion, normal play resumes. The original infants were only a few months old. In the still-face episode, infants first try to re-engage the parent with smiles, coos and reaching, then show reduced positive affect, gaze aversion, distress or crying, self-soothing and loss of postural control, a breakdown in their organised social behaviour. In the reunion, positive affect recovers only partly, a carry-over called the reunion effect. The finding shows that very young infants are active partners who expect contingent, responsive behaviour and try to repair broken interaction. Tronick's mutual regulation model built on this: parent and infant constantly coordinate, mismatch and repair. The paradigm is widely used to study maternal depression, since infants of depressed mothers tend to show flatter, less positive responses, and to predict later attachment. Related procedures: the Strange Situation of Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978) uses separations and reunions at 12 to 18 months; social referencing on the visual cliff shows infants using a parent's expression to guide behaviour; habituation measures declining attention to repeated stimuli. Option d is right because infants show distress and a breakdown of organised behaviour when a responsive caregiver suddenly becomes unresponsive.","traps":{"a":"Infants respond to faces from birth: newborns orient to face-like patterns, and within the first months they track expressions and engage in face-to-face exchange. The still-face effect itself appears in infants only a few months old, who react strongly when the parent's face goes blank. A 12-month threshold contradicts the core finding. It tempts because some face skills, such as social referencing, do mature near the end of the first year.","b":"The still-face effect appears with both mothers and fathers, and the paradigm was not designed to compare attachment strength between parents. Its question is how infants react when any familiar partner suddenly stops responding. Preferences between attachment figures are studied with other methods, such as separation and reunion procedures. It tempts students who link any infant-caregiver experiment with comparisons between mothers and fathers.","c":"Habituation is a decline in attention to a repeated, unchanging stimulus, and it is widely used to study infant perception. The still face does not produce calm habituation; it produces active protest, gaze aversion and distress, which also linger into the reunion episode. The option states the opposite of the finding. It tempts because the still face is unchanging, so habituation sounds like a reasonable prediction."}},{"q":"Piaget's term 'centration' refers to:","concept":"Centration is a term from the stage theory of Jean Piaget (1896 to 1980). It names the preoperational child's tendency (roughly 2 to 7 years) to fix attention on one striking feature of a situation and ignore the other relevant features. The classic demonstration is the conservation of liquid task. Two identical glasses hold the same amount of water, and the experimenter pours one into a taller, thinner glass. The preoperational child says the tall glass now has more, because she centres on height and neglects width. Centration works together with other limits of preoperational thought. Irreversibility is the inability to mentally run an action backwards. Focus on states rather than transformations means the child compares the start and end displays but ignores the pouring that links them. Egocentrism, shown in the three mountains task of Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956), is often described as social centration: the child centres on her own viewpoint. In the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) the child achieves decentration, attending to several dimensions at once, and gains reversible operations. Children then justify conservation with three arguments: identity (nothing was added or taken away), reversibility (pour it back) and compensation (taller but thinner). Conservation of number and substance comes first, weight later and volume last, a staggered pattern Piaget called horizontal decalage. Examiners build variants by swapping in reversibility, animism, egocentrism or hypothetical reasoning and asking which stage each belongs to. Option a is right because centration is precisely the preoperational focus on one salient feature while the other features are ignored.","traps":{"b":"Coordinating schemas, for example looking at and reaching for the same object, or linking what is seen with what is heard, is an achievement of Piaget's sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years). In sub-stage 4 (about 8 to 12 months) infants coordinate schemes into goal-directed means-end behaviour. This is the building of action schemes before symbolic thought, not a limitation of preoperational reasoning. It tempts because the word 'schemas' sounds central to all of Piaget.","c":"Hypothetico-deductive reasoning belongs to Piaget's formal operational stage (about 11 or 12 years onward). Adolescents can generate hypotheses and test them systematically, as in the pendulum problem of Bärbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1958), where only string length changes the speed of swing. This is a mature capacity at the far end of the sequence from centration. It tempts because both terms are Piagetian and examiners like to mix stage labels.","d":"Reversibility is the ability to mentally undo an action, for example imagining the water poured back into the original glass. It is a defining feature of concrete operations (about 7 to 11 years) and, with decentration, it is what makes conservation possible. Centration is the absence of this flexibility, so this option describes the cure rather than the problem. It tempts because reversibility and centration appear together in every conservation explanation."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking place the transition from 'subjective / differentiated' (level 1) to 'self-reflective / reciprocal' (level 2) around:","concept":"Social perspective-taking (role-taking) is the ability to understand that other people have thoughts, feelings and viewpoints different from one's own, and to coordinate those viewpoints. Robert Selman (1980) built a five-level model by interviewing children about social dilemmas. In the best known, Holly has promised her father not to climb trees, then finds a friend's kitten stuck in a tree; children are asked how Holly, her father and her friend each see the situation. Ages are approximate and overlap, and textbooks quote slightly different bands. Level 0, undifferentiated or egocentric (about 3 to 6 years): the child does not separate her own view from others'. Level 1, subjective or social-informational (about 6 to 8): the child knows others may see things differently, but thinks this is only because they have different information. Level 2, self-reflective or reciprocal (about 8 to 10): the child can step into another person's shoes, see her own behaviour from that person's view, and knows others can do the same. Level 3, third-person or mutual (about 10 to 12): the child can step outside a two-person interaction and imagine how a neutral observer would view both parties. Level 4, societal or in-depth (adolescence onward): the person understands that perspectives are shaped by wider social systems and values. The model extends Jean Piaget's idea of decentring to social reasoning and parallels later theory-of-mind research. Option b is right because the move from level 1 to level 2 happens around 7 to 8 years, inside the 5 to 9 year band of early middle childhood.","traps":{"a":"Three to five years falls within Selman's level 0, undifferentiated or egocentric perspective-taking, when a child assumes others think and feel what she does. Even level 1, the recognition that others may have different information, is only beginning at the end of this period. Level 2 reciprocal reflection is not yet possible. It tempts because preschool theory-of-mind milestones, such as passing false-belief tasks around age four, sound like perspective-taking.","c":"Ten to twelve years is the band usually given for level 3, third-person or mutual perspective-taking, when the child can imagine how a neutral observer would see a two-person interaction. By this age level 2 is already established, so the transition asked about has passed. It tempts because textbook bands overlap and some books quote level 2 as running up to about twelve.","d":"Late adolescence is associated with level 4, societal or in-depth perspective-taking, when young people see that viewpoints are shaped by shared social systems, laws and values, and that people can act on motives they do not themselves recognise. This is the top of the sequence, years after the level 1 to level 2 shift. It tempts candidates who equate any higher-order social reasoning with adolescence."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's stage 3 (conventional level) reasoning is best summarised as:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958, doctoral dissertation) extended the work of Jean Piaget (1932) on children's moral judgment into a six-stage theory. He presented boys with moral dilemmas, most famously Heinz, whose wife is dying and who must decide whether to steal an overpriced drug he cannot afford, and scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the answer itself. The stages fall into three levels. Preconventional level: stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation (an act is wrong if it is punished); stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange (what's in it for me; you scratch my back and I scratch yours). Conventional level: stage 3, interpersonal concordance, the good boy or nice girl orientation (being good means meeting the expectations of family and friends, showing trust, loyalty and caring, and winning approval); stage 4, law and order or social system maintenance (doing one's duty, respecting authority, keeping the social order). Postconventional level: stage 5, social contract and individual rights (laws are agreements that can be changed for the common good); stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity, held even against the law. The stages form an invariant sequence. Most adolescents and adults reason at stages 3 and 4, and stage 6 is rare. Carol Gilligan (1982) argued that the scheme favoured a justice orientation and underrated an ethic of care, so women were often scored at stage 3. Option c is right because stage 3 is the good boy or nice girl orientation, in which moral reasoning centres on valuing relationships and winning approval.","traps":{"a":"Obedience to authority to avoid punishment is stage 1 of Kohlberg's preconventional level. Right and wrong are defined by physical consequences: stealing is bad because you get caught and punished. Rules are seen as fixed and imposed by powerful adults, close to Jean Piaget's (1932) heteronomous morality. It tempts because stage 1 and stage 3 both involve pleasing authority figures, but stage 3 is about approval and relationships, not fear.","b":"Instrumental self-interest, or 'what's in it for me', is stage 2 of the preconventional level, also called the instrumental relativist orientation. Fairness is a matter of exchange: I help you if you help me. Other people's needs count only as they serve one's own. It tempts because stage 2 and stage 3 both refer to other people, but stage 2 treats them as trading partners, not as relationships to protect.","d":"Universal ethical principles and human rights describe stage 6, the highest postconventional stage. The person follows self-chosen principles of justice, equality and respect for human dignity, and will break an unjust law to uphold them. Kohlberg found this stage so rare that he came to treat it as largely theoretical. It tempts because it is the most admired answer, but it sits three stages above stage 3."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of an 'internal working model' refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby set out attachment theory in his trilogy Attachment and Loss: Attachment (1969), Separation (1973) and Loss (1980). He argued that infants are biologically prepared to seek closeness to a caregiver, and that repeated experiences with that caregiver are stored as internal working models. He borrowed the phrase from Kenneth Craik (1943), who proposed that the mind builds small-scale models of reality to predict events. An internal working model has two linked parts: a model of the attachment figure (is this person available and responsive when I need them?) and a model of the self (am I worthy of care?). The models are largely unconscious, fairly stable, and act as templates that guide expectations, emotions and behaviour in later relationships. This is how early attachment is thought to carry forward into adulthood. Mary Ainsworth and colleagues (1978) used the Strange Situation to classify infants as secure, anxious-avoidant or anxious-resistant, and Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) added disorganised attachment. Cindy Hazan and Phillip Shaver (1987) applied attachment styles to adult romantic love. Kim Bartholomew and Leonard Horowitz (1991) crossed a positive or negative model of self with a positive or negative model of others to give four adult styles: secure, preoccupied, dismissing and fearful. Variants may ask you to separate working models from Bowlby's other ideas, such as monotropy and a sensitive period for attachment, or from imprinting in birds described by Konrad Lorenz (1935). Option b is right because internal working models are mental representations of self, others and relationships built from early attachment experiences.","traps":{"a":"A circuit linking the hippocampus and amygdala is brain anatomy. The amygdala tags events with emotional, especially fear, significance, and the hippocampus encodes context and declarative memory; their interaction is central to fear-learning research such as that of Joseph LeDoux (1996). Bowlby's working model is a psychological representation, a set of expectations about self and others, not a structure located in tissue. It tempts because attachment research now studies neural correlates.","c":"Genetic templates for fear recall the preparedness theory of Martin Seligman (1971), which holds that evolution makes us quick to fear snakes, heights and other ancestral dangers. These are inborn learning biases shared across a species. An internal working model is the opposite: it is built from each child's own history with caregivers, so two children can form very different models. It tempts because Bowlby also used evolutionary arguments.","d":"The newborn's reflex repertoire includes rooting, sucking, grasping, the Moro (startle) reflex and the Babinski reflex. These are automatic, inborn responses present at birth, used in neurological screening, and many fade during the first year. Bowlby did describe early signalling behaviours, such as crying and smiling, that draw the caregiver close, but a working model is a later, learned representation. It tempts because Bowlby's theory begins with innate behaviours."}},{"q":"Adolescent egocentrism (Elkind) is most directly associated with which two constructs?","concept":"David Elkind (1967) argued that each Piagetian stage brings its own form of egocentrism, a failure to separate one's own point of view from that of others. In adolescence it appears when formal operational thought, described by Jean Piaget, lets young people think about thinking, including other people's thoughts. The adolescent can now imagine what others think, but assumes they are focused on the same thing the adolescent is preoccupied with: himself or herself. Elkind named two products. The imaginary audience is the belief that one is constantly watched and judged, as if on stage. It explains intense self-consciousness, worry about a pimple or a bad haircut, and showing off. The personal fable is the belief that one's experiences and feelings are unique (no one has ever felt what I feel) and, as a common extension, that one is invulnerable (it won't happen to me). The invulnerability strand is often linked to risk-taking such as reckless driving or unprotected sex. David Elkind and Robert Bowen (1979) later developed an Imaginary Audience Scale. Both constructs are thought to peak in early adolescence and fade as social experience grows. For variant questions, remember the other egocentrisms in Piaget's scheme: the infant's sensorimotor egocentrism, and preoperational egocentrism shown in the three mountains task of Piaget and Bärbel Inhelder (1956), where a young child picks her own view when asked what a doll sees. Option d is right because Elkind tied adolescent egocentrism directly to two constructs, the imaginary audience and the personal fable.","traps":{"a":"Conservation (understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in appearance) and reversibility (mentally undoing an action) are achievements of Piaget's concrete operational stage, about 7 to 11 years. They concern logical reasoning about physical quantities, not social self-consciousness. They also mark the decline of preoperational egocentrism rather than the rise of adolescent egocentrism. It tempts because both are Piagetian and egocentrism is a Piagetian word.","b":"Inner speech and scaffolding come from the sociocultural tradition. Lev Vygotsky (1934), in Thought and Language, argued that children's private speech turns into silent inner speech that guides thinking. Scaffolding, a term introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976), is adjustable support from a more skilled partner within the zone of proximal development. Neither concerns adolescent self-focus. It tempts because both are core developmental terms.","c":"Object permanence, Piaget's term for knowing that objects still exist when out of sight, develops through the sensorimotor stage and is secure by about 18 to 24 months. Stranger anxiety, wariness of unfamiliar adults, typically appears around 6 to 8 months of age. Both are infancy milestones, about a decade before adolescent egocentrism. It tempts because stranger anxiety, like the imaginary audience, involves heightened sensitivity to other people."}},{"q":"Habituation paradigms in infants are often used to assess:","concept":"Habituation is the decline in response to a stimulus that is presented repeatedly, the simplest form of learning. Infant researchers turned it into a tool for asking preverbal babies what they can perceive and know. In the habituation and dishabituation (novelty preference) paradigm, an infant is shown one stimulus, say a picture of a cat, again and again. Looking time, sucking rate or heart rate falls as the stimulus becomes familiar. A new stimulus is then shown. If looking recovers (dishabituation), the infant must have detected a difference between old and new, so the method demonstrates discrimination and short-term recognition memory without any words. If the infant is habituated to many different cats and then looks longer at a dog than at a new cat, the infant has formed a category. Robert Fantz (1961) pioneered the related preferential looking method, showing that young infants prefer patterned, face-like displays to plain ones. Renée Baillargeon, Elizabeth Spelke and Stanley Wasserman (1985) adapted habituation into the violation-of-expectation method: after habituating to an event, infants look longer at a physically impossible outcome, which was used to argue for early object permanence. The same logic has tested infant number sense, colour categories and speech-sound discrimination. Distinguish it from the high-amplitude sucking method, from conditioned head-turn procedures, and from the mobile-kicking paradigm, which measures longer-term infant memory. Option a is right because habituation paradigms are mainly used to assess what preverbal infants can discriminate and how they categorise.","traps":{"b":"Long-term declarative memory is conscious memory for facts and events, the episodic and semantic memory of Endel Tulving (1972), usually tested by verbal recall or recognition. Habituation shows only that an infant recognises something seen moments or minutes earlier, closer to short-term recognition memory. Longer-term infant memory is studied with other methods, such as mobile-kicking or deferred imitation tasks. It tempts because dishabituation does depend on memory for the familiar stimulus.","c":"Object permanence, Piaget's term for knowing that hidden objects still exist, has been tested with habituation-based violation-of-expectation studies, such as the drawbridge study of Renée Baillargeon, Elizabeth Spelke and Stanley Wasserman (1985). But that is one application among many. The paradigm is a general method for probing discrimination and categories, not a test reserved for object permanence, and the word 'specifically' makes the option wrong. It tempts because these studies are famous.","d":"Moral reasoning is studied in older children and adults through interviews about dilemmas, as in the work of Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), and it requires language and reflection. Habituation measures attention to perceptual stimuli in babies who cannot yet speak, so it cannot capture the reasons behind moral judgments. It tempts only because moral development is another standard chapter of developmental psychology and appears in the same exam section."}},{"q":"The 'A-not-B error' is a hallmark of which Piagetian sub-stage and reflects:","concept":"Jean Piaget (1954, The Construction of Reality in the Child) traced object permanence, the understanding that objects continue to exist when they cannot be seen, through six sensorimotor sub-stages. In sub-stages 1 and 2 (birth to about 4 months) an object out of sight is out of mind. In sub-stage 3 (about 4 to 8 months) infants retrieve a partly hidden object but not a fully covered one. In sub-stage 4 (about 8 to 12 months), the coordination of secondary circular reactions, infants first search for a fully hidden toy. This is where the A-not-B error appears. A toy is hidden under cloth A several times and the infant finds it each time. Then, in full view, it is hidden under cloth B, yet the infant reaches back to A. Piaget took this to mean the object is still tied to the infant's own successful actions, so permanence is incomplete. In sub-stage 5 (about 12 to 18 months) infants follow visible displacements but fail with invisible ones, and in sub-stage 6 (about 18 to 24 months) mental representation lets them solve invisible displacements. Adele Diamond (1985) linked the error to the immature prefrontal cortex, which weakens working memory and the inhibition of a prepotent, previously rewarded reach; the error grows with longer delays. Linda Smith, Esther Thelen and colleagues (1999) offered a dynamic systems account based on motor habits. Option d is right because the A-not-B error belongs to the sensorimotor period and shows incomplete object permanence with a habitual reach to the previously rewarded location.","traps":{"a":"Formal operational thought (about 11 or 12 years onward) is Piaget's final stage, when adolescents reason about hypotheses, abstract propositions and possibilities. Difficulty with hypotheticals would describe a younger child faced with formal tasks, not a ten-month-old reaching for a toy. The A-not-B task is a search task for infants who have no language at all. It tempts only because it is attached to a confident Piagetian stage label.","b":"Preoperational thought (about 2 to 7 years) is marked by centration, the focus on one salient feature such as the height of water in a glass, along with egocentrism and irreversibility. These limits concern symbolic reasoning in young children, while the A-not-B error is a search error of infants under a year who have not yet reached symbolic thought. It tempts because 'centration on appearance' sounds like a perceptual mistake.","c":"Concrete operational thought (about 7 to 11 years) is when children gain reversibility, the ability to mentally undo an action, which supports conservation. Failure of reversibility is actually a preoperational limitation, so this option mislabels the stage as well as the task. Neither has anything to do with an infant searching for a hidden toy. It tempts because reversibility is a familiar Piagetian term."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of major depressive disorder to be present for at least:","concept":"Major depressive disorder (MDD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) is diagnosed from major depressive episodes. Criterion A requires five or more of nine symptoms present during the same 2-week period, representing a change from previous functioning, with at least one being depressed mood or markedly diminished interest or pleasure (anhedonia). The nine are: depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day; anhedonia; significant weight or appetite change; insomnia or hypersomnia; psychomotor agitation or retardation observable by others; fatigue or loss of energy; feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt; reduced ability to think or concentrate, or indecisiveness; and recurrent thoughts of death, suicidal ideation or a suicide attempt. The symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment, must not be due to a substance or medical condition, and must not be better explained by a psychotic disorder, and there must never have been a manic or hypomanic episode. DSM-5 removed the DSM-IV bereavement exclusion, so depression after a loss can be diagnosed if the criteria are met. Children and adolescents may show irritable rather than depressed mood. ICD-11 also uses a 2-week minimum for a depressive episode. Exams test duration thresholds side by side: manic episode at least 1 week, hypomanic episode at least 4 consecutive days, persistent depressive disorder at least 2 years in adults, PTSD more than 1 month, generalised anxiety disorder and schizophrenia at least 6 months. Option b is right because DSM-5 requires the symptoms of a major depressive episode to be present for at least 2 weeks.","traps":{"a":"One week is the minimum duration of a manic episode in DSM-5, or any duration if hospitalisation is needed. Depressive symptoms lasting only days may fit a short-duration depressive episode (4 to 13 days) under other specified depressive disorder, but not MDD. It tempts because 1 week sits beside 2 weeks in the mood disorders chapter, and the manic and depressive thresholds are easily swapped under exam pressure.","c":"One month is the threshold for several other conditions: PTSD needs symptoms for more than 1 month, schizophrenia needs at least 1 month of active-phase symptoms, and brief psychotic disorder must resolve within 1 month. It is double the MDD requirement. It tempts because many students assume that a serious mood disorder must last at least a month before it can be diagnosed.","d":"Six months is the duration required for generalised anxiety disorder, for the total course of schizophrenia, and typically for specific phobia and social anxiety disorder. No mood episode in DSM-5 uses a 6-month rule; the long mood thresholds are 2 years for persistent depressive disorder and cyclothymic disorder. It tempts because 6 months is the most common duration in the DSM-5 anxiety disorders."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least:","concept":"DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) diagnoses schizophrenia with three layers of criteria. Criterion A: two or more of five symptoms, each present for a significant portion of time during a 1-month period (less if successfully treated), and at least one must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. The five are delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, and negative symptoms (diminished emotional expression or avolition). Criterion B: functioning in work, relationships or self-care is markedly below the previous level. Criterion C: continuous signs of disturbance persist for at least 6 months, which must include at least 1 month of active-phase symptoms and may include prodromal or residual periods, when only negative symptoms or attenuated positive symptoms are present. The duration ladder of the psychotic disorders is a favourite exam target. Brief psychotic disorder lasts at least 1 day but less than 1 month, with eventual full return to previous functioning. Schizophreniform disorder lasts at least 1 month but less than 6 months, and functional decline is not required. Schizophrenia needs 6 months or more. Delusional disorder requires one or more delusions for at least 1 month without other prominent psychotic symptoms. Schizoaffective disorder needs a major mood episode concurrent with Criterion A, plus delusions or hallucinations for 2 weeks or more without a mood episode. ICD-10 and ICD-11 differ: they require only about 1 month of symptoms for schizophrenia. Option c is right because DSM-5 requires continuous signs for at least 6 months, including at least 1 month of active-phase symptoms.","traps":{"a":"One week is not a schizophrenia threshold; it is the minimum duration of a manic episode. A psychotic episode lasting a week would be classified as brief psychotic disorder, which lasts from 1 day to less than 1 month and ends with full recovery, sometimes after a marked stressor or after childbirth. It tempts candidates who confuse the short psychosis thresholds with the full schizophrenia requirement.","b":"One month is the minimum active-phase duration inside the schizophrenia criteria, and also the lower limit of schizophreniform disorder and of delusional disorder. On its own, a month of psychosis that resolves before 6 months is schizophreniform, not schizophrenia. It tempts because ICD-10 and ICD-11 do diagnose schizophrenia after about 1 month, so students who learned the ICD rule pick it.","d":"Twelve months is not used for schizophrenia. A 1-year duration appears elsewhere, for example in Tourette's disorder and in persistent depressive disorder in children and adolescents. Requiring 12 months would set the bar higher than DSM-5 does and leave patients with 7 to 11 months of illness without the diagnosis. It tempts because schizophrenia is thought of as chronic, so a longer period seems right."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia (Schneider) most prominently include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist (Clinical Psychopathology, English edition 1959), listed symptoms that he considered of first rank in diagnosing schizophrenia when no organic brain disease could be found. They share one theme: the boundary between self and world breaks down, so thoughts, feelings and actions feel imposed from outside. They are usually counted as eleven. Auditory hallucinations: audible thoughts (thought echo), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts, and somatic passivity (bodily sensations imposed by an outside agency). Delusional perception: a normal perception given a sudden, private delusional meaning, such as a traffic light meaning the patient has been chosen. Schneider valued them because they are easy to recognise reliably, but he did not claim they were pathognomonic, and they also occur in mania. ICD-10 gave them weight, while DSM-5 removed the earlier special status of bizarre delusions and commenting voices. Compare Eugen Bleuler (1911), whose fundamental symptoms are often summarised as the four As: loosening of associations, blunted affect, ambivalence and autism, which overlap with modern negative symptoms. Timothy Crow (1980) separated type I schizophrenia (positive symptoms, good drug response) from type II (negative symptoms, structural brain change). Option c is right because thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, made feelings and impulses, somatic passivity and third-person voices are the core of Schneider's first-rank symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Avolition and flat affect are negative symptoms, a loss of normal functions: reduced motivation, reduced emotional expression, alogia, anhedonia and asociality. They echo the fundamental symptoms of Eugen Bleuler (1911) and the type II syndrome of Timothy Crow (1980), not Schneider's list, which is made up of positive symptoms. It tempts because negative symptoms are central to schizophrenia and are often more disabling than first-rank symptoms.","b":"Memory impairment and disorientation point to an organic condition such as delirium, dementia or Korsakoff syndrome. In schizophrenia orientation and memory are usually preserved, and Schneider gave his first-rank symptoms diagnostic weight only once organic brain disease had been ruled out. It tempts because cognitive deficits do occur in schizophrenia, but they are problems of attention, working memory and executive function, not disorientation.","d":"Catatonic excitement is purposeless, excessive motor activity not influenced by outside stimuli, one pole of catatonia, which Karl Kahlbaum (1874) first described. Catatonia occurs in schizophrenia but also in mood disorders and medical conditions, and DSM-5 treats it as a specifier across disorders. It is a motor syndrome, not one of Schneider's experiences of thought alienation and passivity. It tempts because catatonia was a classic schizophrenia subtype."}},{"q":"A patient with persistent intrusive thoughts of contamination, accompanied by ritualised hand-washing that consumes >1 hour/day, is best classified under DSM-5 as:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) requires obsessions, compulsions or both. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, urges or images that are experienced as intrusive and unwanted and that cause marked anxiety or distress; the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise them. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, praying, repeating words) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or according to rigid rules. They aim to reduce distress or prevent a feared event, but are not realistically connected to it or are clearly excessive. The symptoms must be time-consuming, taking more than 1 hour a day, or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Specifiers record insight (good or fair, poor, or absent insight with delusional beliefs) and whether there is a current or past tic disorder. Common symptom dimensions are contamination and washing, harm and checking, symmetry and ordering, and unacceptable taboo thoughts. DSM-5 moved OCD out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders, alongside body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania and excoriation disorder. The key contrast with the anxiety disorders is the neutralising ritual and the intrusive, unwanted quality of the thought. First-line treatments are exposure and response prevention, which grew from the work of Victor Meyer (1966), and serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs and clomipramine. Option d is right because intrusive contamination obsessions with washing rituals taking over an hour a day are the textbook picture of OCD.","traps":{"a":"Generalised anxiety disorder (GAD) is excessive, hard-to-control worry about many everyday matters, such as work, health, money and family, on more days than not for at least 6 months, with physical or mental symptoms such as restlessness, fatigue or muscle tension. The worry in GAD is about realistic topics and is not neutralised by fixed rituals. It tempts because both disorders involve anxious, repetitive thinking.","b":"Illness anxiety disorder, which replaced much of DSM-IV hypochondriasis, is a preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, with no or only mild somatic symptoms, lasting at least 6 months, often with repeated body checking or avoidance of doctors. The fear is of having a disease, not of contamination that must be washed away by rituals. It tempts because contamination fears can sound like health anxiety.","c":"Specific phobia is a marked, out-of-proportion fear of a particular object or situation, such as animals, heights, blood or injections, that is actively avoided and typically lasts 6 months or more. A person with a phobia avoids the feared object; there are no intrusive, unwanted thoughts neutralised by hour-long rituals. It tempts because contamination fear looks like the fear of one specific thing, dirt or germs."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder, per DSM-5, requires ≥5 of 9 criteria. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) is a pervasive pattern of instability of interpersonal relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood and present in a variety of contexts. Five or more of nine criteria are needed: (1) frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; (2) unstable, intense relationships alternating between idealisation and devaluation; (3) identity disturbance with a markedly unstable self-image or sense of self; (4) impulsivity in at least two potentially self-damaging areas, such as spending, sex, substance use, reckless driving or binge eating; (5) recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures or threats, or self-mutilating behaviour; (6) affective instability from marked reactivity of mood; (7) chronic feelings of emptiness; (8) inappropriate, intense anger or difficulty controlling anger; (9) transient, stress-related paranoid ideation or severe dissociative symptoms. BPD sits in cluster B with antisocial, histrionic and narcissistic personality disorders. Adolph Stern (1938) used the term borderline for patients on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) later described borderline personality organisation. Marsha Linehan (1993) developed dialectical behaviour therapy from her biosocial theory. The distractor here comes from antisocial personality disorder, which requires age at least 18 and evidence of conduct disorder with onset before age 15, including aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, and serious rule violations. Option b is right because aggression toward animals and rule-breaking before age 15 belong to conduct disorder and antisocial personality disorder, not to the nine BPD criteria.","traps":{"a":"Frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment is criterion 1 of BPD. The person may react to a cancelled meeting or a brief separation with panic or rage, reading it as rejection. It is closely tied to the unstable, intense relationships of criterion 2. It tempts in a NOT question because it sounds like an ordinary emotional reaction, so a student unsure of the list may guess it is the odd one out.","c":"Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures or threats, or self-mutilating behaviour such as cutting or burning, is criterion 5 of BPD. Self-harm often serves to regulate unbearable emotion, and it is a main target of dialectical behaviour therapy. It tempts in an odd-one-out question because self-harm may seem like a depression symptom rather than a personality feature, yet it is one of the most characteristic BPD criteria.","d":"Identity disturbance, a markedly and persistently unstable self-image or sense of self, is criterion 3 of BPD. Goals, values, career plans, friendships and even sexual identity may shift suddenly. Otto Kernberg's concept of identity diffusion lies behind it. It tempts because identity problems are also linked with normal adolescence and with dissociative disorders, so a student may think it belongs to another diagnosis."}},{"q":"Capgras syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the belief that a familiar person, usually a spouse, parent or close relative, has been replaced by an impostor who looks identical. Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923) described it as l'illusion des sosies, the illusion of doubles, in a woman who believed that people around her had been replaced by doubles. It is the best known of the delusional misidentification syndromes. In Fregoli syndrome, described in 1927, the patient believes that a persecutor is disguising himself as various strangers, so strangers are really one familiar person in disguise; it is named after the Italian actor Leopoldo Fregoli, famous for quick changes of character. In intermetamorphosis, people are believed to swap identities with each other. In the syndrome of subjective doubles, the patient believes a double of himself exists. Capgras occurs in schizophrenia, in dementia (especially dementia with Lewy bodies and Alzheimer's disease) and after brain injury. Hadyn Ellis and Andrew Young (1990) proposed that Capgras is a mirror image of prosopagnosia: the face is recognised, but the usual emotional response to a familiar face is missing, so the patient concludes it must belong to a stranger. In line with this, patients show reduced skin conductance responses to familiar faces. Two other named delusions often appear as distractors: Cotard's syndrome, the nihilistic belief that one is dead or does not exist, described by Jules Cotard (1880), and Othello syndrome, delusional jealousy. Option d is right because Capgras is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an impostor.","traps":{"a":"The belief that one is dead, does not exist, has lost blood or internal organs, or is rotting is Cotard's syndrome, a nihilistic delusion described by Jules Cotard (1880). It is classically seen in severe psychotic depression, especially in older adults, and sometimes in schizophrenia or brain disease. It tempts because Cotard and Capgras are both French eponymous delusions and are usually taught on the same page.","b":"Delusional jealousy, the unshakable false belief that one's partner is unfaithful, is called Othello syndrome after Shakespeare's play. The patient may search for evidence, check clothing or follow the partner, and the risk of violence is real. It is linked with alcohol dependence and corresponds to the jealous type of DSM-5 delusional disorder. It tempts because it is another delusion centred on a close person.","c":"The belief that a stranger is really a familiar person in disguise, often a persecutor who keeps changing appearance, is Fregoli syndrome, named after the Italian quick-change actor Leopoldo Fregoli. It is the reverse of Capgras: in Fregoli strangers are wrongly identified as someone familiar, while in Capgras a familiar person is wrongly treated as a stranger. It tempts because the two misidentification delusions are built to be confused."}},{"q":"ICD-11 retains a category of dissociative neurological symptom disorder (also termed 'conversion'). Its core feature is:","concept":"ICD-11 (World Health Organization, in effect from 2022) places dissociative neurological symptom disorder in its chapter on dissociative disorders. The core feature is motor, sensory or cognitive symptoms that suggest neurological disease but are not consistent with any recognised disease of the nervous system, other mental disorder or medical condition, and that are involuntary rather than deliberately produced. Specifiers name the main symptom, for example visual disturbance, non-epileptic seizures, speech disturbance, paresis or weakness, gait disturbance, movement disturbance or sensory alteration. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) calls the same picture conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) and places it under somatic symptom and related disorders; ICD-11's own category title avoids the word conversion. DSM-5 requires clinical findings showing incompatibility with recognised neurological conditions, such as Hoover's sign in functional leg weakness, and dropped the old requirement that a psychological stressor be identified. The term conversion comes from Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895, Studies on Hysteria), who proposed that unresolved psychic conflict is converted into bodily symptoms, while Pierre Janet (1889) had described dissociation. La belle indifférence, apparent unconcern about the symptom, is not reliable for diagnosis. The key variant is intention and motive. In conversion the symptom is not consciously produced. In factitious disorder symptoms are intentionally produced without obvious external reward, to occupy the sick role. In malingering they are intentionally produced for external gain such as money or avoiding duty. Option d is right because the core feature is neurological symptoms incompatible with known disease pathways and not intentionally produced.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain, such as compensation, avoiding work or military duty, evading prosecution or obtaining drugs, is malingering. DSM-5 lists it among other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention, not as a mental disorder. If symptoms are faked without an obvious external reward, the diagnosis is factitious disorder. It tempts because malingering also produces symptoms without disease, but conversion is involuntary.","b":"A persecutory delusion is a fixed false belief that one is being harmed, followed or conspired against. A delusion by definition implies impaired reality testing, so 'intact reality testing' is a contradiction; a belief held with intact reality testing would be an overvalued idea. Persecutory delusions belong to psychotic disorders such as delusional disorder or schizophrenia. It tempts by sounding clinical, but it has nothing to do with bodily symptoms.","c":"A persistent memory deficit caused by organic brain injury is an amnestic or neurocognitive disorder, for example after traumatic brain injury, encephalitis, or thiamine deficiency in Korsakoff syndrome. The symptom is fully explained by demonstrable brain pathology, the opposite of a dissociative neurological symptom, which is incompatible with known disease. It tempts because dissociative amnesia also involves memory loss, but that amnesia is psychogenic and usually autobiographical."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5 requires the symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) moved from the anxiety disorders to a new chapter, Trauma- and Stressor-Related Disorders. Criterion A is exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence: by direct experience, by witnessing it, by learning that it happened to a close family member or friend (if violent or accidental), or by repeated or extreme exposure to aversive details, as in first responders. Exposure through media counts only if it is work-related. Symptoms fall into four clusters: intrusion (at least 1 of 5), avoidance (at least 1 of 2), negative alterations in cognitions and mood (at least 2 of 7), and alterations in arousal and reactivity (at least 2 of 6). Criterion F sets the time rule: the disturbance must last more than 1 month, and it must cause distress or impairment. The time line is a classic exam target. In the first month after trauma, symptoms lasting from 3 days to 1 month are diagnosed as acute stress disorder, which needs 9 of 14 listed symptoms. If symptoms persist beyond 1 month the diagnosis becomes PTSD. The specifier with delayed expression is used when full criteria are not met until at least 6 months after the event. A dissociative subtype covers depersonalisation or derealisation, and separate criteria apply to children aged 6 and younger. Adjustment disorder covers distress after a non-traumatic stressor, starting within 3 months. ICD-11 adds complex PTSD. Option d is right because DSM-5 requires PTSD symptoms to persist for more than 1 month.","traps":{"a":"Two days is below every DSM-5 trauma threshold. Acute stress disorder requires symptoms lasting at least 3 days, so reactions in the first 2 days are treated as normal, expected distress. In ICD-11 a transient reaction of this kind is recorded as an acute stress reaction, which is not classed as a mental disorder. It tempts because early post-trauma symptoms are vivid and students half-remember a threshold counted in days.","b":"One week has no special meaning for PTSD. At 1 week after a trauma, symptoms would fall inside the acute stress disorder window, which runs from 3 days to 1 month. One week is the minimum duration of a manic episode, which is the most likely source of the confusion. It tempts as a middle value between the very short and the longer thresholds.","c":"Six months appears in PTSD only in the delayed expression specifier, used when full criteria are not met until at least 6 months after the trauma, even if some symptoms began earlier. It is not the minimum duration of symptoms. It tempts because DSM-IV split acute from chronic PTSD at 3 months, and because 6 months is the usual duration for anxiety disorders, where PTSD used to sit."}},{"q":"A patient presents with episodes of binge eating ≥1/week for ≥3 months, without compensatory behaviours, with marked distress about the binges. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:","concept":"Binge-eating disorder (BED) became a full diagnosis in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), after appearing in DSM-IV only in an appendix of criteria sets for further study. A binge has two parts: eating, in a discrete period such as within 2 hours, an amount of food definitely larger than most people would eat in similar circumstances, and a sense of loss of control over eating during the episode. In BED the binges are associated with at least three of five features: eating much more rapidly than normal, eating until uncomfortably full, eating large amounts when not physically hungry, eating alone because of embarrassment, and feeling disgusted, depressed or very guilty afterwards. There must be marked distress about bingeing, the binges must occur at least once a week for 3 months, and there must be no regular inappropriate compensatory behaviour. Severity is graded by weekly binge frequency, from mild (1 to 3) to extreme (14 or more). The decisive contrast is with bulimia nervosa, described by Gerald Russell (1979): binges plus recurrent compensatory behaviour (self-induced vomiting, laxatives, diuretics, fasting or excessive exercise), both at least once a week for 3 months, with self-evaluation unduly influenced by body shape and weight. DSM-5 lowered the bulimia frequency from twice a week in DSM-IV. If bingeing or purging occurs only in someone with significantly low body weight, anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type, takes precedence. Body weight is not a criterion. Option a is right because weekly binges for 3 months with marked distress and no compensatory behaviour define binge-eating disorder.","traps":{"b":"Bulimia nervosa also involves recurrent binges at least once a week for 3 months, but it requires recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour, such as self-induced vomiting, laxative or diuretic misuse, fasting or excessive exercise, and self-worth unduly tied to shape and weight. The stem explicitly says there are no compensatory behaviours, which rules bulimia out. It tempts because the binge definition and frequency are identical in both disorders.","c":"Anorexia nervosa, binge-eating/purging type, requires significantly low body weight from restriction, intense fear of weight gain and disturbed body image, with recurrent bingeing or purging in the last 3 months. Nothing in the stem mentions low weight, restriction or body-image disturbance, and purging is absent. It tempts because the subtype name contains the word binge, which matches the patient's main complaint.","d":"Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID), new in DSM-5, is restriction of food intake because of low interest in eating, aversion to sensory features of food, or fear of consequences such as choking or vomiting, leading to weight loss, nutritional deficiency or impaired functioning. There is no body-image concern and no bingeing. It tempts as another new DSM-5 eating disorder, but its core is eating too little."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is the experience of vivid, often complex visual hallucinations in a person with significant visual loss who is otherwise mentally well and usually knows that the images are not real. The Swiss naturalist Charles Bonnet (1760) described the experiences of his grandfather, Charles Lullin, who had poor sight from cataracts and saw people, birds, carriages and buildings that were not there. The images range from simple patterns, grids and colours to detailed faces, figures in costume, animals, landscapes and tiny (Lilliputian) people. They are silent, often appear in the area of lost vision, and are not part of any delusional system. Common causes are age-related macular degeneration, glaucoma, cataract and diabetic retinopathy, so the condition is mostly seen in older adults, but damage anywhere along the visual pathway can produce it. The accepted explanation is deafferentation, a release phenomenon: when the visual cortex loses normal input it becomes hyperexcitable and generates its own images. Because insight is kept, the hallucinations are sometimes called pseudo-hallucinations, though that term is used inconsistently. Treatment is mainly explanation, reassurance and improving vision; many patients fear they are going mad and hide the symptom. Differentiate it from delirium (hallucinations with clouded consciousness and fluctuating attention), from dementia with Lewy bodies (well-formed visual hallucinations with cognitive decline and parkinsonism), from hypnagogic hallucinations at sleep onset, and from musical hallucinations in acquired hearing loss, the auditory analogue. Option c is right because Charles Bonnet syndrome is visual hallucinations in people with significant vision loss who retain insight.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in people with hearing loss do occur, usually as music or voices, and are sometimes popularly called musical ear syndrome. They are described as the auditory analogue of Charles Bonnet syndrome, but the eponym itself refers to visual hallucinations in visual loss. It tempts because the same release mechanism, a sensory cortex deprived of input, is thought to explain both conditions.","b":"Multimodal hallucinations, often visual and frightening, are common in delirium, an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness caused by a medical condition or by drug intoxication or withdrawal, as in delirium tremens. Patients in delirium usually lack insight and their consciousness is clouded. In Charles Bonnet syndrome consciousness is clear and insight is kept. It tempts because both conditions are common in elderly patients.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations, typically an unpleasant smell such as burning rubber, can be the aura of a seizure starting in the medial temporal lobe, classically called uncinate fits. They are brief, stereotyped and followed by the seizure itself, and they have no link to vision loss. It tempts because both are hallucinations explained by abnormal cortical activity in a neurological rather than a psychiatric condition."}},{"q":"Delirium is most reliably differentiated from dementia by:","concept":"Delirium and dementia both cause confusion in older adults, so telling them apart is a core clinical skill. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) delirium is a disturbance in attention (reduced ability to direct, focus, sustain and shift attention) and awareness (reduced orientation to the environment). It develops over a short period, usually hours to a few days, represents a change from baseline, and tends to fluctuate during the day, often worsening at night. There is an additional cognitive disturbance, such as memory deficit, disorientation, language problems or perceptual disturbance, and evidence that it is a direct physiological consequence of a medical condition, intoxication, withdrawal or medication. Delirium may be hyperactive, hypoactive or mixed; the hypoactive form is easily missed. It is usually reversible when the cause is treated. Dementia, renamed major neurocognitive disorder in DSM-5, is a substantial decline in one or more cognitive domains that interferes with independence. Its onset is usually insidious and its course gradually progressive over months to years, while alertness and attention remain relatively preserved until late stages. The Confusion Assessment Method of Sharon Inouye (1990) captures the difference: acute onset and fluctuating course, plus inattention, plus either disorganised thinking or altered level of consciousness. Two points help with variants: dementia with Lewy bodies also shows fluctuating cognition, and dementia is the biggest risk factor for delirium, so the two often coexist. Option c is right because acute onset, a fluctuating course and disturbed attention and consciousness most reliably separate delirium from dementia.","traps":{"a":"Older age at onset does not separate the two: both delirium and dementia become far more common with age, and advanced age is a major risk factor for each. Delirium can occur at any age, for example in children with high fever or young adults withdrawing from alcohol, and early-onset dementias begin before 65. It tempts because both conditions are classically pictured in an elderly patient on a hospital ward.","b":"Confabulation is the production of false memories without intent to deceive, filling gaps in memory. It is the hallmark of Korsakoff syndrome, the amnestic disorder caused by thiamine deficiency in chronic alcohol use, described by Sergei Korsakoff (1887), and it also occurs in some dementias. It does not reliably distinguish delirium from dementia. It tempts because it is a vivid memory sign seen in organic disorders.","d":"Aphasia (loss of language) and apraxia (inability to carry out learned movements) are cortical signs of dementias such as Alzheimer's disease, often taught with agnosia and amnesia as the four As; DSM-IV required memory impairment plus one such deficit for dementia. As early features they suggest specific syndromes such as primary progressive aphasia. It tempts because these signs describe dementia, but they are not the most reliable discriminator."}},{"q":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (PDD) is the DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) diagnosis for chronic depression. It merged two DSM-IV diagnoses, dysthymic disorder and chronic major depressive disorder. Dysthymic disorder itself first appeared in DSM-III (1980), replacing the older label depressive neurosis. Criterion A is depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults; in children and adolescents mood can be irritable and the duration is at least 1 year. While depressed, the person has two or more of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy or fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration or difficulty making decisions, and feelings of hopelessness. During the 2-year period the person has never been without symptoms for more than 2 months at a time. Criteria for major depression may be continuously present, and specifiers note whether there are pure dysthymic symptoms, a persistent major depressive episode, or intermittent major episodes. Major depressive episodes superimposed on dysthymia are commonly called double depression. There must never have been a manic or hypomanic episode, and cyclothymic criteria must never have been met. Cyclothymic disorder uses the same timetable, 2 years in adults and 1 year in youth, but alternates hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria. Contrast this with major depressive disorder, which needs only 2 weeks but five of nine symptoms. Option a is right because PDD requires depressed mood more days than not for at least 2 years in adults and 1 year in children and adolescents.","traps":{"b":"Six months is the minimum duration for generalised anxiety disorder and for the total course of schizophrenia, and the usual guide for specific phobia and social anxiety disorder. No DSM-5 depressive disorder uses a 6-month threshold. It tempts because 6 months feels like a reasonable definition of chronic, and because many students store 6 months as the default DSM duration.","c":"One month is the duration threshold for PTSD (more than 1 month) and for the active phase of schizophrenia, and the upper limit of brief psychotic disorder. A month of low mood is far shorter than chronic depression; if severe, it would be judged against the 2-week rule for major depression. It tempts because one month sounds like a sensible period for a mood that is low most days.","d":"Five years appears in no DSM-5 mood disorder criterion. It overshoots the actual threshold and would exclude many people who have been depressed for two to four years. Persistent depression can indeed last decades, especially when it starts early, which makes a large number feel plausible. It tempts candidates who equate chronic with very long and who do not remember the exact figure."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder per DSM-5 requires multiple motor and ≥1 vocal tic, with onset before age 18 and duration of at least:","concept":"Tics are sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic motor movements or vocalisations. Georges Gilles de la Tourette (1885), a French neurologist, described patients with multiple tics and involuntary vocalisations, and the syndrome bears his name. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) places tic disorders among the neurodevelopmental disorders and orders them by type and duration. Tourette's disorder requires both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics at some time during the illness, though not necessarily at the same time; tics that may wax and wane in frequency but have persisted for more than 1 year since first onset; onset before age 18; and no attribution to a substance or another medical condition. Persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder has the same 1-year and age rules but only motor or only vocal tics, never both. Provisional tic disorder covers tics present for less than 1 year. DSM-5 removed the DSM-IV requirement that there be no tic-free period of more than 3 consecutive months. Tics usually begin between about 4 and 6 years, often peak around 10 to 12, and frequently improve in adulthood. Coprolalia, involuntary swearing, occurs only in a minority. Common comorbidities are ADHD and OCD. Treatment includes habit reversal training, first described by Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn (1973), and its extension the Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics, along with alpha-2 agonists such as clonidine and antipsychotics such as haloperidol or aripiprazole. Option c is right because DSM-5 requires the tics of Tourette's disorder to have persisted for more than 1 year.","traps":{"a":"One month has no role in DSM-5 tic criteria. DSM-IV transient tic disorder required tics for at least 4 weeks but less than 12 consecutive months, and DSM-5 replaced it with provisional tic disorder, which simply requires tics for less than 1 year. It tempts because older textbooks still quote the 4-week minimum, and one month is a common duration elsewhere in DSM-5, such as PTSD.","b":"Three months comes from DSM-IV, which required that during the year of tics there be no tic-free period longer than 3 consecutive months. DSM-5 dropped this clause because tics naturally wax and wane. Three months was never the total duration of Tourette's. It tempts because students who learned DSM-IV remember a 3-month number attached to the tic criteria.","d":"Six months is the duration used for generalised anxiety disorder, the total course of schizophrenia, and the symptom criteria for ADHD, which is also a neurodevelopmental disorder and common in Tourette's. It is not a tic disorder threshold; a person with tics for 6 months would have provisional tic disorder. It tempts because of the ADHD link and the frequency of 6 months across DSM-5."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, per DSM-5 is defined by:","concept":"Charles Lasègue (1873) in France and William Gull (1874) in England described the disorder that Gull named anorexia nervosa. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) defines it with three criteria. A: restriction of energy intake relative to requirements, leading to significantly low body weight in the context of age, sex, developmental trajectory and physical health. B: intense fear of gaining weight or becoming fat, or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain, even at a significantly low weight. C: disturbance in the way body weight or shape is experienced, undue influence of weight or shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the low weight. Two subtypes are defined by the last 3 months. In the restricting type, weight loss is achieved mainly through dieting, fasting or excessive exercise, with no recurrent binge eating or purging. In the binge-eating/purging type there are recurrent binges or purging (self-induced vomiting, or misuse of laxatives, diuretics or enemas). DSM-5 made two changes examiners like to test: it dropped amenorrhoea as a requirement, and it replaced fixed cut-offs (DSM-IV used less than 85 percent of expected weight; ICD-10 used a BMI of 17.5 or less) with clinical judgement. In adults BMI now sets severity: mild 17 or more, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme below 15. Option d is right because the restricting subtype is defined by restriction causing significantly low weight, fear of weight gain and distorted body image, with no recurrent binge or purge episodes in the last 3 months.","traps":{"a":"A BMI cut-off of 17.5 or less was part of the ICD-10 criteria. DSM-5 has no fixed BMI threshold for diagnosis; it requires significantly low body weight judged clinically, and uses BMI only to grade severity, with mild meaning 17 or more. The word 'only' also ignores the fear of weight gain and body-image criteria. It tempts because 17.5 is a widely memorised number.","b":"Selective eating without weight loss sounds like picky eating, or like avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder (ARFID) when it causes nutritional deficiency, supplement dependence or impaired functioning even without weight loss. ARFID has no fear of fatness and no body-image disturbance. Anorexia requires significantly low body weight, so 'without weight loss' excludes it. It tempts because restriction of food is common to both.","c":"Recurrent binge eating with purging describes either bulimia nervosa, when weight is normal or high, or the binge-eating/purging subtype of anorexia, when weight is significantly low. The restricting subtype is defined precisely by the absence of recurrent bingeing or purging in the last 3 months. It tempts because both anorexia subtypes share the same core criteria and differ only in this one feature."}},{"q":"Which symptom cluster is NOT among the DSM-5 PTSD criteria?","concept":"DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) reorganised PTSD symptoms into four clusters, up from three in DSM-IV (re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, and hyperarousal). Criterion B, intrusion, needs at least one of five: recurrent intrusive distressing memories, distressing dreams, dissociative reactions such as flashbacks, intense psychological distress at reminders, and marked physiological reactions to reminders. Criterion C, avoidance, needs at least one of two: avoiding trauma-related memories, thoughts or feelings, and avoiding external reminders such as people, places and activities. Criterion D, negative alterations in cognitions and mood, needs at least two of seven: inability to recall an important aspect of the trauma, persistent exaggerated negative beliefs about oneself or the world, distorted blame of self or others, persistent negative emotions such as fear, anger, guilt or shame, diminished interest in activities, detachment from others, and persistent inability to feel positive emotions. Criterion E, alterations in arousal and reactivity, needs at least two of six: irritability or angry outbursts, reckless or self-destructive behaviour, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration and sleep disturbance. Splitting DSM-IV's avoidance and numbing cluster into C and D was the main structural change. ICD-11 takes a narrower view of PTSD, with three core elements (re-experiencing in the present, avoidance, and a sense of current threat), and adds complex PTSD with disturbances in self-organisation. Magical thinking and ideas of reference belong elsewhere: they are criteria of schizotypal personality disorder, and ideas of reference also occur in psychosis. Option a is right because magical thinking and ideas of reference are not part of any DSM-5 PTSD cluster.","traps":{"b":"Avoidance of trauma reminders is DSM-5 PTSD Criterion C, needing at least one of two: avoiding distressing memories, thoughts or feelings, or avoiding external reminders such as people, places, conversations or objects. Avoidance keeps the fear unprocessed and is targeted by exposure therapies. It tempts in an odd-one-out question because avoidance is also central to phobias, so a student may wrongly file it under anxiety rather than PTSD.","c":"Negative alterations in cognitions and mood is DSM-5 PTSD Criterion D, needing at least two of seven symptoms, such as persistent negative beliefs, distorted self-blame, persistent guilt or shame, detachment and inability to feel positive emotions. It was new in DSM-5, created by splitting DSM-IV's avoidance and numbing cluster. It tempts because students who learned the older three-cluster model may not recognise it as a PTSD cluster.","d":"Intrusion is DSM-5 PTSD Criterion B, needing at least one of five: intrusive memories, nightmares, dissociative flashbacks, and psychological or physiological distress at reminders. It corresponds to DSM-IV re-experiencing and is the most characteristic PTSD feature. It tempts in an odd-one-out question because intrusive thoughts are also central to OCD, so a student may think the term belongs there rather than to PTSD."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is best characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is a neurological disorder of emotional expression, also called pathological laughing and crying, emotional incontinence or involuntary emotional expression disorder. Patients have sudden, brief episodes of crying or laughing, or both, that are out of proportion to, or even opposite to, what they actually feel. A mildly sad comment, or no clear trigger at all, can set off uncontrollable sobbing lasting seconds to minutes, and the patient may say they do not feel sad inside. The episodes are stereotyped and the person cannot stop them. PBA occurs in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), multiple sclerosis, stroke, traumatic brain injury, Alzheimer's disease and Parkinson's disease. The name comes from pseudobulbar palsy, in which bilateral damage to the upper motor neuron (corticobulbar) pathways releases brainstem centres for facial expression from cortical control; cerebellar circuits are also thought to be involved. The key distinction is between mood and affect. Mood is the sustained inner emotional state, and affect is its outward expression, often compared to climate and weather. In depression low mood is pervasive and tearfulness matches it; in PBA mood may be normal and the outbursts are disconnected from it, though the two can coexist. Other affect terms an examiner may swap in include blunted or flat affect (reduced expression, as in schizophrenia), labile affect (rapid shifts that still match a felt emotion), and inappropriate or incongruent affect. Treatment options include a dextromethorphan and quinidine combination and antidepressants. Option c is right because PBA is brief, involuntary laughing or crying, disproportionate to mood, in people with neurological disease.","traps":{"a":"Mood-congruent emotional expression in major depression is the normal coupling of affect and mood: the patient feels sad and looks sad, with tearfulness that matches the inner state and persists. Mood-congruent is also used for psychotic features whose themes, such as guilt or ruin, match the depressed mood. PBA is the opposite, expression disconnected from mood. It tempts because PBA crying is often misdiagnosed as depression.","b":"Restricted or blunted affect is reduced intensity and range of emotional expression, and flat affect is its near-absence. In schizophrenia it is a negative symptom, called diminished emotional expression in DSM-5. PBA involves exaggerated, uncontrollable outbursts rather than reduced expression, and it is caused by neurological disease, not psychosis. It tempts because both are disorders of affect rather than of mood.","d":"Rapidly shifting and shallow expression of emotions is a criterion of histrionic personality disorder, a cluster B disorder with attention-seeking, self-dramatisation and theatrical display. These shifts are part of a lifelong interpersonal style and serve to draw attention, whereas PBA outbursts are involuntary, unwanted and caused by brain disease. It tempts because both involve dramatic, changeable emotional displays."}},{"q":"DSM-5 distinguishes mania from hypomania primarily by:","concept":"Emil Kraepelin (1899) separated manic-depressive insanity from dementia praecox, and DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) now defines the bipolar disorders by the type of elevated episode. A manic episode is a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive or irritable mood and abnormally and persistently increased activity or energy, lasting at least 1 week and present most of the day, nearly every day, or any duration if hospitalisation is necessary. At least three of seven symptoms are required, four if mood is only irritable: inflated self-esteem or grandiosity, decreased need for sleep, pressured speech, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, distractibility, increased goal-directed activity or psychomotor agitation, and risky activities such as spending sprees. The episode causes marked impairment, needs hospitalisation, or has psychotic features. A hypomanic episode has the same symptom list but lasts at least 4 consecutive days, is an unequivocal change in functioning observable by others, and is not severe enough to cause marked impairment or hospitalisation; if psychotic features appear, the episode is by definition manic. One manic episode is enough for bipolar I disorder; a major depressive episode is common but not required. Bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic and at least one major depressive episode, with no mania ever. DSM-5 also counts a full manic episode that emerges during antidepressant treatment and persists beyond the drug effect. Option a is right because mania is marked by impairment, hospitalisation or psychosis and a 1-week minimum, while hypomania lasts at least 4 days without them.","traps":{"b":"Hypomania by definition has no psychotic features. If delusions or hallucinations appear during an elevated episode, DSM-5 classifies the episode as manic whatever its length, which also moves the diagnosis to bipolar I. The option therefore reverses the real rule, since psychosis is a marker of mania. It tempts because examiners deliberately swap the two terms to test which one psychosis belongs to.","c":"Bipolar I disorder requires only one manic episode, and many patients have mania before, or without, any major depressive episode. It is bipolar II that requires a major depressive episode, alongside at least one hypomanic episode. The word 'always' is a warning sign in any option. It tempts because depression is common in bipolar disorder and the classic picture is a cycle between depressive and manic poles.","d":"Hypomania does require an observable change: DSM-5 states that the disturbance in mood and functioning is observable by others and is uncharacteristic of the person when well. What hypomania lacks is marked impairment, hospitalisation and psychosis, so the option reverses a real criterion. It tempts because hypomania is mild, often felt as pleasant productivity, and patients themselves may not see it as illness."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) most often involves:","concept":"Folie à deux, French for madness of two, was described by Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret (1877). A delusion passes from one person, the primary or inducer, to another, the secondary or induced person, who would probably not have developed it alone. The typical primary has a chronic psychotic disorder such as schizophrenia or delusional disorder and is the more dominant, intelligent or verbal partner. The secondary is more passive, dependent or suggestible, often a spouse, sibling, parent or child. The pair usually live in close emotional ties and social isolation, which shields the shared belief from correction; persecutory and grandiose themes are common. Classic subtypes include folie imposée, the commonest, in which the delusion is imposed on the secondary and usually fades once the pair are separated; folie simultanée, in which two predisposed people develop the same psychosis at about the same time; folie communiquée, in which the secondary first resists and then keeps the delusion even after separation; and folie induite, in which an already psychotic person adopts another patient's delusions. Larger groups are called folie à trois or folie à famille. DSM-IV called it shared psychotic disorder and ICD-10 induced delusional disorder. DSM-5 removed the separate diagnosis: the secondary may meet criteria for delusional disorder, or be coded under other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder. Management is separation of the pair and treatment of the primary. Option d is right because folie à deux typically involves a dominant patient with a primary psychosis whose delusion is taken up by a more passive, socially isolated partner.","traps":{"a":"Two people developing identical delusions at the same time corresponds most closely to folie simultanée, a rare subtype in which two predisposed people living together become psychotic together. It is not what folie à deux usually involves, and truly independent identical delusions would be a coincidence, not shared psychosis. It tempts because 'identical' matches the idea of a shared delusion, but the usual mechanism is transmission from one person to another.","b":"Dissociative symptoms are disruptions of memory, identity, perception or consciousness, such as depersonalisation or amnesia, not delusions. A mother and infant cannot share a delusion, because an infant cannot yet hold beliefs of that kind. Folie à deux concerns the transmission of a delusional belief between people who can talk and reason together. It tempts only because mother and child form the closest possible pair.","c":"A purely cultural belief cannot be a delusion. DSM-5 defines delusions as fixed beliefs not amenable to change in light of conflicting evidence, and excludes beliefs ordinarily accepted by other members of the person's culture or subculture, such as articles of religious faith. Folie à deux is a recognised psychiatric phenomenon involving a delusion the wider community does not share. It tempts because shared family beliefs can look cultural."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV (and WAIS-V), the mean and standard deviation of subtest scaled scores are:","concept":"Wechsler scales report scores on two different standard-score metrics. David Wechsler (1939) introduced the Wechsler-Bellevue scale and, with it, the deviation IQ, which replaced the ratio IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) used by Lewis Terman (1916) in the Stanford-Binet. A deviation score tells you where a person stands relative to same-age peers in standard deviation units. On the WAIS-IV (2008) each subtest raw score, such as Vocabulary, Block Design or Digit Span, is converted to a scaled score with a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, ranging from 1 to 19. Subtest scaled scores are then summed to form composite scores: the Full Scale IQ and the index scores (in WAIS-IV, Verbal Comprehension, Perceptual Reasoning, Working Memory and Processing Speed), which use a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15. Every standard score is simply the mean plus SD times z. So a person one SD above average has z = +1, a scaled score of 13, an index score of 115, a T-score of 60, and sits near the 84th percentile. Know the other metrics too: z-scores (mean 0, SD 1), T-scores (mean 50, SD 10, used on the MMPI-2 and many rating scales), stanines (mean 5, SD 2) and stens (mean 5.5, SD 2, used on the 16PF). Subtest scatter (peaks and dips between subtests) is read on this 10 and 3 metric. Option c is right because WAIS subtest scaled scores are set at a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3.","traps":{"a":"Mean 100 and SD 15 is the composite metric on Wechsler scales: the Full Scale IQ and the index scores built from summed subtest scaled scores. Most modern ability tests, including the Stanford-Binet Fifth Edition, use it for overall scores. It tempts because 'IQ 100' is the most famous number in testing, so candidates assume every Wechsler score sits on that scale, forgetting that individual subtests use a smaller metric.","b":"Mean 50 and SD 10 is the T-score, a transformation of z (T = 50 + 10z). It is the reporting metric for the MMPI-2 clinical and validity scales, where a T of 65 or more is treated as clinically significant, and for many child rating scales. It tempts because T-scores appear constantly in clinical reports and are taught in the same chapter, but Wechsler subtests do not use them.","d":"Mean 0 and SD 1 is the z-score, the basic standard score from which all others are derived: z equals the raw score minus the mean, divided by the SD. It allows negative values and decimals, which is why test publishers convert it into friendlier metrics such as scaled scores. It tempts because z-scores are the foundation of every standard-score conversion and appear in every statistics chapter."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 validity scale 'F' (Infrequency) elevated to a T-score >100 most plausibly indicates:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built empirically by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943); the restandardised MMPI-2 (James N. Butcher and colleagues, 1989) has 567 true-false items. Before reading the clinical scales, the clinician checks the validity scales, which show how the person approached the test. The F (Infrequency) scale consists of items that fewer than 10 percent of the normative sample answered in the scored direction, such as odd or extreme symptoms. A person who endorses many of them is answering unlike almost everyone else. That can happen for three reasons: random or careless responding (poor reading, confusion, non-cooperation), genuine severe psychopathology (for example acute psychosis), or deliberate over-reporting of symptoms, called faking bad or malingering. At T scores of 100 or more the profile is usually treated as of doubtful validity until these possibilities are separated. Other scales help separate them: VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) detects random responding, TRIN (True Response Inconsistency) detects indiscriminate yes-saying or no-saying, and F(p) (Infrequency Psychopathology) contains items rarely endorsed even by psychiatric inpatients, so its elevation points to over-reporting rather than real illness. F(B) does the same job as F for the back half of the booklet. Under-reporting shows a different pattern: L (Lie) catches naive claims of saintly virtue, K (Correction) catches subtler defensiveness, and S (Superlative) catches claims of perfect adjustment, usually with F low. Option b is right because a very high F reflects random responding, severe psychopathology or symptom exaggeration.","traps":{"a":"Faking good, or minimisation, means presenting oneself as better adjusted than one is. On the MMPI-2 it shows as raised L (naive denial of common faults), K (subtle defensiveness) or S (claims of superlative adjustment), usually with a low F. A very high F points the opposite way: too many unusual symptoms reported. It tempts because candidates remember that validity scales detect faking but mix up which scale detects which direction.","c":"A high F says nothing good about reliability. Reliability is a property of the test (for example its test-retest or internal consistency coefficients), while validity scales describe whether one person's protocol can be interpreted. A markedly raised F actually casts doubt on the profile, so the clinical scales may not be trusted at all. It tempts candidates who assume any high validity score means a more valid or more reliable profile.","d":"Above-average defensiveness is the territory of the K (Correction) scale, which detects subtle defensiveness and is also used to K-correct several clinical scales, and of the S (Superlative Self-Presentation) scale. Defensive test-takers deny problems, so their F is usually low, not high. It tempts because K and F are discussed together, and the F minus K index compares them to help detect faking bad."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measure, and in classical test theory it is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance. Different kinds of consistency need different coefficients. Internal consistency asks whether the items of a single test, given once, measure the same thing. Early methods split the test into two halves and corrected the correlation between them with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910). G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) gave formula KR-20 for items scored right or wrong. Lee J. Cronbach (1951) generalised this into coefficient alpha, which works for any item format, including Likert scales. Alpha rises when items correlate strongly with each other and when the test has more items: alpha equals k over (k minus 1), times one minus the sum of item variances divided by total score variance. Rough benchmarks are 0.70 acceptable for research and 0.80 or higher good, with about 0.90 preferred for individual clinical decisions; values above about 0.95 may signal redundant items. Alpha is not proof that a scale is unidimensional, and it assumes items contribute equally; McDonald's omega relaxes that assumption. Contrast the other reliability types: test-retest reliability (the coefficient of stability, same test given twice), parallel or alternate forms reliability (the coefficient of equivalence), and inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa, from Jacob Cohen, 1960, or the intraclass correlation). Option c is right because Cronbach's alpha estimates internal consistency reliability, the degree to which items within one administration hang together.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is found by giving the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores, which gives the coefficient of stability. It needs two administrations and is sensitive to the interval, practice effects and real change in the trait. Alpha needs only one administration. It tempts because both are reliability coefficients and both are reported as correlations between 0 and 1.","b":"Construct validity is the degree to which a test measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, a framework set out by Lee J. Cronbach and Paul E. Meehl (1955). It is shown through convergent and discriminant correlations, factor analysis and theory-based predictions. Alpha is a reliability index, and reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. It tempts because Cronbach's name is attached to both ideas.","d":"Inter-rater reliability is the agreement between two or more independent raters scoring the same behaviour or responses, for example two clinicians diagnosing the same patients. It is indexed by percentage agreement, Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects for chance agreement, or the intraclass correlation. Alpha concerns agreement among items, not among raters. It tempts because both ask whether different sources of measurement agree with each other."}},{"q":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards; the Exner Comprehensive System scores responses on dimensions that include all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test was published by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach (1921) in Psychodiagnostik. It has ten standard cards: five achromatic (I, IV, V, VI, VII), two black and red (II, III) and three fully coloured (VIII, IX, X). The person says what each blot might be (response phase), then an inquiry phase establishes where and why. Rival scoring systems grew up, including those of Samuel Beck and Bruno Klopfer, until John E. Exner Jr. (1974) combined their most reliable parts into the Comprehensive System. Each response is coded for location (W whole, D common detail, Dd unusual detail, S white space), developmental quality, determinants (form, human, animal and inanimate movement, chromatic and achromatic colour, shading, form dimension, pairs and reflections), form quality (how well the percept fits the blot: superior, ordinary, unusual or minus), content (human, animal, anatomy and so on), popular responses, organisational activity (Z scores) and special scores for disordered thinking. These codes feed a structural summary of ratios and indices, such as the Perceptual Thinking Index, Depression Index and Suicide Constellation. It is a personality and perceptual-cognitive measure, not an intelligence test, so it never produces an IQ. Since 2011 the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS) of Gregory J. Meyer and colleagues has been replacing the Comprehensive System. Option d is right because an explicit IQ score is not one of the dimensions on which Exner's Comprehensive System scores Rorschach responses.","traps":{"a":"Location is a genuine Comprehensive System code. It records which part of the blot the person used: W for the whole blot, D for a commonly used detail, Dd for an unusual detail, and S when white space is used. Location patterns are read as a sign of how the person approaches and organises a problem. It tempts only because the letter codes look technical and unfamiliar, so candidates may doubt they are real.","b":"Determinants are central to the Comprehensive System. They record which features of the blot produced the response: form, human or animal or inanimate movement, chromatic colour, achromatic colour, shading, form dimension, and pairs or reflections. For example, the balance between human movement and weighted colour responses (the Erlebnistypus) describes a person's coping style. It tempts because the list is so broad it sounds invented.","c":"Form quality is scored for every response and shows how well the percept fits the actual shape of the blot area used: superior, ordinary, unusual but acceptable, or minus for a distorted fit. Many minus responses suggest impaired reality testing and feed indices of disordered perception and thinking. It tempts because 'quality' sounds evaluative, like an ability score, but it is a genuine Exner code."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening instrument is best defined as:","concept":"Screening tests, such as the PHQ-9 for depression or the MMSE for cognitive impairment, are judged against a reference standard (for example a structured diagnostic interview) using a 2 by 2 table. Its cells are true positives (ill and test positive), false negatives (ill but test negative), false positives (well but test positive) and true negatives (well and test negative). Sensitivity is the proportion of people who really have the condition whom the test correctly flags: TP divided by (TP plus FN). Specificity is the proportion of people without the condition whom the test correctly clears: TN divided by (TN plus FP). Both are properties of the test at a given cut-off. Predictive values answer the clinician's question after a result: positive predictive value (PPV) is TP divided by (TP plus FP), the chance that a positive scorer really has the condition, and negative predictive value (NPV) is TN divided by (TN plus FN). Predictive values change with prevalence. Example: a test with 90 percent sensitivity and 90 percent specificity used where prevalence is 1 percent gives a PPV of only about 8 percent, because false positives from the large healthy group swamp the true cases. Lowering the cut-off raises sensitivity but lowers specificity; the receiver operating characteristic (ROC) curve plots this trade-off. The mnemonic SnNout says a negative result on a highly sensitive test rules the condition out. Screening tools favour high sensitivity. Option a is right because sensitivity is the proportion of truly positive cases that the test correctly identifies as positive.","traps":{"b":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified is specificity: TN divided by (TN plus FP), the share of people without the condition whom the test correctly clears. Specificity matters most when false positives are costly, and the mnemonic SpPin says a positive result on a highly specific test rules the condition in. It tempts because sensitivity and specificity are always taught as a pair and differ by a single word.","c":"The probability that a person who scores positive truly has the condition is the positive predictive value: TP divided by (TP plus FP). Unlike sensitivity it depends heavily on prevalence, so the same test has a low PPV in community screening and a higher PPV in a specialist clinic. It tempts because this is what patients and clinicians actually want to know after a positive result.","d":"The probability that a person who scores negative truly lacks the condition is the negative predictive value: TN divided by (TN plus FN). It is high whenever the condition is rare, even for a mediocre test, because most people are well anyway, so it is not a fixed property of the test. It tempts because a negative result 'ruling out' illness sounds like the job of sensitivity in SnNout."}},{"q":"Stanine scores have a mean and SD of:","concept":"A standard score re-expresses a raw score as distance from the mean in standard deviation units, then places it on a convenient scale. The stanine, short for 'standard nine', was developed by psychologists in the United States Army Air Forces during the Second World War so that results could be recorded as a single digit. It divides the normal curve into nine bands. Stanine 5 is the middle band, centred on the mean, and every band except the two end bands is half a standard deviation wide. So the mean is 5 and the standard deviation is 2 (more precisely about 1.96). The approximate percentages of a normal group falling in stanines 1 to 9 are 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7 and 4. Stanines are common in school achievement reports: 1 to 3 below average, 4 to 6 average, 7 to 9 above average. Do not confuse the stanine with the sten ('standard ten') used on the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond B. Cattell (1949), which runs from 1 to 10 with mean 5.5 and SD 2. The other metrics: z-score mean 0 and SD 1; T-score mean 50 and SD 10 (MMPI-2); Wechsler subtest scaled score mean 10 and SD 3; and deviation IQ mean 100 and SD 15 (David Wechsler, 1939). All convert through z: a z of +1 equals stanine 7, T 60, scaled score 13 and IQ 115. Option b is right because stanines are built with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of 2.","traps":{"a":"Mean 50 and SD 10 is the T-score, calculated as 50 plus 10 times z. It removes negative numbers and decimals and is the standard metric on the MMPI-2, where a T of 65 or more marks clinical elevation, and on many child behaviour rating scales. It covers a far wider range than nine bands. It tempts because T-scores and stanines are introduced together in standard-score chapters.","c":"Mean 10 and SD 3 is the scaled score used for individual subtests of the Wechsler scales (WAIS, WISC) and many other cognitive tests, running from 1 to 19. It is finer than the stanine and is designed for profile analysis across subtests. It tempts because both are small, one or two digit scales, and a mean of 10 with SD 3 looks close to a mean of 5 with SD 2.","d":"Mean 100 and SD 15 is the deviation IQ metric introduced by David Wechsler (1939), now used for Full Scale IQ and index scores on the Wechsler scales and for composites on most modern ability tests. It replaced the old ratio IQ of mental age over chronological age. It tempts because it is the most familiar standard score of all, so candidates choose it whenever a question mentions any standardised metric."}},{"q":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II severity cutoff conventionally used to flag 'moderate' depression is approximately:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory was first published by Aaron T. Beck and colleagues (1961). The revised BDI-II, by Aaron T. Beck, Robert A. Steer and Gregory K. Brown (1996), was rewritten to match DSM-IV criteria for major depression. It is a self-report questionnaire of 21 items, each scored 0 to 3, covering the past two weeks (the original covered the past week). Items include sadness, pessimism, loss of pleasure, guilt, self-dislike, suicidal thoughts (item 9, which must always be checked), crying, agitation, changes in sleep and appetite, and fatigue. Totals range from 0 to 63. The manual's cut-offs are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate and 29 to 63 severe. Memorise the upper limits as a sequence: 13, 19, 28, 63. A score estimates severity; it is not a diagnosis, which still needs a clinical interview. Compare the neighbouring instruments examiners use for variants. The Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (Max Hamilton, 1960) is clinician rated, not self-report, usually in a 17-item version. The PHQ-9 (Kurt Kroenke, Robert L. Spitzer and Janet B. W. Williams, 2001) has nine items scored 0 to 3, with cut-offs of 5, 10, 15 and 20 for mild, moderate, moderately severe and severe. The Beck Anxiety Inventory (1988) also has 21 items and a range of 0 to 63 but measures anxiety. The Beck Hopelessness Scale measures pessimism about the future and helps assess suicide risk. Option a is right because a BDI-II total of 20 to 28 falls in the conventional moderate band.","traps":{"b":"A BDI-II total of 14 to 19 is the mild band. It sits just above the minimal range and just below moderate, so it is the closest distractor. Its lower boundary of 14 is the point at which scores begin to suggest clinically relevant depression. It tempts because candidates who remember that 'moderate' is the second clinical band may slide one band too low.","c":"A BDI-II total of 0 to 13 is the minimal band, meaning few or no depressive symptoms of clinical concern; most non-depressed adults score here. Examiners sometimes ask for it directly as the 'normal' range. It tempts candidates who mix up the BDI-II with the PHQ-9, where moderate depression begins at 10, so a band reaching into the low teens can look like a clinical range.","d":"A total of 29 to 63 is the severe band, the top of the scale. It is the widest band because severe depression can produce scores anywhere from 29 up to the maximum of 63, which is 21 items times 3. It tempts candidates who misremember the cut-offs, or who assume any score above about 20 must already be severe, since 20 is about a third of the maximum."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT), unlike Classical Test Theory, models:","concept":"Classical test theory (CTT), rooted in the work of Charles Spearman (1904), says that an observed score equals a true score plus random error. Its statistics are test-level and sample-bound: item difficulty is the proportion of a particular sample passing an item, discrimination is an item-total correlation, and there is one standard error of measurement for everyone. Change the sample and these numbers change. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, works at the level of the single item. Its foundations were laid by Frederic M. Lord and Melvin R. Novick (1968), whose book included Allan Birnbaum's logistic models, and independently by the Danish mathematician Georg Rasch (1960). Each item has an item characteristic curve, an S-shaped function giving the probability of a correct or keyed response at each level of the latent trait, called theta. In the one-parameter or Rasch model, items differ only in difficulty (b, the theta at which the probability is 0.5). The two-parameter model adds discrimination (a, the slope of the curve). The three-parameter model adds pseudo-guessing (c, the lower asymptote), useful for multiple-choice items. Persons and items sit on the same scale, item parameters are in principle invariant across samples, and precision can vary across theta through the test information function. These properties make possible computerised adaptive testing, equating of test forms, item banking and detection of differential item functioning (item bias). Option c is right because IRT models the probability of each item response as a function of a latent trait, with item-level difficulty, discrimination and guessing parameters.","traps":{"a":"Group-level means and variances are the currency of classical test theory and ordinary descriptive statistics. CTT item statistics, such as the proportion passing an item, depend on the particular group tested. IRT goes further, modelling each person's response to each item rather than summarising only the group. The word 'only' makes this an inaccurate description of either theory. It tempts because CTT is the contrast term in the stem.","b":"Test-retest stability is a form of reliability: the correlation between scores from the same test given twice, called the coefficient of stability. It is a classical test theory index estimated from two administrations, and IRT is not designed to model it. In IRT, precision is expressed through the test information function and a standard error that varies with theta. It tempts because reliability and IRT are taught in the same psychometrics unit.","d":"Inter-rater agreement on observed behaviour is the reliability of observers or scorers, measured by percentage agreement, Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) or the intraclass correlation. Generalisability theory, from Lee J. Cronbach and colleagues, can also model rater error as one facet of measurement. IRT instead models how test-takers respond to items. It tempts because both are advanced alternatives to simple reliability coefficients."}},{"q":"The Bender-Gestalt II is primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test was published by the American child neuropsychiatrist Lauretta Bender (1938). She used nine geometric figures (Figure A and Figures 1 to 8) adapted from designs that Max Wertheimer (1923) had used to demonstrate the Gestalt laws of perceptual organisation. The person copies each design onto a blank sheet. Bender's idea was that copying requires perceiving the figure as an organised whole and reproducing it through motor output, so the task samples visual-motor integration, which matures with age and can break down with brain dysfunction. Errors examined include rotation, distortion of shape, perseveration, fragmentation, overlapping difficulty and poor integration of parts. Elizabeth M. Koppitz (1963) developed a widely used developmental scoring system for children, and Gerald Pascal and Barbara Suttell (1951) a system for adults. The Bender-Gestalt II (Gary G. Brannigan and Scott L. Decker, 2003) expanded the set to 16 designs, added a recall phase after copying, supplementary Motor and Perception tests, norms from age 4 to over 85 years, and a global scoring system. Uses include screening for developmental delay, learning disability and visuoconstructional deficits, and a quick screen for organic impairment within a neuropsychological assessment. Neighbouring tests: the Benton Visual Retention Test (Arthur Benton, 1946), which stresses visual memory; the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure, which tests planning and visual memory; and the Beery-Buktenica Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration. Option d is right because the Bender-Gestalt II primarily measures visual-motor integration and is used to screen for organic impairment and developmental status.","traps":{"a":"Premorbid intelligence is the estimated ability level before an illness or injury. It is usually estimated from reading tests of irregular words, such as the National Adult Reading Test (Hazel Nelson, 1982), because word reading is relatively preserved after brain damage, or from demographic formulas. A copying task is sensitive to current damage, so it cannot estimate the pre-injury level. It tempts because premorbid estimates are needed in the same neuropsychological work-ups.","b":"Personality structure is assessed by objective inventories such as the MMPI-2 and 16PF, or projective tests such as the Rorschach and TAT. Elizabeth M. Koppitz (1963) and others did list emotional indicators in Bender drawings, but this projective use is secondary and weakly supported, not the test's primary purpose. It tempts because the Bender is often given in the same battery as projective tests, and drawing tasks feel projective.","c":"Verbal working memory is the ability to hold and manipulate verbal information for a few seconds, the phonological loop in the model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974). It is tested with tasks such as Digit Span, Letter-Number Sequencing and Arithmetic on the WAIS-IV Working Memory Index. The Bender involves no verbal material at all. It tempts because the Bender-Gestalt II recall phase involves memory, but that memory is visual, not verbal."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as which type of test?","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test was introduced by Christiana D. Morgan and Henry A. Murray (1935) at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. The standard set has 31 cards: 30 pictures of ambiguous scenes and one blank card, on which the person imagines a picture. Some cards are meant for particular age and sex groups, and a clinician usually selects about 10 to 20. The person tells a story for each card: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters think and feel, and how it ends. 'Apperception' means interpreting a new perception in the light of past experience. Murray's theory, in Explorations in Personality (1938), held that behaviour comes from internal needs (such as achievement, affiliation and dominance) interacting with environmental presses. The storyteller is assumed to identify with a hero, so needs, presses and recurring themes reveal the teller's own concerns. This makes the TAT a projective test, based on the projective hypothesis named by Lawrence K. Frank (1939): unstructured stimuli let people project their inner world. Gardner Lindzey (1959) grouped projective techniques as association (Rorschach), construction (TAT), completion (sentence completion), choice or ordering, and expression (drawings). Objective tests, by contrast, have structured items, fixed responses and standard scoring. David C. McClelland and colleagues (1953) used TAT-type stories to score need for achievement. For children, Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak (1949) produced the Children's Apperception Test with animal figures. Option a is right because the TAT uses ambiguous pictures and storytelling to reveal personality, which makes it a projective personality test.","traps":{"b":"An objective personality test has structured items, fixed response options such as true-false or Likert ratings, and standard scoring that does not depend on the examiner's interpretation. Examples are the MMPI-2, the 16PF of Raymond B. Cattell (1949) and the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire. The TAT asks for free stories that must be interpreted. It tempts because the TAT does measure personality, and formal TAT scoring systems exist, so it can sound partly objective.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability through problems with right and wrong answers, scored against age norms to give an IQ, as in the Wechsler scales or Raven's Progressive Matrices (John C. Raven, 1938). TAT stories have no correct answer. It tempts because story length, vocabulary and organisation do reflect verbal ability, and because the TAT and intelligence tests appear side by side in the same assessment syllabus.","d":"A neuropsychological battery is a set of tests measuring brain-behaviour relationships across domains such as attention, memory, language, motor and executive functions, for example the Halstead-Reitan or Luria-Nebraska batteries. It is used to detect and localise brain dysfunction. The TAT is a single projective test of needs and motives. It tempts because projective tests were once used, unreliably, to infer 'organicity', so the categories seem to overlap."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is best demonstrated when:","concept":"Validity is the degree to which evidence supports the interpretations made from test scores. Textbooks name three types. Content validity asks whether the items adequately sample the domain, judged by experts. Criterion-related validity asks whether test scores relate to an external criterion, an independent measure of what the test should reflect or predict. Construct validity, set out by Lee J. Cronbach and Paul E. Meehl (1955), asks whether the test behaves as the theory of the construct says it should. Criterion validity comes in two forms that differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the test and the criterion are measured at about the same time: for example, a new brief depression scale is given in the same session as the BDI-II or a structured diagnostic interview, and the two correlate highly. It is useful when a quicker or cheaper test is meant to replace an established one, or to identify a present state. In predictive validity the criterion is measured later, as when an entrance test is correlated with first-year marks. Within construct validity, Donald T. Campbell and Donald W. Fiske (1959) introduced the multitrait-multimethod matrix with two key ideas: convergent validity, high correlations with other measures of the same construct, and discriminant validity, low correlations with measures of different constructs. Reliability limits validity: a validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the test's reliability. Option d is right because concurrent validity is shown when a new test correlates highly with an established measure of the same construct taken at the same time.","traps":{"a":"A high correlation with a different construct measured at the same time is evidence against validity. The discriminant validity of Donald T. Campbell and Donald W. Fiske (1959) requires low correlations with unrelated constructs; if a new anxiety scale correlates very highly with an intelligence test, it may not be measuring anxiety at all. It tempts because it copies the 'same time' feature of concurrent validity and changes only one word, 'different'.","b":"Predicting future performance is predictive validity, the other half of criterion-related validity. The criterion is collected later, as when entrance examination scores are correlated with later course grades, or a risk scale is checked against later relapse. It is the more demanding design because one must wait for the outcome. It tempts because predictive and concurrent validity are always taught as a pair and differ only in timing.","c":"Stable internal consistency over time mixes two reliability ideas: internal consistency (items hanging together, indexed by the coefficient alpha of Lee J. Cronbach, 1951) and stability over time (test-retest reliability). Either way it describes reliability, the consistency of scores, not validity, which concerns what the scores mean. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. It tempts because the reliability and validity chapters sit side by side."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery (HRNB) is the classic fixed battery of neuropsychology. Ward C. Halstead developed the core tests at the University of Chicago to measure what he called biological intelligence (Brain and Intelligence, 1947), and his student Ralph M. Reitan refined the battery and validated it against patients with confirmed brain lesions. Its main tests are the Category Test (abstraction and concept formation), the Tactual Performance Test (a blindfolded form board done with each hand and then both, followed by drawing the board from memory), the Seashore Rhythm Test (auditory attention), the Speech-Sounds Perception Test, the Finger Tapping Test (motor speed), and the Trail Making Test, parts A and B (visual scanning, speed and set shifting). Additional measures include the Reitan-Indiana Aphasia Screening Test, a sensory-perceptual examination, grip strength and lateral dominance testing, with a Wechsler intelligence scale and the MMPI usually given alongside. Several scores combine into the Halstead Impairment Index. A fixed battery gives every patient the same tests; it is contrasted with the flexible or process approach and with the Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery of Charles J. Golden and colleagues (1980), based on the methods of Alexander Luria. Indian clinicians also use local batteries such as the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction and the NIMHANS Neuropsychological Battery. The Rorschach Inkblot Test of Hermann Rorschach (1921) is a projective personality test and is not part of the HRNB. Option d is right because the Rorschach is the only listed test that does not belong to the Halstead-Reitan battery.","traps":{"a":"The Category Test is a core HRNB measure. The person views sets of geometric figures, must work out the principle linking them, and gets feedback after each answer. It measures abstraction, concept formation and learning from feedback, and it is sensitive to brain damage of many kinds. It tempts because its name is generic and sounds as if it could come from any intelligence test, but it genuinely belongs to the battery.","b":"The Tactual Performance Test is a central HRNB test. Blindfolded, the person places blocks into a form board with the dominant hand, the non-dominant hand and then both, and afterwards draws the board from memory. It yields time, memory and localisation scores and taps tactile perception, motor skill and incidental spatial memory. It tempts because many candidates know it as a Seguin form board task rather than as a Halstead-Reitan test.","c":"The Trail Making Test is part of the HRNB. Part A requires joining numbered circles in order; Part B requires alternating between numbers and letters (1, A, 2, B and so on). Part A reflects visual scanning and processing speed, while Part B adds set shifting, an executive function. It tempts because it is now so widely used on its own that candidates forget it belongs to the Halstead-Reitan battery."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily measures:","concept":"Adaptive behaviour means the everyday conceptual, social and practical skills people need to live independently and meet the expectations for their age and culture. The first standard measure was the Vineland Social Maturity Scale of Edgar A. Doll (1935), developed at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey. Sara S. Sparrow, David A. Balla and Domenic V. Cicchetti (1984) revised it as the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales; later editions are the Vineland-II (2005) and Vineland-3 (2016). It is completed through a semi-structured interview or rating form with a parent, caregiver or teacher, so it measures what the person typically does, not what they can do on a test. Core domains are Communication (receptive, expressive, written), Daily Living Skills (personal, domestic, community) and Socialization (interpersonal relationships, play and leisure, coping), with a Motor Skills domain for young children and an optional Maladaptive Behavior section. Domain scores and the Adaptive Behavior Composite use a mean of 100 and SD of 15. Adaptive measures are essential for diagnosing intellectual disability: DSM-5 (2013) requires deficits in intellectual functioning, deficits in adaptive functioning, and onset in the developmental period, and grades severity by adaptive functioning rather than IQ alone. In India the Vineland Social Maturity Scale adapted by A. J. Malin is widely used to calculate a social age and social quotient, much as the old ratio IQ was computed. Option a is right because the Vineland measures adaptive functioning across communication, daily living, socialisation and motor skills.","traps":{"b":"Intelligence quotient is measured by standardised ability tests such as the Wechsler scales (David Wechsler, 1939) or the Stanford-Binet, where the person solves problems under test conditions. The Vineland deliberately measures something different: what the person actually does in daily life, as reported by an informant. The two correlate only moderately, which is why an intellectual disability diagnosis needs both. It tempts because the Vineland is always given alongside an IQ test.","c":"Psychopathology in adolescents is assessed with instruments such as the MMPI-A, the Youth Self-Report and Child Behavior Checklist of Thomas M. Achenbach, or structured interviews like the K-SADS. The Vineland's optional Maladaptive Behavior section touches on problem behaviours, but its core purpose is adaptive skills, not psychiatric diagnosis. It tempts because the Vineland is used with children and adolescents and includes that maladaptive section.","d":"Premorbid personality is the person's characteristic personality before the onset of an illness, assessed mainly through a careful history from the patient and informants. It helps in judging prognosis, for example in schizophrenia. The Vineland measures current adaptive skills, not enduring traits from before an illness. It tempts because both rely on informant interviews and both describe how the person functions in everyday life."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability of 0.80 and a standard deviation of 15, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is approximately:","concept":"In classical test theory every observed score (X) equals a true score (T) plus random error (E), an idea going back to Charles Spearman (1904). The reliability coefficient is the share of observed-score variance that is true-score variance, so 1 minus reliability is the share due to error. The standard error of measurement (SEM) converts this into score units: it is the standard deviation of the scores a person would obtain over many retestings around their true score. The formula is SEM = SD multiplied by the square root of (1 minus r), where r is the reliability coefficient. Here SD = 15 and r = 0.80, so 1 minus r = 0.20, the square root of 0.20 is about 0.447, and 15 times 0.447 is about 6.7. The SEM is used to build confidence bands around an observed score: about 68 percent confidence for plus or minus 1 SEM, and 95 percent for plus or minus 1.96 SEM. With this test, an observed IQ of 110 has a 95 percent band of roughly 97 to 123. Higher reliability gives a smaller SEM; at r = 1 the SEM is 0, and at r = 0 it equals the SD. Do not confuse it with two neighbours. The standard error of estimate describes error in predicting a criterion from a validity coefficient. The standard error of the difference, used to compare two scores, is larger than either SEM. Option a is right because 15 times the square root of 0.20 gives an SEM of about 6.7.","traps":{"b":"An SEM of 1.5 is far too small for these numbers. Working backwards, 15 times the square root of (1 minus r) equals 1.5 only if that square root is 0.1, which needs a reliability of 0.99. It may come from dividing the SD by 10 or misplacing a decimal. It tempts because a small error band feels right for a test described as fairly reliable, but 0.80 still leaves substantial error.","c":"An SEM of 12.0 comes from multiplying the SD by the reliability itself (15 times 0.80), confusing reliability with the error part of the formula. Using the square root of 0.80 instead gives about 13.4, also wrong. The formula needs 1 minus r, the error proportion, inside the square root. It tempts because it uses both numbers from the stem in one simple step, which feels sensible under time pressure.","d":"An SEM of 15.0 is simply the standard deviation. The SEM equals the SD only when reliability is zero, meaning the test is pure error and the observed spread contains no true-score information. With reliability of 0.80 the SEM must be smaller than the SD. It tempts candidates who confuse the standard deviation of the score distribution with the standard error of one individual's score."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale (WMS-IV) yields five Index scores. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"The Wechsler Memory Scale was first published by David Wechsler (1945). The fourth edition, WMS-IV (2009), was co-normed with the WAIS-IV so that memory and intelligence scores can be compared directly. Its Adult battery (ages 16 to 69) yields five index scores, each with a mean of 100 and SD of 15. Auditory Memory comes from Logical Memory (recall of short stories) and Verbal Paired Associates. Visual Memory comes from Designs and Visual Reproduction. Visual Working Memory comes from Spatial Addition and Symbol Span. Immediate Memory and Delayed Memory regroup the auditory and visual subtests by timing: recall straight after presentation versus recall after a 20 to 30 minute delay. So the indexes cover modality (auditory versus visual), working memory, and time (immediate versus delayed). The shorter Older Adult battery (ages 65 to 90) yields four indexes, without Visual Working Memory. An optional Brief Cognitive Status Exam screens for gross impairment. Do not confuse the WMS-IV Visual Working Memory Index with the WAIS-IV Working Memory Index, which is auditory-verbal (Digit Span and Arithmetic). Indian clinicians often use the PGI Memory Scale as a local alternative. Mechanical Reasoning belongs to aptitude testing, chiefly the Differential Aptitude Tests (DAT) of George K. Bennett, Harold G. Seashore and Alexander G. Wesman (1947), which also include Verbal Reasoning, Numerical Ability, Abstract Reasoning, Space Relations and Clerical Speed and Accuracy. Aptitude tests predict success in training or jobs, while memory scales assess clinical memory function. Option b is right because Mechanical Reasoning is an aptitude subtest, not one of the five WMS-IV memory indexes.","traps":{"a":"Auditory Memory is a genuine WMS-IV index. It is built from Logical Memory, where the examinee recalls short stories read aloud, and Verbal Paired Associates, where word pairs are learned over trials, each with immediate and delayed recall. Low scores suggest problems in verbal episodic memory. It tempts in an EXCEPT question only if a candidate forgets that the modality-based indexes are central to the scale.","c":"Visual Working Memory is a real WMS-IV index, built from Spatial Addition (holding and combining dot locations across grids) and Symbol Span (remembering the order of abstract symbols). It appears only in the Adult battery. It tempts because candidates who know the WAIS-IV Working Memory Index, which is auditory-verbal, may doubt that a separate visual working memory index exists in the memory scale.","d":"Visual Memory is a genuine WMS-IV index, built from Designs (recalling the content and location of designs in a grid) and Visual Reproduction (drawing geometric figures from memory), each with immediate and delayed trials. It is the visual counterpart of the Auditory Memory Index. It tempts only because Visual Memory and Visual Working Memory sound like duplicates, so a candidate may suspect one of them is the fake option."}},{"q":"In CBT, the term 'Socratic questioning' refers to:","concept":"Socratic questioning takes its name from the Greek philosopher Socrates, who taught by asking a chain of questions that led students to discover the contradictions in their own views. Aaron T. Beck built it into cognitive therapy (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, by Beck, A. John Rush, Brian F. Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979) as part of collaborative empiricism: therapist and client work as a team of scientists who treat the client's thoughts as hypotheses to be tested, not as facts. Instead of telling the client a thought is wrong, the therapist asks open-ended questions. What is the evidence for this thought? What is the evidence against it? Is there another way to see this? What would you tell a friend in the same situation? What is the worst, best and most realistic outcome? The client gathers the evidence and draws the new conclusion, so the change is owned and more durable. Christine A. Padesky (1993) argued that it should guide discovery rather than change minds, and described four stages: informational questions, empathic listening, summaries, and synthesising questions that ask the client to apply the new information to the original belief. Related CBT tools are the thought record, the downward arrow technique for uncovering core beliefs, and behavioural experiments. Contrast it with the more forceful disputation of the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955), with psychoanalytic free association, and with hypnotic suggestion. Option a is right because Socratic questioning uses open-ended questions that guide clients to examine and reformulate their own thoughts.","traps":{"b":"Direct confrontation of beliefs means the therapist argues that the client's belief is wrong. The rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955) uses vigorous, sometimes forceful disputation of irrational beliefs, which is more direct than Beck's Socratic style. In cognitive therapy, telling clients they are wrong tends to raise resistance. It tempts because Socratic questioning does challenge thoughts, so 'challenging' slides easily into 'confronting'.","c":"Free association is the basic rule of psychoanalysis, introduced by Sigmund Freud in the 1890s: the patient says whatever comes to mind without censoring it, so that unconscious material emerges for the analyst to interpret. It is unstructured and aimed at the past, while Socratic questioning is structured and aimed at present thoughts. It tempts because both are talk-based techniques that help clients discover something about themselves.","d":"Hypnotic suggestion means planting new ideas or beliefs while the person is in a hypnotic, highly suggestible state. Sigmund Freud used hypnosis in his early work before replacing it with free association, and it survives in hypnotherapy. It bypasses the client's critical reasoning, whereas Socratic questioning depends on the client actively weighing evidence. It tempts because both aim to replace unhelpful beliefs with new ones."}},{"q":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) is the first-line behavioural intervention for:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) involves obsessions, which are intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety, and compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared outcome. DSM-5 (2013) moved OCD out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter of obsessive-compulsive and related disorders. The behavioural account uses the two-factor theory of O. Hobart Mowrer (1947): anxiety becomes attached to triggers through classical conditioning, and compulsions and avoidance are maintained by negative reinforcement because they bring quick relief. The relief also prevents the person from learning that the feared outcome would not happen. Exposure and Response Prevention breaks this loop. It was first described by Victor Meyer (1966) with inpatients in Britain and later developed by Edna B. Foa, Michael J. Kozak and colleagues. Client and therapist build a graded hierarchy of feared situations rated in subjective units of distress (SUDS); the client then faces each one, in vivo or in imagination, while refraining from rituals, including mental rituals and reassurance seeking. Anxiety rises and then falls without the compulsion (habituation), and newer inhibitory learning accounts stress that the person learns the feared outcome does not occur. CBT that includes ERP is the first-line psychological treatment for OCD; selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) are the first-line medication, with clomipramine an older alternative. Variants may ask about flooding (exposure at full intensity) or systematic desensitisation, which pairs a hierarchy with relaxation. Option a is right because ERP is the first-line behavioural intervention for obsessive-compulsive disorder.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated first with antipsychotic medication, supported by psychosocial interventions such as family psychoeducation, social skills training, vocational rehabilitation and cognitive behavioural therapy for psychosis. Delusions and hallucinations are not maintained by an anxiety and ritual loop, so ERP does not target them. It tempts because some patients with schizophrenia also have obsessive-compulsive symptoms, and both conditions can involve strange, intrusive-seeming thoughts.","c":"Bipolar I disorder is defined by at least one manic episode. It is treated mainly with mood stabilisers such as lithium and valproate, or antipsychotics, plus psychoeducation, family-focused therapy and interpersonal and social rhythm therapy to stabilise daily routines. Exposure plays no role in mania or bipolar depression. It tempts because candidates often pick any major disorder when unsure, and bipolar disorder is a common distractor.","d":"Antisocial personality disorder is a pervasive pattern of disregard for and violation of the rights of others, diagnosed only from age 18 with evidence of conduct disorder before 15. It has no established first-line treatment; approaches focus on managing aggression, substance use and offending. There is no fear and compulsion cycle for ERP to break. It tempts mainly as a filler, and because some personality disorders do have specialised therapies such as DBT."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT), developed by Marsha Linehan, blends CBT with:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy was developed by the American psychologist Marsha M. Linehan for chronically suicidal women who met criteria for borderline personality disorder (BPD); her first controlled trial appeared in 1991 and her treatment manuals in 1993. Standard behaviour therapy, which pushes for change, made these clients feel invalidated, while pure acceptance did not reduce self-harm. Linehan's answer was a dialectic, a synthesis of opposites drawn from dialectical philosophy: the therapist balances acceptance of the client as they are with pressure to change. Acceptance strategies came largely from Zen practice, which Linehan translated into secular mindfulness skills. Her biosocial theory explains BPD as emotional vulnerability (a biological tendency to strong, slow-to-settle emotions) interacting with an invalidating environment. Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, group skills training, telephone coaching between sessions and a therapist consultation team. The four skills modules are core mindfulness (including 'wise mind'), distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness; the first two are acceptance skills and the last two are change skills. DBT is a first-line psychological treatment for BPD and recurrent self-harm. It belongs to the 'third wave' of behaviour therapy along with acceptance and commitment therapy (Steven C. Hayes and colleagues, 1999) and mindfulness-based cognitive therapy (Zindel V. Segal, J. Mark G. Williams and John D. Teasdale, 2002). Mentalization-based treatment and schema therapy also treat BPD but come from psychodynamic and integrative roots. Option a is right because DBT blends CBT with mindfulness and acceptance practices drawn from Zen and dialectical philosophy.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalytic free association, introduced by Sigmund Freud in the 1890s, asks the patient to say whatever comes to mind so that unconscious conflicts can be interpreted. DBT is structured, present-focused and skills-based, with diary cards and chain analysis of problem behaviours. For BPD, the psychodynamic alternatives are mentalization-based treatment and transference-focused psychotherapy. It tempts because those alternatives exist, so candidates link BPD treatment with psychodynamic methods.","c":"Pharmacological augmentation means adding a second medicine to boost the first, for example adding lithium or an antipsychotic to an antidepressant. DBT is a psychological treatment, and medication is not one of its defining components, though clients may receive medication separately. No medicine is licensed specifically for BPD. It tempts because 'augmentation' sounds like adding something to CBT, which mirrors the structure of the stem.","d":"Bowen family systems theory, developed by Murray Bowen (1978), explains problems through multigenerational family patterns, differentiation of self, emotional triangles and the family projection process. Some adolescent versions of DBT add family skills work, but its theoretical blend is CBT plus acceptance, not Bowen theory. It tempts because DBT's biosocial theory stresses an invalidating family environment, which sounds like a family-systems idea."}},{"q":"Rogers' three core conditions for client-centred therapy are:","concept":"Carl R. Rogers developed a non-directive approach (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942), renamed client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951) and later the person-centred approach. His theory says a person grows towards self-actualisation unless conditions of worth, learned when love is given only for approved behaviour, create incongruence between the self-concept and actual experience. In a 1957 paper Rogers listed six necessary and sufficient conditions for therapeutic personality change: two people in psychological contact; a client who is incongruent, vulnerable or anxious; a therapist who is congruent in the relationship; a therapist who experiences unconditional positive regard; a therapist who experiences empathic understanding of the client's internal frame of reference; and the client perceiving the empathy and regard at least to a minimal degree. The three therapist-provided core conditions are what exams test. Congruence, or genuineness, means the therapist is real and transparent, not hiding behind a professional facade. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the client as a person without conditions, which counteracts conditions of worth. Accurate empathy means sensing the client's private world as if it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and communicating that understanding, often through reflection of feelings. Charles B. Truax and Robert R. Carkhuff (1967) turned these into rating scales. The conditions strongly influenced motivational interviewing and research on the therapeutic alliance. Contrast the techniques of psychoanalysis (free association, dream analysis, transference interpretation) and of cognitive behaviour therapy (disputation, homework, restructuring). Option b is right because Rogers' three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, empathy and congruence.","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, interpretation and transference belong to psychodynamic therapy. Ralph R. Greenson (1967) described the analyst's sequence as confrontation, clarification, interpretation and working through, and transference, the redirection of feelings about early figures onto the therapist, is the main material interpreted. These are expert-led interventions, the opposite of Rogers' non-directive stance. It tempts because 'confrontation' can sound like the honesty of congruence.","c":"Rational disputation, behavioural homework and cognitive restructuring are techniques of the cognitive behavioural therapies, especially the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955) and the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck. They are active, directive methods for changing beliefs and behaviour. Rogers saw techniques as secondary to the relationship. It tempts because modern CBT also values warmth and empathy, so the two approaches seem to overlap.","d":"Free association, dream analysis and transference are the classic tools of psychoanalysis developed by Sigmund Freud, who called dreams the royal road to the unconscious in The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). They aim to make unconscious conflict conscious through the analyst's interpretation. Rogers explicitly rejected the therapist as expert interpreter. It tempts because it is another famous triad, and candidates may simply recognise the words."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, the term 'working through' refers to:","concept":"Sigmund Freud introduced 'working through' in his paper 'Remembering, Repeating and Working-Through' (1914). He had found that a correct interpretation, even one the patient accepts intellectually, rarely produces change on its own, because resistance keeps returning. Patients tend to repeat old conflicts in action, especially in the transference, rather than remember them, a tendency he linked to the compulsion to repeat. Working through is the slow, repeated process in which the same insight is met again and again in new forms: in different memories, current relationships, dreams and the transference to the analyst, while the resistances against it are analysed each time. Each repetition deepens insight from intellectual understanding to emotional conviction and allows lasting change in character and behaviour. Ralph R. Greenson (1967) placed it as the final step in analysing resistance: confrontation (showing the patient something), clarification (bringing it into sharp focus), interpretation (making unconscious meaning conscious) and working through (repeated, progressive elaboration until change is stable). Working through is a major reason classical psychoanalysis takes years. Related terms examiners use: insight (intellectual versus emotional), abreaction or catharsis (emotional discharge, which Freud found did not cure by itself), and the phases of therapy, which run from assessment and engagement through a long middle phase, where most working through happens, to termination. Option a is right because working through is the repeated application of insights across situations until characterological change occurs.","traps":{"b":"Telling the client the correct interpretation once is the very approach Sigmund Freud (1914) found did not work. An interpretation given once may be accepted intellectually yet leave behaviour unchanged, because resistance and repetition continue. The lesson behind working through is that interpretation must be repeated and elaborated in many contexts. It tempts candidates who equate insight with cure, as in the early cathartic idea that remembering alone heals.","c":"A brief intake assessment is the first contact, in which the clinician collects the presenting complaint, history and mental status, and decides suitability for therapy. In psychoanalytic work there may also be trial sessions to judge suitability for analysis. Working through happens much later, in the long middle phase once the transference has developed. It tempts only as a filler, perhaps because 'working' suggests the initial work-up of a case.","d":"Termination is the final phase of therapy, in which ending is planned, the relationship is reviewed and feelings about separation are explored. Termination should come after enough working through has made change stable, so the two are related in time but are not the same thing. It tempts because 'working through' can sound like getting through to the end of therapy."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views of:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, studied the dreams and thoughts of depressed patients and found persistent themes of loss, defeat and inadequacy rather than the hidden anger turned inward that analytic theory predicted. In Depression (1967) he described the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless, defective, unlovable'), of the world, meaning ongoing experience and other people ('Everything is against me; the demands are impossible'), and of the future ('Things will never get better'). The third part is hopelessness, which links the triad to suicide risk. Beck's cognitive model has three levels: core beliefs or schemas formed in early experience, intermediate beliefs (rules and assumptions such as 'If I fail, I am a failure'), and automatic thoughts that pop up in specific situations. Stress activates latent schemas, and systematic cognitive errors keep the negative view going: arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. Cognitive therapy (Beck, A. John Rush, Brian F. Shaw and Gary Emery, 1979) tests these thoughts through Socratic questioning, thought records and behavioural experiments, and uses activity scheduling. Examiners build variants from neighbouring theories: learned helplessness of Martin E. P. Seligman (1975), and the reformulated attributional model of Lyn Y. Abramson, Seligman and John D. Teasdale (1978), in which internal, stable and global attributions for bad events predict depression. Do not confuse the triad with Beck's three levels of cognition. Option b is right because Beck's cognitive triad is a negative view of the self, the world and the future.","traps":{"a":"Past, present and future is a time-based triad that sounds natural but is not Beck's. Beck's triad combines a person (self), a context (the world or ongoing experience) and a time (the future). Rumination about past failures does feature in depression, but the past is not a corner of the triad. It tempts because 'future' is correct, so candidates fill the other two slots with the other tenses.","c":"Mother, father and self suggests the Oedipal triangle of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, or object-relations views that root depression in early relationships and loss. Beck, though analytically trained, moved away from such explanations toward current conscious thinking. It tempts because Freud's 'Mourning and Melancholia' (1917) linked depression to the loss of a loved object, so family figures seem relevant to depression.","d":"Body, mind and spirit is a holistic wellness phrase used in yoga, Ayurveda and integrative health writing. It refers to dimensions of the whole person, not to the content of depressive thinking, and has no role in Beck's cognitive model. It tempts only as a familiar three-part phrase, especially for students who meet holistic models in Indian contexts and guess any well-known triad."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) is grounded in which spirit-of-MI principles?","concept":"Motivational Interviewing (MI) was first described by William R. Miller (1983) for problem drinkers and developed with Stephen Rollnick in their book of 1991. It is a collaborative, person-centred way of talking with people to strengthen their own motivation and commitment to change. It grew out of dissatisfaction with confrontational addiction counselling, which tended to increase resistance. MI separates its 'spirit', the underlying attitude, from its techniques. In the 2002 second edition the spirit was collaboration, evocation and autonomy. In the third edition (2013) it became four elements, remembered as PACE: Partnership (working with the person as the expert on their own life), Acceptance (respecting absolute worth, accurate empathy, autonomy and affirmation), Compassion (actively promoting the person's welfare) and Evocation (drawing out the person's own reasons for change rather than installing them). The third edition also set out four processes: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. Core skills are OARS: open questions, affirmations, reflective listening and summaries. The clinician listens for and strengthens change talk (desire, ability, reasons and need, then commitment, activation and taking steps, abbreviated DARN-CAT) and responds to sustain talk and discord without arguing. Ambivalence is treated as normal. MI is often paired with the stages of change model of James O. Prochaska and Carlo C. DiClemente (1983), especially for people in precontemplation or contemplation, and is widely used for substance use and health behaviour change. Option c is right because partnership, acceptance, compassion and evocation are the elements of the spirit of MI.","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, persuasion, expert authority and compliance describe the traditional directive style of addiction counselling that MI was created to replace. Miller and Rollnick observed that arguing for change makes clients argue against it, increasing resistance. MI calls the urge to fix and persuade the 'righting reflex' and teaches clinicians to resist it. It tempts because these are what many people imagine motivating someone involves.","b":"Free association, transference and abstinence are psychoanalytic concepts. Free association and transference come from Sigmund Freud's technique, and the rule of abstinence means the analyst does not gratify the patient's wishes so that they can be analysed. In addiction work 'abstinence' means stopping substance use, a goal MI may serve but not a principle of its spirit. It tempts through that double meaning of 'abstinence'.","d":"Behavioural shaping (reinforcing successive approximations, from the operant work of B. F. Skinner, 1938) and contingency management (giving vouchers or prizes for verified abstinence) are reinforcement-based techniques. Contingency management works well in substance use and is sometimes combined with MI, but it changes behaviour through external rewards, while MI evokes internal motivation. It tempts because both are used in the same addiction services."}},{"q":"Family therapy's 'enmeshment' (Minuchin) refers to:","concept":"Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin and set out in Families and Family Therapy (1974). He saw a family as a system organised into subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling) separated by boundaries, the invisible rules that define who takes part in what and how. Boundaries lie on a continuum. At one end are diffuse boundaries, producing enmeshment: members are over-involved in each other's lives, privacy is low, a parent may answer for a child, distress in one member spreads instantly to all, and individual autonomy and differentiation are sacrificed for closeness and loyalty. At the other end are rigid boundaries, producing disengagement: members are emotionally distant, independent to the point of isolation, and fail to respond when support is needed. Healthy families have clear boundaries that are firm yet permeable, with a parental hierarchy. Salvador Minuchin, Bernice L. Rosman and Lester Baker (1978) described anorexic 'psychosomatic families' marked by enmeshment, overprotection, rigidity and lack of conflict resolution. Therapy involves joining the family, mapping its structure, enactments and boundary making. Neighbouring concepts: the emotional fusion and low differentiation of self described by Murray Bowen (1978), close to enmeshment; triangulation; the identified patient or scapegoat; and expressed emotion, whose emotional over-involvement component predicts relapse in schizophrenia (George W. Brown, James L. T. Birley and John K. Wing, 1972). Option a is right because enmeshment means diffuse boundaries with over-involvement and loss of individual autonomy.","traps":{"b":"Rigid boundaries with little emotional contact describe disengagement, the opposite pole of Minuchin's boundary continuum. Members of a disengaged family function independently, show little loyalty or concern, and may not notice or respond when one of them is in trouble, so children may lack protection and guidance. It tempts because enmeshment and disengagement are always taught as a pair, and the stem asks for one end of the continuum.","c":"Healthy reciprocal closeness reflects clear boundaries in Minuchin's model: members are close and supportive but respect each other's privacy and autonomy, and parents keep an appropriate hierarchy. Enmeshment is a pathological excess of closeness at the cost of autonomy, not healthy intimacy. It tempts because an enmeshed family can look warm and loving to an outsider, and members often describe themselves as very close.","d":"Scapegoating means the family locates its problems in one member, often a child, who becomes the identified patient and carries the blame, sparing the family from facing conflict elsewhere, such as in the marriage. It is linked with detouring and triangulation, not with boundary diffusion as such. It tempts because scapegoating is another well-known pathological family pattern and can co-exist with enmeshment in the same family."}},{"q":"Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus in group therapy refers to:","concept":"The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy by Irvin D. Yalom (first edition 1970) is the standard text on interpersonal group therapy. It rests on the interpersonal theory of Harry Stack Sullivan (1953): psychological problems are largely problems in relationships. Yalom argued that a freely interacting group becomes a social microcosm: sooner or later each member behaves in the group as they do in outside life, so the same patterns of dominating, withdrawing, pleasing or provoking appear in the room. The here-and-now focus uses this. Attention goes to what is happening between members, and between members and therapist, in the session itself, rather than to there-and-then material such as past history or events outside the group. It has two tiers. First, experiencing: members interact and express immediate reactions to each other. Second, process illumination: the group steps back and examines what just happened and what it says about each member's relationships. Experience without reflection is only emotional release, and reflection without experience is an intellectual exercise; the loop of both produces change. The here-and-now powers interpersonal learning, one of Yalom's eleven therapeutic factors, along with instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, group cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors. Option c is right because the here-and-now means working with interpersonal patterns as they appear in the session itself and then illuminating them.","traps":{"a":"Discussing only events outside the group, or past events, is 'there-and-then' work, the opposite of Yalom's here-and-now. Outside events can be raised but are hard to verify and invite storytelling, advice-giving and intellectualising. Yalom urges therapists to bring such material back into the room by asking how the pattern shows up among members. It tempts because many people imagine therapy groups as places to report on the week.","b":"Strictly limiting the group to didactic content describes a psychoeducational group, such as a class on medication or relapse prevention, where the leader teaches. Imparting information is one of Yalom's eleven therapeutic factors, but in interpersonal groups it is minor, and the here-and-now is experiential, not instructional. It tempts because psychoeducational groups are common in hospital and de-addiction settings and are also called group therapy.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is the D step in the ABCDE model of the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955), where the therapist actively challenges demands such as 'I must be approved of by everyone'. It can be run in groups, but it is didactic and cognitive, not interpersonal process work. It tempts because REBT and CBT groups are common and also focus on the present."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe) is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist, developed systematic desensitisation and described it in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He borrowed the term reciprocal inhibition from the physiologist Charles Sherrington, who used it for the way activation of one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe's principle: if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of an anxiety-provoking stimulus, the bond between the stimulus and anxiety is weakened. A person cannot be fully relaxed and fully anxious at the same time. This is counterconditioning, foreshadowed by Mary Cover Jones (1924), who reduced the boy Peter's fear of a rabbit by bringing the rabbit gradually closer while he ate. Wolpe first worked with cats made fearful in an experimental cage, then fed in settings increasingly similar to it. The human procedure has three steps: training in progressive muscle relaxation, based on Edmund Jacobson (1929); building an anxiety hierarchy from least to most frightening situations, rated in subjective units of disturbance (SUDS); and, while relaxed, imagining each item from the bottom up, moving on only when it no longer causes anxiety. Later research showed that exposure works even without relaxation, so modern explanations stress extinction, habituation and inhibitory learning. Neighbours: flooding (prolonged exposure at full intensity), implosive therapy, aversion therapy, and the two-factor theory of avoidance. Option b is right because systematic desensitisation rests on reciprocal inhibition, the incompatibility of fear and relaxation.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction, from the work of B. F. Skinner (1938), is the weakening of a behaviour when the reinforcement that maintained it is withheld, often after a brief extinction burst, as when a parent stops responding to tantrums. Systematic desensitisation is based on classical conditioning: it replaces a conditioned fear response with relaxation. It tempts because modern accounts say exposure works partly through extinction, though that is respondent, not operant, extinction.","c":"Cognitive disputation is the active challenging of irrational beliefs in the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis (1955), and in a gentler form the Socratic questioning of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy. It changes the thoughts that produce emotion. Wolpe's method works through conditioning without arguing with beliefs at all. It tempts because desensitisation and disputation are both used for anxiety and both appear in CBT packages.","d":"The two-factor theory of O. Hobart Mowrer (1947) says fear is acquired through classical conditioning and avoidance is maintained by operant negative reinforcement, because escaping reduces fear. It explains why phobias and OCD persist and why exposure must block avoidance, but it explains the problem, not the principle of Wolpe's treatment. It tempts because it is the best-known learning theory of fear and avoidance."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) targets which six core processes (the 'hexaflex')?","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT, said as the word 'act') was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their first full manual appeared in 1999. It belongs to the 'third wave' of behaviour therapy, alongside Dialectical Behaviour Therapy and Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy. Its theoretical base is Relational Frame Theory (Steven Hayes, Dermot Barnes-Holmes and Bryan Roche, 2001), a behavioural account of language which argues that words can make private events such as thoughts and memories feel as threatening as real events. ACT therefore does not try to change the content of thoughts, as Beckian cognitive restructuring does; it changes the person's relationship to them. The goal is psychological flexibility, drawn as a hexagon, the 'hexaflex', with six processes: cognitive defusion (seeing a thought as just a thought, for example 'I am having the thought that I am a failure'), acceptance (willingly making room for unpleasant feelings instead of avoiding them), contact with the present moment, self-as-context (the 'observing self' that notices experience without being defined by it), values (freely chosen life directions), and committed action (behaviour guided by those values). Each process has an opposite in the 'inflexahex' of psychological inflexibility: cognitive fusion, experiential avoidance, dominance of the conceptualised past and feared future, attachment to the conceptualised self, lack of values clarity, and inaction or impulsivity. A common memory aid groups the six into three pairs: open up (acceptance, defusion), be present (present moment, self-as-context) and do what matters (values, committed action). Option b is right because it lists exactly these six hexaflex processes.","traps":{"a":"These are the standard techniques of traditional behaviour therapy and cognitive behaviour therapy: Beckian cognitive restructuring, exposure (rooted in Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation, 1958), relaxation training, homework, role-play and contingency management from operant conditioning. ACT does use behavioural work, but it explicitly avoids disputing or restructuring thought content, which is the opposite of defusion. It tempts because ACT is a behaviour therapy and these methods fill every CBT chapter.","c":"Free association, dream analysis, transference, working through, interpretation and the rule of abstinence are the classical techniques of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis, aimed at making unconscious conflict conscious. ACT is a behavioural, present-focused therapy with no theory of repressed drives, so none of these is a hexaflex process. It tempts only because the option also offers six neatly listed items that look like a complete model.","d":"This list mixes the four skills modules of Marsha Linehan's Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (skills manual 1993), namely mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, with problem-solving and behavioural activation from CBT for depression. It is the strongest distractor because DBT is also a third-wave therapy and ACT also uses mindfulness, but in ACT mindfulness is split into acceptance, defusion, present moment and self-as-context, not taught as a skills module."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression typically organises treatment around which problem areas?","concept":"Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman, with Bruce Rounsaville and Eve Chevron, and set out in their 1984 manual Interpersonal Psychotherapy of Depression. It grew from the interpersonal school of Adolf Meyer and Harry Stack Sullivan and from John Bowlby's attachment theory. IPT does not claim that relationships cause depression; it holds that depression always occurs in an interpersonal context, and that improving current relationships relieves symptoms. It is brief (usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions), present-focused and run in three phases. In the initial phase the therapist diagnoses depression as a medical illness, gives the patient the 'sick role', reviews current relationships in an 'interpersonal inventory', and agrees on one or two focal problem areas. The four problem areas are grief (complicated bereavement after a death), interpersonal role disputes (non-reciprocal expectations with a spouse, parent or colleague), role transitions (a life change such as marriage, job loss, migration or retirement) and interpersonal deficits (social isolation or a lack of lasting relationships). The middle phase works on the chosen area using communication analysis, role-play and encouragement of affect, and the final phase consolidates gains and prepares for ending. IPT was one of the treatments tested in the NIMH Treatment of Depression Collaborative Research Program (Irene Elkin and colleagues, 1989) and has adaptations for adolescents (IPT-A) and perinatal depression. Unlike cognitive therapy, it targets relationships rather than thoughts; unlike psychodynamic therapy, it does not interpret transference. Option d is right because grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits are the four standard IPT problem areas.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions and dysfunctional schemas are the targets of cognitive therapy, developed by Aaron T. Beck (Cognitive Therapy of Depression, 1979), which identifies automatic thoughts, tests them against evidence and modifies underlying core beliefs. IPT notices negative thinking but does not work on it directly; it works on the relationships linked to the mood. It tempts because cognitive therapy and IPT are the two best-known brief, evidence-based therapies for depression and are often compared.","b":"Trauma reprocessing through bilateral stimulation describes Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR), developed by Francine Shapiro (first study 1989), in which the client holds a traumatic memory in mind while following side-to-side eye movements or taps. It is a treatment for post-traumatic stress disorder, not a depression model, and it has no interpersonal problem areas. It tempts because it is another named, structured, evidence-based therapy that a candidate may half-remember.","c":"Free association and transference interpretation are the core methods of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis and later psychodynamic therapy: the patient says whatever comes to mind, and the analyst interprets feelings the patient transfers onto the therapist from early relationships. IPT stays with current relationships outside therapy and deliberately does not interpret transference. It tempts because both approaches talk about relationships, and IPT's roots lie in Harry Stack Sullivan's interpersonal psychiatry, which grew out of psychoanalysis."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most closely associated with which therapy?","concept":"The empty chair technique is the signature method of Gestalt therapy, created by Frederick (Fritz) Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; the founding text, Gestalt Therapy, was written by Perls, Ralph Hefferline and Goodman in 1951. Perls borrowed the idea of acting out conversations from Jacob L. Moreno's psychodrama. In the technique the client imagines a significant person, often someone dead or absent, sitting in an empty chair and speaks to them directly in the present tense. In the related two-chair version, the client moves between chairs to voice two conflicting parts of the self, such as Perls's 'topdog' (the demanding, critical voice) and 'underdog' (the excuse-making, resisting voice). The aim is to finish 'unfinished business', meaning unexpressed feelings such as resentment or grief that keep intruding on the present, and to integrate disowned parts of the personality. Gestalt therapy is an experiential, humanistic-existential approach: it stresses awareness in the here and now, asks 'what' and 'how' rather than 'why', and emphasises personal responsibility and contact. Its contact disturbances include introjection, projection, retroflection, confluence and deflection. Do not confuse it with Gestalt psychology, the German school of perception founded by Max Wertheimer (1912). Leslie Greenberg later built empty-chair and two-chair work into Emotion-Focused Therapy, so a variant may name him. Other signatures to keep apart: disputing irrational beliefs (Albert Ellis), the core conditions (Carl Rogers), the genogram (Murray Bowen) and the miracle question (Steve de Shazer). Option a is right because the empty chair is the classic technique of Fritz Perls's Gestalt therapy.","traps":{"b":"Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy was developed by Albert Ellis (as rational therapy, 1955). Its method is the ABC model: an Activating event triggers irrational Beliefs (musts, awfulising, low frustration tolerance) that produce emotional and behavioural Consequences, which the therapist then Disputes. It is a directive, teaching-style cognitive therapy, not an enactment of dialogues. It tempts because REBT is another famous mid-century therapy in the same chapter, and its homework can include role-play.","c":"Person-centred therapy, developed by Carl Rogers (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951), is non-directive. The therapist offers three core conditions, unconditional positive regard, accurate empathy and congruence (genuineness), and mainly reflects feelings so that the client's own actualising tendency can work. Rogers avoided therapist-directed techniques such as chair work. It tempts because Gestalt and person-centred therapy are both humanistic, here-and-now approaches and are usually taught together.","d":"Bowen family systems theory, developed by Murray Bowen, views the family as an emotional unit. Its key concepts are differentiation of self, triangles, the family projection process and multigenerational transmission, and its typical tool is the genogram, a three-generation family map. Bowen therapists stay calm and coach clients rather than stage dramatic enactments. It tempts because some family therapies, especially Salvador Minuchin's structural therapy (1974), do use enactment, so 'family systems' can sound like a home for chair work."}},{"q":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by the American psychologist Francine Shapiro. She reported noticing in 1987 that her own disturbing thoughts faded while her eyes moved rapidly from side to side, and she published the first controlled study with trauma survivors in 1989. The method was first called EMD and later renamed EMDR to stress reprocessing. Its theory is the Adaptive Information Processing model: a traumatic memory is stored in an unprocessed, 'frozen' form, and bilateral stimulation helps link it to adaptive memory networks. Treatment follows eight phases: history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitisation, installation, body scan, closure and re-evaluation. In assessment the client picks a target image, a negative belief (such as 'I am helpless') and a preferred positive belief, then rates distress on the Subjective Units of Disturbance scale (0 to 10, from Joseph Wolpe) and belief strength on the Validity of Cognition scale (1 to 7). During desensitisation the client holds the memory in mind while tracking the therapist's fingers, or receiving taps or tones, in short sets. EMDR is recommended for post-traumatic stress disorder by bodies such as the World Health Organization, alongside trauma-focused CBT. Its mechanism is debated: one view is that eye movements tax working memory and make the memory less vivid, while dismantling studies question whether they add anything beyond exposure. Other trauma names to keep apart: Edna Foa (prolonged exposure), Patricia Resick (cognitive processing therapy), Peter Levine (somatic experiencing), Pat Ogden (sensorimotor psychotherapy) and Judith Herman (Trauma and Recovery, 1992). Option d is right because Francine Shapiro created EMDR.","traps":{"a":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure therapy for post-traumatic stress disorder, based on the emotional processing theory she set out with Michael Kozak (1986). Patients repeatedly retell the trauma memory aloud (imaginal exposure) and approach avoided but safe situations (in vivo exposure). She is also a leading figure in exposure and response prevention for OCD. She tempts because Prolonged Exposure and EMDR are both first-line, memory-focused PTSD treatments and are often compared in trials.","b":"Bessel van der Kolk is a Dutch-born American psychiatrist and trauma researcher, best known for The Body Keeps the Score (2014), which argues that trauma is held in the body and nervous system. He has studied and promoted EMDR, yoga and body-based approaches, which is why his name gets linked to it, but he did not create EMDR. He tempts as probably the most famous popular name in trauma treatment.","c":"Pat Ogden founded Sensorimotor Psychotherapy, a body-oriented trauma therapy that tracks posture, movement and physical sensation and works within the 'window of tolerance' described by Daniel Siegel. It uses the body as the entry point for processing trauma, through mindful attention to bodily responses rather than eye movements. She tempts because sensorimotor work also combines bodily processes with attention during trauma processing, so it can feel like a close cousin of EMDR."}},{"q":"A Type II error occurs when:","concept":"Type I and Type II errors come from the hypothesis-testing framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933), which built on the significance testing of Ronald A. Fisher. A test makes a decision about the null hypothesis (H0, usually 'no effect' or 'no difference'), and reality either matches H0 or does not, giving four possible outcomes. Rejecting a true H0 is a Type I error, a false positive; its probability is alpha, the significance level, usually set at 0.05. Failing to reject a false H0 is a Type II error, a false negative, where a real effect is missed; its probability is beta. The two correct outcomes are retaining a true H0 (probability 1 minus alpha) and rejecting a false H0, which is statistical power (1 minus beta). Jacob Cohen (1988) proposed 0.80 as the conventional minimum power, which implies a beta of 0.20. A courtroom analogy helps: if H0 is 'the accused is innocent', convicting an innocent person is a Type I error and acquitting a guilty one is a Type II error. Beta falls when sample size rises, when the true effect is larger, when variability is smaller, when alpha is relaxed (0.05 rather than 0.01) and when a justified one-tailed test is used. Lowering alpha to avoid false positives therefore raises the risk of a Type II error, so the two must be traded off. Small, underpowered studies are especially prone to Type II errors. Option c is right because a Type II error is the failure to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false.","traps":{"a":"Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true is the Type I error, a false positive, whose probability equals alpha, the significance level (commonly 0.05). It is the mirror image of the Type II error, and the two are the most commonly swapped pair in research methods papers. It tempts because both definitions use the same words, 'null', 'rejected' and 'true', and differ only in which way the mismatch runs.","b":"A wide confidence interval signals low precision: the estimate is uncertain, usually because the sample is small or the data are highly variable. Wide intervals often go with low power and therefore with Type II errors, but width is a feature of estimation, not the definition of any decision error. It tempts because it describes a weak study, and weak studies are exactly where Type II errors happen.","d":"A very large sample does not create a Type II error; it reduces the risk of one, because larger samples increase power. Very large samples instead make tiny, trivial effects statistically significant, which is why effect sizes must be reported alongside p values. It tempts because candidates remember that sample size is linked to error rates and power, but forget the direction of the link."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous outcome with normal distribution?","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher and popularised in his book Statistical Methods for Research Workers (1925); the F statistic is named in his honour. A one-way ANOVA tests whether the means of three or more independent groups differ on one continuous dependent variable, with one independent variable (factor), such as therapy type. Its logic is to split total variability into between-groups variance (how far group means lie from the grand mean) and within-groups or error variance (how far individuals lie from their own group mean). F is the ratio of the between-groups mean square to the within-groups mean square; if H0 (all population means equal) is true, F is close to 1, and a large F gives a small p value. The assumptions are independent observations, roughly normal distributions within groups and homogeneity of variance, checked with Levene's test. Why not run several t-tests? Three pairwise t-tests at 0.05 inflate the family-wise error rate to about 0.14. A significant F says only that at least one mean differs, so post hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD, Scheffe or Bonferroni-corrected comparisons locate the difference. Know the neighbours: two groups need the independent t-test; repeated measures need repeated-measures ANOVA; two factors need two-way (factorial) ANOVA with an interaction term; a covariate needs ANCOVA; several dependent variables need MANOVA. Non-parametric equivalents: Kruskal-Wallis (independent groups) and Friedman (related groups). Option c is right because one-way ANOVA is the parametric test for three or more independent group means on a normal continuous outcome.","traps":{"a":"A paired (dependent-samples) t-test, built on William Sealy Gosset's 'Student' t distribution (1908), compares two related means: the same people measured twice, as in a pre-test and post-test, or matched pairs. It handles only two conditions, and they are related rather than independent. It tempts because it is a test of means for continuous, normal data, so it matches half of the stem; the 'three or more' and 'independent' parts rule it out.","b":"The chi-square test, introduced by Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed and expected frequencies for categorical variables, for example whether diagnosis (yes or no) is associated with gender. It works on counts, not on means of a continuous outcome, so it cannot compare average scores across groups. It tempts because it can handle three or more groups at once, which matches the 'three or more' part of the stem.","d":"The Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947) is the non-parametric alternative to the independent t-test. It ranks the scores of two independent groups and is used when normality cannot be assumed. The stem rules it out twice: there are three or more groups, and the data are normal, so a parametric test is preferred. Its multi-group non-parametric relative, the Kruskal-Wallis test, would not fit either."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 means:","concept":"The odds ratio (OR) is the main measure of association in case-control studies and in logistic regression. Odds are not the same as probability: odds equal the probability that an event happens divided by the probability that it does not, so a probability of 0.20 gives odds of 0.25 (20 to 80). The OR divides the odds of the outcome in an exposed group by the odds in an unexposed group. From a two-by-two table with cells a, b, c and d, OR equals ad divided by bc, the cross-product ratio. Interpretation hinges on 1, the null value. An OR of 1 means the odds are identical in both groups, so exposure and outcome are not associated. An OR above 1 means the exposure is linked to higher odds (a risk factor); an OR of 3 means three times the odds. An OR below 1 means lower odds, a protective association; an OR of 0.5 halves the odds. The OR runs from 0 to infinity, unlike a correlation coefficient, which runs from minus 1 to plus 1. Significance is judged from the 95% confidence interval: if it includes 1, the association is not statistically significant. The relative risk (RR), from cohort studies and trials, divides probabilities rather than odds; the OR approximates the RR only when the outcome is rare (the rare disease assumption) and otherwise exaggerates it. Option c is right because an odds ratio of exactly 1.0 means equal odds in both groups, that is, no association.","traps":{"a":"A strong positive association would show up as an odds ratio well above 1, for example 3 or 5, meaning the exposed group has several times the odds of the outcome. An OR of 1 is the exact point of no difference, so it cannot signal any association, weak or strong. It tempts because 1.0 looks like a 'full' or maximal value, especially to someone thinking of probabilities or correlations, where 1 is the ceiling.","b":"A strong protective effect would appear as an odds ratio well below 1 and approaching 0, for example 0.2, meaning the exposed group has much lower odds of the outcome, as with a vaccine or a protective factor such as social support. An OR of 1 sits exactly between harmful and protective, so it shows neither. It tempts because the null value of 1 and the protective range below 1 are learned together and easily blurred.","d":"Perfect correlation is described by a correlation coefficient such as Karl Pearson's r reaching plus or minus 1, meaning all points fall on a straight line. The correlation coefficient and the odds ratio are different statistics with different scales: r runs from minus 1 to plus 1 with 0 meaning no relationship, whereas the OR runs from 0 to infinity with 1 meaning no association. It tempts because 1 means 'perfect' for r."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of approximately 0.5 indicates:","concept":"Cohen's d is a standardised effect size proposed by Jacob Cohen in Statistical Power Analysis for the Behavioral Sciences (second edition 1988). It expresses the difference between two group means in standard deviation units: d equals mean 1 minus mean 2, divided by the pooled standard deviation. Being unit-free, d allows comparison across studies using different scales, which makes it a building block of meta-analysis. Cohen offered rough benchmarks for the behavioural sciences: d of about 0.2 is small, about 0.5 is medium and about 0.8 is large. He described a medium effect as one large enough to be visible to the naked eye. A d of 0.5 means the average person in one group scores half a standard deviation above the average person in the other, placing that person at roughly the 69th percentile of the comparison group. Effect size is separate from significance: p depends heavily on sample size, while d tells you how big the effect is. Related indices have their own benchmarks: correlation r of 0.10, 0.30 and 0.50 for small, medium and large; eta squared of 0.01, 0.06 and 0.14; and f squared of 0.02, 0.15 and 0.35. Hedges' g (Larry Hedges, 1981) corrects d for bias in small samples, and Glass's delta, named after Gene V. Glass, uses only the control group's standard deviation. Cohen warned that these labels are fallback conventions. Option a is right because a d of about 0.5 is Cohen's benchmark for a medium effect.","traps":{"b":"A small effect in Jacob Cohen's (1988) scheme is d of about 0.2, a real but subtle difference, which Cohen compared to the height difference between 15- and 16-year-old girls. A d of 0.5 is two and a half times that size. It tempts because 0.5 sounds like a modest number, a mere half, and because many psychological interventions do produce small effects, so candidates expect 'small'.","c":"A large effect in Cohen's conventions is d of about 0.8 or more, meaning the group means differ by eight-tenths of a standard deviation or more, so the two distributions overlap much less. A d of 0.5 falls clearly below that cut-off. It tempts because the benchmarks for d (0.2, 0.5, 0.8) are often mixed up with those for the correlation r (0.10, 0.30, 0.50), where 0.5 does mark a large effect.","d":"No effect corresponds to d of 0, meaning the two group means are identical. A d of 0.5 is a half standard deviation difference, which is meaningful. Whether a result is statistically significant is a separate question, decided by the p value and sample size; a non-significant d of 0.5 from a tiny study is still a medium-sized estimate. It tempts candidates who confuse effect size with significance."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling is preferred over simple random sampling when:","concept":"Simple random sampling (SRS) gives every member of the population, and every possible sample of a given size, an equal chance of selection, for example by drawing names from a complete list. It is unbiased, but by chance it can under-represent small subgroups. Stratified random sampling fixes this. The researcher first divides the population into strata: subgroups that are mutually exclusive, together exhaustive, and defined by a variable linked to the outcome, such as gender, urban or rural residence, or socioeconomic class. A separate simple random sample is then drawn from each stratum. In proportionate stratification each stratum appears in the sample in its population proportion; in disproportionate stratification a small group, such as a tribal minority, is deliberately oversampled so it can be analysed on its own, and results are later weighted. Jerzy Neyman (1934) showed how to allocate sample size across strata to maximise precision. When strata differ on the outcome, stratification reduces sampling error compared with SRS of the same size. It needs a sampling frame that records each person's stratum. Distinguish it from cluster sampling, where natural groups such as schools or villages are randomly selected and everyone in them is studied (cheaper but less precise); from systematic sampling (every kth name on a list); from multistage sampling (a combination of methods); and from quota sampling, the non-probability cousin of stratified sampling, in which subgroup quotas are filled by convenience rather than at random. Option c is right because stratified sampling is chosen precisely to guarantee that important subgroups are represented in the sample.","traps":{"a":"When a population is homogeneous on the variables that matter, any random sample will resemble it anyway, so dividing it into strata gains little and simple random sampling is enough. Stratification pays off when the population is heterogeneous and subgroups differ on the outcome. It tempts because good strata are themselves internally homogeneous, so the word appears in every textbook description of stratified sampling, but it describes the strata, not the population.","b":"Stratification can give slightly better precision for the same sample size, but that is a side benefit, not its purpose, and it adds cost because a detailed frame with stratum information is needed. If minimising sample size or cost were the only goal, a researcher would more likely choose cluster or convenience sampling. It tempts because it echoes the efficiency benefit, but the extreme phrase 'at all costs' is itself a warning sign.","d":"Without a sampling frame (a list of the population), no probability method that selects individuals can be used directly. Researchers then turn to cluster or multistage sampling, which needs only a list of groups, or to non-probability methods such as snowball sampling for hidden populations like drug users. Stratified sampling actually needs more information, a frame showing each member's stratum. It tempts because stratified sampling sounds like a workaround for difficult populations."}},{"q":"A scale with high internal consistency but low criterion validity:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measure; validity is whether it measures what it claims to measure. Internal consistency is one type of reliability: it asks whether the items of a scale hang together and measure the same thing. It is estimated with Cronbach's alpha (Lee J. Cronbach, 1951), with the Kuder-Richardson formula 20 for yes-or-no items (G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson, 1937), or with split-half reliability corrected by the Spearman-Brown formula; alpha of about 0.70 or more is usually treated as acceptable. Other reliability types are test-retest, parallel forms and inter-rater reliability. Criterion validity is the correlation between test scores and an external criterion: concurrent if the criterion is measured at the same time (a new depression scale against a psychiatrist's diagnosis), predictive if it is measured later (an entrance test against later grades). Other types are content, face and construct validity, the last set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) and tested through convergent and discriminant validity in the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959). The key logic: reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity. A bathroom scale that always reads 5 kg too heavy is perfectly consistent but wrong. Classical test theory shows that a validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the test's reliability, so an unreliable test cannot be highly valid, while a reliable test can still be invalid. Option a is right because high internal consistency shows the scale is reliable, while low criterion validity shows it is not measuring the intended construct.","traps":{"b":"A scale is both reliable and valid when its scores are consistent and also correlate well with the criterion or otherwise reflect the intended construct. That is the goal of test construction, but the stem explicitly says criterion validity is low, so the scale fails the validity half. It tempts because high internal consistency sounds like the mark of a good test, and candidates may assume a good test must also be valid.","c":"Valid but not reliable is the impossible combination. Random error lowers every correlation a test can achieve, so a validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability; a test that gives inconsistent scores cannot correlate strongly with a criterion. Also, the stem states that reliability, in the form of internal consistency, is high. It tempts because it is the mirror image of the correct answer, and candidates who swap the two terms pick it.","d":"Neither reliable nor valid would describe a scale whose items do not hang together (low alpha) and do not predict the criterion. The stem rules this out because high internal consistency is itself evidence of reliability. It tempts because low validity can feel like a verdict that the whole scale is useless, but reliability and validity are judged separately, and here only validity fails."}},{"q":"Intent-to-treat analysis means that:","concept":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis is the standard primary analysis for randomised controlled trials. Its rule is 'once randomised, always analysed': every participant is analysed in the group to which they were randomly assigned, whatever happened afterwards, including dropping out, poor adherence, receiving the wrong treatment or crossing over to the other arm. The reason is that randomisation is what makes groups comparable on known and unknown confounders. People who drop out are rarely a random subset; in a depression trial, those who stop an antidepressant because of side effects or lack of benefit differ from those who stay. Removing them breaks the balance created by randomisation and introduces attrition bias, usually making the treatment look better than it is. ITT therefore gives a conservative estimate of effectiveness, meaning what happens when a treatment is offered in real practice, where some patients will not take it. The alternatives are per-protocol analysis, which includes only participants who completed treatment as planned and estimates efficacy under ideal adherence, and as-treated analysis, which groups people by the treatment they actually received; both can be secondary analyses. A modified ITT excludes some randomised participants by a pre-stated rule, such as never receiving a single dose. Full ITT needs outcome data on everyone, so missing data are handled by multiple imputation, mixed models or, in older trials, last observation carried forward. The CONSORT reporting statement asks whether analysis was by original assigned groups. Option c is right because ITT analyses participants in their randomised groups regardless of completion or crossover, preserving the benefits of randomisation.","traps":{"a":"Analysing only participants who completed the intervention as planned is per-protocol analysis. It answers whether the treatment works under ideal adherence (efficacy), but it discards dropouts, who usually differ systematically from completers, so it breaks the balance created by randomisation and risks attrition bias. It tempts because analysing only the people who actually received the full treatment sounds like a fairer test of that treatment.","b":"Excluding all non-completers 'to reduce noise' is the reasoning behind a completers-only analysis, again a per-protocol approach. Non-completion is not random noise: patients who drop out because of side effects or no improvement carry important information, and removing them usually inflates the apparent treatment effect. It tempts because it sounds like statistical cleaning, like removing outliers, but here it introduces bias rather than reducing error.","d":"Limiting analyses to per-protocol completers is per-protocol analysis under another wording. It can be reported as a secondary or sensitivity analysis, and in non-inferiority trials it is examined alongside ITT, but it is not intention-to-treat. It tempts because 'protocol' sounds rigorous and rule-bound, and because two options here describe the same idea, a candidate may decide that the repeated idea must be the correct one."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero indicates that:","concept":"A confidence interval (CI), introduced by Jerzy Neyman (1937), gives a range of plausible values for a population parameter, here the true difference between two means. The 95% refers to the method: if the study were repeated many times, about 95% of the intervals built this way would contain the true value. It does not mean a 95% probability that this one interval contains it. A 95% CI is the estimate plus or minus about 1.96 standard errors, and it maps exactly onto a two-sided hypothesis test at alpha 0.05. If the interval for a mean difference contains 0, the value stated by the null hypothesis (no difference) is still plausible, so H0 cannot be rejected and p is greater than 0.05. If the interval excludes 0, p is less than 0.05. For ratio measures such as the odds ratio or relative risk, the null value is 1, not 0, so the question becomes whether the interval includes 1. Width shows precision: larger samples give narrower intervals, and a 99% interval is wider than a 95% one. A non-significant result is not proof of no effect; as Douglas Altman and Martin Bland (1995) put it, absence of evidence is not evidence of absence. A wide interval including zero may reflect low power, while a narrow interval tightly around zero supports a negligible effect. Hence the APA Publication Manual encourages reporting confidence intervals, not p values alone. Option b is right because a 95% CI that includes zero means the difference is not statistically significant at alpha 0.05, two-sided.","traps":{"a":"An interval that includes zero does not show the difference is exactly zero. The point estimate in the middle of the interval may well be non-zero, for example 3 points, with the interval running from minus 1 to 7; zero is simply one of many plausible values. Failing to reject the null is not the same as proving it. It tempts because 'includes zero' is easily misread as 'equals zero'.","c":"Low power can produce a wide interval that crosses zero, but a single interval including zero does not by itself prove the study was underpowered; the true effect may genuinely be zero or tiny. Power is a property of the design, judged before the study from sample size, alpha and the expected effect size. It tempts because underpowered studies often report non-significant results, so candidates link the two automatically.","d":"A larger sample shrinks the standard error and narrows the interval, making it less likely to include zero, so a large sample cannot explain an interval that spans zero. Very large samples have the opposite problem, making trivial differences statistically significant. It tempts because sample size is clearly relevant to confidence intervals, and candidates who remember the link but not its direction may choose it."}},{"q":"A non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test is:","concept":"Parametric tests such as the t-test assume interval or ratio data, an approximately normal distribution and, for independent groups, roughly equal variances. When these assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small samples or heavily skewed scores, researchers use non-parametric (distribution-free) tests, which usually work on ranks rather than raw scores. The non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test is the Mann-Whitney U test (Henry Mann and Donald Whitney, 1947), essentially equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). All scores from both groups are ranked together, and the test asks whether the ranks of one group are systematically higher than the other's. Strictly it compares whole distributions, and it is often reported as a comparison of medians when the two distributions have a similar shape. Learn the full mapping, since examiners swap the pairs: independent t-test with Mann-Whitney U; paired t-test with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (or the simpler sign test); one-way ANOVA with the Kruskal-Wallis test (William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis, 1952); repeated-measures ANOVA with the Friedman test (Milton Friedman, 1937); Pearson's r with Spearman's rho (Charles Spearman, 1904) or Kendall's tau. For categorical data, the chi-square test handles independent groups, and the McNemar test (Quinn McNemar, 1947) handles paired yes-or-no data, such as the same patients before and after treatment. Non-parametric tests are robust but usually have somewhat less power than parametric tests when the parametric assumptions actually hold. Option b is right because the Mann-Whitney U test compares two independent groups using ranks, the direct non-parametric counterpart of the independent-samples t-test.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test, from Frank Wilcoxon (1945), is the non-parametric counterpart of the paired t-test. It ranks the differences between two related measurements, such as pre-test and post-test scores of the same people, and attaches each difference's sign. It tempts strongly because Wilcoxon also devised the rank-sum test, which is equivalent to Mann-Whitney U, so the name 'Wilcoxon' appears on both sides of the correct answer.","c":"The Friedman test, introduced by the economist Milton Friedman (1937), is the non-parametric counterpart of repeated-measures ANOVA. It compares three or more related conditions, such as the same patients rated at baseline, one month and three months, by ranking scores within each person. It fails the stem twice: the groups are independent, and there are only two of them. It tempts as another well-known non-parametric name.","d":"The McNemar test (Quinn McNemar, 1947) analyses paired dichotomous data, for example whether the same patients were depressed (yes or no) before and after treatment, focusing on those who changed category. It deals with related, categorical outcomes, not two independent groups measured on a continuous or ordinal scale. It tempts because it is a non-parametric test listed in the same table, and its paired design is easily forgotten."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) was introduced by Andreas Laupacis, David Sackett and Robin Roberts (1988) as a clinically meaningful way to express a treatment effect. It is the number of patients who must receive the treatment, rather than the control, for one additional patient to benefit (or to avoid one additional bad outcome) over a stated period. It is calculated as 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction (ARR), where ARR is the control event rate (CER) minus the experimental event rate (EER). Worked example: if 20% of patients relapse on placebo and 15% on the new drug, ARR is 0.20 minus 0.15, which is 0.05, so NNT is 1 divided by 0.05, which is 20. Twenty patients must be treated to prevent one relapse. The relative risk (RR) here is 0.15 divided by 0.20, which is 0.75, and the relative risk reduction (RRR) is 1 minus RR, or ARR divided by CER, which is 25%. RRR sounds more impressive than ARR and stays the same whether baseline risk is high or low, which is why drug advertisements prefer it; ARR and NNT show the absolute benefit, which depends on baseline risk. NNT is always rounded up to the next whole number. The ideal NNT is 1, and lower is better. The harm counterpart is Number Needed to Harm (NNH), 1 divided by the absolute risk increase for an adverse effect, where higher is better. Always quote NNT with its outcome and time frame. Option b is right because NNT equals 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction.","traps":{"a":"Relative risk reduction times 100 simply expresses the RRR as a percentage, for example 25%. RRR equals the absolute risk reduction divided by the control event rate, so it shows the proportional benefit, not how many patients must be treated. It ignores baseline risk: a 25% RRR could mean an NNT of 20 or of 2,000. It tempts because RRR is the most commonly quoted trial result, and 'risk reduction' sounds right.","c":"One divided by the relative risk has no standard clinical meaning as a treatment-efficiency count. It simply inverts the ratio, turning a risk ratio of 0.75 into 1.33, which is still a relative, unit-free measure. NNT must come from an absolute difference in event rates. It tempts because it copies the '1 divided by' form of the correct formula, and only the word 'relative' instead of 'absolute' gives it away.","d":"The odds ratio is a measure of association, mainly from case-control studies and logistic regression, comparing the odds of an outcome in two groups. Multiplying it by 100 gives no meaningful quantity, certainly not a number of patients. NNT can be estimated from an odds ratio only with a separate formula that also needs the control event rate. It tempts because the odds ratio is another familiar epidemiological statistic."}},{"q":"The 'Bonferroni correction' is most appropriate when:","concept":"Every significance test run at alpha 0.05 has a 5% chance of a false positive when the null is true. Run many tests and these chances accumulate: the family-wise error rate (FWER), the probability of at least one Type I error across a set of tests, is about 1 minus 0.95 to the power k for k independent tests. With 10 tests it is roughly 0.40. The Bonferroni correction controls the FWER by dividing alpha by the number of comparisons: with 10 comparisons, each is tested at 0.05 divided by 10, which is 0.005 (equivalently, each p value is multiplied by 10). It rests on the Bonferroni inequality, named after the Italian mathematician Carlo Emilio Bonferroni, and was popularised for comparing means by Olive Jean Dunn (1961), hence the name Bonferroni-Dunn. It is simple and valid whether or not the tests are independent, but conservative: as the number of tests grows the threshold becomes very strict, power falls and Type II errors rise. Less conservative alternatives are the Holm step-down procedure (Sture Holm, 1979), which also controls FWER, and the Benjamini-Hochberg procedure (Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, 1995), which controls the false discovery rate, the expected proportion of false positives among significant results. After a significant ANOVA, Tukey's HSD is the usual choice for all pairwise comparisons and Scheffe's test for complex contrasts. A single planned comparison needs no correction. Option d is right because Bonferroni is used when many pairwise comparisons are made and the family-wise Type I error must be controlled.","traps":{"a":"A single planned (a priori) comparison carries only the ordinary 5% risk of a false positive, so there is no multiplicity to correct. Bonferroni exists precisely because error accumulates across many tests; with one test, dividing alpha by 1 changes nothing. It tempts because planned comparisons are taught in the same ANOVA chapter as post hoc corrections, and candidates link the two topics without recalling why corrections are needed.","b":"Reporting effect sizes, such as Cohen's d or eta squared, tells readers how large an effect is, which complements p values but has nothing to do with controlling Type I error across many tests. Bonferroni changes the significance threshold, not the size of the effect. It tempts because effect sizes and multiple-comparison corrections are both modern reporting recommendations, often listed together in guidelines on good statistical practice.","c":"Whether a test is parametric or non-parametric does not decide whether a correction is needed; the number of tests does. Bonferroni is in fact often applied to non-parametric follow-ups, such as pairwise Mann-Whitney tests after a significant Kruskal-Wallis test, but only because several comparisons are being made. It tempts because candidates have seen Bonferroni used alongside non-parametric tests and mistake the setting for the reason."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion: 'Neither the manager nor the staff <u>were</u> aware of the new policy.'","concept":"This item tests subject-verb agreement with correlative conjunctions. When two subjects are joined by 'neither ... nor', 'either ... or' or 'not only ... but also', the verb agrees with the subject closer to it. This is the rule of proximity. So 'Neither the students nor the teacher was present' takes 'was' (teacher is nearer), while 'Neither the teacher nor the students were present' takes 'were'. With 'and' the subject is compound and always plural. Phrases such as 'as well as', 'along with' and 'together with' work differently again: the verb agrees with the first subject. Here the nearer subject is 'staff', a collective noun. A collective noun (staff, team, committee, jury, family, audience) takes a singular verb when the group acts as one unit and a plural verb when its members act as individuals: 'the jury has reached a verdict' but 'the jury were arguing among themselves'. American English almost always treats such nouns as singular; British English often allows the plural ('the staff are'). The key here treats 'staff' as a single body of employees, so the nearer subject is singular and the verb must be singular. The original tense is past ('were'), and an error-correction item should fix only the agreement error, so the tense stays past. In your own writing, put a clearly plural subject last ('nor the staff members were'). Option d is right because, with 'neither ... nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject 'staff', treated as a singular collective noun, which gives 'was' in the past tense.","traps":{"a":"'Were' is the original word, so choosing it means saying the sentence needs no change. It would be acceptable if 'staff' were read as plural, which British usage often allows ('the staff are'), and many speakers would say it naturally. But the item is built on the proximity rule with 'staff' as a singular unit, so the key rejects 'were'. It tempts because two parties are named and feel like a plural subject.","b":"'Is' agrees in number with a singular 'staff', so it applies the proximity rule correctly, but it changes the tense from past to present. In error-correction items you fix only the error that exists, here number agreement, and keep everything else, including the past tense set by the original 'were'. It tempts because candidates who spot the singular subject stop there and pick the first singular verb they see.","c":"'Are' is wrong on both counts: it is plural, so it ignores the singular nearer subject, and it is present tense, so it abandons the original past-tense frame. It tempts only candidates who treat 'neither ... nor' like 'and', adding the two subjects together into a plural, and who also fail to notice the tense shift. It is the weakest of the three distractors here."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence with correct punctuation:","concept":"Two punctuation rules decide this item. First, an independent clause is a group of words with a subject and a finite verb that can stand alone as a sentence ('the results were encouraging'; 'the authors urged caution'). Two independent clauses can be joined correctly in only three ways: with a full stop, with a semicolon, or with a comma plus a coordinating conjunction (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so, remembered as FANBOYS). Joining them with a comma alone creates a comma splice; joining them with nothing creates a run-on or fused sentence. Second, 'however' used as a conjunctive adverb (like therefore, moreover, nevertheless, consequently, furthermore) is not a conjunction. It cannot join clauses by itself. It is followed by a comma at the start of a clause ('However, the results ...'), takes a semicolon before and a comma after when it sits between clauses ('The results were encouraging; however, the authors urged caution.'), and takes paired commas in the middle of a clause ('The authors, however, urged caution.'). A different 'however', meaning 'in whatever way' or 'to whatever degree', takes no comma: 'However hard she tried, she failed.' A semicolon needs a complete clause on both sides, so it can never follow a lone introductory word. A comma should also never separate a subject from its verb ('the results, were' is wrong). Option a is right because it puts a comma after the introductory 'However' and joins the two independent clauses with a semicolon, avoiding a comma splice.","traps":{"b":"Read normally, with 'however' meaning 'but', this sentence lacks the comma after 'However' and joins two independent clauses with only a comma, a comma splice. The only grammatical reading treats 'However' as 'in whatever way', which makes no sense here. It tempts because it reads smoothly aloud, and a comma after the first clause feels like a natural pause in speech.","c":"A semicolon after 'However' is wrong because a semicolon must stand between two complete independent clauses, and 'However' on its own is a single introductory adverb, not a clause. The sentence also still joins the two real clauses with a comma alone, a comma splice. It tempts because it contains a semicolon, the mark candidates know belongs somewhere near 'however', but places it in the wrong spot.","d":"This sentence drops the comma after 'However', inserts a comma between the subject 'the results' and its verb 'were', which is never allowed, and still links two independent clauses with a comma alone. It has three faults, more than any other option. It tempts only candidates who place commas wherever they would pause in speech, treating punctuation as breathing marks instead of grammar."}},{"q":"Choose the most appropriate word: 'The findings of the study are not <u>____</u> to the broader population because the sample was small and self-selected.'","concept":"Fill-in-the-blank vocabulary questions often test word form, not meaning. All four options share the root 'general', and only the suffix changes the part of speech: -isation (or -ization) and -ity make nouns, -able makes an adjective, and -ly makes an adverb. The sentence frame decides which one fits. 'The findings of the study are not ___ to the broader population' uses 'are', a linking verb. After a linking verb (be, seem, appear, become) we need a subject complement, and when it describes the subject it is a predicate adjective, as in 'the findings are reliable'. 'Generalisable' means able to be applied beyond the cases studied, and it takes the preposition 'to', exactly as the sentence has it. British spelling is 'generalisable', American 'generalizable'; both are correct, so spelling is not being tested. The sentence also carries a research methods point that a psychology examiner may test separately. Generalisability is external validity, the extent to which results hold for other people, settings and times; internal and external validity are terms introduced by Donald T. Campbell (1957). A small sample increases sampling error, and a self-selected (volunteer) sample introduces volunteer bias, since people who choose to take part differ from those who do not. Both threaten external validity, while internal validity concerns whether the study shows cause and effect within the sample. Representative probability samples, such as stratified random samples, improve generalisability. Option b is right because the blank after the linking verb 'are not' needs a predicate adjective, and 'generalisable' is the adjective that fits with 'to'.","traps":{"a":"'Generalisation' is a noun meaning the act of generalising or a broad statement drawn from particular cases ('a sweeping generalisation'). In psychology it also names stimulus generalisation in conditioning, from Ivan Pavlov's work. A noun after 'are not' would make the findings equal to a generalisation, which makes no sense, and 'generalisation to' cannot follow here. It tempts because it is the most familiar form of the word in research methods.","c":"'Generality' is an abstract noun meaning the quality of being general or broadly applicable, or a vague statement ('speaking in generalities'). Like any noun, it cannot serve as the describing word after 'are not' in this frame. It tempts because its meaning, broad applicability, is close to what the sentence intends, so a candidate who reads for meaning rather than grammatical form may choose it.","d":"'Generally' is an adverb meaning usually or in most cases ('Findings are generally reported in tables'). Adverbs modify verbs, adjectives or whole clauses; they do not complete a linking verb to describe its subject. 'The findings are not generally to the broader population' is ungrammatical. It tempts because adverbs often sit right after 'are not' in correct sentences, so the position looks familiar."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"English has four main conditional patterns, defined by the tenses of the if-clause and the result clause. The zero conditional states general truths: if + present, present ('If you heat ice, it melts'). The first conditional states real future possibilities: if + present, will + base verb ('If it rains, I will stay home'). The second conditional states unreal present or future situations: if + past simple, would + base verb ('If I knew her number, I would call her'); formal English uses 'were' for all persons ('If I were you'). The third conditional states unreal past situations and their imagined results: if + past perfect (had + past participle), would have + past participle. 'If I had known, I would have called you' means I did not know and did not call. Mixed conditionals combine times: 'If I had taken the job, I would be rich now' (past condition, present result). The third conditional can also be inverted without 'if': 'Had I known, I would have called you.' The commonest error is putting 'would' in the if-clause: 'If I would have known' is nonstandard in formal English, because 'would' belongs in the result clause. Another error is the wrong verb form after a modal: would, could and should are followed by the base form ('would call', 'would have called'), never a past form ('would called', 'would knew'). Option d is right because it pairs the past perfect 'had known' in the if-clause with 'would have called' in the result clause, the correct third conditional.","traps":{"a":"'If I would have known' puts 'would have' in the if-clause, the classic conditional error. It is heard often in casual speech, especially in American English, which makes it sound acceptable, but formal and written English, and every exam key, require the past perfect 'had known' in the if-clause. The result clause 'I would have called you' is correct, which is exactly why this option is the strongest trap.","b":"'If I knew earlier' uses the past simple, the second conditional form, but the adverb 'earlier' points to a completed past situation that calls for the past perfect 'had known'. The result clause 'I would called' is broken because a modal must be followed by a base form ('would call') or by 'have' plus a past participle ('would have called'). It tempts because each half looks like part of a real pattern.","c":"'If I would knew' is ungrammatical twice over: 'would' must not appear in the if-clause, and after a modal the verb must be in its base form, so 'would knew' is impossible. The main clause 'I called you' is a plain past statement reporting a real event, so the sentence is not hypothetical at all. It tempts only careless readers, because its opening resembles the common 'if I would' error."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'ubiquitous':","concept":"'Ubiquitous' (pronounced yoo-BIK-wi-tus) means present, appearing or found everywhere, often at the same time. It comes from the Latin 'ubique', meaning everywhere, and the noun is 'ubiquity'. Typical uses: 'Smartphones have become ubiquitous'; 'Anxiety is ubiquitous in modern life'. The closest synonym is 'omnipresent', built from the Latin 'omnis' (all) and 'praesens' (present), so literally 'present in all places'. Other near-synonyms an examiner might offer are pervasive, universal, prevalent, rife and all-pervading. Antonyms are rare, scarce, uncommon, sparse and infrequent. In synonym questions, use a two-step method. First, define the target word in your own words before looking at the options ('found everywhere'). Second, choose the option whose core meaning matches that definition, not one that is merely associated with it. Distractors are usually of three types: an antonym (here 'rare'), a word with a related but different idea (here 'uniform', which concerns sameness, and 'obscure', which concerns being hidden or little known), and sometimes a word that merely sounds similar. Related 'omni' words worth knowing: omniscient (all-knowing), omnipotent (all-powerful) and omnivorous (eating everything). In psychology the word often describes effects found across cultures, for example the near-universal recognition of basic facial expressions reported by Paul Ekman (1971). Option a is right because 'omnipresent', present everywhere at once, matches the meaning of 'ubiquitous' exactly.","traps":{"b":"'Rare' means seldom found, occurring or seen, as in 'a rare disorder'. It is the near-opposite of ubiquitous, which describes something found everywhere. Examiners routinely place an antonym among synonym options, because a candidate who remembers only that the word concerns how widely something occurs, without its direction, may pick the antonym. Always fix the direction of the meaning before choosing.","c":"'Obscure' means not clearly seen or understood, or little known ('an obscure poet', 'an obscure passage'). It concerns visibility, clarity or fame, not how widely something is spread. If anything, an obscure thing is hard to find, which points away from ubiquitous. It tempts because both are formal, Latin-derived words from the same vocabulary lists, and an unsure candidate may pick an equally difficult-looking word.","d":"'Uniform' means the same throughout, consistent or unvarying ('uniform temperature', 'a uniform standard'). It describes sameness, not presence everywhere: something can be uniform within one room only, and something ubiquitous can vary greatly from place to place. It tempts because the sense of something spread evenly can feel close to 'everywhere', and both words can describe a widespread pattern."}},{"q":"Identify the error: 'The patient, along with her two children, <u>were</u> waiting in the consulting room.'","concept":"This item tests how to find the true subject when extra words sit between the subject and the verb. The rule: phrases introduced by 'along with', 'together with', 'as well as', 'in addition to', 'accompanied by', 'including', 'besides' and 'like' are not part of the grammatical subject. They add information, are usually set off by commas, and do not change the number of the subject. The verb agrees with the noun before the phrase. In 'The patient, along with her two children, were waiting', the subject is 'the patient', which is singular, so the verb must be singular: 'The patient, along with her two children, was waiting in the consulting room.' The plural noun 'children' sits closer to the verb and pulls the ear towards 'were'; this is called an attraction or proximity error, and examiners plant it on purpose. A useful test is to cover the phrase between the commas and read what remains: 'The patient was waiting'. Contrast this with 'and', which creates a compound subject: 'The patient and her two children were waiting' is correct, because two subjects are now joined. Contrast it also with 'neither ... nor' and 'either ... or', where the verb agrees with the nearer subject. The same trap appears with prepositional phrases: 'The quality of the reports is poor', not 'are', since the subject is 'quality'. Option b is right because the singular subject 'the patient' needs the singular verb 'was', and the 'along with' phrase does not make the subject plural.","traps":{"a":"'No error' would be correct only if 'were' agreed with its subject, but the subject is the singular 'the patient'; the phrase 'along with her two children' does not add a second subject. Choosing this means the ear has accepted the plural pull of 'children', which sits right next to the verb. It tempts because 'were waiting' sounds natural when three people are pictured waiting together.","c":"Substituting 'and' for 'with' produces 'The patient, along and her two children, were waiting', which is nonsense, since 'along and' is not English. A true compound subject would need a different rewrite, 'The patient and her two children were waiting', removing 'along' and the commas. It tempts because candidates know that 'and' makes a plural subject and see a way to justify 'were', without checking the literal result.","d":"'Her' correctly refers to the patient, a woman, and is singular to match. Changing it to 'their' would either clash with the singular antecedent or, read as a singular 'they', change nothing important; in either case the plural verb 'were' remains wrong. It tempts because pronoun agreement is another common error type in these items, and the plural 'children' nearby makes 'their' look plausible."}},{"q":"Choose the option closest in meaning to 'salient':","concept":"'Salient' (pronounced SAY-lee-ent) means most noticeable, important or striking: standing out from its surroundings. The salient points of an argument are its main, prominent points. It comes from the Latin 'salire', to leap or jump, so a salient feature is one that 'leaps out' at you. The noun is 'salience' (or 'saliency'). The closest synonym among the options is 'prominent'; others are conspicuous, striking, noticeable, outstanding, notable and pronounced. Antonyms include inconspicuous, minor, insignificant, trivial and hidden. In military language a salient is a part of a front line that bulges out into enemy territory, the same jutting-out idea. The word matters in psychology. Perceptual salience is how much a stimulus stands out, for example a red item among green ones, and salient stimuli capture attention automatically (bottom-up attention). In social perception, salient people or features attract more attention and are often judged more causally important. In psychiatry, Shitij Kapur (2003) proposed the aberrant salience hypothesis: excess dopamine in psychosis makes ordinary stimuli seem unusually significant, and delusions are the person's attempt to explain that feeling. In neuroscience, the salience network, centred on the anterior insula and the dorsal anterior cingulate cortex, detects events that matter and helps switch between the default mode network and the central executive network. An examiner may test the plain English meaning or any of these technical uses, and all of them share the idea of standing out. Option b is right because 'prominent', meaning standing out and easily noticed, is the closest in meaning to 'salient'.","traps":{"a":"'Trivial' means of little value or importance ('a trivial complaint'). It is close to the opposite of salient, which describes the most important or noticeable feature. It tempts because 'salient' is often met in phrases like 'salient details', and a candidate who links 'details' with minor points may drift towards 'trivial'. Antonyms are standard distractors in synonym items, so check direction before answering.","c":"'Concealed' means hidden or kept out of sight ('a concealed weapon'). A salient feature is the opposite: it stands out and is the first thing noticed. It tempts mainly as a near-antonym, the most common type of distractor in synonym questions, and because a candidate unsure of the word may guess any meaning with a slightly mysterious tone. Fix the core sense, 'leaps out', first.","d":"'Uncertain' means not known, not sure or not definite ('an uncertain outcome'). It describes the state of knowledge or confidence, not how noticeable or important something is; a salient fact can be certain or uncertain. It tempts candidates who vaguely associate 'salient' with formal academic discussion of results and findings, where words about uncertainty and doubt are common."}},{"q":"Select the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"Direct speech reports a speaker's exact words inside quotation marks; indirect (reported) speech paraphrases them without quotation marks. Four rules govern a quoted question in direct speech. First, a comma separates the reporting clause from the quotation when the reporting clause comes first: The therapist asked, followed by the quotation. Second, the quotation begins with a capital letter, because the quoted words form a complete sentence of their own. Third, the question mark belongs to the quoted words, so it goes inside the closing quotation mark. Fourth, no full stop follows; the question mark ends the whole sentence. Put together: The therapist asked, 'Have you been sleeping well?' British and Indian writing usually uses single quotation marks, American writing double; the placement rules are the same. Contrast the case where the whole sentence is a question but the quotation is not: Did the patient really say, 'I feel fine'? Here the question mark goes outside, because it belongs to the outer sentence. In indirect speech the question becomes a statement, with no question mark and no quotation marks, an 'if' or 'whether' connector, statement word order and a backshifted tense: The therapist asked whether I had been sleeping well. Common examiner traps are a lowercase first word, a missing comma after the speech verb, a full stop instead of a question mark, and a question mark outside the quotes. Option b is right because it has the comma after 'asked', a capital 'Have', and the question mark inside the closing quotation mark.","traps":{"a":"This version has two faults. The quoted question starts with a lowercase 'have', although a quoted full sentence needs a capital, and the question mark sits outside the closing quotation mark, which wrongly makes the whole sentence the question rather than the quoted words. Outside placement is right only when the outer sentence is the question. It tempts because the comma after 'asked' is correct, so it looks partly right.","c":"This version gets the comma and the capital letter right but ends the quoted question with a full stop. 'Have you been sleeping well' is a direct question, shown by its inverted word order (auxiliary before subject), so it must end with a question mark. A full stop would suit only indirect speech, which has no quotation marks. It tempts because the other two visible rules are followed.","d":"This version leaves out the comma after the reporting verb 'asked' and begins the quoted question with a lowercase 'have'. The question mark is correctly inside the quotation mark, which is why it can look acceptable at a glance. A full quoted sentence after a speech verb needs both the introducing comma and the capital letter. It tempts because the end of the sentence is correct."}},{"q":"Reading comprehension: 'Although early studies suggested that lithium was uniformly effective, more recent meta-analyses have qualified this view: lithium reduces suicide and prevents relapse but is less reliable for acute mania than second-generation antipsychotics.' The passage primarily implies that:","concept":"This item tests inference in reading comprehension: choosing the conclusion the passage actually supports, no more and no less. Start with the structure. 'Although early studies suggested ..., more recent meta-analyses have qualified this view' is a concession-contrast frame: an earlier claim is not rejected but limited. 'Qualified' means modified or restricted, not refuted. The colon then gives the details: lithium reduces suicide and prevents relapse (strengths kept), but is less reliable for acute mania than second-generation antipsychotics (a limit added). So the balanced implication is that lithium stays valuable, though it is not the best choice in every phase. Wrong options in inference questions usually overstate. Watch for absolute words such as no, all, completely, always and never: here 'no role', 'all early studies' and 'completely'. John Cade (1949) in Australia first reported lithium's anti-manic effect, and Mogens Schou in Denmark tested it in a controlled trial (1954) and later showed its value in preventing relapse. Lithium remains a first-line maintenance treatment for bipolar disorder in major guidelines, and it is the mood stabiliser with the strongest evidence for reducing suicide. For acute mania, antipsychotics such as olanzapine, quetiapine, risperidone and aripiprazole often act faster. Lithium has a narrow therapeutic range, so serum levels must be monitored, along with kidney and thyroid function. A meta-analysis statistically pools many studies, so it outweighs single early trials. Option d is right because the passage keeps lithium's value for suicide reduction and relapse prevention while stating that it is not the most reliable agent for acute mania.","traps":{"a":"Saying lithium has no role contradicts the passage, which credits lithium with reducing suicide and preventing relapse, two major clinical benefits. A view that is 'qualified' is limited, not abandoned. The absolute word 'no' is a classic sign of an overstated inference. It tempts because the passage opens by retreating from early enthusiasm, and a fast reader may take any criticism as total rejection.","b":"The passage says early studies suggested lithium was uniformly effective and that later meta-analyses qualified that view. It never says the early studies were badly done; their conclusion was simply refined by more data. 'All early studies were flawed' adds a claim about methodology that the text never makes, and 'all' is an absolute word. It tempts because revising an old view can feel like proving the old work wrong.","c":"The passage says second-generation antipsychotics are more reliable for acute mania only. It keeps lithium's benefits in suicide prevention and relapse prevention, so nothing has been replaced completely. 'Completely' is an extreme word that the text does not support. It tempts because the passage does name antipsychotics as better in one phase, and a reader can stretch that single comparison into a total replacement."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion to be grammatically correct: 'Each of the participants <u>have</u> been informed about the risks.'","concept":"'Each' is a distributive word: it picks out members of a group one at a time, so as a subject it is singular and takes a singular verb. The pattern 'each of + plural noun' is the classic trap, because the plural noun sits next to the verb and pulls the ear towards a plural. The true subject is 'each', and the phrase 'of the participants' only names the group, so the verb must be singular: 'Each of the participants has been informed about the risks.' The verb is in the present perfect passive (has been informed), so only the auxiliary 'have' changes to 'has'; 'been informed' stays. The same rule covers 'every' (every participant has), 'either' and 'neither' used alone ('Neither of the options is correct'), 'one of' ('One of the patients was discharged'), and pronouns such as everyone and nobody. Note three contrasts. First, when 'each' follows a plural subject it does not control the verb: 'The participants each have been informed' is correct, because 'participants' is the subject. Second, 'a number of' is plural ('A number of patients were seen') while 'the number of' is singular ('The number of patients was high'). Third, 'none of' can take a singular or a plural verb in modern usage, though formal style often prefers the singular. In replacement questions, substitute each option and read the whole sentence, since some options fail only with the words that follow. Option b is right because 'each' is singular, so the auxiliary must be 'has', giving 'Each of the participants has been informed'.","traps":{"a":"'Have' is the original word, so choosing it means accepting the error. It agrees with the plural noun 'participants', which is only the object of the preposition 'of', not with the true subject 'each', which is singular. This mistake is called agreement by attraction or proximity. It tempts because 'participants have been informed' sounds natural on its own, which is exactly why examiners build the sentence this way.","c":"Substituted literally, 'had been' gives 'Each of the participants had been been informed', with 'been' doubled, which is ungrammatical. Even read loosely as 'had been informed', it changes the present perfect into the past perfect, which needs an earlier past reference point that the sentence lacks, and it avoids rather than corrects the agreement error. It tempts because 'had' is the same for singular and plural subjects.","d":"'Is' is singular, so it seems to fix agreement, but substituted it gives 'Each of the participants is been informed', and 'is been' is not English. The passive would need 'is informed' (present simple) or 'has been informed' (present perfect). It tempts because candidates who know that 'each' takes a singular verb may grab the first singular form they see without checking how it fits with 'been'."}}],"met-mock-4":[{"q":"GABA-A receptors differ from GABA-B receptors primarily in that GABA-A:","concept":"Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter of the brain. It is made from glutamate by the enzyme glutamic acid decarboxylase, and it acts on two receptor families that differ in structure and speed. GABA-A is ionotropic: the receptor is itself a ligand-gated ion channel built from five subunits around a central pore. When GABA binds, the pore opens within milliseconds and chloride ions flow into the neuron. The inside becomes more negative (hyperpolarised), so a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) is produced and the cell is less likely to fire. GABA-A also carries separate binding sites for many drugs: benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol, some anaesthetics and neurosteroids all enhance its action. This explains their sedative, anxiolytic and anticonvulsant effects and their dangerous combination in overdose. Flumazenil blocks the benzodiazepine site, and bicuculline blocks the GABA site. GABA-B is metabotropic: a G-protein coupled receptor with no pore of its own. Through its G-protein it opens potassium channels (K+ flows out) and closes calcium channels, giving a slow, long-lasting IPSP and less transmitter release from the terminal. Baclofen, used for spasticity, is the classic GABA-B agonist. The same ionotropic versus metabotropic contrast appears for glutamate (AMPA, NMDA and kainate versus mGluRs) and acetylcholine (nicotinic versus muscarinic), so an examiner can rebuild this question around any of them. Glycine receptors are the other main ligand-gated chloride channel, mostly in the spinal cord. Option d is right because GABA-A is the ionotropic, ligand-gated chloride channel that produces fast IPSPs, whereas GABA-B works slowly through G-proteins.","traps":{"a":"Increasing sodium permeability is how excitatory ionotropic receptors work: glutamate's AMPA receptor and the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor open cation channels, Na+ enters, and the cell depolarises to produce an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP). GABA-A does the reverse, letting chloride in and hyperpolarising the cell. It tempts because GABA-A really is a fast ion channel, so a candidate who remembers 'fast channel' but not which ion it carries can pick this.","b":"This is an accurate description of the GABA-B receptor, not GABA-A. GABA-B is metabotropic: GABA binding activates a G-protein, which opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels, producing slow inhibition and reduced transmitter release; baclofen is its standard agonist. It is the most tempting distractor because every word is true for the sister receptor, so it catches candidates who have learned the facts but swapped the labels A and B.","c":"Voltage-gated channels open in response to changes in membrane potential, not to a neurotransmitter; examples are the sodium and potassium channels that generate the action potential and the calcium channels at the axon terminal. GABA-A is ligand-gated, so GABA (or a direct-acting drug) must bind before it opens. It tempts because 'channel' sounds right, and because high-dose barbiturates can open GABA-A without GABA, which some confuse with GABA independence."}},{"q":"The NMDA glutamate receptor is unique among ionotropic receptors because:","concept":"Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter of the brain. It acts on three ionotropic receptors, named after the drugs that selectively activate them (AMPA, kainate and NMDA, from N-methyl-D-aspartate), and on a family of metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluRs). AMPA receptors do the routine work: glutamate binds, Na+ enters, and a fast excitatory postsynaptic potential follows. The NMDA receptor differs in three ways. First, at the resting potential (about minus 70 mV) its pore is plugged by a magnesium ion, so glutamate binding alone lets almost no current through. Second, the Mg2+ plug is expelled only when the postsynaptic membrane is already depolarised, usually by strong AMPA activity. The channel therefore opens only when presynaptic release and postsynaptic activity coincide, which is why it is called a coincidence detector, a molecular version of the postulate of Donald O. Hebb (1949) that a synapse strengthens when one cell repeatedly helps fire another. Third, when open it admits Ca2+ as well as Na+, and the calcium acts as a second messenger that starts long-term potentiation, first reported by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973). It also needs glycine or D-serine as a co-agonist. Clinically, ketamine and phencyclidine block the NMDA channel and can produce psychosis-like states, which supports the glutamate hypothesis of schizophrenia; memantine, a milder blocker, is used in Alzheimer's disease. Excess calcium entry through NMDA receptors also drives excitotoxic cell death after stroke. Option c is right because the NMDA receptor uniquely needs both glutamate binding and depolarisation to remove the Mg2+ block, after which calcium enters.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the real condition. At hyperpolarised or resting potentials the NMDA channel is blocked by Mg2+, which is pulled into the pore by the negative inside of the cell; only depolarisation drives the magnesium out. A channel that opened only when the cell was hyperpolarised could never act as a Hebbian coincidence detector. It tempts because the option mentions membrane potential, which is the correct theme, but gives the wrong direction.","b":"Selective chloride permeability belongs to the inhibitory ionotropic receptors, chiefly GABA-A and the glycine receptor, which hyperpolarise the cell. The NMDA receptor is a non-selective cation channel that passes Na+, K+ and, importantly, Ca2+, and its effect is excitatory. The word 'only' makes the option doubly wrong. It tempts candidates who recall that NMDA has an unusual ion story but cannot remember whether it is the magnesium block or chloride.","d":"Metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluR1 to mGluR8) are G-protein coupled receptors that act slowly through second messengers and modulate excitability and transmitter release. The NMDA receptor is not one of them; it is an ionotropic, ligand-gated channel, as the stem itself says by placing NMDA 'among ionotropic receptors'. It tempts because NMDA is often described as slower and more complex than AMPA, which can be misread as metabotropic."}},{"q":"Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy (as in patient H.M.) produces:","concept":"Henry Molaison, known for decades as patient H.M., had severe epilepsy. In 1953 the neurosurgeon William Beecher Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes on both sides, including most of the hippocampus, the amygdala and nearby parahippocampal cortex. The seizures improved, but Scoville and Brenda Milner (1957) reported a profound memory loss. H.M. had severe anterograde amnesia: he could not form new lasting declarative memories, meaning facts and events he could consciously report. He reread the same magazines and could not recognise people he met after the operation. His retrograde amnesia was limited and temporally graded, covering mainly the years just before surgery, while older childhood memories survived. Several abilities were spared. His IQ stayed in the normal range, his short-term memory was intact (he could hold a number while rehearsing it), and his language and personality were largely preserved. Most strikingly, when Milner taught him mirror drawing (tracing a star seen only in a mirror) over several days, his skill improved steadily even though he did not remember having done the task before. This dissociation showed that declarative memory depends on the medial temporal lobe, while procedural memory relies on other systems such as the basal ganglia and cerebellum. Neal J. Cohen and Larry R. Squire (1980) later framed this as knowing that versus knowing how. Compare Korsakoff's syndrome (mammillary bodies and thalamus, linked to thiamine deficiency), which also produces anterograde amnesia but adds confabulation. Option d is right because bilateral medial temporal removal caused severe anterograde amnesia for declarative memory while procedural learning remained relatively intact.","traps":{"a":"Motor coordination depends on the cerebellum, the basal ganglia and the motor cortex, none of which were removed in H.M. He walked, spoke and handled objects normally, and he even learned new motor skills such as mirror drawing. Loss of all coordination would point to cerebellar or widespread motor damage. It tempts only because the wording sounds dramatic, and because candidates sometimes assume any major brain surgery must disable movement.","b":"This reverses the pattern. Retrograde amnesia is loss of memories formed before the injury; anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new memories after it. H.M.'s main deficit was anterograde, while his retrograde loss was limited and temporally graded. Lost personal past with preserved new learning is closer to dissociative (psychogenic) amnesia. It tempts because the two terms are easily swapped under exam pressure.","c":"Semantic memory (general facts and word meanings) and episodic memory (personal events tied to time and place) are the two parts of declarative memory distinguished by Endel Tulving (1972). H.M. was impaired in forming both kinds of new memory, not semantic memory alone, and his old knowledge of language and facts was largely intact. Selective semantic loss is closer to semantic dementia. It tempts because Tulving's terms are familiar and sound precise."}},{"q":"The amygdala is most directly implicated in:","concept":"The amygdala is an almond-shaped group of nuclei in the anterior medial temporal lobe, just in front of the hippocampus, and it is the core of the brain's threat and emotional-salience system. The basolateral complex receives sensory input and forms associations; the central nucleus sends output to the hypothalamus and brainstem to produce freezing, startle, a racing heart and stress hormone release. In classical fear conditioning, a neutral tone paired with a shock comes to evoke fear, and this learning fails when the amygdala is lesioned. Joseph LeDoux (1996) described two routes: a fast 'low road' from the thalamus straight to the amygdala, which triggers a crude rapid response, and a slower 'high road' through the sensory cortex, which gives detailed analysis. Heinrich Kluver and Paul Bucy (1939) showed that bilateral temporal lobe removal in monkeys, including the amygdala, produced tameness, loss of fear, hyperorality, hypersexuality and psychic blindness (visual agnosia). Ralph Adolphs and colleagues (1994) found that patient S.M., with bilateral amygdala damage from Urbach-Wiethe disease, could not recognise fear in faces. The amygdala also tags emotionally arousing events so that the hippocampus stores them more strongly. Examiners pair it with its neighbours: the hippocampus for declarative memory, the basal ganglia and cerebellum for motor skills, the suprachiasmatic nucleus for circadian timing, and the hypothalamus for drives such as hunger and thirst. Option a is right because the amygdala is most directly responsible for fear conditioning, emotional salience and the detection of threat.","traps":{"b":"Motor sequence learning, such as learning to type or play a tune, is a form of procedural memory supported by the basal ganglia (especially the striatum), the cerebellum and the motor cortex. It is spared in amnesic patients like H.M. and impaired in Parkinson's and Huntington's diseases. The amygdala adds emotional weight to experiences but does not store motor skills. It tempts because the amygdala and basal ganglia are both deep subcortical structures.","c":"Conscious recall of facts is semantic declarative memory, which depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal cortex for encoding and on the neocortex for long-term storage. The amygdala can strengthen memory for emotional events, a modulating role, but it does not hold the facts themselves. It tempts because the amygdala lies right next to the hippocampus in the medial temporal lobe, and both were removed in patient H.M.","d":"Circadian rhythms are generated by the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus, which receives light information from the retina through the retinohypothalamic tract and signals the pineal gland to release melatonin at night. The amygdala has no pacemaker role. It tempts because both are small, deep structures often taught in the same limbic and hypothalamic chapter, and because emotional states can disturb sleep."}},{"q":"The Papez circuit includes which sequence?","concept":"James Papez (1937), an American neuroanatomist, proposed that emotion is not produced by one centre but by a closed loop of connected structures on the medial wall of the brain. The loop runs from the hippocampus (via the subiculum) through the fornix to the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus; from there along the mammillothalamic tract to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus; then to the cingulate gyrus; then through the cingulum bundle to the parahippocampal gyrus and entorhinal cortex, which feeds back into the hippocampus. Papez thought the cingulate cortex was the seat of emotional experience and the hypothalamus the source of emotional expression. Paul MacLean (1952) built on this to coin the term limbic system, adding the amygdala and prefrontal regions. Later research showed that the Papez circuit matters more for memory than for emotion: damage at any link (hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies or anterior thalamus) can produce anterograde amnesia. Korsakoff's syndrome, caused by thiamine deficiency usually in chronic alcohol use, damages the mammillary bodies and medial thalamus, which is why it causes amnesia with confabulation. The amygdala, now seen as the true emotion hub, is not part of the original loop. Examiners build variants with other well-known pathways: the basal ganglia motor loop (cortex, striatum, globus pallidus, thalamus, cortex) and the visual pathway (retina, optic nerve, lateral geniculate nucleus, primary visual cortex). Option a is right because it gives the Papez order: hippocampus, fornix, mammillary bodies, anterior thalamus, cingulate cortex and back to the hippocampus.","traps":{"b":"This is the cortico-basal ganglia-thalamo-cortical loop. The cortex projects to the striatum (caudate and putamen), which projects to the globus pallidus, then to the thalamus and back to the cortex. It selects and initiates movements and habits; its disorders include Parkinson's and Huntington's diseases, and it is implicated in OCD. It tempts because it is also a closed loop that passes through the thalamus, just like the Papez circuit.","c":"This is the primary visual pathway: retina to the lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus, then to primary visual cortex (V1, Brodmann area 17, in the occipital lobe), then to V2 and higher visual areas. It processes sight, not emotion, and it is not a loop. It tempts because it also passes through a thalamic nucleus, and candidates who only remember 'thalamus' as part of Papez may pick any sequence containing it.","d":"This sequence is not a real circuit. The amygdala does project to the hypothalamus, but the cerebellum has no place in the Papez loop, and the sequence never returns to its starting point. The amygdala itself was brought into the limbic system later by Paul MacLean and was not in Papez's original loop. It tempts because amygdala and hypothalamus sound like emotion structures, so the option feels right for an emotion circuit."}},{"q":"Wernicke's area is classically located in:","concept":"Carl Wernicke (1874), a German neurologist, described patients who spoke fluently but whose speech made little sense and who could not understand what was said to them. The damage lay in the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere, roughly Brodmann area 22, next to the primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus, areas 41 and 42). This region, Wernicke's area, maps speech sounds onto meaning. Language is lateralised to the left hemisphere in about 95 percent of right-handers and in most left-handers, which is why textbooks say 'dominant hemisphere' rather than simply 'left'. Wernicke's aphasia is fluent but empty, with paraphasias (wrong sounds or words), neologisms and sometimes word salad; comprehension and repetition are poor, and the patient is often unaware of the problem. Thirteen years earlier, Paul Broca (1861) had linked effortful, non-fluent speech with damage to the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45). The arcuate fasciculus connects the two areas; Wernicke predicted that cutting it would cause conduction aphasia, with fluent speech and good comprehension but very poor repetition. Norman Geschwind (1965) revived this scheme, now called the Wernicke-Geschwind model. Nearby landmarks used as distractors include the angular gyrus (reading and writing; damage causes alexia with agraphia), the occipital lobe (vision) and the postcentral gyrus (primary somatosensory cortex). Option c is right because Wernicke's area is classically placed in the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the language-dominant hemisphere.","traps":{"a":"The occipital pole is the back tip of the occipital lobe and holds primary visual cortex (V1, Brodmann area 17), where the central visual field is represented. Damage there causes visual field defects or, if bilateral, cortical blindness, not a comprehension aphasia. It tempts because reading involves vision, and pure alexia without agraphia, described by Joseph Jules Dejerine (1892), follows left occipital damage, so candidates link the occipital lobe to language.","b":"The inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere (pars opercularis and pars triangularis, Brodmann areas 44 and 45) is Broca's area, identified by Paul Broca (1861). It supports speech production and grammar, and damage causes non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with fairly good comprehension. It tempts because the option correctly says 'dominant hemisphere', and because Broca's and Wernicke's areas are always taught as a pair.","d":"The postcentral gyrus, just behind the central sulcus in the parietal lobe, is primary somatosensory cortex (Brodmann areas 3, 1 and 2). It holds the sensory homunculus mapped by Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950) and receives touch, pain, temperature and position sense from the opposite side of the body. Damage causes sensory loss, not aphasia. It tempts because it is another named gyrus close to the language regions."}},{"q":"The key feature distinguishing Broca's aphasia from Wernicke's aphasia is that Broca's aphasia involves:","concept":"Aphasia is an acquired language disorder after brain damage, usually stroke. The classic Wernicke-Geschwind framework sorts aphasias on three bedside tests: fluency, comprehension and repetition. Paul Broca (1861) studied a patient, Leborgne, nicknamed 'Tan' because it was nearly the only syllable he could say; autopsy showed a lesion in the left inferior frontal gyrus (Brodmann areas 44 and 45). Broca's (expressive, motor) aphasia is non-fluent: speech is slow, effortful and telegraphic, with content words but few function words and grammatical endings (agrammatism), for example 'Walk... dog... park'. Comprehension of everyday speech is relatively preserved, though complex grammatical sentences can be hard. Repetition is impaired. Patients usually know they are making errors and become frustrated, and right-sided weakness often coexists because the motor cortex is nearby. Carl Wernicke (1874) described the mirror image: fluent, effortless but empty speech with paraphasias and neologisms, poor comprehension, poor repetition and poor awareness. Other types used in variants: conduction aphasia (arcuate fasciculus; fluent, good comprehension, very poor repetition); transcortical motor aphasia (non-fluent but repetition intact); transcortical sensory aphasia (fluent, poor comprehension, repetition intact); global aphasia (everything impaired); and anomic aphasia (fluent with word-finding pauses). A quick rule: preserved repetition points to a transcortical aphasia. Option d is right because Broca's aphasia is defined by effortful, non-fluent speech, relatively preserved comprehension and impaired repetition, the reverse of Wernicke's fluent speech with poor comprehension.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with intact comprehension is normal language, or at most anomic aphasia, where the main problem is word-finding with pauses and roundabout descriptions. If repetition were also impaired, it would suggest conduction aphasia from arcuate fasciculus damage. Broca's aphasia is by definition non-fluent. It tempts because it keeps half of the correct answer (preserved comprehension) and swaps in the wrong half (fluent speech).","b":"Fluent but meaningless speech with poor comprehension is Wernicke's aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke (1874) after damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus. Speech flows with normal rhythm but contains paraphasias, neologisms and word salad, and the patient often does not notice the errors. It is the exact opposite of Broca's aphasia on both fluency and comprehension. It tempts because the stem names both aphasias, so a quick reader may describe the wrong one.","c":"Alexia is an acquired loss of reading. Pure alexia (alexia without agraphia), described by Joseph Jules Dejerine (1892), follows damage to the left occipital lobe and the splenium of the corpus callosum: the patient can write but cannot read back what was written. It is a disconnection syndrome, not a disorder of speech output. It tempts because it is another classic language disorder taught in the same neurology chapter as the aphasias."}},{"q":"Which function is most reliably lateralised to the right (non-dominant) hemisphere in right-handers?","concept":"Lateralisation means that the two cerebral hemispheres, though similar, specialise for different functions. Early evidence came from lesions: Paul Broca (1861) and Carl Wernicke (1874) linked language loss to left hemisphere damage. Michael Gazzaniga, Joseph Bogen and Roger W. Sperry (1962) studied split-brain patients, whose corpus callosum had been cut to control epilepsy; Sperry received the Nobel Prize in 1981. When a picture was flashed to the right hemisphere alone, the patient could not name it but could pick it out with the left hand. The Wada test (intracarotid sodium amobarbital), introduced by Juhn Wada (1949), shows that language is left-lateralised in about 95 percent of right-handers. The left hemisphere handles phonology, grammar, vocabulary, verbal memory and analytic, sequential processing. The right hemisphere excels at visuospatial work: mental rotation, spatial attention, block design, drawing, face recognition and holistic or Gestalt perception. It also processes prosody, the melody and emotional tone of speech; right hemisphere damage produces aprosodia, with flat speech and difficulty reading emotion in voices. Right parietal damage typically causes hemispatial neglect of the left side of space and constructional apraxia (difficulty drawing and copying). Lateralisation is relative, and the popular idea of the logical left brain and creative right brain is a myth. Option b is right because visuospatial processing and prosody are the functions most reliably lateralised to the right, non-dominant hemisphere in right-handers.","traps":{"a":"Phonological processing, the analysis and assembly of speech sounds, is a core language function of the left hemisphere, especially the left superior temporal and inferior frontal regions around Wernicke's and Broca's areas. Damage there produces phonemic paraphasias and impaired phonological reading. It tempts because the right hemisphere does process some aspects of sound, such as prosody and music, so candidates may assign every 'sound' function to the right.","c":"Grammar and syntax are among the most strongly left-lateralised functions. Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus is closely tied to syntactic processing, and its damage produces agrammatism, speech stripped of function words and grammatical endings. The right hemisphere has very limited grammar, as split-brain studies showed. It tempts because syntax sounds like an abstract pattern skill, which candidates may wrongly link with the 'holistic' right hemisphere.","d":"Verbal memory, such as learning word lists or stories, depends mainly on the left hemisphere, particularly the left hippocampus and medial temporal lobe. Brenda Milner's temporal lobectomy studies in the 1960s found that left removals tend to impair verbal memory, while right removals impair memory for faces, designs and locations. It tempts because memory seems like a general function, so candidates may not realise it splits by type of material."}},{"q":"Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex is most likely to produce:","concept":"The orbitofrontal cortex (OFC) is the underside of the frontal lobes, sitting just above the eye sockets (orbits). It links the prefrontal cortex with the amygdala, the hypothalamus and the reward circuits, computes the value of rewards and punishments, inhibits impulses and guides behaviour by social rules. The classic case is Phineas Gage, a railway foreman. In 1848 an explosion drove an iron tamping rod through his left frontal lobe. He survived, but his physician John Martyn Harlow (1868) described a man who had become fitful, profane, impatient and unable to keep to plans, so that friends said he was 'no longer Gage'. Hanna Damasio and colleagues (1994) reconstructed the injury and placed it in the ventromedial and orbital prefrontal cortex. Antonio Damasio (1994) described patient Elliot, who after removal of an orbitofrontal tumour kept a normal IQ but made disastrous life decisions; this led to the somatic marker hypothesis. Antoine Bechara and colleagues (1994) created the Iowa Gambling Task, on which such patients keep choosing high-reward, high-loss decks. The orbitofrontal syndrome (disinhibited or pseudopsychopathic type) shows impulsivity, tactless jokes, poor judgement and emotional lability. Contrast the dorsolateral syndrome (apathy, poor planning, perseveration on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test) and the medial or anterior cingulate syndrome (akinetic mutism and apathy). Option d is right because orbitofrontal damage most typically produces disinhibition, impulsivity and impaired social judgement.","traps":{"a":"Pure aphasia means a language disorder without other major deficits. It follows damage to the perisylvian language areas of the dominant hemisphere: Broca's area in the inferior frontal gyrus or Wernicke's area in the posterior superior temporal gyrus. The orbitofrontal cortex lies away from these areas, and its patients usually speak fluently, sometimes too freely. It tempts because both Broca's area and the OFC lie in the frontal lobe.","b":"Cortical blindness is loss of vision caused by bilateral damage to primary visual cortex in the occipital lobes, often from posterior cerebral artery strokes, with normal pupil reflexes. Some patients deny their blindness, a picture called Anton's syndrome. The orbitofrontal cortex lies above the eyes but processes value and social behaviour, not vision. It tempts because the word 'orbit' suggests the eye sockets and therefore sight.","c":"Hemiplegia, paralysis of one side of the body, follows damage to the primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus) or the corticospinal tract, for example in the internal capsule, on the opposite side. The orbitofrontal cortex is part of the prefrontal cortex and does not send direct motor commands. It tempts because the frontal lobe as a whole contains the motor strip, so any frontal lesion may wrongly be linked to weakness."}},{"q":"Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) damage most specifically impairs:","concept":"The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC), roughly Brodmann areas 9 and 46 on the upper outer surface of the frontal lobes, is the main hub for executive function: the control processes that hold goals in mind, plan steps, switch strategies and suppress unwanted responses. Patricia Goldman-Rakic (1987) showed in monkeys that DLPFC neurons keep firing during the delay of a delayed-response task, holding information 'online', which tied the region to working memory as defined by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), especially its central executive. Brenda Milner (1963) showed that patients with dorsolateral frontal lesions perseverate on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) of David A. Grant and Esta A. Berg (1948): they keep sorting by an old rule after it has changed. Other tests sensitive to DLPFC damage include the Tower of London of Tim Shallice (1982) for planning, verbal fluency, the Stroop task and Trail Making Test part B. The dorsolateral syndrome looks like apathy, poor planning, concrete thinking and mental inflexibility, whereas orbitofrontal damage brings disinhibition and poor social judgement, and anterior cingulate damage brings apathy and poor conflict monitoring. In schizophrenia, reduced DLPFC activation during the WCST (hypofrontality) was reported by Daniel Weinberger and colleagues (1986). Long-term declarative memory depends instead on the hippocampus, and colour vision on area V4. Option b is right because DLPFC damage most specifically impairs working memory, planning and executive control.","traps":{"a":"Colour perception depends on the cone cells of the retina and on visual cortex, especially area V4 in the ventral occipitotemporal region, whose colour-selective cells were described by Semir Zeki (1973). Damage there causes cerebral achromatopsia, in which the world looks grey although acuity is fine. The DLPFC is not a visual area. It tempts only as a filler, naming a familiar brain function from a completely different lobe.","c":"Basic motor reflexes, such as the knee jerk or withdrawal from pain, are organised in the spinal cord and brainstem and can work without any cortex at all. In fact, frontal damage can release primitive reflexes such as the grasp reflex, which shows that the cortex normally inhibits them rather than generating them. It tempts because the frontal lobe contains the motor cortex, so candidates link any frontal area with movement.","d":"Long-term declarative memory, the conscious storage of facts and events, depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe for consolidation and on the neocortex for long-term storage, as patient H.M. showed (William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957). The DLPFC supports strategic aspects of memory, such as organising material and working memory, but its damage does not cause amnesia. It tempts because 'memory' appears in both this option and the correct one."}},{"q":"Slow-wave (deep) sleep is characterised on EEG by:","concept":"Sleep is staged from the electroencephalogram (EEG), eye movements and muscle tone. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep, and Allan Rechtschaffen and Anthony Kales (1968) wrote the standard scoring manual, with four non-REM stages plus REM. The American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM, 2007) merged the old stages 3 and 4 into one stage, N3. The waveforms run from fast to slow: beta (above about 13 Hz) in alert, active thinking; alpha (8 to 13 Hz) in relaxed wakefulness with eyes closed; theta (4 to 7 Hz) in stage N1, the drowsy transition; and delta (0.5 to 4 Hz) in deep sleep. Stage N2 is marked by sleep spindles (brief bursts of about 12 to 14 Hz) and K-complexes (large single biphasic waves). N3, slow-wave sleep, is defined by high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves; AASM scoring requires slow waves above 75 microvolts filling at least 20 percent of an epoch. N3 is the hardest stage to wake from. It is most abundant in the first third of the night and in childhood, declines with age, and is when growth hormone release peaks and sleepwalking and sleep terrors tend to occur. REM sleep shows low-voltage, mixed-frequency activity resembling wakefulness, sawtooth waves, rapid eye movements and muscle atonia, and it is most abundant in the last third of the night. Option b is right because slow-wave sleep is defined by high-amplitude delta waves in the 0.5 to 4 Hz range.","traps":{"a":"Sawtooth waves (notched, triangular waves in the theta range) together with rapid eye movements mark REM sleep, first described by Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953). REM also shows low-voltage, mixed-frequency EEG resembling wakefulness and near-total muscle atonia, and most vivid dreaming occurs in it. That is almost the opposite of deep, synchronised slow-wave sleep. It tempts because REM is the best-known sleep stage, and candidates may call any distinctive pattern 'deep sleep'.","c":"Beta waves are fast (above about 13 Hz), low-amplitude and desynchronised, and they dominate alert wakefulness, focused thinking and anxiety; benzodiazepines also increase beta activity. Deep sleep shows the slowest, largest waves, so beta is the reverse of what the stem asks. It tempts mainly as a terminology trap: candidates who confuse the Greek letters may mix up beta with delta, or assume that 'deep' means high frequency.","d":"Sleep spindles (brief bursts of about 12 to 14 Hz) and K-complexes (single large sharp waves, often after a sound) are the defining signs of stage N2, the light sleep that fills about half of an adult night. They can persist into N3, but N3 is defined by delta activity, and the word 'only' rules this out. It tempts because spindles and K-complexes are the most famous named sleep waveforms."}},{"q":"REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is characterised by:","concept":"Normal REM sleep combines an active, dreaming brain with a paralysed body. During REM, circuits in the pons drive inhibitory neurons in the medulla that suppress spinal motor neurons, so skeletal muscles go slack (REM atonia) and we cannot act out our dreams. Michel Jouvet (1965) showed in cats that lesions in the dorsal pons near the locus coeruleus abolished this atonia, so the sleeping animals moved and appeared to attack during REM. In humans, Carlos Schenck and colleagues (1986) described REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD): the atonia fails, and the person punches, kicks, shouts or leaps from bed while enacting vivid, often threatening dreams, sometimes injuring a bed partner. Because it arises from REM, episodes cluster in the second half of the night, and on waking the person can usually recall the dream. Diagnosis needs polysomnography showing REM sleep without atonia. RBD is most common in older men and is a strong early marker of alpha-synucleinopathies: a large share of people with idiopathic RBD later develop Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies or multiple system atrophy. Some antidepressants can trigger it. Treatment is a safe sleeping environment plus melatonin or clonazepam. Distinguish it from non-REM parasomnias (sleepwalking and sleep terrors, arising from N3 early in the night, with little dream recall), from narcolepsy (daytime sleepiness, cataplexy and sleep-onset REM), and from nightmare disorder (frightening dreams without movement). Option b is right because RBD is defined by loss of the normal REM atonia, so the person physically enacts dream content.","traps":{"a":"Complete absence of REM sleep is not a recognised disorder. REM can be reduced by drugs such as many antidepressants or by alcohol, but people with RBD enter REM normally and on time. Their problem is what happens during REM, namely the missing atonia. It tempts because the name 'REM sleep behaviour disorder' sounds as if REM itself is disordered or missing, rather than the motor control that normally accompanies it.","c":"Bedwetting during sleep is enuresis, classed with the elimination disorders in DSM-5. It is common in childhood, and classic accounts linked it to deep non-REM sleep early in the night, though it can occur in any stage. It involves bladder control, not dream enactment, and does not arise from REM. It tempts because it is another night-time problem, and because 'N3' sounds like a technical sleep detail that fits the topic.","d":"Excessive daytime sleepiness without night-time symptoms suggests idiopathic hypersomnia or, with cataplexy and sleep-onset REM periods, narcolepsy, which involves loss of hypocretin (orexin) neurons in the hypothalamus. RBD is defined by night-time dream enactment, so 'without nocturnal symptoms' contradicts it. It tempts because narcolepsy is also a REM-related disorder, with intrusions of REM atonia into wakefulness (cataplexy), the reverse of RBD."}},{"q":"SSRIs typically take 2-4 weeks for clinical antidepressant effect despite rapid synaptic serotonin elevation. The most-supported explanation is:","concept":"Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) such as fluoxetine block the serotonin transporter (SERT), so serotonin (5-HT) stays longer in the synapse. This happens within hours of the first dose, yet mood usually lifts only after two to four weeks or more. The delay was a major problem for the simple monoamine hypothesis of depression, set out by Joseph Schildkraut (1965) for noradrenaline and extended by others to serotonin, which held that depression is simply a shortage of monoamines. Two later explanations fill the gap. First, the autoreceptor account: raised 5-HT around the cell bodies in the raphe nuclei stimulates somatodendritic 5-HT1A autoreceptors, which act as a brake and reduce firing. Over about two weeks these autoreceptors desensitise, firing recovers, and serotonin release in target regions rises. Second, the neurotrophic hypothesis of Ronald Duman and colleagues (1997): chronic antidepressant treatment raises brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF), promotes neurogenesis in the hippocampal dentate gyrus and restores dendritic connections damaged by chronic stress. These slow structural and signalling changes match the clinical time course. Ketamine, an NMDA antagonist, can relieve depressive symptoms within hours, which supports the idea that plasticity, not serotonin level itself, is the final common path. Pharmacokinetic factors such as absorption, entry into the brain and steady-state plasma levels are mostly settled within days, so they cannot explain the lag. Option b is right because the most-supported explanation is slow 5-HT1A autoreceptor desensitisation together with BDNF upregulation and hippocampal neurogenesis.","traps":{"a":"Enzyme induction means a drug increases the liver's production of cytochrome P450 enzymes, speeding the breakdown of itself or other drugs; carbamazepine is the classic psychiatric example. Most SSRIs are enzyme inhibitors rather than inducers (fluoxetine and paroxetine inhibit CYP2D6), and induction would lower drug levels, not create benefit. It tempts because a gradual liver process sounds like a plausible reason for a gradual effect.","c":"SSRIs are small, fat-soluble molecules that cross the blood-brain barrier readily, which is exactly why the stem can say that synaptic serotonin rises rapidly. If the drug could not reach the brain, serotonin would not rise at all, so the stem itself rules this option out. It tempts because the blood-brain barrier is a familiar reason why some drugs act slowly or not at all in the brain.","d":"Most SSRIs reach steady plasma levels within about a week, well before the clinical response appears. Fluoxetine is a partial exception, because its active metabolite norfluoxetine has a very long half-life, but the same lag occurs with SSRIs that lack such metabolites, so metabolite build-up cannot be the general explanation. It tempts because pharmacokinetic accumulation is a real, slow process that sounds scientific."}},{"q":"Lithium is classically associated with which narrow therapeutic concern?","concept":"Lithium is the oldest mood stabiliser. John Cade (1949), an Australian psychiatrist, reported that lithium salts calmed manic patients, and Mogens Schou (1954) confirmed the antimanic effect in Denmark and later showed its value in preventing relapse. It also lowers suicide risk. The central clinical idea is the therapeutic index, the ratio between the toxic dose and the effective dose. Lithium's index is narrow. Usual maintenance serum levels are about 0.6 to 1.0 mmol/L (the same as mEq/L for lithium), with up to about 1.2 sometimes used in acute mania; toxicity often appears above 1.5, and levels above 2.0 are dangerous. So a modest rise can turn treatment into poisoning. Levels are checked about 12 hours after the last dose. Because lithium is excreted almost entirely by the kidneys and handled like sodium, levels rise with dehydration, vomiting, a low-salt diet, thiazide diuretics, ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs. Side effects at therapeutic levels include fine tremor, thirst and polyuria (nephrogenic diabetes insipidus), weight gain, hypothyroidism and raised calcium; use in early pregnancy is linked to Ebstein's anomaly of the heart. Toxicity shows as coarse tremor, vomiting, diarrhoea, ataxia, slurred speech, confusion and seizures. Monitoring therefore covers serum lithium, kidney and thyroid function, and calcium. Valproate (liver toxicity, teratogenic), carbamazepine (enzyme induction, agranulocytosis) and clozapine (constipation, agranulocytosis) have different signature risks. Option a is right because lithium's classic concern is its narrow therapeutic index, with toxic effects appearing close to the therapeutic range.","traps":{"b":"Severe constipation is the signature dose-related problem of clozapine (through its anticholinergic action, sometimes causing fatal bowel obstruction) and of tricyclic antidepressants, not of lithium. Lithium's gut effects are nausea, loose stools and diarrhoea, and diarrhoea with vomiting is an early sign of toxicity. It tempts because constipation is a real side effect of many psychotropic drugs and is often tested with clozapine.","c":"Lithium is cleared almost entirely by the kidneys, so impaired renal function raises serum levels and can quickly cause toxicity. Long-term lithium can itself reduce kidney function and urine-concentrating ability. Renal impairment is therefore a reason for dose reduction, close monitoring or avoiding lithium, never for skipping tests. It tempts only because it is phrased reassuringly; any option claiming a drug is safe 'without monitoring' should raise suspicion.","d":"Lithium is not metabolised by the liver and is not known for acute liver failure. Serious drug-induced liver injury is the classic risk of valproate, especially in young children on several anticonvulsants, and rare liver injury also occurs with carbamazepine. Lithium toxicity is neurological, gastrointestinal and renal. It tempts because candidates who remember that mood stabilisers need blood monitoring may confuse lithium with valproate."}},{"q":"Benzodiazepines and barbiturates both potentiate GABA-A receptor activity but differ in that:","concept":"Both drug classes act at the GABA-A receptor, a ligand-gated chloride channel. When GABA binds, the channel opens, chloride enters, and the neuron is hyperpolarised. Neither class binds the GABA site itself; each binds its own allosteric site and changes how the channel behaves. Benzodiazepines (diazepam, lorazepam, alprazolam, clonazepam) bind at the junction of the alpha and gamma subunits. They are positive allosteric modulators that make the receptor respond more strongly to GABA, so the channel opens more often (increased frequency) when GABA is present. Without GABA they do nothing, which gives a ceiling effect and makes a benzodiazepine overdose taken alone rarely fatal. Flumazenil reverses them by blocking this site. Barbiturates (phenobarbital, thiopental) bind a different site and prolong each opening (increased duration). At high doses they open the channel directly, acting like GABA itself, and they also block excitatory glutamate receptors. They therefore have no ceiling and a low therapeutic index, cause fatal respiratory depression in overdose and strongly induce liver enzymes, which is why benzodiazepines largely replaced them from the 1960s. Alcohol also enhances GABA-A function, so combining any two of these can be lethal, and cross-tolerance explains why benzodiazepines are used to manage alcohol withdrawal. A memory aid is 'frequenzodiazepines' and 'barbidurates'. Z-drugs such as zolpidem act at the benzodiazepine site, and baclofen acts at GABA-B instead. Option a is right because benzodiazepines increase opening frequency and need GABA, whereas barbiturates increase opening duration and can open the channel directly at high doses.","traps":{"b":"GABA-B is a metabotropic, G-protein coupled receptor that opens potassium channels and closes calcium channels; its standard agonist is baclofen, a muscle relaxant. Barbiturates act on GABA-A, the ionotropic chloride channel, as the stem itself says when it states that both drugs potentiate GABA-A. The word 'only' makes the option clearly false. It tempts candidates who expect the difference to be a different receptor subtype.","c":"NMDA receptors are glutamate-gated cation channels involved in excitation and plasticity; drugs acting there include the blockers ketamine, phencyclidine and memantine. Benzodiazepines act at an allosteric site on the GABA-A receptor, which the stem states directly. It tempts because benzodiazepines are used in alcohol withdrawal, a state of glutamate and NMDA overactivity, and because alcohol itself acts at both GABA-A and NMDA receptors.","d":"This option swaps the two mechanisms. Benzodiazepines increase how often the chloride channel opens (frequency); barbiturates increase how long each opening lasts (duration). It is the most dangerous distractor because it uses the correct vocabulary and only reverses the pairing. A quick memory aid is 'frequenzodiazepines' and 'barbidurates', which ties frequency to benzodiazepines and duration to barbiturates."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus most directly involves:","concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) is a lasting increase in the strength of a synapse after brief, intense activity. Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo (1973) found it in the rabbit hippocampus: a short burst of high-frequency stimulation to the perforant path made later single pulses produce larger responses in the dentate gyrus for hours, and in related work for days or weeks. It is the best-studied cellular model of learning and fits the idea of Donald O. Hebb (1949) that connections between co-active neurons grow stronger. At the classic CA1 synapse the steps are: glutamate is released and binds AMPA and NMDA receptors; strong AMPA activity depolarises the postsynaptic cell; depolarisation expels the Mg2+ block from NMDA receptors; calcium flows in; calcium activates enzymes, especially CaMKII; these phosphorylate existing AMPA receptors and drive extra AMPA receptors into the membrane, so the same glutamate release now produces a bigger response. That is early LTP. Late LTP needs new protein synthesis and gene transcription through cAMP, protein kinase A and CREB, and it adds structural change, including enlarged and new dendritic spines. LTP shows input specificity (only active synapses strengthen), cooperativity and associativity (a weak input paired with a strong one is strengthened). Its mirror image, long-term depression (LTD), weakens synapses after low-frequency stimulation, often by removing AMPA receptors. Richard Morris and colleagues (1986) showed that blocking NMDA receptors with AP5 prevents both LTP and spatial learning in the water maze. Option b is right because LTP is triggered by calcium entry through NMDA receptors, which leads to AMPA receptor insertion and stronger synapses.","traps":{"a":"A decrease in dendritic spine density would weaken, not strengthen, synaptic input. LTP is linked to the opposite: enlargement of existing spines and growth of new ones, especially in late LTP that needs protein synthesis. Spine loss is seen with chronic stress, ageing and some neurodegenerative conditions. It tempts because spine density appears in plasticity research, and candidates may remember that synaptic pruning is a normal part of development.","c":"Glutamate is continually recycled. After release it is taken up by transporters on neurons and astrocytes, converted to glutamine in astrocytes and returned to the neuron to be made into glutamate again (the glutamate-glutamine cycle). LTP strengthens transmission, mainly on the postsynaptic side; it does not exhaust transmitter stores. It tempts because intense high-frequency stimulation sounds as if it would use up the transmitter.","d":"Apoptosis is programmed cell death, an orderly self-destruction used in development to remove surplus neurons and also seen after injury. LTP strengthens synapses between living neurons; killing the presynaptic cell would remove the input entirely. Excess calcium entry through NMDA receptors can cause excitotoxic death, but that is a pathology, not LTP. It tempts because both LTP and excitotoxicity begin with the same NMDA calcium signal."}},{"q":"Which hypothalamic nucleus is most directly involved in oxytocin and ADH synthesis?","concept":"The hypothalamus controls the pituitary in two different ways, and examiners test which nuclei go with which route. Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH, also called vasopressin) are made by large magnocellular neurosecretory cells in the supraoptic nucleus (SON), just above the optic chiasm, and the paraventricular nucleus (PVN), beside the third ventricle. Both nuclei make both hormones. The hormones are packaged with carrier proteins (neurophysins) and transported down the axons of the hypothalamo-hypophyseal tract to the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis), which stores and releases them into the blood. The posterior pituitary is therefore nervous tissue and makes no hormones of its own. ADH acts on the collecting ducts of the kidney to save water; lack of it causes central diabetes insipidus, while lithium causes the nephrogenic form, in which the kidney ignores ADH. Oxytocin causes uterine contraction in labour and milk ejection during breastfeeding, and it has been studied for roles in social bonding and trust. By contrast, the anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is a true gland controlled by releasing hormones from small parvocellular neurons, including CRH and TRH from the PVN, carried through the hypophyseal portal blood vessels. Other nuclei that examiners use as distractors: the suprachiasmatic nucleus (circadian clock), the arcuate nucleus (appetite, and dopamine that inhibits prolactin), the ventromedial nucleus (satiety), the lateral hypothalamus (hunger, orexin) and the mammillary bodies (memory, Papez circuit). Option c is right because oxytocin and ADH are synthesised in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei and then released from the posterior pituitary.","traps":{"a":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), a tiny paired nucleus just above the optic chiasm, is the master circadian clock. It receives light information from the retina through the retinohypothalamic tract and times the night-time release of melatonin from the pineal gland. It does not make oxytocin or ADH. It tempts because 'suprachiasmatic' and 'supraoptic' both refer to the optic crossing and lie close together, so the names are easily confused.","b":"The mammillary bodies are paired nuclei at the back of the hypothalamus. They receive input from the hippocampus through the fornix and project to the anterior thalamus, forming part of the Papez circuit (James Papez, 1937); damage, as in Korsakoff's syndrome from thiamine deficiency, causes amnesia. They have no role in hormone synthesis. It tempts because they belong to the hypothalamus and are among its best-known named structures.","d":"The arcuate nucleus, at the base of the hypothalamus near the median eminence, regulates appetite through neurons that respond to leptin and ghrelin. Its dopamine neurons (the tuberoinfundibular pathway) inhibit prolactin release, which is why antipsychotics that block dopamine raise prolactin. It acts on the anterior pituitary, not the posterior. It tempts because it is a well-known hypothalamic nucleus that is directly involved in hormone control."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is synthesised from the amino acid tryptophan?","concept":"Neurotransmitters are built from simple dietary precursors, and exam questions test which precursor goes with which transmitter. Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) is made from the essential amino acid tryptophan in two steps. First, tryptophan hydroxylase adds a hydroxyl group to form 5-hydroxytryptophan (5-HTP); this is the rate-limiting step. Second, aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase removes a carboxyl group to give serotonin. In the pineal gland serotonin is converted further into melatonin. Serotonin neurons arise mainly in the raphe nuclei of the brainstem, although most of the body's serotonin is in the gut. It is broken down by monoamine oxidase A into 5-HIAA, whose low level in cerebrospinal fluid was linked to violent suicide attempts by Marie Asberg and colleagues (1976). A tryptophan-free drink (tryptophan depletion) can briefly bring back low mood in recovered depressed patients. The other pathways: tyrosine (made from phenylalanine) is converted by tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, into L-DOPA, then into dopamine, then noradrenaline (by dopamine beta-hydroxylase) and adrenaline; these are the catecholamines. Glutamate is converted to GABA by glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD). Acetylcholine is made from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase and broken down by acetylcholinesterase. Histamine comes from histidine. Phenylketonuria, an inherited block in converting phenylalanine to tyrosine, shows how a precursor defect can harm brain development. Option d is right because serotonin is the neurotransmitter synthesised from tryptophan, by way of 5-HTP.","traps":{"a":"Acetylcholine is made from choline (from the diet and from recycled acetylcholine) and acetyl coenzyme A by choline acetyltransferase, and it is broken down in the synapse by acetylcholinesterase. It is not derived from an amino acid. It acts at nicotinic and muscarinic receptors and is lost early in Alzheimer's disease, hence cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil. It tempts as a familiar transmitter whose precursor many candidates have not memorised.","b":"Dopamine is a catecholamine made from the amino acid tyrosine. Tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, converts tyrosine to L-DOPA, and a decarboxylase then makes dopamine, which can be converted further to noradrenaline and adrenaline. L-DOPA, not dopamine, is given in Parkinson's disease because it crosses the blood-brain barrier. It tempts because tyrosine and tryptophan are both aromatic amino acids starting with 't', and the same decarboxylase acts in both pathways.","c":"GABA is made from glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter, by glutamic acid decarboxylase (GAD), which needs vitamin B6. So the brain's chief inhibitory transmitter is built from its chief excitatory one. GABA is broken down by GABA transaminase, the target of the anticonvulsant vigabatrin. It does not come from tryptophan. It tempts because GABA is itself an amino acid transmitter, so candidates may assume it comes from a dietary amino acid like tryptophan."}},{"q":"Erikson's first psychosocial stage (0-18 months) involves the conflict of:","concept":"Erik H. Erikson, a German-born psychoanalyst trained in Vienna under Anna Freud, set out eight psychosocial stages in Childhood and Society (1950). He kept Sigmund Freud's idea of developmental stages but shifted the focus from sexual drives to social relationships, and he extended development across the whole lifespan. Each stage poses a crisis between two opposite outcomes; a favourable resolution gives a basic strength (virtue), and the epigenetic principle says each stage builds on the ones before. Stage one, trust versus mistrust, covers roughly birth to 18 months and parallels Freud's oral stage. The infant depends completely on caregivers. When feeding, comfort and care are consistent and responsive, the infant develops basic trust: the sense that the world is safe and people are reliable. Unpredictable or neglectful care breeds mistrust. The virtue is hope. The full order, with typical ages, is: trust versus mistrust (infancy, hope); autonomy versus shame and doubt (about 18 months to 3 years, will); initiative versus guilt (3 to 6 years, purpose); industry versus inferiority (6 to 12, competence); identity versus role confusion (adolescence, fidelity); intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood, love); generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood, care); and integrity versus despair (late adulthood, wisdom). James Marcia (1966) later expanded the identity stage into four identity statuses. The trust stage also links with the attachment theory of John Bowlby (1969), which likewise stresses sensitive early care. Option a is right because Erikson's first stage, from birth to about 18 months, is the conflict of trust versus mistrust.","traps":{"b":"Initiative versus guilt is Erikson's third stage, roughly ages 3 to 6, parallel to Freud's phallic stage. Preschoolers plan activities, play make-believe and take on tasks; encouragement builds initiative and the virtue of purpose, while harsh criticism leaves guilt about their own goals. It tempts because 'guilt', like 'mistrust', sounds like an early negative feeling, and because the stages are easy to scramble in a fixed list.","c":"Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, roughly ages 6 to 12, the school years, parallel to Freud's latency period. Children learn skills and compare their work with peers; success brings competence, while repeated failure brings feelings of inferiority. It is far from infancy. It tempts candidates who remember the stage names as a list but not their order, and because 'inferiority' sounds like a basic early feeling.","d":"Autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, roughly 18 months to 3 years, parallel to Freud's anal stage. Toddlers learn to walk, feed themselves and use the toilet; support for independence builds will, while over-control or ridicule leads to shame and doubt. It tempts most because it immediately follows trust versus mistrust, and its age range begins exactly where the first stage ends."}},{"q":"In Piaget's theory, object permanence is acquired during the:","concept":"Jean Piaget proposed four stages of cognitive development and described the infant's grasp of objects in The Construction of Reality in the Child (1937 in French). The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is when infants know the world through senses and actions; the preoperational stage (about 2 to 7) brings symbols and language, with egocentrism and failure of conservation; the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) brings logical operations on concrete objects, including conservation and classification; and the formal operational stage (from about 11 or 12) brings abstract, hypothetical reasoning. Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist when they cannot be seen, heard or touched. Piaget divided the sensorimotor stage into six substages. Early on, a toy covered by a cloth is treated as gone. In substage 4 (about 8 to 12 months) the infant first searches for a hidden object but makes the A-not-B error: after finding a toy several times at place A, the infant searches at A even after watching it hidden at B. In substage 5 (12 to 18 months) visible moves are followed, and in substage 6 (18 to 24 months) the child can infer invisible displacements, showing full mental representation. Later research challenged the timing. Renee Baillargeon (1987), using violation-of-expectation looking-time methods (the drawbridge study), found signs of object permanence at 3.5 to 4.5 months, suggesting Piaget underestimated infants because his tasks required manual search. Option c is right because Piaget placed the acquisition of object permanence in the sensorimotor stage, emerging around 8 to 12 months.","traps":{"a":"The concrete operational stage, roughly 7 to 11 years, is when children master conservation (knowing that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape), reversibility, classification and seriation, but only for concrete, present objects. By then object permanence has been secure for years. It tempts because conservation and object permanence both concern things 'staying the same', so candidates may confuse the two achievements.","b":"The formal operational stage begins around 11 or 12 years and brings abstract, hypothetical-deductive reasoning, as in the pendulum task of Barbel Inhelder and Jean Piaget (1955), where adolescents test one variable at a time. It is the final stage, far beyond infancy, and many adults do not use formal operations consistently. It tempts only candidates who think object permanence is an abstract idea requiring advanced reasoning.","d":"The preoperational stage, roughly 2 to 7 years, is marked by symbolic thought, language and pretend play, along with egocentrism (the three mountains task), centration, animism and failure on conservation tasks. Object permanence is a prerequisite for this symbolic thinking, so it must already be in place by about age 2. It tempts because 'preoperational' sounds like an early or basic stage, and age 4 seems a plausible milestone."}},{"q":"Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to:","concept":"Lev S. Vygotsky (1896 to 1934), a Russian psychologist, argued that higher mental functions develop first between people and only later inside the individual, through language and cultural tools. His work reached English readers mainly through Thought and Language (1962) and Mind in Society (1978). The zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by independent problem solving, and the level of potential development, shown by problem solving with guidance from an adult or a more capable peer (the more knowledgeable other). Tasks the child can already do alone lie below the zone; tasks the child cannot do even with help lie above it. Teaching is most useful inside the zone, where it leads development rather than waiting for it. Vygotsky also used the ZPD to argue that two children with the same test score may differ in how much they can gain with help, an idea behind dynamic assessment. The term scaffolding was introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976) for the temporary support an adult gives and gradually withdraws as competence grows; Vygotsky himself did not use it. Contrast this with Jean Piaget, who saw the child as a lone scientist whose learning must wait for cognitive readiness, and note private speech, which Vygotsky saw as self-guidance that later becomes inner speech. Option d is right because the ZPD is the gap between what a child can do independently and what the child can do with guidance from a more knowledgeable other.","traps":{"a":"The peer group that most shapes identity relates to adolescent socialisation, to the identity versus role confusion stage of Erik Erikson (1950), and to the group socialisation theory of Judith Rich Harris (1995). Vygotsky did value peers, but only as one kind of more capable partner within the ZPD; the zone is a range of tasks, not a group of people. It tempts because the ZPD is a social concept and the more knowledgeable other can be a peer.","b":"An age window during which language can be acquired is the critical period hypothesis of Eric Lenneberg (1967), who argued that language learning is easiest before puberty because of brain maturation; the case of Genie is often cited. This is a biological, age-based idea, whereas the ZPD is a learning range that exists at every age and for every skill. It tempts because both concepts describe a window of learning potential.","c":"The ZPD is a psychological and educational concept describing a range of tasks, not a place in the brain. Questions about where developmental disorders originate belong to developmental neuroscience, for example frontal and striatal circuits in ADHD. Vygotsky did study children with disabilities (his 'defectology'), but the ZPD is defined by performance with and without help. It tempts only because the word 'zone' sounds anatomical, like a brain region."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958), building on the work of Jean Piaget (1932) on children's moral judgement, studied moral reasoning by presenting moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma: should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife? Kohlberg scored not the answer (steal or not) but the reasoning behind it. He described three levels, each with two stages, passed through in a fixed order. The pre-conventional level, typical of young children, judges right and wrong by external consequences to oneself. Stage 1, punishment and obedience orientation: an act is wrong if it gets punished, and authority must be obeyed. Stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange (individualism): right is what serves one's own interests, with fair deals of the 'you scratch my back' kind. The conventional level, typical of older children, adolescents and many adults, judges by social expectations: Stage 3, the good boy or good girl orientation, seeking approval and conforming to the norms of family and peers; Stage 4, law and order, maintaining social order and doing one's duty. The post-conventional level, reached by a minority: Stage 5, social contract and individual rights, where laws can be changed for the common good; Stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory for male bias and proposed an ethic of care alongside Kohlberg's ethic of justice, and others questioned its cross-cultural fit. Option c is right because pre-conventional reasoning bases moral judgement on avoiding punishment and obtaining rewards.","traps":{"a":"Conformity to the norms of peers and others close to the person defines Kohlberg's Stage 3, the good boy or good girl (interpersonal concordance) orientation, which is the first stage of the conventional level. The reasoning is about being seen as good and gaining approval, not about avoiding punishment. It tempts because peer conformity looks immature, so candidates may assume it belongs to the earliest level.","b":"Maintaining social order and obeying the law for its own sake is Kohlberg's Stage 4, the law and order orientation, the second stage of the conventional level. The person reasons from duty and the need to keep society working, for example 'if everyone stole, there would be chaos'. It tempts because obedience to rules sounds like Stage 1 obedience, but Stage 1 obeys to avoid punishment, not to protect the social system.","d":"Universal ethical principles are Kohlberg's Stage 6, the top of the post-conventional level. The person follows self-chosen, abstract principles such as justice, equality and human dignity, even against the law. Kohlberg found it so rarely that his later scoring manual dropped it as a separately scored stage. It tempts because it is the most memorable stage and the wording sounds admirable, but it is the opposite end of the sequence."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress on separation, ignores the caregiver on reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver similarly is classified as:","concept":"Mary D. S. Ainsworth, working within John Bowlby's attachment theory, designed the Strange Situation with Barbara Wittig (1969) to measure attachment quality in infants of about 12 to 18 months. It has eight short episodes, most about three minutes long, in an unfamiliar playroom: the infant explores with the caregiver present, a stranger enters, the caregiver leaves and returns, the infant is left alone, the stranger returns, and the caregiver returns again. Coders watch separation distress, stranger anxiety, use of the caregiver as a secure base and, above all, behaviour at reunion. Ainsworth, Mary Blehar, Everett Waters and Sally Wall (1978) reported three patterns in their Baltimore sample. Secure (Type B, about two thirds): the infant explores freely, may be upset at separation, seeks contact at reunion and is quickly comforted. Insecure-avoidant (Type A, about a fifth): little visible distress at separation, ignores or turns away from the caregiver at reunion, and treats the stranger much like the caregiver. Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C, about a tenth): very distressed at separation, then both seeks and angrily resists contact and is hard to soothe. Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) added disorganised (Type D): contradictory, frozen or fearful behaviour with no coherent strategy. Ainsworth tied the patterns to caregiver sensitivity: avoidance follows consistently rejecting or unresponsive care, resistance follows inconsistent care. Avoidant infants are not truly calm; they show physiological arousal while masking it. Option b is right because little separation distress, ignoring the caregiver at reunion and treating stranger and caregiver alike define the insecure-avoidant (Type A) pattern.","traps":{"a":"Disorganised attachment (Type D) was identified by Mary Main and Judith Solomon (1986) among infants who did not fit Ainsworth's three groups. These infants show contradictory or odd behaviour at reunion, such as approaching with the head turned away, freezing or looking dazed, and seem frightened of the caregiver. It is linked to maltreatment and unresolved parental trauma. It tempts because it is the newest category and often appears in exam stems.","c":"Insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C) infants are very upset at separation, wary of the stranger and hard to comfort at reunion: they seek contact but then push away, kick or cling angrily. The pattern is linked to inconsistent caregiving. It is the opposite of the stem's calm, indifferent infant. It tempts because both A and C are insecure types, and candidates often swap the letters or the labels.","d":"Secure (Type B) infants, about two thirds of Ainsworth's sample, use the caregiver as a secure base for exploration, may protest at separation, and actively greet or seek contact at reunion before settling back to play. They clearly prefer the caregiver to the stranger. It tempts because the stem's infant looks calm and independent, and lack of distress can be misread as healthy confidence rather than avoidance."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of the internal working model refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby, a British psychiatrist and psychoanalyst, developed attachment theory in his trilogy Attachment and Loss, beginning with Attachment (1969). He argued that infants are biologically prepared to seek closeness to a caregiver for protection, an evolutionary idea shaped by ethology such as the imprinting work of Konrad Lorenz (1935). To explain why early attachment experiences have lasting effects, Bowlby borrowed the idea of internal models from Kenneth Craik (1943), who proposed that the mind builds small-scale models of reality to predict events. In Bowlby's sense, the internal working model is a mental representation built from repeated interactions with attachment figures. It has complementary parts: a model of the self (am I worthy of love and care?), a model of others (are attachment figures available and responsive?), and expectations about how relationships work. A child who receives sensitive care comes to expect that others will help and that the self is lovable; a rejected child may expect the opposite. These models operate mostly outside awareness, guide behaviour in new relationships and tend to be stable, which is how Bowlby explained continuity from infancy to adult relationships. Later work includes the Adult Attachment Interview of Mary Main and colleagues (1985), adult romantic attachment by Cindy Hazan and Phillip Shaver (1987), and the four-category model of Kim Bartholomew and Leonard Horowitz (1991), which crosses views of self and others. Option c is right because the internal working model is a mental representation of self, attachment figures and relationships, formed in infancy and guiding later attachment behaviour.","traps":{"a":"A cognitive map is an internal representation of the layout of an environment, proposed by Edward C. Tolman (1948) after rats in mazes learned routes and shortcuts without reinforcement (latent learning). John O'Keefe and Jonathan Dostrovsky (1971) later found hippocampal place cells, seen as a basis for such maps. It concerns physical space, not relationships. It tempts because both are mental models built from experience.","b":"The neural basis of language acquisition involves left-hemisphere regions such as Broca's and Wernicke's areas, and Noam Chomsky (1965) proposed an innate language acquisition device. These ideas concern how children learn words and grammar, not how they represent attachment relationships. It tempts only because both topics appear in developmental psychology and both describe something built in early childhood, so 'model' and 'acquisition' can blur together.","d":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist when out of sight, which Jean Piaget (1937) placed in the sensorimotor stage, emerging around 8 to 12 months. It is a cognitive milestone rather than a model of relationships, though the two are related: an infant must grasp that the caregiver still exists when absent before separation protest makes sense. It tempts because both are early mental representations formed in infancy."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind (1966, 1967), an American developmental psychologist at Berkeley, observed preschool children and their parents and identified three parenting styles, defined by how parents combine control and warmth. Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) restated them as two independent dimensions: demandingness (expectations, rules, supervision, consistent discipline) and responsiveness (warmth, acceptance, attention to the child's needs). Crossing the two gives four styles. Authoritative parents are high on both: they set clear, firm limits and explain the reasons, encourage discussion and independence, and are warm and supportive. Authoritarian parents are high in demandingness but low in responsiveness: rules are enforced by power with 'because I said so', little warmth and little explanation. Permissive (indulgent) parents are high in responsiveness but low in demandingness: warm and accepting but with few rules. Maccoby and Martin added the fourth cell, neglectful or uninvolved parents, low on both. In Western studies authoritative parenting predicts the best outcomes: self-reliance, social competence, self-control and school achievement. Authoritarian parenting is linked to anxious, withdrawn or less socially skilled children, permissive parenting to impulsivity and poor self-control, and neglectful parenting to the worst outcomes. Cross-cultural work, such as the study of Chinese American families by Ruth Chao (1994), suggests that firm control may carry a different meaning where it is seen as care, so the effects of strict parenting are not identical everywhere. Do not confuse authoritative with authoritarian; the words differ by a few letters but sit in opposite cells for warmth. Option b is right because authoritative parenting is defined by high demandingness combined with high responsiveness.","traps":{"a":"Low demandingness with high responsiveness is the permissive or indulgent style. These parents are warm, accepting and child-centred but set few rules and rarely enforce limits, treating the child almost as a peer. Their children tend to be impulsive, less self-controlled and sometimes lower achieving. It tempts because high warmth is the attractive half of authoritative parenting, so candidates who focus only on warmth pick this.","c":"Low demandingness with low responsiveness is the neglectful or uninvolved style, the fourth cell added by Eleanor Maccoby and John Martin (1983) to Baumrind's original three. These parents provide little warmth, supervision or structure, often because of stress, depression or substance use. It is linked to the poorest outcomes, including behaviour problems and poor achievement. It tempts only as the leftover cell of the two-by-two table.","d":"High demandingness with low responsiveness is the authoritarian style. These parents value obedience, enforce strict rules with punishment and little explanation, and show limited warmth. Children tend to be obedient but anxious, withdrawn or less socially skilled. It is the most tempting distractor because 'authoritarian' and 'authoritative' look almost identical and both share high demandingness; only responsiveness separates them."}},{"q":"The concept of 'goodness of fit' in temperament research (Thomas & Chess) refers to:","concept":"Goodness of fit comes from the New York Longitudinal Study (NYLS), begun in 1956 by the psychiatrists Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess with Herbert Birch, which followed a group of children from infancy into adult life using detailed parent interviews. They rated infants on nine temperament dimensions: activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span with persistence. From these they described three types: easy children (about 40 percent; regular, positive, adaptable), difficult children (about 10 percent; irregular, intense, negative, slow to adapt) and slow-to-warm-up children (about 15 percent; mildly negative, cautious, slow to adapt), with roughly a third fitting no type. Their central finding was that temperament alone did not predict later problems. What mattered was goodness of fit: the match between the child's temperament and the demands, expectations and responses of parents, school and culture. A difficult infant with calm, consistent, patient parents often did well; the same infant with impatient or punitive parents was at higher risk of behaviour problems. Poor fit, not the trait itself, creates the stress. Thomas and Chess set this out in Temperament and Development (1977). Examiners pair this with neighbouring ideas: Jerome Kagan's behavioural inhibition, the EAS model (emotionality, activity, sociability) of Arnold Buss and Robert Plomin, Mary Rothbart's reactivity and self-regulation, and the gene-environment correlations of Sandra Scarr and Kathleen McCartney (1983). Option a is right because goodness of fit is exactly the match between a child's temperament and what the environment or caregiver demands.","traps":{"b":"The IQ difference between identical twins belongs to behaviour genetics. Twin studies, pioneered by Francis Galton and later refined in designs such as the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart led by Thomas Bouchard, compare monozygotic and dizygotic twins to estimate heritability. They ask how much variation is genetic, not how well a temperament suits a caregiver. It tempts because temperament is partly heritable and twin studies sit in the same chapter.","c":"The number of siblings is a family-structure variable, studied in work on birth order, which Alfred Adler emphasised, and on family size and intelligence. It is a fixed demographic count, not a relationship between a child's temperament and environmental demands. It tempts because family environment is part of the 'environment' in goodness of fit, but fit concerns how a child's style matches expectations, not how many children live in the home.","d":"Genetic concordance is the probability that two related people share a trait or disorder, used in family and twin studies; for example, concordance for schizophrenia is much higher in monozygotic than in dizygotic twins. It measures shared genes, not the fit between temperament and caregiving. It tempts because 'fit' sounds like a match between parent and child, but goodness of fit concerns the child's behaviour and the environment's demands, not genetic similarity."}},{"q":"Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow (1958), in his American Psychological Association presidential address 'The Nature of Love', reported experiments with infant rhesus monkeys separated from their mothers soon after birth and reared with two surrogate mothers: one made of bare wire mesh and one covered in soft terry cloth. In the key condition only the wire mother held the feeding bottle. If attachment came from feeding, the infants should have bonded to the wire mother. Instead they spent most of their time clinging to the cloth mother and went to the wire one only to feed. When frightened by a novel object, such as a mechanical toy, they ran to the cloth mother and, once comforted, began to explore, using her as a secure base. Harlow called this contact comfort. The result challenged the drive-reduction or 'cupboard love' view, held by behaviourists and implied by Sigmund Freud's oral-stage account, that the infant loves the mother because she satisfies hunger. It supported John Bowlby (1969), who argued that attachment is an evolved system for keeping close to a protector, and fits Mary Ainsworth's secure base idea, tested in the Strange Situation (1978). Harlow's later isolation studies showed severe, lasting damage: rocking, self-clasping, fearfulness, poor social and sexual behaviour, and neglectful or abusive mothering by isolated females (the 'motherless mothers'). Keep Harlow separate from Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in birds and from Bowlby's maternal deprivation hypothesis. Option b is right because the cloth-versus-wire experiments showed that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of attachment.","traps":{"a":"Imprinting is the rapid early bond that Konrad Lorenz (1935) described in precocial birds such as greylag geese, which follow the first moving object they see during a short critical period after hatching. Primates show no such fixed window of hours; their attachment develops over months. Harlow's work was about the basis of the bond, not its timing. It tempts because imprinting and Harlow appear together in every attachment chapter.","c":"This is the reverse of what Harlow found. Monkeys reared in partial or total isolation developed rocking, self-biting, extreme fear, social withdrawal and abnormal sexual behaviour, and isolated females who later had infants often neglected or attacked them. The damage was worse with earlier and longer isolation. It tempts only as a careless misreading, because the cloth mother study shows that a substitute could ease some distress.","d":"Operant conditioning, developed by B. F. Skinner (1938), explains behaviour by its consequences, and the related drive-reduction view says the infant bonds with whoever reduces its hunger, so food acts as the reinforcer. Harlow's results contradicted this: the wire mother gave milk yet received little attachment. Learning still shapes attachment behaviour, but it does not fully explain it. It tempts because behaviourism dominated psychology when Harlow worked."}},{"q":"Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best demonstrated by:","concept":"Konrad Lorenz, the Austrian ethologist, described imprinting in his 1935 paper on the companion in the bird's world, building on observations by his teacher Oskar Heinroth. In the textbook demonstration he divided a clutch of greylag goose eggs: half were hatched by the mother goose and half in an incubator, where the first moving thing the goslings saw was Lorenz himself. These goslings followed him everywhere and, when the two groups were mixed, each ran to its own 'mother'. Imprinting has four classic features: it happens in species that are mobile at birth (precocial birds such as geese, ducks and chicks); it occurs within a critical period shortly after hatching, and Eckhard Hess later placed the peak at about 13 to 16 hours in mallard ducklings; it forms quickly without any reward; and Lorenz held it to be irreversible. He also described sexual imprinting: birds imprinted on humans later courted humans. Later research showed that the window is less rigid and the bond can be partly reversed, so many textbooks now prefer the term sensitive period. Lorenz shared the 1973 Nobel Prize with Nikolaas Tinbergen and Karl von Frisch. Distinguish imprinting from habituation (a non-associative decline in response), from classical conditioning (which needs pairing of stimuli), and from human attachment, which John Bowlby (1969) modelled partly on imprinting but which forms over months, as Harry Harlow (1958) showed in monkeys. Option b is right because it describes exactly what Lorenz observed: goslings forming a rapid, lasting following response to the first moving object during a critical period.","traps":{"a":"Habituation is a decrease in response to a stimulus that is repeated without consequence, such as no longer noticing a ticking clock. It is the simplest form of non-associative learning and is widely used in infant research, where looking time falls as a picture is repeated. It involves no bond with an object and no critical period. It tempts because, like imprinting, it is a basic early form of learning seen in animals.","c":"Preferring same-sex playmates around ages 3 to 5 is gender segregation in play, studied by Eleanor Maccoby. It is explained by socialisation, by similarity of play styles and by gender schema accounts such as Sandra Bem's (1981) gender schema theory. It is a gradual social preference in children, not an instant, critical-period bond in birds. It tempts because it also describes an early social preference.","d":"Reflexive or endogenous smiling appears in newborns, often during sleep, and is driven by internal states rather than by people. The social smile, directed at faces, emerges at about six to eight weeks. Neither is imprinting: reflexive smiling is an inborn reflex with no learning, no object and no critical period. It tempts because John Bowlby treated smiling as an attachment behaviour, so it sits near imprinting in the syllabus."}},{"q":"Theory of Mind, classically tested with the false-belief task (Sally-Anne), typically emerges around:","concept":"Theory of mind is the ability to attribute mental states, such as beliefs, desires and intentions, to oneself and others, and to understand that another person's belief can differ from reality. The term comes from David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978), who asked whether chimpanzees have one. The standard test is the false-belief task, introduced by Heinz Wimmer and Josef Perner (1983) with a story about Maxi and his chocolate. Simon Baron-Cohen, Alan Leslie and Uta Frith (1985) created the Sally-Anne version: Sally puts a marble in her basket and leaves; Anne moves it to her box; the child is asked where Sally will look. Passing means saying 'the basket', answering from Sally's false belief rather than from reality. Most typical children pass at about age 4, within Jean Piaget's preoperational stage (2 to 7 years); 3-year-olds usually point to where the marble really is. In the 1985 study most children with Down syndrome passed, but most autistic children failed despite higher verbal mental age, which led to the 'mindblindness' account of autism. Variants include the Smarties (unexpected contents) task and second-order false belief ('John thinks that Mary thinks'), passed at around 6 to 7. Looking-time studies suggest implicit sensitivity to false belief in the second year, but explicit verbal success arrives around 4. Do not confuse theory of mind with Piaget's three mountains task (visual perspective) or with adolescent egocentrism. Option a is right because explicit false-belief understanding, as measured by the Sally-Anne task, typically emerges around age 4, in the preoperational period.","traps":{"b":"Around 12 months infants show joint attention, following another person's gaze or point, and social referencing, checking a parent's face before approaching something new. These are precursors of theory of mind, and looking-time studies such as Kristine Onishi and Renee Baillargeon (2005) suggest implicit sensitivity by about 15 months. Explicit false-belief reasoning, which the Sally-Anne task demands, is not present at 12 months. It tempts because infant social cognition research is famous.","c":"Age 8 falls in Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11), marked by conservation, reversibility, seriation and classification. By then children have usually passed first-order false belief for years and are handling second-order beliefs (what one person thinks another thinks), mastered around 6 to 7. So 8 is too late for emergence. It tempts because Piaget said egocentrism declines as concrete operations arrive.","d":"Adolescence brings Piaget's formal operations, David Elkind's (1967) adolescent egocentrism with its imaginary audience and personal fable, and Robert Selman's higher levels of mutual and societal perspective-taking. These are refinements of social understanding, not the first appearance of theory of mind, which is already present in preschoolers. It tempts because perspective-taking keeps developing through the teenage years."}},{"q":"Marcia's identity statuses are defined by two dimensions:","concept":"James Marcia (1966) turned Erik Erikson's fifth psychosocial stage, identity versus role confusion, into something measurable. Using a semi-structured identity status interview with college students, he asked about their commitments in occupation and ideology (religious and political beliefs), and scored two dimensions. Crisis, later renamed exploration, is a period of actively questioning and trying out alternatives. Commitment is a firm personal investment in a chosen set of goals, values and beliefs. Crossing the two gives four statuses. Identity diffusion: no exploration and no commitment; the person drifts and seems uninterested. Foreclosure: commitment without exploration; values are taken over from parents or other authorities, as when a son simply follows the family profession. Moratorium: exploration under way but no commitment yet; the person is actively searching, often anxious. Identity achievement: exploration completed and commitment made. The word moratorium comes from Erikson's psychosocial moratorium, a socially permitted delay of adult commitments. The statuses are not fixed stages; a common route is from diffusion or foreclosure through moratorium to achievement, and adults can cycle back into exploration later in life (the so-called MAMA cycles). Foreclosed people tend to be more conforming and authority-oriented, while achieved people tend to show higher self-esteem and better coping. Exam variants ask which status has commitment without crisis (foreclosure) or crisis without commitment (moratorium), or mix Marcia's statuses with Erikson's own stage names. Option b is right because Marcia defined his four identity statuses by the presence or absence of crisis (exploration) and commitment.","traps":{"a":"IQ, the intelligence quotient introduced by William Stern (1912) as mental age divided by chronological age, and emotional intelligence, defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995), are ability constructs. Marcia did not classify identity by ability at all; a highly intelligent person can still be foreclosed or diffused. It tempts because both are well-known paired constructs in the individual differences chapter.","c":"Locus of control is Julian Rotter's (1966) dimension describing whether people believe outcomes depend on their own actions (internal) or on luck, fate and powerful others (external). It was measured by his I-E Scale and grew out of social learning theory. It is a generalised expectancy, not a description of identity formation. It tempts because Rotter's paper appeared in the same year as Marcia's and both are two-pole dimensions.","d":"Approach and avoidance describe motivation toward desired goals or away from threats. Kurt Lewin built his types of conflict on them (approach-approach, avoidance-avoidance, approach-avoidance), and Jeffrey Gray's behavioural approach and inhibition systems are a biological version. These explain choice and conflict, not identity status. It tempts because a foreclosed or diffused adolescent can look avoidant, but Marcia never used this dimension."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking describe progressive shifts from:","concept":"Robert Selman (1980), in The Growth of Interpersonal Understanding, described how children learn to take the viewpoint of other people. He presented social dilemmas, the best known being Holly, an eight-year-old who has promised her father not to climb trees but then finds a friend's kitten stuck in one, and asked how each character would think and feel. From the answers he described five levels. Level 0, undifferentiated or egocentric perspective-taking (roughly 3 to 6 years): the child does not separate their own view from another's. Level 1, social-informational: the child knows others may think differently, but only because they have different information. Level 2, self-reflective or reciprocal: the child can step into another's shoes and knows the other can do the same. Level 3, third-person or mutual: the child can step outside a two-person interaction and see it as a neutral observer would. Level 4, societal or in-depth: in adolescence, perspectives are understood within a wider social system of norms and laws. Age bands differ across textbooks, so learn the order. Selman built on Jean Piaget's idea that early thought is egocentric and that egocentrism declines with age, and he linked perspective-taking to Lawrence Kohlberg's moral stages, arguing that each perspective-taking level is necessary but not sufficient for the matching moral stage. His work also overlaps with theory of mind research, which focuses on false-belief understanding around age 4. Option b is right because Selman's levels trace a shift from egocentric perspective-taking to mutual and finally societal coordination of multiple viewpoints.","traps":{"a":"Moving from concrete to abstract reasoning describes Jean Piaget's step from the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11) to the formal operational stage (about 11 onward), where hypothetical and abstract thought becomes possible. That is a change in logical thinking about objects and propositions, not about other people's viewpoints. It tempts because Selman's levels run parallel to Piaget's stages, and both describe movement away from concrete, self-bound thinking.","c":"Inductive reasoning moves from specific cases to a general rule; deductive reasoning moves from a general premise to a specific conclusion. Piaget saw concrete operational children as mainly inductive and formal operational adolescents as capable of hypothetico-deductive reasoning. That is the development of logic, not of social understanding. It tempts because it sounds like a developmental sequence and appears in the same cognitive development chapter.","d":"Declarative memory (knowing that: facts and events) and procedural memory (knowing how: skills and habits) were distinguished by Neal Cohen and Larry Squire (1980), using amnesic patients who learned a mirror-reading skill they could not remember learning. This is a split between memory systems, not a developmental sequence, and it says nothing about understanding other people. It tempts as a familiar contrast in cognitive psychology."}},{"q":"Erikson's psychosocial conflict of late adulthood is:","concept":"Erik Erikson (1950), in Childhood and Society, proposed eight psychosocial stages covering the whole life span. Each stage is a crisis or conflict between two poles, and a favourable resolution yields a basic virtue. The stages unfold according to the epigenetic principle: each has its own time of special importance but builds on earlier ones. The sequence is trust versus mistrust (infancy; hope), autonomy versus shame and doubt (early childhood; will), initiative versus guilt (preschool; purpose), industry versus inferiority (school age; competence), identity versus role confusion (adolescence; fidelity), intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood; love), generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood; care), and ego integrity versus despair (late adulthood, roughly 65 onward; wisdom). In the final stage the older person looks back over life. Integrity is accepting one's one and only life as it was, failures included, as meaningful; despair is bitterness, regret and the feeling that time is too short to start again, often with fear of death. Robert Butler's (1963) life review, the basis of reminiscence therapy, describes the same process. Joan Erikson later added a ninth stage for very old age. A common trick is to list the stages out of order or to swap the adult stages: remember intimacy for young adults, generativity (caring for the next generation through children, work or mentoring) for the middle years, and integrity at the end. Another trick is to pair a stage with the wrong virtue. Option b is right because ego integrity versus despair is Erikson's eighth and final conflict, belonging to late adulthood.","traps":{"a":"Identity versus role confusion is Erikson's fifth stage, in adolescence. The young person must build a coherent sense of who they are across roles, values and occupation; success gives the virtue of fidelity, and failure leaves role confusion. James Marcia (1966) later measured it through four identity statuses. It tempts because it is the most famous and most frequently examined of Erikson's stages.","c":"Generativity versus stagnation is the seventh stage, in middle adulthood (roughly 40 to 65). Generativity means caring for and guiding the next generation through parenting, teaching, mentoring or productive work; failure brings stagnation and self-absorption. Its virtue is care. It tempts because it is also an adult stage, and people in their sixties may still be highly generative, so the boundary feels blurred.","d":"Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage, in young adulthood (roughly 20 to 40). The task is to form close, committed relationships, such as deep friendship, partnership and marriage, without losing the identity built in adolescence; failure leads to isolation and loneliness. Its virtue is love. It tempts because loneliness is a common worry in old age, which makes the word 'isolation' feel right."}},{"q":"The DSM-5 minimum duration for a Major Depressive Episode is:","concept":"Major depressive episode criteria come from the American Psychiatric Association's DSM-5 (2013). Criterion A requires five or more of nine symptoms present during the same two-week period, representing a change from previous functioning, with at least one being depressed mood or loss of interest or pleasure. The nine are: depressed mood most of the day, nearly every day; markedly diminished interest or pleasure (anhedonia); significant weight or appetite change (for example more than 5 percent of body weight in a month); insomnia or hypersomnia; psychomotor agitation or retardation observable by others; fatigue or loss of energy; feelings of worthlessness or excessive guilt; reduced ability to think, concentrate or decide; and recurrent thoughts of death, suicidal ideation, a plan or an attempt. Symptoms must cause clinically significant distress or impairment and must not be due to a substance or medical condition. In children and adolescents the mood may be irritable. ICD-11 also uses a two-week minimum. Learn the neighbouring durations: manic episode at least one week, hypomanic episode at least four consecutive days, persistent depressive disorder two years in adults and one year in youth, generalised anxiety disorder six months, schizophrenia six months of total disturbance with one month of active symptoms, PTSD more than one month, acute stress disorder three days to one month. One or more such episodes with no history of mania or hypomania gives major depressive disorder. Option d is right because DSM-5 requires the symptoms to be present for at least two weeks to qualify as a major depressive episode.","traps":{"a":"Six months is the duration for generalised anxiety disorder (excessive worry more days than not), for specific phobia and social anxiety disorder (typically six months or more), and for the total disturbance in schizophrenia. It is also the cutoff for delayed-expression PTSD. No depressive episode requires six months. It tempts because depression is often thought of as long-lasting and six months appears in many DSM criteria.","b":"One month is the minimum active-phase period for schizophrenia, the upper limit of brief psychotic disorder, the minimum for delusional disorder, and the point after which PTSD can be diagnosed. It also appears in panic disorder, where at least a month of worry or behaviour change must follow an attack. It tempts because it sounds like a reasonable length for a serious mood episode, but DSM-5 sets the bar at two weeks.","c":"One week is the minimum duration of a manic episode in DSM-5 (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), while a hypomanic episode needs four days. One week is too short for a major depressive episode. It tempts because mood episodes are taught side by side and students mix up the manic and depressive thresholds; remember mania one week, depression two weeks."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of schizophrenia to be present for at least:","concept":"Schizophrenia was named by Eugen Bleuler (1911), replacing Emil Kraepelin's term dementia praecox. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), Criterion A requires two or more of five symptoms, each present for a significant portion of time during a one-month period (or less if successfully treated): delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, and negative symptoms such as diminished emotional expression or avolition. At least one must be delusions, hallucinations or disorganised speech. Criterion B requires a fall in functioning in work, relationships or self-care. Criterion C is the duration rule: continuous signs of the disturbance for at least six months, including at least one month of active-phase symptoms, with the rest made up of prodromal or residual periods in which only negative symptoms or attenuated forms of Criterion A symptoms may show. Schizoaffective disorder, mood disorders and substance or medical causes must be excluded. Duration separates the psychotic disorders, so learn the ladder: brief psychotic disorder lasts at least one day but less than one month, with full return to functioning; schizophreniform disorder lasts at least one month but less than six months; schizophrenia lasts six months or more. Delusional disorder needs delusions for at least one month without other marked psychotic features. Schizoaffective disorder needs at least two weeks of delusions or hallucinations without a major mood episode. ICD-10 and ICD-11 differ from DSM-5: they need only one month of symptoms for schizophrenia. Option b is right because DSM-5 requires one month of active-phase symptoms within a continuous disturbance lasting at least six months.","traps":{"a":"Three months is not a DSM-5 threshold for schizophrenia. An illness of three months falls inside the schizophreniform range (one month to under six months), and if it continues to six months the diagnosis changes to schizophrenia. It tempts because three months sounds like a sensible midway figure and appears in other criteria, such as the bulimia nervosa and binge eating disorder durations.","c":"Two weeks is the duration for a major depressive episode and, within psychosis, the minimum period of delusions or hallucinations without a mood episode required for schizoaffective disorder. For schizophrenia it is far too short, and 'active phase only' ignores the six-month continuous-disturbance rule that counts prodromal and residual phases. It tempts because it borrows a familiar DSM number.","d":"One year of any psychotic symptom is not a schizophrenia rule. One year appears elsewhere in DSM-5: tic disorders, and persistent depressive disorder and cyclothymia in children and adolescents. A single symptom also fails Criterion A, which needs two or more. It tempts because schizophrenia is a chronic illness and a longer, rounder figure feels more serious."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms classically include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider, a German psychiatrist whose Clinical Psychopathology appeared in English in 1959, listed symptoms of the first rank that, in the absence of organic brain disease, he regarded as strongly suggestive of schizophrenia. Most involve a breakdown of the boundary between self and world: the person experiences their own thoughts, feelings and actions as coming from, or controlled by, an outside agency. The classic list has eleven symptoms in four groups. Auditory hallucinations of three kinds: audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden, thought echo), voices arguing or discussing the patient in the third person, and voices giving a running commentary on the patient's actions. Thought alienation: thought insertion, thought withdrawal and thought broadcasting. Passivity experiences: made feelings, made impulses, made volitional acts and somatic passivity. Delusional perception: a normal perception suddenly given a delusional meaning, for example seeing a red car and knowing at once that one has been chosen. Schneider also named second-rank symptoms such as other hallucinations, perplexity, mood changes, emotional blunting and sudden delusional ideas. Compare Eugen Bleuler's (1911) fundamental symptoms, summarised in textbooks as the four As (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism), which stress negative and formal features. First-rank symptoms shaped ICD-10, and DSM-IV let one bizarre delusion or one commenting or conversing voice satisfy Criterion A, but DSM-5 removed that special weight because these symptoms also occur in mood disorders and are not pathognomonic. Option c is right because thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting, audible thoughts, voices arguing and delusional perception are all classic Schneiderian first-rank symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Echolalia (repeating another person's words) and stereotyped movements are among the twelve catatonia signs in DSM-5, along with stupor, waxy flexibility, mutism, negativism, posturing and echopraxia. They also occur in autism spectrum disorder. Catatonia was described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874). These are motor signs observed by the clinician, not subjective experiences of alien control. They tempt because they are classic schizophrenia-chapter signs.","b":"Olfactory hallucinations, such as smelling burning rubber, suggest an organic cause, especially temporal lobe epilepsy (uncinate fits), and tactile hallucinations such as formication are typical of alcohol withdrawal and cocaine use. Illusions are misperceptions of real stimuli, not hallucinations at all. Schneider counted hallucinations other than his three auditory types as second-rank. It tempts because hallucinations appear in both lists.","d":"Anhedonia and avolition are negative symptoms, the deficit features rated by Nancy Andreasen's Scale for the Assessment of Negative Symptoms and stressed in Timothy Crow's (1980) Type II schizophrenia. Schneider's first-rank list consists of positive, subjective experiences such as thought alienation and passivity. Negative symptoms are part of DSM-5 Criterion A but are not first-rank symptoms. It tempts because they are core schizophrenia features."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires obsessive-compulsive disorder to take up at least:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) requires obsessions, compulsions, or both. Obsessions are recurrent, persistent thoughts, urges or images experienced as intrusive and unwanted, which cause anxiety or distress and which the person tries to ignore, suppress or neutralise. Compulsions are repetitive behaviours (washing, checking, ordering) or mental acts (counting, praying, repeating words) that the person feels driven to perform in response to an obsession or by rigid rules, aimed at reducing distress or preventing a feared event, but which are excessive or not realistically connected to it. Criterion B is the threshold this question tests: the obsessions or compulsions are time-consuming, the manual's example being more than one hour per day, or they cause clinically significant distress or impairment. Either route is enough, so a patient who spends 40 minutes a day checking but is disabled by it still qualifies. There is no minimum duration in weeks or months. Further criteria exclude substance or medical causes and better explanations by other disorders, such as worry in generalised anxiety disorder or appearance preoccupation in body dysmorphic disorder. DSM-5 moved OCD out of the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, obsessive-compulsive and related disorders, with body dysmorphic disorder, hoarding disorder, trichotillomania and excoriation disorder. It rates insight as good or fair, poor, or absent with delusional beliefs, and adds a tic-related specifier. Option b is right because DSM-5 Criterion B is met either by symptoms that take more than one hour a day or by clinically significant distress or impairment.","traps":{"a":"Six hours per day has no place in DSM-5. The manual's example of time-consuming is more than one hour per day, and even that can be replaced by clinically significant distress or impairment. Severe cases can lose many hours to rituals, which is why the option tempts, but severity is rated with tools such as the Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS), not built into the diagnostic threshold.","c":"Obsessions alone are not enough for diagnosis, and they are also not required. DSM-5 accepts obsessions, compulsions, or both, but whatever is present must also meet Criterion B (time-consuming or causing significant distress or impairment) and the exclusion criteria. Intrusive thoughts are common: Stanley Rachman and Padmal de Silva (1978) found that most non-clinical people report them. It tempts because obsessions are the best-known OCD feature.","d":"Two weeks is the duration for a major depressive episode. OCD has no duration criterion in weeks or months at all; the threshold concerns time spent per day and the impact on the person, not how long the illness has lasted. It tempts because many DSM-5 diagnoses carry a duration rule, so students assume OCD must have one too and borrow the most familiar number."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes Borderline PD from Conduct Disorder?","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) and conduct disorder (CD) sit in different DSM-5 chapters (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) and differ in age, core problem and course. BPD is a cluster B personality disorder: a pervasive pattern of instability in relationships, self-image and affects, with marked impulsivity, beginning by early adulthood. Five of nine criteria are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealisation and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas; recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability; chronic emptiness; intense, inappropriate anger; and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. The term borderline goes back to Adolf Stern (1938), and Marsha Linehan (1993) proposed a biosocial theory and dialectical behaviour therapy. CD, in the disruptive, impulse-control and conduct disorders chapter, is a repetitive pattern of violating others' rights or major age-appropriate norms: at least three of fifteen criteria in the past twelve months across four groups (aggression to people and animals, destruction of property, deceitfulness or theft, serious rule violations). It has childhood-onset (a symptom before age 10) and adolescent-onset types and a 'with limited prosocial emotions' specifier. Its adult continuation is antisocial personality disorder, which needs age 18 and evidence of CD before 15, not BPD. Two nuances: DSM-5 allows a personality disorder before 18 if traits have lasted a year (except antisocial PD), and CD can be given after 18 if antisocial PD criteria are not met; the general rule still holds. Option d is right because it correctly contrasts adult, identity-and-affect-centred BPD with childhood or adolescent, rule-breaking CD.","traps":{"a":"Calling them identical ignores almost everything about them. BPD is an adult personality disorder about unstable identity, mood and relationships; CD is a childhood or adolescent disorder about aggression, deceit, property destruction and rule-breaking. They share some impulsivity and anger, and both can involve risky behaviour, which is the only reason this option might tempt a candidate who reads the two criteria lists quickly.","b":"CD does not always progress, and when it does the adult outcome is antisocial personality disorder, which requires evidence of CD before age 15. Terrie Moffitt (1993) showed that most adolescent antisocial behaviour is adolescence-limited and fades, while a smaller life-course-persistent group continues. It tempts because BPD and antisocial personality disorder are both cluster B, so the two adult outcomes are easily swapped.","c":"Both disorders are multifactorial. Linehan's biosocial theory explains BPD as an inborn emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment, and CD reflects genes, temperament, harsh or inconsistent parenting and peers; Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2002) showed an MAOA gene by maltreatment interaction for antisocial behaviour. 'Exclusively' is the warning word. It tempts because a neat nature-versus-nurture split sounds tidy and memorable."}},{"q":"Capgras delusion is the belief that:","concept":"Capgras delusion was described by Joseph Capgras and Jean Reboul-Lachaux (1923) as 'l'illusion des sosies', the illusion of doubles, in a woman who believed her husband, children and others had been replaced by look-alikes. The patient accepts that the person looks the same but insists that he or she is an impostor, double or robot. It is the best known of the delusional misidentification syndromes, which are worth learning as a set. Fregoli delusion: a familiar person, often a persecutor, is disguised as various strangers; it is named after an Italian quick-change actor. Intermetamorphosis: people swap identities with one another, physically and psychologically. Syndrome of subjective doubles: the patient believes a double of himself exists. Related delusions that examiners mix in are Cotard delusion (nihilistic: one is dead, rotting or has no organs), Othello syndrome (delusional jealousy about a partner's fidelity; John Todd and Kenneth Dewhurst, 1955), and de Clerambault syndrome or erotomania (a person of higher status is secretly in love with the patient). Capgras occurs in schizophrenia, in dementias such as Alzheimer's and Lewy body disease, and after right hemisphere brain injury. Hadyn Ellis and Andrew Young (1990) explained it as a mirror image of prosopagnosia: conscious face recognition works, but the automatic emotional response of familiarity is lost, so the face looks right yet feels wrong, and the mind explains the gap with an impostor. Patients show reduced skin conductance responses to familiar faces. Option d is right because Capgras is the belief that a familiar person has been replaced by an impostor.","traps":{"a":"This is Cotard delusion, the nihilistic belief that one is dead, does not exist, is rotting, or has lost blood or internal organs. It is named after the French neurologist Jules Cotard and is seen mainly in severe psychotic depression, especially in older patients, and sometimes in schizophrenia or brain disease. It tempts because Cotard and Capgras are both French eponymous delusions and are often paired in questions.","b":"This is Othello syndrome, or morbid jealousy: a delusional conviction that one's partner is unfaithful, defended with trivial 'evidence' such as stains or a changed routine. It was named by John Todd and Kenneth Dewhurst (1955) after Shakespeare's play, is linked with alcohol misuse, and carries a real risk of violence. It tempts because it also involves a delusion about a spouse, the person most often 'replaced' in Capgras.","c":"This is Fregoli delusion, the reverse of Capgras: a known person, usually a persecutor, is believed to be disguising himself as different strangers. It is named after Leopoldo Fregoli, an Italian actor famous for rapid changes of costume and character. Capgras sees a stranger inside a familiar face; Fregoli sees a familiar person behind strangers' faces. It tempts because both are misidentification delusions and are easily reversed."}},{"q":"The defining feature distinguishing conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) from malingering is that conversion disorder involves:","concept":"Conversion disorder, renamed functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), sits in the somatic symptom and related disorders chapter. The person has one or more symptoms of altered voluntary motor or sensory function, such as weakness or paralysis, tremor, gait problems, non-epileptic seizures, loss of speech or sensation, or blindness, and clinical findings show the symptom is incompatible with recognised neurological disease. Positive signs help, for example Hoover's sign in functional leg weakness. The name comes from Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud's Studies on Hysteria (1895), which proposed that psychic conflict is converted into a bodily symptom; relief from the conflict is 'primary gain', while sympathy or time off is 'secondary gain'. The essential contrast is intent and motive. In conversion disorder the symptoms are not consciously produced. In factitious disorder symptoms are deliberately faked or induced without obvious external reward, to occupy the sick role; Richard Asher (1951) called the severe form Munchausen syndrome. In malingering symptoms are deliberately produced for an external incentive such as money, avoiding work or military duty, evading prosecution or obtaining drugs; DSM-5 lists it as a condition that may be a focus of clinical attention, not a mental disorder. DSM-5 dropped the old requirement to prove the absence of feigning, because that cannot be judged reliably, but clear evidence of feigning points to factitious disorder or malingering instead. Option d is right because conversion symptoms are produced unintentionally and are not feigned for gain, which is exactly what separates them from malingering.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain is the definition of malingering, such as faking back pain for compensation or feigning psychosis to avoid trial. DSM-5 advises suspecting it in a medicolegal context, when claims and objective findings clearly differ, when the person does not cooperate, and when antisocial personality disorder is present. It tempts because the stem names malingering, and a hurried reader picks the option describing it.","b":"Conscious production of symptoms to occupy the sick role, without external reward, defines factitious disorder. Patients may inject contaminants, tamper with tests or invent histories, and may move between many hospitals; the severe form is Munchausen syndrome, and making a dependant appear ill is factitious disorder imposed on another. It tempts because it also involves medically unexplained symptoms, but the faking is deliberate.","c":"Faking symptoms in legal proceedings is one setting where malingering is common, which is why DSM-5 lists a medicolegal context as a reason to suspect it. But malingering is not restricted to courts, and in any case it is deliberate, whereas conversion is not. The word 'only' makes the option wrong twice. It tempts because forensic assessment of feigning is a well-known clinical role."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires PTSD symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) entered the manual with DSM-III (1980), and DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) moved it from the anxiety disorders into a new chapter, trauma- and stressor-related disorders. Criterion A requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence, by experiencing it directly, witnessing it, learning that it happened to a close family member or friend (if violent or accidental), or repeated or extreme exposure to aversive details, as with police or first responders; media exposure counts only if work-related. Symptoms fall into four clusters: intrusion (B, at least one), avoidance (C, at least one), negative alterations in cognition and mood (D, at least two), and alterations in arousal and reactivity (E, at least two). Criterion F is the duration rule: the disturbance must last more than one month. Criterion G requires distress or impairment, and H excludes substance or medical causes. Timing separates the neighbouring diagnoses. Acute stress disorder covers the period from three days to one month after the trauma. If full PTSD criteria are not met until at least six months after the event, the specifier 'with delayed expression' applies. Adjustment disorder follows a stressor of any severity within three months and does not persist more than six months after the stressor and its consequences end. DSM-5 also has a dissociative subtype (depersonalisation or derealisation) and separate criteria for children aged six and younger. Option b is right because DSM-5 PTSD requires the disturbance to last more than one month, with symptoms from all four clusters.","traps":{"a":"One week matches no PTSD rule. A person with marked symptoms one week after a trauma could receive acute stress disorder, which covers three days to one month, but PTSD cannot be diagnosed until the disturbance has lasted more than a month, because many early reactions settle naturally. One week is the minimum for a manic episode, which is why the number feels familiar.","c":"Six months marks the 'with delayed expression' specifier: full PTSD criteria are not met until at least six months after the event, although some symptoms may begin at once. It is a specifier about onset, not the minimum duration for diagnosis. It tempts because six months is the familiar duration for generalised anxiety disorder and for the total disturbance in schizophrenia.","d":"Three days is the lower limit of acute stress disorder, which DSM-5 places from three days to one month after trauma exposure and which needs nine or more of fourteen symptoms. The three-day floor exists because intense reactions in the first days are common and often pass. Three days is far too short for PTSD. It tempts because the two disorders share the same trauma criterion."}},{"q":"DSM-5 criteria for Binge Eating Disorder require binge episodes to occur on average at least:","concept":"Binge eating disorder (BED) was first described by Albert Stunkard (1959) in people with obesity, appeared in DSM-IV only as a research diagnosis in an appendix, and became a full diagnosis in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013). A binge has two parts: eating, in a discrete period such as two hours, an amount definitely larger than most people would eat in similar circumstances, and a sense of loss of control over eating. BED requires recurrent binges with at least three of five features: eating much more rapidly than normal, eating until uncomfortably full, eating large amounts when not physically hungry, eating alone because of embarrassment, and feeling disgusted, depressed or very guilty afterwards. There must be marked distress about the bingeing. The frequency rule is at least once a week, on average, for three months. Crucially, there is no regular compensatory behaviour such as vomiting, laxatives, fasting or excessive exercise, and the bingeing does not occur only during bulimia or anorexia nervosa. Severity is graded by binges per week: mild 1 to 3, moderate 4 to 7, severe 8 to 13, extreme 14 or more. Bulimia nervosa uses the same frequency and duration but requires both bingeing and compensatory behaviour. DSM-IV used stricter numbers: twice a week for three months for bulimia, and binge days twice a week for six months in the research criteria for BED; DSM-5 lowered both, which is the source of the distractors. Option c is right because DSM-5 requires binge episodes on average at least once weekly for three months.","traps":{"a":"Once weekly matches the DSM-5 frequency, but six months is the old DSM-IV research duration for BED, which also required bingeing on two days a week. DSM-5 shortened the duration to three months to match bulimia nervosa. The option mixes the new frequency with the old duration, which is why it tempts candidates who learned from older textbooks or notes.","b":"Twice monthly for one month is too infrequent and too brief. DSM-5 needs roughly four binges a month, sustained for three months, before the pattern counts as a disorder. Occasional overeating at festivals or parties is common and not pathological. It tempts because 'twice' echoes the old DSM-IV figure of twice a week, which some candidates misremember as twice a month.","d":"Once daily for a year far exceeds the threshold. Seven binges a week would fall into the DSM-5 moderate severity band (4 to 7 a week), which is a severity rating applied after diagnosis, not a requirement for it. Requiring daily episodes would miss most patients. It tempts because people picture binge eating as a constant daily habit, and a year sounds suitably chronic."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is named after the Swiss naturalist and philosopher Charles Bonnet, who in 1760 described the experiences of his grandfather, Charles Lullin, an elderly man with cataracts who saw people, birds, carriages and buildings that were not there, while knowing they were not real. The syndrome has three defining features: complex, formed visual hallucinations (faces, figures in costume, small people, animals, patterns or buildings); significant visual loss, most often from age-related macular degeneration, glaucoma, cataract or diabetic retinopathy; and preserved cognition with intact insight, so the person knows the images are not real. Hallucinations in other senses, delusions or cognitive decline argue against it. The accepted mechanism is deafferentation or 'release': when the visual cortex loses normal input it becomes hyperexcitable and generates its own images, much as an amputee feels a phantom limb. It is common among people with low vision but under-reported, because patients fear being thought mad, so explanation and reassurance are the main treatment. Exams build variants by contrast. Lewy body dementia also produces vivid visual hallucinations, but with fluctuating cognition and parkinsonism. Delirium brings hallucinations with clouded consciousness and inattention. Peduncular hallucinosis follows midbrain or thalamic lesions. Hypnagogic hallucinations occur only while falling asleep. Schizophrenia has mainly auditory hallucinations, usually without insight. The auditory counterpart of Charles Bonnet syndrome, in people with hearing loss, is musical hallucinosis. Option c is right because Charles Bonnet syndrome means visual hallucinations in visually impaired, cognitively intact people who keep their insight.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in dementia occur as part of psychosis in Alzheimer's disease and other dementias, often together with delusions. Charles Bonnet syndrome is purely visual and, by definition, occurs in people without significant cognitive decline. The auditory counterpart linked to hearing loss is musical hallucinosis. It tempts because both conditions are common in elderly patients and both involve hallucinations without schizophrenia.","b":"Tactile hallucinations in alcohol withdrawal, especially formication (insects crawling on or under the skin), are a feature of delirium tremens, which also brings clouded consciousness, tremor, autonomic instability and vivid visual hallucinations, usually two to three days after the last drink. The person is confused and lacks insight, unlike in Charles Bonnet syndrome. It tempts because it is another classic named hallucination syndrome.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations before a seizure are the aura of temporal lobe epilepsy, classically uncinate fits arising from the medial temporal lobe and described by John Hughlings Jackson, in which patients smell something unpleasant such as burning. They are brief, stereotyped and caused by epileptic discharge. Charles Bonnet syndrome is visual and caused by sensory loss, not seizures. It tempts because both are organic, non-psychiatric hallucinations."}},{"q":"The most reliable clinical feature distinguishing delirium from dementia is:","concept":"Delirium and dementia both impair cognition, but they differ in onset, course, attention and consciousness. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013), delirium is a disturbance of attention (reduced ability to direct, focus, sustain or shift it) and awareness that develops over a short period, usually hours to a few days, represents a change from baseline and tends to fluctuate during the day, often worsening at night. There is an additional cognitive disturbance, such as memory loss, disorientation, language or perceptual change, and evidence that it is a direct consequence of a medical condition, intoxication, withdrawal, medication or toxin. It may be hyperactive, hypoactive (easily missed) or mixed. The bedside tool is the Confusion Assessment Method of Sharon Inouye (1990): acute onset with fluctuating course, plus inattention, plus either disorganised thinking or an altered level of consciousness. The EEG usually shows diffuse slowing. Delirium is often reversible once the cause, such as infection, dehydration or a drug, is treated. Dementia, called major neurocognitive disorder in DSM-5, is a significant decline in one or more cognitive domains that interferes with independence. Most forms begin insidiously over months or years and progress steadily, with alertness normal and attention relatively spared early; in Alzheimer's disease memory usually fails first. The two often coexist: dementia is a major risk factor for delirium, and older inpatients with dementia often develop delirium on top. Depressive pseudodementia is a third possibility. Option c is right because acute onset, fluctuation, inattention and a reversible cause mark delirium, while insidious, progressive decline with early attention preserved marks dementia.","traps":{"a":"Delirium is not limited to the young; it is most common in older people, especially after surgery such as hip fracture repair, in intensive care, and in those with existing dementia, sensory impairment or many medications. Children with high fever can also become delirious, but older age raises the risk. It tempts because people associate feverish confusion with children.","b":"Memory is impaired in both, but in delirium it suffers mainly because the patient cannot attend to new information; inattention is the cardinal sign. In dementia, especially Alzheimer's disease, memory loss is the core early deficit while attention is relatively spared. So memory loss is not greater in delirium. It tempts because acutely confused patients often cannot recall recent events or even where they are.","d":"Most dementias, such as Alzheimer's disease, vascular dementia, Lewy body dementia and frontotemporal dementia, are progressive and irreversible. Only a small minority of cognitive decline comes from treatable causes such as hypothyroidism, vitamin B12 deficiency, normal pressure hydrocephalus or depression. It is delirium that is usually reversible. The option swaps the two, and tempts because 'reversible dementia' is a well-known exam phrase."}},{"q":"DSM-5 Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires symptoms to be present for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (PDD) was introduced in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) by merging two DSM-IV diagnoses, chronic major depressive disorder and dysthymic disorder; the term dysthymia had entered the manual with DSM-III (1980). The core requirement is depressed mood for most of the day, on more days than not, for at least two years in adults, by self-report or observation. In children and adolescents the mood can be irritable and the duration is at least one year. While depressed, the person has at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy or fatigue, low self-esteem, poor concentration or difficulty deciding, and hopelessness. During the two years the person has never been free of symptoms for more than two months at a time. Criteria for major depressive disorder may be continuously present, but there has never been a manic or hypomanic episode and cyclothymia criteria have never been met. Specifiers include early onset (before age 21) or late onset, and whether major depressive episodes are present. When a major depressive episode develops on top of long-standing dysthymia, clinicians call it double depression. Cyclothymic disorder uses a parallel framework: two years in adults, one year in youth, no symptom-free gap over two months, but with alternating hypomanic and depressive symptoms that never meet full episode criteria. Option b is right because DSM-5 sets the minimum for persistent depressive disorder at one year in children and adolescents and two years in adults.","traps":{"a":"Five years appears in no DSM-5 mood disorder criterion. It tempts because PDD is described as chronic and long-lasting, and clinical case histories often stretch over many years, which makes a large number feel right. The actual minimum is two years in adults, and only one year in children and adolescents, so a five-year rule would exclude many genuine cases.","c":"Two weeks is the minimum for a major depressive episode, the acute and more severe syndrome needing five or more of nine symptoms. PDD is the reverse: fewer symptoms (two of six) over a much longer period. A person can have both at once, called double depression. It tempts because two weeks is the most memorised number in the depression chapter.","d":"Six months is the duration for generalised anxiety disorder and for the total disturbance in schizophrenia, and it marks delayed-expression PTSD. It is also the DSM-5 limit for how long adjustment disorder can persist after the stressor ends. It is far too short for PDD. It tempts because six months is the default 'chronic' figure across many DSM-5 diagnoses."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder requires:","concept":"Tourette's disorder is named after the French neurologist Georges Gilles de la Tourette (1885), who described patients with motor tics, vocal tics and, in some, coprolalia. A tic is a sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic movement (motor tic, such as blinking or shoulder shrugging) or vocalisation (vocal tic, such as sniffing, throat clearing or words). Tics are often preceded by a premonitory urge and can be briefly suppressed. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) places tic disorders among the neurodevelopmental disorders and orders them by type and duration. Tourette's disorder: both multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics have been present at some time during the illness, not necessarily together; tics may wax and wane but have persisted for more than one year since first onset; onset is before age 18; and the tics are not due to a substance or a medical condition such as Huntington's disease or post-viral encephalitis. Persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder: motor or vocal tics but never both, lasting more than one year. Provisional tic disorder: tics of any kind present for less than one year. DSM-IV's rule against a tic-free period of more than three months was dropped. Coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscene words, is the popular image but occurs in only a minority and is not required. ADHD and OCD are common comorbidities. Option c is right because Tourette's requires both motor and vocal tics, not necessarily at the same time, lasting more than a year, with onset before 18.","traps":{"a":"Coprolalia, the involuntary shouting of obscene or socially unacceptable words, is the image of Tourette's in films and media, but it appears in only a minority of patients and is not a criterion. Related complex tics include echolalia, palilalia (repeating one's own words) and copropraxia (obscene gestures). It tempts because it is the one symptom most people associate with the disorder.","b":"Vocal tics alone can never give Tourette's, which needs motor tics as well. Vocal tics only for just over a month would be provisional tic disorder, since they have lasted under a year; if they lasted more than a year, the diagnosis would be persistent (chronic) vocal tic disorder. It tempts because vocal tics are the most noticeable feature of Tourette's.","d":"Motor tics only for six months would be provisional tic disorder, because the duration is under one year. With more than a year of motor tics but no vocal tics, the diagnosis would be persistent (chronic) motor tic disorder. Tourette's needs vocal tics as well and a duration of more than a year. It tempts because motor tics usually appear first in Tourette's."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, is defined in DSM-5 by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa was named by the English physician William Gull in the 1870s, around the time Charles Lasegue described 'hysterical anorexia' in France. In DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) it needs three features: restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight for age, sex, development and physical health; intense fear of gaining weight or persistent behaviour that interferes with weight gain; and disturbed experience of body weight or shape, undue influence of shape on self-evaluation, or persistent lack of recognition of the seriousness of the low weight. DSM-5 removed the DSM-IV amenorrhoea criterion and the fixed cut-off of 85 percent of expected weight. Severity in adults uses body mass index: mild 17 or more, moderate 16 to 16.99, severe 15 to 15.99, extreme below 15. Two subtypes are defined by the last three months. Restricting type: no recurrent episodes of binge eating or purging (self-induced vomiting or misuse of laxatives, diuretics or enemas); weight loss comes mainly from dieting, fasting and/or excessive exercise. Binge-eating/purging type: recurrent bingeing or purging during that period. Patients can move between subtypes over the course of illness. Do not confuse the binge/purge subtype with bulimia nervosa: bulimia needs bingeing plus compensatory behaviour at least weekly for three months, usually at normal or above-normal weight, and if weight is significantly low, anorexia takes precedence. Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder restricts eating without any body-image disturbance. Option b is right because the restricting subtype means no bingeing or purging during the last three months, with weight lost through dieting, fasting or exercise.","traps":{"a":"Self-induced vomiting is a purging behaviour. Its recurrent presence during the last three months places a patient in the binge-eating/purging subtype, so it cannot be required for the restricting subtype. No form of anorexia requires vomiting at all; the core is restriction and low weight. Vomiting is also a compensatory behaviour in bulimia nervosa. It tempts because vomiting is the image many people have of eating disorders.","c":"Recurrent bingeing or purging during the last three months defines the binge-eating/purging subtype of anorexia nervosa, the exact opposite of the restricting subtype. The patient still has significantly low weight, which separates this subtype from bulimia nervosa. It tempts because it uses the same three-month window as the correct answer, so a candidate who remembers only the time frame can be misled.","d":"Laxative misuse is purging, so recurrent use in the last three months would place the patient in the binge-eating/purging subtype. No subtype requires laxatives. The weekly frequency is borrowed from bulimia nervosa and binge eating disorder, which require episodes at least once a week for three months. It tempts because laxative misuse is common in eating disorders and the weekly figure looks like a DSM criterion."}},{"q":"The four DSM-5 PTSD symptom clusters are:","concept":"DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) organises the twenty symptoms of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) into four clusters that follow a Criterion A trauma (actual or threatened death, serious injury or sexual violence). Cluster B, intrusion (one of five needed): intrusive distressing memories, nightmares, dissociative reactions such as flashbacks, intense distress at reminders, and physiological reactions to reminders. Cluster C, avoidance (one of two): avoiding internal reminders (thoughts, feelings) and external reminders (people, places, situations). Cluster D, negative alterations in cognitions and mood (two of seven): dissociative amnesia for important parts of the trauma, persistent exaggerated negative beliefs about self or world, distorted blame of self or others, persistent negative emotions such as fear, horror, guilt or shame, loss of interest, detachment from others, and inability to feel positive emotions. Cluster E, alterations in arousal and reactivity (two of six): irritability or angry outbursts, reckless or self-destructive behaviour, hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, poor concentration and sleep disturbance. DSM-IV had three clusters (re-experiencing, avoidance and numbing, hyperarousal) and seventeen symptoms; DSM-5 split avoidance from the numbing items, expanded those into Cluster D, and added reckless behaviour, distorted blame and persistent negative emotions. ICD-11 is narrower: PTSD needs re-experiencing in the present, avoidance and a sense of current threat, and complex PTSD adds disturbances in self-organisation (affect dysregulation, negative self-concept and relationship problems). DSM-5 also allows a dissociative subtype. Option a is right because intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition and mood, and alterations in arousal and reactivity are exactly the four DSM-5 PTSD clusters.","traps":{"b":"Hyperarousal is only Cluster E. DSM-5 needs symptoms from all four clusters: at least one intrusion, one avoidance, two negative cognition or mood symptoms and two arousal symptoms. Mood changes are not optional; they form a whole required cluster. It tempts because hyperarousal (startle, hypervigilance, poor sleep) is the most visible sign of trauma, and older models gave arousal great weight.","c":"Denial, anger and bargaining come from Elisabeth Kubler-Ross's (1969) five stages of grief in dying patients: denial, anger, bargaining, depression and acceptance. Only flashbacks belong to PTSD, as an intrusion symptom. The option mixes one true PTSD feature with a grief model to look plausible. It tempts because trauma and loss are often taught together and anger does appear in PTSD Cluster E.","d":"Dissociation, anxiety, depression and mania are broad symptom domains or separate disorders, not PTSD clusters. Dissociation appears in PTSD as flashbacks, dissociative amnesia and the dissociative subtype, and depression is a frequent comorbidity, but mania has no place in PTSD criteria. It tempts because trauma survivors often show dissociative and depressive symptoms, so the first items look right."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA), also called pathological laughing and crying, emotional incontinence or involuntary emotional expression disorder, is a neurological disorder of emotional expression. Patients have sudden, brief, uncontrollable episodes of crying or laughing that are out of proportion to, or do not match, what they actually feel; a patient may sob at a mildly sad remark, or laugh while feeling sad. Samuel Alexander Kinnier Wilson (1924) gave an early classic account. The name comes from pseudobulbar palsy, in which lesions of the corticobulbar pathways above the medulla (the bulb), rather than of the medulla itself, release brainstem centres for facial and vocal expression from cortical control; cortico-ponto-cerebellar circuits are also implicated. It occurs in stroke, multiple sclerosis, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, traumatic brain injury, Parkinson's disease and dementias. Severity can be rated with the Center for Neurologic Study-Lability Scale. Treatment includes dextromethorphan with quinidine, SSRIs and tricyclic antidepressants. The concept tests the difference between affect and mood. Mood is the sustained inner emotional state (the climate); affect is its observable expression (the weather). Normally affect is congruent with mood; in PBA expression breaks free of mood. Neighbouring terms: blunted or flat affect (reduced or absent expression, a negative symptom), labile affect (rapid shifts in expressed emotion that follow changing feelings, as in mania or borderline personality disorder), inappropriate affect (expression unsuited to the content, as in schizophrenia), and post-stroke depression, where mood itself is low. Option c is right because PBA consists of sudden, involuntary crying or laughing outbursts incongruent with mood, caused by neurological disease.","traps":{"a":"Anxious affect in generalised anxiety disorder is the visible tension, worry and restlessness of a person whose inner mood is anxious, so affect and mood match. GAD itself is defined by excessive, hard-to-control worry on more days than not for at least six months, with physical symptoms. There are no sudden involuntary outbursts. It tempts because it also uses the word affect.","b":"Blunted affect is a reduction in the intensity of emotional expression, and flat affect its near absence; both are negative symptoms of schizophrenia, grouped in DSM-5 as diminished emotional expression. They show too little expression, while PBA shows sudden excessive expression. It tempts because schizophrenia is the default psychiatric answer and affective abnormalities are central to it.","d":"Mood-congruent affect in major depression means a sad, tearful expression that fits a low inner mood, which is normal congruence. The term mood-congruent is also used for psychotic features whose content fits the mood, such as delusions of guilt or ruin. PBA is defined by the opposite, incongruence. It tempts because crying is common in depression, and PBA is often misdiagnosed as depression."}},{"q":"The key DSM-5 feature distinguishing mania from hypomania is:","concept":"Mania and hypomania in DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) share the same symptom list but differ in duration, severity and consequences. Criterion A for both is a distinct period of abnormally and persistently elevated, expansive or irritable mood, plus abnormally and persistently increased activity or energy, the energy part being new in DSM-5. Criterion B needs three or more of seven symptoms (four if mood is only irritable), remembered as DIGFAST: distractibility, indiscretion (risky, impulsive activities), grandiosity or inflated self-esteem, flight of ideas or racing thoughts, increased goal-directed activity or psychomotor agitation, sleep need decreased, and talkativeness or pressured speech. A manic episode lasts at least one week, most of the day nearly every day, or any duration if hospitalisation is needed, and is severe enough to cause marked impairment, need hospitalisation, or include psychotic features. A hypomanic episode lasts at least four consecutive days and is an unequivocal change in functioning observable by others, but does not cause marked impairment or need hospitalisation; if psychotic features are present, the episode is by definition manic. One manic episode is enough for bipolar I disorder; bipolar II needs at least one hypomanic and one major depressive episode with no mania ever. A full episode that emerges during antidepressant treatment and persists beyond the drug's physiological effect counts toward a bipolar diagnosis. Option a is right because mania can involve psychosis, causes marked impairment or hospitalisation and lasts at least seven days, whereas hypomania needs only four days, causes no marked impairment and never includes psychosis.","traps":{"b":"Euphoria is a core feature of mania, not something it lacks. DSM-5 Criterion A requires elevated, expansive or irritable mood, so euphoria is one of the accepted forms, though some manic patients are mainly irritable or dysphoric, and mixed features can be specified. It tempts because irritable mania is real and frequently taught, and a candidate may overgeneralise from it.","c":"This reverses the durations. Hypomania needs at least four consecutive days and mania at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed). Real hypomanic episodes can last longer than four days, but the definitional threshold for mania is the longer one. It tempts because a milder state is easily imagined as a drawn-out one, so the numbers get swapped.","d":"This inverts the rule. Psychotic features, such as grandiose delusions, automatically make an episode manic; DSM-5 states that hypomania by definition has no psychosis. Mania with mood-congruent or mood-incongruent psychotic features is common in bipolar I disorder. It tempts because psychosis is associated with severity, and a hurried reader may swap the two episode labels."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) describes:","concept":"Folie a deux, French for 'madness of two', was described by Charles Lasegue and Jules Falret (1877). A delusion held by one person, the primary or inducer, who has a psychotic disorder such as delusional disorder or schizophrenia, is taken on by a second person, the secondary, who was previously well. The pair usually live together in close emotional ties and relative social isolation: spouses, sisters, or a parent and child. The primary is typically dominant and the secondary more dependent or suggestible. The shared delusion is usually persecutory and fairly plausible. Classic subtypes are folie imposee (the Lasegue and Falret form, where the delusion is imposed on the secondary and often fades after separation), folie simultanee (both develop the same delusion at once), folie communiquee (the secondary resists at first, then adopts the belief and keeps it even after separation), and folie induite (an already psychotic person adds the other's delusions). Larger groups give folie a trois or folie a famille. The first step in treatment is to separate the pair; the secondary often recovers, while the primary needs treatment of the underlying psychosis. DSM-III-R called it induced psychotic disorder, DSM-IV shared psychotic disorder, and ICD-10 induced delusional disorder. DSM-5 (American Psychiatric Association, 2013) dropped it as a separate category: the secondary is diagnosed with delusional disorder if criteria are met, or otherwise with other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder. Option c is right because folie a deux is a delusion transmitted to a previously healthy person closely associated with someone who already has an established delusion.","traps":{"a":"Shared beliefs in cults and mass gatherings are group phenomena, closer to mass psychogenic illness or social conformity than to a delusional disorder. DSM-5 does not count beliefs accepted by a person's culture or subculture as delusions. Folie a deux concerns two people in an intimate, isolated relationship and involves delusions, not hallucinations. It tempts because cults also involve unusual beliefs spreading between people.","b":"Two unrelated patients who happen to have identical hallucinations show coincidence, not transmission. Folie a deux needs a close relationship and an inducer whose delusion is adopted by a previously well secondary, and its core is a shared delusion rather than a shared hallucination. It tempts because the option mentions two patients with identical symptoms, which echoes the 'two' in the French name.","d":"Genetic transmission of schizophrenia is a question of heritability: risk rises with genetic closeness to an affected relative, from about 1 percent in the general population to roughly 50 percent in a monozygotic co-twin, as summarised by Irving Gottesman (1991). Folie a deux is psychological induction of a delusion in a close contact, and the secondary usually improves on separation. It tempts because the pairs are often blood relatives."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, the four index scores are:","concept":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale descends from the Wechsler-Bellevue scale that David Wechsler published in 1939, followed by the WAIS (1955), WAIS-R (1981), WAIS-III (1997) and WAIS-IV (2008). For decades the Wechsler tests reported three scores: Verbal IQ, Performance IQ and Full Scale IQ. Factor analysis kept showing that the verbal and performance halves each mixed different abilities, so WAIS-III added four factor indices alongside the IQs, and WAIS-IV dropped VIQ and PIQ entirely. WAIS-IV covers ages 16 to 90 and has 10 core and 5 supplementary subtests grouped into four indices. The Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI) uses Similarities, Vocabulary and Information. The Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI) uses Block Design, Matrix Reasoning and Visual Puzzles. The Working Memory Index (WMI) uses Digit Span and Arithmetic. The Processing Speed Index (PSI) uses Symbol Search and Coding. Each index, like the Full Scale IQ built from the 10 core subtests, has a mean of 100 and SD of 15; subtest scaled scores have a mean of 10 and SD of 3. Two optional composites also exist: the General Ability Index (VCI plus PRI) and the Cognitive Proficiency Index (WMI plus PSI). Variant questions often target the WISC-V (2014), which splits perceptual reasoning into a Visual Spatial Index and a Fluid Reasoning Index, giving five primary indices. In WAIS-III the equivalent of PRI was called the Perceptual Organization Index. Option b is right because VCI, PRI, WMI and PSI are exactly the four WAIS-IV index scores, with FSIQ as the overall composite.","traps":{"a":"Crystallised (Gc), fluid (Gf), visual (Gv) and quantitative (Gq) abilities are broad abilities in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which grew from Raymond Cattell's fluid-crystallised distinction and John B. Carroll's (1993) three-stratum theory. Tests such as the Woodcock-Johnson are built on CHC labels. Some clinicians interpret WAIS-IV through CHC ideas, but its indices are not named this way. It tempts because the terms sound like official intelligence factors.","c":"VIQ and PIQ are the Verbal and Performance IQs that the Wechsler scales reported up to WAIS-III; WAIS-IV removed them. 'Memory' and 'Attention' are general domain names, not WAIS-IV indices, though the older WISC-III had a 'Freedom from Distractibility' index that was later renamed Working Memory. It tempts because it half-matches the older structure, and working memory sounds like memory plus attention.","d":"This mixes two older instruments. Verbal IQ, Performance IQ and Full Scale IQ were the classic WAIS and WAIS-R outputs, and the Memory Quotient was the single summary score of the original Wechsler Memory Scale (1945). WAIS-IV reports no VIQ or PIQ and has never reported an MQ. It tempts because these are the most famous Wechsler scores and they still appear in older textbooks."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2, the F (Infrequency) scale is used to detect:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was built by Starke R. Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley at the University of Minnesota and published in 1943. They used empirical criterion keying: an item went into a scale if patients with a diagnosis answered it differently from normal people, whatever its content. The MMPI-2 (1989), restandardised by James Butcher and colleagues, has 567 true-false items. Because self-report can be distorted, the test includes validity scales that are read before any clinical scale. The Cannot Say (?) score counts omitted items. L (Lie) catches naive attempts to look virtuous, such as denying ordinary faults. K (Correction) picks up subtler defensiveness and is also added to some clinical scales. F (Infrequency) contains items that fewer than about 10 percent of the normal sample endorsed, mostly bizarre or severe symptoms. A high F therefore means random or careless answering, deliberate exaggeration (faking bad, a 'cry for help', malingering), or genuinely severe psychopathology. To tell these apart, examiners check VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) for random answering and TRIN (True Response Inconsistency) for yea-saying or nay-saying, then look at Fb (Back F, covering the second half of the booklet) and Fp (Infrequency-Psychopathology, which is less affected by real illness). The classic exam contrast: L and K high with F low suggests faking good, while F high with L and K low suggests faking bad. Option c is right because the F scale detects random responding or over-reporting of rare, severe symptoms, the faking bad pattern.","traps":{"a":"No MMPI-2 validity scale measures religious bias. The only link is historical: the MMPI-2 revision deleted or reworded some original MMPI items judged objectionable, including items about religious beliefs. Validity scales assess test-taking attitude (random answering, exaggeration, defensiveness), not attitudes toward religion. It tempts only as a filler, or for a candidate who links 'infrequency' with unusual beliefs.","b":"Answering in the socially desirable direction, or faking good, is picked up by the L (Lie) and K (Correction) scales, and in MMPI-2 also by the S (Superlative Self-Presentation) scale. Allen L. Edwards (1957) made social desirability famous as a response set. The F scale detects the opposite direction, presenting oneself as worse off than one is. It tempts because social desirability is the best-known response bias in personality testing.","d":"Defensiveness, minimising problems to appear well adjusted, is what L and K (and S) measure. The F scale points the other way: it rises when a person over-reports or answers randomly. The word 'only' also makes this option too narrow, since no validity scale has just one cause for elevation. It tempts because candidates remember that validity scales detect 'faking' without fixing which scale catches which direction."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is most commonly used to estimate:","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of a measure, and classical test theory treats it as the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance. There are several kinds, each answering a different question. Test-retest reliability (the coefficient of stability) correlates scores from the same test given twice. Alternate or parallel forms reliability (the coefficient of equivalence) correlates two versions. Inter-rater reliability checks agreement between scorers, using Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) or the intraclass correlation. Internal consistency asks whether the items of one test, given once, hang together as measures of the same thing. The earliest method was split-half reliability, corrected for the shortened length with the Spearman-Brown prophecy formula. G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) gave KR-20 for items scored right or wrong. Lee J. Cronbach (1951) generalised this to any item format, including Likert ratings, as coefficient alpha: alpha equals k/(k-1) multiplied by (1 minus the sum of item variances divided by total-score variance). Alpha can be thought of as the mean of all possible split-half coefficients. It usually runs from 0 to 1; about .70 is a common minimum for research scales, and .90 or more is wanted for individual clinical decisions. Two cautions are examined: alpha rises simply by adding more items, and a high alpha does not prove the scale is unidimensional. Very high values can also mean redundant items. McDonald's omega is a modern alternative. Option b is right because Cronbach's alpha estimates the internal consistency reliability of a multi-item scale from a single administration.","traps":{"a":"Construct validity asks whether a test really measures the theoretical trait it claims to measure. Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955) set out the idea of a nomological network, and Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959) added convergent and discriminant validity through the multitrait-multimethod matrix. Alpha is a reliability index, not validity evidence. It tempts because both ideas carry Cronbach's name, and a consistent scale can still measure the wrong construct.","c":"Predictive validity is a type of criterion-related validity in which test scores are correlated with a criterion measured later, for example an entrance test against later course grades. It tells you whether the test forecasts real outcomes, which is a validity question. Alpha says nothing about prediction; it only shows that items agree with one another. It tempts because reliability and validity coefficients are both correlations reported in the same manual.","d":"Test-retest reliability gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the scores with Pearson's r, producing the coefficient of stability. It is affected by the interval, practice effects, memory and real change in the trait. Alpha needs only one administration and examines item agreement, not stability over time. It tempts because test-retest is also a reliability coefficient and is the first type most students learn."}},{"q":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the major scoring system for:","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test was published by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach in 1921 in his book Psychodiagnostik. It uses 10 symmetrical inkblot cards: five black and grey, two black and red, and three multicoloured. The person says what each blot might be (the response phase), and the examiner then goes back through the answers to learn where and why each percept was seen (the inquiry). After Rorschach's early death, five rival American scoring systems grew up, those of Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, and David Rapaport with Roy Schafer, so research findings could not be compared. John E. Exner Jr. combined the empirically sound parts of these into the Comprehensive System, first published in 1974, with standard administration and norms. Each response is coded for location (whole W, common detail D, unusual detail Dd, white space S), developmental quality, determinants (form, movement, colour, shading), form quality, content, popular responses and special scores. The codes feed a structural summary of ratios and indices, such as the Erlebnistypus, which compares human movement with colour responses. The Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS), developed by Gregory Meyer and colleagues in 2011, is now its main successor. For variants, remember the other projective landmarks: the Thematic Apperception Test of Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935), the Rotter Incomplete Sentences Blank of Julian Rotter (1950), and the House-Tree-Person test of John Buck (1948). Option c is right because Exner's Comprehensive System is the standard scoring system for the Rorschach Inkblot Test.","traps":{"a":"The MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) is an objective, true-false self-report inventory built by empirical criterion keying. It is scored by counting keyed answers and converting them to T scores on validity and clinical scales, then reading code types. There is nothing to code for location or determinants, so Exner's system has no role. It tempts because the MMPI is the best-known personality test, and the stem says 'major'.","b":"The Thematic Apperception Test (Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, 1935) asks the person to tell stories about ambiguous pictures of people. It is usually interpreted qualitatively through Murray's needs and press, with formal systems such as David McClelland's achievement-motive scoring or Drew Westen's SCORS. It tempts because, like the Rorschach, it is a projective test, and candidates mix up which scoring system goes with which projective.","d":"The Wechsler scales are individually administered intelligence tests. Raw scores are converted into age-normed scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3) and then into composites such as index scores and Full Scale IQ (mean 100, SD 15), following the publisher's manual, not Exner. It tempts because Wechsler and Exner are both big names in clinical assessment, and the Wechsler tests are the most widely used clinical scales."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening test refers to:","concept":"Screening test accuracy is taught with a 2 by 2 table that crosses the test result (positive or negative) with the true condition, established by a gold standard such as a structured clinical interview. The four cells are true positives (TP, ill and test positive), false positives (FP, well but test positive), false negatives (FN, ill but test negative) and true negatives (TN). Sensitivity is TP divided by (TP + FN): of everyone who really has the disorder, what proportion does the test catch? If 100 people have depression and a questionnaire flags 90, sensitivity is 90 percent. Specificity is TN divided by (TN + FP): of everyone who is well, what proportion does the test clear? These two are conditioned on true status and are fairly stable properties of the test. Predictive values are conditioned on the test result. Positive predictive value, TP divided by (TP + FP), is the chance that a positive result is a real case; negative predictive value, TN divided by (TN + FN), is the chance that a negative result is truly clear. Predictive values change with prevalence, so the same test gives more false alarms in a low-prevalence community sample. Moving the cut-off trades sensitivity against specificity, which the ROC curve plots. Mnemonics: SnNOut (a negative result on a highly sensitive test rules the disorder out) and SpPIn (a positive result on a highly specific test rules it in). Option c is right because sensitivity is the true positive rate, the proportion of real cases correctly identified as positive.","traps":{"a":"The probability that a positive result reflects true disease is the positive predictive value, TP divided by (TP + FP). Unlike sensitivity, it depends heavily on prevalence: a test with good sensitivity can still have a low PPV when the disorder is rare. It tempts because it reverses the conditional. Sensitivity is the probability of a positive test given disease; PPV is the probability of disease given a positive test.","b":"The probability of disease before testing is the pre-test probability, which for a population equals the prevalence or base rate. Paul Meehl and Albert Rosen (1955) showed that ignoring base rates makes psychological signs and cut-offs look more useful than they are. Prevalence is a property of the population, not of the test, so it cannot be sensitivity. It tempts because prevalence enters the calculation of predictive values.","d":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified as negative is specificity, TN divided by (TN + FP), the true negative rate. It is the mirror image of sensitivity: a highly specific test seldom labels well people as cases, so a positive result helps rule the disorder in (SpPIn). It tempts because the wording is parallel to the correct option and differs only in 'negatives' versus 'positives'."}},{"q":"A stanine score of 5 corresponds to which position on the normal curve?","concept":"Stanine is short for 'standard nine'. It was introduced in the United States Army Air Forces during the Second World War. Stanines are normalised standard scores running from 1 to 9 with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of about 2. Each band from 2 to 8 is half a standard deviation wide, and 1 and 9 are open-ended tails. Stanine 5 is centred on the mean and spans from 0.25 SD below to 0.25 SD above it. The percentage of a normal group in each stanine follows the pattern 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7, 4. Adding these up gives the percentile bands: stanine 1 covers roughly the 1st to 4th percentile, 2 the 4th to 11th, 3 the 11th to 23rd, 4 the 23rd to 40th, 5 the 40th to 60th, 6 the 60th to 77th, 7 the 77th to 89th, 8 the 89th to 96th and 9 above the 96th. Stanines 1 to 3 are usually read as below average, 4 to 6 as average and 7 to 9 as above average. Compare other standard scores: z scores (mean 0, SD 1), T scores (mean 50, SD 10, used by the MMPI), deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15), Wechsler scaled scores (mean 10, SD 3) and stens (standard ten, mean 5.5, SD 2, used in the 16PF of Raymond Cattell). Option c is right because stanine 5 is the middle band, mean plus or minus 0.25 SD, which covers about the 40th to 60th percentile.","traps":{"a":"The 23rd to 40th percentile is stanine 4, the band from 0.75 SD to 0.25 SD below the mean, holding about 17 percent of a normal group. It is the lower edge of the average range, which is why it is labelled just below average. It tempts because stanines 4 and 5 are neighbours and both count as average, so a one-band slip is easy under exam pressure.","b":"Above the 95th percentile describes the top tail of the distribution, roughly stanine 9, which starts at about the 96th percentile and holds only the top 4 percent (more than 1.75 SD above the mean). A stanine of 5 is the exact middle, not the top. It tempts candidates who read '5' as a high score, for example on a 5-point rating scale where 5 is the maximum.","d":"The 1st to 4th percentile is stanine 1, the open-ended lowest band covering the bottom 4 percent of a normal group, more than 1.75 SD below the mean. It is the mirror image of stanine 9. It tempts candidates who confuse the stanine number with a percentile, or who forget that the scale runs from 1 to 9 with 5 in the middle."}},{"q":"BDI-II severity bands are conventionally:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory was introduced by Aaron T. Beck and colleagues in 1961 as a measure of depression severity, not a diagnostic tool. The second edition, the BDI-II, was published by Beck, Robert Steer and Gregory Brown in 1996 to match the DSM-IV criteria for major depression. It has 21 self-report items, each scored 0 to 3, so totals run from 0 to 63, and the person rates the past two weeks, including today. Compared with the earlier version, four items were replaced (weight loss, body image change, somatic preoccupation and work difficulty gave way to agitation, worthlessness, concentration difficulty and loss of energy), and the sleep and appetite items now allow for both increase and decrease. The manual's cut-offs are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate, and 29 to 63 severe. Do not mix these with the original BDI bands, commonly given as 0 to 9 minimal, 10 to 18 mild, 19 to 29 moderate and 30 to 63 severe. Neighbouring scales use different ranges. The PHQ-9 of Kurt Kroenke, Robert Spitzer and Janet Williams (2001) runs 0 to 27, with 5, 10, 15 and 20 marking mild, moderate, moderately severe and severe. The clinician-rated Hamilton Depression Rating Scale of Max Hamilton (1960) is not a self-report. Beck's family of scales also includes the Beck Anxiety Inventory and the Beck Hopelessness Scale. Option a is right because 0-13 minimal, 14-19 mild, 20-28 moderate and 29-63 severe are the conventional BDI-II severity bands given in the 1996 manual.","traps":{"b":"These narrow bands (0-5, 6-10, 11-15, 16 and above) do not belong to the BDI-II. On a scale whose total can reach 63, labelling everything from 16 upwards as severe would classify many mildly distressed people as severely depressed. It tempts because short scales with low maximums, such as the PHQ-9 with cut-offs at 5, 10, 15 and 20, do use small numbers, and candidates may borrow that pattern.","c":"This set (0-9, 10-15, 16-23, 24-40) is not the BDI-II scoring. Its 0-9 minimal band echoes the original 1961 BDI, but the remaining cut-offs and the ceiling of 40 are wrong; the BDI-II maximum is 63 because there are 21 items scored 0 to 3. It tempts because 0-9 looks familiar from older textbooks that give the first-edition BDI bands.","d":"Splitting 0 to 60 into three equal thirds is not how clinical cut-offs are made. BDI-II bands come from comparing scores with clinical diagnoses, which is why they are uneven and cluster at the low end. This option also omits a mild band and has the wrong ceiling, since the real maximum is 63. It tempts because equal-width bands look tidy and logical to a candidate who is guessing."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT) differs from Classical Test Theory (CTT) primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Classical test theory (CTT), rooted in Charles Spearman's work in the early 1900s, says every observed score is a true score plus random error (X = T + E). Item difficulty is the proportion passing an item (the p value), item discrimination is the item-total correlation, and one reliability coefficient and one standard error of measurement apply to everyone. The weakness is that these numbers depend on the sample tested. Item response theory (IRT), also called latent trait theory, models each item separately. Georg Rasch (1960) gave the one-parameter model, and Frederic Lord with Allan Birnbaum developed the two- and three-parameter logistic models, summarised in Lord and Melvin Novick (1968). The item characteristic curve is an S-shaped curve showing the probability of a correct answer at each level of the latent ability, theta. Three item parameters shape it: b (difficulty, the ability level at the curve's midpoint, where it rises most steeply), a (discrimination, the slope) and c (pseudo-guessing, the lower floor). The 1PL or Rasch model uses only b, the 2PL adds a, and the 3PL adds c. If the model fits, item parameters do not depend on the sample and ability estimates do not depend on which items were taken. Precision comes from the test information function and varies across ability levels. This allows computer-adaptive testing, in which each next item is chosen to suit the current ability estimate. Option a is right because IRT models the probability of a correct response from person ability and item parameters, which makes computer-adaptive testing possible.","traps":{"b":"IRT is not CTT renamed. CTT works at the level of the total score (true score plus error) and gives sample-dependent item statistics, while IRT models the response to each item as a mathematical function of ability and item parameters, under assumptions such as unidimensionality and local independence. It tempts only because both theories produce difficulty and discrimination indices, so on the surface they can look alike.","c":"IRT certainly estimates item difficulty: it is the b parameter, the location of the item characteristic curve on the ability scale, and it is present in every IRT model, including the one-parameter Rasch model. CTT expresses difficulty more crudely as the proportion of people who pass the item. It tempts because the question invites contrasts, and a candidate may wrongly assume IRT dropped an old concept.","d":"Even the simplest IRT model, the one-parameter model of Georg Rasch (1960), has one item parameter (difficulty) and a person parameter (ability, theta). A model without a person parameter would not be IRT, since the whole point is to place persons and items on the same scale. It tempts because the Rasch model is famous for using 'only one parameter', and candidates forget this refers to items."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Bender-Gestalt II) primarily assesses:","concept":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test was published by the American child psychiatrist Lauretta Bender in 1938. She took nine geometric designs (card A and cards 1 to 8) from Max Wertheimer's 1923 Gestalt studies of perceptual organisation and asked people simply to copy them on a blank sheet. Copying a design needs visual perception, motor coordination and the integration of the two, so errors appear when development is immature or the brain is damaged. Typical error signs are rotation of the figure, distortion of shape, perseveration (repeating dots or loops beyond what is needed), fragmentation, overlapping difficulty and poor integration of parts. Elizabeth Koppitz (1963) developed the Developmental Scoring System for young children, which made the test a standard school-readiness and learning-disability screen, while Gerald Pascal and Barbara Suttell (1951) produced an adult scoring system. In adults the test became a classic quick screen for 'organicity', though it cannot localise a lesion and misses many deficits. Max Hutt later offered a projective reading of the drawings, a secondary and weakly supported use. The Bender-Gestalt II of Gary Brannigan and Scott Decker (2003) has 16 designs, a copy and a recall phase, supplementary motor and perception tests, and norms from age 4 into late adulthood. Neighbouring tests include the Beery-Buktenica Developmental Test of Visual-Motor Integration, the Benton Visual Retention Test of Arthur Benton, and the Rey-Osterrieth Complex Figure. Option b is right because the Bender-Gestalt mainly measures visual-motor integration and serves as a screen for developmental and neuropsychological dysfunction.","traps":{"a":"Auditory memory is the ability to hold and recall information that is heard, tested by tasks such as Digit Span or the Logical Memory stories of the Wechsler Memory Scale. The Bender-Gestalt is entirely visual and motor: designs are shown on cards and copied by hand, and even the recall phase of Bender-Gestalt II asks for visual designs. It tempts because the newer recall phase makes the test sound memory-related.","c":"Verbal abstraction is the ability to see the common principle between words or ideas, tested by the Similarities subtest of the Wechsler scales or by proverb interpretation in a mental status examination. The Bender-Gestalt involves no language beyond simple instructions and measures the copying of geometric designs. It tempts because 'Gestalt' suggests understanding wholes, which candidates may equate with abstract thinking.","d":"Max Hutt and others did read Bender drawings projectively, treating size, placement and pressure as signs of personality. But this is a secondary and weakly validated use, and the word 'only' makes the option wrong outright. The test's primary purpose is visual-motor integration. It tempts because the test is often taught alongside projective drawing tests such as the House-Tree-Person and the Draw-A-Person."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as a:","concept":"Projective techniques present an ambiguous stimulus and an open task, on the assumption, called the projective hypothesis, that people reveal their own needs, conflicts and ways of seeing relationships in how they structure it. Lawrence K. Frank (1939) gave the approach its name. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) was introduced by Christiana Morgan and Henry A. Murray at the Harvard Psychological Clinic in 1935. The 1943 set has 31 cards, mostly vague black-and-white scenes of people in situations, plus one blank card; usually about 8 to 12 are chosen for a person. The instruction is to tell a story: what led up to the scene, what is happening, what the characters feel and think, and how it ends. 'Apperception' means interpreting a new perception in the light of past experience. Interpretation follows Murray's personology (1938): identify the hero the teller identifies with, the hero's needs (such as achievement, affiliation, aggression), the press or environmental forces acting on the hero, and the outcomes, which combine into themes. Gardner Lindzey (1959) classified projectives by response type: association (Rorschach), construction (TAT), completion (sentence completion), choice or ordering, and expression (drawings). Related tests include the Children's Apperception Test of Leopold Bellak (1949), with animal figures, and Uma Chowdhury's Indian adaptation of the TAT. Contrast these with objective tests, which use structured items and fixed scoring, such as the MMPI, 16PF or NEO inventories. Option d is right because the TAT is a projective technique in which people tell stories about ambiguous interpersonal scenes, developed by Morgan and Murray in 1935.","traps":{"a":"Achievement tests measure what a person has already learned in a specific area, such as reading or arithmetic, and are scored objectively against a key; the Wide Range Achievement Test is a familiar example. The TAT has no right or wrong answers and measures personality and motives, not attainment. It tempts because Murray's need for achievement, later scored from TAT stories by David McClelland, contains the word 'achievement'.","b":"Self-report personality inventories, such as the MMPI-2, the 16PF of Raymond Cattell and the NEO Personality Inventory, ask people to answer fixed statements about themselves and are scored against norms. The TAT asks for free stories and relies on interpretation of fantasy material. It tempts because both are personality measures, and the TAT does ask the person to reveal thoughts and feelings, though indirectly through story characters.","c":"A structured neuropsychological battery, such as the Halstead-Reitan or the Luria-Nebraska, uses standard tasks to assess brain functions like attention, memory, language and motor speed, and compares performance with norms to detect impairment. The TAT is not designed to detect brain damage and has no fixed, scored task structure. It tempts mainly because the TAT, like such batteries, comes as a set of materials in the clinical psychologist's toolkit."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is established by:","concept":"Validity is the degree to which evidence supports the interpretation of test scores for a given purpose. The classic 'trinitarian' division, still used in Indian textbooks, has three types: content, criterion-related and construct validity. Content validity asks whether the items adequately sample the domain, and it is judged by experts against a test blueprint. Face validity is only whether the test looks relevant to those taking it, and is not technical evidence. Criterion-related validity asks whether scores relate to an external criterion, such as a clinical diagnosis, job rating or exam result. It has two forms that differ only in timing. In concurrent validity the criterion is measured at about the same time as the test: a new short depression scale is given to patients on the same day they receive a structured clinical interview, or complete an established scale such as the BDI-II, and the two sets of scores are correlated. Concurrent validity suits tests meant to describe present status or to replace a longer, costlier measure. In predictive validity the criterion is collected later, as when an entrance test is correlated with grades a year on. The resulting correlation is the validity coefficient. Construct validity, set out by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), is broader, and includes convergent and discriminant evidence from the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959). Option a is right because concurrent validity correlates a new test with an established criterion measured at approximately the same time.","traps":{"b":"Correlating a test with future performance is predictive validity, the other form of criterion-related validity. The criterion is collected after a delay, for example correlating an aptitude test taken at admission with grades at the end of the course. Concurrent and predictive validity use the same logic and statistics and differ only in when the criterion is measured. It tempts because the two are always taught as a pair.","c":"Expert agreement that items represent the construct describes content validity, judged against a test blueprint, while the phrase 'appear to measure' describes face validity, which is how relevant the test looks to test takers and is not statistical evidence. Neither involves correlating scores with an external criterion. It tempts because expert judgment sounds like a respectable way of establishing validity, and content validity is taught in the same chapter.","d":"Comparing scores across two cultures addresses cross-cultural equivalence, measurement invariance or test bias, for example checking whether an imported Western scale works the same way in an Indian sample, or looking for differential item functioning. That is about fairness and generalisability, not criterion-related validity. It tempts because adapting foreign tests is a common Indian research theme and sounds like a validation step."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes which classic subtest?","concept":"Fixed neuropsychological batteries give every patient the same set of standardised tests so that patterns of impairment can be compared with norms. The best known is the Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery. Ward Halstead began it at the University of Chicago while studying patients with frontal lobe lesions, and described a 'biological intelligence' in his 1947 book Brain and Intelligence. His student Ralph Reitan refined and validated it from the 1950s. The core tests are the Category Test (abstract concept formation, 208 items), the Tactual Performance Test (placing blocks in a form board while blindfolded, with each hand and then both, followed by drawing the board from memory), the Seashore Rhythm Test (comparing pairs of rhythms, an attention measure), the Speech-Sounds Perception Test (matching spoken nonsense words to written choices), and the Finger Tapping Test (motor speed of each hand). Allied tests include Trail Making Parts A and B (visual scanning, speed and set-shifting), the Reitan-Indiana Aphasia Screening Test, sensory-perceptual examinations and grip strength. The Halstead Impairment Index is the proportion of core scores falling in the impaired range; the higher it is, the more likely brain dysfunction. The main rival is the Luria-Nebraska Neuropsychological Battery of Charles Golden and colleagues, built from Anne-Lise Christensen's (1975) version of Alexander Luria's qualitative methods. Indian clinicians also use the NIMHANS Neuropsychology Battery and the PGI Battery of Brain Dysfunction. Option a is right because the Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making, Speech-Sounds Perception and Finger Tapping are all classic Halstead-Reitan components.","traps":{"b":"The BDI (Beck Depression Inventory) is a 21-item self-report of depressive severity by Aaron T. Beck, and the HAM-D is the clinician-rated Hamilton Depression Rating Scale of Max Hamilton (1960). Both measure mood, not brain function, and neither is part of any neuropsychological battery. It tempts only because neuropsychological assessments often screen for depression, which can lower cognitive test performance and mimic impairment.","c":"The WAIS and the MMPI were often given alongside the Halstead-Reitan in a full workup, since a comprehensive examination usually included a Wechsler scale and personality testing, but they are separate instruments and not the battery's defining tests. The word 'only' also rules this option out. It tempts because the WAIS and MMPI are the two most famous tests in clinical psychology and were commonly reported together with the battery.","d":"The Bender-Gestalt Test of Lauretta Bender (1938) is a brief visual-motor copying test, often used in India as a quick screen for organicity. It is a single test, not a battery, and is not one of the Halstead-Reitan core tests. The word 'only' makes the option wrong outright. It tempts because both are associated with detecting brain damage, so candidates link them in memory."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily assess:","concept":"Adaptive behaviour is what a person actually does every day to meet the personal and social demands of their age and culture: communicating, looking after themselves, getting along with others and moving about safely. The idea goes back to Edgar A. Doll's Vineland Social Maturity Scale (1935), developed at the Vineland Training School in New Jersey, which yielded a social age and a social quotient. In India, A. J. Malin's adaptation of this scale is still widely used alongside IQ tests when assessing intellectual disability. Sara Sparrow, David Balla and Domenic Cicchetti revised it as the Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales in 1984, followed by Vineland-II (2005) and Vineland-3 (2016). Information comes from a caregiver or teacher, through a semi-structured interview or rating forms, and norms span birth to late adulthood. The four domains are Communication (receptive, expressive, written), Daily Living Skills (personal, domestic, community), Socialization (interpersonal relationships, play and leisure, coping) and Motor Skills (fine and gross, scored for younger children), combined into an Adaptive Behavior Composite with a mean of 100 and SD of 15. An optional Maladaptive Behavior section records problem behaviours. Its main use is diagnosing intellectual disability: DSM-5 (2013) requires deficits in both intellectual and adaptive functioning (conceptual, social and practical domains) beginning in the developmental period, and grades severity by adaptive functioning rather than IQ. Option d is right because the Vineland assesses adaptive functioning in communication, daily living skills, socialisation and motor skills across the lifespan.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is measured with tools such as the Beck Scale for Suicide Ideation or the Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS), which ask about thoughts, plans, intent and past attempts. The Vineland has no suicide ideation scale; its domains concern everyday skills such as communication and self-care. It tempts only as an unrelated clinical filler for a candidate who has not met the test before.","b":"Personality disorders are enduring patterns of inner experience and behaviour, assessed with structured interviews such as the SCID-5-PD or with inventories such as the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977). The Vineland measures everyday skills, not personality traits or pathology. It tempts because 'social' appears in both, and poor socialisation scores might be mistaken for interpersonal personality problems.","c":"Cognitive ability is measured by intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales or the Stanford-Binet, which assess what a person can do under standard conditions. The Vineland measures what the person typically does in daily life, so a person with average IQ can still have poor adaptive skills. The word 'only' rules this option out. It tempts because adaptive behaviour and IQ are both required for diagnosing intellectual disability."}},{"q":"The standard error of measurement (SEM) of a test is calculated as:","concept":"Classical test theory assumes that every observed score (X) is a true score (T) plus random error (E). The standard error of measurement (SEM) is the standard deviation of the scores a person would obtain over endless repeated testing, spread around the true score. Reliability (r) is the proportion of observed-score variance that is true-score variance, so the error proportion is 1 minus r, and error variance is SD squared times (1 minus r). Taking the square root gives SEM = SD multiplied by the square root of (1 minus r). Example: an IQ test with SD 15 and reliability .96 gives 15 times the square root of .04, which is 15 times .2, an SEM of 3 points. The SEM is used to build confidence bands around an obtained score: about 68 percent of the time the true score lies within 1 SEM, and about 95 percent within 1.96 SEM. So an IQ of 70 with SEM 3 has a 95 percent band of roughly 64 to 76, one reason why intellectual disability is not diagnosed from a single cut-off. Two limits help in variants: if r equals 1, SEM is zero; if r equals 0, SEM equals the SD. Do not confuse it with the standard error of estimate, used in prediction from a validity coefficient, or the standard error of the mean, SD divided by the square root of N. Option c is right because the SEM equals the test's SD multiplied by the square root of (1 minus its reliability).","traps":{"a":"SD divided by reliability gives a number larger than the SD whenever reliability is below 1, so a less reliable test would have an 'error' bigger than the spread of scores itself; with r = .50 and SD 15 it gives 30. The correct formula shrinks error as reliability rises. It tempts because it contains the right two ingredients, SD and reliability, combined in a plausible-looking way.","b":"SD multiplied by (1 + reliability) grows as reliability improves, which is backwards: a perfectly reliable test (r = 1) would have the largest error, twice the SD, instead of zero. Error must fall as reliability rises, which is why the real formula uses (1 minus r) under a square root. It tempts because it looks like the correct formula with only a sign changed and the root removed.","d":"SD multiplied by reliability also increases as reliability rises, so a perfect test (r = 1) would have an error equal to the full SD, and a useless test (r = 0) would have zero error, the reverse of reality. The proper formula needs the unreliable portion, 1 minus r, and its square root. It tempts because it is the simplest possible combination of the two terms."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale-IV (WMS-IV) yields which primary indices?","concept":"David Wechsler published the first Wechsler Memory Scale in 1945. It produced a single Memory Quotient, on the same mean-100 metric as IQ, so clinicians could compare memory with intelligence. Later revisions, WMS-R (1987) and WMS-III (1997), replaced the MQ with several index scores. The fourth edition, WMS-IV (2009), covers ages 16 to 90 and was co-normed with the WAIS-IV, so memory and intelligence scores can be compared directly. Its main subtests are Logical Memory I and II (recall of two short stories, immediately and after a delay of about 20 to 30 minutes), Verbal Paired Associates I and II (learning word pairs), Visual Reproduction I and II (drawing designs from memory), Designs I and II (recalling the content and position of designs on a grid), Spatial Addition and Symbol Span (visual working memory), plus an optional Brief Cognitive Status Exam screener. These combine into five indices for the Adult battery: Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory and Delayed Memory, each with a mean of 100 and SD of 15. The Older Adult battery (ages 65 to 90) drops Designs and Spatial Addition, so it has no Visual Working Memory Index. Auditory working memory tasks such as Digit Span and Letter-Number Sequencing were moved to the WAIS-IV, where they form its Working Memory Index. Clinically, a large gap between immediate and delayed indices points to rapid forgetting, as seen in Alzheimer's disease. Option c is right because Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory and Delayed Memory are the five primary WMS-IV indices.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ and Performance IQ were the two halves of the older Wechsler intelligence scales, from the Wechsler-Bellevue up to WAIS-III. They are intelligence scores, not memory indices, and were dropped from WAIS-IV in 2008. The WMS was never scored this way. It tempts because the WMS-IV belongs to the same Wechsler family and is co-normed with the WAIS-IV, so candidates borrow the most famous Wechsler labels.","b":"Crystallised and fluid intelligence come from Raymond Cattell's theory, later part of the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model: crystallised ability is accumulated knowledge, fluid ability is reasoning with novel problems. They are intelligence constructs, measured for example by the WISC-V Fluid Reasoning Index, not memory indices. The word 'only' adds a second error. It tempts because memory and intelligence are often assessed together in the same evaluation.","d":"Attention, concentration and vigilance are separate cognitive functions, measured by tasks such as digit span, cancellation tests or continuous performance tests. The older WMS-R did have an Attention/Concentration Index, but WMS-IV dropped it and moved auditory span tasks to the WAIS-IV. The word 'only' also makes the option too narrow. It tempts because attention underlies all memory performance and appeared in earlier editions."}},{"q":"Socratic questioning in CBT is used primarily to:","concept":"Socratic questioning takes its name from the Greek philosopher Socrates, who taught by asking a series of questions that led the learner to examine and revise his own ideas. Aaron T. Beck built it into cognitive therapy, described in Cognitive Therapy of Depression by Beck, John Rush, Brian Shaw and Gary Emery (1979). The therapy assumes that distress is maintained by automatic thoughts, quick evaluations such as 'I failed the test, so I am useless', which come from deeper intermediate beliefs and core beliefs. Rather than telling the client the thought is wrong, therapist and client act as co-investigators, a stance Beck called collaborative empiricism, and the thought is treated as a hypothesis to test. Typical questions are: What is the evidence for this thought? What is the evidence against it? Is there another way of looking at it? What is the worst that could happen, and how would you cope? What would you tell a friend who thought this? The aim is guided discovery: the client reaches a more balanced conclusion through their own reasoning, so the new belief is more convincing. It works alongside thought records, the downward arrow technique of David Burns (1980) for uncovering core beliefs, and behavioural experiments that test predictions in real life. Albert Ellis's rational emotive behaviour therapy (1955) uses more direct, didactic disputation. Option d is right because Socratic questioning helps clients examine the evidence for and against automatic thoughts and reach more adaptive conclusions through guided discovery.","traps":{"a":"Free association is the basic rule of psychoanalysis introduced by Sigmund Freud: the patient says whatever comes to mind, without censoring, and the analyst interprets the material, often tracing it back to childhood conflicts. It works on unconscious content through the past, while Socratic questioning works on conscious thoughts in the present. It tempts because both are verbal techniques in which the client does most of the talking.","b":"Exposure therapy, confronting feared situations until anxiety reduces or feared predictions are disconfirmed, is a behavioural method and remains first-line for phobias, panic and OCD. Socratic questioning does not replace it; in CBT for anxiety the two are combined, with questions used to prepare for exposure and to process what was learned. It tempts because both are core CBT tools, and candidates may think the cognitive one supersedes the behavioural one.","c":"Telling the client what to think is the opposite of the Socratic method. Direct persuasion or lecturing tends to provoke resistance and leaves the client dependent on the therapist. Beck's approach relies on the client discovering the alternative view through questions, and even Albert Ellis's more directive disputation aims at the client's own rethinking. It tempts because cognitive therapy is about changing thoughts, which candidates may misread as instructing."}},{"q":"The first-line behavioural treatment for OCD is:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves obsessions, intrusive and unwanted thoughts, images or urges that raise anxiety, and compulsions, repetitive acts or mental rituals done to reduce that anxiety or prevent a feared outcome. O. Hobart Mowrer's (1947) two-factor theory explains the maintenance: fear becomes attached to a trigger through classical conditioning, and the compulsion is then kept going by negative reinforcement because it brings quick relief. Each ritual also stops the person learning that the feared catastrophe would not have happened. Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) targets both links. Victor Meyer (1966) first reported it with inpatients in London, and it was refined by Isaac Marks, Stanley Rachman, and Edna Foa with Michael Kozak, whose emotional processing theory (1986) explained why it works. Therapist and client build a hierarchy of triggers rated in Subjective Units of Distress (SUDS), a measure from Joseph Wolpe, then the client faces them step by step, in real life (in vivo) and in imagination, while refraining from all rituals, including checking, washing, mental counting and reassurance-seeking. Anxiety falls with repeated exposure, and the feared outcome is disconfirmed. Newer inhibitory learning accounts, associated with Michelle Craske, stress violating expectancies rather than waiting for habituation. Clinical guidelines such as those of NICE and the American Psychiatric Association recommend CBT with ERP as the first-line psychological treatment, with SSRIs or clomipramine as first-line medication, often combined for severe cases. Option a is right because Exposure and Response Prevention is the first-line behavioural treatment for OCD, breaking the cycle between obsessional anxiety and compulsive relief.","traps":{"b":"Hypnotherapy uses a hypnotic trance, a state of focused attention and heightened suggestibility, to deliver therapeutic suggestions; its modern roots lie with James Braid in the 1840s, and Freud briefly used hypnosis before abandoning it. There is no good evidence that it treats OCD, and guidelines do not recommend it. It tempts because it sounds like a direct way to remove unwanted thoughts by suggestion.","c":"Free association is Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic technique of saying whatever comes to mind so the analyst can interpret unconscious conflicts. Freud wrote about obsessional neurosis, notably in the Rat Man case (1909), and traced it to anal-stage conflicts, but insight-oriented methods have weak evidence for reducing OCD symptoms. It tempts because OCD was historically a central psychoanalytic diagnosis and the technique is famous.","d":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe (1958), pairs relaxation with a graded, usually imagined fear hierarchy, based on reciprocal inhibition. It works well for specific phobias, but without blocking rituals it leaves the negative reinforcement of compulsions intact, and its results in OCD were poor. The word 'only' adds another flaw. It tempts because it also uses a hierarchy and gradual exposure, so it looks similar to ERP."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy was developed by Marsha M. Linehan at the University of Washington. She first tried standard cognitive behaviour therapy with chronically suicidal, self-harming women, many of whom met criteria for Borderline Personality Disorder (BPD), and found that a pure focus on change made them feel criticised, so many dropped out. Her first randomised trial was published in 1991, and the treatment and skills manuals in 1993. Her biosocial theory holds that BPD arises from a biological emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, intense reactions, slow return to baseline) interacting with an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotional expression. 'Dialectical' refers to holding and synthesising opposites, above all acceptance and change: the therapist validates the client while pushing for new behaviour, combining behaviour therapy with acceptance practices drawn from Zen. Standard DBT has four modes: weekly individual therapy, a weekly skills training group, telephone coaching between sessions, and a consultation team for therapists. The four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness. Individual sessions follow a target hierarchy: life-threatening behaviours first, then therapy-interfering behaviours, then quality-of-life problems, analysed with behavioural chain analysis. DBT has since been adapted for adolescents, substance use and eating disorders. Other evidence-based BPD therapies, useful for variants, are mentalization-based treatment of Anthony Bateman and Peter Fonagy, transference-focused psychotherapy of Otto Kernberg, and schema therapy of Jeffrey Young. Option d is right because Linehan created DBT for chronically suicidal clients with Borderline Personality Disorder.","traps":{"a":"Childhood ADHD is treated with behavioural parent training and classroom management for young children, and with stimulant medication such as methylphenidate for school-age children, often combined. DBT was built for adult emotional dysregulation and suicidality; adolescent adaptations exist, but ADHD was never its target. It tempts because ADHD involves impulsivity, and impulsivity is also a core feature of Borderline Personality Disorder.","b":"Panic disorder is treated with cognitive behavioural therapy based on David M. Clark's (1986) cognitive model, in which bodily sensations are catastrophically misinterpreted, together with interoceptive exposure, as in David Barlow's Panic Control Treatment. DBT was not designed for panic. It tempts because DBT teaches distress tolerance and emotion regulation skills, which sound relevant to managing panic attacks.","c":"Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotic medication, supported by family psychoeducation, social skills training, cognitive remediation and CBT for psychosis. DBT does not target positive or negative psychotic symptoms. It tempts because Linehan's clients were severely ill and often hospitalised, and because candidates sometimes confuse borderline personality with the old idea of being on the border of psychosis."}},{"q":"Rogers' core conditions for client-centered therapy are:","concept":"Carl Rogers developed client-centred therapy in the 1940s (Counseling and Psychotherapy, 1942; Client-Centered Therapy, 1951), later called the person-centred approach. His theory is that everyone has an actualising tendency toward growth, but children who receive love only when they behave in certain ways absorb conditions of worth. They then deny or distort experiences that do not fit their self-concept, and this incongruence between self and experience produces anxiety. In his 1957 paper on the 'necessary and sufficient conditions of therapeutic personality change', Rogers listed six conditions: two people in psychological contact; a client who is incongruent, vulnerable or anxious; a therapist who is congruent in the relationship; a therapist who feels unconditional positive regard for the client; a therapist who feels empathic understanding of the client's internal frame of reference; and the client perceiving this empathy and regard at least to a minimal degree. The three therapist conditions became the 'core conditions'. Congruence or genuineness means the therapist is real, with no professional facade. Unconditional positive regard means warm acceptance of the person without judgement, which removes conditions of worth. Accurate empathy means sensing the client's world as if it were one's own, without losing the 'as if' quality, and reflecting it back. Charles Truax and Robert Carkhuff later turned these into rating scales. Rogers' claim that they are sufficient is disputed, but they fed into common factors research and Edward Bordin's (1979) working alliance of goals, tasks and bond. Option c is right because Rogers' core conditions are empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence.","traps":{"a":"Interpretation, transference and free association are the tools of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis. The analyst interprets unconscious meaning, works with the patient's transference of past relationship patterns onto the analyst, and relies on the patient saying whatever comes to mind. Rogers rejected interpretation as the therapist imposing an expert view. It tempts because these are the most famous therapy terms, and candidates may match any set of three to Rogers.","b":"Modeling, role-play and homework are techniques of behavioural and cognitive behavioural therapy. Modeling comes from Albert Bandura's social learning theory, shown in the Bobo doll studies (1961); role-play is used in assertiveness and social skills training; homework extends practice between sessions. Rogers' approach is non-directive and uses none of these structured methods. It tempts because they are familiar therapy ingredients listed in a neat set of three.","d":"Confrontation, paradoxical intention and prescription are directive techniques. Paradoxical intention comes from Viktor Frankl's logotherapy: the client deliberately tries to produce the feared symptom. Symptom prescription is used in strategic family therapy, for example by Jay Haley, and confrontation features in some addiction and Gestalt work. These are the opposite of Rogers' non-directive acceptance. It tempts because they sound like named conditions of a therapy school."}},{"q":"Working through in psychoanalytic therapy refers to:","concept":"In psychoanalysis, insight alone rarely changes a person. Sigmund Freud (1914) explained why in his paper 'Remembering, Repeating and Working-Through'. Patients often cannot remember a conflict; instead they repeat it in action, especially in the transference, where old feelings toward parents are relived with the analyst. When the analyst interprets a resistance, the resistance does not vanish. Freud said the patient must be given time to become familiar with it and to work through it, continuing the analysis in defiance of it. Working through is therefore the slow, repeated process of meeting the same conflict, defence and interpretation in many different contexts: in dreams, in memories, in current relationships and in the transference. Each time, the link is seen again from a new angle until it is integrated emotionally, not just understood intellectually, and lasting change in feeling and behaviour follows. Ralph Greenson (1967) placed it as the final step in a sequence of analytic interventions: confrontation (pointing to a phenomenon), clarification (sharpening its details), interpretation (making its unconscious meaning conscious), and working through. Catharsis or abreaction, from Josef Breuer and Freud's Studies on Hysteria (1895), is emotional release when a memory is recalled. Corrective emotional experience, from Franz Alexander and Thomas French (1946), is re-experiencing an old conflict under better conditions. Working through is what turns a single insight into durable change. Option b is right because working through is the repeated re-examination of interpretations, resistances and conflicts in different contexts until insight is integrated and behaviour changes.","traps":{"a":"Solving problems through direct advice is a feature of supportive counselling, problem-solving therapy and guidance, where the helper suggests practical solutions. Classical psychoanalysis avoids advice, following Freud's principle of abstinence and the analyst's neutrality, because advice gratifies dependency and bypasses unconscious conflict. It tempts because 'working through' in everyday English means sorting out a problem, which sounds like practical problem solving.","c":"Ending therapy after a single insight is the reverse of working through. Freud's point was precisely that one insight, even a dramatic one, does not produce lasting change, because resistances return and the conflict reappears in new forms. Termination in analysis comes only after prolonged working through. It tempts because popular images of therapy show a sudden breakthrough moment that cures the patient.","d":"Free association without interpretation describes only the patient's side of the basic rule, saying whatever comes to mind. Freud's method requires the analyst to interpret the material, especially resistance and transference, and working through then repeats and deepens those interpretations. Association without interpretation would leave the unconscious meaning unspoken. It tempts because free association is the most recognisable psychoanalytic technique."}},{"q":"Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck, a psychiatrist trained in psychoanalysis, noticed in studies around 1960 that his depressed patients' dreams and spontaneous thoughts were full of themes of defeat and loss. In Depression: Clinical, Experimental, and Theoretical Aspects (1967) he proposed that depression is driven by distorted thinking rather than by anger turned inward. His cognitive model has three parts. First, the cognitive triad: a negative view of the self ('I am worthless, unlovable, inadequate'), of the world or ongoing experience ('Everything goes wrong; people reject me; demands are overwhelming'), and of the future ('It will never get better'). Second, negative schemas, stable core beliefs formed through early experiences that lie dormant until activated by a stressor such as loss or failure. Third, systematic cognitive errors that keep the schemas intact, including arbitrary inference, selective abstraction, overgeneralisation, magnification and minimisation, personalisation and dichotomous thinking. The future part of the triad is hopelessness, measured by the Beck Hopelessness Scale (1974) and a strong predictor of suicide. Variant questions contrast the triad with neighbouring theories. Martin Seligman's learned helplessness, based on his 1967 experiments with dogs, explains depression through perceived lack of control. Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale's reformulation (1978) adds internal, stable and global attributions for bad events. Albert Ellis's ABC model locates distress in irrational beliefs. Cognitive therapy (Beck, Rush, Shaw and Emery, 1979) tests and modifies the triad with thought records and Socratic questioning. Option c is right because Beck's cognitive triad consists of negative views of the self, the world or experience, and the future.","traps":{"a":"Mood, behaviour and somatic state are symptom domains of depression: the affective, behavioural and physical or vegetative features such as low mood, withdrawal, poor sleep and poor appetite. Beck's triad is not a list of symptoms but the content of depressive thinking, three targets of negative evaluation. It tempts because CBT formulations, such as the five-areas model, link thoughts with mood, behaviour and bodily sensations.","b":"Past, present and future sounds like a complete time frame, but only the future belongs to Beck's triad. Rumination about past failures is common in depression and is studied in Susan Nolen-Hoeksema's response styles theory (1991), yet the triad concerns the self, the world and the future. It tempts because the future element is correct, so the option looks partly right, and the three time points feel natural.","d":"Self, mother and father points to psychoanalytic and family-systems ideas, such as Freud's Oedipal triangle or Murray Bowen's triangles in family systems theory. Beck did see early family experience as the source of negative schemas, but the triad itself is about how the depressed person views the self, the world and the future. It tempts because 'self' is correct and the word 'triad' suggests a family triangle."}},{"q":"The 'spirit' of Motivational Interviewing is captured by the acronym PACE, which stands for:","concept":"Motivational Interviewing (MI) was first described by William R. Miller (1983) from his work with problem drinkers, and developed with Stephen Rollnick in books of 1991, 2002 and 2013. It is a collaborative, goal-oriented conversation that strengthens a person's own motivation for change by exploring and resolving ambivalence. Miller and Rollnick insist that MI is not a bag of tricks: its underlying spirit matters more than technique. In the third edition (2013) the spirit is summarised as PACE. Partnership means the counsellor works with the client as an equal, not as an expert who fixes. Acceptance has four parts: respect for the person's absolute worth, accurate empathy, support for autonomy, and affirmation of strengths. Compassion means actively promoting the client's welfare. Evocation means drawing out the client's own reasons for change rather than installing the counsellor's. The second edition described the spirit as collaboration, evocation and autonomy. The 2013 model has four processes: engaging, focusing, evoking and planning. The core skills are OARS: open questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries. Counsellors listen for change talk, summarised as DARN-CAT (desire, ability, reasons, need; commitment, activation, taking steps), as opposed to sustain talk, and they resist the righting reflex, the urge to argue for change, which only provokes the client to defend the status quo. MI is often paired with the transtheoretical stages of change of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983), from precontemplation to maintenance, but the two models are distinct. Option a is right because PACE stands for Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion and Evocation, the spirit of MI.","traps":{"b":"Presence, Affirmation, Confrontation, Empathy mixes real MI ideas (affirmation is one of the OARS skills, and empathy sits within acceptance) with confrontation, which MI explicitly rejects. Miller developed MI partly as an alternative to the confrontational style then common in addiction treatment, because arguing with clients increases resistance. It tempts because three of its four words sound warm and client-centred, making the impostor easy to miss.","c":"Practice, Active listening, Change, Engagement borrows general counselling vocabulary. Engaging is the first of the four MI processes and active listening resembles reflective listening, but these are skills and processes, not elements of the spirit, and 'practice' and 'change' are not part of PACE. It tempts because it fits the PACE letters and includes words that genuinely appear in MI teaching.","d":"Plan, Act, Check, Evaluate resembles the Plan-Do-Check-Act cycle of quality improvement associated with W. Edwards Deming, and generic problem-solving steps. It describes a management routine, not the relational attitude of MI. Planning is MI's fourth process, which adds to the confusion. It tempts because it is a familiar four-letter cycle that sounds orderly and practical, and it starts with the right letter."}},{"q":"In Minuchin's structural family therapy, enmeshment refers to:","concept":"Salvador Minuchin developed structural family therapy in the 1960s and 1970s, working with poor urban families and later at the Philadelphia Child Guidance Clinic; his key book is Families and Family Therapy (1974). He saw a family as a system organised by an invisible structure: subsystems (spousal, parental, sibling), a hierarchy in which parents hold executive authority, and boundaries, the rules that decide who takes part in what and how. Healthy boundaries are clear: members support one another but each keeps some autonomy, and the parental subsystem is protected from children's interference. Boundaries go wrong in two directions. Diffuse boundaries produce enmeshment: members are over-involved, intrude on one another's feelings and decisions, speak for each other, and react strongly when one member is upset, so belonging is high but autonomy is low. A mother who reads her teenager's diary and cannot tolerate any separation is a classic example. Rigid boundaries produce disengagement: members are isolated and independent, and support arrives only under extreme stress. Minuchin, Bernice Rosman and Lester Baker (1978) described 'psychosomatic families', for example in anorexia nervosa, as showing enmeshment, overprotectiveness, rigidity and lack of conflict resolution. Therapy starts with joining the family, then mapping its structure, then restructuring through enactments, unbalancing and boundary making. Do not confuse enmeshment with Murray Bowen's emotional fusion, the opposite of differentiation of self, or with triangulation, in which a child is pulled into parental conflict. Option a is right because enmeshment means diffuse boundaries with over-involvement, intrusion and loss of autonomy among family members.","traps":{"b":"Strategic paradoxical interventions belong to strategic family therapy, associated with Jay Haley, Cloe Madanes and the Mental Research Institute group in Palo Alto, and to the Milan school. The therapist may prescribe the symptom or forbid change so that resistance itself produces improvement. These are techniques, not a description of family structure. It tempts because Haley worked with Minuchin in Philadelphia, so the two schools are often taught together.","c":"Rigid boundaries with disengagement are the opposite pole of Minuchin's boundary continuum. Disengaged families have walls between members: little contact, low warmth, high independence, and support only in crisis. Enmeshment is the diffuse end, with too much closeness rather than too little. It tempts because both terms come from the same theory and are always taught as a pair, so candidates mix up which label goes with which boundary.","d":"A complete lack of communication is an extreme form of disengagement, not enmeshment, and even disengaged families communicate in some way. Communication problems were the focus of other approaches, such as the double bind hypothesis of Gregory Bateson and colleagues (1956) and Virginia Satir's communication stances. Enmeshed families, if anything, communicate too intrusively. It tempts because family dysfunction is often equated with poor communication."}},{"q":"Yalom's emphasis on the 'here-and-now' in group therapy is intended to:","concept":"Irvin D. Yalom set out his interpersonal model of group therapy in The Theory and Practice of Group Psychotherapy, first published in 1970 and later revised with Molyn Leszcz. Drawing on Harry Stack Sullivan's view that psychological problems are largely problems in relationships, Yalom argued that a therapy group becomes a social microcosm. Given time and freedom, each member starts to behave in the group the way they behave in the rest of their life: the person who feels exploited at work soon feels exploited by fellow members. The group therefore does not need to dwell on distant events, the 'there and then'; the problem appears live in the room. Working in the here-and-now has two stages. First, activation: members notice and express what they feel toward one another, the group and the therapist right now. Second, process illumination: the group steps back to reflect on what just happened and why, the self-reflective loop. Experience without reflection is only catharsis; reflection without experience is an intellectual exercise. Through feedback, members learn how they affect others and try new behaviour, which Yalom called interpersonal learning. It is one of his 11 therapeutic factors, along with instillation of hope, universality, imparting information, altruism, corrective recapitulation of the primary family group, development of socialising techniques, imitative behaviour, group cohesiveness, catharsis and existential factors. Option d is right because Yalom uses immediate group interactions as a social microcosm, observing and reflecting on patterns as they emerge in real time to drive change.","traps":{"a":"Yalom does not ban outside material. Members naturally talk about their lives, and the therapist's task is to link that content back to what is happening in the group, for example by asking whether the same pattern is showing up with other members. Avoiding all outside discussion would be a rigid rule, not the here-and-now method. It tempts because the here-and-now does shift attention away from the 'there and then'.","b":"Yalom never claimed that group therapy should permanently replace individual therapy. The two can be combined, and the choice depends on the client's problems and goals; group work suits interpersonal difficulties especially well. The here-and-now is a technique within the group, not a policy about other treatments. It tempts because Yalom argued that group therapy is as effective as individual therapy for many problems.","c":"Bypassing group dynamics is the opposite of Yalom's approach. Process illumination, the second stage of the here-and-now, is exactly the examination of group dynamics: who speaks, who is silent, who allies with whom, and how members react to the leader. Without that reflection, experience stays raw and no learning follows. It tempts only through careless reading, since the option contradicts the central idea of the method."}},{"q":"Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition is the basis for:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist, set out reciprocal inhibition in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He borrowed the term from the physiologist Charles Sherrington, who described how activating one muscle inhibits its opposing muscle. Wolpe's psychological version states that if a response incompatible with anxiety is made to occur in the presence of anxiety-provoking stimuli, so that the anxiety is wholly or partly suppressed, the bond between those stimuli and anxiety is weakened. You cannot be deeply relaxed and highly anxious at the same moment. His evidence came from cats given shocks in an experimental cage: they became 'neurotic' and refused to eat there, but when fed first in rooms very unlike the cage and then in progressively more similar rooms, the fear faded. This is counterconditioning, foreshadowed by Mary Cover Jones's (1924) treatment of a boy named Peter who feared rabbits. Wolpe applied it as systematic desensitisation in three steps: train deep muscle relaxation, based on Edmund Jacobson's (1929) progressive relaxation; build an anxiety hierarchy ranked in Subjective Units of Distress (SUDS, 0 to 100); then imagine each item while relaxed, moving up only when the current item no longer causes anxiety. Assertive and sexual responses can also serve as anxiety inhibitors. Later research showed that exposure itself is the key ingredient and relaxation is not essential, which led to in vivo exposure and flooding. Option b is right because Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition principle is the basis of systematic desensitisation, which pairs relaxation with graded exposure to a fear hierarchy.","traps":{"a":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, set out by Steven C. Hayes, Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson (1999) and grounded in relational frame theory, aims not to reduce anxiety but to change the person's relationship to it, through acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment awareness and committed action guided by values. It deliberately drops the goal of suppressing anxiety that reciprocal inhibition depends on. It tempts because both belong to the broad behavioural tradition.","c":"Paradoxical intention, introduced by Viktor Frankl in logotherapy, asks the client to try deliberately to bring on the feared symptom, for instance to try hard to stay awake in insomnia, which breaks the cycle of anticipatory anxiety. It works through humour and self-distancing, not by pairing an incompatible response with anxiety. It tempts because it is also used for anxiety and phobias and is another named technique from the same era.","d":"Free association is Sigmund Freud's basic rule of psychoanalysis: the patient reports every thought without censorship so the analyst can uncover unconscious conflicts. Wolpe rejected psychoanalytic explanations of neurosis and built his method on learning theory instead. Nothing in free association pairs an antagonistic response with anxiety. It tempts because Wolpe was a psychiatrist treating neurotic anxiety, the same kind of patients Freud had treated."}},{"q":"The 'hexaflex' of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) consists of:","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) was developed by Steven C. Hayes with Kirk Strosahl and Kelly Wilson; their main text appeared in 1999. It is a third-wave behaviour therapy rooted in functional contextualism and in Relational Frame Theory, Hayes's account of how language lets humans relate events to each other, so that a thought can hurt like a real threat. ACT does not try to change the content of thoughts, as the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck does; it changes the person's relationship to them. The goal is psychological flexibility: staying open to the present moment and acting on chosen values even while painful thoughts and feelings are present. The hexaflex is the hexagon diagram of the six processes that build flexibility. Acceptance means willingly making room for unpleasant experience. Cognitive defusion means seeing thoughts as passing words, not literal truths. Self-as-context is the observing self that notices experience without being defined by it. Contact with the present moment is flexible, mindful attention to now. Values are freely chosen life directions. Committed action is behaviour guided by those values. Each process has an opposite in the inflexibility model of suffering: experiential avoidance, cognitive fusion, attachment to the conceptualised self, rigid focus on past and future, unclear values, and inaction or impulsivity. Examiners pair ACT with other third-wave therapies: Dialectical Behaviour Therapy by Marsha Linehan (1993) and Mindfulness-Based Cognitive Therapy by Zindel Segal, Mark Williams and John Teasdale (2002). Option d is right because it lists exactly the six core processes of the ACT hexaflex.","traps":{"a":"Neurotransmitter systems such as dopamine, serotonin, noradrenaline, acetylcholine, GABA and glutamate belong to biological psychiatry and psychopharmacology. ACT is a process-based psychological therapy that works on how a person relates to thoughts and feelings, not on brain chemistry. The number six is the only link to the hexaflex. It tempts candidates who remember that the hexaflex has six points but not what those points are.","b":"The DSM groups disorders into chapters, and DSM personality disorders fall into three clusters (A odd-eccentric, B dramatic-erratic, C anxious-fearful), not six. ACT is deliberately transdiagnostic: the hexaflex describes processes that cut across diagnoses rather than diagnostic categories. It tempts because 'six' plus a familiar clinical term sounds like a real model, and DSM clusters are a common exam topic.","c":"These are basic domains of general and cognitive psychology, roughly the chapter headings of an introductory textbook. ACT does train attention (contact with the present moment) and works with emotion and behaviour, which gives the option surface overlap. But the hexaflex names therapeutic processes of flexibility, not mental functions. It tempts because it also lists six psychological terms in a neat sequence."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) targets four main problem areas:","concept":"Interpersonal Psychotherapy (IPT) was developed by Gerald L. Klerman and Myrna M. Weissman as a brief treatment for major depression; its manual appeared in 1984. Its roots are the interpersonal school of Harry Stack Sullivan, the psychobiology of Adolf Meyer, and the attachment theory of John Bowlby. The central idea is that depression, whatever its causes, occurs in an interpersonal context: mood affects relationships and relationships affect mood. So IPT works on current relationships in the here and now, not on childhood conflicts (psychodynamic therapy) or on distorted thoughts (cognitive therapy). It is time-limited, usually 12 to 16 weekly sessions, in three phases. In the initial phase the therapist diagnoses depression as a medical illness, gives the patient the 'sick role', takes an interpersonal inventory, and links the depression to one or two problem areas. The four areas are grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes (conflicting expectations with a spouse, parent or colleague), role transitions (life changes such as divorce, retirement, job loss or becoming a parent), and interpersonal deficits (social isolation or a lack of sustaining relationships, chosen when none of the others applies). The middle phase works on the chosen area; the final phase consolidates gains. IPT was one of the treatments tested in the NIMH Treatment of Depression Collaborative Research Program, alongside CBT and imipramine. Adaptations include IPT for adolescents and the Interpersonal and Social Rhythm Therapy of Ellen Frank for bipolar disorder. Option d is right because grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits are the four IPT problem areas.","traps":{"a":"Schemas, automatic thoughts and behaviours are the targets of the cognitive model of Aaron T. Beck, whose cognitive therapy manual for depression appeared in 1979, and biological factors belong to biopsychosocial formulation. CBT changes how a person thinks and acts; IPT changes how the person handles current relationships. It tempts because CBT and IPT are the two best-evidenced brief therapies for depression and are always compared.","b":"Id, ego, superego and defences come from the structural model of Sigmund Freud (1923) and from Anna Freud's (1936) work on defence mechanisms. Psychodynamic therapy explores unconscious conflict rooted in the past. IPT deliberately avoids interpreting intrapsychic conflict and stays with present relationships. It tempts because IPT grew partly out of psychodynamically trained clinicians and the interpersonal theory of Harry Stack Sullivan.","c":"Cognitive, affective, motor and somatic are symptom domains, roughly how the symptoms of a depressive episode are grouped: negative thoughts, low mood, psychomotor change, and sleep or appetite change. They describe what depression looks like, not where IPT intervenes. IPT relieves symptoms by targeting the interpersonal problem area linked to their onset. It tempts because it sounds like a neat four-part clinical list."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which therapeutic school?","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding text, written with Ralph Hefferline, appeared in 1951, and Perls later popularised it at the Esalen Institute. It borrowed the name, and the idea of figure and ground, from Gestalt perception psychology, but it is a humanistic-existential therapy. Its aims are awareness in the here and now, taking responsibility for one's own feelings, and completing 'unfinished business', the unexpressed feelings (resentment, grief, guilt) toward people or events that keep intruding into present life. Therapists ask 'what' and 'how' rather than 'why', and use active experiments instead of interpretation. The best-known experiment is the empty chair. The client imagines a person, often someone dead or absent such as a parent, sitting in an empty chair and speaks to them directly in the present tense. In the two-chair variant the client switches seats to voice both sides of an inner conflict, for example the demanding 'top dog' and the evasive 'underdog', or a disowned part of the self, so that split-off feelings are owned and integrated. Related Gestalt methods include the hot seat in groups and exaggerating a gesture. Distinguish it from psychodrama, the group method of Jacob L. Moreno, which uses a protagonist, auxiliary egos and role reversal, and from which Perls is often said to have borrowed the chair. Leslie Greenberg later built empty-chair and two-chair work into emotion-focused therapy with research support. Option c is right because the empty chair is the signature experiment of Gestalt therapy as developed by Fritz Perls.","traps":{"a":"Person-centred therapy was developed by Carl Rogers; his book Client-Centered Therapy appeared in 1951. The therapist offers three core conditions (empathy, unconditional positive regard and congruence) and follows the client non-directively, mainly through reflection. Setting up a chair dialogue is a directive experiment, so it does not fit. It tempts because Rogers and Perls both belong to humanistic psychology and are taught in the same chapter.","b":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy was developed by Marsha Linehan, with the main manual published in 1993, for chronically suicidal people with borderline personality disorder. It balances acceptance and change through four skills modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation and interpersonal effectiveness, plus individual therapy and phone coaching. It has no chair dialogue. It tempts because DBT also works with intense emotion and uses experiential, mindfulness-based exercises.","d":"Cognitive Behaviour Therapy, built on the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck and the rational emotive behaviour therapy of Albert Ellis, works by identifying and testing thoughts through thought records, Socratic questioning and behavioural experiments. Its focus is the content and accuracy of beliefs, not emotional enactment. Some CBT therapists borrow role-play, which gives the option slight appeal, but the empty chair is not a CBT technique."}},{"q":"Francine Shapiro's EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing) is recommended primarily for:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by the American psychologist Francine Shapiro. She reported noticing in 1987 that moving her eyes rapidly from side to side seemed to reduce the distress of disturbing thoughts, and she published her first controlled study with trauma survivors in 1989. In EMDR the client briefly holds a traumatic memory in mind, with its image, negative self-belief and body sensations, while following the therapist's fingers back and forth, or receiving alternating taps or tones; this is called bilateral or dual-attention stimulation. Treatment follows an eight-phase protocol: history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitisation, installation of a positive belief, body scan, closure and re-evaluation. Shapiro's explanation is the adaptive information processing model, in which trauma memories are stored unprocessed and EMDR helps integrate them. A popular research explanation is working memory taxation: doing an eye-movement task while recalling makes the memory less vivid and less emotional. Whether the eye movements themselves are necessary is still debated. Even so, EMDR is recommended for post-traumatic stress disorder by the World Health Organization (2013) and by NICE, alongside trauma-focused CBT, the Prolonged Exposure therapy of Edna Foa and the Cognitive Processing Therapy of Patricia Resick. For variant questions, remember the first-line pairings: OCD with exposure and response prevention, schizophrenia with antipsychotics plus family intervention and CBT for psychosis, and bipolar disorder with mood stabilisers such as lithium plus psychoeducation. Option a is right because EMDR was designed for, and has its main evidence base in, PTSD and other trauma-related conditions.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder treated first with antipsychotic medication, supported by family interventions, CBT for psychosis and rehabilitation. EMDR is not a primary treatment for it, although trials have used it to treat comorbid PTSD in people with psychosis. It tempts because trauma is common in people with psychosis, and because exams like to list a severe disorder as a distractor.","c":"Bipolar disorder is managed mainly with mood stabilisers such as lithium and valproate, plus psychoeducation and therapies such as the Interpersonal and Social Rhythm Therapy of Ellen Frank, which stabilises daily routines and sleep. EMDR is not a standard treatment for manic or depressive episodes. It tempts because bipolar disorder is a major diagnosis and some patients also carry a trauma history.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder is treated first with exposure and response prevention, which grew from the work of Victor Meyer (1966) and was developed further by Edna Foa, and with SSRIs or clomipramine. ERP has the person face feared triggers while blocking rituals. EMDR is not first-line. It tempts because both involve confronting distressing material, and 'desensitization' sounds like exposure therapy."}},{"q":"A Type II error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"Null hypothesis significance testing starts from the null hypothesis (H0), usually that there is no difference or no relationship, and asks whether the data are unlikely enough under H0 to reject it. The decision framework of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson (1933) recognises that any such decision can be wrong in two ways. A Type I error is rejecting H0 when it is actually true: a false positive, concluding there is an effect that does not exist. Its probability is alpha, the significance level fixed in advance, usually 0.05. A Type II error is failing to reject H0 when it is actually false: a false negative, missing an effect that really exists. Its probability is beta. Statistical power is 1 minus beta, the probability of detecting a real effect; Jacob Cohen (1988) recommended power of at least 0.80, which allows beta of 0.20. Power, and so protection against Type II error, increases with a larger sample, a larger true effect size, lower variability, a more lenient alpha, and a one-tailed test in the correct direction. The two errors trade off: making alpha stricter (say 0.01) reduces Type I errors but raises Type II errors for a fixed sample. In the fable of the boy who cried wolf, believing him when there is no wolf is Type I, and ignoring him when the wolf is real is Type II. Option c is right because a Type II error means not rejecting a false null, a false negative that misses a true alternative.","traps":{"a":"The p-value is not set; it is calculated from the data. What the researcher sets is alpha, the significance threshold. A very low alpha, such as 0.001, does make Type II errors more likely, because a real effect must be very strong to pass. So this option names a possible contributing cause, not the definition. It tempts because it confuses p with alpha and hints at a genuine trade-off.","b":"A very large sample increases power, so it reduces the chance of a Type II error rather than causing it. The real risk with huge samples is the opposite: trivially small effects become statistically significant, which is why effect sizes such as Cohen's d should be reported alongside p-values. It tempts because candidates sense that sample size affects errors but reverse the direction.","d":"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error, a false positive, whose probability is alpha (usually 0.05). It is the error of claiming an effect that does not exist, for example concluding that a drug works when it does not. It tempts because it is the twin definition and the two are easy to swap under exam pressure; remember Type I is a false alarm and Type II is a miss."}},{"q":"ANOVA (analysis of variance) is most appropriate when comparing:","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher in the 1920s and set out in his 1925 book Statistical Methods for Research Workers; the F statistic was later named in his honour by George Snedecor. ANOVA tests whether the means of three or more groups differ on a continuous dependent variable. Why not run several t-tests? Each test carries a 5 percent risk of a Type I error, so three groups needing three t-tests push the family-wise error rate to about 14 percent. ANOVA asks one omnibus question instead. It splits total variability into between-group variance (how far group means lie from the grand mean) and within-group variance (how far individuals lie from their own group mean). F equals mean square between divided by mean square within, with k minus 1 and N minus k degrees of freedom. If the null hypothesis is true, F is close to 1; a large F means group differences exceed chance. A significant F says only that some means differ, so post-hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD, Scheffe or Bonferroni locate which pairs. Assumptions are normality, independence and homogeneity of variance, checked with Levene's test. Variants: factorial (two-way) ANOVA tests two independent variables and their interaction; repeated-measures ANOVA handles the same participants measured several times; ANCOVA adds a covariate; MANOVA handles several dependent variables; Kruskal-Wallis is the non-parametric version. With exactly two groups, F equals t squared. Option d is right because one-way ANOVA is designed to compare three or more independent group means on a continuous outcome.","traps":{"a":"Two paired observations on the same subject, such as a pre-test and post-test score, are analysed with the paired (dependent) samples t-test, or its non-parametric partner the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. Repeated-measures ANOVA is usually kept for three or more measurements of the same people. It tempts because repeated-measures ANOVA exists, so candidates think any within-subject design belongs to ANOVA.","b":"Two categorical variables, such as gender and diagnosis, are tested for association with the chi-square test of independence of Karl Pearson (1900), using counts in a contingency table. ANOVA needs a categorical independent variable but a continuous dependent variable, because it compares means, and categories have frequencies, not means. It tempts because ANOVA does use a categorical grouping variable, so 'categorical' sounds relevant.","c":"Two independent group means on a continuous outcome are compared with the independent-samples t-test, based on the t distribution of William Sealy Gosset, who published as 'Student' in 1908. ANOVA would give the same answer here, since F equals t squared with two groups, but the t-test is the conventional choice. It tempts because it is half right; ANOVA becomes the most appropriate test once there are three or more groups."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 indicates:","concept":"The odds ratio (OR) is a measure of association between an exposure and an outcome, used especially in case-control studies and logistic regression. Odds are not the same as probability: odds equal the probability that an event happens divided by the probability that it does not, so a probability of 0.5 gives odds of 1 (1 to 1). In a 2 x 2 table with cells a (exposed, outcome), b (exposed, no outcome), c (unexposed, outcome) and d (unexposed, no outcome), the OR is (a/b) divided by (c/d), which equals ad/bc. Interpretation turns on the null value 1. An OR of 1 means the odds of the outcome are the same in exposed and unexposed people, so there is no association. An OR above 1 means the exposure goes with higher odds (a risk factor); an OR of 2 means the odds are doubled. An OR below 1 means lower odds (a protective factor); 0.5 halves the odds. If the 95 percent confidence interval of an OR includes 1, the association is not statistically significant. Neighbouring measures share the same null value: the relative risk or risk ratio (risk in exposed divided by risk in unexposed, from cohort studies and trials) and the hazard ratio. The OR approximates the relative risk when the outcome is rare, the rare disease assumption shown by Jerome Cornfield (1951). For differences the null value is 0, not 1. Option d is right because an odds ratio of exactly 1.0 means equal odds in both groups, that is, no association.","traps":{"a":"A protective exposure is one associated with lower odds of the outcome, shown by an odds ratio below 1. For example, an OR of 0.6 for exercise and depression would mean exercisers have 40 percent lower odds of depression. At exactly 1.0 there is no difference in either direction. It tempts because candidates sometimes treat 1 as the cut-off for protection and forget that it marks no effect.","b":"Causation cannot be read from any single odds ratio, whatever its value. Odds ratios usually come from observational designs such as case-control studies, where confounding and bias are possible; causal judgement needs criteria such as those of Austin Bradford Hill (1965), for example temporality, strength, consistency and dose-response. An OR of 1.0 shows not even an association. It tempts because '1' can feel like a definite, positive result.","c":"An exposure that doubles the odds of the outcome has an odds ratio of 2.0, because the odds in the exposed group are twice those in the unexposed group. An OR of 1.0 means the two odds are equal. It tempts because candidates sometimes treat an OR as an increase added to a baseline, imagining that 1.0 means 'one extra time' or 100 percent more, which is wrong."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.5 conventionally represents:","concept":"Cohen's d, proposed by Jacob Cohen in his book on statistical power (second edition 1988), is the standardised mean difference: the difference between two group means divided by the pooled standard deviation. Because it is in standard-deviation units, it lets you compare effects across studies that used different scales, and it is a basic currency of meta-analysis. Cohen offered rough benchmarks for the behavioural sciences: d of about 0.2 is small, 0.5 is medium and 0.8 is large. He described a medium effect as one visible to the naked eye, and illustrated it with the height difference between 14- and 18-year-old girls. A d of 0.5 means the two group means are half a standard deviation apart; with normal distributions, the average member of the higher group scores above about 69 percent of the lower group. Effect size differs from statistical significance: a p-value depends on sample size, whereas d describes magnitude, so a tiny effect can be significant in a huge sample. Know the parallel benchmarks, which examiners swap in: for Pearson's r, 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large; for eta squared in ANOVA, 0.01, 0.06 and 0.14; for Cohen's f, 0.10, 0.25 and 0.40. Hedges' g is a version of d corrected for small-sample bias, and Glass's delta uses only the control group's standard deviation. Cohen himself warned that the labels are conventions, to be used only when no field-specific yardstick exists. Option b is right because, by Cohen's conventions, a d of 0.5 represents a medium effect.","traps":{"a":"Cohen gave no 'trivial' category, but values well below 0.2 are often described informally as trivial or negligible, because the two groups overlap almost completely. A d of 0.5 is two and a half times the small benchmark, so it is far from trivial. It tempts because half a standard deviation sounds like a small number, and candidates confuse the raw size of the figure with its meaning.","c":"A small effect is a d of about 0.2, a difference of one fifth of a standard deviation that is real but hard to see without statistics, like the height difference between 15- and 16-year-old girls in Cohen's example. A d of 0.5 is well above that. It tempts because any value below 1 can feel small, and candidates who only remember that 0.2 is the first cut-off guess downward.","d":"A large effect is a d of about 0.8 or more, nearly a full standard deviation, which Cohen likened to the height difference between 13- and 18-year-old girls. A d of 0.5 falls short of that. It tempts because 0.5 is the large benchmark for Pearson's correlation r (0.1, 0.3, 0.5), so candidates who mix up the d and r scales choose large."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling differs from simple random sampling in that it:","concept":"Sampling methods fall into two families. Probability sampling gives every member of the population a known, non-zero chance of selection, which allows sampling error to be estimated and results generalised. Non-probability sampling (convenience, volunteer, purposive, quota, snowball) does not. Simple random sampling is the baseline: every member, and every possible sample of that size, has an equal chance, as in a lottery. Its weakness is that, by chance, a small subgroup may be under-represented. Stratified random sampling fixes this. The researcher first divides the population into mutually exclusive, exhaustive strata on a variable related to the outcome, such as gender, age band, or rural versus urban residence, and then draws a simple random sample within each stratum. In proportionate stratification each stratum's share of the sample matches its share of the population; in disproportionate stratification small strata are oversampled so they can be analysed separately, and weights are applied later. When strata are internally similar, stratification gives more precise estimates than a simple random sample of the same size. Do not confuse it with cluster sampling, which randomly selects whole natural groups (schools, villages, hospital wards): strata should be internally homogeneous and all are sampled, while clusters should each resemble the population and only some are chosen. Systematic sampling takes every kth person after a random start. Quota sampling resembles stratification but fills each subgroup quota non-randomly, so it is non-probability. Option d is right because stratified sampling divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, guaranteeing every stratum is represented.","traps":{"a":"Selecting every nth (or kth) subject from a list after a random start is systematic sampling. The sampling interval k is the population size divided by the sample size. It is quick and spreads the sample evenly, but it can be biased if the list has a periodic pattern that matches the interval. It tempts because systematic sampling is also a probability method and appears in the same textbook list.","b":"Selecting intact natural groups, such as schools, villages or hospital wards, is cluster sampling. Only some clusters are chosen, so whole subgroups can be missed, and precision is usually lower than with stratification. It is used because it is cheap when no full list of individuals exists. It tempts because both methods start by dividing the population into groups, but stratified sampling samples from every group.","c":"Using only volunteers is volunteer or self-selected sampling, a non-probability method closely related to convenience sampling. Volunteers tend to differ from non-volunteers, for example by being more motivated or more educated, so the sample is biased and results generalise poorly. Stratified sampling is a probability method in which the researcher, not the participant, controls selection. It tempts mainly as a filler, since many real studies rely on volunteers."}},{"q":"Reliability and validity differ in that:","concept":"Reliability and validity are the two core psychometric properties of any test. Reliability is consistency: does the test give the same result under the same conditions, free of random error? In classical test theory an observed score equals true score plus error, and the reliability coefficient is the proportion of observed variance that is true-score variance, ranging from 0 to 1. Types are test-retest (stability over time), parallel or alternate forms, internal consistency (split-half with the Spearman-Brown formula, Kuder-Richardson 20, and the coefficient alpha of Lee Cronbach, 1951), and inter-rater reliability. Validity is accuracy: does the test measure the construct it claims to measure, and are inferences from its scores justified? Types are face validity, content validity, criterion validity (concurrent and predictive), and construct validity, introduced by Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl (1955), with convergent and discriminant evidence assessed through the multitrait-multimethod matrix of Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske (1959). The key relationship is one-way. Reliability is necessary but not sufficient for validity: a bathroom scale that always reads 5 kg too high is perfectly reliable but not valid. An unreliable test cannot be valid, because its scores are mostly noise; formally, the validity coefficient cannot exceed the square root of the reliability coefficient. The archery analogy helps: arrows clustered tightly show reliability, arrows clustered on the bullseye show validity. Option a is right because it defines reliability as consistency and validity as measuring the intended construct, and states their correct one-way relationship.","traps":{"b":"Inter-rater reliability, the agreement between independent scorers, is only one form of reliability; it is measured by percentage agreement, Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960) or the intraclass correlation. Reliability also includes test-retest, parallel forms and internal consistency. Many tests, such as self-report questionnaires, have no rater at all yet still need reliability. It tempts because inter-rater agreement is the type most discussed for clinical diagnosis.","c":"Reliability and validity are separate concepts. Reliability is about random error and consistency; validity is about systematic accuracy and meaning. A test can be highly consistent while measuring the wrong thing, such as using head circumference as a test of intelligence. It tempts because both words describe 'good' tests, everyday speech uses them loosely, and both are often reported as coefficients.","d":"There is no rule that validity must be established first. In practice reliability is usually examined early in test development, because an unreliable test cannot be valid; reliability sets an upper limit on validity. Validation is an ongoing process of gathering evidence, not a single step in a fixed order. It tempts because validity sounds like the more important property, so candidates assume it must come first."}},{"q":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:","concept":"Randomisation is the strength of a randomised controlled trial (RCT): assigning participants to groups by chance balances both known and unknown confounders, so a later difference in outcome can be attributed to the treatment. Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis protects that balance. Every participant is analysed in the group to which they were randomised, whether or not they took the treatment, adhered to it, dropped out, or crossed over to the other arm; the rule is 'once randomised, always analysed'. Why? People who stop treatment are not a random subset: they may be sicker, have more side effects, or be improving anyway. Removing them breaks randomisation and usually exaggerates the benefit. ITT therefore gives a conservative estimate of effectiveness, the effect of offering a treatment in real practice, and it is the primary analysis expected for superiority trials under the CONSORT reporting guidelines, first issued in 1996. Its cost is that missing outcome data must be handled, for example by multiple imputation. The contrasting approach is per-protocol analysis, which includes only participants who completed treatment as planned; it estimates efficacy under ideal conditions but is vulnerable to attrition bias. An as-treated analysis groups people by the treatment they actually received, regardless of randomisation. A modified ITT excludes some randomised people, for example those who never received a single dose. In non-inferiority trials both ITT and per-protocol results are reported, because ITT can make two treatments look falsely similar. Option a is right because ITT analyses all randomised participants in their original groups regardless of adherence, drop-out or crossover.","traps":{"b":"Analysing only participants who completed the intended treatment is per-protocol (or completers) analysis. It answers 'does the treatment work when taken properly?', an efficacy question, but it loses the protection of randomisation, because completers often differ from drop-outs in prognosis and motivation. It usually inflates the treatment effect. It tempts because the phrase 'intended treatment' echoes 'intention to treat' while meaning the opposite.","c":"Analysing only responders, those who improved, is not a recognised trial analysis at all; it builds in selection bias and guarantees a favourable result by excluding everyone who did not improve. A legitimate responder analysis compares the proportion of responders between the randomised groups, which still counts everyone. It tempts because 'treating' and 'responding' sound connected, and responder rates are often reported in trials.","d":"An analysis that includes only the control group cannot estimate a treatment effect, because there is nothing to compare it with. The control arm (placebo, waiting list or usual care) exists only as a comparison for the intervention arm, and ITT keeps both arms intact. This is a filler option. It tempts only candidates who guess that 'intention' refers to people who were meant to receive no treatment."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference of 0.4 that ranges from −0.1 to 0.9 implies:","concept":"A confidence interval (CI) gives a range of plausible values for a population parameter, built from the sample estimate plus or minus a margin of error. The idea was formalised by Jerzy Neyman (1937). For a mean difference, the 95 percent CI is roughly the estimate plus or minus 1.96 standard errors. The correct reading is about the method: if the study were repeated many times, about 95 percent of intervals built this way would contain the true value. It is not strictly a 95 percent probability that this particular interval contains it. A CI links directly to hypothesis testing. For a difference between means, the null value is 0. If a 95 percent CI excludes 0, the result is significant at alpha 0.05 (two-tailed); if it includes 0, it is not. For ratios such as odds ratios and relative risks, the null value is 1. Here the estimate is 0.4 and the interval runs from minus 0.1 to 0.9. Because it includes 0, a zero difference remains plausible, and p is greater than 0.05. It does not prove there is no effect: the interval also includes 0.9, a sizeable effect, so the study is simply inconclusive, often because it was underpowered. Width reflects precision: larger samples and lower variability give narrower intervals. CIs are preferred to bare p-values because they show both the size and the uncertainty of an effect. Option d is right because the interval crosses 0, so the result is not statistically significant at alpha 0.05.","traps":{"a":"A highly significant result would need an interval well away from 0, for example 0.3 to 0.5, which would go with a very small p-value. This interval includes 0, so p is above 0.05 and the result is not significant at all. It tempts because the point estimate of 0.4 looks like a respectable effect, and candidates judge significance from the estimate instead of from the interval.","b":"A non-significant result does not show that there is no effect. The interval runs up to 0.9, so a moderate or even large true effect is still compatible with the data, as is a small negative one. The correct conclusion is that the evidence is inconclusive. It tempts because 'not significant' is so often misread as 'no difference', a classic error in reading research.","c":"A larger sample shrinks the standard error and so narrows the confidence interval. A wide interval that crosses 0 suggests the sample was too small, if anything, not too large. Very large samples cause a different problem: trivially small effects become statistically significant. It tempts because candidates know that sample size affects the interval but get the direction wrong."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric analogue of:","concept":"Parametric tests such as the t-test and ANOVA assume that the data come from populations with a particular distribution, usually normal, with roughly equal variances, and that the dependent variable is measured on an interval or ratio scale. When these assumptions fail, for example with ordinal ratings, small skewed samples or heavy outliers, researchers use non-parametric (distribution-free) tests, which usually work on ranks rather than raw scores. The Mann-Whitney U test was published by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney (1947), extending the rank-sum test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945), so it is also called the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. It compares two independent groups. All scores from both groups are pooled and ranked from lowest to highest; the ranks are summed for each group, and U counts how often a score in one group exceeds a score in the other. If the groups do not differ, their mean ranks should be similar. It is often described as comparing medians, which is accurate when the two distributions have similar shapes. Learn the pairings: independent-samples t-test with Mann-Whitney U; paired t-test with the Wilcoxon signed-rank test; one-way ANOVA with the Kruskal-Wallis H test of William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952); repeated-measures ANOVA with the Friedman test of Milton Friedman (1937); Pearson's r with the rho of Charles Spearman (1904) or Kendall's tau. Chi-square is itself non-parametric, used for frequencies. When assumptions do hold, non-parametric tests have slightly less power. Option d is right because the Mann-Whitney U test compares two independent groups, the job done by the independent-samples t-test.","traps":{"a":"The chi-square test of independence, from Karl Pearson (1900), tests whether two categorical variables are associated, using frequency counts in a contingency table. It is already a non-parametric test, so it is not the parametric partner of anything here. Mann-Whitney needs data that can at least be ranked. It tempts because chi-square is the best-known non-parametric test and appears in every list of them.","b":"The paired t-test compares two related measurements, such as the same people before and after therapy. Its non-parametric analogue is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (Frank Wilcoxon, 1945), which ranks the absolute differences within pairs. Mann-Whitney is for independent groups, not matched pairs. It tempts because Wilcoxon's name is attached both to the signed-rank test and to the rank-sum test, which is equivalent to Mann-Whitney.","c":"One-way ANOVA compares three or more independent groups, and its non-parametric analogue is the Kruskal-Wallis H test of William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952). Kruskal-Wallis is in fact the extension of Mann-Whitney to more than two groups, also based on pooled ranks. It tempts because the two tests are closely related, so candidates who sense the family resemblance pick the wrong member."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) was introduced by Andreas Laupacis, David Sackett and Robin Roberts (1988) to express trial results in clinically meaningful terms. It is the number of patients who must receive the treatment, instead of the control, for one additional patient to benefit (or to avoid one bad outcome) over the trial period. It is built from absolute risk. The control event rate (CER) is the proportion of bad outcomes in the control group; the experimental event rate (EER) is the proportion in the treated group. Absolute risk reduction (ARR) equals CER minus EER, and NNT equals 1 divided by ARR, rounded up to a whole patient. Worked example: if 40 percent of patients relapse on placebo and 20 percent on the drug, ARR is 0.20 and NNT is 1 divided by 0.20, which is 5. Treat five people and one extra stays well. A smaller NNT means a more effective treatment, and an NNT of 1 would mean every treated patient benefits. Relative risk reduction (RRR) equals ARR divided by CER, here 50 percent; relative measures stay the same whether a disease is common or rare, which can make small absolute benefits look impressive, so NNT is reported alongside them. The harmful counterpart is the Number Needed to Harm (NNH), 1 divided by the absolute risk increase for an adverse effect. Comparing NNT with NNH helps judge the balance of benefit and harm. Option a is right because NNT is calculated as the reciprocal of the absolute risk reduction.","traps":{"b":"Odds ratio minus 1 is not a recognised formula for NNT. An odds ratio is a relative measure; subtracting 1 gives only a rough sense of excess relative odds, something like a percentage increase, with no link to how many patients need treatment. NNT requires an absolute difference in risk between groups. It tempts because it looks like a quick calculation using a familiar statistic.","c":"Relative risk multiplied by the control event rate gives the experimental event rate, the risk in the treated group, since RR equals EER divided by CER. That is one step on the road but not the NNT. To get NNT you subtract EER from CER to find ARR, then take its reciprocal. It tempts because it uses the right ingredients in a plausible-looking formula.","d":"Number of patients enrolled divided by number of events is the reciprocal of an overall event rate, telling you how many patients there are per event in the whole sample. It mixes both groups together and ignores the comparison between treatment and control, which is the whole point of NNT. It tempts because it has the same 'one divided by a proportion' shape as the real formula."}},{"q":"The Bonferroni correction adjusts the alpha threshold when conducting multiple comparisons by:","concept":"When a researcher runs many significance tests, the chance of at least one false positive grows quickly. With alpha at 0.05 per test, the family-wise error rate (FWER), the probability of one or more Type I errors across the whole set, is 1 minus 0.95 to the power k for k independent tests: about 0.14 for three tests and about 0.40 for ten. This is the multiple comparisons problem, which arises with post-hoc comparisons, many outcome measures and subgroup analyses. The Bonferroni correction, based on the probability inequality of Carlo Emilio Bonferroni (1936) and popularised for multiple comparisons by Olive Jean Dunn (1961), keeps the FWER at or below alpha by testing each comparison at alpha divided by k. With five comparisons, each must reach p below 0.01 to count as significant at the 0.05 family level. Equivalently, each p-value can be multiplied by k and compared with 0.05. It is simple and works whether or not the tests are independent, but it is conservative: with many tests it lowers power and raises the risk of Type II errors. Less conservative alternatives include the step-down method of Sture Holm (1979) and the Sidak correction. A different philosophy is the false discovery rate procedure of Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg (1995), which controls the expected proportion of false positives among significant results. For pairwise comparisons after ANOVA, Tukey's HSD and Scheffe's test are dedicated options. Option a is right because Bonferroni divides alpha by the number of comparisons, a conservative control of the family-wise error rate.","traps":{"b":"Converting a p-value to a Z-score, or the reverse, is just a change of scale on the normal distribution, used when reporting results or combining them across studies. It does nothing to control error across multiple tests. Bonferroni changes the significance threshold, not the test statistic. It tempts because Z-scores and p-values are both part of significance testing, and the swap sounds technical.","c":"Ignoring the number of comparisons is the uncorrected approach that creates the problem in the first place: testing each of many comparisons at 0.05 inflates the family-wise error rate, so some 'significant' findings are likely to be chance. This practice, called fishing or p-hacking when done selectively, is exactly what Bonferroni guards against. It tempts only as the opposite of the right answer.","d":"Multiplying alpha by the number of comparisons would make the threshold more lenient, for example 0.05 times 5 equals 0.25, increasing false positives instead of controlling them. What Bonferroni does allow is multiplying each p-value by k and comparing it with the original alpha, which is equivalent to dividing alpha. It tempts because the word 'multiply' does appear in that adjusted p-value version."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","concept":"A clause is a group of words with a subject and a verb. An independent (main) clause can stand alone as a sentence: 'we decided to walk'. A dependent (subordinate) clause cannot, because it begins with a subordinating conjunction such as although, because, since, when, if, unless, while or after: 'although it was raining'. A sentence that joins one of each is a complex sentence, and the comma rule depends on the order. When the dependent clause comes first, as an introductory clause, put a comma after it, before the main clause begins: 'Although it was raining, we decided to walk.' The comma marks where the introduction ends. When the dependent clause comes second, usually no comma is needed: 'We decided to walk although it was raining.' Some writers add one before 'although', but the introductory comma is the rule examiners test. Three errors are common. First, putting a comma straight after the conjunction ('Although, it was raining'), which cuts the conjunction off from its own clause; 'however' takes a comma in that position, 'although' does not. Second, using a semicolon, which may only join two independent clauses ('It was raining; we walked anyway') or separate items in a complex list. Third, inserting a stray comma inside the main clause, which separates a verb from its object. Compare 'although' with 'despite', which takes a noun phrase ('despite the rain'), not a clause. Option d is right because it places a single comma after the introductory dependent clause and nowhere else.","traps":{"a":"'Although, it was raining we decided to walk' puts a comma straight after the subordinating conjunction, which wrongly separates 'although' from the clause it introduces, and it leaves out the comma needed after 'raining'. A comma after the first word works for conjunctive adverbs such as 'however' or 'nevertheless', not for 'although'. It tempts because many sentences do begin with a single word followed by a comma.","b":"'Although it was raining; we decided to walk' uses a semicolon after a dependent clause. A semicolon can only join two complete independent clauses, such as 'It was raining; we decided to walk', or separate items in a list that already contains commas. 'Although it was raining' cannot stand alone, so the semicolon is wrong. It tempts because the semicolon sits exactly where the comma should go.","c":"'Although it was raining we decided, to walk' misses the comma after the introductory clause and inserts one between the verb 'decided' and its object, the infinitive 'to walk'. A comma should not split a verb from its object or complement. It tempts candidates who know a comma belongs somewhere in the sentence but place it by the rhythm of speech rather than by grammar."}},{"q":"Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence: 'The data ___ inconclusive.'","concept":"Subject-verb agreement requires a plural verb with a plural subject and a singular verb with a singular subject. The difficulty with 'data' is that it is a Latin plural: the singular is 'datum', meaning 'a thing given', so traditional and formal usage says 'the data are', 'these data show', 'the data were collected'. English kept several Latin and Greek plurals that behave the same way: criterion and criteria, phenomenon and phenomena, medium and media, stimulus and stimuli, hypothesis and hypotheses, analysis and analyses, curriculum and curricula. Exams love 'the criteria is' and 'this phenomena', both wrong in formal English. In everyday and much technical writing, 'data' is now often treated as a mass noun, like 'information', taking a singular verb ('the data is'), and many modern dictionaries accept this. However, academic and scientific style, including the style manual of the American Psychological Association used in psychology, treats 'data' as plural. For an entrance exam set by psychology examiners, the formal plural is the expected answer. Check the other options with the same rule. 'Was', 'is' and 'has been' are all singular forms (compare 'were', 'are' and 'have been'), so they fail for the same reason. A useful test is to substitute an obviously plural noun: 'the results ___ inconclusive' needs 'are', and so does 'the data' in formal register. Tense is not the issue here; 'were' would also have been acceptable if it had been offered. Option a is right because 'data' is formally a plural noun and takes the plural verb 'are'.","traps":{"b":"'Was' is the singular past form of 'to be', so 'the data was inconclusive' treats 'data' as a singular mass noun. That is common in everyday speech and journalism, but formal academic style prefers the plural. Its plural counterpart 'were' would have been acceptable. It tempts because the singular use of 'data' is so widespread that it sounds natural to most readers.","c":"'Has been' is the singular present perfect; with a plural subject it would be 'have been' ('the data have been inconclusive'). So the option fails on number, not on tense. It tempts because the perfect tense sounds formal and careful, and candidates who focus on making the sentence sound academic overlook the agreement error built into 'has'.","d":"'Is' is the singular present form, and 'the data is inconclusive' reflects the informal treatment of 'data' as a mass noun, like 'information'. Many dictionaries now accept it, but formal and scientific style still requires 'the data are'. It tempts most strongly because it is the form most people actually say, and it matches the present tense the stem invites."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence that uses 'who' / 'whom' correctly.","concept":"'Who' and 'whom' are two case forms of the same pronoun. 'Who' is the subjective form, used when the pronoun does the action of a verb, like 'he', 'she' and 'they'. 'Whom' is the objective form, used when the pronoun receives the action or follows a preposition, like 'him', 'her' and 'them'. The reliable test is substitution: answer the question or rewrite the clause with he or him. If 'he' fits, use 'who'; if 'him' fits, use 'whom' (both end in m). The trap in this question is the inserted phrase 'shall I say'. In 'Who shall I say is calling?' the words 'shall I say' are a parenthetical insertion; remove them and the core is 'Who is calling?' The pronoun is the subject of 'is calling' (he is calling), so 'who' is correct, even though it sits next to 'I say'. The same trap appears with 'I think', 'do you believe' and 'they said': 'the man who I think stole the car' is correct because he stole the car. By contrast, 'Whom did you meet?' is correct because you met him, and 'To whom did you give the book?' is correct because 'whom' is the object of the preposition 'to'. In relaxed modern speech 'who' is widely used in object positions ('Who did you give it to?'), but formal grammar, which exams test, requires 'whom' there. 'Whom' can never be the subject of a verb such as 'is'. Option a is right because 'who' is the subject of 'is calling', with 'shall I say' merely inserted.","traps":{"b":"'Whom is at the door?' uses the objective form as the subject of the verb 'is'. Test it: the answer would be 'He is at the door', not 'Him is', so the subject form is required: 'Who is at the door?' It tempts because some candidates think 'whom' simply sounds more formal or correct and use it everywhere, a mistake called hypercorrection.","c":"'Who did you give the book to?' is common and acceptable in conversation, but in formal grammar the pronoun is the object of the preposition 'to' (you gave the book to him), so it should be 'whom': 'Whom did you give the book to?' or 'To whom did you give the book?' It tempts because it sounds completely natural and is what most people actually say.","d":"'Whom shall I say is calling?' is a classic hypercorrection. The writer sees 'I say' and assumes the pronoun is its object, but 'shall I say' is only an inserted phrase. Removing it leaves 'Whom is calling?', which is clearly wrong because the pronoun is the subject of 'is calling' (he is calling). It tempts because it looks polite and formal, exactly the register where people expect 'whom'."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"Conditional sentences have two parts: the if-clause, which states the condition, and the main clause, which states the result. Grammar books describe four main types, each with a fixed tense pattern. The zero conditional states general truths: if plus present simple, present simple ('If you heat ice, it melts'). The first conditional describes real, likely future events: if plus present simple, will plus base verb ('If it rains, I will stay home'). The second conditional describes unreal or unlikely present or future situations: if plus past simple, would plus base verb ('If I knew her number, I would call her'); formal English uses 'were' for all persons ('If I were you'). The third conditional describes unreal past situations, things that did not happen, and their imagined results: if plus past perfect, would have plus past participle ('If I had known, I would have come'). The meaning is that I did not know, so I did not come. Mixed conditionals combine a past condition with a present result ('If I had studied medicine, I would be a doctor now') or the reverse. The commonest error, tested here, is putting 'would' or 'would have' inside the if-clause: 'If I would have known' is frequent in casual speech, but standard grammar keeps 'would' in the result clause only. A formal inverted version drops 'if' and reads 'Had I known, I would have come', with the same meaning. Option d is right because it follows the third conditional pattern exactly: if plus past perfect, then would have plus past participle.","traps":{"a":"'If I would have known, I would have come' puts 'would have' in both clauses. Standard grammar keeps 'would' out of the if-clause; the condition takes the past perfect 'had known'. This form is common in casual speech, especially in American English, which is why it sounds acceptable to many ears. It tempts because the result clause is correct, and candidates check only the second half.","b":"'If I would know, I came' places 'would' in the if-clause and then uses a simple past 'came' in the result clause, which fits no conditional type. A correct second conditional would be 'If I knew, I would come', and a third conditional 'If I had known, I would have come'. It tempts only as a jumble of conditional-looking words; it fails in both clauses.","c":"'If I knew, I have come' starts like a second conditional (if plus past simple) but then switches to the present perfect 'have come' instead of 'would come'. The two clauses point to different times and moods, so the sentence is incoherent. The correct second conditional is 'If I knew, I would come'. It tempts because the if-clause on its own is correctly formed."}},{"q":"Select the sentence with a misplaced modifier.","concept":"A modifier is a word, phrase or clause that describes another part of the sentence. A modifier should sit next to the word it describes. A misplaced modifier is in the wrong position, so it seems to describe the wrong word: 'She served sandwiches to the children on paper plates' suggests the children are on the plates; better is 'She served the children sandwiches on paper plates'. Single words such as 'only', 'almost' and 'just' are often misplaced: 'I only ate two' and 'I ate only two' differ in emphasis. A dangling modifier is a special case where the word being described is missing altogether. Introductory participial phrases are the usual culprit. An opening phrase such as 'Running quickly' is understood to describe the subject that comes right after the comma. In 'Running quickly, the marathon finish line came into view', that subject is 'the marathon finish line', which cannot run; the runner is never mentioned. Exams often use 'misplaced modifier' as an umbrella term, as this question does. There are two fixes: make the doer the subject of the main clause ('Running quickly, she saw the finish line come into view'), or turn the phrase into a full clause ('As she ran quickly, the finish line came into view'). To check, ask who or what is doing the action in the opening phrase, then see whether that word follows the comma. Option b is right because 'Running quickly' has no logical subject and seems to describe the finish line.","traps":{"a":"'He drove to the office in the morning' has two adverbial phrases, 'to the office' (where) and 'in the morning' (when), both clearly attached to the verb 'drove'. Nothing is left dangling and there is no absurd reading. It tempts because a sentence ending in a time phrase can look like a modifier puzzle, but placing time phrases at the end is normal English.","c":"'The teacher gave the test on Friday' attaches 'on Friday' to the action of giving, which is the natural reading. In theory it could be read as 'the test on Friday', but either reading makes sense and neither is absurd, so it is not treated as a misplaced modifier. It tempts candidates who look for any phrase that could attach to two different words.","d":"'She ate the soup that was hot' places the relative clause 'that was hot' directly after 'soup', the noun it describes, so the modifier is correctly placed. The sentence is wordy, since 'She ate the hot soup' is neater, but wordiness is a style issue, not a modifier error. It tempts because it sounds slightly awkward, and awkward sentences look like the faulty one."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to UBIQUITOUS.","concept":"'Ubiquitous' is an adjective meaning present, appearing or found everywhere; it describes something so common that it seems to be all around you. It comes from the Latin 'ubique', meaning 'everywhere', built on 'ubi', meaning 'where'. Typical uses are 'Smartphones have become ubiquitous' and 'the ubiquitous chai stall on every street corner'. The noun is 'ubiquity' and the adverb 'ubiquitously'. Close synonyms are omnipresent, pervasive, universal, prevalent, widespread and all-pervading. 'Omnipresent' is the nearest, though it is also used in a religious sense for a god who is present everywhere. Antonyms are rare, scarce, uncommon, sparse and limited. Vocabulary questions of this type test two skills: knowing the core meaning, and eliminating options that share only a surface feature with it. The core meaning of ubiquitous is about extent in space (everywhere), not about time (persisting), age (old or new), or manner (quiet or loud). Watch for sound-alike traps that examiners use in variants: 'iniquitous' means grossly unfair or wicked, 'iniquity' is wickedness, and 'equitable' means fair. A strategy for unfamiliar words is to look for roots that signal extent: 'ubi' (where), 'omni' (all), 'pan' (all), 'uni' (one). Also check the direction of meaning, because synonym questions often hide the antonym among the options; if the question asked for the opposite of ubiquitous, the answer would be 'rare'. Option c is right because 'universal / present everywhere' captures the meaning of ubiquitous, found everywhere.","traps":{"a":"'Quietly persistent' describes something that continues steadily over time without drawing attention, close to 'unobtrusive' or 'tenacious'. Ubiquitous is about how widely something is spread in space, not how long it lasts or how loudly it acts. It tempts because ubiquitous things, such as background music or advertising, often feel quietly constant, so the two ideas overlap in everyday experience.","b":"'Outdated' means old-fashioned or obsolete, with synonyms such as archaic, antiquated and passe. It concerns age and fashion, not extent. A thing can be ubiquitous and modern (smartphones) or ubiquitous and old (bricks). It tempts mainly candidates who do not know the word and guess from its unusual Latin look that it must describe something ancient or out of use.","d":"'Rare' means seldom found or occurring, and it is the direct antonym of ubiquitous, not a synonym. Exams place antonyms among synonym options deliberately, because a candidate who half-knows the word senses that 'rare' is related in meaning but forgets the direction. If the question had asked for the opposite of ubiquitous, 'rare' would have been the answer."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence in passive voice.","concept":"Voice tells you whether the subject of a sentence does the action or receives it. In the active voice the subject is the doer (agent): 'The committee approved the proposal.' In the passive voice the subject is the receiver, and the doer either appears in a 'by' phrase or is dropped: 'The proposal was approved by the committee' or simply 'The proposal was approved.' The passive is formed with a form of the verb 'be' plus the past participle of the main verb, and only transitive verbs (verbs that take an object) can be made passive. To convert active to passive, the object becomes the subject, 'be' takes the tense of the original verb, and the old subject moves into the 'by' phrase. The tense is carried by 'be': 'is approved' (present), 'was approved' (past), 'will be approved' (future), 'has been approved' (present perfect), 'is being approved' (present continuous). Do not confuse tense or sentence type with voice. Questions ('Did the committee approve it?') and future statements ('The committee will approve it') are still active if the committee is the doer. The passive is used when the doer is unknown, unimportant or obvious, or when the writer wants to focus on the receiver, which is why research reports often use it ('Participants were randomly assigned'). Option d is right because 'the proposal' receives the action, the verb is 'was approved' (be plus past participle), and the agent appears in a 'by' phrase.","traps":{"a":"'The committee will approve the proposal' is in the active voice, future tense. The subject 'the committee' performs the action and 'the proposal' is the object. Its passive form would be 'The proposal will be approved by the committee'. It tempts because the auxiliary 'will' adds a helping verb, and candidates sometimes treat any multi-word verb phrase as passive.","b":"'Did the committee approve the proposal?' is an active interrogative in the simple past. The helping verb 'did' forms the question, but the committee is still the doer. The passive question would be 'Was the proposal approved by the committee?' It tempts because questions rearrange word order, and candidates confuse the inversion of subject and auxiliary with a change of voice.","c":"'The committee approved the proposal' is the plain active sentence in the simple past: subject (doer), verb, object (receiver). It is the active version of the correct answer, with the same meaning. It tempts because it contains the same key words in a different arrangement and uses the past tense, so candidates who look only for 'approved' can pick it by mistake."}},{"q":"Which option corrects the run-on sentence: 'She studied hard she passed the exam.'?","concept":"An independent clause has a subject and a verb and can stand alone: 'She studied hard' and 'she passed the exam' are both independent. When two independent clauses are joined incorrectly, the result is a run-on sentence. There are two kinds. A fused sentence joins them with no punctuation at all, as in the stem. A comma splice joins them with only a comma: 'She studied hard, she passed the exam.' There are four standard corrections. First, use a full stop: 'She studied hard. She passed the exam.' Second, use a semicolon: 'She studied hard; she passed the exam.' Third, use a semicolon with a conjunctive adverb such as however, therefore, consequently, moreover or thus, followed by a comma: 'She studied hard; consequently, she passed the exam.' Fourth, use a comma plus a coordinating conjunction, remembered as FANBOYS (for, and, nor, but, or, yet, so): 'She studied hard, so she passed the exam.' Or subordinate one clause: 'Because she studied hard, she passed the exam.' The key distinction examiners test is between coordinating conjunctions, which can follow a comma, and conjunctive adverbs, which cannot; 'She studied hard, consequently she passed' is still a comma splice. Some style guides allow the comma before a coordinating conjunction to be dropped when both clauses are very short, so the comma-less 'so' version is borderline rather than clearly wrong, but it is not the model correction. Option c is right because a semicolon plus 'consequently' and a comma is a fully correct way to join the two clauses.","traps":{"a":"'She studied hard so she passed the exam' uses the coordinating conjunction 'so', which is a valid repair strategy, but standard punctuation puts a comma before it when it joins two independent clauses: 'She studied hard, so she passed the exam.' Some style guides excuse the missing comma in short sentences, which makes this the strongest distractor, but it is not the fully correct form that the key requires.","b":"'She studied, hard she passed the exam' moves the problem rather than fixing it. The comma now splits the verb 'studied' from its adverb 'hard', and 'hard she passed the exam' still runs on with no proper boundary between clauses. The two independent clauses remain fused. It tempts candidates who see that a comma is needed somewhere but place it by sound rather than by clause structure.","d":"'She studied hard, she passed the exam' joins two independent clauses with only a comma, which is a comma splice, itself a type of run-on sentence. A comma is too weak to separate complete sentences on its own; it needs a coordinating conjunction after it, or should be replaced by a semicolon or full stop. It tempts because it looks like a minimal, natural fix."}},{"q":"Read the brief passage and answer:\n'Despite mounting evidence of climate disruption, public policy responses have been hampered by short electoral cycles and powerful incumbent interests, leaving long-horizon problems chronically under-addressed.'\nThe author's main point is best described as:","concept":"Main idea questions ask for the central claim the author makes, not a detail, an outside fact or an opinion you hold. A reliable method has three steps. First, find the grammatical core of the sentence. Here it is 'public policy responses have been hampered by short electoral cycles and powerful incumbent interests'. Second, read the framing words. 'Despite mounting evidence of climate disruption' is a concessive phrase: it grants that the problem is real and well evidenced, which sets up a contrast with the weak response. Third, read the consequence. 'Leaving long-horizon problems chronically under-addressed' tells you the result, and it widens the claim from climate change to long-term problems in general. Put together, the author argues that two causes, short political time horizons (governments think only as far as the next election) and entrenched interests (established industries and groups with power to resist change), prevent adequate action on long-term problems, with climate change as the example. The right answer should cover the whole claim, stay within the text, and match its tone. Wrong options in such questions fall into predictable types: contradiction (saying the opposite of the text), outside information (a claim the text never makes, even if true), extreme or prescriptive leaps (turning a diagnosis into a radical remedy), and too narrow (a single detail). In this passage the author diagnoses a problem but recommends nothing. Option a is right because it restates the passage's claim that short political horizons and entrenched interests block an adequate response to long-term problems like climate change.","traps":{"b":"'Climate change is not happening' contradicts the passage, which opens by referring to 'mounting evidence of climate disruption' and treats the problem as real. A contradiction option is the easiest type to eliminate, because the text explicitly asserts the opposite. It tempts only careless readers who notice the word 'despite' and wrongly assume the author is doubting the evidence rather than conceding it.","c":"'Renewable energy is more profitable than fossil fuels' brings in outside information. The passage says nothing about renewable energy, profits or comparisons between energy sources; it is about political obstacles to policy. Even if the claim were true in the real world, it cannot be the author's main point. It tempts candidates who answer from general knowledge about climate debates instead of from the text.","d":"'Elections should be abolished to address climate change' is an extreme, prescriptive leap. The author identifies short electoral cycles as one cause of the problem but proposes no solution, and certainly not ending democracy. Turning a diagnosis into a radical remedy is a classic distractor pattern. It tempts because it reuses the passage's own idea about elections, so it feels connected to the text."}},{"q":"Choose the correct option to complete: 'Neither the manager nor the employees ___ available for comment.'","concept":"Subject-verb agreement normally depends on whether the subject is singular or plural, and compound subjects need special rules. Subjects joined by 'and' are usually plural: 'The manager and the employees were available.' Subjects joined by 'or' or by the correlative pairs 'either ... or', 'neither ... nor' and 'not only ... but also' follow the rule of proximity: the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it. In this sentence the nearer subject is 'the employees', which is plural, so the verb must be plural: 'Neither the manager nor the employees were available for comment.' Reverse the order and the verb changes: 'Neither the employees nor the manager was available.' Style guides advise putting the plural subject second, as the stem does, because a singular verb right after a plural noun sounds awkward even when correct. The rule also covers person: 'Either you or I am going' (the verb agrees with 'I'). Do not confuse these with other tricky subjects. 'Neither' or 'either' used alone takes a singular verb: 'Neither of the employees was available.' 'Each' and 'every' are singular. Phrases such as 'as well as', 'along with' and 'together with' do not change the number of the main subject: 'The manager, along with the employees, was available.' Tense is not the issue in this question; the deciding factor is number. Option a is right because the verb agrees with the nearer plural subject 'employees', so the plural 'were' is required.","traps":{"b":"'Has been' is singular. It would fit if the nearer subject were singular, as in 'Neither the employees nor the manager has been available'. Here the noun closest to the verb is the plural 'employees', so the plural 'have been' would be needed instead. It tempts because candidates fix on the first subject, 'the manager', and forget the proximity rule.","c":"'Was' is the singular past form. It would be correct only if the order were reversed, with the singular noun nearer the verb: 'Neither the employees nor the manager was available.' It tempts strongly because 'neither' used alone takes a singular verb ('Neither of them was available'), and many candidates wrongly carry that rule over to 'neither ... nor'.","d":"'Is' is singular and present tense. It fails the proximity rule, since the nearer subject 'employees' is plural; the plural present form would be 'are'. It tempts candidates who treat 'neither' as always singular, and who also find the present tense natural for a news-style sentence about whether someone is available to comment."}}],"diagnostic-1":[{"q":"A researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true. This is:","concept":"In null hypothesis significance testing there are two possible mistakes. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: you reject the null hypothesis when it is actually true, concluding an effect exists when it does not. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: you retain a false null. The probability of a Type I error is set by the significance level alpha (commonly 0.05). Rejecting a true null is by definition a Type I error.","traps":{"b":"A Type II (beta) error is the opposite mistake, failing to reject a null that is actually false, not rejecting a true one.","c":"Low statistical power is (1 minus beta) being small, which raises the risk of Type II errors, not the act of rejecting a true null.","d":"A confounding error refers to a third variable distorting the observed relationship, a design/validity issue, not the decision error described here."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups?","concept":"When comparing means across three or more independent groups, the correct test is a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA), which uses the F-ratio (between-group variance over within-group variance). Running multiple t-tests instead inflates the family-wise Type I error rate, which is exactly why ANOVA exists. A single t-test can only compare two group means.","traps":{"a":"An independent samples t-test compares the means of only two independent groups, not three or more.","c":"Pearson correlation measures the linear association between two continuous variables, it does not compare group means.","d":"A chi-square test analyses associations between categorical (frequency) variables, not differences between group means."}},{"q":"The non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test is the:","concept":"Non-parametric tests are used when data violate the assumptions of parametric tests (e.g., non-normal, ordinal). The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test: it compares two independent groups by ranking all scores together. Its paired counterpart is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired (related) t-test, not the independent-samples t-test.","b":"The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA (three or more independent groups).","d":"The Friedman test is the non-parametric equivalent of repeated-measures ANOVA (three or more related conditions)."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha, developed by Lee Cronbach, is an index of internal consistency reliability: it estimates how strongly the items of a scale intercorrelate, that is, whether they measure the same underlying construct. Values range from 0 to 1, with roughly 0.70 and above considered acceptable. It is a reliability index, not a validity index.","traps":{"a":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts or agrees with an external criterion, a form of validity, not internal consistency.","b":"Test-retest reliability assesses stability of scores over time using two administrations, whereas alpha is computed from a single administration.","c":"Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between different raters/observers, not the internal coherence of items."}},{"q":"A correlation coefficient of −0.85 indicates:","concept":"A correlation coefficient conveys two things: its sign shows direction and its absolute value (closeness to 1) shows strength. A value of minus 0.85 has a negative sign (as one variable rises, the other falls) and a large magnitude, so it indicates a strong negative relationship. Strength is unrelated to the sign.","traps":{"b":"A weak negative relationship would be a small magnitude such as minus 0.15 or minus 0.20, not minus 0.85.","c":"No relationship corresponds to a coefficient near 0, whereas minus 0.85 is far from zero.","d":"A strong positive relationship would require a positive sign (e.g., plus 0.85); the minus sign here makes it negative."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROBABILITY sampling method?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling means every element has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which supports generalisation and estimation of sampling error. Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, so it is a probability method. Quota, purposive, and snowball are non-probability methods that rely on the researcher's judgement or convenience.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category quotas using non-random selection, making it a non-probability method.","c":"Purposive (judgemental) sampling deliberately selects cases the researcher deems informative, so selection probabilities are unknown.","d":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing ones, a non-probability chain-referral technique."}},{"q":"For a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 20, the degrees of freedom are:","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values free to vary when estimating a parameter. For a one-sample t-test, one parameter (the mean) is estimated, so df equals n minus 1. With n equal to 20, df equals 19.","traps":{"a":"20 is the sample size n itself, not df; df subtracts one for the estimated mean.","b":"18 would be n minus 2, the df formula for an independent two-sample situation per adjustment, not a one-sample t-test.","d":"21 is n plus 1, which reverses the correct subtraction."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROJECTIVE test of personality?","concept":"Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli and assume the person 'projects' unconscious needs and conflicts onto them. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, asks the person to tell stories about ambiguous picture cards, so it is projective. Objective inventories instead use structured, self-report items with fixed response formats.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is Raymond Cattell's objective self-report personality inventory measuring 16 traits, not a projective test.","b":"The MMPI-2 is an objective true/false self-report inventory of psychopathology, not projective.","c":"The NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae) is an objective self-report questionnaire measuring the Big Five traits, not projective."}},{"q":"A T-score has a mean and standard deviation of:","concept":"Standardised scores rescale raw scores to a fixed mean and standard deviation. A T-score has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, so a T-score of 60 lies one SD above the mean. This distinguishes it from z-scores and deviation IQ scores.","traps":{"b":"Mean 100, SD 15 describes the deviation IQ metric (e.g., Wechsler scales), not a T-score.","c":"Mean 0, SD 1 describes the z-score (standard score) distribution, not a T-score.","d":"Mean 500, SD 100 describes large-scale test scaling such as the SAT/GRE metric, not a T-score."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 contains how many clinical scales?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2) contains 10 clinical scales (e.g., Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia), alongside a set of validity scales (such as L, F, and K) that detect response distortion. The number of clinical scales is 10.","traps":{"a":"4 does not match; the validity indicators are several, and the core clinical scales number 10.","c":"16 confuses the MMPI-2 with Cattell's 16PF, which measures 16 personality factors.","d":"13 is not the count; the standard MMPI-2 clinical scale set is 10."}},{"q":"Sten scores (range 1–10) are most associated with which test?","concept":"A sten ('standard ten') score divides the distribution into ten units, ranging from 1 to 10 with a mean of 5.5 and a standard deviation of 2. This scoring metric is the signature of Raymond Cattell's 16PF personality questionnaire.","traps":{"a":"Wechsler scales report deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15) and scaled subtest scores (mean 10), not stens.","b":"The Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) uses a deviation IQ metric, not the 1 to 10 sten scale.","d":"The Rorschach is a projective inkblot test scored by systems such as Exner's, not by stens."}},{"q":"Administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores assesses:","concept":"Reliability concerns consistency of measurement. Test-retest reliability specifically assesses the temporal stability of scores: the same test is given to the same people on two occasions and the two sets of scores are correlated. A high correlation indicates scores are stable over time.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency (e.g., Cronbach's alpha) is computed from a single administration by examining item intercorrelations, not two occasions.","b":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended theoretical construct, a validity question, not consistency over time.","c":"Content validity concerns whether items adequately sample the domain, judged by expert review, not by re-testing."}},{"q":"Loosening of associations and 'knight's move' thinking are characteristic of:","concept":"Formal thought disorder is a disturbance in the form or flow of thought inferred from disorganised speech. Loosening of associations (ideas slipping onto unrelated tracks) and 'knight's move' thinking (illogical jumps between topics) are classic signs, seen most characteristically in schizophrenia. They reflect disorganised thinking rather than mood or anxiety pathology.","traps":{"b":"Major depressive disorder is a mood disorder marked by low mood and anhedonia, not by loosening of associations.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder features intrusive obsessions and compulsions with generally intact thought form.","d":"Specific phobia involves marked fear of a specific object or situation, with no formal thought disorder."}},{"q":"Compared with DSM-IV-TR, the DSM-5 (2013):","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5, 2013) abolished the multiaxial system that DSM-IV-TR used. Former Axes I, II, and III (clinical disorders, personality disorders/intellectual disability, and medical conditions) were merged into a single list of diagnoses, Axis IV became separate notation for psychosocial and contextual factors, and Axis V (the Global Assessment of Functioning) was dropped.","traps":{"a":"DSM-5 did not add a sixth axis; it removed the axial system entirely.","c":"Retaining the five-axis system describes DSM-IV/DSM-IV-TR, not DSM-5.","d":"DSM-5 kept its own diagnostic criteria and did not replace diagnoses with ICD codes only, though it lists corresponding ICD codes."}},{"q":"Anhedonia refers to:","concept":"Anhedonia is the markedly diminished ability to experience interest or pleasure in activities that were previously enjoyable. It is one of the two cardinal (core) symptoms of a major depressive episode, alongside depressed mood, in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual.","traps":{"a":"Reduced need for sleep is a feature of a manic episode, not the definition of anhedonia.","b":"Excessive fear of social situations describes social anxiety disorder, not anhedonia.","d":"Loss of contact with reality describes psychosis, a different construct from the loss of pleasure."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia were described by:","concept":"The First-Rank Symptoms of schizophrenia (e.g., audible thoughts, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, delusional perception, passivity phenomena) were described by Kurt Schneider in 1959 as symptoms with strong (though not pathognomonic) diagnostic weight. Other theorists shaped the concept differently: Kraepelin and Bleuler defined the disorder, and Jaspers advanced its phenomenology.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin coined 'dementia praecox' and its subtypes, not the First-Rank Symptoms.","b":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' and described the four A's (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism), not first-rank symptoms.","c":"Karl Jaspers pioneered descriptive psychopathology and phenomenology but did not formulate the first-rank symptoms."}},{"q":"The minimum duration of symptoms required to diagnose schizophrenia under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Under the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), a schizophrenia diagnosis requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months in total (which may include prodromal or residual periods), of which at least 1 month must contain active-phase symptoms. This contrasts with ICD, which requires about 1 month.","traps":{"b":"1 month is the active-phase requirement within DSM-5 and the ICD threshold, not the total duration DSM-5 demands.","c":"1 week is too short and corresponds to no standard schizophrenia criterion (it is closer to a brief psychotic episode boundary).","d":"2 years is not required; it overstates the duration criterion for schizophrenia."}},{"q":"Which cluster of DSM-5 personality disorders is described as 'dramatic, emotional, erratic'?","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual groups personality disorders into three clusters. Cluster B, described as dramatic, emotional, and erratic, comprises Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, and Narcissistic personality disorders. (Cluster A is odd/eccentric; Cluster C is anxious/fearful.)","traps":{"a":"Cluster A is the 'odd or eccentric' group (Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal), not dramatic/emotional.","c":"Cluster C is the 'anxious or fearful' group (Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-Compulsive personality disorders).","d":"There is no Cluster D in the DSM classification; only clusters A, B, and C exist."}},{"q":"Unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence are the core conditions described by:","concept":"Carl Rogers, founder of Person-Centred (client-centred) therapy, specified three core conditions he called necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change: unconditional positive regard, empathic understanding, and congruence (genuineness). These describe the therapist's way of being with the client rather than specific techniques.","traps":{"a":"Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy, focused on identifying and testing automatic thoughts and schemas, not the Rogerian core conditions.","b":"Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy centred on disputing irrational beliefs, not unconditional positive regard.","d":"Fritz Perls developed Gestalt therapy, known for techniques like the empty chair, not the three core conditions."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitization, based on reciprocal inhibition, was developed by:","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe. It rests on reciprocal inhibition, the principle that a response incompatible with anxiety (typically deep muscle relaxation) can inhibit anxiety. The client is gradually exposed to a hierarchy of feared stimuli while relaxed, so the feared cues lose their power.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning (reinforcement and punishment), not systematic desensitization.","b":"John Watson pioneered behaviourism and the 'Little Albert' fear-conditioning study but did not create systematic desensitization.","c":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, which underlies the phenomenon, but the therapy itself was Wolpe's."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:","concept":"The 'empty chair' (and 'two-chair') technique is a hallmark of Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self seated in an empty chair, promoting present-moment awareness and integration of conflicting feelings.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) uses free association, dream analysis, and transference interpretation, not the empty-chair technique.","c":"Cognitive therapy focuses on identifying and restructuring maladaptive thoughts, not empty-chair dialogue.","d":"Behaviour therapy uses techniques like exposure and reinforcement, not the Gestalt empty chair."}},{"q":"In Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model stands for:","concept":"In Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the ABC model holds that emotional consequences arise not directly from events but from beliefs about them. A is the Activating event, B is the Belief (especially irrational beliefs), and C is the Consequence (emotional or behavioural). Ellis later added D (disputing the belief) and E (the new effect).","traps":{"a":"Behaviour is not what B stands for; the model targets beliefs as the mediator between event and consequence.","c":"Baseline is a measurement concept from behavioural assessment, not a component of the ABC model.","d":"Bias is a general cognitive term, not the specific 'B' (Belief) element in Ellis's model."}},{"q":"Transference and free association are central techniques of:","concept":"Free association (saying whatever comes to mind), dream analysis, transference (redirecting feelings from past figures onto the therapist), and interpretation are the central techniques of psychoanalytic therapy founded by Sigmund Freud. They aim to make unconscious conflicts conscious.","traps":{"a":"Behaviour therapy uses conditioning-based methods such as exposure and reinforcement, not free association or transference.","b":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on the therapist's core conditions (empathy, congruence, unconditional positive regard), not transference work.","d":"Reality therapy, developed by William Glasser, focuses on present choices and responsibility, not free association."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat borderline personality disorder and chronic suicidal/self-harming behaviour. It blends cognitive-behavioural change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness, organised around modules of mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotics and psychosocial rehabilitation, not DBT as its primary indication.","b":"Autism spectrum disorder is addressed with behavioural and developmental interventions, not DBT.","c":"Specific phobias are treated with exposure-based behaviour therapy, not DBT."}},{"q":"Object permanence, according to Piaget, first develops during the:","concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen. According to Jean Piaget, it first develops during the sensorimotor stage (roughly birth to 2 years), as infants gradually learn to represent absent objects, shown by searching for a hidden toy.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism, but object permanence is already established before it.","c":"The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings conservation and logical operations on concrete material, well after object permanence.","d":"The formal operational stage (about 11 years onward) introduces abstract and hypothetical reasoning, long after object permanence emerges."}},{"q":"In Erikson's theory, the psychosocial crisis of adolescence is:","concept":"Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages, each defined by a central crisis. Adolescence corresponds to the fifth stage, Identity versus Role Confusion, in which the person works to form a coherent sense of self and future direction; failure leaves confusion about one's roles and values.","traps":{"a":"Trust versus mistrust is Erikson's first stage, occurring in infancy, not adolescence.","c":"Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, spanning the school-age years before adolescence.","d":"Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage, the crisis of young adulthood, not adolescence."}},{"q":"The 'Strange Situation' procedure for assessing attachment was devised by:","concept":"The 'Strange Situation' is a structured laboratory procedure devised by Mary Ainsworth to assess infant attachment through a series of separations and reunions with the caregiver. It classified infants into secure, anxious-avoidant, and anxious-resistant (ambivalent) patterns; a disorganised category was added later by Mary Main.","traps":{"a":"John Bowlby founded attachment theory and its ethological framework but did not design the Strange Situation procedure.","b":"Harry Harlow conducted the rhesus-monkey surrogate-mother studies on contact comfort, not the Strange Situation.","d":"Konrad Lorenz studied imprinting in geese, an ethological phenomenon distinct from the Strange Situation."}},{"q":"The 'Zone of Proximal Development' is a concept proposed by:","concept":"The Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) was proposed by Lev Vygotsky. It is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other. Support tailored to this zone, later termed 'scaffolding', is gradually withdrawn as competence grows.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget emphasised individual, stage-based cognitive construction, not the socially mediated ZPD.","b":"Lawrence Kohlberg developed a theory of moral development stages, unrelated to the ZPD.","c":"Jerome Bruner popularised the term 'scaffolding' and modes of representation but did not originate the ZPD concept."}},{"q":"The tendency for individuals to be less likely to help when others are present is the:","concept":"The bystander effect is the finding that people are less likely to help a victim when other people are present, largely because responsibility is diffused across the group and each person assumes someone else will act. It was studied experimentally by John Darley and Bibb Latané following the Kitty Genovese case.","traps":{"b":"Social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898) is the improvement of performance on simple tasks in the presence of others, not reduced helping.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is a bias where one positive trait colours overall impressions of a person, unrelated to helping behaviour.","d":"The just-world phenomenon (Melvin Lerner) is the belief that people get what they deserve, which can lead to victim-blaming, not diffusion of responsibility."}},{"q":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by:","concept":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by Leon Festinger in 1957. It holds that holding two inconsistent cognitions (beliefs, attitudes, or a belief and a behaviour) creates uncomfortable tension, which motivates the person to reduce it by changing an attitude, adding consonant beliefs, or minimising the inconsistency.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority experiments, not cognitive dissonance.","c":"Solomon Asch is known for the conformity line-judgement experiments, not dissonance theory.","d":"Philip Zimbardo conducted the Stanford Prison Experiment on situational roles, not cognitive dissonance."}},{"q":"Overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational causes of others' behaviour is the:","concept":"The fundamental attribution error, described by Lee Ross (1977), is the tendency to explain other people's behaviour by their internal traits or personality (dispositional causes) while discounting the power of the situation they are in. If someone cuts you off in traffic, you decide they are rude rather than considering they might be rushing to a hospital. It reflects a systematic bias in how observers attribute the causes of others' actions.","traps":{"a":"Self-serving bias is taking personal credit for successes but blaming external factors for failures, which protects one's own self-esteem rather than judging others' behaviour.","b":"Actor-observer bias (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) is the broader pattern that we attribute our OWN behaviour to the situation but others' behaviour to their disposition, whereas this question is specifically about the disposition-over-situation error for others.","d":"Confirmation bias (Peter Wason, 1960) is seeking or interpreting information so it confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, a reasoning bias unrelated to attributing causes of behaviour."}},{"q":"The classic line-judgment experiments demonstrating conformity to group pressure were conducted by:","concept":"Solomon Asch's line-judgment experiments (1951) had participants match the length of a line to comparison lines while surrounded by confederates who deliberately gave the same wrong answer. About one third of responses conformed to the incorrect majority, demonstrating normative social influence, the pressure to go along with a group to fit in even when one privately knows it is wrong.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority studies (shock experiments), which measured compliance with an authority figure, not conformity to peers.","b":"Philip Zimbardo ran the Stanford Prison Experiment on how situational roles shape behaviour, not perceptual conformity.","c":"Muzafer Sherif used the autokinetic effect to study the formation of group norms in an ambiguous task and the Robbers Cave study of intergroup conflict, not the unambiguous line-judgment conformity paradigm."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease is primarily associated with a deficiency of which neurotransmitter?","concept":"Parkinson's disease results primarily from the degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which projects along the nigrostriatal pathway to the basal ganglia. Loss of this dopamine disrupts the control of movement, producing the classic triad of resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia. This is why treatment relies on the dopamine precursor levodopa.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin deficiency is more associated with depression and anxiety disorders, not the motor degeneration of Parkinson's disease.","c":"Acetylcholine loss is centrally implicated in Alzheimer's disease; in Parkinson's the relative excess of acetylcholine activity is a downstream consequence, not the primary deficiency.","d":"GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is linked to anxiety and seizure disorders, not the dopaminergic loss defining Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The 'rest and digest' functions of the body are governed by the:","concept":"The parasympathetic nervous system is the branch of the autonomic nervous system responsible for 'rest and digest' functions: it slows the heart rate, stimulates digestion, and conserves the body's energy when it is at ease. It works in opposition to the sympathetic system, which mobilizes the body for 'fight or flight' (Walter Cannon). Both are subdivisions of the autonomic nervous system.","traps":{"a":"The sympathetic nervous system does the opposite, mobilizing the body for 'fight or flight' by raising heart rate and releasing adrenaline.","c":"The somatic nervous system controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement and carries sensory information, not the involuntary visceral 'rest and digest' functions.","d":"The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord as a whole; 'rest and digest' is governed specifically by an autonomic (peripheral) branch, not the CNS at large."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's area lies in the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe and is critical for speech production. Damage causes Broca's (expressive/non-fluent) aphasia: speech is slow, effortful, and agrammatic (missing small connecting words), yet the person can still largely understand what is said to them. The dissociation between poor output and preserved comprehension is the diagnostic clue.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia from temporal-lobe damage, the near-opposite of Broca's.","b":"Complete loss of hearing is a sensory/auditory deficit, not a language-production disorder produced by frontal-lobe damage.","d":"Inability to recognize faces is prosopagnosia, caused by damage to the fusiform face area in the occipitotemporal region, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"The hippocampus is most critical for:","concept":"The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, is essential for consolidating new declarative (episodic and semantic) long-term memories, converting short-term experiences into durable stored memories. The famous patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), whose hippocampi were removed, could no longer form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) though older memories and skills survived. This shows the hippocampus builds new memories rather than storing all of them permanently.","traps":{"a":"Regulating body temperature is a function of the hypothalamus, not the hippocampus.","b":"Controlling balance and coordination is the role of the cerebellum.","c":"Producing speech is governed by Broca's area in the frontal lobe, not the hippocampus."}},{"q":"The working memory model with a phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and central executive was proposed by:","concept":"Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) proposed the working memory model, replacing the passive short-term store with an active system. It comprises a central executive (an attentional controller) that directs two slave systems: the phonological loop for verbal and acoustic information and the visuospatial sketchpad for visual and spatial information; Baddeley later added the episodic buffer (2000).","traps":{"b":"Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968) proposed the multi-store (modal) model of sensory, short-term, and long-term memory, not the multi-component working memory model.","c":"Endel Tulving distinguished episodic from semantic long-term memory, a different contribution to memory theory.","d":"George Sperling studied the capacity and rapid decay of iconic (visual sensory) memory using partial-report experiments, not working memory structure."}},{"q":"When previously learned information interferes with the recall of newly learned information, it is called:","concept":"Proactive interference occurs when previously learned (older) information disrupts the recall of newly learned information; the learning moves forward in time to interfere. For example, an old phone number keeps intruding when you try to recall a new one. It contrasts with retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts the recall of old material.","traps":{"a":"Retroactive interference is the reverse: NEW learning interferes with the recall of OLD information (it works backward).","c":"Decay theory attributes forgetting to the fading of a memory trace over time through disuse, not to interference from other information.","d":"Encoding failure is forgetting because the information was never properly stored in the first place, not competition between two learned sets."}},{"q":"George Miller's classic estimate of the capacity of short-term memory is:","concept":"George Miller's 1956 paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two' estimated that short-term memory can hold about 7 plus or minus 2 (roughly 5 to 9) items at once. Crucially, the unit of capacity is the 'chunk', a meaningful grouping, so chunking can pack more raw information into each of those slots. This defines the limited capacity of short-term memory.","traps":{"a":"3 plus or minus 1 items is closer to Nelson Cowan's later, more conservative estimate of working memory capacity, not Miller's classic figure.","b":"12 plus or minus 3 items overstates short-term capacity well beyond any accepted estimate.","d":"Unlimited capacity describes long-term memory, not the sharply limited short-term store."}},{"q":"Negative reinforcement involves:","concept":"Negative reinforcement, an operant conditioning concept from B. F. Skinner, means removing or avoiding an aversive stimulus so that the behaviour that ended it becomes MORE likely to recur. Taking an aspirin to stop a headache reinforces pill-taking because the unpleasant pain is removed. 'Negative' refers to taking something away, not to punishment, and reinforcement always increases behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Presenting an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour is positive punishment, not negative reinforcement.","b":"Removing a reward to decrease behaviour is negative punishment (for example a time-out or a fine).","c":"Presenting a reward to increase behaviour is positive reinforcement, which adds a pleasant stimulus rather than removing an aversive one."}},{"q":"The 'Little Albert' experiment, demonstrating the conditioning of a fear response, was conducted by:","concept":"John B. Watson, the founder of behaviourism, together with Rosalie Rayner conducted the 'Little Albert' experiment in 1920. They paired a white rat (a neutral stimulus) with a loud, frightening noise until the infant, Albert, came to fear the rat alone, demonstrating that emotional responses like fear can be classically conditioned. It applied Pavlovian principles to human emotion.","traps":{"b":"B. F. Skinner is associated with operant conditioning (the Skinner box and reinforcement), not the classical conditioning of Little Albert.","c":"Edward Thorndike formulated the law of effect using puzzle-box experiments with cats, a precursor to operant conditioning.","d":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning with dogs and salivation; Watson applied Pavlov's principles to Little Albert, but Pavlov did not run the study."}},{"q":"Learning that occurs without reinforcement and is not demonstrated until there is an incentive (shown in Tolman's rat maze studies) is called:","concept":"Latent learning, demonstrated by Edward Tolman with rats in mazes, is learning that occurs without any reinforcement and remains hidden until an incentive gives the animal a reason to show it. Rats allowed to explore a maze with no reward later ran it quickly once food was introduced, revealing they had learned the layout all along. Tolman argued they had formed a cognitive map, challenging strict stimulus-response behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913).","traps":{"a":"Insight learning, studied by Wolfgang Kohler with chimpanzees, is the sudden 'aha' grasp of a solution, not learning that stays hidden until rewarded.","c":"Observational learning, associated with Albert Bandura, is acquiring behaviour by watching a model, not the unreinforced latent learning of Tolman's mazes.","d":"Vicarious learning refers to learning from the consequences experienced by others (a facet of Bandura's social learning), not from unrewarded personal exploration."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five (OCEAN) personality traits?","concept":"The Big Five, remembered by the acronym OCEAN, are the five broad dimensions of personality supported by factor analysis: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. Intelligence is a cognitive ability measured separately and is not one of these trait dimensions. The model is one of the most empirically robust frameworks in personality psychology.","traps":{"a":"Openness to experience is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'O' in OCEAN), so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Conscientiousness is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'C' in OCEAN).","d":"Neuroticism is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'N' in OCEAN), capturing emotional instability."}},{"q":"In Freud's structural model, the part of the personality that operates on the 'pleasure principle' is the:","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's structural model of personality, the id is the primitive, unconscious reservoir of drives that operates on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification of instincts regardless of reality or morality. The ego mediates using the reality principle, and the superego supplies moral standards. The id is present from birth and is entirely unconscious.","traps":{"a":"The ego operates on the reality principle, negotiating between the id's demands and external constraints, not on the pleasure principle.","b":"The superego embodies morality and conscience (the internalized rules of parents and society), the opposite of the pleasure-seeking id.","c":"The preconscious is a level of awareness (material that can be brought to mind), not a structural component driven by the pleasure principle."}},{"q":"The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) was established as a statutory body under the RCI Act of:","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India was established as a statutory body under the Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992. The Act makes registration with the RCI mandatory in order to practise as a clinical psychologist or other rehabilitation professional, and it regulates training standards and maintains a Central Rehabilitation Register. Practising without registration is a punishable offence.","traps":{"b":"1987 is the year of the old Mental Health Act, not the RCI Act.","c":"2002 is not the founding year of the RCI; no core disability-professional statute of that year applies here.","d":"2016 is the year of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, a different piece of legislation from the RCI Act."}},{"q":"The concept of an 'Advance Directive' and a 'Nominated Representative' for mental healthcare in India comes from the:","concept":"The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 introduced the rights-based provisions of an advance directive (letting a person state in advance how they wish to be treated if they lose capacity) and a nominated representative to act on their behalf. It also affirmed the right to access mental healthcare and effectively decriminalized attempted suicide. It replaced the older Mental Health Act, 1987.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the earlier, more custodial law that the 2017 Act repealed; it contained no advance directives or nominated representatives.","c":"The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 addresses disability rights broadly, not the specific advance-directive and nominated-representative mechanisms of mental healthcare.","d":"The RCI Act, 1992 regulates the training and registration of rehabilitation professionals, not patients' advance directives."}},{"q":"The 'scientist-practitioner' model of clinical psychology training (Ph.D.) originated at the 1949 conference held at:","concept":"The scientist-practitioner (Boulder) model of clinical psychology training was formulated at the 1949 Boulder Conference in Boulder, Colorado. It holds that clinical psychologists should be trained both as researchers who generate science and as practitioners who apply it, and it is associated with the Ph.D. degree. A later 1973 Vail Conference produced the practitioner-scholar (Vail) model tied to the Psy.D.","traps":{"a":"Vail (1973) gave rise to the practitioner-scholar model and the Psy.D., not the 1949 scientist-practitioner model.","b":"Salt Lake City is a distractor location not associated with either founding training-model conference.","d":"Adelphi is linked to early professional (Psy.D.-style) training programs, not the 1949 Boulder scientist-practitioner conference."}},{"q":"The legal principle that a therapist has a 'duty to warn/protect' an identifiable third party from a client's threats stems from:","concept":"The duty to warn or protect an identifiable third party stems from Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California (1976), following the killing of Tatiana Tarasoff. The court ruled that when a therapist determines a client poses a serious danger to an identifiable victim, the therapist has a duty to take reasonable steps to protect that person, creating a recognized limit to client confidentiality.","traps":{"a":"The Durham rule is an insanity-defence standard (the 'product' test), concerning criminal responsibility, not a therapist's duty to warn.","b":"The Daubert standard governs the admissibility of expert scientific testimony in court, unrelated to protecting third parties.","c":"The M'Naghten rule is the classic 'right from wrong' test for the insanity defence, again about criminal responsibility rather than confidentiality limits."}},{"q":"'Dhat syndrome', characterized by anxiety over the loss of semen, is a culture-bound syndrome reported in:","concept":"Dhat syndrome is a culture-bound syndrome reported in the Indian subcontinent (South Asia), in which men experience anxiety, fatigue, and somatic complaints that they attribute to the loss of semen (dhatu), believed to be a vital bodily essence. It illustrates how cultural beliefs shape the presentation of distress. It is recognized in cultural formulations of illness such as those in the ICD and DSM.","traps":{"b":"Malaysia is the setting for culture-bound syndromes such as amok and latah, not dhat.","c":"Latin America is associated with syndromes like susto and ataque de nervios, not semen-loss anxiety.","d":"The Arctic is linked to pibloktoq (Arctic hysteria) among Inuit populations, not dhat syndrome."}},{"q":"In India, the Act that provides for the protection of children from sexual offences is the POCSO Act of:","concept":"The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act was enacted in 2012 to protect children from sexual assault, harassment, and pornography. It is gender-neutral, defines a child as any person below 18 years of age, and provides for child-friendly procedures for reporting, recording evidence, and trial through Special Courts. It filled major gaps in earlier laws on child sexual abuse.","traps":{"a":"2005 is the year of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, not POCSO.","c":"2009 is the year of the Right to Education Act, unrelated to child sexual offences.","d":"2015 is the year of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, a distinct statute from POCSO."}},{"q":"In the Mental Status Examination (MSE), 'insight' refers to:","concept":"In the Mental Status Examination, 'insight' refers to the patient's awareness that they are ill and their understanding of the nature and cause of that illness. It is typically graded along a spectrum from complete denial of illness to intellectual and finally true emotional insight, and it has bearing on treatment adherence and prognosis. It is distinct from orientation, cognition, and perception, which are assessed separately.","traps":{"a":"Orientation to time, place, and person is a separate MSE component (part of the cognition/sensorium assessment), not insight.","b":"Serial subtraction (for example serial sevens) tests attention and concentration, a cognitive function distinct from insight.","d":"The presence of hallucinations falls under the perception section of the MSE, not insight."}},{"q":"The first psychological clinic and the term 'clinical psychology' are credited to:","concept":"Lightner Witmer founded the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology'. His clinic focused on assessing and helping children with learning and behavioural difficulties, marking the applied, individual-case beginnings of the discipline. He is therefore regarded as the founder of clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, a theory and therapy of the unconscious, not the field of clinical psychology or its first clinic.","b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879), which was for research, not clinical service.","c":"G. Stanley Hall was a pioneer of developmental and educational psychology and the first APA president, but he did not coin 'clinical psychology'."}},{"q":"The founder of psychophysics, who studied the relationship between physical stimuli and sensations, is:","concept":"Gustav Fechner is regarded as the founder of psychophysics, the study of the relationship between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. In his Elements of Psychophysics (1860) he built on Ernst Weber's findings to formulate the Weber-Fechner law, showing that sensation grows as the logarithm of stimulus intensity. This gave psychology one of its first quantitative methods.","traps":{"b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory and structuralism-oriented experimental psychology, not psychophysics as a field.","c":"Ernst Weber discovered the just-noticeable-difference and Weber's law, providing the foundation Fechner extended, but Fechner is credited as the founder of psychophysics.","d":"Hermann Ebbinghaus pioneered the experimental study of memory and forgetting (the forgetting curve), not psychophysics."}},{"q":"The first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879) was established by:","concept":"Wilhelm Wundt established the first laboratory dedicated to experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in 1879, an event conventionally treated as the birth of psychology as an independent scientific discipline. He used methods such as introspection to study conscious experience and trained many early psychologists. This separated psychology from philosophy and physiology.","traps":{"a":"William James was a founder of American psychology and functionalism, but he did not establish the first experimental laboratory at Leipzig.","c":"Edward Titchener was Wundt's student who brought structuralism to America; he did not found the original Leipzig lab.","d":"Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis clinically in Vienna and had no role in founding the first experimental psychology laboratory."}},{"q":"Functionalism, emphasizing the purpose of mental processes in adaptation, is most associated with:","concept":"Functionalism is the school of psychology, most associated with William James, that emphasized the purpose or function of mental processes in helping an organism adapt to its environment, drawing heavily on Charles Darwin's evolutionary thinking. Rather than dissecting consciousness into elements, it asked what mind and behaviour are for. James's 'Principles of Psychology' (1890) was its landmark text.","traps":{"a":"Wilhelm Wundt is linked to the experimental study of the structure of consciousness, not the adaptive-function emphasis of functionalism.","b":"Edward Titchener championed structuralism, which broke conscious experience into basic elements, the rival school to functionalism.","d":"John Watson founded behaviourism, which rejected the study of mental processes altogether in favour of observable behaviour."}},{"q":"The Gestalt principle stating that objects close together are perceived as a group is the law of:","concept":"The Gestalt law of proximity states that elements which are close together in space are perceived as belonging to the same group. It is one of the Gestalt grouping principles showing that the mind organizes perception into wholes rather than isolated parts, following the idea that 'the whole is different from the sum of its parts'. Spacing alone can create the impression of distinct clusters.","traps":{"a":"The law of similarity groups elements by shared features such as colour or shape, not by their nearness.","b":"The law of continuity (good continuation) makes us perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks, not grouping by closeness.","c":"The law of closure fills in gaps so an incomplete figure is seen as whole, a different grouping principle from proximity."}},{"q":"When participants change their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed/studied, this is the:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect (Hawthorne studies by Elton Mayo and colleagues, 1924-1932; term by Henry Landsberger, 1958) is the tendency for people to change or improve their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual experimental manipulation. It is named after the Western Electric Hawthorne Works studies, where worker productivity rose with almost any change in conditions. It is an important threat to the internal validity of research.","traps":{"b":"The placebo effect is improvement caused by the expectation of treatment when receiving an inactive substance, not by being observed.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is letting one positive trait bias overall judgment of a person, a social-perception bias unrelated to being watched.","d":"The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is when higher expectations from others (for example teachers) lead to improved performance, not a reaction to observation itself."}},{"q":"At the apex of Maslow's hierarchy of needs is:","concept":"At the apex of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, in the classic five-level pyramid, is self-actualization: the drive to realize one's full potential and become everything one is capable of becoming. Lower needs (physiological, safety, belongingness, esteem) must be reasonably met before this growth need is pursued. It represents the fulfilment of one's unique talents and purpose.","traps":{"a":"Esteem needs sit just below self-actualization in the pyramid, not at its apex.","c":"Safety needs are near the base of the hierarchy, second from the bottom, far below the top.","d":"Belongingness (love) needs occupy the middle of the hierarchy, below esteem and self-actualization."}},{"q":"The extent to which a test score predicts performance on a future criterion is called:","concept":"Predictive validity is the degree to which a test score forecasts performance on a FUTURE criterion measured later in time, for example an entrance exam predicting later academic success. It is one form of criterion-related validity. Its defining feature is the time gap between administering the test and collecting the outcome it is meant to predict.","traps":{"a":"Content validity is how well a test's items sample the full domain it is meant to cover, not its ability to forecast a future outcome.","b":"Concurrent validity correlates test scores with a criterion measured at the SAME time, not a future one.","d":"Face validity is merely whether a test appears to measure what it claims on the surface, the weakest and most superficial form of validity."}},{"q":"The two-factor theory of intelligence, proposing a general factor 'g' and specific factors 's', was given by:","concept":"Charles Spearman proposed the two-factor theory of intelligence, arguing that performance on any mental task reflects a general intelligence factor 'g' common to all tasks plus a specific factor 's' unique to that particular task. He derived this using factor analysis, a statistical technique he helped pioneer, after noticing that scores on diverse cognitive tests tend to correlate positively.","traps":{"a":"L. L. Thurstone rejected a single 'g' and proposed seven primary mental abilities instead of a two-factor structure.","b":"J. P. Guilford proposed the Structure of Intellect model with up to 120 or more distinct factors, not Spearman's two factors.","c":"Howard Gardner proposed the theory of multiple intelligences (linguistic, musical, spatial, and so on), a modern pluralistic view opposed to a single general factor."}}],"bio-testing-paper":[{"q":"The 12 cranial nerves in order from I to XII begin with which sensory nerve, the only one that does NOT pass through the thalamus before reaching its primary cortex?","concept":"The olfactory nerve (Cranial Nerve I) is the only sensory system whose afferents reach the cerebral cortex without first relaying in the thalamus: olfactory receptor axons synapse in the olfactory bulb, then project directly via the olfactory tract to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex. Every other sensory modality (vision, hearing, somatosensation, taste) synapses in a thalamic nucleus before reaching its primary cortex. This direct, phylogenetically old routing is why smell has such immediate links to emotion and memory.","traps":{"b":"The trigeminal nerve (V) carries facial somatosensation and does relay through the ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus, so it is not the thalamus-bypassing pathway.","c":"The optic nerve (II) relays in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus before reaching the primary visual cortex, so vision does pass through the thalamus.","d":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) sends auditory signals through the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex, so hearing is thalamically relayed."}},{"q":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands. Where does it decussate?","concept":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands from the motor cortex to lower motor neurons. About 80 to 90 percent of its fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the lower (caudal) medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract that then descends on the contralateral side to synapse on anterior-horn motor neurons. This is why a lesion above the medulla produces contralateral weakness. The remaining 10 to 15 percent stay ipsilateral as the anterior corticospinal tract and decussate only at their spinal segmental level.","traps":{"a":"The third ventricle is a fluid-filled cavity in the diencephalon, not a site of motor fibre crossing; no tract decussates there.","b":"Claiming no decussation contradicts the defining feature of the corticospinal system and would wrongly predict ipsilateral rather than contralateral hemiplegia after a cortical lesion.","c":"The mid-pons is where corticopontine fibres synapse en route to the cerebellum, not where the corticospinal tract crosses; the crossing is lower, in the medulla."}},{"q":"The direct pathway through the basal ganglia is most accurately described as:","concept":"The direct pathway of the basal ganglia facilitates movement. Cortex excites striatal neurons bearing D1 receptors, which inhibit the internal globus pallidus and substantia nigra pars reticulata (GPi/SNr); because GPi/SNr normally inhibit the thalamus, this inhibition disinhibits the thalamus, which then drives the cortex and promotes movement. Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta reinforces this by exciting the D1 direct pathway while inhibiting the D2 indirect pathway. Loss of dopamine impairs the direct pathway (Parkinsonian hypokinesia); overactivity produces hyperkinetic chorea.","traps":{"a":"This describes the indirect pathway (D2 receptors, external GP, subthalamic nucleus) whose net effect inhibits movement, not the movement-facilitating direct pathway.","c":"Hippocampus to fornix to thalamus is the Papez memory circuit, not a basal ganglia motor loop.","d":"The cortico-cerebellar loop coordinates movement but always relays through the thalamus back to cortex, so 'no thalamic relay' is wrong and it is not the basal ganglia direct pathway."}},{"q":"MAO-A and MAO-B enzymes principally metabolise:","concept":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) enzymes catabolise monoamine neurotransmitters. MAO-A preferentially degrades serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, and dietary tyramine, and predominates in the gut and liver; MAO-B preferentially breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine and predominates in the brain. This is why monoamine oxidase inhibitors raise monoamine levels and act as antidepressants, why selective MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline) help Parkinson's, and why tyramine plus a non-selective MAOI can trigger a hypertensive crisis.","traps":{"b":"GABA is inactivated mainly by GABA transaminase and reuptake, not by monoamine oxidase, since it is an amino-acid transmitter, not a monoamine.","c":"Glutamate and glycine are amino-acid transmitters cleared by transporters and other enzymes, not substrates of MAO.","d":"Acetylcholine is broken down by acetylcholinesterase, an entirely different enzyme from monoamine oxidase."}},{"q":"Astrocytes perform all of the following functions EXCEPT:","concept":"Astrocytes are the principal supporting glia of the central nervous system, but they do not conduct action propagating action potentials; they lack the density of propagating voltage-gated sodium channels needed and instead signal slowly via calcium waves. Their genuine roles include maintaining the blood-brain barrier through perivascular end-feet, buffering extracellular potassium, taking up and recycling glutamate through the glutamate-glutamine cycle, providing metabolic substrates, and forming glial scars. Conducting action potentials is the job of neurons.","traps":{"a":"Recycling glutamate via the glutamate-glutamine cycle is a genuine astrocyte function, so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Maintaining the blood-brain barrier with end-feet on capillaries is a real astrocyte role, so it is not the exception.","d":"Potassium buffering and supplying metabolic substrates to neurons are established astrocyte functions, so this is not the exception."}},{"q":"Microglia function chiefly as:","concept":"Microglia are the resident macrophages and immune cells of the central nervous system, derived from yolk-sac progenitors rather than neural tissue. They perform immune surveillance, phagocytose debris and pathogens, release cytokines, and prune synapses (especially during development). Activated microglia drive neuroinflammation and are implicated in Alzheimer's disease, multiple sclerosis, and chronic pain. Myelination is handled by oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the periphery.","traps":{"a":"Myelin in the CNS is made by oligodendrocytes, not microglia, so this describes the wrong glial cell.","b":"Potassium buffering around neurons is a hallmark astrocyte function, not a microglial one.","d":"Sensing voltage is a property of neuronal ion channels, not a function of microglia."}},{"q":"Mirror neurons, first described in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5), fire when:","concept":"Mirror neurons, first described by Rizzolatti, Gallese, di Pellegrino and colleagues in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5) in the 1990s, fire both when the animal performs a goal-directed action and when it observes another individual performing the same action. This dual firing has been proposed as a neural substrate for action understanding, imitation, and empathy, with a putative human mirror system in the inferior frontal gyrus, inferior parietal lobule, and superior temporal sulcus. The correct option captures this defining execution-plus-observation property.","traps":{"b":"Firing only during self-executed action describes ordinary motor neurons, missing the observation component that defines mirror neurons.","c":"Firing only during observation describes purely visual or perceptual neurons, missing the execution component of mirror neurons.","d":"Restricting activity to REM sleep is unrelated to mirror neurons, which are defined by waking action and observation."}},{"q":"The locus coeruleus is the principal source of which neurotransmitter in the CNS?","concept":"The locus coeruleus, a small pigmented nucleus in the dorsal pons near the fourth ventricle, is the principal source of norepinephrine in the central nervous system, sending widespread projections to the cortex, hippocampus, and cerebellum. It governs arousal, attention, and the stress response. Knowing the other principal sources helps: the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra supply dopamine, the raphe nuclei supply serotonin, and the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum supply acetylcholine.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin originates from the raphe nuclei along the brainstem midline, not from the locus coeruleus.","c":"Acetylcholine in the CNS comes chiefly from the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum, not the locus coeruleus.","d":"Dopamine arises mainly from the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra pars compacta, not the locus coeruleus."}},{"q":"The raphe nuclei are arranged along which axis and are the major source of:","concept":"The raphe nuclei are clusters of serotonergic neurons arranged along the midline of the brainstem and are the major source of serotonin (5-HT) projections in the brain; the rostral group projects to the forebrain while the caudal group projects to the spinal cord. They modulate mood, sleep, appetite, and pain, and the dorsal raphe is the largest of them. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors act downstream of these projections at serotonergic synapses.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum and acetylcholine have nothing to do with the raphe nuclei, which are brainstem serotonergic clusters.","b":"The raphe nuclei lie along the brainstem, not the spinal cord, and release serotonin rather than dopamine.","d":"Oxytocin comes from hypothalamic neurons (paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei), not from the raphe nuclei."}},{"q":"The hypothalamus connects to the anterior pituitary primarily via:","concept":"The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is controlled by releasing and inhibiting hormones that the hypothalamus secretes into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal venous system at the median eminence; these hormones (including CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, and dopamine) travel through blood to reach the gland. This vascular link contrasts with the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis), which receives direct axons from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei that release oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone straight into the circulation.","traps":{"a":"Cerebrospinal fluid is not the route by which hypothalamic hormones reach the anterior pituitary; a dedicated portal blood system is.","b":"Direct neural axons connect the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary, not the anterior pituitary, which is controlled hormonally.","d":"The vagus nerve is a parasympathetic nerve to the viscera and has no role in anterior pituitary control."}},{"q":"Pre-ganglionic sympathetic neurons originate from spinal cord segments:","concept":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons arise from the thoracolumbar spinal cord, specifically segments T1 to L2, where their cell bodies sit in the intermediolateral cell column. Sympathetic preganglionic fibres are relatively short (synapsing in nearby sympathetic chain ganglia) with long postganglionic fibres. This contrasts with the parasympathetic (craniosacral) outflow from cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X plus sacral segments S2 to S4. The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion releasing epinephrine into the blood.","traps":{"a":"Cervical levels contain no lateral horn for sympathetic outflow; the sympathetic origin is thoracolumbar (T1 to L2).","c":"Cranial nerves carry parasympathetic (and other) fibres, not the sympathetic preganglionic outflow.","d":"S2 to S4 is the sacral parasympathetic outflow, not the sympathetic origin."}},{"q":"The somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus shows that the largest cortical representation belongs to:","concept":"Penfield's somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus is grossly distorted: body parts with dense sensory receptors and fine discriminative ability, namely the hand, lips, and tongue, occupy disproportionately large cortical territory, while the trunk and proximal limbs occupy small areas. This cortical magnification reflects receptor density and functional importance rather than physical skin area, which is why our fingertips and mouth are so sensitive.","traps":{"a":"The trunk and back have sparse sensory innervation and therefore a small, not large, cortical representation.","b":"The knee and hip are proximal, low-acuity regions with modest cortical territory.","c":"The dorsum of the foot has low receptor density and only a small cortical area, unlike the hand and face."}},{"q":"Parvocellular vs magnocellular layers of the LGN differ in that:","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus has six layers with a functional division of labour. Magnocellular layers (1 and 2) contain large cells that are fast-conducting, achromatic, and specialised for motion and contrast, feeding the dorsal 'where/how' stream. Parvocellular layers (3 to 6) contain small cells that are slower, colour-sensitive (red-green), and specialised for fine detail, feeding the ventral 'what' stream. Koniocellular layers between them handle blue-yellow signals. The correct option pairs each layer set with its true properties.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the roles: magnocellular handles motion and contrast, while parvocellular handles colour and fine detail.","b":"Magnocellular layers feed the dorsal motion stream, not exclusively area V4, which is a colour and form region of the ventral stream.","c":"The two layer types carry distinctly different information (motion versus colour and detail), so they are not identical."}},{"q":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound waves into neural signals primarily via:","concept":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound by mechanotransduction, not by chemical or purely electrical triggering. As the basilar membrane moves, the stereocilia of inner hair cells bend; this bending pulls tip-links that mechanically open cation channels, allowing potassium to flow in from the potassium-rich endolymph, which depolarises the cell and triggers glutamate release onto spiral ganglion fibres of cranial nerve VIII. Outer hair cells amplify the signal via the motor protein prestin, and frequency is coded tonotopically (high frequencies at the base, low at the apex).","traps":{"a":"Transduction opens cation channels via mechanical bending; it is not driven by closure of voltage-gated sodium channels.","b":"GABA is not the trigger; the depolarising event is potassium influx through mechanically gated channels, and hair cells release glutamate.","d":"Olfactory chemical signalling is an entirely different modality and has nothing to do with cochlear mechanotransduction."}},{"q":"The vestibular system's semicircular canals detect:","concept":"The three semicircular canals (anterior, posterior, and lateral) of the vestibular system detect rotational, that is angular, acceleration of the head in three planes: endolymph movement deflects the cupula within the ampulla, bending hair cells. This is distinct from the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), which detect linear acceleration and head tilt via otoconia. Vestibular signals travel through cranial nerve VIII to the vestibular nuclei, driving the vestibulo-ocular reflex and postural control.","traps":{"a":"Static head position and tilt are sensed by the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), not the semicircular canals.","c":"Auditory pitch is coded by the cochlea, not by the vestibular semicircular canals.","d":"Linear acceleration is detected by the otolith organs; the semicircular canals detect angular, not linear, acceleration."}},{"q":"BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) is most strongly implicated in:","concept":"Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) is a neurotrophin that binds TrkB receptors and drives signalling supporting neuronal survival, dendritic growth, long-term potentiation, and hippocampal neurogenesis. It is central to the neurotrophic hypothesis of depression: BDNF is reduced in major depressive disorder, and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, electroconvulsive therapy, exercise, and ketamine all raise it. The Val66Met polymorphism, which affects activity-dependent BDNF secretion, is studied as a risk variant for depression and cognitive outcomes.","traps":{"b":"Firing individual action potentials is a fast electrical event mediated by ion channels, not the trophic, plasticity-related role of BDNF.","c":"BDNF promotes survival and growth rather than being an 'apoptosis only' factor, so this is the opposite of its main action.","d":"Myelin synthesis is carried out by oligodendrocytes and is not the defining function of BDNF."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) at hippocampal synapses differs from LTP in that LTD involves:","concept":"Long-term depression (LTD) is the mirror image of long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973) and weakens synaptic strength. At hippocampal synapses, brief low-frequency stimulation produces a smaller but more prolonged rise in postsynaptic calcium, which favours protein phosphatases (PP1 and PP2A) over kinases, leading to removal of AMPA receptors from the membrane and a sustained decrease in synaptic strength. Together LTP and LTD allow bidirectional, tunable plasticity; a distinct cerebellar LTD at climbing-fibre to Purkinje-cell synapses underlies motor learning.","traps":{"a":"LTD weakens a synapse but does not kill the cell; it is a reversible change in synaptic strength, not permanent cell death.","b":"Glutamate release does have a postsynaptic effect in LTD, namely a modest calcium rise that triggers AMPA receptor removal.","d":"High-frequency stimulation with NMDA blockade would prevent plasticity, whereas LTD is induced by low-frequency stimulation that still admits calcium through NMDA receptors."}},{"q":"The P300 event-related potential is most associated with:","concept":"The P300 (or P3) is a positive event-related potential appearing about 300 milliseconds after an infrequent target stimulus in the oddball paradigm, reflecting attention allocation, stimulus categorisation, and context updating. Its amplitude is reduced and its latency prolonged in schizophrenia and Alzheimer's disease, and it is studied as an endophenotype. Other components differ: N400 indexes semantic incongruence, P600 indexes syntactic violations, and mismatch negativity reflects pre-attentive auditory change detection.","traps":{"a":"Motor command generation relates to the readiness potential (Bereitschaftspotential), not the P300.","b":"REM sleep onset is not indexed by the P300, which is a waking attentional response to oddball stimuli.","c":"The initial visual cortex response is an early sensory component (such as P1 or C1), far earlier and different from the cognitive P300."}},{"q":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is most directly caused by:","concept":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome results directly from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, classically in chronic alcohol use. Wernicke's encephalopathy is the acute phase with the triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia; Korsakoff's is the chronic phase with anterograde amnesia, confabulation, and apathy, reflecting damage to the mammillary bodies and thalamus. Treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose, because administering glucose first can precipitate or worsen the syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Hypoxia causes diffuse ischaemic brain injury, not the specific thiamine-dependent mammillary and thalamic pathology of this syndrome.","c":"Lead poisoning causes peripheral neuropathy and encephalopathy but is not the cause of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","d":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration and megaloblastic anaemia, a different deficiency from the thiamine loss that causes this syndrome."}},{"q":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics primarily exert clinical effects via blockade of which receptors, and which side-effect cluster results?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol, chlorpromazine, and fluphenazine exert their antipsychotic effect mainly by blocking dopamine D2 receptors. Blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive symptoms, but blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces extrapyramidal symptoms (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia), and blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Atypicals add serotonin 5-HT2A blockade (the Meltzer hypothesis), reducing extrapyramidal symptoms but increasing metabolic side effects.","traps":{"a":"5-HT2A blockade and metabolic syndrome characterise atypical antipsychotics, not the D2-driven typicals in question.","b":"NMDA glutamate involvement relates to models of psychosis and drugs like ketamine, not the mechanism of typical antipsychotics.","c":"GABA-A modulation with sedation describes benzodiazepines, not the dopamine-blocking action of typical antipsychotics."}},{"q":"The Yerkes-Dodson law describes the relationship between:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson, 1908) describes the relationship between arousal and task performance as an inverted-U: performance improves as arousal rises to a moderate optimum and then declines with further arousal. Crucially, the optimum is lower for difficult or novel tasks and higher for simple, well-learned ones, which is why a little anxiety can help but too much impairs. Related ideas include the Easterbrook cue-utilisation hypothesis of attentional narrowing under arousal.","traps":{"b":"The relationship between IQ and emotional intelligence (Peter Salovey and John Mayer, 1990) is a separate individual-differences question, not the Yerkes-Dodson arousal law.","c":"Salary and job satisfaction is an organisational-psychology topic unrelated to the arousal-performance curve.","d":"Punishment severity and learning rate concerns reinforcement and conditioning, not the inverted-U of arousal and performance."}},{"q":"The 'two-streams' (dorsal/ventral) visual processing hypothesis (Ungerleider & Mishkin, 1982) proposes:","concept":"The two-streams hypothesis of Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982) holds that after the primary visual cortex, visual information splits into two pathways: a dorsal stream running to the parietal lobe that handles 'where' and 'how' (spatial location and action guidance), and a ventral stream running to the inferior temporal lobe that handles 'what' (object recognition, including faces and places). Milner and Goodale later reframed this as an action versus perception distinction, supported by the double dissociation between optic ataxia (dorsal damage) and visual agnosia (ventral damage).","traps":{"a":"This reverses the mapping: the dorsal stream handles spatial and motion-related 'where/how' processing, while colour is a ventral-stream property.","b":"Both streams are visual; neither is auditory, so this mischaracterises the pathways entirely.","d":"The streams diverge after V1 rather than converging in it, so 'both converge in V1' is incorrect."}},{"q":"Tonic-clonic seizures involve:","concept":"A generalised tonic-clonic seizure (grand mal) involves loss of consciousness with widespread cortical hyperexcitation, unfolding as a tonic phase of generalised muscle stiffening (with apnoea and cyanosis, lasting about 10 to 30 seconds), followed by a clonic phase of rhythmic jerking (about 1 to 2 minutes), then post-ictal confusion. The EEG shows high-amplitude generalised spike-and-wave activity. Treatment options include valproate, levetiracetam, and lamotrigine.","traps":{"a":"A brief staring spell without loss of tone describes a typical absence seizure, not a tonic-clonic one.","c":"Motor symptoms confined to one limb with preserved awareness describe a simple partial (focal) seizure, not a generalised tonic-clonic event.","d":"A sleep-only seizure with no EEG correlate does not fit tonic-clonic seizures, which have a florid generalised spike-and-wave EEG signature."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease pathology principally involves degeneration of:","concept":"Parkinson's disease is defined pathologically by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, with alpha-synuclein-containing Lewy body inclusions. Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine impairs facilitation of the basal ganglia direct pathway, producing bradykinesia, rigidity, a resting tremor (3 to 5 Hz), and postural instability. Treatment is symptomatic: L-DOPA combined with carbidopa (a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor), dopamine agonists, and MAO-B inhibitors.","traps":{"a":"Degeneration of spinal motor neurons characterises amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not Parkinson's disease.","b":"Loss of hippocampal pyramidal cells relates to amnestic and Alzheimer-type pathology, not the nigral degeneration of Parkinson's.","c":"Cholinergic basal forebrain neuron loss is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the dopaminergic loss of Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The amygdala receives a 'low road' input from the thalamus that bypasses the cortex (LeDoux). This pathway:","concept":"Joseph LeDoux's dual-route model of fear describes a fast subcortical 'low road' in which the sensory thalamus projects directly to the amygdala, bypassing the cortex, to permit rapid but crude detection of potential threat and quick defensive responses before conscious recognition. A slower 'high road' runs from the sensory thalamus through the cortex to the amygdala, providing more accurate identification. The low road's value is speed for survival-critical reactions, even at the cost of precision.","traps":{"a":"The low road provides only crude, fast threat signals, not the full detailed content of conscious vision, which depends on cortical processing.","c":"Voluntary motor output is generated by motor cortical and basal ganglia circuits, not by the thalamo-amygdala threat pathway.","d":"Slower, more accurate object identification is the function of the cortical 'high road', not the fast 'low road'."}},{"q":"Wakefulness is associated on EEG with which dominant frequencies?","concept":"On the EEG, alert wakefulness is dominated by beta activity (13 to 30 Hz), reflecting focused, active cognition, while relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows posterior alpha rhythm (8 to 13 Hz) that blocks (attenuates) when the eyes open. As one moves through drowsiness and sleep the frequencies slow: theta (4 to 8 Hz) in N1 drowsiness, sleep spindles and K-complexes in N2, and high-amplitude delta (0.5 to 4 Hz) in N3 deep sleep. The correct option pairs both waking states with their true bands.","traps":{"b":"Theta alone characterises drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not the fully awake states.","c":"Sleep spindles are a signature of N2 sleep, not of wakefulness.","d":"Delta predominance marks deep N3 sleep, the opposite of alert wakefulness."}},{"q":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are most densely concentrated in:","concept":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are the most abundant G-protein-coupled receptors in the brain, densely expressed in the hippocampus (memory), basal ganglia and cerebellum (motor control), cortex, hypothalamus, and amygdala. Acting through Gi/o proteins, they reduce presynaptic neurotransmitter release, and their endogenous ligands are anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol. This central distribution explains cannabis's effects on memory, movement, and reward. By contrast, CB2 receptors are found mainly on immune cells and microglia.","traps":{"a":"CB1 receptors are widespread in the brain, not confined to spinal motor neurons, so limiting them to the cord is wrong.","c":"Immune cells express mainly CB2, not CB1, so 'peripheral immune cells exclusively' misattributes the receptor.","d":"The adrenal cortex is not the dense CB1 site; CB1 concentrates in brain regions like the hippocampus and basal ganglia."}},{"q":"The hippocampal formation is critical for:","concept":"The hippocampal formation is critical for encoding and consolidating declarative memory, especially episodic and spatial memory (its place cells earned John O'Keefe a share of the 2014 Nobel Prize). Importantly, it is not the permanent storage site of remote memories: over time, according to the standard consolidation model, memories become dependent on distributed neocortical stores, though multiple-trace theory argues the hippocampus stays involved for detailed episodic recall. Procedural and motor memory instead depend on the striatum and cerebellum.","traps":{"b":"Procedural and motor memory rely on the striatum and cerebellum, not the hippocampus, which handles declarative memory.","c":"Olfactory transduction occurs in the olfactory epithelium and bulb, not the hippocampus.","d":"Voluntary motor execution is a function of the motor cortex and basal ganglia, not the hippocampal formation."}},{"q":"Multiple sclerosis is characterised by:","concept":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune, T-cell-mediated demyelinating disease of central nervous system white matter, in which oligodendrocytes and their myelin are the target, producing lesions disseminated in time and space (the basis of the McDonald criteria). Typical lesions are periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, and spinal, causing optic neuritis, paraesthesias, weakness, and ataxia; MRI shows T2 hyperintense plaques. Early on there is demyelination with relative axonal preservation, and treatment uses disease-modifying therapies such as interferon-beta, glatiramer, and monoclonal antibodies.","traps":{"a":"Alpha-synuclein deposits in the substantia nigra define Parkinson's disease, not the demyelinating pathology of multiple sclerosis.","c":"Cortical beta-amyloid plaques characterise Alzheimer's disease, not the white-matter demyelination of multiple sclerosis.","d":"Lower motor neuron degeneration describes disorders like amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not the myelin-targeted autoimmunity of multiple sclerosis."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is most sensitive to dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948) requires the subject to sort cards by an unstated rule (colour, shape, or number) and to shift sets when feedback signals the rule has changed. It is most sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal cortex dysfunction, which produces perseverative errors (continuing to sort by the old rule), and it is widely used in schizophrenia, frontal lobe injury, and ADHD. For contrast, the Stroop taps the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate, the Iowa Gambling Task taps the orbitofrontal cortex, and the Tower of London taps planning.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex governs movement execution, not the set-shifting and rule-updating measured by the card sorting test.","c":"The hippocampus supports declarative memory, whereas the card sorting test probes executive set-shifting.","d":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and some cognition, but the card sorting test's perseverative-error sensitivity localises to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex."}},{"q":"The WAIS-IV Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI) consists of which core subtests?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV), the Verbal Comprehension Index taps acquired verbal knowledge and reasoning, and its three core subtests are Similarities, Vocabulary, and Information (Comprehension is supplementary). Each of the four indexes draws on its own cluster of subtests, so the correct grouping is defined by which construct the index measures. VCI reflects crystallised verbal ability, distinct from perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed.","traps":{"a":"Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, and Visual Puzzles are the core subtests of the Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), which measures nonverbal fluid reasoning, not verbal comprehension.","b":"Coding, Symbol Search, and Cancellation belong to the Processing Speed Index (PSI), tapping speed of simple visual scanning rather than verbal knowledge.","c":"Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing make up the Working Memory Index (WMI), which measures mental manipulation and attention, not verbal comprehension."}},{"q":"Raven's Progressive Matrices is considered a 'culture-fair' test because it measures:","concept":"Raven's Progressive Matrices, devised by John C. Raven in 1936, presents abstract visual patterns with a missing piece that the examinee completes by inferring the underlying rule. Because the task uses no words and minimal cultural content, it is regarded as a 'culture-fair' measure of fluid (nonverbal) reasoning, or Gf in Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory. It is one of the purest markers of general reasoning ability, though education and culture still exert some real-world influence.","traps":{"a":"Reading comprehension is a verbal, language-loaded skill; the whole point of Raven's is to avoid such content so it can be culture-fair.","c":"Crystallised verbal intelligence (Gc) reflects acquired knowledge and vocabulary, the opposite of the nonverbal fluid reasoning Raven's is designed to isolate.","d":"Memory alone is not what the matrices assess; the task requires active rule induction and pattern completion, not simple recall."}},{"q":"On the Stanford-Binet 5 (SB5), the five factors are:","concept":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, Fifth Edition (SB5, Gale H. Roid, 2003), is organised around five Cattell-Horn-Carroll factors, each assessed in both a verbal and a nonverbal domain, giving ten subtests. The five factors are Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing, and Working Memory. This yields a Full Scale IQ plus Verbal IQ, Nonverbal IQ, and factor index scores.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, Motor, Memory, Attention, and Language describe a neuropsychological domain breakdown, not the CHC factor structure of the SB5.","b":"The Verbal IQ / Performance IQ split belongs to older Wechsler editions (e.g. WAIS-III); the SB5 crosses verbal and nonverbal domains onto five factors instead.","d":"The Big Five personality factors measure personality, not intelligence, and have nothing to do with the SB5 cognitive structure."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 clinical scale 'Pd' (Psychopathic Deviate, Scale 4) classically elevates in:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory, Second Edition (MMPI-2), Scale 4, Psychopathic Deviate (Pd), was originally developed to identify social maladjustment and rebelliousness. High scorers characteristically show antisocial behaviour, conflict with family and authority, impulsivity, and a sense of alienation. It is not a mood or anxiety scale; it captures a behavioural-dyscontrol and rule-defiance profile.","traps":{"a":"Mania is indexed by Scale 9 (Ma, Hypomania), not by Scale 4; elevation on Pd is not exclusive to mania.","b":"Depression is captured by Scale 2 (D); Pd is not a depression-specific scale even though depressive features can co-occur.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive and anxiety features load on Scale 7 (Pt, Psychasthenia), not on the Psychopathic Deviate scale."}},{"q":"The MoCA (Montreal Cognitive Assessment) differs from the MMSE in that MoCA:","concept":"The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA; Ziad Nasreddine, 2005) is a 30-point, roughly 10-minute cognitive screen designed to catch mild cognitive impairment that the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE; Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975) often misses. It samples executive function (trail-making, alternating sequence), clock-drawing, abstraction, and delayed recall, giving it greater sensitivity to frontal-executive and early deficits. A common screening cut-off is a score of 25 or below out of 30, with an education adjustment.","traps":{"a":"The MoCA tests memory (delayed recall) plus multiple other domains; claiming it omits memory is simply false.","c":"The MoCA is a brief screen usable by trained clinicians, nurses, and general practitioners, not something restricted to neurologists.","d":"The MoCA and MMSE take comparable time (about 10 minutes each); the real distinction is sensitivity and executive-function coverage, not a 5-versus-30-minute gap."}},{"q":"The PANSS (Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale) is used to assess:","concept":"The Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein, and Lewis Opler, 1987) is a 30-item, clinician-rated instrument for grading symptom severity in schizophrenia. Items divide into a 7-item Positive subscale, a 7-item Negative subscale, and a 16-item General Psychopathology subscale, each rated 1 to 7. It is the standard outcome measure in schizophrenia trials, distinct from scales targeting mood, personality, or cognition.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with instruments like the SCID-5-PD or PCL-R (Robert Hare), not the PANSS, which is schizophrenia-specific.","b":"Depression severity is rated by scales such as the Hamilton or Beck inventories; the PANSS measures psychotic and general psychopathology, not depression per se.","c":"Cognitive impairment is screened by tools like the MoCA or MMSE (Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975); the PANSS rates symptom severity, not cognitive status."}},{"q":"The Y-BOCS (Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale) rates:","concept":"The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS; Wayne Goodman et al., 1989) is the gold-standard clinician-rated measure of OCD severity. It has 10 items, five rating obsessions and five rating compulsions, each scored 0 to 4 across the dimensions of time occupied, interference, distress, resistance, and control, for a total of 0 to 40. Crucially it rates severity independent of symptom content, which a preceding symptom checklist catalogues.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is measured by scales like the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, not the Y-BOCS, which is OCD-specific.","b":"Depression severity is captured by instruments such as the Beck Depression Inventory; the Y-BOCS does not rate mood.","c":"Hallucination frequency is a psychotic symptom rated on scales like the PANSS (Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987) or SAPS, unrelated to the obsessive-compulsive focus of the Y-BOCS."}},{"q":"The PCL-R (Psychopathy Checklist–Revised) was developed by:","concept":"The Psychopathy Checklist-Revised (PCL-R) was developed by Robert D. Hare in 1991, building on Hervey Cleckley's clinical description of psychopathy in 'The Mask of Sanity' (1941). It comprises 20 items scored 0 to 2 by a trained clinician using a semi-structured interview plus file review, giving a maximum of 40, with 30 the conventional North American cut-off for psychopathy. It is a central tool in forensic risk assessment.","traps":{"a":"Hervey Cleckley (1941) provided the foundational clinical concept of psychopathy but did not create the PCL-R; Hare operationalised it decades later.","b":"Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy and its depression and anxiety inventories, not a psychopathy checklist.","d":"Hans Eysenck built dimensional personality theory (the PEN model) and its questionnaires, not the PCL-R."}},{"q":"Cohen's kappa (κ) is used to estimate:","concept":"Cohen's kappa is a chance-corrected index of agreement between two raters classifying cases into nominal or categorical categories, computed as kappa = (Po minus Pe) divided by (1 minus Pe), where Po is observed and Pe is expected-by-chance agreement. It answers how reliably two raters assign the same category beyond what coincidence would produce. Landis and Koch benchmarks label values above 0.61 as substantial and above 0.81 as almost perfect.","traps":{"b":"Predictive validity concerns how well a test forecasts a future criterion, a validity question, not the inter-rater agreement kappa quantifies.","c":"Effect size for ANOVA is captured by statistics like eta-squared or Cohen's f, not by kappa.","d":"Internal consistency (how well items hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha or KR-20, not by a chance-corrected agreement coefficient."}},{"q":"Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts:","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts how a test's reliability changes when its length is altered by a factor k, via r_new = (k times r_old) divided by (1 plus (k minus 1) times r_old). Adding parallel items raises reliability with diminishing returns. Its classic use is correcting split-half reliability, estimating the full test's reliability from a single half, and planning how many items are needed to hit a target reliability.","traps":{"a":"The validity of a new test concerns its correlation with a criterion or construct, which the Spearman-Brown formula does not address; it deals with reliability and length.","b":"Effect size in ANCOVA is a separate statistical quantity; the formula makes no prediction about experimental effect sizes.","c":"The factor structure of the WAIS is a matter for factor analysis, not for a reliability-by-length formula."}},{"q":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) differs from Cronbach's alpha in that KR-20:","concept":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) is an internal-consistency reliability coefficient that applies specifically to dichotomously scored items, those marked right/wrong or yes/no. It is in fact the special case of Cronbach's alpha for two-category items; when items are polytomous (e.g. Likert scales) Cronbach's alpha generalises the same idea. KR-21 is a simplified variant assuming equal item difficulty.","traps":{"b":"KR-20 does estimate internal consistency; saying it cannot is the opposite of its purpose.","c":"KR-20 and Cronbach's alpha are not identical in scope: KR-20 handles only dichotomous items, whereas alpha extends to multi-point scoring.","d":"KR-20 is used for right/wrong dichotomous items (often ability tests), not exclusively for personality items."}},{"q":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) differs from Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA) primarily in that CFA:","concept":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) tests a factor structure that the researcher specifies in advance, fixing which items load on which latent factors and then evaluating model fit with indices such as CFI, TLI, RMSEA, and SRMR. Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA), by contrast, is data-driven: it discovers how many factors exist and how items cluster without an a priori model. CFA is the measurement component of structural equation modelling.","traps":{"a":"CFA does not always use oblique rotation; rotation is an EFA concern, and CFA instead constrains loadings within a specified model.","c":"CFA is not limited to dichotomous data; it handles continuous and ordinal indicators with appropriate estimators.","d":"Having no a priori model describes EFA, not CFA; CFA is defined precisely by testing a pre-specified model."}},{"q":"A Likert-type scale differs from a Thurstone scale in that Likert items are:","concept":"A Likert-type scale, introduced by Rensis Likert in 1932, asks respondents to rate their agreement with each statement on a symmetric continuum (for example 1 to 5 or 1 to 7), and the endorsed ratings are summed into a total score assuming roughly equal item weights. The key contrast with a Thurstone scale is that Thurstone items are pre-scaled by judges before administration, whereas Likert items are weighted equally and scaled by the respondents themselves.","traps":{"a":"Likert items are not dichotomous; they use a graded multi-point agree-disagree continuum, which is what distinguishes them.","c":"Pre-scaling of items by judges before administration is the defining feature of the Thurstone equal-appearing-intervals method, not the Likert method.","d":"Drawing responses from a sentence-completion stimulus describes a projective technique, not a Likert rating scale."}},{"q":"Q-sort methodology was developed by William Stephenson and Carl Rogers to:","concept":"Q-sort methodology, developed by William Stephenson and adopted by Carl Rogers, has a respondent sort a set of descriptive statements (such as self-descriptors) into a forced quasi-normal distribution from most to least characteristic. This captures idiographic, within-person structure rather than comparing one person against others. Rogers famously used it to measure the congruence between self-concept and ideal self before and after therapy.","traps":{"a":"Q-sort was not designed to replace the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943); it is a distinct idiographic sorting method, not a substitute clinical inventory.","c":"Infant reflex testing belongs to neurodevelopmental examination, entirely unrelated to Q-sort card sorting.","d":"Q-sort is not a diagnostic instrument for schizophrenia; it measures self-perception and within-person variation."}},{"q":"The Hawthorne effect refers to:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect, named after the Western Electric Hawthorne plant studies (Roethlisberger and Dickson, 1924 to 1932), is the tendency of people to change their behaviour, typically improving performance, simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual intervention. It is a form of reactivity or observer effect. Recognising it matters because apparent treatment gains may reflect being watched rather than the manipulation itself.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics arising from instructions are cues about the hypothesis (Orne's concept), distinct from the mere effect of being observed.","b":"Experimenters unconsciously biasing the data describes experimenter expectancy or the Rosenthal/Pygmalion effect, not the Hawthorne effect.","d":"The placebo effect is improvement from an inert treatment believed to be active, a different mechanism from reactivity to observation."}},{"q":"Demand characteristics, as described by Martin Orne, are:","concept":"Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne in 1962, are the cues within an experimental setting, including instructions, the setting itself, rumours, and procedures, from which participants infer what the experimenter expects and then adjust their behaviour accordingly. They threaten internal validity by turning the participant into a hypothesis-guesser. Controls include blinding, unobtrusive measures, deception with debriefing, and post-experimental inquiry.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics are cues in the situation, not properties of the experimental hypothesis itself.","b":"Statistical demands of a test refer to assumptions or requirements of an analysis, not to situational cues that shape participant behaviour.","c":"The amount of effort an item requires is an item-difficulty or workload notion, unrelated to Orne's concept of situational expectancy cues."}},{"q":"A ceiling effect on a test means:","concept":"A ceiling effect occurs when so many examinees score at or near the maximum that the test can no longer discriminate among the highest performers, compressing variance at the top. Its mirror image, the floor effect, bunches low performers at the minimum. Both restrict range, lower reliability and validity for the affected end, and can mask genuine differences or intervention effects; the remedy is adding items of appropriate difficulty.","traps":{"b":"Items being too difficult overall would drive scores down toward the minimum (a floor effect), which is the opposite of a ceiling effect.","c":"Reliability cannot exceed 1, and a ceiling effect actually reduces reliability at the top rather than being about impossible reliability values.","d":"A normal distribution of scores implies good spread; a ceiling effect specifically produces a skewed pile-up at the maximum, not normality."}},{"q":"Convergent validity is demonstrated when:","concept":"Convergent validity is evidence for construct validity shown when a test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, for example a depression scale correlating strongly with another depression measure. It is one half of construct validation (Cronbach and Meehl, 1955), paired with discriminant validity (low correlation with unrelated constructs). Campbell and Fiske's multitrait-multimethod matrix (1959) evaluates both at once.","traps":{"b":"Two raters agreeing on a score is inter-rater reliability, not convergent validity across measures of a construct.","c":"A test predicting future criteria is predictive (criterion) validity, a separate form of validity from convergent evidence.","d":"Failing to correlate with measures of unrelated constructs defines discriminant validity, the complement of convergent validity, not convergent validity itself."}},{"q":"Criterion contamination refers to:","concept":"Criterion contamination occurs when the predictor, or knowledge of the predictor's scores, influences the criterion measure, so that the observed predictor-criterion correlation is spuriously inflated. A classic case is a supervisor who knows employees' test scores and then rates their job performance partly on that knowledge. Remedies include blinding raters and using independent criteria; it differs from criterion deficiency, where the criterion omits relevant content.","traps":{"a":"Random measurement error in the criterion is unreliability, which attenuates (lowers) correlations, the opposite of the inflation criterion contamination causes.","b":"Replacing the criterion mid-study is a design change, not the overlap-driven artefactual inflation that defines contamination.","d":"A criterion with too few items concerns criterion reliability or breadth, not the predictor-into-criterion leakage that constitutes contamination."}},{"q":"A z-score of +1.5 on a normally distributed test corresponds to approximately which percentile?","concept":"On the standard normal distribution a z-score expresses how many standard deviations a score lies above or below the mean, and it maps directly onto a percentile via the cumulative normal curve. A z of +1.5 corresponds to roughly the 93rd percentile (about 93.3 percent of the area lies below it). Useful anchors are z = 0 at the 50th, z = +1 at the 84th, and z = +2 at about the 98th percentile.","traps":{"a":"The 99th percentile corresponds to about z = +2.33, well above +1.5.","b":"The 84th percentile corresponds to z = +1.0, not +1.5.","c":"The 50th percentile is z = 0 (the mean), far below a z of +1.5."}},{"q":"T-scores (as used on the MMPI, NEO-PI-R) have a mean and SD of:","concept":"T-scores are a standardised score scale with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, used on instruments such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943), the NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae), and the ASEBA. Converting to a common metric lets scores across scales be compared, with values above 65 (1.5 SD) typically flagged as clinically elevated on the MMPI. This differs from the Wechsler IQ metric (mean 100, SD 15) and the z-score metric (mean 0, SD 1).","traps":{"a":"Mean 100, SD 15 is the deviation IQ scale (Wechsler, Stanford-Binet), not the T-score metric.","c":"Mean 500, SD 100 is the old SAT/GRE scaling, not the T-score.","d":"Mean 0, SD 1 is the z-score, the raw standard-normal metric from which T-scores are derived, not the T-score itself."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2-RF, the RC (Restructured Clinical) scales differ from the original clinical scales in that RC scales:","concept":"On the MMPI-2-RF the Restructured Clinical (RC) scales, developed by Auke Tellegen and colleagues (2003), were built by first extracting the pervasive 'demoralisation' factor into its own scale (RCd) so that the remaining RC scales each capture a more distinctive core dimension of psychopathology with cleaner discriminant validity. This addressed the heavy intercorrelation and construct overlap of the original clinical scales. The RC scales serve as the core of the MMPI-2-RF (2008) and MMPI-3 (2020).","traps":{"a":"The RC scales were not dropped in the MMPI-2-RF; they are its core scales.","b":"The RC scales are not defined by using only true/false items (the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) item format is already true/false); their innovation is removing demoralisation variance.","d":"The RC scales are deliberately not identical to the original clinical scales; they were restructured to reduce overlap and improve discriminant validity."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality on which model?","concept":"The NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, 1992) is a 240-item self-report questionnaire operationalising the Five-Factor Model, or Big Five: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism (OCEAN), with six facets measured under each domain. The five factors recur across languages and cultures, consistent with the lexical hypothesis. A 60-item short form, the NEO-FFI, measures the domains without facets.","traps":{"a":"The Cattell 16PF measures 16 primary factors from Cattell's model, not the five-factor structure the NEO-PI-R is built on.","b":"The MBTI's 16-type typology derives from Jungian dichotomies, a categorical model distinct from the continuous Big Five.","d":"Eysenck's PEN model has three dimensions (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism), not the five factors of the NEO-PI-R."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) is most associated with:","concept":"Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) is a factor-analytically derived inventory that reduces the natural-language descriptors of personality to 16 primary 'source' traits (such as Warmth, Reasoning, and Emotional Stability). These primary traits in turn combine into five second-order global factors that broadly approximate the Big Five. Cattell's lexical, factor-analytic approach makes it a landmark trait instrument.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is a general normal-personality measure, not restricted to diagnosing personality disorders.","b":"Q-sort methodology is Stephenson's forced-distribution sorting technique, unrelated to Cattell's factor-based questionnaire.","d":"Projective interpretation applies to instruments like the Rorschach or TAT; the 16PF is an objective, self-report questionnaire."}},{"q":"Generalizability theory (G theory, Cronbach et al. 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by:","concept":"Generalizability theory (Lee Cronbach and colleagues, 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by decomposing observed-score variance into multiple 'facets' of measurement, such as raters, items, and occasions, using analysis of variance to estimate each source of error separately. Rather than lumping everything into one undifferentiated error term, a G-study quantifies variance components and a follow-up D-study projects how reliability would change under different administration designs. It complements but does not replace Item Response Theory.","traps":{"b":"Treating measurement error as a single undifferentiated term is precisely the Classical Test Theory limitation that G theory was created to overcome.","c":"G theory does not eliminate the need for reliability estimation; it provides a richer, multi-faceted way of estimating it.","d":"Modelling item response curves as a function of latent ability is the province of Item Response Theory, not generalizability theory."}},{"q":"The Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) in IRT plots:","concept":"In Item Response Theory the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) plots the probability of a correct (or keyed) response on the y-axis against the examinee's latent ability, theta, on the x-axis, producing an S-shaped logistic curve. Item parameters shape it: b (difficulty) locates the curve along the theta axis, a (discrimination) sets its steepness, and c (guessing) sets the lower asymptote. The 1PL/Rasch model uses only b, the 2PL adds a, and the 3PL adds c.","traps":{"b":"Validity coefficients across studies are the material of validity generalisation or meta-analysis, not what an ICC plots.","c":"Internal consistency by item is a reliability concern (item-total statistics), not the ability-versus-probability relationship an ICC displays.","d":"Test reliability against sample size is a different relationship entirely; the ICC's x-axis is latent ability, not sample size."}},{"q":"Test-retest reliability is most appropriate to estimate when:","concept":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by administering the same test twice and correlating the scores, so it is only appropriate when the construct being measured is expected to remain stable across the retest interval, as with intelligence or trait personality. For state-like constructs such as current mood or anxiety, genuine change over time would lower the correlation and be misread as unreliability. Short intervals also risk memory carryover, while long ones confound true change with error.","traps":{"a":"A current mood state is exactly the kind of unstable construct for which test-retest reliability is inappropriate, since real change would depress the correlation.","b":"Having no opportunity to administer twice makes test-retest impossible to compute, not appropriate; it points toward alternative reliability methods.","c":"Having only one rater speaks to whether inter-rater reliability applies, not to when test-retest reliability is appropriate."}},{"q":"The Standard Error of Measurement (SEM = SD × √(1 − r)) for a test with SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91 is approximately:","concept":"The Standard Error of Measurement quantifies the expected spread of an individual's observed scores around their true score, computed as SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus reliability). With SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91, SEM = 15 times the square root of 0.09 = 15 times 0.3 = 4.5. Higher reliability and lower SD both shrink the SEM, tightening the confidence interval around a score.","traps":{"a":"15.0 would result only if reliability were 0 (all variance error); with reliability 0.91 the SEM is far smaller.","b":"10.0 does not follow from the formula; the square root of 0.09 is 0.3, giving 4.5, not a value near two-thirds of the SD.","c":"1.5 underestimates the SEM; it would require a reliability of 0.99, not 0.91."}},{"q":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots:","concept":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots sensitivity (the true positive rate) on the y-axis against 1 minus specificity (the false positive rate) on the x-axis across every possible cut-off value of a test. The Area Under the Curve (AUC) summarises overall discrimination as the probability that a randomly chosen positive case scores higher than a randomly chosen negative, with 0.5 meaning chance and values above 0.9 outstanding. ROC curves guide selection of clinical cut-off scores.","traps":{"a":"Predictive validity plotted against reliability is not what ROC displays; ROC plots true positive against false positive rates.","c":"Item difficulty versus discrimination is an item-analysis plot from IRT or classical analysis, not an ROC curve.","d":"Score versus age describes a developmental norming or growth curve, unrelated to the sensitivity-specificity trade-off of ROC."}},{"q":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are best classified as:","concept":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are all structured or semi-structured interview and observation instruments used in developmental and psychiatric assessment rather than self-report questionnaires or lab paradigms. The Vineland-3 is a caregiver interview for adaptive behaviour, the K-SADS is a semi-structured interview for childhood psychiatric disorders, the ADI-R is a caregiver autism interview, and the ADOS-2 is a direct interaction-based autism observation, with ADI-R plus ADOS-2 forming the gold-standard autism diagnostic combination.","traps":{"a":"EEG paradigms are electrophysiological recordings; none of these four instruments involves brain-wave measurement.","b":"Personality projective techniques (like the Rorschach) present ambiguous stimuli for interpretation, unlike these structured diagnostic interviews and observations.","c":"Self-report cognitive tests are completed directly by the examinee; these instruments instead rely on caregiver interview or clinician observation of the child."}}],"nimhans-mock-1":[{"q":"In a cohort study investigating the association between a new psychological intervention and relapse rates in schizophrenia, the risk ratio is calculated as 1.0. This finding indicates that:","concept":"The risk ratio (relative risk) divides the incidence of the outcome in the exposed group by the incidence in the unexposed group. When RR equals exactly 1.0, the two incidences are identical, meaning the exposure (the intervention) is not associated with any change in relapse risk. By convention RR greater than 1 indicates increased risk (harm) and RR less than 1 indicates reduced risk (protection).","traps":{"a":"An RR of 2.0, not 1.0, would mean the exposed group's incidence is double that of the unexposed group.","b":"A significant protective effect would require RR below 1.0 with a confidence interval that does not cross 1, which is not the case here.","d":"This describes the interpretation of an odds ratio of 1.0 framed by exposure odds, whereas the question specifically asks about a risk ratio."}},{"q":"A researcher conducts a systematic review on the efficacy of CBT for insomnia. To ensure transparency and replicability in the methodology, the researcher should adhere to which of the following guidelines?","concept":"PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) is the reporting guideline designed specifically to make systematic reviews transparent and replicable, covering search strategy, study selection, and the flow of records. Each major study design has its own reporting standard, so matching the guideline to the design is the key exam skill here.","traps":{"a":"The CONSORT Statement is the reporting standard for randomized controlled trials, not for systematic reviews.","c":"The STROBE checklist governs the reporting of observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional).","d":"CARE guidelines are for writing up individual case reports, not synthesized reviews of many studies."}},{"q":"To investigate a rare psychological outcome (e.g., a specific dissociative disorder) and its potential past exposures (e.g., childhood trauma), which of the following epidemiological study designs would be the MOST feasible and appropriate?","concept":"A case-control study starts by identifying people who already have the outcome (cases) and comparable people without it (controls), then looks backward at prior exposures. Because it recruits on the basis of the outcome, it is the most efficient and feasible design for rare outcomes, where waiting for cases to appear in a cohort would be impractical.","traps":{"a":"A prospective cohort would require following an enormous sample forward for years to accumulate enough cases of a rare disorder, making it impractical.","b":"A cross-sectional study measures exposure and outcome at a single time point, so it cannot establish temporal order and captures too few rare cases.","d":"A randomized controlled trial deliberately assigns an intervention and cannot ethically or practically assign a harmful past exposure like childhood trauma."}},{"q":"A researcher randomly selects 5 districts from each state, then 10 schools per district, then 30 students per school. This sampling technique is known as:","concept":"Multi-stage cluster sampling proceeds through a hierarchy of naturally occurring groups (clusters), selecting randomly at each level: first districts, then schools within chosen districts, then students within chosen schools. It is used when a full sampling frame of individuals is unavailable but the population is organized into nested units.","traps":{"a":"Stratified random sampling divides the whole population into strata and samples from every stratum, rather than randomly discarding entire clusters at each stage.","b":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills preset category targets by convenience, with no random selection at any level.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately handpicks participants judged to be informative, unlike the random multi-level selection described here."}},{"q":"Which sampling method relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are 'typical' or 'representative,' and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?","concept":"Quota sampling is a non-probability technique in which the researcher uses personal judgment to select participants believed to be typical or representative until preset category quotas are filled. Because selection is not random and each unit's probability of inclusion is unknown, sampling error cannot be estimated.","traps":{"a":"Simple random sampling gives every individual a known equal chance of selection, so sampling error is calculable.","b":"Stratified random sampling is a probability method that randomly samples within defined strata and permits sampling error estimation.","d":"Cluster sampling randomly selects intact groups and is a probability method, allowing sampling error to be quantified."}},{"q":"On a Scree plot, eigenvalues drop sharply from 3.5 to 1.8, then plateau at 0.9, 0.7, 0.5. By the Kaiser-Guttman rule (eigenvalue > 1) and the elbow, how many factors should be retained?","concept":"The Kaiser-Guttman rule retains only factors whose eigenvalue exceeds 1.0, because such factors explain more variance than a single standardized variable. Here only the eigenvalues 3.5 and 1.8 exceed 1.0, so two factors are retained, and the scree plot's elbow (the point where the curve flattens after 1.8) agrees.","traps":{"a":"Retaining all five would ignore the eigenvalue-greater-than-1 criterion, keeping factors (0.9, 0.7, 0.5) that explain less variance than one original variable.","b":"Retaining three would incorrectly include the 0.9 eigenvalue, which falls below the Kaiser threshold of 1.0.","d":"Retaining only one drops the 1.8 eigenvalue, which clearly exceeds 1.0 and lies above the scree elbow."}},{"q":"When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the standard non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, comparing two unrelated groups by ranking all observations rather than assuming normality. It is chosen whenever two independent samples must be compared and parametric assumptions (normality, interval data) are not met.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two related or paired samples, not independent groups.","c":"The Kruskal-Wallis test extends the logic to three or more independent groups, more than the two specified here.","d":"The Friedman test handles three or more repeated (within-subjects) measures, not two independent samples."}},{"q":"Cohen's Kappa is a statistical measure used to assess which of the following?","concept":"Cohen's Kappa quantifies the agreement between two raters classifying items into categories, correcting for the agreement that would be expected purely by chance. It is the standard index of inter-rater reliability for categorical judgments.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating the same measure across two time points, typically with Pearson's r, not Kappa.","b":"Internal consistency (how well items on a scale hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha.","d":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts an external standard, which is a validity question, not rater agreement."}},{"q":"When two predictors in a regression analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:","concept":"Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables in a regression are highly correlated with each other, which inflates the standard errors of the coefficients and makes the individual effects unstable and hard to interpret. It is diagnosed with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF above 10) or tolerance below 0.1.","traps":{"a":"Heteroscedasticity refers to non-constant variance of the residuals across levels of the predictor, not correlation between predictors.","c":"Autocorrelation is the correlation of residuals with each other across observations (common in time-series data), not correlation among predictors.","d":"Homogeneity of variance is an assumption of equal variances across groups, relevant to ANOVA and t-tests, not a predictor-correlation problem."}},{"q":"In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?","concept":"A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true, that is, a false positive; its probability is the significance level alpha. This contrasts with a Type II error, which is failing to reject a false null hypothesis (a false negative).","traps":{"b":"A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, the opposite mistake to the one described.","c":"Sampling error is the natural variability between a sample statistic and the true population parameter, not a hypothesis-testing decision error.","d":"Standard error is a measure of the precision of a sample estimate, not a category of decision error."}},{"q":"Which statistical measure tells how far individual data points are dispersed from the mean expressed in standard deviation units?","concept":"A Z-score (standard score) expresses how far a raw score lies from the mean in units of standard deviation, calculated as (X minus the mean) divided by the standard deviation. Because it is scaled in standard deviation units, it allows scores from different distributions to be compared directly.","traps":{"a":"Variance is the average of squared deviations from the mean and is expressed in squared units, not standard deviation units per data point.","c":"The coefficient of variation is the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean, used to compare relative dispersion across datasets, not to locate a single point.","d":"Standard error describes the variability of a sample statistic (like the mean) across repeated samples, not the position of an individual score."}},{"q":"When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?","concept":"When data are normally distributed and the goal is to compare the means of two independent (unrelated) groups, the independent-samples t-test is the appropriate parametric test. If the assumption of equal variances is violated, Welch's t-test is used instead.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative, used when normality cannot be assumed, so it is unnecessary here.","c":"The Chi-square test compares frequencies of categorical variables, not means of continuous data.","d":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is non-parametric and for paired data, not independent groups under normality."}},{"q":"Interaction effects between two or more independent variables can be detected and analyzed using which statistical technique?","concept":"Factorial ANOVA involves two or more independent variables (factors) and can test not only their separate main effects but also their interaction, that is, whether the effect of one IV on the dependent variable depends on the level of another. Detecting interaction effects requires this factorial structure.","traps":{"a":"Correlation analysis measures the strength and direction of a linear association between variables and cannot partition main effects from interactions.","b":"The independent-samples t-test handles only one independent variable with two levels, so it cannot test interactions.","d":"The Chi-square test examines associations between categorical variables and does not model interaction effects on a continuous outcome."}},{"q":"If a researcher wants to subtract out the influence of number of children from the relationship between marital stress and length of marriage, which statistical method is most appropriate?","concept":"Partial correlation measures the association between two variables (marital stress and length of marriage) while statistically holding constant, or subtracting out, the influence of a third variable (number of children). This isolates the direct relationship after removing the confound.","traps":{"a":"Biserial correlation estimates the relationship between a continuous variable and an artificially dichotomized one, not the control of a confounder.","b":"Multiple correlation relates one dependent variable to several predictors jointly, rather than removing the effect of a single control variable.","c":"Linear correlation simply measures the bivariate association without controlling for any third variable."}},{"q":"A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom (df) for a 3 × 4 contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:","concept":"For a chi-square test on a contingency table, degrees of freedom equal (number of rows minus 1) multiplied by (number of columns minus 1). For a 3 by 4 table this is 2 multiplied by 3, giving 6.","traps":{"a":"12 is simply the total number of cells (3 times 4), not the degrees of freedom.","b":"7 does not follow the (r minus 1)(c minus 1) formula and likely comes from adding rather than the correct calculation.","d":"11 is the total number of cells minus 1, which is the wrong rule for a two-way contingency table."}},{"q":"In Phenylketonuria (PKU), the body fails to convert phenylalanine into which substance due to a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase?","concept":"Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive disorder in which a deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase prevents the conversion of phenylalanine into tyrosine. Phenylalanine therefore accumulates to toxic levels while tyrosine falls, and untreated cases develop intellectual disability, which newborn screening and a low-phenylalanine diet prevent.","traps":{"a":"Urea is the nitrogen waste product of the urea cycle and is unrelated to the phenylalanine hydroxylase reaction.","c":"Tryptophan is a separate essential amino acid and the precursor of serotonin, not the product blocked in PKU.","d":"Histidine is another amino acid and is not the substance that phenylalanine is converted into."}},{"q":"What is the alternative name for adrenaline, the hormone released by the adrenal medulla during the fight-or-flight response?","concept":"Adrenaline and epinephrine are two names for the same catecholamine hormone released by the adrenal medulla during sympathetic fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) activation. Epinephrine is the term standardized in medical and pharmacological usage.","traps":{"a":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a closely related but distinct catecholamine and is not simply the alternative name for adrenaline.","c":"Dopamine is a precursor catecholamine and neurotransmitter, not another name for adrenaline.","d":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid released by the adrenal cortex, not the medulla, and is chemically unrelated to adrenaline."}},{"q":"Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the anterior pituitary gland?","concept":"The anterior pituitary secretes growth hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, thyroid stimulating hormone, follicle stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and prolactin. Melatonin is not among them; it is produced by the pineal gland to regulate the sleep-wake cycle.","traps":{"a":"Growth hormone is a genuine anterior pituitary hormone, so it is not the exception.","b":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary to drive the adrenal cortex, so it is not the exception.","d":"Thyroid stimulating hormone is an anterior pituitary product acting on the thyroid, so it is not the exception."}},{"q":"Which brain structure serves as the primary control center for integrating the endocrine, visceral, and autonomic nervous systems?","concept":"The hypothalamus is the master integrating center that links the nervous and endocrine systems, controlling the pituitary gland and governing homeostatic functions such as temperature, hunger, thirst, autonomic outflow, and the sleep-wake cycle. This coordinating role over endocrine, visceral, and autonomic systems makes it the answer.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not endocrine and autonomic integration.","c":"The thalamus is chiefly a sensory relay station directing signals to the cortex, not the endocrine control center.","d":"The brainstem controls vital reflexes like breathing and heart rate but does not serve as the master endocrine-autonomic integrator."}},{"q":"In neuroimaging, which technique directly measures the electrical activity of the brain with high temporal resolution, akin to ECG in cardiology?","concept":"Electroencephalography (EEG) records the summed electrical activity of cortical neurons through scalp electrodes with millisecond temporal resolution, directly analogous to how the ECG records the heart's electrical activity. Its strength is temporal precision, though its spatial resolution is poor.","traps":{"a":"fMRI measures the blood-oxygen-level-dependent signal indirectly and offers high spatial but poor temporal resolution, and it is not electrical.","b":"PET tracks metabolic or receptor activity using radioactive tracers, not direct electrical activity, and has slow temporal resolution.","d":"MEG measures the magnetic fields generated by neural currents rather than the electrical potentials themselves, and is far less commonly used."}},{"q":"The lateral ventricles, the largest of the ventricles in the brain, are filled with:","concept":"All four ventricles of the brain, including the large paired lateral ventricles, are filled with cerebrospinal fluid produced by the choroid plexus. CSF cushions the brain, removes waste, and supports nutrient exchange, flowing from the lateral ventricles to the third and fourth ventricles.","traps":{"a":"Blood circulates in vessels and sinuses, not within the ventricular cavities.","c":"There is no fluid called 'synaptic fluid'; the ventricles contain CSF.","d":"Myelin is the lipid sheath insulating axons, not a fluid that fills ventricles."}},{"q":"Patient H.M. underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes (incl. hippocampus) for epilepsy. His most profound deficit was in:","concept":"After bilateral removal of his medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, patient Henry Molaison (H.M.) was unable to form new declarative (conscious) memories, a condition called anterograde amnesia. This case established the hippocampus as essential for consolidating new long-term memories, while sparing working memory and most older memories.","traps":{"a":"H.M.'s procedural (skill) memory was preserved, as shown by his improvement on motor tasks, so it was not his profound deficit.","b":"Semantic memory acquired before surgery remained largely intact; his core problem was forming new memories, not accessing old facts.","d":"His retrograde amnesia was comparatively limited; the hallmark and most profound deficit was the anterograde loss."}},{"q":"Despite his severe amnesia, H.M. learned and improved on a mirror-tracing task across days, with no conscious recollection of practising it. This demonstrates the preservation of:","concept":"Despite being unable to consciously recall practising, H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957) steadily improved on the mirror-tracing task, demonstrating intact procedural (skill) memory. Procedural memory depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum rather than the hippocampus, and this dissociation is key evidence for separate memory systems.","traps":{"a":"Episodic memory (recollection of specific events) was severely impaired in H.M., as he could not remember the practice sessions.","b":"Declarative memory, the conscious memory of facts and events, was exactly the system he had lost.","d":"Semantic memory, general knowledge of facts, is a declarative system and was not what the mirror-tracing task tapped."}},{"q":"Loss of memories for events that happened BEFORE a brain injury, such as a concussion or stroke, is termed:","concept":"Retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories for events that occurred before the brain injury, whereas anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new memories after it. According to Ribot's law, more recent memories are typically lost first while remote memories are relatively spared.","traps":{"a":"Anterograde amnesia is the inability to lay down new memories after the injury, the opposite temporal direction to what is described.","c":"Source amnesia is forgetting where or how information was learned while retaining the information itself, not a time-based loss around an injury.","d":"Dissociative amnesia is a psychogenic memory loss for personal information triggered by trauma or stress, not caused by a physical brain injury."}},{"q":"The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:","concept":"Wernicke's area, in the left superior temporal gyrus, is responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language. Damage produces fluent but meaningless speech (word salad) with impaired understanding, and its connection to Broca's area via the arcuate fasciculus supports the flow from comprehension to production.","traps":{"a":"Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus governs speech production, not comprehension.","c":"The primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus) detects and processes sound, but does not extract linguistic meaning.","d":"The angular gyrus contributes to reading, writing, and integrating information, but it is not the primary language-comprehension center."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's aphasia is an expressive (non-fluent) aphasia resulting from damage to Broca's area, marked by effortful, telegraphic, agrammatic speech while comprehension remains relatively intact. Because they understand their errors, patients are typically aware and frustrated.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia, not Broca's aphasia.","c":"Anomia (word-finding or naming difficulty) is a broader symptom seen across many aphasias and is not the defining feature of Broca's aphasia.","d":"Prosopagnosia, the inability to recognize faces, results from damage to the fusiform face area, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The reticular activating system (RAS), located in the brainstem, is crucial for the regulation of:","concept":"The ascending reticular activating system (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949) in the brainstem regulates cortical arousal, wakefulness, and attention, and controls transitions between sleep and waking. Severe damage to it can produce coma, underscoring its role in maintaining consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Fine motor coordination is a function of the cerebellum and basal ganglia, not the reticular activating system.","c":"Emotional memory is associated with the amygdala and hippocampus, not the RAS.","d":"Balance and posture are governed by the cerebellum and vestibular system, not the arousal-regulating RAS."}},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia on long-term antipsychotics presents with repetitive, involuntary lip-smacking, tongue movements, and grimacing. This is most likely:","concept":"Tardive dyskinesia is a late-onset, often irreversible movement disorder caused by chronic dopamine D2 receptor blockade from long-term antipsychotic use, presenting with repetitive involuntary oro-facial movements such as lip-smacking, tongue protrusion, and grimacing. Risk is higher with typical (first-generation) antipsychotics, and it is monitored using the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale.","traps":{"a":"Akathisia is a subjective inner restlessness with an urge to move, not the repetitive oro-facial dyskinesia described.","b":"Acute dystonia is a sudden, early sustained muscle contraction (such as torticollis or oculogyric crisis) occurring soon after starting medication, not a late-onset movement.","d":"Drug-induced parkinsonism presents with tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia, not the choreoathetoid oro-facial movements of tardive dyskinesia."}},{"q":"What is the primary function of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve axons?","concept":"The myelin sheath insulates axons and enables saltatory conduction, in which the action potential jumps between the Nodes of Ranvier, dramatically increasing the speed of nerve impulse conduction (roughly 10 to 100 times). Demyelinating diseases such as multiple sclerosis slow this transmission and cause neurological deficits.","traps":{"a":"Structural support to neurons is provided mainly by glial cells and the cytoskeleton, not the primary role of myelin.","c":"Neurotransmitters are stored in synaptic vesicles at axon terminals, not within the myelin sheath.","d":"Action potentials are generated at the axon hillock and Nodes of Ranvier by ion channels; myelin speeds conduction rather than generating the impulse."}},{"q":"A patient presents with severe memory deficits, confabulation, and nystagmus, with a history of chronic alcohol use. This clinical picture is classic for:","concept":"The triad of severe anterograde memory deficits, confabulation, and eye signs such as nystagmus in a chronic alcohol user is classic for Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Wernicke's encephalopathy presents acutely with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia, and if untreated progresses to the amnestic, confabulating Korsakoff psychosis; treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a gradual degenerative dementia of older age without the nystagmus or alcohol-linked thiamine deficiency seen here.","c":"Vascular dementia produces stepwise cognitive decline linked to cerebrovascular events, not the confabulation-plus-nystagmus picture of thiamine deficiency.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia presents with early personality change and language decline, not the acute ocular signs and confabulation described."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Folie à deux,' or Shared Psychotic Disorder, is characterized by:","concept":"Folie a deux (Shared Psychotic Disorder) occurs when a dominant, psychotic 'inducer' transmits a fixed delusional belief to a passive, closely associated 'recipient' who normally would not be psychotic. The relationship (often shared isolation and dependency) is the vehicle: separating the pair frequently resolves the recipient's symptoms. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition removed it as a standalone entity (folded under delusional disorder / other specified psychotic disorder), but the term remains clinically useful.","traps":{"a":"Two independent simultaneous psychoses describes coincident illness, not the defining transmission of one person's delusion to another within a relationship.","c":"A delusion shared by a whole cultural group is closer to a culturally sanctioned belief or mass phenomenon, not the dyadic inducer-recipient pattern of folie a deux.","d":"A brief stress-triggered psychosis in two people describes shared acute stress reactions, missing the core mechanism of belief transmission from a dominant inducer."}},{"q":"The specific phobia of pain is known as:","concept":"The specific phobia of pain is algophobia, from the Greek 'algos' meaning pain. Specific phobias are named by their Greek or Latin root plus '-phobia', so knowing the root reliably identifies the fear. Algophobia is a marked, persistent fear of experiencing or anticipating physical pain.","traps":{"a":"Nosophobia is the fear of having or developing a specific disease (from 'nosos', disease), not the fear of pain.","c":"Cynophobia is the fear of dogs (from 'kyon/kynos', dog), an animal-type specific phobia.","d":"Hematophobia is the fear of blood (from 'haima', blood), part of the blood-injection-injury phobia cluster."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent admiration-seeking but presents with excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This is most characteristic of:","concept":"Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) is defined by pervasive excessive emotionality and attention-seeking: theatrical, seductive or provocative behavior, shallow and rapidly shifting affect, suggestibility, and discomfort when not the center of attention. The key discriminator here is that the person seeks admiration through dramatic emotional display rather than through beliefs of superiority. That absence of grandiosity and entitlement rules out narcissism.","traps":{"a":"Narcissistic personality disorder also craves admiration but does so through grandiosity, entitlement, and a need for status, not theatrical seductiveness and shallow emotionality.","c":"Borderline personality disorder centers on identity disturbance, abandonment fears, affective instability, and impulsivity, not primarily attention-seeking through seductive display.","d":"Dependent personality disorder involves clinging, submissive behavior and needing others to take responsibility, not dramatic attention-seeking."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent and frightening experiences of their surroundings seeming unreal, distorted, or dreamlike. This symptom is best described as:","concept":"Derealization is a dissociative experience in which the external world feels unreal, distorted, dreamlike, foggy, or lifeless, though the person retains intact reality testing (they know it only feels that way). It is distinguished from depersonalization, which targets the self rather than the surroundings. Both can co-occur in depersonalization/derealization disorder, panic attacks, and post-traumatic stress disorder.","traps":{"b":"Depersonalization is feeling detached from or unreal within one's own self, thoughts, or body, whereas the described symptom concerns the surroundings.","c":"Deja vu is a fleeting sense that a novel situation has been experienced before, not a persistent dreamlike unreality of the environment.","d":"A hallucination is a false sensory perception without an external stimulus; here perception is accurate but feels unreal, so reality testing is preserved."}},{"q":"A patient presents with a fixed delusional belief that their spouse is being unfaithful, despite no evidence. This specific form of delusional jealousy is known as:","concept":"Othello syndrome (morbid or delusional jealousy) is a fixed, false belief that one's partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite absent or contradictory evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's jealous Othello and can drive dangerous stalking or violence. It is classified among the delusional misidentification and content-specific delusional syndromes.","traps":{"a":"Capgras syndrome is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor, not a belief about infidelity.","c":"Fregoli syndrome is the delusion that different strangers are actually one persecutor in disguise, a misidentification of identity rather than jealousy.","d":"De Clerambault's syndrome (erotomania) is the delusion that another person, often of higher status, is secretly in love with the patient, the opposite theme to jealous suspicion."}},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with mild cognitive impairment insists that his wife of 40 years is an identical 'duplicate' impostor, despite acknowledging she looks exactly like her. Most likely diagnosis:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the delusional belief that a familiar person (here the wife) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor or 'duplicate', even while the patient admits the resemblance is perfect. It is thought to arise from a disconnection between intact face recognition (ventral visual stream) and the emotional response that normally accompanies recognizing a loved one, so the face looks right but 'feels' wrong. It commonly appears in dementia, schizophrenia, and other neurological conditions.","traps":{"a":"Fregoli syndrome is the reverse error: believing strangers are one familiar person in disguise, not that a familiar person is an impostor.","c":"Cotard syndrome is a nihilistic delusion of being dead or nonexistent, unrelated to misidentifying another person.","d":"Reduplicative paramnesia is the delusion that a place has been duplicated or relocated, concerning locations rather than identifying a person as an impostor."}},{"q":"Cotard syndrome, a rare neuropsychiatric condition, is primarily characterized by which type of delusional belief?","concept":"Cotard syndrome (walking corpse or nihilistic delusion) is characterized by the fixed belief that one is dead, does not exist, is decaying, or has lost internal organs or one's soul. It reflects an extreme nihilistic delusional content and is most often seen in severe psychotic depression, but also in schizophrenia and neurodegenerative disease. The defining feature is denial of one's own existence or life.","traps":{"a":"Grandiose delusions of special powers are the opposite in content and typify mania or grandiose-type delusional disorder, not Cotard.","b":"Persecutory delusions of being followed define paranoid presentations, not the nihilistic self-denial of Cotard.","d":"Somatic delusions of harboring a serious illness point to illness-themed delusional disorder, distinct from the belief that one is already dead or nonexistent."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with sudden right-arm paralysis after an argument; neuro exam is normal and she appears strikingly unconcerned ('It's not really bothering me'). This presentation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) presents with neurological symptoms such as sudden paralysis, blindness, or seizures that are incompatible with recognized neurological disease and are often precipitated by psychological stress (here an argument). A classic accompanying sign is la belle indifference, a striking lack of concern about a dramatic deficit. The symptoms are not consciously produced, distinguishing it from feigning.","traps":{"b":"Borderline personality disorder involves affective instability and interpersonal chaos, not the sudden loss of a neurological function with normal exam findings.","c":"Major depressive disorder is a mood syndrome; it would not produce isolated limb paralysis with intact neurology and indifferent affect.","d":"Factitious disorder involves deliberate fabrication or induction of symptoms to assume the sick role, whereas conversion symptoms are unconsciously produced."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman exhibits distinct personalities with their own names, memories, and behaviour, with amnesia between episodes; she finds clothes she does not remember buying. Most consistent diagnosis:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder is defined by two or more distinct identity states (each with its own name, memories, and behavior) accompanied by recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory that exceed ordinary forgetting, such as finding purchases one does not remember making. It is strongly linked to severe, chronic early childhood trauma. The presence of multiple identities plus inter-identity amnesia is the diagnostic hallmark.","traps":{"a":"Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information without distinct alternate identities, so it cannot explain the separate named personalities.","c":"Depersonalization/derealization disorder involves feelings of unreality of self or surroundings without amnesia or distinct identity states.","d":"Dissociative fugue involves sudden purposeful travel with amnesia for one's past, a single episode rather than recurring distinct identities."}},{"q":"Persistent preoccupation with perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable to others is characteristic of which disorder?","concept":"Body dysmorphic disorder is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are unobservable or appear only slight to others, coupled with repetitive behaviors such as mirror checking, grooming, or reassurance seeking. It sits within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition and carries a notably high suicide risk. The focus on appearance flaws distinguishes it from illness- or symptom-focused disorders.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom disorder centers on distressing physical symptoms and excessive health-related thoughts and behaviors, not on perceived appearance defects.","c":"Illness anxiety disorder is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, not with a flaw in how one looks.","d":"Conversion disorder involves neurological-type functional deficits, unrelated to preoccupation with appearance."}},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of 'ego-dystonic' as it applies to obsessions in OCD?","concept":"Ego-dystonic means an experience is felt as alien, intrusive, unwanted, and inconsistent with one's sense of self, which is exactly how obsessions in obsessive-compulsive disorder are experienced: the person recognizes the thoughts as their own but resists and is distressed by them. This contrasts with ego-syntonic experiences, which feel congruent with the self (as in many personality disorder traits). The distress and rejection of the thought define ego-dystonia.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing obsessions as a core part of identity describes ego-syntonic content, the opposite of the ego-dystonic quality of OCD obsessions.","c":"Deriving pleasure from the thoughts contradicts the distressing, unwanted nature of obsessions.","d":"Being completely unaware that the obsessions are unreasonable describes poor or absent insight, which is a separate specifier, not the meaning of ego-dystonic."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Dementia praecox,' which translates to 'premature dementia,' is historically most directly associated with which contemporary diagnostic category?","concept":"Dementia praecox ('premature dementia') is the historical forerunner of schizophrenia. Emil Kraepelin coined the term to describe an early-onset, deteriorating psychotic illness, and Eugen Bleuler later renamed it schizophrenia in 1908, emphasizing the splitting of psychic functions and his four A's (loosening of associations, affect disturbance, ambivalence, autism). The term maps onto the modern category of schizophrenia, not a dementia.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a neurodegenerative dementia of late life; despite the word 'dementia' in the old term, dementia praecox is a psychotic, not neurodegenerative, illness.","b":"Bipolar I disorder derives historically from Kraepelin's separate category of manic-depressive insanity, which he explicitly distinguished from dementia praecox.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia is a genuine neurodegenerative dementia and is unrelated to the historical concept of dementia praecox."}},{"q":"According to the ICD-10, what is the minimum duration of symptoms required for a definitive diagnosis of Schizophrenia?","concept":"Under the International Classification of Diseases, Tenth Revision, a definitive diagnosis of schizophrenia requires the characteristic symptoms to be present for most of the time during at least one month. This one-month threshold is a common exam contrast with the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, which requires at least six months of total disturbance including at least one month of active-phase symptoms. The question specifies ICD-10, so the answer is one month.","traps":{"a":"One week corresponds roughly to brief psychotic disorder or acute presentations, not the ICD-10 threshold for schizophrenia.","c":"Three months matches ICD durations for some other conditions but is not the schizophrenia requirement.","d":"Six months is the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition criterion, not the ICD-10 one that the question asks about."}},{"q":"Which of the following is considered a negative symptom of schizophrenia, characterized by reduced quantity and quality of verbal expression?","concept":"Alogia (poverty of speech) is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, reflecting a reduction in the quantity and content of spontaneous verbal output. Negative symptoms represent a loss or diminution of normal function and are often summarized as the five A's: alogia, anhedonia, avolition, affective flattening, and asociality. This contrasts with positive symptoms, which are additions to normal experience.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom, an added abnormal perception rather than a reduction of function.","c":"Persecutory delusions are positive symptoms, representing added false beliefs.","d":"Disorganized behavior is generally classed among positive or disorganization symptoms, not a negative symptom of reduced speech."}},{"q":"A patient with Korsakoff's psychosis fabricates detailed and believable stories about their past to fill memory gaps, without intent to deceive. This is:","concept":"Confabulation is the production of false or distorted memories without any conscious intent to deceive, typically to fill gaps left by amnesia; the patient genuinely believes the fabricated account. It is a hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome and is also seen with frontal lobe damage and anterior communicating artery aneurysm. The absence of deceptive intent distinguishes it from lying and malingering.","traps":{"b":"Confusion is a global disturbance of clear thinking and orientation, not the specific filling of memory gaps with fabricated stories.","c":"Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, a broader state rather than the specific memory phenomenon described.","d":"Malingering is the deliberate fabrication of symptoms for external gain, which requires the conscious intent to deceive that confabulation lacks."}},{"q":"According to Thomas and Chess's temperament model, a child who is generally negative in mood, slow to adapt, and has irregular biological rhythms would be classified as:","concept":"In Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess's New York Longitudinal Study temperament model, the 'difficult' child is characterized by predominantly negative mood, slow adaptability to change, withdrawal from new stimuli, high intensity of reactions, and irregular biological rhythms (sleep, feeding). Their framework also describes 'easy' and 'slow-to-warm-up' patterns, with 'goodness of fit' between temperament and environment predicting adjustment. The cluster of negativity, poor adaptability, and irregular rhythms defines the difficult temperament.","traps":{"a":"The easy temperament shows positive mood, regular rhythms, and ready adaptability, the opposite of the described profile.","c":"The slow-to-warm-up child is mild and initially withdrawn but has more regular rhythms and adapts gradually, lacking the pervasive negativity and irregularity of the difficult type.","d":"Insecure attachment is a relational classification from attachment theory (John Bowlby), not a temperament category in the Thomas and Chess model."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Marshmallow Test,' pioneered by Walter Mischel, primarily measures a child's capacity for:","concept":"Walter Mischel's marshmallow test presented preschoolers with an immediate small reward and the option to wait alone for a larger one, measuring the capacity for delay of gratification, that is, forgoing an immediate reward for a greater future one. Longer wait times predicted later self-regulation and achievement outcomes, though later replications with socioeconomic controls found smaller effects. The construct assessed is specifically delay of gratification.","traps":{"a":"Sustained attention concerns maintaining focus over time, which is not what the wait-for-a-bigger-reward paradigm was designed to measure.","b":"Resistance to temptation is a loose description, but the precise psychological construct Mischel named and measured is delay of gratification.","d":"Impulse control in social settings narrows the construct to interpersonal contexts, whereas the task measured general self-imposed delay for a future reward."}},{"q":"A child sees two identical balls of clay; one is rolled into a sausage. When asked if the amount is the same, the child says 'yes.' Per Piaget, this child is most likely in which stage?","concept":"Conservation, the understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape or arrangement, emerges in Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years). A child who correctly says the rolled-out clay still has the same amount has decentered and can mentally reverse the transformation, hallmarks of concrete operations. Preoperational children typically fail this by centering on one dimension such as length.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is dominated by object permanence and motor exploration, far too early for verbal conservation reasoning.","b":"A preoperational child would usually be misled by the change in shape and deny that the amount is conserved, so success here points beyond this stage.","d":"The formal operational stage adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning; conservation of mass is mastered earlier, in concrete operations."}},{"q":"A 5-year-old sorts cards by colour but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:","concept":"Centration is Jean Piaget's term for the preoperational tendency to focus on a single salient feature of a situation while ignoring other relevant dimensions. The child who can sort only by color and cannot flexibly re-sort by suit is fixed on one attribute, unable to shift, which is centration. Overcoming it (decentration) is part of the transition into concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"Egocentrism is the inability to take another person's perspective, not the fixation on one feature of a task.","b":"Animism is attributing life and intentions to inanimate objects, unrelated to sorting inflexibility.","d":"Transductive reasoning is drawing links between specific events without true logical cause, not the focusing-on-one-dimension error described."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial stages, adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 years) centers on the crisis of identity versus role confusion, in which the young person works to form a coherent, integrated sense of self. Successful resolution yields the virtue of fidelity. James Marcia later operationalized this into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement).","traps":{"a":"Industry versus inferiority is the school-age stage (about 6 to 11 years), concerning competence and productivity, not the adolescent identity task.","c":"Intimacy versus isolation is the young-adult stage focused on forming committed relationships, which follows adolescence.","d":"Generativity versus stagnation is the middle-adulthood crisis about contributing to the next generation, much later than adolescence."}},{"q":"A middle-aged adult finds meaning by mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation. According to Erikson, this individual is successfully navigating:","concept":"Mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation is the essence of generativity in Erik Erikson's middle-adulthood stage (about 40 to 65 years), the crisis of generativity versus stagnation. Successful resolution produces the virtue of care, whereas failure leads to self-absorption and stagnation. Guiding and investing in others is the defining marker of generativity.","traps":{"a":"Intimacy versus isolation is the earlier young-adult stage about forming close partnerships, not about mentoring the next generation.","c":"Ego integrity versus despair is the late-life stage of reviewing and accepting one's life, which comes after middle adulthood.","d":"Identity versus role confusion is the adolescent stage of self-definition, far earlier than the described middle-aged mentoring."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary virtue that emerges from successfully resolving 'Trust vs. Mistrust' in infancy is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's first psychosocial stage, trust versus mistrust in infancy, consistent and responsive caregiving lets the infant develop basic trust, and the virtue that emerges is hope. Each Erikson stage yields a specific virtue when resolved well, and hope is the earliest of these. Reliable care teaches the infant that the world can be depended upon.","traps":{"a":"Will is the virtue of the autonomy versus shame and doubt stage in toddlerhood, not of infancy.","b":"Purpose arises from the initiative versus guilt stage in the preschool years.","d":"Competence is the virtue of the industry versus inferiority stage in the school-age years."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, abstract reasoning and hypothetical thinking abilities are primarily developed during which developmental stage?","concept":"Abstract reasoning, hypothetical and hypothetico-deductive thinking, and propositional logic are the defining achievements of Jean Piaget's formal operational stage, beginning around 11 years and into adolescence. At this stage a person can reason about possibilities and abstractions rather than only concrete, present realities. This capacity for 'what if' systematic reasoning is what distinguishes formal from concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage involves early sensory and motor learning and object permanence, with no abstract thought.","b":"The preoperational stage features symbolic play and language but is limited by egocentrism and centration, lacking logical reasoning.","c":"The concrete operational stage allows logical reasoning only about tangible, concrete material, not abstract or hypothetical propositions."}},{"q":"Which parenting style is characterized by emotional support, clear boundaries, and accountability for children's behaviour?","concept":"In Diana Baumrind's typology, the authoritative parenting style combines high warmth and emotional support with clear, firm boundaries and expectations of accountability. It consistently predicts the best child outcomes in competence, self-regulation, and social adjustment. The balance of responsiveness plus reasonable demandingness is its defining signature.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high in control but low in warmth, demanding obedience without emotional support, so it lacks the warmth described.","c":"Permissive parenting is high in warmth but low in control, indulgent and lacking the clear boundaries and accountability described.","d":"Neglectful (uninvolved) parenting is low in both warmth and control, the opposite of the supportive, boundaried style described."}},{"q":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her work on which aspect of developmental psychology?","concept":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her contributions to attachment theory, particularly the Strange Situation procedure, which classified infant-caregiver attachment into secure, insecure-avoidant, and insecure-resistant patterns. Her work operationalized and extended John Bowlby's attachment ideas, and a disorganized category was later added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon. Attachment is the domain that defines her legacy.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive development is associated with Jean Piaget, not Ainsworth.","b":"Moral development is associated with Lawrence Kohlberg (and Jean Piaget's earlier work), not Ainsworth.","d":"Language acquisition is linked to theorists such as Noam Chomsky, not to Ainsworth's attachment research."}},{"q":"A 10-year-old illiterate child from a rural area is brought for intellectual assessment. Which test would be MOST appropriate to minimize cultural and educational bias?","concept":"Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence is the most appropriate choice for an illiterate rural child because its five performance subtests require no reading or writing and it carries Indian norms, minimizing cultural and educational bias. Performance-based, nonverbal assessment avoids penalizing a child for lack of schooling or literacy. This makes it the standard Indian tool for low-literacy assessment.","traps":{"b":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, Fourth Edition, is heavily verbal and Western-normed, disadvantaging an illiterate child from a rural Indian background.","c":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fifth Edition, is also verbally loaded and Western-normed, poorly suited to minimizing cultural and educational bias here.","d":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, though Indian-adapted, contains substantial verbal subtests requiring language competence that an illiterate child may lack."}},{"q":"A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group, then re-administers the same scale two weeks later, correlating the two sets of scores. This establishes:","concept":"Administering the same test to the same group on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores establishes test-retest reliability, an index of the stability or consistency of scores over time (usually a Pearson correlation). It answers whether the measure yields the same result when repeated. The two-week interval and re-administration of the identical scale are the giveaways.","traps":{"a":"Split-half reliability is estimated from a single administration by correlating two halves of the test, a measure of internal consistency, not stability over time.","b":"Inter-rater reliability concerns agreement between different scorers or observers, not repeated administration to the same people.","d":"Parallel forms reliability uses two equivalent versions of the test rather than the same test re-administered."}},{"q":"A clinician wants a client to reveal characteristic ways of thinking and feeling in close relationships by telling stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test is:","concept":"Henry Murray's Thematic Apperception Test presents ambiguous interpersonal pictures and asks the person to tell stories about them; the narratives reveal characteristic needs, drives, conflicts, and relationship themes through projection. It is the projective test of choice when the goal is to elicit interpersonal and motivational content. The storytelling-to-ambiguous-pictures format defines the TAT.","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses inkblots and focuses on perception and form quality rather than eliciting interpersonal narratives.","c":"The Sentence Completion Test uses stems to complete rather than storytelling about pictures, giving less rich narrative material about relationships.","d":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is an objective, self-report questionnaire, not a projective storytelling task."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, a scaled score of 10 on a subtest indicates performance that is:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, individual subtest scaled scores have a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, so a scaled score of 10 is exactly average, at the 50th percentile. This is distinct from the index and full-scale composite scores, which use a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. A score of 10 sits right at the center of the distribution.","traps":{"a":"The 16th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 7 (one standard deviation below the mean), not 10.","c":"The 84th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 13 (one standard deviation above the mean), not 10.","d":"The 2nd percentile corresponds to about two standard deviations below the mean (a scaled score near 4), far below the average score of 10."}},{"q":"In the WAIS-IV, how many CORE subtests are there in the Verbal Comprehension Index and the Perceptual Reasoning Index respectively?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, the Verbal Comprehension Index has three core subtests (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) and the Perceptual Reasoning Index has three core subtests (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), giving three and three. The Working Memory Index and Processing Speed Index each add two more core subtests. So the correct pairing is three verbal comprehension and three perceptual reasoning.","traps":{"a":"Three and four misstates the perceptual reasoning core count, which is three, not four.","c":"Four and three overstates the verbal comprehension core, which has three core subtests, not four.","d":"Two and four is wrong on both indices, understating verbal comprehension and overstating perceptual reasoning."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire) is primarily designed to measure:","concept":"The 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), built by Raymond Cattell, uses factor analysis to reduce the surface language of personality into 16 underlying source traits (for example warmth, reasoning, dominance, vigilance). It is a self-report inventory of normal-range personality, organized hierarchically so the 16 primary traits load onto 5 global factors that broadly map to the Big Five. Its purpose is describing personality structure, not measuring cognitive ability, so the correct answer is that it measures personality traits.","traps":{"a":"Fluid intelligence is Cattell's own construct (reasoning with novel problems independent of learning), but that belongs to his theory of intelligence, not to the 16PF personality inventory.","b":"Crystallized intelligence, also a Cattell concept, refers to acquired knowledge and vocabulary and is assessed by ability tests, not by a personality questionnaire.","d":"General intelligence (Spearman's g-factor) is captured by IQ batteries such as the Wechsler scales, whereas the 16PF deliberately measures traits rather than overall cognitive capacity."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a recognized Rorschach inkblot test scoring system?","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test spawned several competing scoring systems in the twentieth century: Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, and the Rapaport-Schafer approach, later consolidated by John Exner into the Comprehensive System and, more recently, R-PAS. Henry Murray is associated with the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), a different projective built from ambiguous interpersonal pictures, not inkblots. Therefore the Murray system is not a recognized Rorschach scoring system.","traps":{"a":"The Beck system is a genuine early Rorschach scoring approach developed by Samuel Beck, so it is a recognized system, not the exception.","b":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the most widely used empirically grounded Rorschach scoring method, so it does not fit a NOT question.","d":"The Klopfer system is a legitimate Rorschach scoring tradition emphasizing experiential and qualitative aspects, so it is a valid system rather than the odd one out."}},{"q":"The original Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale used which method for calculating IQ scores?","concept":"The original Stanford-Binet, adapted by Lewis Terman from the Binet-Simon scale, expressed intelligence as the Ratio IQ using William Stern's formula: mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. This works in childhood but breaks down in adulthood because chronological age keeps climbing while mental age plateaus, deflating scores. David Wechsler later replaced it with the Deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15), now the standard, so the original method was the Ratio IQ.","traps":{"a":"Deviation IQ with standard scores is Wechsler's later innovation that fixed the ratio method's flaws, not the technique the original Stanford-Binet used.","c":"A percentile ranking system reports relative standing but was not the computational method used to derive the original Stanford-Binet IQ score.","d":"T-score transformation (mean 50, standard deviation 10) is a scaling used on some personality and clinical measures, not the intelligence-quotient formula of the early Stanford-Binet."}},{"q":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is best described as a(n):","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is the prototypical objective personality test: it uses a large fixed set of true/false items, standardized scoring, and built-in validity scales (L, F, K) to detect response distortion. Its scales were derived by empirical (criterion) keying, contrasting clinical groups with normals. Because responses are structured and scored to a fixed key rather than interpreted from ambiguous stimuli, it is objective, not projective.","traps":{"a":"Projective personality tests (Rorschach, TAT) rely on ambiguous stimuli and open interpretation, which is the opposite of the MMPI's fixed items and scoring.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability; the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) measures psychopathology and personality, so this misreads its purpose.","d":"A neuropsychological screening battery assesses brain-behavior functions such as memory and attention, not the self-reported personality and symptom domains the MMPI covers."}},{"q":"A 35-year-old presents with persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, and concentration difficulties for 2 months. Which assessment would be MOST appropriate to further evaluate her symptoms?","concept":"The vignette describes a two-month depressive picture (persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, poor concentration). When you already suspect depression and want to quantify its severity, the most appropriate instrument is a targeted, validated depression measure. The Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), Aaron T. Beck's 21-item self-report, does exactly this, directly grading symptom severity, so it is the best fit.","traps":{"b":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test is a broad projective measure of personality and thought process, far less efficient or specific than a symptom scale for grading a clear depressive presentation.","c":"The MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is a wide-band personality and psychopathology inventory; it is overly broad and time-consuming when the goal is simply to quantify depression severity.","d":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale measures IQ and is irrelevant to evaluating mood symptoms in this case."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic modality was pioneered by Maxwell Jones and is characterized by democratic involvement of staff and patients in decision-making and daily activities?","concept":"The therapeutic community was pioneered by Maxwell Jones at the Henderson Hospital and described in his 1953 work. Its defining features are a flattened hierarchy in which staff and patients share decision-making, plus the principles of communalism, permissiveness, democratization, and reality confrontation (later summarized by Rapoport). Because the question stresses democratic involvement of staff and patients, the answer is the therapeutic community.","traps":{"a":"Encounter groups grew from the humanistic and Carl Rogers tradition, focused on intense interpersonal experience and personal growth, not on Jones's democratic ward structure.","c":"Group analysis is S. H. Foulkes's psychoanalytically informed group method, not a whole-ward democratic community model.","d":"Milieu therapy uses the treatment environment therapeutically and overlaps conceptually, but it is a broader later concept, whereas the specific model Maxwell Jones founded is the therapeutic community."}},{"q":"A client with a phobia of dogs is asked to relax and then imagine a graded series of fear-provoking interactions with a dog. This technique is called:","concept":"Pairing relaxation with a graded, imagined hierarchy of feared situations is Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitization. It works through reciprocal inhibition: a relaxation response incompatible with anxiety is conditioned to progressively stronger fear cues, so anxiety is displaced step by step up the hierarchy. The graded, relaxed, imaginal quality is what distinguishes it, making it the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"Flooding exposes the client to the full-intensity feared stimulus at once, without a graded hierarchy or relaxation, which is the opposite of the described technique.","c":"Implosive therapy (Stampfl) uses intense imagined exposure to exaggerated, often psychodynamic fear themes without graduation or relaxation.","d":"Modeling teaches new responses by having the client observe someone else cope with the feared stimulus, not by relaxing through a personal imagined hierarchy."}},{"q":"Viktor Frankl's concept of the 'will to meaning,' the fundamental motivational force in humans to find purpose, is central to which therapeutic school?","concept":"The 'will to meaning,' the idea that the primary human motivation is to find purpose, is the cornerstone of Viktor Frankl's logotherapy, set out in Man's Search for Meaning. Frankl proposed three pathways to meaning: creative work or deeds, experiences and love, and the attitude one takes toward unavoidable suffering. So the correct school is logotherapy.","traps":{"a":"Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (Albert Ellis) targets irrational beliefs through the ABC model, not the search for existential meaning.","c":"Existential-Humanistic therapy shares themes with Frankl but is a broader tradition (Yalom, May); the specific term 'will to meaning' is Frankl's logotherapy.","d":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (Steven Hayes) uses values-based action and psychological flexibility within a behavioral framework, distinct from Frankl's concept of the will to meaning."}},{"q":"A therapist helps a client identify and challenge irrational beliefs such as 'I absolutely must be approved by everyone' (musterbation). This therapist is most likely practising:","concept":"Challenging absolutist demands like 'I must be approved by everyone,' which Albert Ellis called musturbation, is the signature of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT). Ellis's ABC model holds that an Activating event does not cause the Consequence directly; irrational Beliefs (musts, shoulds, awfulizing, low frustration tolerance) do, and the therapist actively disputes them. The focus on rigid 'must' demands identifies REBT.","traps":{"a":"Beck's Cognitive Therapy targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through collaborative empiricism and gentle Socratic questioning, not the forceful disputation of absolutist 'musts' that is Ellis's trademark.","c":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (Marsha Linehan) balances acceptance and change with skills training for emotion dysregulation, not disputation of irrational beliefs.","d":"Reality Therapy (William Glasser) focuses on present choices and responsibility to meet basic needs, not on challenging irrational 'must' beliefs."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024, 2025] The Premack Principle states that:","concept":"The Premack Principle, formulated by David Premack in 1965, states that a more probable (higher-frequency) behavior can serve as a reinforcer for a less probable (lower-frequency) behavior. This is 'Grandma's rule': eat your vegetables (low probability) and then you get dessert (high probability). So access to the preferred activity reinforces the less-preferred one, making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"This reverses the principle; a low-probability behavior cannot reinforce a high-probability one, since it is the preferred (high-probability) activity that does the reinforcing.","c":"Reinforcement after every correct response describes a continuous reinforcement schedule, a separate operant concept unrelated to the Premack relationship between behaviors.","d":"The claim that punishment beats reinforcement is a general (and incorrect) behavioral assertion, not a statement of the Premack Principle."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] A therapist uses 'time-out' to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as:","concept":"In operant terms, the label depends on whether something is added or removed and whether behavior goes up or down. Time-out (time-out from positive reinforcement) removes access to a desirable stimulus such as attention or activity, and it is used to decrease behavior. Removing a pleasant stimulus to reduce behavior is negative punishment, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus (for example a reprimand) to decrease behavior, whereas time-out takes something away rather than adding it.","b":"Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior, the opposite of time-out's goal of reducing behavior.","d":"Positive reinforcement adds a desirable stimulus to increase behavior, which is the reverse of removing reinforcement to suppress aggression."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic approach focuses on the 'real relationship' and the therapist's unconditional positive regard, rather than the working alliance per se?","concept":"Carl Rogers's Person-Centered Therapy holds that the therapeutic relationship itself is the engine of change. Its three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding. Because the emphasis is on the genuine, prizing relationship and unconditional positive regard rather than on a task-focused working alliance (Edward Bordin, 1979), the answer is Person-Centered Therapy.","traps":{"a":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) works through transference interpretation and insight into unconscious conflict, not through Rogers's unconditional positive regard.","b":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy centers on modifying maladaptive thoughts and behaviors, using the relationship as a vehicle rather than the primary change agent.","d":"Interpersonal Therapy (Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman) is a structured, time-limited treatment focused on current relationship problems (grief, role transitions, disputes), not on unconditional positive regard as the core mechanism."}},{"q":"In Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT), the 'core mindfulness' skills are primarily derived from which tradition?","concept":"Marsha Linehan built Dialectical Behavior Therapy by integrating standard cognitive-behavioral change strategies with acceptance practices drawn from Zen Buddhism. The core mindfulness skills (observe, describe, participate; nonjudgmentally, one-mindfully, effectively) come directly from that Zen tradition. DBT's four skill modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, so the source of core mindfulness is Zen Buddhism.","traps":{"a":"Stoicism influenced cognitive therapy's view that beliefs shape distress, but it is not the source of DBT's mindfulness skills.","c":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) contributed concepts of the unconscious and defenses, unrelated to DBT's mindfulness practices.","d":"Existentialism informs meaning-focused therapies like logotherapy (Viktor Frankl), not the Zen-derived mindfulness training in DBT."}},{"q":"Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder is characterized in object-relations theory (Otto Kernberg, building on Melanie Klein) by splitting, the failure to integrate good and bad images of self and others. People are experienced as all-good or all-bad, and these views flip rapidly, driving the idealization-devaluation cycle in relationships. So splitting is the defense most characteristic of BPD.","traps":{"a":"Repression, keeping unacceptable material out of awareness, is a broad neurotic-level defense central to psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) generally, not the hallmark of BPD.","c":"Sublimation, channeling drives into socially valued activity, is a mature adaptive defense, not the primitive defense typical of borderline organization.","d":"Rationalization, offering plausible justifications for behavior, is a neurotic-level defense and not the signature mechanism of BPD."}},{"q":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support which population of clients?","concept":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support LGBTQ+ clients. It validates non-heteronormative sexual orientations and gender identities, addresses minority stress arising from stigma and discrimination, and explicitly rejects conversion or reparative therapy. Because its defining aim is affirming these identities, the correct population is LGBTQ+ individuals.","traps":{"a":"Individuals with anxiety disorders are treated with modalities like CBT or exposure; affirmative therapy is defined by the population it serves, not by an anxiety focus.","b":"Elderly clients with cognitive decline are served by geriatric and cognitive interventions, not by the identity-affirming framework of affirmative therapy.","d":"Children with developmental delays receive developmental and behavioral services, which is unrelated to the LGBTQ+ focus of affirmative therapy."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:","concept":"The bystander effect, that people are less likely to help when others are present, was explained by Bibb Latane and John Darley through two main mechanisms. Diffusion of responsibility means each onlooker assumes someone else will act, so personal obligation is diluted. Pluralistic ignorance means each person reads others' apparent calm as a sign that nothing is wrong. Together these give option b.","traps":{"a":"Social loafing (reduced effort in groups) and deindividuation (loss of self-awareness in crowds) are real group phenomena but are not the standard explanation for bystander non-intervention.","c":"Social comparison (Leon Festinger, 1954) and normative influence concern how we evaluate ourselves and conform to be liked, not the specific dynamics of emergency helping.","d":"Group polarization (Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni, 1969) and groupthink (Irving Janis, 1972) describe how group discussion shifts and distorts decisions, which is unrelated to why individual bystanders fail to help."}},{"q":"The classic 'bystander apathy' effect was demonstrated in the laboratory following the real-life murder of:","concept":"The laboratory study of bystander apathy was prompted by the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in Queens, New York, reportedly witnessed by many neighbors who did not intervene. Although the original claim of 38 unresponsive witnesses has since been historically disputed, the case still motivated Latane and Darley's foundational research on bystander intervention. So the answer is Kitty Genovese.","traps":{"a":"Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated in 1968, a politically motivated killing unconnected to bystander-effect research.","c":"Emmett Till's 1955 murder was a landmark in civil-rights history, not the case that inspired bystander-intervention experiments.","d":"Malala Yousafzai survived a 2012 attack and is an education activist, entirely unrelated to the origins of bystander research."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'just-world hypothesis' explains victim-blaming as a result of:","concept":"The just-world hypothesis, developed by Melvin Lerner, is the belief that the world is orderly and fair so that people get what they deserve and deserve what they get. When an innocent victim's suffering threatens this comforting belief, observers often restore it by derogating or blaming the victim rather than accepting that bad things happen randomly. So victim-blaming stems from the need to believe the world is fair, option b.","traps":{"a":"Attributing victim-blaming to inherent sadism misreads the motive; the theory says it comes from a need for a predictable, fair world, not a wish to see suffering.","c":"Attributing behavior to dispositional factors describes the fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977), a related but distinct bias, not the just-world motive.","d":"Comparing oneself with a less fortunate other is downward social comparison, a self-esteem mechanism, not the fairness belief behind just-world reasoning."}},{"q":"The phenomenon where qualified individuals (especially women / minorities) are blocked from advancing to higher career positions by an invisible barrier is called:","concept":"The glass ceiling is the invisible, often informal barrier that keeps qualified people, especially women and minorities, from rising into top organizational positions despite their competence. The term was popularized in a 1986 Wall Street Journal article by Carol Hymowitz and Timothy Schellhardt. Because the question describes an unseen barrier to advancement, the answer is the glass ceiling effect.","traps":{"a":"The Matthew effect is the accumulation of advantage in which the already successful gain more, not a barrier blocking advancement.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is a bias where one positive impression colors judgments of other traits, unrelated to career-advancement barriers.","d":"The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is the self-fulfilling prophecy by which higher expectations boost performance, the opposite of a barrier holding people back."}},{"q":"Learned helplessness, in social learning theory, refers to:","concept":"Learned helplessness, demonstrated by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967), is the passivity that develops after exposure to uncontrollable aversive events. Dogs given inescapable shocks later failed to escape shocks they could have avoided, having learned that responding was futile. The reformulated model (Lyn Abramson and colleagues) linked it to a depressogenic attributional style (internal, stable, global). So the correct description is repeated unavoidable aversive events leading to later passivity, option c.","traps":{"a":"Performing a behavior to escape an aversive stimulus is escape (negative reinforcement) learning, effectively the opposite of the helpless failure to escape.","b":"Observing a model rewarded for aggression describes Bandura's observational learning, not the effect of uncontrollable aversive events.","d":"Failing to receive reinforcement for a previously learned behavior describes extinction, which is not the same as helplessness produced by uncontrollability."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Type D' personality, identified as a risk factor for poor cardiovascular prognosis, is characterized by:","concept":"The Type D ('distressed') personality, identified by Johan Denollet, combines two stable traits: high negative affectivity (a tendency to experience worry, gloom, and irritability) and high social inhibition (suppressing the expression of these emotions in social settings). This combination roughly doubles the risk of cardiac morbidity and mortality independent of disease severity. So the correct answer is negative affectivity plus social inhibition, option b.","traps":{"a":"Hostility and competitiveness describe the Type A behavior pattern (Friedman and Rosenman), a different cardiovascular risk profile, not Type D.","c":"An optimistic, resilient outlook is protective, the opposite of the distressed Type D constellation.","d":"Perfectionism and rigidity are personality features, but they do not define the specific two-factor Type D pattern of negative affect and social inhibition."}},{"q":"The 'halo effect' is a cognitive bias in which:","concept":"The halo effect, first documented by Edward Thorndike in 1920, is a cognitive bias in which a global impression of a person spills over into ratings of their specific, unrelated traits. For example, someone judged attractive is often assumed to be more competent or kinder. Because the described bias is an overall impression coloring specific trait judgments, the answer is option a.","traps":{"b":"Taking credit for success while blaming external factors for failure is the self-serving bias, an attributional bias, not the halo effect.","c":"Overestimating how much others share one's opinions is the false consensus effect (Lee Ross and colleagues, 1977), a different social-perception error.","d":"Letting an initial impression anchor later judgments describes anchoring or the primacy effect, related but distinct from the trait-spillover of the halo effect."}},{"q":"The 'looking-glass self,' introduced by Charles Horton Cooley, posits that our self-concept is primarily formed by:","concept":"Charles Horton Cooley's looking-glass self (1902) proposes that our self-concept is socially constructed from how we believe others see us. It has three steps: we imagine how we appear to another person, we imagine that person's judgment of that appearance, and we react with a self-feeling such as pride or shame. So self-concept is formed by imagining how we appear to others and how they judge us, option c.","traps":{"a":"Innate unconscious drives shaping the self is a psychoanalytic (Freudian) view, not Cooley's socially reflected self.","b":"Comparing ourselves with similar others is Leon Festinger's social comparison theory, a different account of self-evaluation.","d":"Resolving psychosocial crises at each stage is Erik Erikson's developmental theory, not Cooley's looking-glass self."}},{"q":"In social psychology, the concept of reactance refers to which of the following phenomena?","concept":"Reactance, described in Jack Brehm's reactance theory, is the motivational arousal that arises when a person perceives that a specific freedom is threatened or eliminated. The restricted option becomes more attractive, and the person is driven to reassert the lost freedom, which explains backlash against bans and paradoxical failures of persuasion. So reactance is increased motivation to perform a behavior when freedom to do it is threatened, option b.","traps":{"a":"Complying with authority figures is obedience (studied by Stanley Milgram), essentially the opposite of the defiance seen in reactance.","c":"Changing one's attitude to match the majority is conformity, a distinct social-influence process, not reactance.","d":"Evaluating one's abilities by comparison with others is social comparison (Leon Festinger, 1954), unrelated to freedom-threat and reactance."}},{"q":"The sociological concept of anomie, as introduced by Émile Durkheim, refers to which of the following?","concept":"Anomie, introduced by Emile Durkheim, refers to a state of normlessness in which social norms become unclear, weak, or inapplicable, often during rapid social change. In this condition the usual regulation of behavior breaks down, increasing deviance and, in Durkheim's analysis, suicide. Robert Merton later extended the idea into strain theory (a gap between cultural goals and legitimate means). So anomie is a state of normlessness, option a.","traps":{"b":"The progression from traditional to modern societies is a broad theme of social evolution and modernization, not the meaning of anomie.","c":"Adapting to the values of a dominant culture is acculturation or assimilation, a different sociological process.","d":"Internalizing societal norms and values describes socialization, which is essentially the opposite of the normlessness that anomie names."}},{"q":"In Baddeley's model of working memory, the subsystem responsible for integrating information from the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single coherent episode is the:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley's model of working memory, the episodic buffer (added in 2000) is the component that integrates information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single, coherent, multimodal episode. It is a limited-capacity temporary store controlled by the central executive. Because the question asks about binding across subsystems, the answer is the episodic buffer.","traps":{"a":"The central executive is the attentional control system that allocates resources and coordinates the subsystems, but it is not itself the storage buffer that binds them into an episode.","c":"The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information only, so it is one of the slave systems being integrated, not the integrator.","d":"The phonological loop stores and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, again a slave system feeding into the buffer rather than the binding store."}},{"q":"The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:","concept":"Graham Wallas, in The Art of Thought (1926), described four stages of the creative process in this order: Preparation (gathering information and defining the problem), Incubation (unconscious processing while attention is elsewhere), Illumination (the sudden 'aha' insight), and Verification (testing and refining the idea). So the correct sequence is Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification, option c.","traps":{"a":"This places Illumination before Incubation, which is impossible since insight follows the unconscious incubation period.","b":"This starts with Incubation before Preparation, but one cannot incubate before gathering and defining the problem in the preparation stage.","d":"This begins with Illumination and ends with Incubation, completely reversing the logical flow of the creative process."}},{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?","concept":"Albert Bandura's model of observational (social) learning specifies four necessary sub-processes: Attention to the model, Retention of what was observed, Reproduction (motor reproduction) of the behavior, and Motivation to perform it. Crucially, direct external reinforcement is not required; vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model rewarded) can suffice. This distinguished Bandura from Skinner, so the component that is NOT necessary is reinforcement, option c.","traps":{"a":"Attention is one of Bandura's four essential processes; without attending to the model, learning cannot begin, so it is required.","b":"Motivation is a genuine component; it determines whether the learned behavior is actually performed, so it is necessary rather than the exception.","d":"Reproduction (motor reproduction) is required because the learner must be able to enact the observed behavior, so it belongs to the model."}},{"q":"The backward digit span test, where participants must repeat a sequence of numbers in reverse order, is primarily used to assess which type of memory system?","concept":"Digit span comes in two forms. Forward digit span, simply holding and repeating a sequence, taps short-term storage (the phonological loop). Backward digit span requires holding the numbers while mentally reversing their order, which demands active manipulation of stored information. That manipulation engages the central executive (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), making backward digit span a measure of working memory, option b.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory stores information over minutes to years; the brief, immediate reversal task of backward digit span does not tap it.","c":"Sensory memory is a very brief, high-capacity trace (iconic or echoic) that fades in under a second, not the manipulated span being tested.","d":"Procedural memory is implicit memory (Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter, 1985) for skills and habits, unrelated to the conscious manipulation of a digit sequence."}},{"q":"In psychoanalytic theory, what term describes the mental representation of psychic energy being invested or attached to a particular object, person, or idea?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, cathexis (German Besetzung) is the investment or attachment of psychic energy (libido) onto a mental representation of an object, person, or idea. The withdrawal of that energy is decathexis. Because the question asks for the term describing psychic energy invested in an object, the answer is cathexis.","traps":{"a":"Libido is the psychic energy itself (the drive energy), not the process of attaching that energy to a particular object.","c":"Fixation is the arrest of development at an earlier psychosexual stage due to over- or under-gratification, not the investment of energy in an object.","d":"Sublimation is a defense mechanism that redirects unacceptable drives into socially acceptable outlets, not the concept of energy attachment."}},{"q":"'Crystallized intelligence' is best defined as:","concept":"In the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, Raymond Cattell distinguished two broad intelligences: crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of accumulated factual knowledge, vocabulary, and verbal skills built up through education and life experience, and it tends to rise or stay stable across the lifespan. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve novel problems independent of prior learning, and it peaks in early adulthood then declines. Option c captures Gc because it names acquired knowledge and verbal skill derived from experience, which is the defining feature of crystallized intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Solving novel problems is the definition of fluid intelligence (Gf), the reasoning ability that is independent of accumulated knowledge, so it describes the opposite construct.","b":"Speed of neural processing refers to mental or processing speed (Gs), a distinct CHC ability sometimes underlying Gf, not the stored knowledge that defines crystallized intelligence.","d":"The capacity of working memory is short-term/working memory (Gsm), a separate cognitive resource for holding and manipulating information, not the experiential knowledge base of Gc."}},{"q":"Which personality inventory is directly based on Carl Jung's theory of psychological types?","concept":"The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator was developed by Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers directly from Carl Gustav Jung's theory of psychological types. It sorts people on four dichotomies (Extraversion/Introversion, Sensing/Intuition, Thinking/Feeling, Judging/Perceiving) to yield sixteen types, operationalizing Jung's attitudes and functions. Option b is correct because among these inventories only the MBTI was built explicitly on Jungian typology.","traps":{"a":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is an empirically keyed clinical psychopathology inventory, not a measure derived from Jung's type theory.","c":"The 16PF was developed by Raymond Cattell using factor analysis of trait descriptors, an empirical/statistical origin rather than Jungian typology.","d":"The NEO-PI-R by Costa and McCrae measures the Big Five (Five-Factor Model) traits, a lexical/dimensional framework unrelated to Jung's types."}},{"q":"According to Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression, the pattern of negative thoughts about self, world, and future is known as:","concept":"Aaron Temkin Beck's cognitive theory of depression proposes that depressed individuals hold pervasive negative views across three domains: the self ('I am worthless'), the world/current experience ('everything is against me'), and the future ('nothing will improve'). Beck named this pattern the negative cognitive triad, and it is fueled by underlying schemas and surfaces as automatic thoughts. Option b is correct because the triad specifically refers to negative cognition about self, world, and future.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions are the faulty processing styles (for example overgeneralization, catastrophizing) that bias thinking, not the three-domain content pattern named by the triad.","c":"Automatic thoughts are the involuntary, situation-specific negative thoughts that pop up in the moment; they are a product of the triad, not the triad itself.","d":"Dysfunctional assumptions are the deeper conditional rules or intermediate beliefs ('If I fail, I am worthless') beneath automatic thoughts, distinct from the self-world-future triad."}},{"q":"The phonological loop, a component of Baddeley's model of working memory, is primarily responsible for:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's working memory model, the phonological loop is the subsystem that handles speech-based and verbal information. It has two parts: a phonological store that briefly holds sound-based traces (about two seconds) and an articulatory rehearsal process that refreshes them by subvocal repetition. Option b is correct because storing and rehearsing auditory/verbal material is precisely the loop's function, supported by the word-length effect, articulatory suppression, and phonological similarity effect.","traps":{"a":"Visual and spatial processing is the job of the visuospatial sketchpad, the separate slave system for imagery and spatial location, not the phonological loop.","c":"Coordinating and integrating information from different sources describes the central executive (and the later episodic buffer), the attentional controller rather than the verbal store.","d":"Managing long-term memory retrieval is not the loop's role; the episodic buffer, added later, links working memory to long-term memory."}},{"q":"Weber's Law is best described by which of the following statements?","concept":"Weber's Law, from Ernst Heinrich Weber, states that the just-noticeable difference (JND) between two stimuli is a constant proportion of the original stimulus intensity, expressed as the Weber fraction delta-I over I equals k. So a heavier baseline weight or brighter baseline light requires a proportionally larger change to be detected. Option b is correct because it states this proportional (constant-fraction) relationship; Gustav Fechner later extended it into a logarithmic law relating sensation to intensity.","traps":{"a":"A fixed absolute JND for any stimulus is exactly what Weber's Law denies; the JND grows with stimulus magnitude rather than staying a constant amount.","c":"Detection depending solely on exposure duration is a temporal-summation idea, not Weber's Law, which concerns the ratio of intensity change to baseline intensity.","d":"Claiming perception is unaffected by intensity changes contradicts the whole premise of difference thresholds and Weber's proportional relationship."}},{"q":"Wolpe's systematic desensitisation pairs graded exposure to a feared stimulus with deep muscle relaxation. It is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation is grounded in the principle of reciprocal inhibition: a response that is physiologically incompatible with anxiety, deep muscle relaxation, is paired with a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli so that relaxation displaces and inhibits the anxiety response. Because relaxation and anxiety cannot occur simultaneously, repeated pairing weakens the conditioned fear. Option b is correct because reciprocal inhibition is the explicit mechanism Wolpe proposed.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withholding reinforcement to reduce a behavior; systematic desensitisation works by counter-conditioning with an incompatible response, not by removing a reinforcer.","c":"Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behavior with an unpleasant stimulus to suppress it, the opposite aim of desensitisation, which reduces anxiety through relaxation.","d":"Flooding is a competing exposure technique using intense, ungraded exposure without relaxation, so it relies on habituation/extinction rather than reciprocal inhibition."}},{"q":"The presence of two or more distinct personality states together with recurrent gaps in autobiographical recall is the core feature of:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder (DID) is defined by the presence of two or more distinct personality (identity) states, each with its own pattern of experiencing, together with recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events and personal information that exceed ordinary forgetting. In both the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM-5) and ICD-11, the combination of distinct identities plus dissociative amnesia is the core. Option c is correct because only DID pairs distinct personality states with autobiographical memory gaps.","traps":{"a":"Depersonalisation/derealisation disorder involves feelings of detachment from oneself or unreality of surroundings while reality testing stays intact, without distinct identities or amnesia.","b":"Dissociative amnesia features the memory gaps but lacks the two or more distinct personality states that define DID, so it captures only half the described picture.","d":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder marked by hallucinations, delusions, and disorganization, not a dissociative disorder with alternating identities and autobiographical amnesia."}},{"q":"The extrapyramidal side effects (dystonia, parkinsonism) of typical antipsychotics arise mainly from dopamine D2 blockade in which pathway?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors throughout the brain. Extrapyramidal side effects such as acute dystonia, drug-induced parkinsonism, akathisia, and tardive dyskinesia arise from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway, which normally regulates motor control. Option d is correct because the nigrostriatal (substantia nigra to striatum) dopamine route governs movement, so its blockade produces EPS.","traps":{"a":"The mesolimbic pathway's D2 blockade reduces the positive symptoms of psychosis (hallucinations, delusions); it is the therapeutic target, not the source of movement side effects.","b":"The mesocortical pathway, when blocked, tends to worsen negative and cognitive symptoms; it does not cause the dystonia and parkinsonism of EPS.","c":"Tuberoinfundibular D2 blockade removes dopamine's inhibition of prolactin, causing hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, menstrual changes), not extrapyramidal motor effects."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales are primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales, developed from Edgar Doll's work, assess adaptive functioning: the everyday practical skills a person actually performs across communication, daily living skills, socialisation, and motor skills. They are administered via informant interview and are central to diagnosing intellectual disability, where deficits in adaptive behaviour must accompany low IQ. Option b is correct because the Vineland measures adaptive functioning in daily living rather than any single cognitive ability.","traps":{"a":"Intelligence quotient (William Stern, 1912) is measured by IQ tests like the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet scales; the Vineland complements these but does not itself yield an IQ.","c":"Personality structure is assessed by inventories or projective tests (for example the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) or Rorschach), not by an adaptive behaviour scale.","d":"Neuropsychological memory deficits are evaluated with memory batteries (for example the Wechsler Memory Scale); the Vineland targets real-world adaptive skills, not memory."}},{"q":"Which sampling method gives every member of the population a known, non-zero probability of being selected?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling is defined by the fact that every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, which is what allows the sample to be representative and sampling error to be estimated. Its forms include simple random, stratified, cluster, and systematic sampling. Option c is correct because only probability sampling guarantees a known non-zero selection probability for each unit.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category targets using the researcher's judgment, so selection probabilities are unknown; it is a non-probability method.","b":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing subjects, giving unknown and unequal selection chances, typical of hard-to-reach populations.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately selects specific units based on the researcher's judgment of relevance, so members have no known probability of inclusion."}}]});
